; typecheck.ax — semantic analysis in Axiom.
;
; This is type checking proper: the checker infers a type for every
; expression and compares them exactly where stage0 compares them,
; because Phase 3's acceptance criterion is byte-identical AXDL
; against stage0 (the self-hosting record), and the sweep in
; scripts/check-diagnostics.sh requires total silence on the
; compiler's own source. Both constraints bind at once, so every rule
; here is either a probed/read stage0 behavior reproduced exactly, or
; a recorded gap in the UNDER-reporting direction - a checker that
; rejects a valid program is worse than no checker.
;
; The stage0 model, replicated:
;
;   * Types are structural nodes (TAG_T_* in parser.ax). `tyCompat`
;     treats a type VARIABLE on either side, at any depth, as matching
;     anything, and the poison type TAG_T_ERR matches everything so one
;     root cause never cascades.
;
;     This paragraph said "there is no unification and no substitution
;     anywhere" until 2026-08-23, and it is not what the compiler does.
;     A signature's variables are instantiated PER CALL SITE, so a
;     variable repeated across parameters is checked:
;
;         (:: pick (-> a a Int))
;         (pick "a string" 42)   -> AX3004, expected String, found Int
;
;     and the instantiation does not leak between call sites - `(+
;     (pick "s" "t") (pick 1 2))` is accepted in either order, probed
;     both ways. The `String`/`Int` fiat named here really was deleted
;     on 2026-08-15; `(wantsInt "not an int")` is AX3004 today.
;
;     WHAT IS UNSOUND is narrower and is not this rule. A variable in
;     RESULT position that no parameter mentions is chosen by the
;     caller and witnessed by nothing, so the word comes back as
;     whatever they name - see AX3040.
;
;   * Function types come from signatures, authoritatively. A `fn`
;     with no signature gets a placeholder `_fn_N` type variable and
;     every parameter typed `Int` - Int, not a wildcard (probed:
;     stage0 2185). Parameters BEYOND a signature's arrows bind at
;     fresh `_tN` wildcards. The fresh-name counter's increment sites
;     are copied from stage0 exactly, because `_tN` names render
;     inside AX3004 messages.
;
;   * Two flavors of type variable. A RENDERED one (`_tN`, `_fn_N`, a
;     source tyvar) follows stage0 everywhere, including the
;     "expected function type, found _t0" arm. A SILENT one (name "")
;     marks what stage1 cannot know - effect operations, macro heads,
;     unparseable types - and is never rendered and never the subject
;     of a diagnostic: applying it is quiet, reading a field off it
;     is quiet. Under-report, never mis-report.
;
;   * Emission anchors: expression positions anchor through spanOf,
;     which mirrors stage0's Expr::span() (an application's span is
;     its head's, a field access's its BASE's, a let's its body's). A
;     node stage1 kept no span for suppresses the diagnostic rather
;     than mis-pointing it; suppression only ever omits lines, never
;     reorders the ones stage0 also emits.
;
; Pass order (probed, pinned by goldens 040/130): every AX3006, then
; every AX3015, then the body walk in declaration order, with
; signature scope entries pushed AS ENCOUNTERED - a `set` in
; an earlier body does not see a later signature (probed: AX3001, not
; AX3012).
;
; Dependencies: core.ax, parser.ax, diag.ax, Mem, Str, Fmt, Vec.
(import core)
(import Mem)
(import Str)
(import Fmt)
(import Vec)
(import Link)
(import lexer)
(import parser)
(import diag)
(import raw)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Type rendering: stage0's `Display for TypeId`, byte for byte, since
; these strings appear verbatim inside AX3004 messages and the gate
; compares whole lines. The shapes, pinned by `axiom symbols` probes
; and tests/selfhost/720-type-render.ax:
;
;   {unknown}                        poison (and an absent type)
;   a, _t3                           type variable, its name alone
;   Int / Maybe Int                  constructor; args space-separated,
;                                    NO parens - nested applications
;                                    flatten (`Option List Int`)
;   (Int -> (Int -> Bool))           arrow, always parenthesised, binary
;   () / (Int, Bool)                 tuple; empty tuple is unit
;   [Int]                            list
;   *Int                             pointer
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A type variable is born `_t<N>` from ONE counter that runs across the
; whole program, standard library first, so the name a diagnostic showed
; moved every time the library grew - fixture 498 read `_t255` until
; `Html.ax` landed and `_t265` after, with nothing about the fixture
; changed. Rendering names variables by order of first appearance
; instead: `_a`, `_b`, ... `_z`, `_a1`, ... The table is built per
; RENDER, and `emitMismatchTy` builds one table for both of a
; mismatch's types, so the same variable on both sides is the same
; letter and two different ones are not. Only the checker's own `_t<N>`
; variables are renamed: a variable the program spelled (`a` in
; `(-> a (Option a))`) keeps its spelling, in hover text and here. The
; `_t<N>` name stays the variable's identity inside the checker.
(pub :: tyRender (-> ASTNode String))
(pub fn (tyRender t)
  {
    (let names vecNew)
    {
      (tyVarsInto t names)
      (tyRenderIn t names)
    }
  })

(pub :: tyVarIndex (-> Strings String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (tyVarIndex names name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr names i) name)
      (Some i)
      (tyVarIndex names name (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tyVarLabel (-> Int String))
(pub fn (tyVarLabel i)
  {
    (let letter (strFromByte (+ 97 (% i 26))))
    (if (< i 26)
      (concat "_" letter)
      (concat (concat "_" letter) (fmtInt (/ i 26))))
  })

(pub :: tyVarsInto (-> ASTNode Strings Int))
(pub fn (tyVarsInto t0 names)
  ; a pinned placeholder is not a variable the reader must be shown
  {
    (let t (tyResolve t0))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      0
      {
        (let tag (nodeTag t))
        (if (== tag TAG_T_VAR)
          ; ANY minted prefix, not just `_t`. There are two now -
          ; `_iN` from instantiation, which may be pinned, and `_tN`
          ; from "not known yet", which may not - and both are names
          ; the user did not write, so both get a reader-friendly
          ; label. Testing `_t` alone printed a raw `_i267` into a
          ; diagnostic the moment the second prefix existed.
          (if (&& (strStartsWith (nodeAName t) "_") (isNone (tyVarIndex names (nodeAName t) 0)))
            {
              (vecPush names (nodeAName t))
              0
            }
            0)
          (if (== tag TAG_T_CON)
            (tyVarsIntoAll (nodeBChildren t) 0 names)
            (if (== tag TAG_T_ARR)
              {
                (tyVarsInto (nodeAChild t) names)
                (tyVarsInto (nodeBChild t) names)
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_T_TUP)
                (tyVarsIntoAll (nodeAChildren t) 0 names)
                (if (== tag TAG_T_PTR)
                  (tyVarsInto (nodeAChild t) names)
                  0)))))
      })
  })

(pub :: tyVarsIntoAll (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Strings Int))
(pub fn (tyVarsIntoAll xs i names)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    0
    {
      (tyVarsInto (vecGet xs i) names)
      (tyVarsIntoAll xs (+ i 1) names)
    }))

(pub :: tyRenderIn (-> ASTNode Strings String))
(pub fn (tyRenderIn t0 names)
  (tyRenderScoped t0 names None))

(:: tyRenderScoped (-> ASTNode Strings (Option String) String))
(fn (tyRenderScoped t0 names owner)
  ; render what a placeholder was PINNED to, not the placeholder
  {
    (let t (tyResolve t0))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      "{unknown}"
      {
        (let tag (nodeTag t))
        (if (== tag TAG_T_VAR)
          (if (let (Some index) (tyVarIndex names (nodeAName t) 0))
            (tyVarLabel index)
            (nodeAName t))
          (if (== tag TAG_T_CON)
            (tyRenderAppScoped (typeNameForScope (nodeAName t) owner) (nodeBChildren t) names owner)
            (if (== tag TAG_T_ARR)
              (concat
                (concat (concat (concat "(" (tyRenderScoped (nodeAChild t) names owner)) " -> ")
                  (tyRenderScoped (nodeBChild t) names owner))
                ")")
              (if (== tag TAG_T_TUP)
                (concat (concat "(" (tyRenderJoinScoped (nodeAChildren t) names owner)) ")")
                (if (== tag TAG_T_PTR)
                  (concat "*" (tyRenderScoped (nodeAChild t) names owner))
                  "{unknown}")))))
      })
  })

(:: tyRenderAppScoped (-> String (Vec ASTNode) Strings (Option String) String))
(fn (tyRenderAppScoped head args names owner)
  {
    (let out (strBuilderPush strBuilderNew head))
    (for i in 0..(vecLen args)
      {
        (strBuilderPush out " ")
        (strBuilderPush out (tyRenderScoped (vecGet args i) names owner))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

(:: tyRenderJoinScoped (-> (Vec ASTNode) Strings (Option String) String))
(fn (tyRenderJoinScoped xs names owner)
  {
    (let out strBuilderNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
      {
        (if (> i 0)
          (strBuilderPush out ", "))
        (strBuilderPush out (tyRenderScoped (vecGet xs i) names owner))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

(:: typeNameForScope (-> String (Option String) String))
(fn (typeNameForScope name owner)
  (if (== (typeModuleSame (modOf name) owner) 1)
    (bareOf name)
    (srcSpelling name)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Type predicates and constructors.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: tyIsErr (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsErr t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ERR)
      1
      0)))

; The silent wildcard: a nameless type variable. It marks the types
; stage1 cannot know (effect operations, macro spines, a type its
; grammar could not represent) and is never rendered - tyCompat lets
; it match anything, and the two places stage0 would still complain
; about a TVar (applying a non-function, reading a field) go quiet
; for it specifically.
(pub :: tyIsSilentWild (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsSilentWild t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    1
    (if (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR) (== (strLen (nodeAName t)) 0))
      1
      0)))

(pub :: mkSilentWild ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkSilentWild)
  (mkTVar ""))

; Whether this type is still unknown in the sense that refuses
; consumption: a `_tN` "not known yet" placeholder, after resolving
; whatever instantiation already pinned. Source variables are rigid
; user polymorphism with their own refusal machinery (a `+` over `a`
; already draws AX3004); minted `_iN` placeholders belong to the
; instantiation env; the silent wildcard and `0` mark what stage1
; alone cannot know, and refusing those would refuse the unknowable
; rather than the untyped. Answers on the RESOLVED type, so a
; placeholder pinned to ground is ground.
(pub :: tyIsUnknown (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsUnknown t0)
  {
    (let t (tyResolve t0))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      0
      (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR)
        0
        ; Exactly the `_tN` class: `freshTVar`'s "not known yet".
        ; Every other placeholder is lenient by construction - `_iN`
        ; instantiation variables (pinned through their cells),
        ; `_fn_N` declaration placeholders (ground truth exists;
        ; entry-first order may simply not have inferred it yet, as
        ; a cross-module macro's nullary `made` proves), the empty
        ; variable, and source variables. A name a user WROTE that
        ; starts with `_t` is indistinguishable from a minted one
        ; and refuses with it; nobody writes that name. A future
        ; placeholder class is lenient until it opts in here: the
        ; fail-closed direction is silence, the trunk behavior.
        (if (strStartsWith (nodeAName t) "_t")
          1
          0)))
  })

; Whether this type is ground enough to consume an unknown against:
; anything that resolves to a non-variable. A variable on this side
; - `_tN`, an unpinned `_iN`, a source `a` - means both sides are
; still open, and the ignorance propagates to the next consumer
; rather than refusing here.
(pub :: tyIsGround (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsGround t0)
  {
    (let t (tyResolve t0))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      0
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR)
        0
        1))
  })

; AX3004 at a value of unknown type consumed where a ground type is
; required: the emitter must choose a representation - `add` or
; `fadd`, an `i64` or a `double` slot - and ignorance is not a
; choice. `(lambda (x) (+ x 1))` applied to `1.5` ran integer `add`
; on double bits, at exit 1, with no diagnostic; the same shape
; through a call argument, an `if` condition, a struct field and a
; `set` value. The `found` is fixed: these types have no source
; spelling to render (a `_tN` would print as the `_a` label of a
; name the user never wrote).
(pub :: refuseUnknownOperand (-> TC String ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (refuseUnknownOperand tc want valTy span)
  (if (== (tyIsUnknown valTy) 0)
    0
    (if (== span 0)
      0
      {
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
              (concat (concat "type mismatch: expected " want) ", found {unknown}") None
              (Some "declare the type with a `(:: ...)` signature")
              "no signature declares this value's type - a `lambda` parameter has nowhere to declare one, so name the function and sign it - and each representation lowers differently")
            "this has an unknown type"))
        1
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; USE-DRIVEN LAMBDA INFERENCE (grounding). A `let`-bound lambda used
; to be checked where it sat, against nothing: every parameter a
; fresh `_tN`, and every consumption of one a `{unknown}` refusal -
; or, before the refusals, a silent hole the emitter filled with a
; guess that read back 122. The use knows what the definition
; cannot: defer the check to the first application, bind the
; parameters to the argument types it carries, and check the body
; then. What no use witnesses is flushed, not guessed: a lambda
; that escapes (stored, returned, sequenced past, outlived by its
; scope) is checked standalone and only its `{unknown}` refusals
; are dropped - a bad name is bad whatever the use, but ignorance
; no use witnesses is not a refusal.
;
; A pending lambda flows as THE NODE ITSELF where a type is
; expected. `tyRender` falls through to `{unknown}` on it, which is
; what a diagnostic about one should say, and `tyCompat` answers 1
; on it - there is no arrow yet to compare against, and answering
; 0 refused sound programs with `expected {unknown}, found
; {unknown}`. Every consumer that CAN judge it still does: a use
; grounds it, a declaration checks it, an escape flushes it.
;
; THE STAMP is the lambda's own ty slot (word 6), which nothing
; downstream reads for a lambda: codegen consults it for
; declarations, fields and applications, and `resolveStructField`
; for fields - never for `TAG_E_LAM`. Five states, two of them
; terminal:
;
;   0         ungrounded (fresh, or flushed and still pending)
;   TAG_T_ARR grounded: the arrow over the REMAINING parameters -
;             this level's and whatever deeper levels are still
;             pending; captures and supplied outer parameters the
;             body reads but the arrow must not name
;   TAG_NIL   partial: a record whose `a` is a vector of `SEnt`s
;             (captures, then the outer parameters witnessed so
;             far) and whose `b` is the node's OWN position in it.
;             The outer node and the rest node hold two records
;             over one shared entries vector, their `b` words one
;             apart.
;   __grounding
;             in progress: the first pass is running; recursive
;             uses read this (a name-compared type variable that
;             never escapes the grounding)
;   TAG_T_ERR broken: the body failed and its diagnostics are out,
;             so later uses go poison-quiet (cascade suppression)
;             rather than re-checking (duplicates)
;
; First use wins: a second use of a grounded node verifies against
; the arrow through the normal application path, and a second use
; of a partial outer node verifies its argument against the record.
; No body is ever checked twice for one grounding - except a
; self-recursive one, which gets exactly two passes (below).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1 when `t` is a lambda node flowing as a type: an ungrounded
; closure whose parameters no use has witnessed yet.
(pub :: isPendingNode (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (isPendingNode t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_E_LAM)
      1
      0)))

(pub :: lamStamp (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (lamStamp node)
  (nodeTyChild node))

(pub :: setLamStamp (-> ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (setLamStamp node s)
  (setNodeTyChild node s))

(pub :: stampIsArrow (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (stampIsArrow s)
  (if (nodeSame s nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag s) TAG_T_ARR)
      1
      0)))

(pub :: stampIsPartial (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (stampIsPartial s)
  (if (nodeSame s nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag s) TAG_NIL)
      1
      0)))

(pub :: mkPartialRec (-> (Vec SEnt) Int Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (mkPartialRec ents own sp)
  (nodeBuildPartial ents own sp))

; Only a partial-record stamp owns this payload: its retained vector
; contains scope entries, while the sealed node arena erases that type.
(pub :: partialEnts (-> ASTNode (Vec SEnt)))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (partialEnts s)
  (as (nodeAWords s) (Vec SEnt)))

; The node's OWN position: where its parameters start in the
; record (captures below, witnessed parameters above). It rides
; the RECORD's `b` word, not the lambda's `c` word: a seed-built
; stage1 segfaults emitting any lambda whose `c` word is nonzero
; (measured 2026-10-02, `d1/axc` on a two-line partial
; application), so the checker's stamp touches only the ty slot it
; owns, and the node itself is never written.
(pub :: partialOwn (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (partialOwn s)
  (nodeB s))

(pub :: stampIsProg (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (stampIsProg s)
  (if (nodeSame s nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag s) TAG_T_VAR)
      (if (strEq (nodeAName s) "__grounding")
        1
        0)
      0)))

(pub :: mkProgStamp ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkProgStamp)
  (mkTVar "__grounding"))

(pub :: stampIsBroken (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (stampIsBroken s)
  (if (nodeSame s nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag s) TAG_T_ERR)
      1
      0)))

; The out-vector swap: a throwaway home for a speculative pass's
; diagnostics. The swapper either restores filtered (a flush keeps
; what it keeps) or restores bare (a first pass discards all).
(pub :: outvecSwapOut (-> TC Diags))
(pub fn (outvecSwapOut tc)
  {
    (let real (tcOutVec tc))
    (tcSetOutVec tc (:: vecNew Diags))
    real
  })

(pub :: outvecSwapIn (-> TC Diags Int))
(pub fn (outvecSwapIn tc real)
  (tcSetOutVec tc real))

; 1 when `d` is an `{unknown}` refusal: AX3004 whose message names
; the unknowable.
(pub :: diagIsUnknownRefusal (-> diag::Diag Int))
(pub fn (diagIsUnknownRefusal d)
  (if (strEq (diagCode d) "AX3004")
    (if (isSome (strFind (diagMsg d) "{unknown}" 0))
      1
      0)
    0))

(pub :: outvecCarryOver (-> TC Diags Int Int))
(pub fn (outvecCarryOver tc fresh i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fresh))
    0
    {
      (if (== (diagIsUnknownRefusal (vecGet fresh i)) 0)
        (vecPush (tcOutVec tc) (vecGet fresh i)))
      (outvecCarryOver tc fresh (+ i 1))
    }))

; Restore `real`, carrying over every diagnostic EXCEPT the
; `{unknown}` refusals.
(pub :: outvecRestoreFiltered (-> TC Diags Int))
(pub fn (outvecRestoreFiltered tc real)
  {
    (let fresh (tcOutVec tc))
    (tcSetOutVec tc real)
    (outvecCarryOver tc fresh 0)
  })

; FLUSH: verify a pending lambda that escapes without a use. The
; standalone check runs with fresh parameters; its `{unknown}`
; refusals are dropped (no use witnesses them, so there is nothing
; to refuse) and everything else is kept. The stamp is restored after
; checking so a later use still grounds. While checking, the in-progress
; marker stops a recursive alias from flushing the same body again.
; Grounded and broken nodes are not flushed - one is verified, the
; other's diagnostics are out.
; Answers 0.
; Refuse a pending lambda where a ground value is required: the body
; is verified LENIENTLY first (its own errors are still reported -
; refusing the shape must not silence the body), then the shape
; refusal names the position. Refusal paths keep both diagnostics.
(pub :: refusePendingOperand (-> TC ASTNode String Int Int))
(pub fn (refusePendingOperand tc node expected span)
  {
    (flushPending tc node span)
    (emitMismatch tc expected "unapplied function" span)
  })

; FLUSH, answering the arrow: for a use that witnesses NOTHING
; about the parameters (a type-variable parameter, a returned
; closure, a lambda nested in a body) the body is verified
; leniently and the INFERRED arrow answers - the old standalone
; behavior, which pinning, evidence, and rendering require to be
; a TYPE, never a pending node. Stamps the arrow (first use
; wins: later uses verify against it instead of re-checking the
; body). The in-progress marker guards re-entry: a recursive use
; during the check answers poison after naming the too-early
; call, which the filter below KEEPS (only `{unknown}` drops).
(pub :: groundStandalone (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundStandalone tc t span)
  (if (== (isPendingNode t) 0)
    t
    {
      (let s (lamStamp t))
      (if (== (stampIsArrow s) 1)
        s
        (if (== (stampIsBroken s) 1)
          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
          (if (== (stampIsProg s) 1)
            {
              (emitDiag tc
                (diagSetLabel
                  (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
                    "type mismatch: cannot check this function while its parameter types are still being inferred"
                    None
                    (Some
                      "use it after its definition is complete - a use during its own inference resolves nothing yet")
                    "inference grounds a lambda's parameters from its uses; this use runs before the grounding finished")
                  "this use is too early to check"))
              (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
            }
            {
              (let real (outvecSwapOut tc))
              {
                (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
                {
                  (let arr
                    {
                      (if (== (stampIsPartial s) 1)
                        (groundPushPrefix tc (partialEnts s) 0))
                      (setLamStamp t (mkProgStamp))
                      (checkLam tc t)
                    })
                  {
                    (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
                    (outvecRestoreFiltered tc real)
                    (groundFinish tc t arr)
                  }
                }
              }
            })))
    }))

(pub :: flushPending (-> TC ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (flushPending tc t span)
  (if (== (isPendingNode t) 0)
    0
    {
      (let s (lamStamp t))
      (if (== (stampIsArrow s) 1)
        0
        (if (== (stampIsBroken s) 1)
          0
          (if (== (stampIsProg s) 1)
            {
              (groundStandalone tc t span)
              0
            }
            ; A partial node flushes with its PREFIX in scope: the
            ; outer parameters are witnessed, and checking without
            ; them would refuse names the grounding binds. The
            ; `{unknown}` filter still drops what no use witnesses.
            {
              (let real (outvecSwapOut tc))
              {
                (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
                {
                  (if (== (stampIsPartial s) 1)
                    (groundPushPrefix tc (partialEnts s) 0))
                  (setLamStamp t (mkProgStamp))
                  (checkLam tc t)
                  (setLamStamp t s)
                  (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
                  (outvecRestoreFiltered tc real)
                }
              }
            })))
    }))

; GROUND one application level: witness this level's argument
; against `node` and answer the grounded arrow - or, for the outer
; level of a curried chain, the REST node still pending, which the
; enclosing application grounds in turn. Broken nodes answer
; poison quietly; nodes mid-grounding (a recursive use during the
; first pass) answer poison after naming the recursion, which the
; first pass's swap discards for honest self-recursion and the user
; sees only when an alias hid the recursion from the two-pass
; trigger.
(pub :: groundLamArg (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundLamArg tc node argTy span)
  ; Grounded nodes answer the arrow: first use wins, and the
  ; normal path verifies this use against it. Re-grounding here
  ; would check the body WITHOUT its prefix (the record is gone,
  ; replaced by the arrow) - refusing names the first grounding
  ; bound, and duplicating every diagnostic the body earned.
  {
    (let s (lamStamp node))
    (if (== (stampIsArrow s) 1)
      s
      (if (== (stampIsProg s) 1)
        {
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
                "type mismatch: cannot call this function while its parameter types are still being inferred"
                None
                (Some
                  "call it after its definition is complete - a recursive call through a `let` alias hides the recursion from inference")
                "inference grounds a lambda's parameters from its uses; this use runs before the grounding finished")
              "this call is too early to check"))
          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
        }
        (if (== (stampIsBroken s) 1)
          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
          (groundLamStage tc node argTy span s))))
  })

; The staging half of grounding. The prefix (the record's entries,
; or nothing) names the outer parameters an earlier level
; witnessed; this level's argument witnesses the next one.
(pub :: groundLamStage (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundLamStage tc node argTy span s)
  ; The parser curries, so one parameter is the shape; a flat
  ; spelling is curried at defer time, and the flat arm is its
  ; backstop.
  {
    (let prefix
      (if (== (stampIsPartial s) 1)
        (partialEnts s)
        vecNew))
    (if (!= (vecLen (nodeANames node)) 1)
      (groundLamFlat tc node span)
      (if (== (nodeTag (nodeBChild node)) TAG_E_LAM)
        (groundLamOuter tc node argTy span prefix)
        (groundLamSelf tc node argTy span prefix)))
  })

; The outer level of a curried chain: witness, record, and answer
; the REST pending. No check runs - the rest's body is checked when
; the rest is applied, with the full prefix in scope. The node's
; OWN position rides its record's `b` word (0 when there is no
; record yet): a use after the first at the same level verifies
; against the record instead of rebinding (first use wins; uses
; disagree at the use, not in the body), while a MIDDLE level's
; first use witnesses a NEW position - the record then holds
; exactly the outer positions. Records are immutable: the new
; entries vector is a clone plus the new entry, shared by two
; records whose `b` words differ (the node's own position, and the
; rest's, one deeper), so outer levels keep witnessing what they
; witnessed.
(pub :: groundLamOuter (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int (Vec SEnt) ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundLamOuter tc node argTy span prefix)
  {
    (let rest (nodeBChild node))
    {
      (let own
        {
          (let st (lamStamp node))
          (if (== (stampIsPartial st) 1)
            (partialOwn st)
            0)
        })
      (if (> (vecLen prefix) own)
        {
          (if (== (tyCompat tc argTy (sEntTy (vecGet prefix own))) 0)
            (emitMismatchTy tc (sEntTy (vecGet prefix own)) argTy span))
          rest
        }
        {
          (let ents (prefixClonePlus prefix (vecGet (nodeANames node) 0) argTy))
          {
            (setLamStamp node (mkPartialRec ents own span))
            (setLamStamp rest (mkPartialRec ents (+ own 1) span))
            rest
          }
        })
    }
  })

(pub :: prefixClonePlus (-> (Vec SEnt) String ASTNode (Vec SEnt)))
(pub fn (prefixClonePlus prefix name ty)
  {
    (let ents vecNew)
    {
      (prefixCloneInto prefix ents 0)
      (vecPush ents (SEnt :name name :mutable 0 :bsp 0 :ty ty :used 0))
      ents
    }
  })

(pub :: prefixCloneInto (-> (Vec SEnt) (Vec SEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (prefixCloneInto prefix ents i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen prefix))
    0
    {
      (vecPush ents (vecGet prefix i))
      (prefixCloneInto prefix ents (+ i 1))
    }))

; The innermost level: the full environment is known (the prefix
; plus this argument), so the body is CHECKED and the node answers
; a grounded arrow. Self-recursion gets exactly two passes: the
; first grounds under a throwaway vector (recursive uses read the
; in-progress marker), the second verifies against the first's
; arrow. `checkLamAgainst` binds the parameter from the built
; arrow, recursing through nothing - this shape's body is not a
; lambda - so its fallback (a flat multi-parameter spelling, or an
; arrow run out) is unreachable here.
(pub :: groundLamSelf (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int (Vec SEnt) ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundLamSelf tc node argTy span prefix)
  {
    (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
    {
      (groundPushPrefix tc prefix 0)
      (setLamStamp node (mkProgStamp))
      ; A FORWARD alias: the body names this lambda through a
      ; name unbound at the defer, so the capture record does
      ; not hold it. The emitter calls a `let`-bound name
      ; directly (`call i64 @g`), which is undefined for an
      ; alias - accepting would miscompile where the old
      ; checker refused AX3001 - so the grounding refuses and
      ; the node goes broken-terminal.
      {
        (let arrow (mkTArr argTy (freshTVar tc 1)))
        {
          (let ans
            {
              (let sm (groundSelfMention tc node (nodeBChild node) prefix))
              (if (== sm 2)
                {
                  (emitDiag tc
                    (diagSetLabel
                      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
                        "type mismatch: this function calls itself through an alias bound after its definition, which cannot be compiled"
                        None
                        (Some
                          "name the function with `fn` and recurse through its name - a top-level name compiles to a direct call")
                        "inference grounds a lambda's parameters from its uses; a use through a forward alias resolves only after the definition, past what the emitter can call")
                      "this call cannot be compiled"))
                  (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                }
                (if (== sm 1)
                  (groundLamRec tc node arrow)
                  (checkLamAgainst tc node arrow)))
            })
          {
            (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
            (groundFinish tc node ans)
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: groundPushPrefix (-> TC (Vec SEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (groundPushPrefix tc prefix i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen prefix))
    0
    {
      (vecPush (tcScopeVec tc) (vecGet prefix i))
      (groundPushPrefix tc prefix (+ i 1))
    }))

; The first pass grounds under a throwaway vector; the second
; verifies against the first's arrow, whose parameters are the
; argument types (unchanged between passes), so recursive calls
; check their arguments honestly. The first pass's diagnostics are
; discarded and the second's are definitive: no diagnostic is
; emitted twice for one grounding.
(pub :: groundLamRec (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundLamRec tc node arrow)
  {
    (let real (outvecSwapOut tc))
    {
      (let pre (checkLamAgainst tc node arrow))
      {
        (outvecSwapIn tc real)
        (setLamStamp node pre)
        (checkLamAgainst tc node arrow)
      }
    }
  })

; Stamp the answer: poison anywhere in it means the body's
; diagnostics are already out, so the node goes broken-terminal
; (later uses go quiet); otherwise the answer stamps the node as
; is. It names exactly the REMAINING parameters - the node's own
; (bound from this level's argument) plus whatever deeper levels
; are still pending - because the prefix it was checked under
; holds only captures and already-supplied outer parameters, which
; the body reads but the arrow must not name (naming a capture
; would answer `(a -> (Int -> Int))` for a one-parameter closure
; over `z : a`, refusing every honest call).
(pub :: groundFinish (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundFinish tc node ans)
  (if (== (tyHasPoison ans) 1)
    {
      (setLamStamp node (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
      ans
    }
    {
      (setLamStamp node ans)
      ans
    }))

; 0 when `body` never names `node`, 1 when it names it through
; the record (a capture the defer resolved: the two-pass trigger),
; 2 when it names it through a FORWARD alias (unbound at the
; defer, so the record does not hold it: refused, since the
; emitter cannot call it). `capScanInto` collects the free
; variables (shadowing-correct: inner binders shadow, outer names
; resolve through the scope); each is resolved, compared by
; pointer, and looked up in the record. A false positive only buys
; a second pass (or a refusal the user can lift by naming the
; function), while a miss would leave recursion unverified - and a
; miss still answers audibly, through the in-progress marker,
; never silently. Forms `capScanInto` does not walk (a `cast` over
; the call, and the like) miss by construction.
(pub :: groundSelfMention (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode (Vec SEnt) Int))
(pub fn (groundSelfMention tc node body prefix)
  {
    (let bound vecNew)
    {
      (let acc vecNew)
      {
        (capScanInto body bound acc)
        (groundAccMentions tc acc node prefix 0)
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: groundAccMentions (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode (Vec SEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (groundAccMentions tc acc node prefix i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen acc))
    0
    {
      (let found (scopeFindFrame tc (nodeAName (vecGet acc i))))
      (if (let (Some ent) found)
        (if (nodeSame ent.ty node)
          (if (== (groundNameInPrefix prefix (nodeAName (vecGet acc i)) 0) 1)
            1
            2)
          (groundAccMentions tc acc node prefix (+ i 1)))
        (groundAccMentions tc acc node prefix (+ i 1)))
    }))

; 1 when `name` is bound anywhere in `prefix` (captures and outer
; parameters alike): the defer (or an earlier level) resolved it,
; so it is not forward.
(pub :: groundNameInPrefix (-> (Vec SEnt) String Int Int))
(pub fn (groundNameInPrefix prefix name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen prefix))
    0
    (if (strEq (sEntName (vecGet prefix i)) name)
      1
      (groundNameInPrefix prefix name (+ i 1)))))

; Flat multi-parameter lambdas never reach grounding (the defer
; curries them), so this arm refuses audibly rather than guessing:
; it is unreachable in practice, and loud if it ever is not.
(pub :: groundLamFlat (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundLamFlat tc node span)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
          "type mismatch: cannot infer the parameter types of this multi-parameter lambda" None
          (Some "name the function and sign it with a `(:: ...)` signature")
          "inference grounds one parameter per application level; this lambda kept a flat spelling")
        "cannot ground this call"))
    (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
  })

; DEFER: validate a lambda literal NOW (a nullary one can never be
; called; `true`/`false` refuse as binders) and answer the NODE
; ITSELF pending - its body is checked when a use grounds it, or
; when it escapes. A flat multi-parameter spelling is curried into
; a COPY (the emitter still sees the original, which checks the
; same: same parameters, same body, regrouped), so grounding meets
; one parameter per level everywhere. The defer STAMPS the node's
; captures: the free variables resolve NOW (their bindings die at
; the let-exit the pending node outlives) into a record the
; grounding and the flush push before they check. Captures are
; entries 0..c of the same record the prefix extends, and the
; record's `b` word holds the node's own position - one record,
; one rule.
(pub :: deferLam (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (deferLam tc e)
  {
    (checkLamValidate tc e)
    {
      (let n
        (if (> (vecLen (nodeANames e)) 1)
          (curryLam (nodeANames e) (nodeBChild e) (nodeSpan e) 0)
          e))
      {
        (deferStampCaptures tc n)
        n
      }
    }
  })

; Collect the node's free variables (`capScanInto`: inner binders
; shadow, so parameters are never captures), resolve each through
; the current frame (globals resolve nowhere and need no record -
; they are visible everywhere), and stamp COPIES of the entries:
; types are static, so a copy carries what the later check needs.
; A node that already carries a grounding - an arrow, a breakage,
; or a record with witnessed parameters - keeps it: re-deferring
; (a flush checks the outer node, which re-defers the rest) must
; refresh captures, never clobber evidence.
(pub :: deferStampCaptures (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (deferStampCaptures tc n)
  {
    (let s (lamStamp n))
    (if (|| (== (stampIsArrow s) 1) (== (stampIsBroken s) 1))
      0
      (if (&& (== (stampIsPartial s) 1) (> (vecLen (partialEnts s)) (partialOwn s)))
        0
        {
          (let bound vecNew)
          {
            (let acc vecNew)
            {
              (capScanInto n bound acc)
              {
                (let ents vecNew)
                {
                  (deferResolveCaps tc acc ents 0)
                  (setLamStamp n (mkPartialRec ents (vecLen ents) (nodeSpan n)))
                  0
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }))
  })

(pub :: deferResolveCaps (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) (Vec SEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (deferResolveCaps tc acc ents i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen acc))
    0
    {
      (let found (scopeFindFrame tc (nodeAName (vecGet acc i))))
      {
        (if (let (Some ent) found)
          (vecPush ents
            (SEnt :name ent.name :mutable ent.mutable :bsp ent.bsp :ty ent.ty :used ent.used)))
        (deferResolveCaps tc acc ents (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; Flush every pending lambda bound at or above `mark`: the scope is
; closing, so these bindings outlive no further use IN this scope.
; (Aliases outside it still ground: the flush leaves the stamp
; alone.) Grounded and broken nodes are skipped by `flushPending`
; itself; the seen-set skips aliases of one node within the scan,
; so one escaping lambda is flushed once however many names it has
; here. Answers 0.
(pub :: flushScopePending (-> TC Int Int))
(pub fn (flushScopePending tc mark)
  {
    (let seen vecNew)
    (flushScopeVec tc (tcScopeVec tc) mark seen)
  })

(pub :: flushScopeVec (-> TC (Vec SEnt) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (flushScopeVec tc scope i seen)
  (if (>= i (vecLen scope))
    0
    {
      (let ty (sEntTy (vecGet scope i)))
      {
        (if (== (isPendingNode ty) 1)
          (if (== (flushSeen seen ty 0) 0)
            {
              (vecPush seen ty)
              (flushPending tc ty (spanOf ty))
            }))
        (flushScopeVec tc scope (+ i 1) seen)
      }
    }))

(pub :: flushSeen (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (flushSeen seen ty i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen seen))
    0
    (if (nodeSame (vecGet seen i) ty)
      1
      (flushSeen seen ty (+ i 1)))))

; Check an application head and ground it from the already-checked
; argument type: a pending head witnesses this level's argument.
; Anything else flows through untouched.
(pub :: groundHead (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundHead tc e argTy0)
  {
    (let h (checkExprH tc (nodeAChild e) 1))
    (if (== (isPendingNode h) 1)
      (groundLamArg tc h argTy0 (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
      h)
  })

; Ground an ARGUMENT by declaration: a pending lambda passed where
; an arrow is declared is checked AGAINST it, so its parameters
; carry the types the author wrote - the evidence-safe direction,
; the same rule `checkHandler` holds handlers to - and the answer
; stamps the node. Grounded nodes answer their arrow (the normal
; path verifies it); broken nodes answer poison quietly; nodes
; mid-grounding name the too-early recursion. Anything else -
; including a pending lambda where a NON-arrow is declared, which
; the normal mismatch below owns - flows through untouched.
; None means no declared parameter is available. Some keeps poison
; and inference types present. A partial node grounds with its prefix
; in scope, as a flush does.
(pub :: groundArgByDecl (-> TC ASTNode (Option ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (groundArgByDecl tc argTy declaredParam span)
  (if (== (isPendingNode argTy) 0)
    argTy
    (match declaredParam
      ((None) argTy)
      ((Some param)
        (if (!= (nodeTag param) TAG_T_ARR)
          argTy
          {
            (let s (lamStamp argTy))
            (if (== (stampIsArrow s) 1)
              s
              (if (== (stampIsBroken s) 1)
                (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                (if (== (stampIsProg s) 1)
                  {
                    (emitDiag tc
                      (diagSetLabel
                        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
                          "type mismatch: cannot pass this function while its parameter types are still being inferred"
                          None
                          (Some
                            "pass it after its definition is complete - a use through a `let` alias hides the recursion from inference")
                          "inference grounds a lambda's parameters from its uses; this use runs before the grounding finished")
                        "this use is too early to check"))
                    (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                  }
                  {
                    (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
                    {
                      (if (== (stampIsPartial s) 1)
                        (groundPushPrefix tc (partialEnts s) 0))
                      (setLamStamp argTy (mkProgStamp))
                      {
                        (let ans (checkLamAgainst tc argTy param))
                        {
                          (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
                          (if (== (tyHasPoison ans) 1)
                            (setLamStamp argTy (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
                            (setLamStamp argTy ans))
                          ans
                        }
                      }
                    }
                  })))
          })
      ))))

; Is this type EXACTLY `String`? Deliberately not "compatible with
; String": while the uniform-representation fiat lived, `tyCompat`
; said the two names were interchangeable, and asking it here would
; have answered yes for every `Int` in the program - turning every
; integer comparison into a string comparison. The fiat is deleted;
; the narrow question stays narrow because a type VARIABLE still
; matches anything. This asks the narrow question - did the checker
; CONCLUDE `String` - which is the same question `tyIsFloatTy` asks for
; the `fadd`/`add` choice.
(pub :: tyIsStringTy (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsStringTy t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      0
      (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "String")
        1
        0))))

; `Float` and no other: `Double`/`F32`/`F64` stood here while the
; emitter keyed float arithmetic on `Float` alone, which is the
; split-brain MM-VAL-4b measured - the checker called them floats, the
; emitter emitted integer `add` on double bit patterns. The names are
; refused now (`typeKeywordKnown`), and this list matches the emitter's
; one-name rule by construction.
(pub :: tyIsFloatTy (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsFloatTy t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      0
      (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "Float")
        1
        0))))

(pub :: mkIntTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkIntTy)
  (mkTCon "Int" vecNew))

(pub :: mkBoolTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkBoolTy)
  (mkTCon "Bool" vecNew))

(pub :: mkFloatTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkFloatTy)
  (mkTCon "Float" vecNew))

(pub :: mkCharTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkCharTy)
  (mkTCon "Char" vecNew))

(pub :: mkStringTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkStringTy)
  (mkTCon "String" vecNew))

; The spawn handle (MM-PAR-8): what a spawn primitive answers.
(pub :: mkSpawnTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkSpawnTy)
  (mkTCon "Spawn" vecNew))

(pub :: mkUnitTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkUnitTy)
  (mkTTup vecNew))

; `(Vec Int)`, the one instantiation this file writes for itself: the
; rendering builtin's ancestor stack. Before `Vec` carried an element
; type the generated signature said `Int` and was right by accident - the
; handle and the machine word were the same type. They are not any
; more, and `vecPush`/`vecLen`/`vecGet` in the generated body ask for
; the container.
(pub :: mkVecIntTy ASTNode)
(pub fn (mkVecIntTy)
  (mkTCon "Vec"
    {
      (let v vecNew)
      {
        (vecPush v mkIntTy)
        v
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Compatibility - stage0's `compatible_with`. Rules in order: a type
; variable on EITHER side matches anything, so `Tree a` vs `Tree Int`
; passes; poison matches anything; constructors need equal names, equal
; argument counts, and pairwise compatibility; arrows, tuples, lists
; pairwise; pointers also need EQUAL mutability. An absent type (0)
; matches anything - stage1's spelling of "I kept no type here".
;
; Two claims stood here until 2026-08-23 and both were stale. It said
; `String` and `Int` are "mutually compatible BY NAME" - that fiat was
; deleted on 2026-08-15 and the header paragraph says so, but this copy
; was not swept; `(wantsInt "not an int")` is AX3004. And it called
; this function "the whole unifier" and said "no substitution exists",
; which sends a reader looking for a checker rewrite when the actual
; unsoundness is one signature shape (AX3040). Instantiation happens
; per call site above this function, not in it.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; PINNING: the half of unification this checker did not have
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `tyCompat` used to COMPARE and record nothing, so a minted
; placeholder matched anything and went on matching anything. That is
; sound for a value read once and unsound for one that is BOUND and
; then used twice, which is exactly a container:
;
;     (let ((v vecNew))            ; v : (Vec _t1)
;       { (vecPush v 42)           ; (Vec _t1) vs (Vec _t2), then Int vs _t2
;         (needVec (vecGet v 0)) })
;
; Each `vecPush` matched its OWN fresh placeholder, `_t1` was never
; pinned, and `check` answered OK for a program that exits 139 - an
; `Int` read back as a block header with no `cast` written anywhere.
; That is `AX3040`'s failure reached without `AX3040`'s coercion, and
; `scripts/check-type-pinning.sh` keeps the refusal.
;
; So a placeholder now BINDS. Word 2 of a `TAG_T_VAR` node is 0 until
; something pins it; `parser.ax` documents that tag as `a=name`, and
; every consumer in the tree dispatches on the tag and reads only the
; name, so the slot aliases nothing.
;
; FOUR OBLIGATIONS MAKE THIS SOUND, each a rule below rather than a
; hope:
;
;   1. ONLY A MINTED PLACEHOLDER BINDS. A source variable is rigid -
;      that is what makes `(-> a a Bool)` say something - and the
;      empty-named wildcard `mkSilentWild` means "no information", so
;      pinning it would turn a silence into a claim.
;   2. NOTHING BINDS TO POISON. `TAG_T_ERR` is compatible with
;      everything so one error does not cascade; pinning a placeholder
;      to it would spread the error instead of stopping it.
;   3. THE OCCURS CHECK IS NOT OPTIONAL. Binding `_t` to a type
;      containing `_t` builds a cycle, and `tyResolve` and the
;      reference-map walk would both follow it forever. The comparison
;      must FAIL too: silently leaving it unbound accepts a container
;      holding itself, then lets a later use claim its elements are
;      Strings or Ints instead of containers.
;   4. THERE IS NO BACKTRACKING. Binding is monotone, so it is sound
;      only if no caller tries a match and discards it. All 53
;      `tyCompat` call sites were read: every one either reports on
;      failure or walks positional arguments. None speculate.
;
; It is nearly inert today, which is the fifth thing worth saying: 47
; of 4,434 signatures in `core/` and `stdlib/` mention a source
; type variable at all, so almost nothing is instantiated and almost
; no placeholder exists to pin. It becomes load-bearing when a
; parameterised container lands.
; A minted placeholder - `_tN`. The only thing that may be bound.
(pub :: tyIsMintedVar (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsMintedVar t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR)
      0
      (if (== (tyIsSrcVar t) 1)
        0
        ; `_iN` and nothing else. The empty name is `mkSilentWild` and
        ; `_tN` is a "not known yet" placeholder; neither may be
        ; pinned - see `freshInstVar` for what the prefix separates.
        (if (< (strLen (nodeAName t)) 2)
          0
          (if (== (strByte (nodeAName t) 1) 105)
            1
            0))))))

; Follow a bound placeholder to whatever it stands for.
(pub :: tyResolve (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyResolve t)
  (if (== (tyIsMintedVar t) 0)
    t
    {
      (let b (nodeBChild t))
      (if (nodeSame b nilNode)
        t
        (tyResolve b))
    }))

; Does `v` - a placeholder NODE, compared by identity - occur in `t`?
; Obligation 3.
(pub :: tyOccurs (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyOccurs v t)
  {
    (let r (tyResolve t))
    (if (nodeSame r nilNode)
      0
      (if (nodeSame r v)
        1
        {
          (let tg (nodeTag r))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
            (tyOccursVec v (nodeBChildren r) 0)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
              (if (== (tyOccurs v (nodeAChild r)) 1)
                1
                (tyOccurs v (nodeBChild r)))
              (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
                (tyOccursVec v (nodeAChildren r) 0)
                (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                  (tyOccurs v (nodeAChild r))
                  0))))
        }))
  })

(pub :: tyOccursVec (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tyOccursVec v xs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    0
    (if (== (tyOccurs v (vecGet xs i)) 1)
      1
      (tyOccursVec v xs (+ i 1)))))

; Pin `v` to `t`, answering whether the two are now compatible.
; Poison and unavailable type information remain compatible without
; recording a binding. An occurs-check failure is different: `a` and
; `(Vec a)` cannot be equal finite types, so it MUST return 0. Merely
; declining to record that equation would forget a real constraint.
(pub :: tyPin (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyPin v t)
  (if (nodeSame v t)
    1
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      1
      ; obligation 2 - never pin to poison
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ERR)
        1
        ; nor to a placeholder that is not itself pinnable - the
        ; silent wildcard and `_tN` both mean "no information", and
        ; pinning one would turn a silence into a claim
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR) (&& (== (tyIsSrcVar t) 0) (== (tyIsMintedVar t) 0)))
          1
          (if (== (tyOccurs v t) 1)
            0
            {
              (writeNodeChild v 2 t)
              1
            }))))))

(pub :: tyCompat (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))

; Checked nominal names already include declaration identity. Builtins
; keep their canonical names and entry-file types keep their bare names.
(pub :: tySameDecl (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tySameDecl tc a b)
  (if (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b))
    1
    0))

(pub fn (tyCompat tc a0 b0)
  ; RESOLVE FIRST - a placeholder pinned by an earlier comparison must be read as what it was pinned TO
  ; A pending lambda is compatible with anything it meets here:
  ; its arrow does not exist yet (no use or declaration has
  ; witnessed it), so there is nothing to compare against, and
  ; answering 0 would refuse sound programs with `expected
  ; {unknown}, found {unknown}` - a diagnostic that names
  ; nothing. This is not a hole: the pending side is verified
  ; where it is GROUNDED (a use checks it with the argument
  ; types) or where it is FLUSHED (an escape checks it
  ; standalone), so every consumer that matters still judges it.
  {
    (let a (tyResolve a0))
    (let b (tyResolve b0))
    (if (|| (== (isPendingNode a) 1) (== (isPendingNode b) 1))
      1
      (if (|| (nodeSame a nilNode) (nodeSame b nilNode))
        1
        ; Poison BEFORE variables. The two orders differ on exactly one
        ; pair - a source type variable against `TAG_T_ERR` - and
        ; getting it wrong turns cascade suppression off for every
        ; polymorphic signature.
        {
          (let ta (nodeTag a))
          (let tb (nodeTag b))
          (if (|| (== ta TAG_T_ERR) (== tb TAG_T_ERR))
            1
            (if (|| (== ta TAG_T_VAR) (== tb TAG_T_VAR))
              (tyVarCompat a b ta tb)
              (if (&& (== ta TAG_T_CON) (== tb TAG_T_CON))
                ; The String/Int fiat lived here from the first checker
                ; until 2026-08-15: the two names were mutually compatible
                ; so that `Int` could be the universal heap-handle type.
                ; Its deletion was priced before it happened - a four-slice
                ; campaign took the fiat-off error count to zero for the
                ; compiler and the whole stdlib, and the corpus's 17
                ; dependent fixtures were retyped honestly (container
                ; value positions became type variables; string equality
                ; answers Bool ahead of the numeric matrix) until the
                ; exit-status differential read zero across all 297. A
                ; string is a String now, everywhere, which is what
                ; MM-ALLOC-20's static half demanded and what MM-LIFE-2d's
                ; reference maps stand on.
                (if (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren a)) (vecLen (nodeBChildren b)))
                  (if (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b))
                    (tyCompatVec tc (nodeBChildren a) (nodeBChildren b) 0)
                    0)
                  0)
                (if (&& (== ta TAG_T_ARR) (== tb TAG_T_ARR))
                  (if (== (tyCompat tc (nodeAChild a) (nodeAChild b)) 1)
                    (tyCompat tc (nodeBChild a) (nodeBChild b))
                    0)
                  (if (&& (== ta TAG_T_TUP) (== tb TAG_T_TUP))
                    (if (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren a)) (vecLen (nodeAChildren b)))
                      (tyCompatVec tc (nodeAChildren a) (nodeAChildren b) 0)
                      0)
                    (if (&& (== ta TAG_T_PTR) (== tb TAG_T_PTR))
                      (tyCompat tc (nodeAChild a) (nodeAChild b))
                      0))))))
        }))
  })

(pub :: tyCompatVec (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tyCompatVec tc xs ys i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    1
    (if (== (tyCompat tc (vecGet xs i) (vecGet ys i)) 1)
      (tyCompatVec tc xs ys (+ i 1))
      0)))

; Subtype table lookup. A `subtype` declaration names a new type whose
; values are a subset of its base type's, bounded by 1-2 Int expressions
; in the declaration's c slot (a Vec of EXPR nodes, not types).
;
; The subtype declaration for a type node, or None when it is not a subtype.
(pub :: tySubtypeDecl (-> TC ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (tySubtypeDecl tc name)
  (if (== (nodeTag name) TAG_T_CON)
    {
      (let n (nodeAName name))
      (tySubtypeDeclByName tc n 0 (vecLen (tcSubtypesVec tc)))
    }
    None))

(pub :: tySubtypeDeclByName (-> TC String Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (tySubtypeDeclByName tc n i lim)
  (tyFindAnyVisSyntax tc TY_SUBTYPE n))

; Look up a type name in the subtype table and return its base type,
; or None when the name is not a subtype.
(pub :: tySubtypeBase (-> TC ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (tySubtypeBase tc name)
  (if (let (Some declaration) (tySubtypeDecl tc name))
    (Some (nodeBChild declaration))
    None))

; Return Some bounds for a known subtype, including an empty bounds vector.
; None means the type is not a known subtype. The bounds hold expression
; nodes: (lower) or (lower upper).
(pub :: tySubtypeBounds (-> TC ASTNode (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (tySubtypeBounds tc name)
  (if (let (Some declaration) (tySubtypeDecl tc name))
    (Some (nodeCChildren declaration))
    None))

; Walk a (possibly-subtype) type to its non-subtype base for uses
; that dispatch on the type's NAME (rendering, numeric ops): a
; `Positive` behaves as the `Int` it constrains.
(pub :: showBaseOf (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (showBaseOf tc ty)
  (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let b (tySubtypeBase tc ty))
      (if (let (Some b) b)
        (showBaseOf tc b)
        ty)
    }))

; Argument compatibility with subtypes, where `tyCompat` stays pure.
; Answers 0 = mismatch, 1 = compatible with no check, 2 = compatible
; with a runtime range check (expected is a subtype and the actual
; holds a value of its base).
;
;   same subtype -> 1 (no re-check; the value already proved its range)
;   actual subtype, expected its base -> 1 (widening is always safe)
;   expected subtype, actual its base -> 2 (narrowing checks the range)
;   expected subtype, actual a DIFFERENT subtype of a compatible base
;     -> 2 (ranges differ, so the value must prove the new one)
;   otherwise -> 0
(pub :: subtypeArgAction (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (subtypeArgAction tc expected actual)
  (if (|| (nodeSame expected nilNode) (nodeSame actual nilNode))
    0
    (if (|| (== (nodeTag expected) TAG_T_ERR) (== (nodeTag actual) TAG_T_ERR))
      1
      {
        (let expDecl (tySubtypeDecl tc expected))
        {
          (let actDecl (tySubtypeDecl tc actual))
          (if (let (Some expDecl) expDecl)
            (if (let (Some actDecl) actDecl)
              (if (nodeSame expDecl actDecl)
                1
                {
                  (let expBase (nodeBChild expDecl))
                  (let actBase (nodeBChild actDecl))
                  (if (== (tyCompat tc actBase expBase) 1)
                    2
                    (if (== (tyCompat tc actual expBase) 1)
                      2
                      0))
                })
              {
                (let expBase (nodeBChild expDecl))
                (if (== (tyCompat tc actual expBase) 1)
                  2
                  0)
              })
            (if (let (Some actDecl) actDecl)
              (if (== (tyCompat tc (nodeBChild actDecl) expected) 1)
                1
                0)
              0))
        }
      })))

; 1 when the two types are compatible once subtypes are allowed
; (either direction, with or without a check). Used to suppress false
; AX3004s at positions that cannot insert a check (struct fields,
; returns, patterns); cast and call-arg sites use `subtypeArgAction`
; and wrap instead.
(pub :: subtypeCompatAllows (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (subtypeCompatAllows tc expected actual)
  (if (== (tyCompat tc expected actual) 1)
    1
    (if (!= (subtypeArgAction tc expected actual) 0)
      1
      (if (!= (subtypeArgAction tc actual expected) 0)
        1
        0))))

; Build a TAG_E_SUBCHECK node checking `value` against `targetTy`'s
; range. `boundsH` is the bounds vector from `tySubtypeBounds`
; (shared, never mutated). The ty slot carries the target subtype.
(pub :: mkSubtypeCheckNode (-> ASTNode Nodes ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (mkSubtypeCheckNode value boundsH targetTy sp)
  {
    (let msg (nodeBuildEStr "\"axiom: subtype check failed\\n\"" 0 0 sp))
    (setNodeTyChild (nodeBuildESubcheck value boundsH msg sp) targetTy)
  })

; Check each bound expression of a SUBCHECK node's bounds Vec.
; Bounds must be Int; a non-Int bound is AX3004 at the bound.
(pub :: checkSubtypeBounds (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkSubtypeBounds tc bounds i)
  (if (== (as bounds Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen bounds))
      0
      {
        {
          (let bty (checkExprH tc (vecGet bounds i) 0))
          (if (&& (!= (tyIsErr bty) 1)
                (&& (!= (tyIsSilentWild bty) 1) (== (tyCompat tc bty mkIntTy) 0)))
            (emitMismatchTy tc mkIntTy bty (spanOf (vecGet bounds i))))
        }
        (checkSubtypeBounds tc bounds (+ i 1))
      })))

; A declared subtype result over a base-typed body is a CHECKED
; conversion, like a cast: wrap the body so emission proves the
; range, and answer the declared type for the return check below.
; Widening (subtype body, base declared) needs no check. Answers the
; type `checkDeclaredReturn` should compare.
(pub :: maybeWrapSubtypeReturn (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (maybeWrapSubtypeReturn tc d resTy bodyTy)
  (if (|| (nodeSame resTy nilNode) (== (nodeC d) 0))
    bodyTy
    (if (!= (subtypeArgAction tc resTy bodyTy) 2)
      bodyTy
      {
        (let bh (tySubtypeBounds tc resTy))
        (if (let (Some bh) bh)
          {
            (checkSubtypeBounds tc bh 0)
            (writeNodeChild d 3
              (mkSubtypeCheckNode (nodeCChild d) bh resTy (spanOf (nodeCChild d))))
            resTy
          }
          bodyTy)
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Three flavours of type variable, and only one of them is rigid.
;
;   `_tN` / `_fn_N`  placeholders this checker mints - "I have no type
;                    here". They match anything, which is what makes a
;                    `fn` with no signature usable at all.
;   `""`             the silent wildcard: what stage1 cannot know -
;                    macro heads, unparseable types. Matches anything,
;                    renders nowhere.
;   `a`, `b`, ...    written by the PROGRAMMER in a signature. RIGID:
;                    inside the body it stands for ONE unknown type
;                    and supports no operation that needs a concrete
;                    one; at a reference site it is INSTANTIATED to a
;                    fresh placeholder, so a caller may still pass
;                    anything.
;
; Until 2026-08-10 all three matched anything at any depth, and the
; consequence was that writing `a` was strictly WEAKER than writing
; `Int`:
;
;   (:: bad (-> Int Int))  (fn (bad x) (+ x 1))
;   (bad (Pt 3 4))         AX3004: expected Int, found Pt        exit 1
;
;   (:: bad (-> a   Int))  (fn (bad x) (+ x 1))
;   (bad (Pt 3 4))         prints 4372529169                     exit 0
;
; The struct handle arrived in `x`, `+` added one to a pointer, and
; the program answered an address. README.md listed "polymorphic
; signatures" under **Complete** while the rule disabled checking
; rather than generalising it.
;
; The discriminator is the leading `_`, and it is disjoint by
; construction rather than by convention: `parser.ax`'s
; `lexStartsLower` admits bytes 97-122 and nothing else, so a
; source-written type variable always starts with a lowercase letter
; and can never collide with a minted one.
; Compatibility when at least one side is a variable. A minted
; placeholder still matches anything - that is the whole reason it
; exists. Two source variables agree when they are the SAME variable,
; which is what makes `(-> a a Bool)` say something. A source variable
; against a concrete type does not match, and that is the rule the
; body of a polymorphic function now obeys.
(pub :: tyVarCompat (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (tyVarCompat a b ta tb)
  (if (&& (== ta TAG_T_VAR) (== tb TAG_T_VAR))
    (if (&& (== (tyIsSrcVar a) 1) (== (tyIsSrcVar b) 1))
      (if (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b))
        1
        0)
      ; One of them is minted or a wildcard. Pinning one to the other
      ; is what makes two uses of ONE binding agree; `tyPin` declines
      ; on a wildcard, so a silence stays a silence.
      (if (== (tyIsMintedVar a) 1)
        (tyPin a b)
        (if (== (tyIsMintedVar b) 1)
          (tyPin b a)
          1)))
    (if (== ta TAG_T_VAR)
      (if (== (tyIsSrcVar a) 1)
        0
        (if (== (tyIsMintedVar a) 1)
          (tyPin a b)
          1))
      (if (== (tyIsSrcVar b) 1)
        0
        (if (== (tyIsMintedVar b) 1)
          (tyPin b a)
          1)))))

; Does this type mention a source-written variable anywhere?
; Asked before every instantiation, and the answer is 0 for every
; declaration in this repository - 0 of 1,733 signatures in
; `core/` + `stdlib/` and 0 of 549 in `tests/` use one - so the
; walk is what keeps instantiation off the hot path rather than a
; flag that would have to be maintained.
(pub :: tyHasSrcVar (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyHasSrcVar t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (tyIsSrcVar t)
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (tyHasSrcVarVec (nodeBChildren t) 0)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            (if (== (tyHasSrcVar (nodeAChild t)) 1)
              1
              (tyHasSrcVar (nodeBChild t)))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (tyHasSrcVarVec (nodeAChildren t) 0)
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (tyHasSrcVar (nodeAChild t))
                0)))))
    }))

(pub :: tyHasSrcVarVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tyHasSrcVarVec xs i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      0
      (if (== (tyHasSrcVar (vecGet xs i)) 1)
        1
        (tyHasSrcVarVec xs (+ i 1))))))

; Instantiation creates one fresh placeholder per declared variable,
; shared across the whole call. Arguments pin those placeholders, so
; repeated occurrences must agree. Definition variables remain rigid.
; `env` pairs each source name with its type in a TypeBinding.
(pub :: tyInstIn (-> TC ASTNode TypeEnv ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyInstIn tc t env)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (if (== (tyIsSrcVar t) 0)
          t
          (tyInstVar tc (nodeAName t) env 0))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (mkTCon (nodeAName t) (tyInstVec tc (nodeBChildren t) env vecNew 0))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            (mkTArr (tyInstIn tc (nodeAChild t) env) (tyInstIn tc (nodeBChild t) env))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (mkTTup (tyInstVec tc (nodeAChildren t) env vecNew 0))
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (mkTPtr (tyInstIn tc (nodeAChild t) env))
                t)))))
    }))

(pub :: tyInstVar (-> TC String TypeEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyInstVar tc name env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen env))
    {
      (let fresh (freshInstVar tc))
      {
        (vecPush env (TypeBinding :name name :ty fresh))
        fresh
      }
    }
    (if (strEq (typeBindingName (vecGet env i)) name)
      (typeBindingTy (vecGet env i))
      (tyInstVar tc name env (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tyInstVec (-> TC Nodes TypeEnv Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (tyInstVec tc xs env acc i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      acc
      {
        (vecPush acc (tyInstIn tc (vecGet xs i) env))
        (tyInstVec tc xs env acc (+ i 1))
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; MM-LIFE-2d, the evidence half - checker side. A call to a
; polymorphic function is STAMPED: the head E_VAR's ty slot gains a
; TAG_EVSTAMP node whose witness vec says, per callee type
; parameter, what this call instantiates it at - 0 (not a
; reference, or unknowable: the conservative direction), 1 (a
; reference), or 2+k (whatever bit k of the CALLER's own evidence
; word says at run time). Codegen reads the stamp and passes the
; word; a stamp is a function of the node alone, so it also carries
; the declaration that wrote it, and emission ignores a stamp
; written under a different declaration.
;
; THE ONE ORDERING CONVENTION: a signature's type parameters are
; ordered by first occurrence in a left-to-right depth-first walk
; of its type tree. codegen derives the same ordering from the same
; D_SIG tree with the same walk (`cgSigVarsInto` there); the two are
; pinned against each other by fixture. Alias expansion writes ONE
; tree into both the D_SIG node and the entry (tcExpandSigAliases),
; so the walks see the same object; the poisoned copy a bad
; signature gets stamps all zeros instead of trusting a diverged
; ordering.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: sigTyVarsInto (-> ASTNode Names Int))
(pub fn (sigTyVarsInto t acc)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (sigTyVarPush acc t)
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (sigTyVarsVec (nodeBChildren t) acc)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            {
              (sigTyVarsInto (nodeAChild t) acc)
              (sigTyVarsInto (nodeBChild t) acc)
            }
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (sigTyVarsVec (nodeAChildren t) acc)
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (sigTyVarsInto (nodeAChild t) acc)
                0)))))
    }))

(pub :: sigTyVarsVec (-> Nodes Names Int))
(pub fn (sigTyVarsVec xs acc)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    0
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
        (sigTyVarsInto (vecGet xs i) acc))
      0
    }))

(pub :: tyHasPoison (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyHasPoison t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_ERR)
        1
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (tyHasPoisonVec (nodeBChildren t))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            (if (== (tyHasPoison (nodeAChild t)) 1)
              1
              (tyHasPoison (nodeBChild t)))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (tyHasPoisonVec (nodeAChildren t))
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (tyHasPoison (nodeAChild t))
                0)))))
    }))

(pub :: tyHasPoisonVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (tyHasPoisonVec xs)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    0
    {
      (let mut found 0)
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
          (if (== (tyHasPoison (vecGet xs i)) 1)
            (set found 1)))
        found
      }
    }))

; The scalar family, spelled once here and once in codegen's
; scalarTyName - the two lists must agree, and the evidence fixture
; is what notices a drift.
;
; `Vec` is in neither: a vector is a counted value (MM-LIFE-2m), so
; evidence classifies it a reference, as the reference map does.
; `Foreign` is listed here, not fallen through to in `evClassOf`: a
; `Foreign` is a pointer into memory Axiom never allocated, so the
; release walk skips it (`tests/ffi/demo/410-foreign-not-walked.ax`
; pins the map) and capturing one races no count. It answered 0
; before through `evDataTyKnown`'s default for undeclared names - the
; same answer, but a default is not a promise, and AX3064's rule
; below treats every 0 as one. `externTypeRefusal` names it beside
; this list already; the answers there do not move either.
;
; `Spawn` and the word structs are class 0 as well, and are NOT here:
; `externTypeRefusal` admits every name on this list to an `extern`
; signature, and a Rust function answering a handle would forge one.
; `evClassOf` answers them in an arm of its own (`tcWordTyName`).
; `Link` is here: an index holds no address, and a link always has type
; arguments, which `externTypeRefusal` refuses on their own account.
(pub :: evScalarName (-> String Int))
(pub fn (evScalarName n)
  (if (|| (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
        (|| (strEq n "Bool")
          (|| (strEq n "Char") (|| (strEq n "Float") (|| (strEq n "Foreign") (strEq n "Link"))))))
    1
    0))

; A result the release walk may treat as alias-free: 1 for the four
; machine words and the empty tuple, 0 for everything else. This is
; NOT `evScalarName`, which answers a different question: "takes no
; share". A `Vec` is a block that can hold the argument's block, and
; releasing the argument under a `Vec` answer would free storage the
; answer points at. So `Vec` is 0 here, and so is every name this does
; not spell: a type
; variable, an unexpanded alias, `Foreign`, `Handle`, `String`, an arrow, and no
; type at all. Each of those is a missed elision, never an unsound
; one; the zero default is what makes an unstamped node keep its
; release. Pure readers, so untagged like the setters beside it.
(pub :: evResultWord (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (evResultWord funcTy)
  (if (!= (nodeTag funcTy) TAG_T_ARR)
    0
    (resWordTy (nodeBChild funcTy))))

(pub :: resWordTy (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (resWordTy t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        {
          (let n (nodeAName t))
          (if (|| (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
                (|| (strEq n "Float") (|| (strEq n "Bool") (|| (strEq n "Char") (strEq n "Link")))))
            1
            0)
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
          (if (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren t)) 0)
            1
            0)
          0))
    }))

(pub :: evDataTyKnown (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (evDataTyKnown tc name)
  (if (|| (! (isNone (findDataAnyVis tc name))) (! (isNone (findStructAnyVis tc name))))
    1
    0))

; Classify one witness type: 0 scalar-or-unknowable, 1 reference,
; 2+k the enclosing signature's own variable k. Unknown types carry
; no reference bit. A known nominal reference must retain its bit
; through qualified names and private signatures; losing it can free
; a value still stored in a generic container.
; The witness for "this lifted lambda's own argument", read at run time
; out of the evidence word its APPLICATION passes. It is a code in the
; same small-int space as the others - 0 not a reference, 1 a
; reference, `2+k` bit k of the enclosing function's word, -1 says
; nothing - and it is negative so that it cannot collide with a `2+k`
; for any k. `evMeet` needs no case for it: -1 takes it by the
; first-set rule, agreement keeps it, and any disagreement collapses to
; 0, which is exactly right for a lambda applied at two types.
(pub :: EV_LAMARG Int)
(pub fn (EV_LAMARG)
  (- 0 2))

; How many lambdas out the parameter named `n` is, or None if it is
; not a lambda parameter. 0 is the lambda being checked; 1 is the one enclosing it, whose
; parameter the PARSER has already turned into a capture - `(lambda (a
; b) ..)` is `(lambda (a) (lambda (b) ..))`, so a store of `a` runs
; inside the inner lambda with `a` read out of the closure record.
;
; The stack is searched from the INNERMOST end, so a parameter that
; shadows an outer one of the same name resolves to the nearer.
; Witness `EV_LAMARG - d` carries the depth, and codegen reads
; `"__evwa."` at depth 0 and `"__evwa.<d>"` above it - the enclosing
; lambda's own word, captured into this one's record.
(pub :: lamVarDepth (-> TC String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (lamVarDepth tc n)
  {
    (let st tc.lamVars)
    (let mut i (- (vecLen st) 1))
    (let mut found None)
    (while (>= i 0)
      {
        (if (&& (isNone found) (strEq (vecGet st i) n))
          (set found (Some (- (- (vecLen st) 1) i))))
        (set i (- i 1))
      })
    found
  })

; Enter a lambda: push its parameter's name, or the empty string for a
; lambda whose parameter needs no runtime word (a ground declared
; type). The empty string still occupies a level, because DEPTH is
; counted in lambdas and not in words - a ground parameter between two
; variable ones must not renumber them.
(pub :: lamVarPush (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (lamVarPush tc n)
  {
    (vecPush tc.lamVars n)
    (vecLen tc.lamVars)
  })

(pub :: lamVarTrunc (-> TC Int Int))
(pub fn (lamVarTrunc tc n)
  (scopeTrunc tc.lamVars n))

(pub :: evClassOf (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (evClassOf tc t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        ; THE ENCLOSING LAMBDA'S OWN PARAMETER FIRST, whether the
        ; author wrote its type or this checker minted one. A minted
        ; placeholder has nowhere else to get a class from. A SOURCE
        ; variable does - bit k of the enclosing function's word - and
        ; the application's answer is still the better one, because a
        ; variable that appears only in the factory's RESULT is
        ; witnessed by nobody at the factory's call and arrives as the
        ; constant 0.
        ;
        ; MATCHING A SOURCE VARIABLE BY NAME IS SOUND, and the reason
        ; is worth stating because the obvious objection is not. `a`
        ; certainly denotes different VALUES inside the body - the
        ; parameter, a capture of the same type, a temporary - but the
        ; evidence word is a fact about a TYPE, not about a value, and
        ; a type variable denotes one type throughout its scope. If the
        ; application hands over a `String` for `a`, then every `a` in
        ; that body is a String. There is no shadowing to confuse it
        ; with: type variables come from the signature being checked,
        ; and expressions cannot introduce their own.
        ;
        ; A PINNED placeholder is the type it was pinned to. `out` in
        ; `(let ((out vecNew)) ...)` is `(Vec _iN)`, and the first
        ; `vecPush` of a `String` pins `_iN`; a later
        ; `(vecSet out k (vecGet out j))` witnesses `a` only through
        ; `_iN`. Read as "says nothing", that store took no share
        ; while `vecSet` released the element it displaced.
        ;
        ; ONE PIN AT A TIME, so the lambda question above is asked of
        ; every link: a captured vector's element pinned to the
        ; enclosing lambda's own parameter is that parameter, whatever
        ; the parameter is later pinned to.
        (if (let (Some depth) (lamVarDepth tc (nodeAName t)))
          (- EV_LAMARG depth)
          (if (&& (== (tyIsMintedVar t) 1) (!= (nodeB t) 0))
            (evClassOf tc (nodeBChild t))
            (if (== (tyIsSrcVar t) 1)
              {
                (let sv (tcCurSigVars tc))
                (if (== (vecLen sv) 0)
                  0
                  (match (strVecIdx sv (nodeAName t))
                    ((None) 0)
                    ((Some k) (+ 2 k))))
              }
              ; THE INNERMOST LAMBDA'S OWN PARAMETER is the one
              ; placeholder whose class something will know: not here,
              ; where the lambda is checked, but at the closure's
              ; APPLICATION, which is the only place the argument's type
              ; exists. It answers `EV_LAMARG`, and codegen turns that
              ; into a retain under bit 0 of the word the application
              ; passes.
              ;
              ; Any OTHER placeholder still says NOTHING about the class,
              ; and the meet must not hear it as "a scalar": inside
              ; `(fn (singleton x) (PCons x (PNil)))` the `(PNil)`
              ; argument's `(PL _t)` met x's bit k and collapsed the
              ; site to 0, so the cell stored x uncounted with no map
              ; bit while the flow model trusted the store (event 6)
              ; and the caller released the temporary under it
              (- 0 1))))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          1
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (if (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren t)) 0)
              0
              1)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
              {
                (let n (nodeAName t))
                (if (== (evScalarName n) 1)
                  0
                  (if (|| (strEq n "String") (|| (strEq n "Handle") (strEq n "Vec")))
                    1
                    ; A WORD STRUCT, and `Spawn`: one machine word that
                    ; takes no share (MM-VAL-10a). Asked before
                    ; `evDataTyKnown`, which answers 1 for every
                    ; struct and would retain a handle as if it were
                    ; a block.
                    (if (== (tcWordTyName tc n) 1)
                      0
                      (evDataTyKnown tc n))))
              }
              0))))
    }))

; MEET over every occurrence of one variable: unset takes the new
; class, agreement keeps it, and ANY disagreement - including a
; second distinct symbolic - collapses to 0. First-occurrence-wins
; was the review's wrong-free generator: tyCompat accepts
; `(f "s" 42)` against `(-> a a Int)`, and a bit-1 stamp with an
; integer in the cell frees the integer.
(pub :: evMeet (-> Int Int Int))
(pub fn (evMeet old new)
  (if (== new (- 0 1))
    old
    (if (== old (- 0 1))
      new
      (if (== old new)
        old
        0))))

; Instantiate AND stamp: `tyInstIn` through a fresh env, plus a
; TAG_EVSTAMP on the reference node when the declaration has type
; variables. This is the only instantiation entry point - the
; unstamped `tyInst` it grew out of was removed once every reference
; site needed the stamp. A
; poisoned signature stamps all zeros through an EMPTY env - no
; fill can land, so the zeros survive.
(pub :: tyInstStamped (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyInstStamped tc t e)
  (if (== (tyHasSrcVar t) 0)
    t
    {
      (let env vecNew)
      {
        (let it (tyInstIn tc t env))
        {
          {
            (let vars vecNew)
            {
              (sigTyVarsInto t vars)
              (if (! (== (vecLen vars) 0))
                {
                  (let w vecNew)
                  (let poisoned (tyHasPoison t))
                  {
                    (for i in 0..(vecLen vars)
                      (vecPush w
                        (if (== poisoned 1)
                          0
                          (- 0 1))))
                    (writeNodeChild e 6
                      (setNodeTyNames
                        (nodeBuildEvstampCallOptional (tcCurDeclNode tc) w
                          (if (== poisoned 1)
                            vecNew
                            env))
                        vars))
                  }
                })
            }
          }
          it
        }
      }
    }))

; Fill one call level's witnesses: co-walk the instantiated param
; tree beside the argument's inferred type; wherever the param tree
; is one of this stamp's minted placeholders, the matching subtree
; of the argument type is that variable's witness at this level.
; A cast proven to preserve its operand's type keeps that evidence.
; A reinterpretation still classifies 0: it can erase an ownership
; relation the checker cannot recover from the resulting word.
(pub :: evStampFill (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (evStampFill tc e funcTy argTy)
  ; The application's own answer, beside the argument's: 1 when the
  ; arrow's result is a proven word (see `evResultWord`), 0
  ; otherwise. The chain walker spends it - a word answer cannot
  ; alias the argument, so the argument's share can go with the
  ; call. Stamped here, next to the argument's stamp, because this
  ; is the one place with the arrow in scope; idempotent, so
  ; re-checking a body rewrites the same bit.
  {
    (setNodeResWord e (evResultWord funcTy))
    (if (!= (nodeTag funcTy) TAG_T_ARR)
      0
      {
        (let head (spineHead e))
        (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
          0
          {
            (let st (nodeTyChild head))
            (if (nodeSame st nilNode)
              0
              (if (!= (nodeTag st) TAG_EVSTAMP)
                0
                {
                  (let an (nodeBChild e))
                  {
                    (let castArg
                      {
                        (let ah (spineHead an))
                        (if (&& (== (nodeTag ah) TAG_E_VAR)
                              (&& (== (isCastLike (nodeAName ah)) 1) (!= (nodeResWord an) 3)))
                          1
                          0)
                      })
                    (evWalk tc st (nodeAChild funcTy) argTy castArg 0)
                  }
                }))
          })
      })
  })

; Co-walk the parameter tree beside the argument's type. `late` is 0
; while the call is checked, and 1 when `evSettle` replays a witness
; after the body is typed.
;
; A WITNESS THROUGH A PIN WAITS FOR THE BODY. A placeholder is pinned in
; program order, and a call checked before the pin that decides it
; would read "says nothing": in a loop body, `(vecPush b (vecGet a i))`
; can come before the `(vecPush a s)` that says `a` holds Strings. So
; while the call is checked, a placeholder argument says nothing, as it
; always has, and the pair is kept for `evSettle`. In the replay the
; structural arms read the argument through its pins, and a
; `(Vec _iN)` parameter met by a placeholder pinned to `(Vec String)`
; has a witness inside it.
(pub :: evWalk (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (evWalk tc st pt at0 castArg late)
  (if (nodeSame pt nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag pt))
      (let at
        (if (== late 1)
          (tyResolve at0)
          at0))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (evWalkVar tc st pt at0 castArg late)
        (if (&& (== late 0) (&& (== castArg 0) (== (tyIsMintedVar at0) 1)))
          (evNotePending tc st pt at0)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
            (if (&& (! (nodeSame at nilNode))
                  (&& (== (nodeTag at) TAG_T_CON) (strEq (nodeAName pt) (nodeAName at))))
              (evWalkVec tc st (nodeBChildren pt) (nodeBChildren at) castArg late)
              0)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
              (if (&& (! (nodeSame at nilNode)) (== (nodeTag at) TAG_T_ARR))
                {
                  (evWalk tc st (nodeAChild pt) (nodeAChild at) castArg late)
                  (evWalk tc st (nodeBChild pt) (nodeBChild at) castArg late)
                }
                0)
              (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
                (if (&& (! (nodeSame at nilNode)) (== (nodeTag at) TAG_T_TUP))
                  (evWalkVec tc st (nodeAChildren pt) (nodeAChildren at) castArg late)
                  0)
                0)))))
    }))

(pub :: evWalkVec (-> TC ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (evWalkVec tc st ps as castArg late)
  (if (|| (== (as ps Int) 0) (== (as as Int) 0))
    0
    {
      (let mut i 0)
      {
        (while (&& (< i (vecLen ps)) (< i (vecLen as)))
          {
            (evWalk tc st (vecGet ps i) (vecGet as i) castArg late)
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        0
      }
    }))

; One variable's witness, met into its slot.
;
; While the call is checked, an instantiation placeholder says nothing,
; pinned or not, unless it is the enclosing lambda's own parameter
; (`evClassOf` answers that first), and the pair waits for `evSettle`.
;
; In the replay the placeholder is read through its pins, and a slot
; already holding `EV_LAMARG` keeps it. Another witness of the same call
; said the value is the enclosing lambda's own argument, and the
; application's word answers for that argument. A pinned witness names
; the same type, but the lambda's own placeholder is often a `_tN` that
; nothing pins, so the two can disagree only where the checker accepted
; an argument it knew nothing about. Met as a disagreement, they would
; collapse to 0 and drop the share the application asks for.
(pub :: evWalkVar (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (evWalkVar tc st pt at castArg late)
  ; the minted placeholder is one shared node per variable, so
  ; identity finds its source name
  {
    (let env (nodeCTypeEnv st))
    {
      (let mut src "")
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen env)
          (if (nodeSame (typeBindingTy (vecGet env i)) pt)
            (set src (typeBindingName (vecGet env i)))))
        (if (strEq src "")
          0
          (match (strVecIdx (nodeTyNames st) src)
            ((None) 0)
            ((Some idx)
              {
                (let w (nodeBWords st))
                (let early
                  (&& (== late 0)
                    (&& (== castArg 0)
                      (&& (== (tyIsMintedVar at) 1) (isNone (lamVarDepth tc (nodeAName at)))))))
                (if early
                  (evNotePending tc st pt at)
                  {
                    (let c
                      (if (== castArg 1)
                        0
                        (evClassOf tc at)))
                    (if (&& (== late 1) (< (vecGet w idx) (- 0 1)))
                      0
                      {
                        (vecSet w idx (evMeet (vecGet w idx) c))
                        0
                      })
                  })
              }
            )))
      }
    }
  })

; Keep a stamp witness for `evSettle`, with the parameter tree and
; argument type it meets. Application witnesses use their own variant
; and keep the checked application whose `ty` slot holds the class.
(pub :: evNotePending (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (evNotePending tc st pt at)
  {
    (vecPush (evPendingOf tc) (EvStampWitness st pt at (tcCurSigVars tc)))
    0
  })

(pub :: evNotePendingApplication (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (evNotePendingApplication tc node at)
  {
    (vecPush (evPendingOf tc) (EvApplicationWitness node at (tcCurSigVars tc)))
    0
  })

; An application's class, kept when no pin has reached its placeholder
; yet. One already pinned has its answer: `evClassOf` reads pins.
(pub :: evNotePendingNode (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (evNotePendingNode tc e at c)
  (if (!= c (- 0 1))
    0
    (if (== (tyIsMintedVar (tyResolve at)) 0)
      0
      (evNotePendingApplication tc e at))))

; The pending witnesses, TC word 44's word 6.
(pub :: evPendingOf (-> TC (Vec EvPend)))
(pub fn (evPendingOf tc)
  tc.unsafe.evPending)

; Replay every pending witness now that its body is typed: a stamp's
; through `evWalk` in its late mode, an application's by asking its
; class again. A placeholder still unpinned says nothing, and its slot
; keeps the answer it already has.
(pub :: evSettle (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (evSettle tc)
  {
    (let ps (evPendingOf tc))
    (if (== (vecLen ps) 0)
      0
      {
        (let sv0 (tcCurSigVars tc))
        {
          (for p in ps
            (match p
              ((EvStampWitness st pt at sigVars)
                {
                  (tcSetCurSigVars tc sigVars)
                  (evWalk tc st pt at 0 1)
                }
              )
              ((EvApplicationWitness node at sigVars)
                {
                  (tcSetCurSigVars tc sigVars)
                  (let c (evClassOf tc at))
                  (if (! (< c 0))
                    (setNodeTyWord node c))
                  0
                }
              )))
          (tcSetCurSigVars tc sv0)
          (vecClear ps)
          0
        }
      })
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Which namespace a declaration defines into, or 0 for a declaration
; that defines nothing. Values and types are separate namespaces in
; stage0, so `(data Foo ...)` and `(fn (Foo) ...)` do not collide.
;
; `fn` - and `define`, its documented synonym, which stage0 parses
; through the same function - is the only value definition that
; reaches here.
;
; `type` was the RECORDED GAP here until this line changed: stage0
; counts an alias in the type namespace and stage1 did not, so two
; `(type Amt ...)` in one entry file were silent while two
; `(struct Amt ...)` were AX3006 - and the second one won every
; lookup. It is NS_TYPE now, which also makes `(type Amt Int)`
; beside `(struct Amt ...)` the duplicate it is: both bind the same
; name in the same namespace, and `tcAliasFind` and `findStruct` read
; one table.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: NS_NONE Int)
(pub fn (NS_NONE)
  0)

(pub :: NS_VALUE Int)
(pub fn (NS_VALUE)
  1)

(pub :: NS_TYPE Int)
(pub fn (NS_TYPE)
  2)

; Signatures, for the duplicate index alone: a second `(:: name ...)`
; for one name is its own duplicate, and a signature never collides
; with the definition it describes.
(pub :: NS_SIG Int)
(pub fn (NS_SIG)
  3)

; The namespace the duplicate index files a declaration under:
; `declNamespace`'s answer, or NS_SIG for a signature.
(pub :: dupNamespace (-> Int Int))
(pub fn (dupNamespace tag)
  (if (== tag TAG_D_SIG)
    NS_SIG
    (declNamespace tag)))

; A MACRO occupies the value namespace too. It defines a name that an
; expression can be headed by, and expansion consults the macro table
; before anything else, so a macro and a function of the same name are
; not two definitions that coexist - the macro simply wins and the
; function becomes unreachable. Measured before this line existed:
; `(macro (thing x) (+ x 1))` beside `(fn (thing n) (* n 2))` checked
; `OK` and answered 11 where the function says 20, with no diagnostic
; from either compiler.
(pub :: declNamespace (-> Int Int))
(pub fn (declNamespace tag)
  (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_FN) (|| (== tag TAG_D_MACRO) (== tag TAG_D_EXTERN)))
    NS_VALUE
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
          (|| (== tag TAG_D_STRUCT) (|| (== tag TAG_D_ALIAS) (== tag TAG_D_SUBTYPE))))
      NS_TYPE
      NS_NONE)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Index of the first declaration before `i` that defines the same name
; in the same namespace, or None.
;
; The FIRST one, not the nearest: stage0 records a name's first span
; and never overwrites it, so three definitions of `main` produce two
; diagnostics that both point back at the first (verified by probe).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Answered from an INDEX rather than by re-scanning. `firstDefinerFrom`
; walked `decls[0..i)` once per declaration, which is quadratic in the
; entry file's own declaration count, and `main.ax` hides it with 21
; entry declarations. Measured on N top-level declarations before the
; index: N=2000 0.04s, 4000 0.19s, 8000 0.78s - ratios 4.00, 4.75,
; 4.11 per doubling.
;
; `dupIdxBuild` keeps one bucket table per namespace. Entries are
; appended in declaration order, so within a bucket the indices
; increase, and the FIRST entry that matches is the smallest index.
; The bucket interleaves names that hash together and that does not
; disturb the order.
; One packed word per definition while its fields fit. The name lives
; in `DupIdx.names` and
; the word holds a link to it, so the entry takes no share and the
; vector frees the strings with itself (MM-VAL-10b). `ns` picks which
; bucket table the word sits in, which is what keeps both the link and
; the declaration index at 32 bits: a third field would not fit.
; Wider populations use owning fallback rows before either field truncates.
(struct DupRef word
  (name : (Link String UInt32))
  (idx : UInt32))

; Definitions past either packed-width bound keep a full-width owning row.
; Once this starts, later definitions stay here in insertion order.
(struct DupWide
  (name : String)
  (ns : Int)
  (idx : Int))

(struct DupIdx
  (names : (Vec String))
  (value : (Vec (Vec DupRef)))
  (types : (Vec (Vec DupRef)))
  (sigs : (Vec (Vec DupRef)))
  (wide : (Vec DupWide))
  (mut compact : Bool)
  (limit : Int))

; A UInt32 Link stores index plus one; the scalar declaration index
; can use the same maximum itself. Check both before narrowing.
(:: dupLinkLimit Int)
(fn (dupLinkLimit)
  4294967295)

; Capacity is injectable for bounded tests. An oversized population
; starts with one unused packed bucket rather than allocating from it.
(:: dupIdxNew (-> Int Int DupIdx))
(fn (dupIdxNew hint capacity)
  {
    (let limit
      (if (< capacity 0)
        0
        (if (> capacity dupLinkLimit)
          dupLinkLimit
          capacity)))
    (let compact (&& (>= hint 0) (<= hint limit)))
    (let n
      (if compact
        (fnIdxBuckets hint)
        1))
    (DupIdx
      :names vecNew
      :value (dupBucketVec n)
      :types (dupBucketVec n)
      :sigs (dupBucketVec n)
      :wide vecNew
      :compact compact
      :limit limit)
  })

; Keep the bucket selector's existing catch-all signature namespace.
(:: dupTableNS (-> Int Int))
(fn (dupTableNS ns)
  (if (== ns NS_VALUE)
    NS_VALUE
    (if (== ns NS_TYPE)
      NS_TYPE
      NS_SIG)))

(:: dupBucketVec (-> Int (Vec (Vec DupRef))))
(fn (dupBucketVec n)
  {
    (let bs vecNew)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< i n)
        {
          (vecPush bs (:: vecNew (Vec DupRef)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      bs
    }
  })

(:: dupTable (-> DupIdx Int (Vec (Vec DupRef))))
(fn (dupTable idx ns)
  (if (== ns NS_VALUE)
    idx.value
    (if (== ns NS_TYPE)
      idx.types
      idx.sigs)))

(pub :: firstDefiner (-> DupIdx Int Int String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (firstDefiner idx i ns name)
  {
    (let found
      (firstDefinerScan idx
        (vecGet (dupTable idx ns) (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen (dupTable idx ns)))) i name 0))
    (if (let (Some at) found)
      (Some at)
      (firstDefinerWide idx.wide i (dupTableNS ns) name 0))
  })

(pub :: firstDefinerScan (-> DupIdx (Vec DupRef) Int String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (firstDefinerScan idx bucket i name k)
  (if (>= k (vecLen bucket))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet bucket k))
      (let at (as e.idx Int))
      (if (&& (< at i) (strEq (linkGet idx.names e.name) name))
        (Some at)
        (firstDefinerScan idx bucket i name (+ k 1)))
    }))

; Every packed insertion precedes every fallback row, so the packed
; match above remains first. The fallback preserves insertion order too.
(:: firstDefinerWide (-> (Vec DupWide) Int Int String Int (Option Int)))
(fn (firstDefinerWide rows i ns name k)
  (if (>= k (vecLen rows))
    None
    {
      (let entry (vecGet rows k))
      (if (&& (== entry.ns ns) (&& (< entry.idx i) (strEq entry.name name)))
        (Some entry.idx)
        (firstDefinerWide rows i ns name (+ k 1)))
    }))

; One entry per (name, namespace) a declaration defines, carrying the
; declaration's index. An `effect` contributes one per operation and
; an `extern` block one per item, which is why this is built from `declFields`
; and `declNamespace` rather than from `nodeA` alone: those two are
; where the multi-name declarations are named, and this index is the
; only thing that asks them. The `declDefinesNS` predicate that used
; to be the source of truth was superseded by this index and removed.
(pub :: dupIdxBuild (-> (Vec ASTNode) DupIdx))
(pub fn (dupIdxBuild decls)
  {
    (let idx (dupIdxNew (vecLen decls) dupLinkLimit))
    (for i in 0..(vecLen decls)
      (dupIdxAddDecl idx (vecGet decls i) i))
    idx
  })

(pub :: dupIdxAddDecl (-> DupIdx ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (dupIdxAddDecl idx d i)
  (if (let (Some fields) (declFields d))
    (dupIdxAddFields idx fields i 0)
    {
      (let ns (dupNamespace (nodeTag d)))
      (if (== ns NS_NONE)
        0
        (dupIdxPut idx (nodeAName d) ns i))
    }))

(pub :: dupIdxAddFields (-> DupIdx (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (dupIdxAddFields idx fs i k)
  (if (>= k (vecLen fs))
    0
    {
      (dupIdxPut idx (nodeAName (vecGet fs k)) NS_VALUE i)
      (dupIdxAddFields idx fs i (+ k 1))
    }))

(pub :: dupIdxPut (-> DupIdx String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (dupIdxPut idx name ns i)
  (if (< i 0)
    __indexTrap
    (if (&& idx.compact (&& (<= i idx.limit) (< (vecLen idx.names) idx.limit)))
      {
        (let bs (dupTable idx ns))
        (let b (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen bs)))
        (let id (vecLen idx.names))
        (vecPush idx.names name)
        (vecPush (vecGet bs b) (DupRef :name (linkTo idx.names id) :idx (as i UInt32)))
        0
      }
      {
        (set idx.compact false)
        (vecPush idx.wide (DupWide :name name :ns (dupTableNS ns) :idx i))
        0
      })))

; The TAG_FIELD vector of a declaration that defines MORE THAN ONE
; value name, or None for the ordinary kind that defines just `nodeA`.
; Some empty is a multi-name declaration with no fields.
;
; Two declarations were in this class and both were invisible to the
; duplicate check: an `effect` keeps its operations' names in slot b
; and the same names with their TYPES in slot c, which is the vector
; with the spans on it, and a `trait` kept its methods in slot b until
; 0.6.0 took traits out of the language.
(pub :: declFields (-> ASTNode (Option Nodes)))
(pub fn (declFields d)
  (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EFFECT)
    (Some (nodeCChildren d))
    (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EXTERN)
      (Some (nodeCChildren d))
      None)))

; The span to blame for that definition. For an effect it is the
; OPERATION name's span, not the declaration's own:
; the label says "first defined here", and the line the reader has to
; change is the `where` entry.
(pub :: declDefSpan (-> ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (declDefSpan d name)
  (if (let (Some fields) (declFields d))
    (if (let (Some field) (findFieldNamed fields name 0))
      (nodeSpan field)
      (nodeSpan d))
    (nodeSpan d)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Pass 1: duplicate definitions.
;
; Only the entry file's declarations are examined. stage0 keys this
; check by (name, module) - a name defined once per module in two
; modules is legal, and that is the ambiguity check's business, not
; this one - and stage1's merged declaration list does not record which
; module each declaration came from. Checking the entry file alone is
; the sound subset: it reports exactly the duplicates stage0 reports
; for a single-file program and never invents one, which is the
; property that matters, since a checker that rejects the compiler's
; own source is worse than no checker. Duplicates *inside an imported
; module* go unreported until declarations carry their origin.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkDuplicates (-> DupIdx (Vec ASTNode) Diags Int Diags))
(pub fn (checkDuplicates bs decls out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    out
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        {
          (let fields (declFields d))
          (if (let (Some fs) fields)
            ; An effect defines one value name per operation and
            ; an `extern` block one per item, so these are the
            ; declarations that need a loop here.
            (checkFieldDupes bs decls out i fs 0)
            {
              (let ns (dupNamespace (nodeTag d)))
              (if (! (== ns NS_NONE))
                {
                  (let name (nodeAName d))
                  (if (let (Some j) (firstDefiner bs i ns name))
                    (if (== ns NS_SIG)
                      (dupSigDiag decls out j name (nodeSpan d))
                      (dupDiag decls out j name (nodeSpan d))))
                })
            })
        }
        (checkDuplicates bs decls out (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Names the emitted runtime owns. The backend defines `axiom_alloc`
; unconditionally (the allocator - MM-ALLOC-2), so an entry-file
; definition of the same name passed `check` and died in `opt` as
;   error: invalid redefinition of function 'axiom_alloc'
; - the documented "override seam" that never worked (MM-ALLOC-8;
; three documents described it as working until 2026-08-14). Refusal
; is the option that rule names, and whether a real seam should exist
; is re-decided with the ARC release path (MM-LIFE-2e). Only the
; ENTRY file can collide: a module's declaration is mangled to
; `Mod$axiom_alloc`, a different symbol.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkReservedNames (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec diag::Diag) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkReservedNames decls out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    ; The FN only: the DEFINITION is what emits the colliding
    ; symbol. A signature alone emits nothing (and draws
    ; AX3015), and refusing both would report one collision
    ; twice. The set grew from one name to three when the
    ; MM-LIFE-2b ownership primitives joined the emitted
    ; runtime; each name carries its own message, because
    ; "reserved" is only half the fact - WHAT the runtime does
    ; with the name is the half a reader can act on.
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
          (if (strEq (nodeAName d) "axiom_alloc")
            (vecPush out
              (diagSetLabel
                (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3026" "reserved-runtime-name" (nodeSpan d)
                  "`axiom_alloc` is the emitted runtime's allocator symbol; a program cannot define it"
                  None
                  (Some
                    "rename the function - the override seam does not exist (docs/memory-model.md MM-ALLOC-8)")
                  "the backend emits its own definition of `axiom_alloc` unconditionally; before this refusal the build died in `opt` as a duplicate symbol after `check` said OK")
                "this name belongs to the runtime"))
            (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName d) "axiom_retain") (strEq (nodeAName d) "axiom_release"))
              (vecPush out
                (diagSetLabel
                  (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3026" "reserved-runtime-name" (nodeSpan d)
                    (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName d))
                      "` is the emitted runtime's ownership primitive; a program cannot define it")
                    None
                    (Some
                      "rename the function - retain and release belong to the reference-counting runtime (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2b)")
                    "the backend emits its own definition unconditionally, exactly as it does `axiom_alloc`'s")
                  "this name belongs to the runtime"))
              ()))
          ())
        (checkReservedNames decls out (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: checkFieldDupes (-> DupIdx (Vec ASTNode) (Vec diag::Diag) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkFieldDupes bs decls out i fields k)
  (if (>= k (vecLen fields))
    0
    {
      (let m (vecGet fields k))
      {
        (if (let (Some j) (firstDefiner bs i NS_VALUE (nodeAName m)))
          (dupDiag decls out j (nodeAName m) (nodeSpan m)))
        (checkFieldDupes bs decls out i fields (+ k 1))
      }
    }))

; A second signature for one name. Each signature states the whole
; type, so two of them can't both hold, and taking the later one would
; let the earlier be read as true while the checker ignores it.
(pub :: dupSigDiag (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec diag::Diag) Int String Int Int))
(pub fn (dupSigDiag decls out j name sp)
  {
    (vecPush out
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3006" "duplicate-definition" sp
          (concat (concat "a second signature for `" name) "`")
          (diagRelated (nodeSpan (vecGet decls j))
            (concat (concat "`" name) "`'s signature is first written here"))
          (Some "keep one signature for each function, and remove the other"))
        "this signature is a second one"))
    0
  })

(pub :: dupDiag (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec diag::Diag) Int String Int Int))
(pub fn (dupDiag decls out j name sp)
  {
    (vecPush out
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3006" "duplicate-definition" sp
          (concat (concat "duplicate definition `" name) "`")
          (diagRelated (declDefSpan (vecGet decls j) name)
            (concat (concat "`" name) "` first defined here"))
          (Some "rename one of the definitions, or remove the duplicate"))
        (concat (concat "`" name) "` redefined here")))
    0
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Pass 2: a signature with no definition behind it.
;
; The signatures examined are the entry file's; the definitions that
; satisfy them come from ANY module, exactly as stage0 does it - an
; entry-file signature over an imported definition is the same
; signature-then-definition pattern with the definition arriving
; through an import.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Does `full` name the definition an entry-file signature for `bare`
; is looking for?
;
; Not string equality, because by the time the checker runs, import
; resolution has rewritten every *imported* declaration's name in place
; to `Mod$name` (codegen.ax's `mangleDecl`). Entry-file declarations
; keep their bare name. A signature in the entry file for a function
; that arrives through an import - which stage0 accepts, counting
; definitions from any module - therefore never matched, and every such
; program drew a false AX3015.
(pub :: nameMatches (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (nameMatches full bare)
  (if (strEq full bare)
    1
    {
      (let m (strLen full))
      (let n (strLen bare))
      (if (<= m (+ n 1))
        0
        (if (&& (== (strByte full (- (- m n) 1)) 36) (strEq (strSlice full (- m n) n) bare))
          1
          0))
    }))

; A definition that is not exported does not answer an ENTRY-FILE
; signature: the entry file cannot name it, so a `(:: f T)` with only
; a private `M$f` behind it is still AX3015, and saying so here is
; what keeps it from becoming an undefined symbol at link time.
(pub :: isDefined (-> StringRows String Int))
(pub fn (isDefined bs name)
  (defIdxHas (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen bs))) name 0))

(pub :: defIdxHas (-> Strings String Int Int))
(pub fn (defIdxHas v name k)
  (if (>= k (vecLen v))
    0
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr v k) name)
      1
      (defIdxHas v name (+ k 1)))))

; The set of names an exported definition answers to, which is what
; `isDefined` used to compute by scanning the WHOLE program per
; signature - `main.ax` pays that 21 times against a 1,326-function
; closure, and a generated entry file pays it per declaration.
;
; The keys are exactly the strings `nameMatches full k` accepts, and
; that equivalence is the whole correctness argument: `full` itself,
; and the suffix after every `$` at a position past the first byte.
; The position matters - `nameMatches` demands `m > n + 1`, so a name
; that merely BEGINS with `$` matches nothing, and inserting that
; suffix would invent a definition. `Mod$f` answers to `Mod$f` and to
; `f`; `$f` answers only to itself.
(pub :: defIdxBuild (-> (Vec ASTNode) StringRows))
(pub fn (defIdxBuild allDecls)
  {
    (let nb (fnIdxBuckets (vecLen allDecls)))
    (let bs vecNew)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< i nb)
        {
          (vecPush bs vecNew)
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (for i in 0..(vecLen allDecls)
        {
          ; A definition that is not exported does not answer an
          ; entry-file signature, so it is not in the index at all -
          ; the `nodeVis` test the linear scan made, made once.
          (let d (vecGet allDecls i))
          (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN) (== (nodeVis d) 1))
            (defIdxAddKeys bs (nodeAName d))
            ; An `extern` block DEFINES every name in it, so a `::`
            ; written over one is answered rather than drawing a false
            ; AX3015. Without this arm the index holds only TAG_D_FN
            ; and every signature over a binding is "a signature with
            ; no definition behind it".
            (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EXTERN) (== (nodeVis d) 1))
              (defIdxAddExternKeys bs (nodeCChildren d) 0)))
        })
      bs
    }
  })

(pub :: defIdxAddExternKeys (-> StringRows (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (defIdxAddExternKeys bs names i)
  (if (== (as names Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen names))
      0
      {
        (if (== (nodeVis (vecGet names i)) 1)
          (defIdxAddKeys bs (nodeAName (vecGet names i))))
        (defIdxAddExternKeys bs names (+ i 1))
      })))

(pub :: defIdxAddKeys (-> StringRows String Int))
(pub fn (defIdxAddKeys bs full)
  {
    (defIdxPut bs full)
    (defIdxAddSuffixes bs full 1)
  })

(pub :: defIdxAddSuffixes (-> StringRows String Int Int))
(pub fn (defIdxAddSuffixes bs full i)
  (if (>= i (strLen full))
    0
    {
      (if (== (strByte full i) 36)
        (defIdxPut bs (strSlice full (+ i 1) (- (strLen full) (+ i 1)))))
      (defIdxAddSuffixes bs full (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: defIdxPut (-> StringRows String Int))
(pub fn (defIdxPut bs name)
  {
    (vecPush (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen bs))) name)
    0
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `(import M (a b))` naming something `M` does not export.
;
; The list was never read after `resolveDeclsPhase` used it to decide
; visibility, so `(import M (noSuch))` was accepted in silence and the
; mistake surfaced as an AX3001 wherever the name was used - or as
; nothing at all if it never was, which is the case for a typo in a
; list of otherwise-good names.
;
; Entry imports live in `entryDecls`. Each imported unit retains its
; own import sites because resolution consumes them before `allDecls`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkImportNames (-> Decls Decls (Vec diag::Diag) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (checkImportNames sites allDecls out unit i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen sites))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet sites i))
      {
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_IMPORT)
          {
            (let names (nodeBNames d))
            (if (! (== (as names Int) 0))
              (checkImportNameList (nodeAName d) names (nodeSpan d) allDecls out unit 0))
          })
        (checkImportNames sites allDecls out unit (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: checkImportedImportNames (-> Units Decls (Vec diag::Diag) Int Int))
; Units is a packed ABI registry of live CUnit2 owners. Retain their typed
; import vectors while the checker reads the consumed import forms.
(pub fn (checkImportedImportNames units allDecls out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen units))
    0
    {
      (let unit (as (vecGet units i) CUnit2))
      (checkImportNames unit.imports allDecls out i 0)
      (checkImportedImportNames units allDecls out (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: checkImportNameList (-> String Names Int Decls (Vec diag::Diag) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (checkImportNameList modName names sp allDecls out unit i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    0
    {
      {
        (let n (vecGetStr names i))
        (if (declaredInModule allDecls modName n 0)
          (if (! (exportedFromModule allDecls modName n 0))
            (importDiag out unit sp
              (concat (concat (concat (concat "module `" modName) "` declares `") n)
                "` but does not export it")
              "write `pub` on its declaration"))
          (importDiag out unit sp
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "module `" modName) "` declares no `") n) "`")
            "check the spelling, or import the module without a name list"))
      }
      (checkImportNameList modName names sp allDecls out unit (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: importDiag (-> (Vec diag::Diag) Int Int String String Int))
(pub fn (importDiag out unit sp msg help)
  {
    (vecPush out
      (diagSetUnit
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3023" "private-name" sp msg None (Some help)
            "an import's name list is checked against what the module exports")
          "not exported")
        unit))
    0
  })

(pub :: declaredInModule (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String Int Bool))
(pub fn (declaredInModule allDecls modName n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen allDecls))
    false
    {
      (let d (vecGet allDecls i))
      (if (&& (isSome (nodeModuleOpt d))
            (&& (strEq (nodeModuleName d) modName) (moduleDeclNames d n)))
        true
        (declaredInModule allDecls modName n (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: exportedFromModule (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String Int Bool))
(pub fn (exportedFromModule allDecls modName n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen allDecls))
    false
    {
      (let d (vecGet allDecls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeVis d) 1)
            (&& (isSome (nodeModuleOpt d))
              (&& (strEq (nodeModuleName d) modName) (moduleDeclExports d n))))
        true
        (exportedFromModule allDecls modName n (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: moduleDeclNames (-> ASTNode String Bool))
(pub fn (moduleDeclNames d name)
  (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EXTERN)
    (isSome (findFieldNamed (nodeCChildren d) name 0))
    (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName d)) name)))

(pub :: moduleDeclExports (-> ASTNode String Bool))
(pub fn (moduleDeclExports d name)
  (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EXTERN)
    (if (let (Some field) (findFieldNamed (nodeCChildren d) name 0))
      (== (nodeVis field) 1)
      false)
    (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName d)) name)))

(pub :: checkMissingDefs (-> StringRows (Vec ASTNode) Diags Int Names Diags))
(pub fn (checkMissingDefs bs entryDecls out i missing)
  (if (>= i (vecLen entryDecls))
    out
    {
      (let d (vecGet entryDecls i))
      (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG)
        (checkMissingDefs bs entryDecls out (+ i 1) missing)
        {
          (let name (nodeAName d))
          (if (== (isDefined bs name) 1)
            (checkMissingDefs bs entryDecls out (+ i 1) missing)
            {
              (vecPushStr missing name)
              (vecPush out
                (diagSetLabel
                  (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3015" "missing-definition" (nodeSpan d)
                    (concat (concat "`" name) "` has a signature but no definition") None
                    (Some
                      "add the definition, or if it lives in another module, mark that definition `pub` so the import carries it"))
                  (concat (concat "no `fn` defines `" name) "`")))
              (checkMissingDefs bs entryDecls out (+ i 1) missing)
            })
        })
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The checker's tables. Axiom has no globals, so one TC handle rides
; through the whole walk.
;
;   fns      Vec of FnEnt, in registration order - which is also the
;            suggestion pool's visit order, so it is semantic:
;            operators, then primitives, then declarations as they
;            appear in the resolved module list.
;   datas    Vec of DataEnt; the builtin Option is FIRST, which is
;            what puts `Some`/`None` ahead of every user constructor
;            in suggestion and resolution order.
;   structs  Vec of StructEnt.
;   scope    one Vec: module-level entries (signatures, pushed as the
;            declaration walk reaches them - order matters, probed)
;            below frameBase, the current function's frame above it.
;            Value resolution reads the FRAME only;
;            `set` target resolution reads the whole vec - stage0's
;            asymmetry, kept deliberately.
;   counter  the fresh-type-variable counter. Its increment sites are
;            copied from stage0 exactly, because `_tN` names render.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `effects` is a Vec of TAGGED effect strings: "b:IO" for a built-in,
; "c:Console" for a custom one. The tag is not decoration - stage0's
; `Effect::IO` and `Effect::Custom("IO")` are different values, so an
; `(effect IO ...)` declaration's operations do NOT become handled by
; a handle list naming the built-in `IO`. Storing the display name
; alone would silently merge them.
; `eparams` is the list of parameter indices this function is
; EFFECT-TRANSPARENT in: it performs whatever a value passed there
; performs. `apply` is `{effects: [], eparams: [0]}` - exactly as pure
; as its argument.
;
; `uses` holds an `EpUse` per effect-transparent parameter: whether the
; body calls it inside a `handle`, and which custom effects every such
; call discharges, so a caller knows what its argument performs there.
;
; `mayCalls` are the edges the body MAY take: a call through a field or
; a local that holds one of several functions reaches each of them,
; but no one of them for certain. The fixpoint follows them, so a row
; that depends on one is recomputed, and so do the call-graph checks
; (`graphWitnessMay`, `graphCycleMay`): a path that needs one makes a
; claim unverifiable, and no path through either kind proves it.
;
; `unsafe` is the declaration's place in the unsafe boundary (R-B6,
; `MM-EXEC-9d`), read off its tags by `tcUnsafeRoles` before the
; effect fixpoint runs: 0 for a declaration that says nothing, 1 for a
; TRUSTED encapsulation - `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)`, whose author
; vouches that every well-typed call is safe, so its callers inherit
; no `Unsafe`.
(pub data LambdaCast
  (NoLambdaCast)
  (MaybeLambdaCast)
  (DefiniteLambdaCast))

(pub type FnSuffixIndex (Vec (Vec FnEnt)))

(pub struct FnIndexNode word sealed
  (entry : (Link FnEnt UInt32))
  (next : (Link FnIndexNode UInt32)))

(pub struct FnIndexBucket word sealed
  (head : (Link FnIndexNode UInt32))
  (tail : (Link FnIndexNode UInt32)))

(pub struct FnIndex sealed
  (entries : (Vec FnEnt))
  (nodes : (Vec FnIndexNode))
  (buckets : (Vec FnIndexBucket))
  (mut indexed : Bool))

(pub struct FnEnt
  (name : String)
  (mut ty : ASTNode)
  (mut paramCount : (Option Int))
  (isBuiltin : Int)
  (isEffectOp : Int)
  (effects : Strings)
  (eparams : (Vec Int))
  (calls : Names)
  (mut rgn : (Option RgnFacts))
  (mut unsafe : Int)
  (uses : (Vec EpUse))
  (mayCalls : Names)
  (mut pending : Int)
  (scoped : (Vec Int))
  (compute : (Vec Int))
  (restrictions : Strings)
  (mut lambdaCast : LambdaCast))

; The region FACTS of this function (`rgnFactsNew`, stage S3 of
; docs/memory-model.md): which parameters its body stores
; a fresh value into, which parameters flow into which, and where
; its result comes from. 0 until `rgnEnsureFacts` has run, which it
; does only for a program that names a region or claims
; `restrict(no-escape)` - every other program never allocates one.
; `fieldNames` is Some declared names for a struct variant (including
; an empty named variant), and None for a positional constructor. A NAMED pattern is
; reordered against it.
(pub struct CtorEnt
  (name : String)
  (fieldTys : (Vec ASTNode))
  (resTy : ASTNode)
  (ctorTy : ASTNode)
  (fieldNames : (Option Names)))

; `module` is Some declaring module, or None for the entry file and
; builtins. It exists so a QUALIFIED reference can be
; checked rather than merely stripped: `mangleDecl` rewrites `fn` and
; `::` declarations to `Mod$name`, so `Mod::f` resolves by matching a
; rewritten declaration - but it does not rewrite `data` or `struct`,
; so `Mod::Ctor` matched nothing and every qualified constructor
; reference was a false AX3001 on a valid program. Recording the
; module here answers it without renaming anything: see `ctorMatches`.
(pub struct DataEnt
  (name : String)
  (ctors : (Vec CtorEnt))
  (module : (Option String))
  (arity : Int))

; Every declaration has a real field collection, including a fieldless struct.
(pub struct StructEnt
  (name : String)
  (fields : Nodes)
  (module : (Option String))
  (tyvars : Names)
  (repr : Int))

; Typed reads of the other entries the checker's tables hold as words.
(pub :: fnEntMayCalls (-> FnEnt Names))
(pub fn (fnEntMayCalls ent)
  ent.mayCalls)

(pub :: structEntName (-> StructEnt String))
(pub fn (structEntName ent)
  ent.name)

(pub :: structEntNoFields (-> StructEnt Bool))
(pub fn (structEntNoFields ent)
  (== (vecLen ent.fields) 0))

(pub :: structEntFields (-> StructEnt Nodes))
(pub fn (structEntFields ent)
  ent.fields)

(pub :: structEntModule (-> StructEnt (Option String)))
(pub fn (structEntModule ent)
  ent.module)

; The declaring module's name. Only ask it when `structEntModule` is
; not 0, which is the entry file.
(pub :: structEntModuleName (-> StructEnt String))
(pub fn (structEntModuleName ent)
  (match ent.module
    ((None) "")
    ((Some name) name)))

(pub :: structEntTyvars (-> StructEnt Names))
(pub fn (structEntTyvars ent)
  ent.tyvars)

(pub :: structEntRepr (-> StructEnt Int))
(pub fn (structEntRepr ent)
  ent.repr)

(pub :: dataEntName (-> DataEnt String))
(pub fn (dataEntName ent)
  ent.name)

(pub :: dataEntModule (-> DataEnt (Option String)))
(pub fn (dataEntModule ent)
  ent.module)

(pub :: dataEntCtors (-> DataEnt (Vec CtorEnt)))
(pub fn (dataEntCtors ent)
  ent.ctors)

(pub :: ctorEntFieldTys (-> CtorEnt Nodes))
(pub fn (ctorEntFieldTys ent)
  ent.fieldTys)

(pub :: ctorEntResTy (-> CtorEnt ASTNode))
(pub fn (ctorEntResTy ent)
  ent.resTy)

(pub :: ctorEntFieldNames (-> CtorEnt (Option Names)))
(pub fn (ctorEntFieldNames ent)
  ent.fieldNames)

(pub :: ctorEntName (-> CtorEnt String))
(pub fn (ctorEntName ent)
  ent.name)

(pub :: ctorEntTy (-> CtorEnt ASTNode))
(pub fn (ctorEntTy ent)
  ent.ctorTy)

(pub :: dataEntArity (-> DataEnt Int))
(pub fn (dataEntArity ent)
  ent.arity)

; Function tables, indexes and graph paths retain typed entries.
; These readers expose their fields without forging reference views.
(pub :: fnEntName (-> FnEnt String))
(pub fn (fnEntName ent)
  ent.name)

(pub :: fnEntTy (-> FnEnt ASTNode))
(pub fn (fnEntTy ent)
  ent.ty)

(pub :: fnEntParamCount (-> FnEnt (Option Int)))
(pub fn (fnEntParamCount ent)
  ent.paramCount)

(pub :: fnEntIsBuiltin (-> FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnEntIsBuiltin ent)
  ent.isBuiltin)

(pub :: fnEntIsEffectOp (-> FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnEntIsEffectOp ent)
  ent.isEffectOp)

(pub :: fnEntEffects (-> FnEnt Strings))
(pub fn (fnEntEffects ent)
  ent.effects)

(pub :: fnEntEparams (-> FnEnt (Vec Int)))
(pub fn (fnEntEparams ent)
  ent.eparams)

(pub :: fnEntCalls (-> FnEnt Names))
(pub fn (fnEntCalls ent)
  ent.calls)

(pub :: fnEntRgn (-> FnEnt (Option RgnFacts)))
(pub fn (fnEntRgn ent)
  ent.rgn)

(pub :: fnEntUnsafe (-> FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnEntUnsafe ent)
  ent.unsafe)

(pub :: fnEntUses (-> FnEnt (Vec EpUse)))
(pub fn (fnEntUses ent)
  ent.uses)

(pub :: fnEntPending (-> FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnEntPending ent)
  ent.pending)

(pub :: fnEntScoped (-> FnEnt (Vec Int)))
(pub fn (fnEntScoped ent)
  ent.scoped)

(pub :: fnEntCompute (-> FnEnt (Vec Int)))
(pub fn (fnEntCompute ent)
  ent.compute)

(:: fnEntSetTy (-> FnEnt ASTNode Int))
(fn (fnEntSetTy ent v)
  {
    (set ent.ty v)
    0
  })

(:: fnEntSetParamCount (-> FnEnt (Option Int) Int))
(fn (fnEntSetParamCount ent v)
  {
    (set ent.paramCount v)
    0
  })

(:: fnEntSetRgn (-> FnEnt (Option RgnFacts) Int))
(fn (fnEntSetRgn ent v)
  {
    (set ent.rgn v)
    0
  })

(:: fnEntSetUnsafe (-> FnEnt Int Int))
(fn (fnEntSetUnsafe ent v)
  {
    (set ent.unsafe v)
    0
  })

(:: fnEntSetPending (-> FnEnt Int Int))
(fn (fnEntSetPending ent v)
  {
    (set ent.pending v)
    0
  })

; A struct's representation, from its markers (`parser.ax`'s
; `structIsWord` and `structIsShared`): 0 a heap block, 1 a word
; (MM-VAL-10a), 3 a word a `parallel` binding may capture. Word 4 of
; the `StructEnt`, beside the name and module the lookups key on.
(pub :: structReprOf (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (structReprOf d)
  (+
    (if (structHasMarker d "sealed")
      4
      0)
    (+
      (if (structIsWord d)
        1
        0)
      (if (structIsShared d)
        2
        0))))

(pub :: structEntIsWord (-> (Option StructEnt) Int))
(pub fn (structEntIsWord ent)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    (if (== (% entry.repr 2) 1)
      1
      0)
    0))

; A heap struct its module declared `sealed shared` (MM-PAR-16): a
; counted handle a `parallel` form lends its bindings (`capBorrowableIn`).
(pub :: structEntIsSharedHeap (-> (Option StructEnt) Int))
(pub fn (structEntIsSharedHeap ent)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    (if (== (& entry.repr 7) 6)
      1
      0)
    0))

(pub :: structEntIsShared (-> (Option StructEnt) Int))
(pub fn (structEntIsShared ent)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    (if (&& (== (structEntIsWord ent) 1) (!= (& entry.repr 2) 0))
      1
      0)
    0))

; The type parameters the declaration named, in order - the vector
; `parseStructDecl` hung on the D_STRUCT node. Empty for every
; struct written before 2026-08-30 and for every one written without
; parameters since, which is what keeps `(mkTCon name vecNew)` the
; right answer in the unparameterised case.
(struct EraseCount
  (entry : FnEnt)
  (count : Int))

(struct ExprChecks
  (formatChecked : (Vec ASTNode))
  (computeRequests : (Vec ComputeRequest))
  (mut computeEnabled : Int)
  (mut validatingEffects : Bool)
  (mut restrictionsStrict : Bool)
  (mut unsignedParams : Nodes))

(pub data ContractContext
  (ContractPre)
  (ContractPost))

(pub data CtResult
  (CtUnseen)
  (CtWalking)
  (CtKnown Int))

(pub struct TC
  (fns : (Vec FnEnt))
  (datas : (Vec DataEnt))
  (structs : (Vec StructEnt))
  (scope : (Vec SEnt))
  (mut out : Diags)
  (mut counter : Int)
  (mut frameBase : Int)
  (macros : Nodes)
  (effects : Nodes)
  (entryDecls : (Vec ASTNode))
  (allDecls : (Vec ASTNode))
  (mut curParams : Strings)
  (mut curCallable : (Vec Int))
  (units : Units)
  (mut curUnit : Int)
  (ambTbl : (Vec AmbEnt))
  (mut curModule : (Option String))
  (aliases : Nodes)
  (mut pairFns : Names)
  (privates : (Option Names))
  (mut fnIdx : (Option FnIndex))
  (mut ambIdx : (Option AmbIndex))
  (mut lamBase : Int)
  (mut curSigVars : Names)
  (mut curDeclNode : (Option ASTNode))
  (mut sigTags : (Option Nodes))
  (mut fnSufIdx : (Option FnSuffixIndex))
  (mut privIdx : (Option StringRows))
  (mut rgnFactsDone : Int)
  (mut curCalls : (Option Names))
  (mut tyIdx : (Option TypeIndex))
  (mut deprecated : (Option Nodes))
  (lamVars : Names)
  (mut rgnGaveUp : Int)
  (mut contractTag : (Option ContractContext))
  (regions : (Vec RgEnt))
  (mut pairSites : (Option Nodes))
  (floatOps : (Vec ASTNode))
  (subtypes : Nodes)
  (mut rgnHasForm : Int)
  (mut rgnStamping : Int)
  (rgnRefused : (Vec Int))
  (wordLower : (Vec ASTNode))
  (wordStructs : Int)
  (unsafe : UState)
  (parLends : (Vec ASTNode))
  (exprChecks : ExprChecks))

; Shared parser Span arena handles, kept at the existing Span ABI boundary.
; Word 46, `exprChecks`: formatting-cache pairs, compute requests and
; the compute activation flag. The formatting cache is a stack of (expression, checked type)
; pairs, scoped to a formatting rewrite. The argument is checked once
; to select its renderer; the rewritten call reuses that result while
; checking the generated head and arguments. Nested formatting pushes
; and pops its own pair. Deferred lambda stamps and their diagnostics
; therefore survive together.
; Word 45, `parLends`: `parallel`'s borrow placeholders (MM-PAR-6b), two
; words per form checked so far - the borrow record's name node, whose
; spelling carries the form's number, and the brace block of
; `(parallel$lends b {})`. `capBorrowLend` appends to that block each
; `String` a thunk of the same form captures, so codegen lends exactly
; what the bindings borrow.
; Word 43, `wordStructs`: how many word structs the program declares,
; counted over every declaration before any is checked
; (`countWordStructs`). A program that declares none never asks the type
; index whether a name is one (`tcWordEnt`): that question runs at every
; call site's evidence, field read and construction, and asked of a
; program with no word struct it cost `check` of the compiler 58%.
;
; Word 42, `wordLower`: pairs of AST nodes, (site, operand), one per
; construction or field read of a WORD struct the checker accepted
; (MM-VAL-10a). `wordLowerApply` copies each operand over its site
; once every pass has run, so what reaches the emitter is the word
; itself and never a constructor or a field read - see
; `wordLowerRecord` for why the lowering is here rather than there.
; Word 44, `unsafe`, is the unsafe boundary's (R-B6): one `UState`,
; because TC's own reference map is full at 47 words. Its fields,
; read through the `tc...` accessors after `TC` (`tcUnsafeSink`,
; `tcForgeSites`, and so on):
;
;   0 `unsafeSink`  Some Vec while `checkAxtags` walks one body's effects,
;                    None otherwise: every reference that walk resolves to
;                    an extern is pushed here, so AX3073 names the
;                    call the way it names a primitive.
;   1 `forgeSites`  Some Vec while `tcCheckFn` checks one body, None
;                    otherwise: every `cast` in it that may forge
;                    (`castNoteForge`), narrowed to the ones that do
;                    once the body is typed (`unsafeSettleForges`). The
;                    source type exists only there, which is why a cast
;                    is judged with its body and not by the effect walk.
;   2 `forgeDecls`  every entry whose body forges, for the pass after
;                    the walk (`unsafeLatePass`): a forging callee the
;                    walk had not reached yet when its caller's
;                    `restrict(no-unsafe)` was read.
;   3 `unsafePending` every declaration whose `restrict(no-unsafe)` its
;                    row could not refute, for that same pass.
;   4 `ctClaimed`   every declaration with a `ct(...)` claim, found the
;                    first time one is checked (`ctTable`), 0 before.
;   5 `ctResults`   the result taint of each, beside it (`ctSummary`).
;   6 `evPending`   the evidence witnesses no pin had reached yet when
;                    their call was checked (`evNotePending`), asked
;                    again once the body is typed (`evSettle`).
;   7 `eraseSites`  Some Vec while `tcCheckFn` checks one body, None
;                    otherwise: every `cast` in it whose target is a
;                    word (`castNoteForge`), three words each, judged
;                    once the body is typed (`eraseSettle`).
;   8 `eraseCounts` records (entry, count): each declaration whose body erases
;                    a reference to a word n times, which `symbols`
;                    prints as `#erasures=n` (`tcErasuresOf`).
;   9 `fieldVals`   the `FieldTable` of what the program stores in each
;                    function-typed field, built before the effect
;                    fixpoint (`fieldStoresBuild`), 0 before. It rides
;                    here for the reason this record exists.
;  10 `mayCalls`    the `FnEnt` word 11 of the function the fixpoint is
;                    walking, as word 29 is its word 7; 0 otherwise.
;  11 `walkCtx`     the `BCtx` every function body's walk starts from,
;                    emptied at each start (`bodyScopeOf`): walks of two
;                    bodies never overlap, and one context per walk was
;                    a kilobyte per function the compiler never gave
;                    back.
;  12 `discard`     1 while the expression being checked has its value
;                    dropped (`tcDiscard`): a block's earlier statements,
;                    a loop's body, and what they reach.
;  13 `unknownCallees` the names a call may reach that have no body:
;                    an undefined name (AX3001) and a signature with no
;                    definition (AX3015). The effect walk reads a call to
;                    one as unresolved (`tcIsUnknownCallee`), so what it
;                    performs is unknown rather than nothing.
;  14 `regionScratch` reusable typed buffers, with no checker backedge;
;                    active region contexts own their checker separately.
;  15 `fieldDecls`  every struct field that declares its effects, with
;                    the row a call through it is charged
;                    (`fieldDeclsBuild`), read before the fixpoint.
;  16 `fieldDeclNames` the same rows by field name, for a read whose
;                    struct the walk cannot see (`fieldDeclRowAt`).
;  17 `fieldStoreSpans` the stores into declared fields already
;                    answered, so a value walked twice draws one
;                    diagnostic (`fieldStoreCheck`).
(struct UState
  (mut unsafeSink : (Option Nodes))
  (mut forgeSites : (Option Nodes))
  (forgeDecls : (Vec FnEnt))
  (unsafePending : (Vec ASTNode))
  (mut ctClaimed : (Option Nodes))
  (mut ctResults : (Vec CtResult))
  (evPending : (Vec EvPend))
  (mut eraseSites : (Option Nodes))
  (eraseCounts : (Vec EraseCount))
  (fieldVals : (Vec FieldStore))
  (mut mayCalls : (Option Names))
  (walkCtx : (Vec BCtx))
  (mut discard : Int)
  (unknownCallees : Names)
  (mut regionScratch : (Option RgnScratch))
  (fieldDecls : (Vec FieldDecl))
  (fieldDeclNames : (Vec FieldDeclName))
  (fieldStoreSpans : (Vec Int)))

; A pending witness owns exactly the nodes its variant needs. The
; declaration-order snapshot of `sigVars` gives late placeholders the
; same enclosing signature they had when the call was checked.
(data EvWitness
  (EvStampWitness ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Names)
  (EvApplicationWitness ASTNode ASTNode Names))

(type EvPend EvWitness)

; Word 18 was `traits`, the `trait` declaration nodes `symbols`
; walked, and word 28 was `implIdx`, every `impl` declaration
; precomputed for the effect fixpoint. Both were RETIRED IN PLACE
; rather than removed when traits went: every reader of this record
; indexes it by position (`memGetWord tc 26`, and so on, in two
; files), so closing a hole in the middle renumbers thirteen later
; fields at eighty-odd sites - which is how the unit table broke the
; last time this record grew in the middle.
;
; Both words are LIVE AGAIN as of 2026-09-03, for the register-pair
; mirror (`tcPairFnsBuild` and what follows it): 18 holds the
; mangled names of every function the emitter gives a `@F$pair`,
; and 28 the per-declaration site record `tcPairSitesOf` builds -
; the tail leaves and match scrutinees that are NOT boxed, which is
; what the effect walk needs to know to charge `Alloc` where the
; block is really built. Reusing the holes is the same arithmetic
; that retired them.
; Word 28 was `retired28`, the second dead word the comment above
; explains. It holds the one bit stage S3 needs at module level: 1
; once `rgnEnsureFacts` has run the region-facts fixpoint, so that a
; `restrict(no-escape)` claim inside `tcWalkDecls` and the region
; pass after it share one computation. It is 2 when that fixpoint
; stopped at `rgnRounds`' round cap instead of converging -
; `rgnFactsTruncated` reads that, and every other reader asks
; `!= 0`, for which 2 is still "done".
; Names carrying `;@axiom:deprecated`, built ONCE and lazily, on
; `sigTags`'s pattern - an empty Vec is a real handle, so "built and
; empty" and "not built" stay distinguishable. It is its own table
; rather than a scan of `sigTags` because that one holds every
; tagged signature (a few hundred, mostly `effect(io)`) and this is
; asked at EVERY resolved reference: scanning it there is the O(n^2)
; this compiler has already written once. Ask the cheap question
; first - `vecLen` of a table that is empty in every program today.
; The NAME of the innermost lambda's parameter placeholder, or 0
; outside a lambda body. `evClassOf` answers `-1` to a minted
; placeholder - "says nothing" - and -1 emits no `__retainref`; this
; is what lets it answer `EV_LAMARG` instead for the one placeholder
; the closure's own application will witness. Saved and restored
; around a lambda body exactly as `lamBase` is, so nesting keeps the
; innermost.
;
; INNERMOST, and only the innermost. `(lambda (a b) ..)` is
; `(lambda (a) (lambda (b) ..))` by the parser, so inside the inner
; lambda `a` is a CAPTURE rather than a parameter, and its evidence
; would have to travel in the closure record under a name the inner
; lambda's own word does not shadow. Until that exists `a` stays -1,
; which under-retains - the leaking direction, and what it already
; did.
; The `;@axiom:pre(...)`/`post(...)` tag whose expression is being
; checked, or 0 outside one. Appended, for the reason every field
; before it was.
;
; It exists so that `checkVar` can tell ONE name apart: `result`
; naming nothing inside a contract is `AX3050` (a `pre` has no
; result yet, and a `post` on a declaration with no signature has no
; declared result type to give it), where the same name outside one
; is the ordinary `AX3001`. Asking it in `checkVar` rather than
; walking the contract for a `result` reference is what makes the
; question TOTAL: a hand-written walk that missed a form would
; answer AX3001 for a shape it did not cover, which is a check
; reporting less than it knows.
; 1 while the restriction set being checked carries `strict`.
;
; `strict` is a MODIFIER and not a restriction: it does not name
; something a body must not do, it says what happens when the walk
; cannot SETTLE what the body does. Without it an unsettleable claim
; is `AX3051`, a warning, for the reason `severity.policy` records -
; refusing it would make dispatch through a stored function
; unwriteable under a restriction rather than unverified. With it the
; same claim is `AX3057`, an error: the author has said that an
; unproven guarantee is not a guarantee, which is the whole of what a
; restriction is for on a sensitive operation.
;
; A field rather than a parameter because the answer is needed at the
; bottom of a chain of ten - `checkOneRestrict` and the seven
; `restrictX` checks - and threading a flag through all of them to
; read it in one place is more edits and more places to get it wrong.
; The stack of OPEN `region` forms while a body is checked, one
; `RgEnt` per form, innermost last (`checkRegion`). Appended, for the
; reason every field before it was. It is what S2 scope-checks a
; region NAME against (AX3058) and what tells `checkSet` whether a
; store's target was bound before the innermost region opened
; (AX3059). Names identify scopes in diagnostics and macro expansion.
; Word 36: the register-pair call sites of the declaration being
; checked (`tcSiteIn`/`tcSiteAdd`); word 28 is S3's `rgnFactsDone`,
; which the pair work also wanted, so this one was appended.
; Word 37: the head node of every `+`, `-`, `*`, `/`, `%`, `<<`
; or `>>` application whose
; operands `checkNumeric` typed FLOAT. Appended, for the reason
; every field before it was.
;
; It exists for `restrict(no-wrap)` and `restrict(no-trap)` and
; nothing else. Those checks are
; lexical - they match the operator's SPELLING - and on `Float`
; operands these spellings lower to `fadd`/`fsub`/`fmul`/`fdiv`
; (`fbinopToLLVM`), which have no wraparound to refuse and no integer
; trap to require: refusing one
; reported a violation that could not happen and prescribed
; `addChecked`/`divChecked`, which are `(-> Int Int (Result Int Error))` and do
; not typecheck against a `Float`. The operand types are known in
; exactly one place, `checkNumeric`, and are not kept on the nodes,
; so the sites are recorded as the checker meets them.
;
; A whole-program list rather than a per-declaration one because a
; node handle is unique: membership is exact, and `tcWalkDecls`
; checks a body BEFORE it reads that declaration's tags, so every
; site a restricted body contains is already in here when
; `restrictEmitWraps` asks.
; Subtype declarations, collected for lookup by name. Each entry is
; a TAG_D_SUBTYPE node carrying the subtype's name, base type, and
; range constraint.
; The module whose body is being checked, or None for the
; entry file. A bare reference inside it resolves to
; that module's own definition first - the same rule
; codegen's `mangledFor` and stage0's
; `resolve_bare_fn` apply, and all three have to agree
; or the checker types against one definition while
; the emitter calls another.
; `type` declaration nodes, in collection order.
; Nothing in checking or emission reads them - they
; exist for `symbols`, whose A rows walk them the way
; stage0 walks tc.aliases.
; The mangled `Mod$name` of every declaration carried
; into the program that its own module does not export.
; Empty for every program in this repository. See the
; visibility section for why it lives here and not on
; the entries.
; The exact-name index over `fns`, or 0 before it is
; built. Appended, like every field before it: every
; reader indexes this record by position, which is how
; the unit table broke the last time it grew in the
; middle. See the exact-name index section.
; The same thing for the ambiguity table, and appended
; for the same reason. `ambFind` was a linear scan of a
; Vec that grows with the PROGRAM, and `ambRecord` calls
; it once per name it records, so building the table was
; quadratic on its own - 935 of ~1200 profile samples on
; a 32,000-declaration entry file, after the two entry
; passes were indexed and while nothing else was close.
; The name-index slice fixed `tcFns` and left this one.
; The scope length at the innermost lambda's entry, or 0
; outside every lambda. `checkSet` compares the target's
; scope INDEX against it: an entry below it was captured,
; capture is by value (MM-VAL-16), and an assignment to
; it could never be observed - codegen refused exactly
; this as AX4002, whose own note says the check that
; should have run did not (MM-MUT-1a). Saved and
; restored around each lambda body, so nesting keeps the
; innermost boundary.
; MM-LIFE-2d evidence: the ordered tyvar names of the
; function whose body is being checked (0 outside a
; body, or when its signature has none) - what turns a
; witness that IS one of those variables into the
; symbolic code 2+k - and the declaration node itself,
; stamped into every evidence stamp the body creates so
; emission can refuse a stamp another declaration wrote
; (a macro-spliced argument node is a shared object).
; The TAG_D_SIG nodes carrying an AXTAG, built
; once and 0 before that. Appended, for the reason
; every field before it was. Scanning `allDecls` per
; function instead measured 1000 -> 1233 ms.
; The SUFFIX index over `fns`, or 0 before it is built.
; Appended, for the reason every field before it was.
; See the suffix index section.
; `privates` as a set. 0 exactly when `privates` is.
; The TYPE-name index, or 0 before it is built. Appended, for the
; reason every field before it was: every reader indexes this record
; by position. See the type namespace section.
; Whether any `(region r ...)` form has been checked, 1 once seen and
; 0 until then. Set by `checkRegion` as a side effect of the S2 walk
; that already visits every region form, so `rgnCheckAll` reads it in
; O(1) with no additional body scan. Appended, for the reason every
; field before it was. The design note calls this slice 2b's
; parser-set bit; it is checker-set during the existing walk instead,
; because the parser has no TC to set and changing `parseModuleWith`'s
; PResult shape touches every caller, while the S2 walk already sees
; every form exactly once.
; Whether the region pass may stamp call nodes with proven freshness,
; 1 only during the post-fixpoint walks `rgnCheckAll` runs. The same
; `rgnPass` with report 0 also runs inside `rgnRounds` on every round
; while facts are still moving, so the report value cannot distinguish
; them; this word can. Appended, for the reason every field before it
; was.
; The spans where S2 refused a store (AX3059 shapes b/c), so S3's
; reporting walk can suppress the same-store double (AX3060) while
; reporting everything S2 cannot see - a store hidden behind a call.
; S2 runs during `tcWalkDecls`, S3 after it, same TC, so the set is
; complete before the first suppression is asked. Appended, for the
; reason every field before it was.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Typed access to the checker state. Every phase holds the `TC` record
; as a word, so each reader below views it through the struct's own
; declaration and answers the field at the type it holds. A getter is
; one load (and the retain a reference result takes); `opt` inlines
; it. A reference field is written through `memSetWord`, which takes a
; share of the new value and leaves the old one, exactly as the
; record's other writers do; an `Int` field is written with `set`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: tcFnsVec (-> TC (Vec FnEnt)))
(pub fn (tcFnsVec tc)
  tc.fns)

(pub :: tcDatasVec (-> TC (Vec DataEnt)))
(pub fn (tcDatasVec tc)
  tc.datas)

(pub :: tcStructsVec (-> TC (Vec StructEnt)))
(pub fn (tcStructsVec tc)
  tc.structs)

(pub :: tcScopeVec (-> TC (Vec SEnt)))
(pub fn (tcScopeVec tc)
  tc.scope)

(pub :: tcOutVec (-> TC Diags))
(pub fn (tcOutVec tc)
  tc.out)

(:: tcSetOutVec (-> TC Diags Int))
(fn (tcSetOutVec tc v)
  {
    (set tc.out v)
    0
  })

(pub :: tcCounter (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (tcCounter tc)
  tc.counter)

(pub :: tcBumpCounter (-> TC TC))
(pub fn (tcBumpCounter tc)
  {
    (set tc.counter (+ tc.counter 1))
    tc
  })

(pub :: tcFrameBase (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (tcFrameBase tc)
  tc.frameBase)

(:: tcSetFrameBase (-> TC Int TC))
(fn (tcSetFrameBase tc v)
  {
    (set tc.frameBase v)
    tc
  })

(pub :: tcMacrosVec (-> TC Nodes))
(pub fn (tcMacrosVec tc)
  tc.macros)

(pub :: tcEffectsVec (-> TC Nodes))
(pub fn (tcEffectsVec tc)
  tc.effects)

(:: tcEntryDecls (-> TC Nodes))
(fn (tcEntryDecls tc)
  tc.entryDecls)

(:: tcCurParams (-> TC Strings))
(fn (tcCurParams tc)
  tc.curParams)

(:: tcSetCurParams (-> TC Strings TC))
(fn (tcSetCurParams tc v)
  {
    (set tc.curParams v)
    tc
  })

(:: tcCurCallable (-> TC (Vec Int)))
(fn (tcCurCallable tc)
  tc.curCallable)

(:: tcSetCurCallable (-> TC (Vec Int) TC))
(fn (tcSetCurCallable tc v)
  {
    (set tc.curCallable v)
    tc
  })

(pub :: tcUnits (-> TC (Vec core::CUnit2)))
(pub fn (tcUnits tc)
  tc.units)

(:: tcCurUnit (-> TC Int))
(fn (tcCurUnit tc)
  tc.curUnit)

(:: tcSetCurUnit (-> TC Int TC))
(fn (tcSetCurUnit tc v)
  {
    (set tc.curUnit v)
    tc
  })

(:: tcAmbTbl (-> TC (Vec AmbEnt)))
(fn (tcAmbTbl tc)
  tc.ambTbl)

; The module being checked, or None for the entry file.
(:: tcCurModule (-> TC (Option String)))
(fn (tcCurModule tc)
  tc.curModule)

(:: tcSetCurModule (-> TC (Option String) TC))
(fn (tcSetCurModule tc v)
  {
    (set tc.curModule v)
    tc
  })

(pub :: tcAliasesVec (-> TC Nodes))
(pub fn (tcAliasesVec tc)
  tc.aliases)

(:: tcPairFns (-> TC Names))
(fn (tcPairFns tc)
  tc.pairFns)

(:: tcSetPairFns (-> TC Names TC))
(fn (tcSetPairFns tc v)
  {
    (set tc.pairFns v)
    tc
  })

; Private names, absent when no privacy or library visibility key is needed.
(:: tcPrivates (-> TC (Option Names)))
(fn (tcPrivates tc)
  tc.privates)

; The exact-name index, absent until `tcNew` builds its typed buckets.
(:: tcFnIdx (-> TC (Option FnIndex)))
(fn (tcFnIdx tc)
  tc.fnIdx)

(:: tcSetFnIdx (-> TC FnIndex TC))
(fn (tcSetFnIdx tc v)
  {
    (set tc.fnIdx (Some v))
    tc
  })

(:: tcAmbIdx (-> TC (Option AmbIndex)))
(fn (tcAmbIdx tc)
  tc.ambIdx)

(:: tcSetAmbIdx (-> TC AmbIndex TC))
(fn (tcSetAmbIdx tc v)
  {
    (set tc.ambIdx (Some v))
    tc
  })

(:: tcLamBase (-> TC Int))
(fn (tcLamBase tc)
  tc.lamBase)

(:: tcSetLamBase (-> TC Int TC))
(fn (tcSetLamBase tc v)
  {
    (set tc.lamBase v)
    tc
  })

; The signature's type-variable names, empty outside its body.
(:: tcCurSigVars (-> TC Names))
(fn (tcCurSigVars tc)
  tc.curSigVars)

(:: tcSetCurSigVars (-> TC Names TC))
(fn (tcSetCurSigVars tc v)
  {
    (set tc.curSigVars v)
    tc
