; A fresh `_tN`. stage0 mints the name from the current counter and
; increments at SOME sites but not others; callers say which.
; An INSTANTIATION placeholder, named `_iN` rather than `_tN`, and the
; prefix is load-bearing: it is the only kind `tyPin` may bind.
;
; `freshTVar` mints for two different jobs and only one of them may be
; pinned. Instantiating a DECLARED signature mints "the type the caller
; chose for `a` at this call", and two uses of one binding have to
; agree about that or a container can be written at `Int` and read at
; `String`. Every other use - a pattern binder, a missing
; parameter type - mints "not known", and pinning
; THAT reports an error where the checker simply has no information.
;
; Measured: without the split, `(fn (untyped x) x)` applied to an `Int`
; and then a `String` was refused. The function is correct and the
; refusal was invented by pinning - "under-report, never mis-report" is
; this file's rule, and one prefix is what keeps it.
(pub :: freshInstVar (-> TC ASTNode))
(pub fn (freshInstVar tc)
  {
    (let t (mkTVar (concat "_i" (fmtInt (tcCounter tc)))))
    {
      (tcBumpCounter tc)
      t
    }
  })

(pub :: freshTVar (-> TC Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (freshTVar tc bump)
  {
    (let t (mkTVar (concat "_t" (fmtInt (tcCounter tc)))))
    {
      (if (== bump 1)
        (tcBumpCounter tc))
      t
    }
  })

; Int -> Int -> ... -> Int with `arity` arrows; arity 0 is bare Int.
(pub :: mkIntArrow (-> Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (mkIntArrow arity)
  (if (== arity 0)
    mkIntTy
    (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkIntArrow (- arity 1)))))

; A primitive that CALLS the function it is given at position `i`:
; the recovery point's thunk, and a spawn's. The position is marked
; effect-transparent with one use and nothing discharged, so what a
; caller hands it counts where the call is - inside whatever `handle`
; surrounds it, which a forked child inherits. A thread does not
; inherit the evidence slots, so an operation in a thread's thunk is
; discharged here by a handler that is not installed there; that one
; is under-reported.
(pub :: primCallsAt (-> (Vec FnEnt) String Int Int))
(pub fn (primCallsAt fns name i)
  {
    (let entFound (findFnEntExact fns name 0))
    (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
      {
        (vecPush (fnEntEparams ent) i)
        (epUseNote (fnEntUses ent) i vecNew 0)
      }
      0)
  })

(pub :: regFn (-> (Vec FnEnt) String ASTNode (Vec FnEnt)))
(pub fn (regFn fns name ty)
  (vecPush fns
    (FnEnt
      :name name
      :ty ty
      :paramCount None
      :isBuiltin 1
      :isEffectOp 0
      :effects vecNew
      :eparams vecNew
      :calls vecNew
      :rgn None
      :unsafe 0
      :uses (vecWithCapacity 1)
      :mayCalls (vecWithCapacity 1)
      :pending 0
      :scoped vecNew
      :compute vecNew
      :restrictions vecNew
      :lambdaCast NoLambdaCast)))

; A primitive that PERFORMS something, registered with the effect it
; performs rather than with the empty set every other one gets.
;
; `MM-EXEC-9a` records that the inferred effect set is an
; UNDER-approximation and says a conforming implementation SHOULD make
; it an over-approximation, then lists the measured ways it is not.
; Every row that was a PRIMITIVE registered as though it computed is
; closed by this function, and the rest of that table is not about
; primitives at all.
;
; `__alloc` went first, on 2026-08-23, and the inversion is the part
; worth naming. `Alloc` fired only for the `(alloc T)` keyword, a form
; that allocates nothing and that `MM-LIFE-7` records as
; definable-but-uncallable, and never for the operation the whole heap
; goes through. So the effect existed, was spellable, was checkable,
; and was attached to the wrong thing.
;
; THE OTHER SIX WENT ON 2026-08-25, and they are the same defect with
; the inversion left out - a primitive that performs, registered like
; `__load64`, which computes:
;
;   `__store8`/`__store64` -> `Mut`. They write memory at an address,
;   which is visible through every alias of whatever is there. That is
;   the definition `Mut` already carried for `(set base.field v)`, and
;   a field store lowers to one of these.
;
;   `__argc`/`__argv` -> `IO`. Reading the command line is reading
;   input the process did not compute, which is what `MM-EXEC-9b` says
;   a purity claim ought to exclude and did not. It BROADENS `IO` from
;   "reaches a `__syscallN`" to "reaches the outside world", and
;   `reference.md`'s table is corrected to match rather than left to
;   disagree.
;
;   the three arena primitives -> `Alloc`. `Alloc` is the heap-MACHINERY
;   effect and not strictly an allocation: `walkEffectsHandle` already
;   contributes it for installing handler evidence, which allocates
;   nothing either. A reset is the operation that ends every block
;   allocated since a mark, so a function performing one is as far from
;   pure as a function performing the allocation. `__axiom_arena_mark`
;   only reads the bump pointer and is over-approximated on purpose -
;   the rule's own SHOULD asks for over-approximation, and a mark is
;   only ever written to be paired with a reset.
;
; Measured, this tree, counted the way `scripts/check-agent-policy.sh`
; counts - one probe importing every module, 578 declarations listed:
; 216 carried an effect before and 261 after, so it discriminates
; rather than blankets. TWO exhaustive `handle` lists had to name `Mut`,
; which is `AX3011` working rather than a cost - and the second is the
; measurement lesson: `tests/stdlib/320-effect-gc-roots.ax` is a
; checked-in fixture, and the other is a program `check-recover.sh`
; BUILDS FROM A HEREDOC, which a differential over `tests/**/*.ax`
; cannot see. A gate that generates a program is a second corpus. Registering these is not adding noise to the analysis;
; it is removing a lie from it.
;
; What deliberately does NOT get one: `__retain`/`__release`, whose
; writes are the runtime's own bookkeeping and invisible to the
; program's meaning - giving them `Mut` marks every function that
; touches a reference and deletes the discrimination the rest of this
; buys.
; A primitive with two effects: `IO` and the refinement that says what
; kind of IO - a spawn is `IO` and `Spawn`, a join `IO` and `Block`.
(pub :: regFnEff2 (-> (Vec FnEnt) String ASTNode String String (Vec FnEnt)))
(pub fn (regFnEff2 fns name ty eff1 eff2)
  {
    (let ent (regFnEff fns name ty eff1))
    {
      (vecPush (fnEntEffects (vecGet fns (- (vecLen fns) 1))) eff2)
      ent
    }
  })

(pub :: regFnEff (-> (Vec FnEnt) String ASTNode String (Vec FnEnt)))
(pub fn (regFnEff fns name ty eff)
  {
    (let effs vecNew)
    {
      (vecPush effs eff)
      (vecPush fns
        (FnEnt
          :name name
          :ty ty
          :paramCount None
          :isBuiltin 1
          :isEffectOp 0
          :effects effs
          :eparams vecNew
          :calls vecNew
          :rgn None
          :unsafe 0
          :uses (vecWithCapacity 1)
          :mayCalls (vecWithCapacity 1)
          :pending 0
          :scoped vecNew
          :compute vecNew
          :restrictions vecNew
          :lambdaCast NoLambdaCast))
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Registration: stage0's TypeChecker::new(), in its order - the order
; is observable through suggestion tie-breaking. Arithmetic and
; bitwise operators are Int -> Int -> Int, comparisons return Bool,
; the logical pair takes Bool; then every primitive at its arity;
; then the builtin Option, whose constructors are in scope in every
; program without being declared in any of them.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every carried-but-not-exported declaration, keyed the way the
; lookups ask: `Mod$name`. `fn` and `::` already carry that spelling
; because `mangleDecl` rewrote them; `data` and `struct` do not, so
; theirs is built from the module recorded on the node.
;
; Collected once, before anything is looked up, and empty unless the
; program actually declares something private.
; The duplicate test is asked of a SET. It was `strInVec out k 0`, a
; scan of everything collected so far, so collecting N private names
; cost N^2/2 string compares - quadratic in the count of a thing the
; standard library now has 151 of, and 18.4% of a check over a module
; of 8,000 private declarations. The Vec is still what this answers,
; because word 19 is read as a Vec; the set is scaffolding and is
; dropped when the walk ends.
; The private set, extended for the standard library's `Internal`
; modules (AX5002). A public name of an internal module is private to
; the library: its key joins the set beside an `internal ` marker, and
; each library module's name joins as a `library ` marker, so
; `privBlocks` can let another library module through. The entry file
; has no name, so a library entry file, such as a module checked on its
; own, is the bare `library ` marker. A marker holds a space, which no
; key or module name can. A program that reaches no internal module
; keeps the set it had.
(pub :: withInternalVisibility (-> Nodes Units (Option Names) (Option Names)))
(pub fn (withInternalVisibility decls units privs)
  {
    (let internals (internalUnitNames units 0 vecNew))
    (if (== (vecLen internals) 0)
      privs
      {
        (let out
          (match privs
            ((None) vecNew)
            ((Some names) names)))
        (internalKeysInto decls internals out 0)
        (libraryMarksInto units out 0)
        (Some out)
      })
  })

; Whether a unit is one of the standard library's `Internal` modules: it
; was found through the library's own search directories, and its name
; lies under `Internal`.
(pub :: unitIsInternal (-> CUnit2 Bool))
(pub fn (unitIsInternal unit)
  (&& unit.library
    (if (let (Some name) (unitName unit))
      (strStartsWith name "Internal.")
      false)))

(pub :: internalUnitNames (-> Units Int Strings Strings))
(pub fn (internalUnitNames units i out)
  (if (>= i (vecLen units))
    out
    {
      (let unit (as (vecGet units i) CUnit2))
      (if (unitIsInternal unit)
        (if (let (Some name) (unitName unit))
          (vecPush out name)))
      (internalUnitNames units (+ i 1) out)
    }))

(pub :: internalKeysInto (-> (Vec ASTNode) Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (internalKeysInto decls internals out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (isSome (nodeModuleOpt d))
            (&& (== (nodeVis d) 1) (== (strInVec internals (nodeModuleName d) 0) 1)))
        {
          (let k
            (if (|| (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN) (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG))
              (nodeAName d)
              (privKey (nodeModuleOpt d) (nodeAName d))))
          (vecPush out k)
          (vecPush out (concat "internal " k))
        })
      (internalKeysInto decls internals out (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: libraryMarksInto (-> Units Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (libraryMarksInto units out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen units))
    0
    {
      (let unit (as (vecGet units i) CUnit2))
      (if unit.library
        (vecPush out
          (match (unitName unit)
            ((None) "library ")
            ((Some name) (concat "library " name)))))
      (libraryMarksInto units out (+ i 1))
    }))

; AX5002 at an import: a file outside the standard library importing
; one of the library's `Internal` modules. The entry file's imports are
; its own declarations; every other unit's were recorded on it as the
; resolver read them.
(pub :: checkInternalImports (-> Units (Vec ASTNode) (Vec diag::Diag) Int))
(pub fn (checkInternalImports units entryDecls out)
  (if (== (vecLen (internalUnitNames units 0 vecNew)) 0)
    0
    {
      (let entry (as (vecGet units 0) CUnit2))
      (if (! entry.library)
        (internalImportsIn units entryDecls out 0 0))
      (let mut i 1)
      (while (< i (vecLen units))
        {
          (let unit (as (vecGet units i) CUnit2))
          (if (! unit.library)
            (internalImportsIn units unit.imports out i 0))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      0
    }))

(pub :: internalImportsIn (-> Units (Vec ASTNode) (Vec diag::Diag) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (internalImportsIn units sites out unit i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen sites))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet sites i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_IMPORT) (unitNamedInternal units (nodeAName d) 0))
        (vecPush out
          (diagSetUnit
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX5002" "internal-module" (nodeSpan d)
                (concat (concat "module `" (nodeAName d))
                  "` is internal to the standard library, and only the library may import it")
                None
                (Some
                  "use the library's typed interface instead; raw memory access belongs in your own function, written with the primitives under `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)`"))
              "internal to the standard library")
            unit)))
      (internalImportsIn units sites out unit (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: unitNamedInternal (-> Units String Int Bool))
(pub fn (unitNamedInternal units name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen units))
    false
    {
      (let unit (as (vecGet units i) CUnit2))
      (if (unitNameIs unit name)
        (unitIsInternal unit)
        (unitNamedInternal units name (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: collectPrivates (-> Nodes Names Int (Option Names)))
(pub fn (collectPrivates decls out i)
  {
    (let n (countPrivates decls 0 0))
    (if (== n 0)
      None
      (collectPrivatesSeen decls out (privSetNew n) i))
  })

; How many declarations the walk below can record, counted first so the
; set is sized by ITS population rather than by the program's.
;
; Sizing it by `vecLen decls` was wrong in the direction that costs a
; program which declares nothing private ANYTHING - the branch that
; writes to the set never runs, and `fnIdxBuckets` had still built
; 2N+17 slots one `vecPush` at a time, so a 16,000-declaration public
; module paid ~256 KB of array plus every doubling intermediate the
; bump allocator does not free, to read none of it. The code this
; replaced allocated nothing on that input. `axiom repl` calls
; `checkModule` per line and resets no arena, so it was retained for
; the session.
;
; A declaration count is an upper bound on the KEY count, which is the
; same relationship `fnIdxBuildSized` takes as its hint, and zero is
; exact: nothing private means no set at all.
(pub :: countPrivates (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (countPrivates decls i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    acc
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (countPrivates decls (+ i 1)
        (if (isNone (nodeModuleOpt d))
          acc
          (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EXTERN)
            (countPrivateExtern (nodeCChildren d) 0 acc)
            (if (== (nodeVis d) 0)
              (+ acc 1)
              acc))))
    }))

(pub :: collectPrivatesSeen (-> Nodes Names StringRows Int (Option Names)))
(pub fn (collectPrivatesSeen decls out seen i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    (if (== (vecLen out) 0)
      None
      (Some out))
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (if (isSome (nodeModuleOpt d))
          (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EXTERN)
            (collectPrivateExtern (nodeCChildren d) (nodeModuleOpt d) out seen 0)
            (if (== (nodeVis d) 0)
              {
                (let k
                  (if (|| (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN) (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG))
                    (nodeAName d)
                    (privKey (nodeModuleOpt d) (nodeAName d))))
                (if (! (== (privIdxHas seen k) 1))
                  {
                    (defIdxPut seen k)
                    (vecPush out k)
                  })
              })))
        (collectPrivatesSeen decls out seen (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: countPrivateExtern (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (countPrivateExtern items i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen items))
    acc
    (countPrivateExtern items (+ i 1)
      (if (== (nodeVis (vecGet items i)) 0)
        (+ acc 1)
        acc))))

(pub :: collectPrivateExtern (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Option String) Strings StringRows Int Int))
(pub fn (collectPrivateExtern items mod out seen i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen items))
    0
    {
      {
        (let f (vecGet items i))
        (if (== (nodeVis f) 0)
          {
            (let k (privKey mod (nodeAName f)))
            (if (== (privIdxHas seen k) 0)
              {
                (defIdxPut seen k)
                (vecPush out k)
              })
          })
      }
      (collectPrivateExtern items mod out seen (+ i 1))
    }))

; THE DEVICE PRIMITIVES (R-D2, 2026-09-27): memory-mapped I/O at the
; widths a device register has, and the AArch64 system operations a
; bare-metal program needs around it. Registered in one block, called
; from `tcNew` beside `__store8v` - the byte store that was the whole
; of this family before - so the family reads as one list and a merge
; touching the rest of `tcNew` does not touch it.
;
; `__vloadN addr` / `__vstoreN addr v` for N in 8, 16, 32, 64: ONE
; access of N bits at the byte address `addr` itself (not `base + i`,
; which is `__store8v`'s older shape), lowered to LLVM `load volatile`
; / `store volatile` of `iN` with natural alignment; a load answers
; the value zero-extended to the word, a store narrows `v` and answers
; 0. `docs/memory-model.md` MM-FFI-8 is the rule: volatile keeps every
; access, its width and its order against other volatile accesses, and
; is NOT a synchronisation edge - that is what the atomics are for
; (MM-PAR-9). All eight are `Unsafe` (`isUnsafeDevicePrim`) and carry
; `Mut`: a store writes, and a device READ may change device state (a
; FIFO pops, an interrupt is acknowledged), so a load is not the
; side-effect-free read `__load64` is and its row says so - the
; over-approximation MM-EXEC-9a asks for.
;
; `__arm_*`: AArch64 only, refused on any other target at build time
; as AX4008 (`devicePrimRefused` in codegen.ax, reported by `main.ax`
; the way AX4006 is). The rows follow the rules the older primitives
; set: a barrier orders writes and carries `Mut` as `__fence` does; a
; read of a register the HARDWARE owns - the counter, its frequency,
; the cache geometry - is input the program did not compute and
; carries `IO` as `__argc` does, and so does `__arm_wfi`, which waits
; on the outside world; a write that arms the timer, masks interrupts
; or maintains the cache carries `Mut`; the thread-ID register is
; state the program itself stored, read with no effect as `__load64`
; reads a word. The two cache operations take an address and are
; `Unsafe` beside the eight accesses; the rest touch no address.
(pub :: tcRegDevicePrims (-> (Vec FnEnt) Int))
(pub fn (tcRegDevicePrims fns)
  {
    (regFnEff fns "__vload8" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__vload16" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__vload32" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__vload64" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__vstore8" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__vstore16" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__vstore32" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__vstore64" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_dmb" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_dsb" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_isb" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_cntvct" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "IO"))
    ; `RNDR`, FEAT_RNG's random-number register: one word of hardware
    ; entropy, or 0 when the generator could not produce one in time.
    ; `IO` and `Entropy`, which is what makes a bare-metal program that
    ; draws randomness say so. `baremetal-aarch64` only (AX4008): an
    ; aarch64 host without FEAT_RNG answers the read with SIGILL.
    (regFnEff2 fns "__arm_rndr" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "IO") (builtinEff "Entropy"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_cntfrq" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "IO"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_ctr" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "IO"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_set_cntv_cval" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_set_cntv_ctl" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_irq_mask" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_irq_unmask" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_wfi" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "IO"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_dc_cvac" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_dc_civac" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    (regFn fns "__arm_tpidr" (mkIntArrow 0))
    (regFnEff fns "__arm_set_tpidr" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Mut"))
    0
  })

; `__asm.N`, the primitive an `asm` form lowers to (MM-FFI-9,
; `expandAsm` in expand.ax): a description literal, then N `Int`
; inputs, answering an `Int`. The `.` keeps it out of any source's
; reach, so only the expansion calls it. Its row is `Mut`, because
; every block clobbers memory, and `isUnsafePrim` adds `Unsafe`: the
; checker can't see what the instructions do, so the declaration that
; holds one vouches for them. N runs to `expAsmMaxValues`.
(pub :: tcRegAsmPrims (-> (Vec FnEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcRegAsmPrims fns n)
  (if (> n 16)
    0
    {
      (regFnEff fns (concat "__asm." (fmtInt n)) (mkTArr mkStringTy (mkIntArrow n))
        (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (tcRegAsmPrims fns (+ n 1))
    }))

(pub :: checkIntegerWord (-> TC ASTNode String ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkIntegerWord tc value name)
  {
    (let ty (checkExprH tc value 0))
    {
      (if (== (isPendingNode ty) 1)
        (refusePendingOperand tc ty "an integer" (spanOf value)))
      (if (== (tyIsErr ty) 1)
        mkTErr
        {
          (let code (tyIntegerCode tc ty))
          (let expected (strSlice name 15 (- (strLen name) 15)))
          (if (== code 0)
            {
              (emitMismatch tc "an integer" (tyRender ty) (spanOf value))
              mkTErr
            }
            (if (!= code (integerTypeCode expected))
              {
                (emitMismatch tc expected (tyRender ty) (spanOf value))
                mkTErr
              }
              mkIntTy))
        })
    }
  })

(pub :: tcRegIntegerWords (-> (Vec FnEnt) Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (tcRegIntegerWords fns names i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    0
    {
      {
        (let name (vecGet names i))
        (regFn fns (concat "__integer.word." name) (mkTArr (mkTCon name vecNew) mkIntTy))
      }
      (tcRegIntegerWords fns names (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: tcNew (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Units TC))
(pub fn (tcNew entryDecls allDecls units)
  {
    (let fns vecNew)
    (let datas vecNew)
    {
      (regFn fns "+" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "-" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "*" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "/" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "%" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "&" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "|" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "^" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "<<" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns ">>" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "==" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns "!=" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns "<" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns ">" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns "<=" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns ">=" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns "&&" (mkTArr mkBoolTy (mkTArr mkBoolTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns "||" (mkTArr mkBoolTy (mkTArr mkBoolTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns "!" (mkTArr mkBoolTy mkBoolTy))
      (regFn fns "__syscall0" (mkIntArrow 1))
      (regFn fns "__syscall1" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFn fns "__syscall2" (mkIntArrow 3))
      (regFn fns "__syscall3" (mkIntArrow 4))
      (regFn fns "__syscall4" (mkIntArrow 5))
      (regFn fns "__syscall5" (mkIntArrow 6))
      (regFn fns "__syscall6" (mkIntArrow 7))
      (regFn fns "__load8" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFnEff fns "__store8" (mkIntArrow 3) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      ; `__store8v` stores with LLVM `volatile`: the middle end keeps
      ; every store and their order, which is what MMIO needs and what
      ; `__store8` must not promise (a volatile store is never folded,
      ; which would pessimize every ordinary byte store). Same shape
      ; and row as `__store8` in every other respect.
      (regFnEff fns "__store8v" (mkIntArrow 3) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (tcRegDevicePrims fns)
      (tcRegAsmPrims fns 0)
      ; `(__asm.sym f)`: an `asm` form's `sym` operand (`expandAsm`).
      ; The argument is checked as the reference it is - an undefined
      ; name is AX3001 at the name - and `checkAsmSym` holds it to a
      ; function with a symbol. Codegen never evaluates the call: it
      ; reads the name and passes the symbol to the instructions.
      (regFn fns "__asm.sym" (mkTArr (mkTVar "a") mkIntTy))
      (regFn fns "__load64" (mkIntArrow 2))
      ; `(__call_word fn arg)`: call a C function `i64 (i64)` by its
      ; ADDRESS - the destructor word of a foreign-form block
      ; (pkg/axiom-oxide/axiom/Ffi.ax `ffiHandleClose`); the release runtime makes
      ; the same call when such a block dies
      (regFn fns "__call_word" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFnEff2 fns "__resource_new"
        (mkTArr (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTCon "Handle" vecNew)))
        (builtinEff "Alloc") (builtinEff "IO"))
      (regFn fns "__resource_get" (mkTArr (mkTCon "Handle" vecNew) mkIntTy))
      (regFnEff fns "__resource_close" (mkTArr (mkTCon "Handle" vecNew) mkIntTy) (builtinEff "IO"))
      (regFnEff fns "__resource_disarm" (mkTArr (mkTCon "Handle" vecNew) mkIntTy) (builtinEff "IO"))
      ; 1 while a recovery abort runs the cleanup of the owners made
      ; inside its extent (docs/memory-model.md MM-EXEC-20), else 0.
      (regFn fns "__resource_unwinding" (mkIntArrow 0))
      ; `(__contract cond "message")` - the run-time half of
      ; `;@axiom:pre(...)`/`post(...)`. Not spellable in source in the
      ; sense that matters: `expLowerContracts` is the only writer, and
      ; nothing documents it, exactly as `__streq` is only ever written
      ; by `checkNumeric`.
      ;
      ; THE CONDITION'S TYPE IS A VARIABLE, and deliberately: a
      ; contract that is not `Bool` is `AX3050` with the contract's own
      ; span and the contract's own explanation, so this signature must
      ; not produce an `AX3004` about a node the author did not write
      ; on top of it. `checkContractForm` is what checks the condition
      ; and it does so once.
      ;
      ; REGISTERED WITH NO EFFECT ROW. It aborts; it does not return.
      ; That is the same reading `/` already has - a division by zero
      ; writes to fd 2 and exits 72, and `/` carries no `IO` - and
      ; giving it one would put `IO` into the row of every function
      ; that states a contract and draw a false `AX3042` on it.
      (regFn fns "__contract" (mkTArr (mkTVar "a") (mkTArr mkStringTy mkIntTy)))
      (regFnEff fns "__store64" (mkIntArrow 3) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (regFnEff fns "__alloc" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Alloc"))
      (regFn fns "__retain" (mkIntArrow 1))
      (regFn fns "__release" (mkIntArrow 1))
      ; `__retainref` is `__retain`'s TYPE-DIRECTED twin, and the only
      ; primitive whose signature is polymorphic on purpose: it takes
      ; a share of its argument exactly when the argument is a
      ; reference, which the call's evidence stamp answers - a
      ; constant for a known type, a bit of the caller's own evidence
      ; word for a type variable, and NOTHING AT ALL for an Int.
      ; `__retain` cannot do this and must not try: it is `Int -> Int`
      ; and retaining an Int above 4096 dereferences it.
      ;
      ; It exists for the two places a reference LEAVES the type
      ; system through a `cast Int` - `Mem.memSetWord` and the AST's
      ; `mkNode` - which is where MM-LIFE-2c's co-ownership blocker
      ; actually lives (docs/memory-model.md).
      (regFn fns "__retainref" (mkTArr (mkTVar "a") mkIntTy))
      ; `(__indexTrap)` NEVER RETURNS, so it inhabits every result type:
      ; its type is a bare variable, freshly instantiated at each use.
      ; That is AX3040's own stated way out - "make it DIVERGE, so every
      ; path ends in a call that never returns, which is what makes
      ; `for all a` honest" - and it is why a container can refuse an
      ; index without fabricating an element.
      ;
      ; No effect row: the trap writes to fd 2 and exits, and giving it
      ; `IO` would put an effect on every function that merely REFUSES
      ; an index, which is a claim about the happy path nobody takes.
      (regFn fns "__indexTrap" (mkTVar "a"))
      (regFnEff fns "__argc" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "IO"))
      (regFnEff fns "__argv" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "IO"))
      ; `(__axiom_recover mark thunk)` - MM-ALLOC-16a. The second
      ; argument is spelled `(-> Int Int)` rather than `Int` because it
      ; is CALLED, and a handle type here would let any word through to
      ; an indirect call through word 0 of whatever it points at.
      (regFn fns "__axiom_recover" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) mkIntTy)))
      (primCallsAt fns "__axiom_recover" 1)
      (regFnEff fns "__axiom_arena_mark" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "Alloc"))
      (regFnEff2 fns "__axiom_arena_mark_into" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Alloc")
        (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (regFnEff fns "__axiom_arena_reset" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Alloc"))
      (regFnEff fns "__axiom_arena_reset_keeping" (mkIntArrow 3) (builtinEff "Alloc"))
      ; `(__axiom_mem_stat k)` reads the allocator's own counts
      ; (MM-ALLOC-24). `Alloc`, as `__axiom_arena_mark` is: the answer
      ; is the allocator's history, so no `pure` body may depend on it.
      ; Not unsafe - it names no address and writes nothing.
      (regFnEff fns "__axiom_mem_stat" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Alloc"))
      ; `(__axiom_arena_cap n)` caps the bytes the calling arena may map
      ; (MM-PAR-17) and answers the cap it replaces. `Alloc` and `Mut`,
      ; as `__axiom_arena_mark_into` is: it changes what the allocator
      ; will do. Not unsafe - a cap too low is an out-of-memory trap,
      ; status 70, never a wrong access.
      (regFnEff2 fns "__axiom_arena_cap" (mkIntArrow 1) (builtinEff "Alloc") (builtinEff "Mut"))
      ; `(__axiom_stack_run bytes thunk arg)` runs `(thunk arg)` on a
      ; stack of `bytes` the call maps, with a guard below it whose
      ; touch traps 86 (MM-PAR-17). `IO`: it maps memory and sets signal
      ; actions through the kernel. The thunk is CALLED, so it is
      ; spelled `(-> Int Int)` for `__axiom_recover`'s reason, and what
      ; it performs counts at the call. Not unsafe: a stack too small is
      ; a trap, never a wrong access.
      (regFnEff fns "__axiom_stack_run"
        (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy)))
        (builtinEff "IO"))
      (primCallsAt fns "__axiom_stack_run" 1)
      ; The five atomic primitives (2026-08-29): the machine word at a
      ; BYTE ADDRESS, read, written, added to, compared-and-swapped and
      ; fenced, sequentially consistent (codegen.ax `emitPrimAtomic`).
      ; Registered with their effects on arrival, which is the shape
      ; the comment above asks for and the first time it was met
      ; rather than corrected. The four that WRITE or ORDER carry
      ; `Mut`, for `__store64`'s reason: a store, an add and a
      ; compare-and-swap write a word every alias sees, and a fence
      ; exists only to order such writes. `__atomic_load` deliberately
      ; does not: it reads a word exactly as `__load64` does, and what
      ; the atomic spelling changes is the instruction's ordering, not
      ; what the function PERFORMS. Giving the load `Mut` would mark
      ; every reader of a shared counter the way `Mut` on `__retain`
      ; would mark every reader of a reference - the discrimination the
      ; comment above buys, deleted - and it is the control that lets
      ; `check-agent-policy.sh` tell "the atomics that write carry Mut"
      ; from "everything spelled `__atomic` does".
      (regFn fns "__atomic_load" (mkIntArrow 1))
      (regFnEff fns "__atomic_store" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (regFnEff fns "__atomic_add" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (regFnEff fns "__atomic_cas" (mkIntArrow 3) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (regFnEff fns "__fence" (mkIntArrow 0) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      ; The six `parallel` primitives - S5 and S6 of
      ; docs/memory-model.md, lowered by `emitPrimPar` in
      ; codegen.ax. A spawn takes the thunk to run and the word to hand
      ; it and answers a handle; a join takes the handle and answers the
      ; word the thunk answered. `IO`, for the syscall primitives'
      ; reason: a fork and a thread creation reach the kernel, so a
      ; `parallel` in a body claiming `no-io` is refused exactly as a
      ; `__syscall3` there is. The thunk is spelled `(-> Int Int)` and
      ; not `Int` because it is CALLED - `__axiom_recover`'s reason
      ; above - and its answer is a WORD, which is the limit codegen's
      ; header states: that is all a join can hand back across a
      ; process or out of another thread's arena today. `__par_*` is
      ; what `(parallel ...)` desugars to (`core/parser.ax`) and
      ; follows `--threads`; the other two pairs name their lowering.
      ; Each pair has a third spelling, `__*_join_nr`: the join that
      ; does NOT re-raise a failed child, answering the thunk's word
      ; and writing the decoded wait status - 0, an exit code, or 128
      ; plus the signal - to the out-cell its second argument names,
      ; so a pool can record per-slot failure and carry on
      ; (`stdlib/Task.ax`, `taskMapWordsChecked`). Same lowering rule as
      ; its raising twin: `__par_` follows the flag, the other two
      ; name theirs.
      ;
      ; THE HANDLE IS A `Spawn`, not an `Int` (MM-PAR-8): a word the
      ; type system tracks, which only a spawn answers, only a join
      ; takes, and no concurrent binding may capture (AX3064). The word
      ; is a slot in the runtime's handle table, so a join of a handle
      ; already joined - or of one that never was a handle - traps 85
      ; before it reads the page the slot named.
      (regFnEff2 fns "__par_spawn" (mkTArr (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) (mkTArr mkIntTy mkSpawnTy))
        (builtinEff "IO") (builtinEff "Spawn"))
      (primCallsAt fns "__par_spawn" 0)
      (regFnEff2 fns "__par_join" (mkTArr mkSpawnTy mkIntTy) (builtinEff "IO") (builtinEff "Block"))
      (regFnEff2 fns "__par_join_nr" (mkTArr mkSpawnTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy)) (builtinEff "IO")
        (builtinEff "Block"))
      (regFnEff2 fns "__thread_spawn" (mkTArr (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) (mkTArr mkIntTy mkSpawnTy))
        (builtinEff "IO") (builtinEff "Spawn"))
      (primCallsAt fns "__thread_spawn" 0)
      (regFnEff2 fns "__thread_join" (mkTArr mkSpawnTy mkIntTy) (builtinEff "IO")
        (builtinEff "Block"))
      (regFnEff2 fns "__thread_join_nr" (mkTArr mkSpawnTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy))
        (builtinEff "IO") (builtinEff "Block"))
      (regFnEff2 fns "__proc_spawn" (mkTArr (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) (mkTArr mkIntTy mkSpawnTy))
        (builtinEff "IO") (builtinEff "Spawn"))
      (primCallsAt fns "__proc_spawn" 0)
      (regFnEff2 fns "__proc_join" (mkTArr mkSpawnTy mkIntTy) (builtinEff "IO")
        (builtinEff "Block"))
      (regFnEff2 fns "__proc_join_nr" (mkTArr mkSpawnTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy)) (builtinEff "IO")
        (builtinEff "Block"))
      ; `parallel`'s borrow scope (MM-PAR-6b): `(parallel$borrow 0)`
      ; answers a fresh borrow record, codegen lends it each thunk's
      ; captures, and `(parallel$return b)` restores their counts. The
      ; `$` keeps both out of a program's reach: only the desugaring in
      ; `core/parser.ax` names them, around spawns and joins that
      ; run nothing else.
      (regFnEff fns "parallel$borrow" (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) (builtinEff "IO"))
      (regFnEff fns "parallel$return" (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy) (builtinEff "IO"))
      (regFnEff fns "parallel$lends" (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkIntTy)) (builtinEff "IO"))
      ; The pid a forked binding runs as, read from the page its handle
      ; names - `stdlib/Task.ax` kills a task past its deadline with it.
      ; The handle is checked first, as a join checks it (85), and a
      ; thread's handle is another kind and traps too, so the page
      ; layout stays the runtime's and no library reads it.
      (regFn fns "__spawn_pid" (mkTArr mkSpawnTy mkIntTy))
      ; The HANDLE TABLE (MM-PAR-8, `emitHandleRuntime` in codegen.ax):
      ; `(__handle_new kind addr)` answers a fresh handle word naming
      ; `addr`, or 0 when the table is full; `(__handle_get kind h)`
      ; answers the address a live handle of that kind names;
      ; `(__handle_free kind h)` answers it and retires the handle.
      ; Either of the last two TRAPS 85 on a word that is not a live
      ; handle of that kind - freed, forged or of another kind -
      ; without reading anything the word points at. Words in, words
      ; out: a library wraps the handle in a word struct of its own
      ; (`stdlib/Chan.ax`). They take and answer addresses a caller
      ; will dereference, so all three are `Unsafe`
      ; (`isUnsafeHandlePrim`).
      (regFnEff fns "__handle_new" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (regFn fns "__handle_get" (mkIntArrow 2))
      (regFnEff fns "__handle_free" (mkIntArrow 2) (builtinEff "Mut"))
      (regFn fns "__addr" (mkTArr mkStringTy mkIntTy))
      ; Not spellable in source - `checkNumeric` rewrites `==`/`!=`
      ; into these. They are registered anyway because the rewritten
      ; node outlives the check: `axiom symbols`, the language server
      ; and any second pass over the same tree meet it, and an
      ; unregistered head is AX3001 on a program that compiles.
      (regFn fns "__streq" (mkTArr mkStringTy (mkTArr mkStringTy mkBoolTy)))
      (regFn fns "__strne" (mkTArr mkStringTy (mkTArr mkStringTy mkBoolTy)))
      (tcRegIntegerWords fns (strSplit "Int Int8 Int16 Int32 UInt8 UInt16 UInt32 UInt64" 32) 0)
      (regFn fns "__floatToBits" (mkTArr mkFloatTy mkIntTy))
      (regFn fns "__floatFromBits" (mkTArr mkIntTy mkFloatTy))
      (regFn fns "__intToFloat" (mkTArr mkIntTy mkFloatTy))
      (regFn fns "__floatToInt" (mkTArr mkFloatTy mkIntTy))
      {
        (let optRes
          (mkTCon "Option"
            {
              (let v vecNew)
              {
                (vecPush v (mkTVar "a"))
                v
              }
            }))
        (let optCtors vecNew)
        {
          (vecPush optCtors
            (CtorEnt
              :name "Some"
              :fieldTys
                {
                  (let v vecNew)
                  {
                    (vecPush v (mkTVar "a"))
                    v
                  }
                }
              :resTy optRes
              :ctorTy (mkTArr (mkTVar "a") optRes)
              :fieldNames None))
          (vecPush optCtors
            (CtorEnt :name "None" :fieldTys vecNew :resTy optRes :ctorTy optRes :fieldNames None))
          (vecPush datas (DataEnt :name "Option" :ctors optCtors :module None :arity 1))
          ; `Vec`, ABSTRACT AND PARAMETERISED. No constructors: a `Vec`
          ; is made by `vecNew` and read by `vecGet`, never matched, so
          ; there is nothing for a pattern to name. What the entry buys
          ; is the one thing `Vec` has never had - an ELEMENT TYPE - so
          ; that `vecPush`'s `a` and `vecGet`'s answer are the same `a`
          ; instead of an `a` going in and an `Int` coming out.
          ;
          ; The runtime shape does not move: a `Vec` is the handle it
          ; always was, one word, allocated by `vecNew`. This is a
          ; type-level change only, which is what makes it separable
          ; from the reference-class question - `fldClass` answers for
          ; `Vec` exactly what it answered for the `Int` it replaces.
          (vecPush datas (DataEnt :name "Vec" :ctors vecNew :module None :arity 1))
          ; `(Link T W)`, abstract like `Vec`: an index into a `(Vec T)`
          ; that a struct field stores in `W` bytes (MM-VAL-10b). The
          ; entry gives it its two parameters; `stdlib/Link.ax` makes
          ; and follows one, and every classifier answers "word".
          (vecPush datas (DataEnt :name "Link" :ctors vecNew :module None :arity 2))
        }
      }
      ; The exact-name index, before collection rather than after:
      ; collection itself looks a name up per declaration.
      ; The TYPE index FIRST, before every other table: the
      ; alias pass below is the first thing that puts anything
      ; in it, and `tyIdxPut` is a NO-OP while word 30 is still
      ; 0 - so an index built after that pass would be an index
      ; missing every alias, and missing it silently.
      {
        (let tc
          (TC
            :fns fns
            :datas datas
            :structs vecNew
            :scope vecNew
            :out vecNew
            :counter 0
            :frameBase 0
            :macros vecNew
            :effects vecNew
            :entryDecls entryDecls
            :allDecls allDecls
            :curParams vecNew
            :curCallable vecNew
            :units units
            :curUnit 0
            :ambTbl vecNew
            :curModule None
            :aliases vecNew
            :pairFns vecNew
            :privates (withInternalVisibility allDecls units (collectPrivates allDecls vecNew 0))
            :fnIdx None
            :ambIdx None
            :lamBase 0
            :curSigVars vecNew
            :curDeclNode None
            :sigTags None
            :fnSufIdx None
            :privIdx None
            :rgnFactsDone 0
            :curCalls None
            :tyIdx None
            :deprecated None
            :lamVars vecNew
            :rgnGaveUp 0
            :contractTag None
            :regions vecNew
            :pairSites None
            :floatOps vecNew
            :subtypes vecNew
            :rgnHasForm 0
            :rgnStamping 0
            :rgnRefused vecNew
            :wordLower vecNew
            :wordStructs (countWordStructs allDecls 0 0)
            :unsafe
              (UState
                :unsafeSink None
                :forgeSites None
                :forgeDecls vecNew
                :unsafePending vecNew
                :ctClaimed None
                :ctResults vecNew
                :evPending vecNew
                :eraseSites None
                :eraseCounts vecNew
                :fieldVals vecNew
                :mayCalls None
                :walkCtx (bsCtxOf vecNew vecNew 0 vecNew)
                :discard 0
                :unknownCallees vecNew
                :regionScratch None
                :fieldDecls vecNew
                :fieldDeclNames vecNew
                :fieldStoreSpans vecNew)
            :parLends vecNew
            :exprChecks
              (ExprChecks
                :formatChecked vecNew
                :computeRequests vecNew
                :computeEnabled 0
                :validatingEffects false
                :restrictionsStrict false
                :unsignedParams vecNew)))
        {
          (tcSetTyIdx tc (tyIdxNew (vecLen allDecls)))
          ; The builtin `Option` went into `datas` before this record
          ; existed, so it has no collection pass to be indexed by.
          (tyIdxSeedDatas tc (tcDatasVec tc) 0)
          (tcSetFnIdx tc (fnIdxBuildSized (tcFnsVec tc) (vecLen allDecls)))
          (tcSetAmbIdx tc (ambIdxNew (vecLen allDecls)))
          ; The suffix index, in the same place and for the same
          ; reason: a lookup that falls through the exact pass during
          ; collection would otherwise scan the whole table.
          (tcSetFnSufIdx tc (fnSufIdxBuildSized (tcFnsVec tc) (vecLen allDecls)))
          (tcSetPrivIdx tc (privIdxBuild (tcPrivates tc)))
          ; Aliases first, then record fields, then everything else.
          ; `tcCollect` precomputes each constructor's field vector AND
          ; its arrow from the field types AS WRITTEN, so an expansion
          ; that ran after it would leave those two readers holding the
          ; unexpanded spelling - which is the shape of the defect this
          ; fixes, one level up. Expanding before collection means there
          ; is no second reader to keep in step.
          (tcIndexTypeDecls tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCollectAliasDecls tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCheckAliasDecls tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCheckAliasCycles tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCheckTypeParams tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCheckDupCtors tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCollectSubtypeDecls tc allDecls 0)
          (tcExpandFieldAliases tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCollect tc allDecls 0)
          (tcCheckDeclaredTypeUses tc allDecls 0)
          ; Type constructors named in signatures, once collection has
          ; seen every `data`/`struct`/`type` - a signature may name a
          ; type declared later in the file or in another module.
          (tcCheckSigTypes tc allDecls 0)
          ; Result-only type variables, AFTER that loop rather than
          ; inside it: deciding whether one of them diverges is a
          ; question about the whole set at once, and the set is not
          ; complete until every signature has been seen.
          (tcCheckResultOnly tc allDecls)
          ; The ambiguity table, once: builtins are module-less
          ; winners and have no declaration to walk, so they are
          ; seeded rather than collected.
          (ambRecord tc "None" None)
          (ambRecord tc "Some" None)
          (ambBuild tc allDecls 0)
          ; Every function's place in the unsafe boundary, read off its
          ; tags, BEFORE the fixpoint: a trusted callee's `Unsafe` stops
          ; at it (`effUnionCallee`), so the rows need the roles to be
          ; right.
          (tcUnsafeRoles tc allDecls 0)
          ; Effects are inferred once, after every declaration is
          ; registered and before any body is checked, because an
          ; AX3011 at a handle asks what the body performs and the
          ; answer may come from a function declared later.
          (inferEffects tc allDecls)
          ; Both of these read the finished row, so they run here
          ; rather than in `tcWalkDecls`: the row is what says whether
          ; an effect reached `main` with nothing handling it, and it
          ; is not final until the fixpoint above has closed.
          (checkEffectDeclAxtags tc allDecls 0)
          (checkStrayAxtags tc allDecls 0)
          (checkUnhandledAtMain tc allDecls)
          tc
        }
      }
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Collection: one pass over the RESOLVED declaration list, in order,
; mirroring stage0's collect_declarations. Keying is exact-name -
; imported names arrive pre-mangled to `Mod$name`, which is stage0's
; (name, module) key spelled as one string. A later signature for the
; same name overwrites (stage0 1905).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; MM-LIFE-2d's capacity cliff, constructor half: a cell's payload is
; the tag word plus the fields, the shape word's reference map covers
; payload words 0..46 (bit 63 is the i64 sign bit, reserved by
; construction), so a constructor may declare at most 46 fields.
; Refusing at the declaration is the whole point - a map that
; silently dropped a field would free what it named and leak what it
; could not, which is the quiet wrongness a diagnostic exists to
; prevent.
(pub :: checkCtorWidths (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (checkCtorWidths tc ctors)
  {
    (for i in 0..(vecLen ctors)
      {
        (let c (vecGet ctors i))
        (if (> (nodeB c) 46)
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3029" "record-bitmap-capacity" (nodeSpan c)
                (concat (concat (concat "constructor `" (as (nodeA c) String)) "` has ")
                  (concat (fmtInt (nodeB c))
                    " fields, and a cell's reference map covers 46 beside the tag word"))
                None
                (Some
                  "split the declaration: a nested struct or constructor cell is ONE word of the outer block, and every block carries its own map")
                "the shape word packs one bit per payload word - is this word a reference the release path must follow - beside the form bit and the word count; bits 16..62 give 47 mappable words, the tag takes the first, and the widest declaration in this repository is the compiler's own 37-field CG record (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d)")
              "too wide for one reference map")))
      })
    0
  })

(pub :: evSigVarCount (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (evSigVarCount t)
  (if (== (tyHasSrcVar t) 0)
    0
    {
      (let acc vecNew)
      {
        (sigTyVarsInto t acc)
        (vecLen acc)
      }
    }))

(pub :: evCapDiag (-> TC Int String Int Int))
(pub fn (evCapDiag tc span what n)
  (emitDiag tc
    (diagSetLabel
      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3030" "evidence-word-capacity" span
        (concat (concat (concat "this " what) " declares ")
          (concat (fmtInt n) " type variables, and the evidence word carries 64"))
        None
        (Some
          "split the declaration, or make some positions concrete - each variable needs one bit")
        "a symbolic witness reads its bit with a shift by the variable's index, and a shift of 64 or more is poison in the emitted LLVM - an arbitrary answer at run time, which under reference maps is a wrong free; the widest signature in this repository declares 4")
      "too many type variables for one word")))

; An effect operation's declared type must stay variable-free: the
; handler protocol carries no evidence word (EffEnt has no types at
; emission), so a variable there would be silently unevidenced -
; refused instead, MM-LIFE-2d. AX3017 because the shape is
; unsupported, not wrong.
(pub :: checkEffectOpTyvars (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkEffectOpTyvars tc fields)
  (if (== (as fields Int) 0)
    0
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen fields)
        {
          ; the operation's declared TYPE rides in slot b of its
          ; TAG_FIELD node - the same slot tcCollectEffectOps
          ; registers from
          (let f (vecGet fields i))
          (if (== (tyHasSrcVar (nodeBChild f)) 1)
            (emitDiag tc
              (diagSetLabel
                (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3017" "effect-handler-unsupported" (nodeSpan f)
                  "an effect operation's type cannot mention a type variable" None
                  (Some "give the operation a concrete type")
                  "a handler is installed and called through a protocol that carries no pointerhood evidence, so a variable here would be invisible to the reference maps (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d)")
                "no evidence crosses a handler")))
        })
      0
    }))

(pub :: tcCollect (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (tcCollect tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    ; An UNEXPANDED declaration-position `syntax/for`. The
    ; main pipelines never deliver one - the expander refuses
    ; it and re-tags it TAG_NIL before the checker runs - but
    ; the REPL accumulates declaration lines lazily and types
    ; the next expression WITHOUT expansion, so the tag can
    ; arrive here. Refuse it the way the expander would (and
    ; the way checkArms refuses an unexpanded arm), rather
    ; than silently skipping a declaration the user typed.
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (let tag (nodeTag d))
        {
          (if (== tag TAG_D_SYNFOR)
            (emitDiag tc
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3028" "syntax-query" (nodeSpan d)
                "syntax/for stands in a declaration macro's template, not at top level" None
                (Some
                  "iteration is an expansion-time facility: move these declarations into a rule-form macro's template, or write them by hand")
                "the query vocabulary is CLOSED and compiler-implemented (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5/6): every query is answered from the declaration list at expansion time"))
            (if (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
              {
                (checkCtorWidths tc (nodeBChildren d))
                (if (if (== (nodeTy d) 0)
                  false
                  (> (vecLen (nodeTyNames d)) 64))
                  (evCapDiag tc (nodeSpan d) "data type" (vecLen (nodeTyNames d))))
                (tcCollectData tc d)
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_D_STRUCT)
                ; MM-LIFE-2d's capacity cliff, struct half: the
                ; shape word maps payload words 0..46 (bit 63 is
                ; the sign bit, reserved), so a struct block wider
                ; than 47 fields cannot describe itself to the
                ; release path. Refused here, at declaration, not
                ; truncated at run time - a map that dropped its
                ; 48th field would free everything it named and
                ; leak the one it could not.
                {
                  (if (> (vecLen (nodeBChildren d)) 47)
                    (emitDiag tc
                      (diagSetLabel
                        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3029" "record-bitmap-capacity" (nodeSpan d)
                          (concat (concat (concat "struct `" (as (nodeA d) String)) "` has ")
                            (concat (fmtInt (vecLen (nodeBChildren d)))
                              " fields, and a block's reference map covers 47"))
                          None
                          (Some
                            "split the declaration: a nested struct or constructor cell is ONE word of the outer block, and every block carries its own map")
                          "the shape word packs one bit per payload word - is this word a reference the release path must follow - beside the form bit and the word count; bits 16..62 give 47 mappable words, and the widest declaration in this repository is the compiler's own 37-field CG record (docs/memory-model.md MM-LIFE-2d)")
                        "too wide for one reference map")))
                  ; A field with no readable type: AX3056, before the
                  ; struct registers. `fldClass` cannot classify an
                  ; empty type variable, so such a field is dropped
                  ; from the block's reference map and from event 5's
                  ; store retain - a use-after-free out of a program
                  ; `check` accepted (exit 139).
                  (checkStructFieldTyped tc (nodeBChildren d) (nodeAName d))
                  ; The representation markers, AX3084: what `word`
                  ; and `shared` require of the declaration.
                  (checkStructMarkers tc d)
                  {
                    (let sent
                      (StructEnt
                        :name (nodeAName d)
                        :fields (nodeStructFields d)
                        :module (nodeModuleOpt d)
                        :tyvars (nodeTyNames d)
                        :repr (structReprOf d)))
                    {
                      (vecPush (tcStructsVec tc) sent)
                      (tyIdxPut tc (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d) (TyStruct sent))
                    }
                  }
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_D_SIG)
                  ; MM-LIFE-2d's OTHER capacity, and this one is
                  ; soundness rather than honesty: a symbolic
                  ; witness emits `lshr %__evw.h, k`, and a shift of
                  ; 64 or more is LLVM poison - an arbitrary bit at
                  ; run time, which is a wrong free. Refused on the
                  ; MERGED list, because an imported signature's
                  ; bits are read too. Measured max today: 4.
                  {
                    (if (> (evSigVarCount (nodeTyChild d)) 64)
                      (evCapDiag tc (nodeSpan d) "signature" (evSigVarCount (nodeTyChild d))))
                    (if (! (sigRepeated tc decls i d))
                      (tcCollectSig tc d))
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_D_FN)
                    (tcCollectFn tc d)
                    (if (== tag TAG_D_EFFECT)
                      ; The whole declaration node, not just its name:
                      ; AX3017 reports how many operations an effect
                      ; declares, and that count is `(vecLen (nodeB d))`.
                      {
                        (vecPush (tcEffectsVec tc) d)
                        ; MM-LIFE-2d: an effect operation whose declared
                        ; type mentions a variable would need evidence the
                        ; handler protocol does not carry (EffEnt has no
                        ; types by emission); refused rather than silently
                        ; unevidenced. Population today: zero.
                        (checkEffectOpTyvars tc (nodeCChildren d))
                        ; An operation with no declared type: AX3055,
                        ; before it registers (as poison) below.
                        (checkEffectOpTyped tc (nodeCChildren d) (nodeAName d))
                        ; And the effect's own name: AX3054 when it is
                        ; one a handle list would resolve to a built-in.
                        (checkEffectNameReserved tc d)
                        ; Slot c, not slot b: the TYPES the parser kept
                        ; beside the names, so an operation is a callable
                        ; with a real arrow and a real arity. It used to
                        ; take slot b - the bare names - and register a
                        ; silent wildcard at arity -1, which made
                        ; `(two 3 4 5)` check clean and SIGSEGV and
                        ; `(two 3)` check clean and answer a heap address.
                        (tcCollectEffectOps tc (nodeCChildren d) 0 (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d))
                      }
                      (if (== tag TAG_D_EXTERN)
                        ; Slot c, the TYPED items - the same choice the effect
                        ; arm above records: slot b is the bare names, and
                        ; registering those would mean a silent wildcard at
                        ; arity -1 and no saturation checking at all.
                        (tcCollectExternItems tc (nodeCChildren d) 0 (nodeModuleOpt d))
                        (if (== tag TAG_D_MACRO)
                          ; The NODE, not the name: a macro its module does not
                          ; export is not a macro name HERE, and deciding that
                          ; needs its module and its visibility flag. Storing
                          ; only the string made a private macro's invocation
                          ; type-check as a macro (silent wildcard), reach
                          ; codegen as a call to a symbol nothing defines, and
                          ; fail in `opt` at exit 4 - the link failure this
                          ; repository twice records as the wrong trade.
                          (vecPush (tcMacrosVec tc) d)
                          ()))))))))
          ; `TAG_D_ALIAS` is collected by
          ; `tcCollectAliasDecls`, which runs before this
          ; pass so record fields can be expanded before
          ; anything reads one.
          (tcCollect tc decls (+ i 1))
        }
      }
    }))

; Every operation of `(effect E ...)` carries `Custom(E)` as its
; INHERENT effect - stage0 attaches it at registration so the
; collector needs no special case for operations at all.
; An operation of an IMPORTED effect is registered twice: under its
; bare name and under `Mod$op`. stage1 resolves a qualified reference
; by mangling it into a flat name, which works for every other kind of
; declaration because `mangleDecl` rewrote the declaration to match -
; and an `effect` declaration is one of the two it does not rewrite.
; So `(EffMod::ask 21)`, a program stage0 compiles and runs, drew a
; false `AX3001 undefined variable EffMod$ask`. Registering the
; mangled spelling costs one entry and no rule: every lookup that goes
; by name now finds the operation under either spelling, with the same
; silent-wildcard type and the same inherent `Custom(E)`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; An operation must DECLARE its type. Two checks stand on that arrow
; and on nothing else: the handler a `handle` installs is held to it
; (`checkHandler`, AX3004), and a call to the operation is held to its
; arity. A field the parser left at 0 - no `::`, or a type it could
; not read - gave both nothing to stand on: the operation registered
; as a silent wildcard at arity -1, so `(op 1 2 3)` on a one-argument
; handler checked clean and, at run time, applied the handler's RESULT
; as a function (exit 139, SIGSEGV). AX3055, an ERROR, at the
; operation's name; `tcAddEffectOp` then registers the operation as
; poison rather than a wildcard, so its uses stay quiet behind this
; one report. Population in tree: 0 untyped operations (the syntax zoo
; types all three of its own).
(pub :: checkEffectOpTyped (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) String Int))
(pub fn (checkEffectOpTyped tc fields effName)
  (if (== (as fields Int) 0)
    0
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen fields)
        {
          (let f (vecGet fields i))
          (if (&& (== (nodeB f) 0) (!= (nodeSpan f) 0))
            (emitDiag tc
              (diagSetLabel
                (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3055" "effect-op-untyped" (nodeSpan f)
                  (concat
                    (concat (concat (concat "operation `" (nodeAName f)) "` of effect `") effName)
                    "` declares no type; a handler is checked against it and a call is checked against its arity")
                  None (Some (concat (concat "write `(" (nodeAName f)) " :: (-> Arg Result))`")))
                "no `::` type after the operation's name")))
        })
      0
    }))

; A struct field must DECLARE its type, and the reason is the same one
; AX3055 gives for an operation's arrow: a declaration nothing stands
; on is not a weaker check, it is no check. `(struct Box (msg String))`
; omits the `:` that makes the form a declaration at all
; (`tree-sitter-axiom/grammar.js`: "a distinction that only becomes
; visible at the `:`"), and `parseOneFieldNamed` answers a field whose
; type is `(mkTVar "")` with the written type skipped. Nothing then
; refuses it: `fldClass` cannot classify a variable, so the field is
; left OUT of the block's reference map and out of `MM-LIFE-2c` event
; 5's store retain, and an owned value stored into it is released by
; its own owner while the field still points at the block.
;
; Measured before this code existed, on a compiler built from the
; parent, with `(struct Box (msg String))` and a callee storing its
; argument into `b.msg`: `check` reported nothing, the field read back
; 38 NUL bytes where the message was, and the program exited 139 -
; SIGSEGV. The same spelled `(msg : String)` is correct. Three shapes
; reach the empty variable and all three ran 139: no `:` at all, a `:`
; whose type the parser could not read (`(msg : 5)`), and a bare
; `(msg)`. A wildcard `(msg : _)` is a different node and is already
; refused at its use sites, so it is not this code's business.
;
; AX3056, an ERROR, at the field's name, once per field. Population in
; tree: 0 - every struct in `stdlib/`, `core/`, `tests/` and
; `examples/` spells the colon, which is why this went unreported.
(pub :: checkStructFieldTyped (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) String Int))
(pub fn (checkStructFieldTyped tc fields structName)
  (if (== (as fields Int) 0)
    0
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen fields)
        {
          (let f (vecGet fields i))
          (if (&& (== (fieldTyIsUnread f) 1) (!= (nodeSpan f) 0))
            (emitDiag tc
              (diagSetLabel
                (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3056" "struct-field-untyped" (nodeSpan f)
                  (concat
                    (concat (concat (concat "field `" (nodeAName f)) "` of struct `") structName)
                    "` declares no type; a field's type is what decides whether the release path follows it")
                  None
                  (Some
                    (concat (concat "write `(" (nodeAName f))
                      " : Type)` - the `:` is what makes this a field declaration")))
                "no `:` type after the field's name")))
        })
      0
    }))

; The field type the parser could not read, in either of the two ways
; it fails to: no node at all, or the empty type variable
; `typeNodeOrWildcard` answers for a missing `:` and for a `:` whose
; type is not a type. A NAMED variable CAN arrive here since
; 2026-08-30, when structs gained type parameters: `(struct Box (a)
; (v : a))` declares a field whose type is the variable `a`, and that
; reads perfectly well. The empty name is still the whole test, and
; always was - this comment asserted a reason the code never rested
; on.
(pub :: fieldTyIsUnread (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (fieldTyIsUnread f)
  {
    (let t (nodeTyChild f))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      1
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR) (== (strLen (nodeAName t)) 0))
        1
        0))
  })

; `fields` is the effect declaration's `opFields` - TAG_FIELD nodes
; carrying the operation's NAME and its DECLARED TYPE. The parser has
; kept both since effects landed, and `symbols` walks exactly this
; vector to print an operation's real arrow; this collector took the
; parallel name-only vector and registered a silent wildcard with
; arity -1 instead. One site discarding a type the parser already
; kept, which is the same sentence the self-hosting record writes
; about `parseSigDecl`.
(pub :: tcCollectEffectOps (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int String (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectEffectOps tc fields i effName modName)
  (if (== (as fields Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
      0
      {
        (let f (vecGet fields i))
        {
          (tcAddEffectOp tc (nodeAName f) (nodeBChild f) effName)
          (if (let (Some moduleName) modName)
            ; `concat` twice, from `Str`: nothing from codegen.ax
            ; is imported here - stage0's flat
            ; namespace resolves it across the merged declaration list
            ; and stage1 refuses it, which is the open B4 item and the
            ; stricter of the two answers.
            (tcAddEffectOp tc (concat (concat moduleName "$") (nodeAName f)) (nodeBChild f)
              effName))
          (tcCollectEffectOps tc fields (+ i 1) effName modName)
        }
      })))

; An operation's arity is its declared arrow's depth. `->` right-folds,
; so `(-> Int Int Int)` IS `(-> Int (-> Int Int))` and the depth is the
; only reading available - but for an OPERATION it is also the right
; one, because the lowering applies the handler to the operation's
; arguments and there is no partial application to preserve.
;
; A type the parser could not read leaves 0 in the field. That field
; has already drawn AX3055 (`checkEffectOpTyped`, in the same collect
; arm), so the operation registers as POISON with unknown arity: `tyCompat`
; matches poison with anything and `repArity` answers `None`, and every
; use of the operation is quiet behind the one report at its
; declaration. It used to register as a silent WILDCARD at arity -1,
; which is the same silence with no report behind it - `(effect E
; (op))` checked clean and `(op 1 2 3)` SIGSEGVed.
(pub :: tcAddEffectOp (-> TC String ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (tcAddEffectOp tc name ty effName)
  {
    (tcPushFn tc
      (FnEnt
        :name name
        :ty
          (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
            mkTErr
            ty)
        :paramCount
          (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
            None
            (Some (arrowArity ty)))
        :isBuiltin 0
        :isEffectOp 1
        :effects
          {
            (let v vecNew)
            {
              (vecPushStr v (customEff effName))
              v
            }
          }
        :eparams vecNew
        :calls vecNew
        :rgn None
        :unsafe 0
        :uses (vecWithCapacity 1)
        :mayCalls (vecWithCapacity 1)
        :pending 0
        :scoped vecNew
        :compute vecNew
        :restrictions vecNew
        :lambdaCast NoLambdaCast))
    0
  })

; An `extern` item. Modelled on `tcAddEffectOp` above, and for the
; same reason: an operation's FnEnt arrives with its effects already
; SEEDED, so the collector needs no special case and the existing
; monotone fixpoint in `inferEffects` propagates them transitively for
; free.
;
; Like a syscall, a foreign call seeds IO and Unsafe: the foreign
; implementation may read, write or free the addresses it receives.
; `isSyscallPrim` stays a literal
; seven-way match on `__syscall0`..`__syscall6`; neither effect walk
; learns a new shape; nothing else moves.
;
; `paramCount` retains the declared arrow's real depth. An unknown
; non-builtin arity makes `repArity` answer `None`, which disables both the AX3013
; bare-value refusal and the AX3009/AX3013 saturation check. An extern
; therefore gets arity checking that `foreign` never had.
(pub :: tcAddExtern (-> TC String ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcAddExtern tc name ty)
  {
    (tcPushFn
      tc
      (FnEnt
        :name name
        :ty
          (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
            mkSilentWild
            ty)
        :paramCount
          (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
            None
            (Some (arrowArity ty)))
        :isBuiltin 0
        :isEffectOp 2
        :effects
          {
            (let v vecNew)
            {
              ; word 4 is 2, not 0: an extern is neither an
              ; ordinary function nor an effect operation, and
              ; `symbols` has to tell it from both. Its two
              ; readers compare `== 1` (checkBareValue here,
              ; the isOp test in symbols.ax), so 2 is inert to
              ; each - and right for the first, since a nullary
              ; extern IS a legitimate bare value.
              (vecPushStr v (builtinEff "IO"))
              (vecPushStr v (builtinEff "Unsafe"))
              v
            }
          }
        :eparams vecNew
        :calls vecNew
        :rgn None
        :unsafe 0
        :uses (vecWithCapacity 1)
        :mayCalls (vecWithCapacity 1)
        :pending 0
        :scoped vecNew
        :compute vecNew
        :restrictions vecNew
        :lambdaCast NoLambdaCast))
    0
  })

; An imported item has one checker identity, `Mod$name`, like an
; ordinary imported function. Bare references resolve through the module
; view. Registering a second bare entry loses declaration provenance
; when several modules export the same name.
(pub :: tcCollectExternItems (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectExternItems tc fields i modName)
  (if (== (as fields Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
      0
      {
        (let f (vecGet fields i))
        (tcAddExtern tc (privKey modName (nodeAName f)) (nodeBChild f))
        (tcCollectExternItems tc fields (+ i 1) modName)
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Effects, as tagged strings. `effEq` is plain string equality, which
; is why the tag has to be part of the string rather than a separate
; word: these live in Vecs and are compared by value.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: builtinEff (-> String String))
(pub fn (builtinEff n)
  (concat "b:" n))

(pub :: customEff (-> String String))
(pub fn (customEff n)
  (concat "c:" n))

; The name stage0's `Display for Effect` prints, which is what an
; AX3011 message quotes: the tag's two bytes removed - and the `~` of
; a possible spelling before them, because a name is a name whichever
; way the effect arrived (`effIsPossible`).
(pub :: effRender (-> String String))
(pub fn (effRender e)
  {
    (let d (effDefiniteOf e))
    (strSlice d 2 (- (strLen d) 2))
  })

(pub :: effIn (-> Strings String Int))
(pub fn (effIn v e)
  (strInVec v e 0))

; Union into `acc`, answering 1 when anything was added - the
; fixpoint's "changed" signal.
(pub :: effUnion (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effUnion acc src i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    0
    {
      (let rest (effUnion acc src (+ i 1)))
      (if (== (effIn acc (vecGetStr src i)) 1)
        rest
        {
          (vecPush acc (vecGet src i))
          1
        })
    }))

(pub :: effAdd (-> Strings String Int))
(pub fn (effAdd acc e)
  (if (== (effIn acc e) 1)
    0
    {
      (vecPushStr acc e)
      1
    }))

(pub :: tcCollectData (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectData tc d)
  {
    (let tvs (nodeTyNames d))
    {
      (let tvNodes vecNew)
      {
        (tvNamesToNodes tvs tvNodes 0)
        {
          (let resTy (mkTCon (nominalName (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d)) tvNodes))
          (let ctors vecNew)
          {
            (tcCollectCtors (nodeBChildren d) resTy ctors 0)
            {
              (let dent
                (DataEnt
                  :name (nodeAName d)
                  :ctors ctors
                  :module (nodeModuleOpt d)
                  :arity
                    (if (== (as tvs Int) 0)
                      0
                      (vecLen tvs))))
              {
                (vecPush (tcDatasVec tc) dent)
                (tyIdxAddData tc dent)
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: tvNamesToNodes (-> Names Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (tvNamesToNodes tvs acc i)
  (if (== (as tvs Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen tvs))
      0
      {
        (vecPush acc (mkTVar (vecGetStr tvs i)))
        (tvNamesToNodes tvs acc (+ i 1))
      })))

; A constructor's type is its fields curried onto the APPLIED data
; type - `Nil : List a`, never bare `List` (stage0 registers nullary
; constructors with the type arguments too, a recorded old bug).
(pub :: tcCollectCtors (-> Nodes ASTNode (Vec CtorEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectCtors ctorNodes resTy acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctorNodes))
    0
    {
      (let c (vecGet ctorNodes i))
      {
        (let fieldTys
          (if (== (nodeTy c) 0)
            vecNew
            (nodeTyChildren c)))
        {
          (vecPush acc
            (CtorEnt
              :name (nodeAName c)
              :fieldTys fieldTys
              :resTy resTy
              :ctorTy (ctorArrowOf fieldTys resTy (- (vecLen fieldTys) 1))
              :fieldNames (nodeFieldNamesOpt c)))
          (tcCollectCtors ctorNodes resTy acc (+ i 1))
        }
      }
    }))

(pub :: ctorArrowOf (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (ctorArrowOf fieldTys acc i)
  (if (< i 0)
    acc
    (ctorArrowOf fieldTys (mkTArr (vecGet fieldTys i) acc) (- i 1))))

(pub :: tcCollectSig (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectSig tc d)
  {
    (let ty
      (if (== (nodeTy d) 0)
        mkSilentWild
        (nodeTyChild d)))
    {
      (let entFound (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        (fnEntSetTy ent ty)
        {
          (tcPushFn tc
            (FnEnt
              :name (nodeAName d)
              :ty ty
              :paramCount None
              :isBuiltin 0
              :isEffectOp 0
              :effects vecNew
              :eparams vecNew
              :calls vecNew
              :rgn None
              :unsafe 0
              :uses (vecWithCapacity 1)
              :mayCalls (vecWithCapacity 1)
              :pending 0
              :scoped vecNew
              :compute vecNew
              :restrictions vecNew
              :lambdaCast NoLambdaCast))
          0
        })
    }
  })

; Whether declaration `i` is a second signature for its name in its
; module. Such a signature is AX3006, and the first one stays the
; function's type, so its mistakes don't add errors of their own. The
; scan runs only when the name already has a typed entry: a definition
; collected first leaves a `_fn_` placeholder, which a signature
; replaces, and most names meet their signature first.
(pub :: sigRepeated (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (sigRepeated tc decls i d)
  {
    (let entFound (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
    (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
      {
        (let ty (fnEntTy ent))
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_VAR) (strStartsWith (nodeAName ty) "_fn_"))
          false
          (sigEarlier decls i (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d) 0))
      }
      false)
  })

(pub :: sigEarlier (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int String (Option String) Int Bool))
(pub fn (sigEarlier decls i name m j)
  (if (>= j i)
    false
    {
      (let e (vecGet decls j))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag e) TAG_D_SIG)
            (&& (== (typeModuleSame (nodeModuleOpt e) m) 1) (strEq (nodeAName e) name)))
        true
        (sigEarlier decls i name m (+ j 1)))
    }))

; Every push to `fns` goes through here, so the exact-name index cannot
; fall behind the table it indexes - including if a later pass ever
; pushes after `checkModuleTc` has built it, which nothing does today
; and which would otherwise be a silently wrong lookup rather than a
; slow one.
(pub :: tcPushFn (-> TC FnEnt FnEnt))
(pub fn (tcPushFn tc ent)
  {
    (vecPush (tcFnsVec tc) ent)
    (fnIdxNote tc ent)
    ent
  })

(pub :: tcCollectFn (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectFn tc d)
  {
    (let ent0Found (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
    {
      (let ent
        (if (let (Some ent0) ent0Found)
          ent0
          {
            (let ph (mkTVar (concat "_fn_" (fmtInt (tcCounter tc)))))
            {
              (tcBumpCounter tc)
              (tcPushFn tc
                (FnEnt
                  :name (nodeAName d)
                  :ty ph
                  :paramCount None
                  :isBuiltin 0
                  :isEffectOp 0
                  :effects vecNew
                  :eparams vecNew
                  :calls vecNew
                  :rgn None
                  :unsafe 0
                  :uses (vecWithCapacity 1)
                  :mayCalls (vecWithCapacity 1)
                  :pending 0
                  :scoped vecNew
                  :compute vecNew
                  :restrictions vecNew
                  :lambdaCast NoLambdaCast))
            }
          }))
      (fnEntSetParamCount ent (Some (vecLen (nodeBNames d))))
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Lookups. Exact name first over the whole table, then the `Mod$name`
; suffix pass - which reproduces stage0's entry-first (module-less
; first) resolution, because entry declarations keep bare names and a
; bare reference matches them exactly while an imported one only
; matches through its suffix.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: findFnEntExact (-> (Vec FnEnt) String Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEntExact fns name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fns))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet fns i))
      (if (strEq e.name name)
        (Some e)
        (findFnEntExact fns name (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: findFnEntSuffix (-> (Vec FnEnt) String Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEntSuffix fns name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fns))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet fns i))
      (if (== (nameMatches e.name name) 1)
        (Some e)
        (findFnEntSuffix fns name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Visibility.
;
; A declaration that is not exported is still IN the program - its own
; module's bodies call it - so every lookup that can be reached from
; outside that module has to refuse it, and these are the predicates
; they share. Privacy used to be enforced by the declaration's
; ABSENCE, which is why it took the module's own calls down with it.
;
; It is recorded in ONE place, `tc`'s word 19: the mangled `Mod$name`
; of every carried-but-not-exported declaration. Not on the entries -
; a field on `FnEnt` costs a word on all ~1,600 of them, in the table
; `findFnEnt` scans linearly and where a self-check spends most of its
; time, and it measured 1.30s -> 1.38s. This Vec is EMPTY for every
; program in this repository and for every program that declares
; nothing private - answered as 0 rather than as an empty Vec, so
; every lookup's guard is one word compare against a constant.
;
; `data` and `struct` names are not rewritten to `Mod$name` - only
; `fn` and `::` are - so their key is built from the module recorded
; beside them.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: privKey (-> (Option String) String String))
(pub fn (privKey declMod name)
  (if (let (Some nameOfModule) declMod)
    (concat (concat nameOfModule "$") name)
    name))

; 1 when a reference made from `curMod` may NOT name `key`.
;
; Asked of a SET, not of the Vec. `strInVec` walked all 307 private
; names for every candidate a lookup considered, and with the table
; scans indexed that walk became the largest single cost left in
; `check core/main.ax` at 11.2% of the profile. Word 27 is the
; same bucket shape the other indexes use, over the same strings, and
; `privates` at word 19 is still the Vec because `emitUndefinedVar`
; reads it to tell "no such name" from "private" - a cold path that a
; set would only complicate.
;
; The ownership test builds no string either. It ran for every
; reference a module makes to its OWN private names, which for a
; module of private helpers is most of them, and
; `(concat curMod "$")` allocated a prefix per call on an allocator
; that never frees.
(pub :: privBlocks (-> (Option StringRows) String (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (privBlocks privs key curMod)
  (if (let (Some rows) privs)
    (if (== (privIdxHas rows key) 0)
      0
      (if (let (Some moduleName) curMod)
        (if (== (privOwnedBy key moduleName) 1)
          0
          (if (privLibraryReach rows key moduleName)
            0
            1))
        (if (privLibraryReach rows key "")
          0
          1)))
    0))

; A public name of an `Internal` module, reached from another module of
; the standard library: the library shares it (AX5002). Asked only once
; a key is known to be private and not owned, so the two lookups below
; never run on an ordinary reference.
(pub :: privLibraryReach (-> StringRows String String Bool))
(pub fn (privLibraryReach privs key curMod)
  (&& (== (privIdxHas privs (concat "internal " key)) 1)
    (== (privIdxHas privs (concat "library " curMod)) 1)))

; Is `key` spelled `m$...`? Answered in place, without building `m$`.
(pub :: privOwnedBy (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (privOwnedBy key m)
  (if (>= (strLen m) (strLen key))
    0
    (if (== (strByte key (strLen m)) 36)
      (strEqAt key 0 m 0)
      0)))

; Does `s` appear in `e` starting at `off`? The one byte-compare these
; allocation-free name tests share.
(pub :: strEqAt (-> String Int String Int Int))
(pub fn (strEqAt e off s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    1
    (if (== (strByte e (+ off i)) (strByte s i))
      (strEqAt e off s (+ i 1))
      0)))

; The private-name set. 0 when nothing is private, so every guard that
; compares word 19 against 0 reads the same of word 27.
(pub :: privSetNew (-> Int StringRows))
(pub fn (privSetNew n)
  {
    (let nb (fnIdxBuckets n))
    (let bs vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..nb
        (vecPush bs vecNew))
      bs
    }
  })

(pub :: privIdxBuild (-> (Option Names) (Option StringRows)))
(pub fn (privIdxBuild privs)
  (if (let (Some names) privs)
    {
      (let bs (privSetNew (vecLen names)))
      (for name in names
        (defIdxPut bs name))
      (Some bs)
    }
    None))

(pub :: privIdxHas (-> StringRows String Int))
(pub fn (privIdxHas bs key)
  (defIdxHas (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash key) (vecLen bs))) key 0))

(pub :: findFnEntVisibleExact
  (-> (Option StringRows) (Vec FnEnt) String (Option String) (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEntVisibleExact privs fns name curMod)
  (findFnEntVisibleExactFrom privs fns name curMod 0))

(pub :: findFnEntVisibleExactFrom
  (-> (Option StringRows) (Vec FnEnt) String (Option String) Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEntVisibleExactFrom privs fns name curMod i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fns))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet fns i))
      (if (&& (strEq e.name name) (== (privBlocks privs e.name curMod) 0))
        (Some e)
        (findFnEntVisibleExactFrom privs fns name curMod (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: findFnEntVisibleSuffix
  (-> (Option StringRows) (Vec FnEnt) String (Option String) (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEntVisibleSuffix privs fns name curMod)
  (findFnEntVisibleSuffixFrom privs fns name curMod 0))

(pub :: findFnEntVisibleSuffixFrom
  (-> (Option StringRows) (Vec FnEnt) String (Option String) Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEntVisibleSuffixFrom privs fns name curMod i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fns))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet fns i))
      (if (&& (== (nameMatches e.name name) 1) (== (privBlocks privs e.name curMod) 0))
        (Some e)
        (findFnEntVisibleSuffixFrom privs fns name curMod (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The module-local step needs no visibility test - it asks for
; `Mod$name` with `Mod` the module being checked, so anything it finds
; is that module's own. Both steps after it can reach another module's
; declaration, and when anything in the program is private both are
; filtered.
;
; Getting this wrong is not a missing diagnostic, it is a link
; failure: `mangledFor` holds no bare entry for a private name, so a
; reference the checker lets through is emitted verbatim and `opt`
; reports `use of undefined value` against generated code, at exit 4,
; blaming the toolchain. The clean AX3023 is this function's job.
(pub :: findFnEnt (-> TC String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEnt tc name)
  (resolveSignatureEntry tc (findFnEntDeclared tc name)))

; A signature without a local definition describes an imported function.
; It must resolve to that function's entry, including its effects, arity,
; ownership facts and unsafe role, rather than an empty signature entry.
(:: signatureEntryOnly (-> FnEnt Bool))
(fn (signatureEntryOnly ent)
  (&& (isNone ent.paramCount) (&& (== ent.isBuiltin 0) (== ent.isEffectOp 0))))

(:: resolveSignatureEntry (-> TC (Option FnEnt) (Option FnEnt)))
(fn (resolveSignatureEntry tc ent)
  (if (let (Some candidate) ent)
    (if (signatureEntryOnly candidate)
      {
        (let name (bareOf candidate.name))
        (let bucket
          (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnSufIdx tc))
            (vecGet buckets (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen buckets)))
            (tcFnsVec tc)))
        (Some (signatureDefinitionIn tc bucket name 0 candidate))
      }
      (Some candidate))
    None))

(:: signatureDefinitionIn (-> TC (Vec FnEnt) String Int FnEnt FnEnt))
(fn (signatureDefinitionIn tc entries name i fallback)
  (if (>= i (vecLen entries))
    fallback
    {
      (let candidate (vecGet entries i))
      (if (&& (! (signatureEntryOnly candidate))
            (&& (== (nameMatches candidate.name name) 1)
              (&& (== (fnEntOutOfModuleScope tc (tcCurModule tc) candidate.name candidate) 0)
                (== (privBlocks (tcPrivIdx tc) candidate.name (tcCurModule tc)) 0))))
        candidate
        (signatureDefinitionIn tc entries name (+ i 1) fallback))
    }))

(:: findFnEntDeclared (-> TC String (Option FnEnt)))
(fn (findFnEntDeclared tc name)
  {
    (let m (tcCurModule tc))
    (let local
      (if (let (Some localModule) m)
        (fnEntQual tc localModule name)
        None))
    (if (let (Some entry) local)
        (Some entry)
      (isNone (tcPrivates tc))
        (if (let (Some entry) (fnEntExact tc name))
          (if (== (fnEntOutOfModuleScope tc m name entry) 0)
            (Some entry)
            (fnEntModuleSuffix tc name))
          (fnEntSuffix tc name))
      (if (let (Some entry) (fnEntVisibleExact tc (tcPrivIdx tc) name (tcCurModule tc)))
        (if (== (fnEntOutOfModuleScope tc m name entry) 0)
          (Some entry)
          (fnEntModuleSuffix tc name))
        (fnEntVisibleSuffix tc (tcPrivIdx tc) name (tcCurModule tc))))
  })

; 1 when `e`, the exact match for a BARE reference made inside module
; `m`, may be the entry file's declaration, which no module can see
; (AN-52). A module can't import the entry file, so an entry-file
; `strLen` must not answer `IO`'s call to `Str`'s: the lookup then
; asks the suffix pass for a visible module's `Mod$name`. A missing
; imported definition stays unresolved. Builtins and effect operations
; remain globally visible. Neither can be the
; entry file's, and a module's `extern` item or effect operation is
; also registered as `Mod$name`, so the suffix pass answers the same
; declaration for them. `mangledForCg` makes the same choice, through
; the module view `mangleRecordModuleView` records.
(pub :: fnEntOutOfModuleScope (-> TC (Option String) String FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnEntOutOfModuleScope tc m name e)
  (if (isNone m)
    0
    (if (!= (fnEntIsBuiltin e) 0)
      0
      (if (== (fnEntIsEffectOp e) 1)
        0
        (if (== (nameHasDollar name 0) 1)
          0
          1)))))

; Search only legitimate module candidates after excluding an exact
; entry-file match. The ordinary suffix index also contains exact keys.
(pub :: fnEntModuleSuffix (-> TC String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnEntModuleSuffix tc name)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnSufIdx tc))
    (fnEntModuleSuffixIn tc (vecGet buckets (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen buckets))) name 0)
    (fnEntModuleSuffixIn tc (tcFnsVec tc) name 0)))

(pub :: fnEntModuleSuffixIn (-> TC (Vec FnEnt) String Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnEntModuleSuffixIn tc fns name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fns))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet fns i))
      (if (&& (== (nameMatches e.name name) 1)
            (&& (== (fnEntOutOfModuleScope tc (tcCurModule tc) e.name e) 0)
              (== (privBlocks (tcPrivIdx tc) e.name (tcCurModule tc)) 0)))
        (Some e)
        (fnEntModuleSuffixIn tc fns name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Does `s` contain `$` (36) at or after `i`? Asked without building a
; string.
(pub :: nameHasDollar (-> String Int Int))
(pub fn (nameHasDollar s i)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    0
    (if (== (strByte s i) 36)
      1
      (nameHasDollar s (+ i 1)))))

; The same question asked of the whole table rather than of the
; visible part of it: used only to tell "there is no such name" from
; "there is one and it is private", which are the same AX3001 today
; and should not read the same to whoever gets it.
(pub :: findFnEntAnywhere (-> TC String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (findFnEntAnywhere tc name)
  (if (let (Some entry) (fnEntExact tc name))
    (Some entry)
    (fnEntSuffix tc name)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The exact-name index.
;
; `findFnEnt` performed up to three full linear scans of `tcFns` per
; reference - `Mod$name` exactly, then the bare name exactly, then the
; `$`-suffix pass - over a table holding about 1,600 entries when the
; compiler checks itself. A per-call-site sampling profile of `axiom
; check core/main.ax` put **83% of the program in the two EXACT
; passes** and under 5% in the suffix one. So only the exact passes are
; indexed, and `findFnEntSuffix` is left exactly as it was.
;
; That split is the whole design. Indexing an exact match is a
; statement about string equality and nothing else; indexing the suffix
; pass would mean reasoning about where `$` falls in a name, which
; module mangling has already rewritten, and which of several matches
; wins - in the function whose own comment says getting it wrong "is
; not a missing diagnostic, it is a link failure".
;
; Exact buckets keep packed head/tail Links into a word-node vector.
; Each node links to the owning entry vector and its next node, so no
; bucket allocates a separate vector. Entries and nodes are append-only;
; links remain valid when their vector's data buffer grows. Adding at
; the tail keeps table order, and lookup takes the first match, including
; duplicate names registered by `tcAddEffectOp`.
;
; The index owns its entry vector independently of TC. Its sealed fields
; keep the vectors that define each Link's identity inside this module.
; A full UInt32 index or oversized size hint uses the same owning entries
; with linear lookup. No encoded link is truncated to make it fit.
;
; The hash is the shape `stdlib/Intern.ax` uses - a multiplicative hash
; with an odd multiplier, reduced mod 2^31-1 at every step - spelled out
; here rather than imported. `typecheck.ax` importing `Intern` pulls
; `Map` with it and puts 63 declarations into six entry files' import
; closures for one hash function, into a flat namespace six entry
; files already share. Intern's own note explains why the
; running value stays under the prime: the product of a value below
; 2^31 and a multiplier below 2^31 is below 2^62, so nothing overflows
; into the sign bit before the `%` brings it back down, and no negative
; index can reach `vecGet`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: fnIdxMul Int)
(pub fn (fnIdxMul)
  1099087573)

(pub :: fnIdxSeed Int)
(pub fn (fnIdxSeed)
  1234567891)

(pub :: fnIdxPrime Int)
(pub fn (fnIdxPrime)
  2147483647)

(pub :: fnIdxHash (-> String Int))
(pub fn (fnIdxHash s)
  (fnIdxHashFrom s 0 fnIdxSeed))

(pub :: fnIdxHashFrom (-> String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (fnIdxHashFrom s i h)
  (if (>= i (strLen s))
    h
    (fnIdxHashFrom s (+ i 1) (% (+ (* h fnIdxMul) (strByte s i)) fnIdxPrime))))

; Buckets for a table of `n` entries: enough that the average bucket
; holds well under one, and never fewer than 17 so a tiny program still
; spreads. Odd, because the hash's low bits are the ones `%` keeps.
(pub :: fnIdxBuckets (-> Int Int))
(pub fn (fnIdxBuckets n)
  (+ (* 2 n) 17))

; Built with the eventual size in hand rather than the current one.
;
; The index is created in `tcNew`, BEFORE collection, because
; `tcCollectSig` and `tcCollectFn` each look a name up before deciding
; whether to push - so a table indexed only afterwards leaves collection
; itself a linear scan per declaration, which is quadratic in the
; declaration count and, on a single large module, becomes the dominant
; cost once the body-checking scans are gone. Measured on a generated
; module of N cross-referencing functions before this: N=1000 0.05s,
; N=2000 0.15s.
;
; Sizing needs a hint because the table is nearly empty at that point -
; about sixty builtins - and these buckets never grow. The declaration
; count is the hint: every `::` and `fn` in the program contributes at
; most one entry, so it is an upper bound on the table and the average
; bucket stays well under one.
; A UInt32 Link encodes index plus one. Larger tables use the same
; owning entry vector with linear lookup rather than truncating an index.
(:: fnIdxLinkLimit Int)
(fn (fnIdxLinkLimit)
  4294967295)

(:: fnIdxHead (-> FnIndex Int (Link FnIndexNode UInt32)))
(fn (fnIdxHead index hash)
  {
    (let bucket (vecGet index.buckets (% hash (vecLen index.buckets))))
    bucket.head
  })

(:: fnIdxChain (-> FnIndex String (Link FnIndexNode UInt32) (Option FnEnt)))
(fn (fnIdxChain index name cursor)
  (if (linkIsSome cursor)
    {
      (let node (linkGet index.nodes cursor))
      (let entry (linkGet index.entries node.entry))
      (if (strEq entry.name name)
        (Some entry)
        (fnIdxChain index name node.next))
    }
    None))

(:: fnIdxVisibleChain
  (-> FnIndex (Option StringRows) String (Option String) (Link FnIndexNode UInt32) (Option FnEnt)))
(fn (fnIdxVisibleChain index privs name curMod cursor)
  (if (linkIsSome cursor)
    {
      (let node (linkGet index.nodes cursor))
      (let entry (linkGet index.entries node.entry))
      (if (&& (strEq entry.name name) (== (privBlocks privs entry.name curMod) 0))
        (Some entry)
        (fnIdxVisibleChain index privs name curMod node.next))
    }
    None))

(:: fnIdxQualChain (-> FnIndex String String (Link FnIndexNode UInt32) (Option FnEnt)))
(fn (fnIdxQualChain index moduleName name cursor)
  (if (linkIsSome cursor)
    {
      (let node (linkGet index.nodes cursor))
      (let entry (linkGet index.entries node.entry))
      (if (== (nameIsQual entry.name moduleName name) 1)
        (Some entry)
        (fnIdxQualChain index moduleName name node.next))
    }
    None))

(pub :: fnIdxBuildSized (-> (Vec FnEnt) Int FnIndex))
(pub fn (fnIdxBuildSized fns hint)
  {
    (let n
      (if (> hint (vecLen fns))
        hint
        (vecLen fns)))
    (let indexed (<= n fnIdxLinkLimit))
    (let nb
      (if indexed
        (fnIdxBuckets n)
        17))
    (let buckets (:: (vecWithCapacity nb) (Vec FnIndexBucket)))
    (for i in 0..nb
      (vecPush buckets (FnIndexBucket :head linkNone :tail linkNone)))
    (let index
      (FnIndex
        :entries (vecWithCapacity (vecLen fns))
        :nodes
          (vecWithCapacity
            (if indexed
              (vecLen fns)
              1))
        :buckets buckets
        :indexed indexed))
    (for i in 0..(vecLen fns)
      (fnIdxAdd index (vecGet fns i)))
    index
  })

(pub :: fnIdxAdd (-> FnIndex FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnIdxAdd index ent)
  {
    (let entries index.entries)
    (let nodes index.nodes)
    (let buckets index.buckets)
    (let position (vecLen entries))
    (vecPush entries ent)
    (if (&& index.indexed (&& (< position fnIdxLinkLimit) (< (vecLen nodes) fnIdxLinkLimit)))
      {
        (let bucketIndex (% (fnIdxHash ent.name) (vecLen buckets)))
        (let bucket (vecGet buckets bucketIndex))
        (vecPush nodes (FnIndexNode :entry (linkTo entries position) :next linkNone))
        (let added (linkTo nodes (- (vecLen nodes) 1)))
        (if (linkIsSome bucket.tail)
          {
            (let previous (linkGet nodes bucket.tail))
            (vecSet nodes (linkIndex bucket.tail) (FnIndexNode :entry previous.entry :next added))
            (vecSet buckets bucketIndex (FnIndexBucket :head bucket.head :tail added))
            ()
          }
          {
            (vecSet buckets bucketIndex (FnIndexBucket :head added :tail added))
            ()
          })
      }
      (set index.indexed false))
    0
  })

(pub :: fnIdxGet (-> FnIndex String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnIdxGet index name)
  (if index.indexed
    (fnIdxChain index name (fnIdxHead index (fnIdxHash name)))
    (fnIdxScan index.entries name 0)))

(pub :: fnIdxScan (-> (Vec FnEnt) String Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnIdxScan v name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    (if (strEq (fnEntName (vecGet v i)) name)
      (Some (vecGet v i))
      (fnIdxScan v name (+ i 1)))))

; The exact-name question, however it is currently answerable. Word 20
; is 0 until `checkModuleTc` builds the index after collection, and
; during collection the scan answers - which is the same answer, just
; the slow way, and is why the three push sites also maintain the index
; when it exists rather than relying on an ordering.
(pub :: fnEntExact (-> TC String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnEntExact tc name)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnIdx tc))
    (fnIdxGet buckets name)
    (findFnEntExact (tcFnsVec tc) name 0)))

; The same question with the visibility filter on it.
;
; The index carried the exact pass only while `tc` word 19 was 0, and
; the comment above `privKey` said that was every program in this
; repository. It is not one any more - `core/` and `stdlib/`
; declare 307 things without `pub` - so the private branch of
; `findFnEnt` is the one a self-check actually takes, and it went back
; to scanning all ~1,600 entries per reference. A sampling profile of
; `check core/main.ax` put `findFnEntVisibleExactFrom` at 54% of
; the process.
;
; Filtering inside the bucket answers the same entry, and the argument
; is the one `fnIdxScan` already rests on: a bucket keeps insertion
; order, so the first entry in it satisfying a predicate is the first
; entry in the TABLE satisfying it, restricted to one name. The
; predicate here is the scan's own conjunction - the name matches
; exactly AND `privBlocks` allows it - so a blocked entry is stepped
; over rather than ending the search, exactly as before.
;
; The suffix pass is deliberately left alone; see the index's note.
(pub :: fnEntVisibleExact (-> TC (Option StringRows) String (Option String) (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnEntVisibleExact tc privs name curMod)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnIdx tc))
    (fnIdxGetVisible buckets privs name curMod)
    (findFnEntVisibleExact privs (tcFnsVec tc) name curMod)))

(pub :: fnIdxGetVisible (-> FnIndex (Option StringRows) String (Option String) (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnIdxGetVisible index privs name curMod)
  (if index.indexed
    (fnIdxVisibleChain index privs name curMod (fnIdxHead index (fnIdxHash name)))
    (fnIdxScanVisible index.entries privs name curMod 0)))

(pub :: fnIdxScanVisible
  (-> (Vec FnEnt) (Option StringRows) String (Option String) Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnIdxScanVisible v privs name curMod i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet v i))
      (if (&& (strEq e.name name) (== (privBlocks privs e.name curMod) 0))
        (Some e)
        (fnIdxScanVisible v privs name curMod (+ i 1)))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The module-local probe, without building `Mod$name`.
;
; `findFnEnt` asks this FIRST for every reference made from inside a
; module, and it built the key with two `concat`s - two allocations
; and two copies, per reference, on an allocator that never frees.
;
; Neither is needed. The hash is a left fold over bytes, so it can be
; fed the module, then `$`, then the name, and reach the same value
; the concatenated string would have hashed to; and the bucket's
; equality test can compare the three pieces where they already are.
; So the answer is identical and the allocation is gone.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: fnEntQual (-> TC String String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnEntQual tc m name)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnIdx tc))
    (fnIdxGetQual buckets m name)
    (findFnEntExact (tcFnsVec tc) (concat (concat m "$") name) 0)))

; `fnIdxHash` of `m` ++ "$" ++ `name`, computed over the pieces. 36 is
; `$`. The fold is `fnIdxHashFrom`'s, unchanged, resumed three times.
(pub :: fnIdxHashQual (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (fnIdxHashQual m name)
  (fnIdxHashFrom name 0 (% (+ (* (fnIdxHashFrom m 0 fnIdxSeed) fnIdxMul) 36) fnIdxPrime)))

(pub :: fnIdxGetQual (-> FnIndex String String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnIdxGetQual index m name)
  (if index.indexed
    (fnIdxQualChain index m name (fnIdxHead index (fnIdxHashQual m name)))
    (fnIdxScanQual index.entries m name 0)))

(pub :: fnIdxScanQual (-> (Vec FnEnt) String String Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnIdxScanQual v m name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet v i))
      (if (== (nameIsQual e.name m name) 1)
        (Some e)
        (fnIdxScanQual v m name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Is `e` exactly `m` ++ "$" ++ `name`? The length test first, so the
; two byte walks only run for a candidate that can still match.
(pub :: nameIsQual (-> String String String Int))
(pub fn (nameIsQual e m name)
  (if (!= (strLen e) (+ (+ (strLen m) 1) (strLen name)))
    0
    (if (!= (strByte e (strLen m)) 36)
      0
      (if (== (strEqAt e 0 m 0) 1)
        (strEqAt e (+ (strLen m) 1) name 0)
        0))))

; Keep the indexes in step with a push. A no-op before they exist.
(pub :: fnIdxNote (-> TC FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnIdxNote tc ent)
  {
    (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnIdx tc))
      (fnIdxAdd buckets ent))
    (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnSufIdx tc))
      (fnSufIdxAdd buckets ent))
    0
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The suffix index.
;
; The exact-name index's note left this pass a linear scan on the
; measurement that 83% of a self-check was in the two EXACT passes and
; under 5% here. Both halves of that have expired. The exact passes are
; indexed, and a self-check's references are BARE (`vecPush`) while
; every imported definition's name was rewritten to `Mod$vecPush` - so
; the exact pass misses on purpose for every cross-module reference and
; this pass answers it. Re-profiled after the visible-exact fix,
; `nameMatches` plus `findFnEntVisibleSuffixFrom` were 38.5% of `check
; core/main.ax` and nothing else was close.
;
; WHY THIS IS INDEXABLE, which the older note doubted. `nameMatches
; full bare` is true exactly when `full` IS `bare`, or `full` ends with
; `$bare` and that `$` is not the first byte - so the set of strings
; one entry answers to is finite and enumerable: the name itself, and
; the suffix after every `$` at a position past the first byte. That is
; not a new argument. `defIdxBuild` already builds precisely this key
; set for `isDefined`, from the same predicate, with the same reasoning
; written above `defIdxAddKeys` - this indexes entries where that
; indexes names.
;
; The bucket is a SUPERSET filter, never the answer: a candidate is
; still put through `nameMatches` and `privBlocks`. So a key derived
; too generously costs a comparison and cannot change a result, and the
; correctness obligation reduces to the one direction that matters -
; every entry the linear scan would have accepted is in the bucket for
; the name asked. It is, because that name is one of the entry's keys.
;
; ORDER, which is the half the older note was right to be careful
; about. The scan answered the FIRST match in table order, and a
; bucket holds entries in insertion order, so the first match in a
; bucket is the first match in the table restricted to one key. Other
; keys hashing into the same bucket interleave and do not disturb that,
; exactly as `dupIdxBuild` records. An entry whose own keys collide in
; one bucket appears twice; the second copy is unreachable behind the
; first, which answers identically.
;
; Sized for its own population rather than the table's: an entry
; contributes one key per `$` in its name plus one, and a self-check's
; names are `Mod$name`, so twice the declaration count is the hint.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: fnSufIdxBuildSized (-> (Vec FnEnt) Int FnSuffixIndex))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxBuildSized fns hint)
  {
    (let n
      (if (> hint (vecLen fns))
        hint
        (vecLen fns)))
    (let nb (fnIdxBuckets (* 2 n)))
    (let bs vecNew)
    (for i in 0..nb
      (vecPush bs vecNew))
    (for i in 0..(vecLen fns)
      (fnSufIdxAdd bs (vecGet fns i)))
    bs
  })

(pub :: fnSufIdxAdd (-> FnSuffixIndex FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxAdd bs ent)
  {
    (fnSufIdxPut bs ent.name ent)
    (fnSufIdxAddSuffixes bs ent.name ent 1)
  })

; From 1, not from 0: `nameMatches` demands the `$` be past the first
; byte, so a name that merely BEGINS with `$` answers only to itself
; and inserting that suffix would invent a match.
(pub :: fnSufIdxAddSuffixes (-> FnSuffixIndex String FnEnt Int Int))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxAddSuffixes bs full ent i)
  (if (>= i (strLen full))
    0
    {
      (if (== (strByte full i) 36)
        (fnSufIdxPut bs (strSlice full (+ i 1) (- (strLen full) (+ i 1))) ent))
      (fnSufIdxAddSuffixes bs full ent (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: fnSufIdxPut (-> FnSuffixIndex String FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxPut bs key ent)
  {
    (vecPush (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash key) (vecLen bs))) ent)
    0
  })

(pub :: fnSufIdxGet (-> FnSuffixIndex String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxGet bs name)
  (fnSufIdxScan (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen bs))) name 0))

(pub :: fnSufIdxScan (-> (Vec FnEnt) String Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxScan v name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet v i))
      (if (== (nameMatches e.name name) 1)
        (Some e)
        (fnSufIdxScan v name (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: fnSufIdxGetVisible
  (-> FnSuffixIndex (Option StringRows) String (Option String) (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxGetVisible bs privs name curMod)
  (fnSufIdxScanVisible (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen bs))) privs name curMod 0))

(pub :: fnSufIdxScanVisible
  (-> (Vec FnEnt) (Option StringRows) String (Option String) Int (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnSufIdxScanVisible v privs name curMod i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    {
      (let e (vecGet v i))
      (if (&& (== (nameMatches e.name name) 1) (== (privBlocks privs e.name curMod) 0))
        (Some e)
        (fnSufIdxScanVisible v privs name curMod (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The suffix question, however it is currently answerable - the same
; shape as `fnEntExact`, and 0 during collection means the scan
; answers, which is the same answer the slow way.
(pub :: fnEntSuffix (-> TC String (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnEntSuffix tc name)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnSufIdx tc))
    (fnSufIdxGet buckets name)
    (findFnEntSuffix (tcFnsVec tc) name 0)))

(pub :: fnEntVisibleSuffix (-> TC (Option StringRows) String (Option String) (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnEntVisibleSuffix tc privs name curMod)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcFnSufIdx tc))
    (fnSufIdxGetVisible buckets privs name curMod)
    (findFnEntVisibleSuffix privs (tcFnsVec tc) name curMod)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE TYPE NAMESPACE, AND ITS INDEX.
;
; Type names resolve through a bucketed namespace index. A reference
; chooses its own module's declaration, then a unique visible imported
; declaration. Entry declarations are visible in the entry module; seeded
; Option and Vec types are visible everywhere. Ambiguous references are
; refused before expansion can erase their spelling.
;
; Checked data, struct and subtype names include their declaring module.
; Canonical names survive generic instantiation, substitution, alias
; expansion and all type cloning. Comparing names therefore compares
; declarations rather than whichever type the caller's scope selects.
;
; Constructors and field accesses retain the resolved declaration for
; codegen, whose registry contains matching qualified entries.
; The four things a name can be bound to in this table. `TY_ANY` is
; not a kind an entry carries - it is the QUESTION a `TAG_T_CON` asks,
; "anything a type annotation may name", which is the three declared
; kinds and not a constructor.
(pub :: TY_ANY Int)
(pub fn (TY_ANY)
  0)

(pub :: TY_STRUCT Int)
(pub fn (TY_STRUCT)
  1)

(pub :: TY_DATA Int)
(pub fn (TY_DATA)
  2)

(pub :: TY_ALIAS Int)
(pub fn (TY_ALIAS)
  3)

(pub :: TY_CTOR Int)
(pub fn (TY_CTOR)
  4)

; A range-constrained subtype declaration node. Registered like an
; alias so signatures may name it; the checker reads the base and
; bounds from the declaration itself.
(pub :: TY_SUBTYPE Int)
(pub fn (TY_SUBTYPE)
  5)

; Source declarations resolve nominal identity before fields and aliases
; are expanded. They do not participate in ordinary type lookups.
(:: TY_DECL Int)
(fn (TY_DECL)
  6)

(:: tcIndexTypeDecls (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(fn (tcIndexTypeDecls tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (declNamespace (nodeTag d)) NS_TYPE)
          (tyIdxPut tc (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d) (TyDecl d)))
      }
      (tcIndexTypeDecls tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; A checked nominal name includes its declaring module. Every type
; transformation keeps this string, so substitution and instantiation
; cannot resolve an imported type again in a caller's namespace.
(:: nominalName (-> String (Option String) String))
(fn (nominalName name owner)
  (if (let (Some moduleName) owner)
    (concat moduleName (concat "$" (bareOf name)))
    (bareOf name)))

(:: tyNominalName (-> TC String String))
(fn (tyNominalName tc name)
  (if (|| (typeKeywordKnown name) (isSome (modOf name)))
    name
    (if (== (tySourceAmbiguous tc name) 1)
      name
      {
        (let d (tyCanonicalDecl tc name))
        (if (let (Some d) d)
          (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_ALIAS)
            name
            (nominalName (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d)))
          name)
      })))

; Public signatures can expose an opaque private type. When no visible
; declaration owns a written name, preserve the identity of a unique
; private declaration too. Several private candidates remain unresolved.
(:: tyCanonicalDecl (-> TC String (Option ASTNode)))
(fn (tyCanonicalDecl tc name)
  {
    (let visible (tyFindSyntax tc TY_DECL name 0))
    (if (let (Some visible) visible)
      (Some visible)
      {
        (let first (tyFindAnyVisFromSyntax tc TY_DECL name 0))
        (let last (tyFindAnyVisFromSyntax tc TY_DECL name 1))
        (if (let (Some first) first)
          (if (let (Some last) last)
            (if (! (nodeSame first last))
              None
              (if (&& (isSome (tcCurModule tc)) (isNone (nodeModuleOpt first)))
                None
                (Some first)))
            None)
          None)
      })
  })

(:: tySourceAmbiguous (-> TC String Int))
(fn (tySourceAmbiguous tc name)
  (if (|| (isSome (modOf name)) (== (tyMultiDecl tc TY_DECL name) 0))
    0
    (if (>= (vecLen (tyAmbigMods tc TY_DECL name)) 2)
      1
      0)))

; A typed payload retains its declaration and, for a constructor, its
; owning data type. Constructor visibility uses the owner's module and
; name. None names the entry file or a builtin; Some retains a module
; name even when that string is empty.
(pub data TyPayload
  (TyStruct StructEnt)
  (TyData DataEnt)
  (TyCtor CtorEnt DataEnt)
  (TyAlias ASTNode)
  (TySubtype ASTNode)
  (TyDecl ASTNode))

(pub struct TyEnt
  (name : String)
  (module : (Option String))
  (payload : TyPayload))

(pub type TypeIndex (Vec (Vec TyEnt)))

(:: tyEntName (-> TyEnt String))
(fn (tyEntName te)
  te.name)

(:: tyEntKind (-> TyEnt Int))
(fn (tyEntKind te)
  (match te.payload
    ((TyStruct _) TY_STRUCT)
    ((TyData _) TY_DATA)
    ((TyCtor _ _) TY_CTOR)
    ((TyAlias _) TY_ALIAS)
    ((TySubtype _) TY_SUBTYPE)
    ((TyDecl _) TY_DECL)))

(:: tyEntModule (-> TyEnt (Option String)))
(fn (tyEntModule te)
  te.module)

; Buckets over the entries, keyed on the BARE name - the same shape
; and the same hash `ambIdxNew` and `privSetNew` use, sized from the
; declaration count, which is an upper bound on the type count.
(pub :: tyIdxNew (-> Int TypeIndex))
(pub fn (tyIdxNew hint)
  {
    (let nb (fnIdxBuckets hint))
    (let buckets vecNew)
    (for i in 0..nb
      (vecPush buckets vecNew))
    buckets
  })

; Appended AS EACH DECLARATION IS COLLECTED, beside the `vecPush` that
; puts it in `tcDatas`/`tcStructs`/`tcAliases`, so the index and the
; Vec can never be out of step - collection itself looks names up.
(pub :: tyIdxPut (-> TC String (Option String) TyPayload Int))
(pub fn (tyIdxPut tc name m payload)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcTyIdx tc))
    {
      (let bare (bareOf name))
      (vecPush (vecGet buckets (% (fnIdxHash bare) (vecLen buckets)))
        (TyEnt :name bare :module m :payload payload))
      0
    }
    0))

(pub :: tyIdxAddData (-> TC DataEnt Int))
(pub fn (tyIdxAddData tc ent)
  {
    (tyIdxPut tc ent.name ent.module (TyData ent))
    (tyIdxAddCtors tc ent ent.ctors 0)
  })

(pub :: tyIdxAddCtors (-> TC DataEnt (Vec CtorEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (tyIdxAddCtors tc owner ctors i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    0
    {
      (let ctor (vecGet ctors i))
      (tyIdxPut tc ctor.name owner.module (TyCtor ctor owner))
      (tyIdxAddCtors tc owner ctors (+ i 1))
    }))

; The builtin `Option` is registered into `datas` before the record
; exists, so it has no collection pass to be indexed by. Seeded from
; whatever is already in the Vec rather than by naming `Option`, so a
; second builtin type cannot be added and silently left unindexed.
(pub :: tyIdxSeedDatas (-> TC (Vec DataEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (tyIdxSeedDatas tc datas i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen datas))
    0
    {
      (tyIdxAddData tc (vecGet datas i))
      (tyIdxSeedDatas tc datas (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: tyIdxBucket (-> TC String (Vec TyEnt)))
(pub fn (tyIdxBucket tc name)
  (if (let (Some buckets) (tcTyIdx tc))
    (vecGet buckets (% (fnIdxHash (bareOf name)) (vecLen buckets)))
    vecNew))

; A bucket holds whatever hashes together, so every walk below still
; asks `declMatches` - which is `nameMatches` for a bare reference and
; a real module comparison for a qualified one.
(pub :: tyEntMatches (-> TyEnt Int String Int))
(pub fn (tyEntMatches te kind ref)
  (if (== kind TY_ANY)
    (if (|| (== (tyEntKind te) TY_CTOR) (== (tyEntKind te) TY_DECL))
      0
      (declMatches (tyEntModule te) (tyEntName te) ref))
    (if (!= (tyEntKind te) kind)
      0
      (declMatches (tyEntModule te) (tyEntName te) ref))))

; A constructor's key is its DATA TYPE's, which is what
; `findCtorFrom` asked before this and what `collectPrivates` records.
(pub :: tyPrivKey (-> TyEnt String))
(pub fn (tyPrivKey te)
  (match te.payload
    ((TyCtor _ owner) (privKey owner.module owner.name))
    ((TyStruct _) (privKey te.module te.name))
    ((TyData _) (privKey te.module te.name))
    ((TyAlias _) (privKey te.module te.name))
    ((TySubtype _) (privKey te.module te.name))
    ((TyDecl _) (privKey te.module te.name))))

(pub :: tyVisible (-> (Option StringRows) TyEnt (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (tyVisible privs te curMod)
  (if (let (Some _) privs)
    (if (&& (isSome curMod) (&& (isNone (tyEntModule te)) (== (tyBuiltinEntry te) 0)))
      0
      (if (== (privBlocks privs (tyPrivKey te) curMod) 1)
        0
        1))
    1))

; Entry declarations are not imports of another module. Only seeded
; Option/Vec entries are visible everywhere; any-visibility classification
; still finds a checked entry type through its canonical name.
(:: tyBuiltinEntry (-> TyEnt Int))
(fn (tyBuiltinEntry te)
  (match te.payload
    ((TyData _)
      (if (sizedTyIn te.name "Option Vec Link")
        1
        0)
    )
    ((TyCtor _ owner)
      (if (strEq owner.name "Option")
        1
        0)
    )
    ((TyStruct _) 0)
    ((TyAlias _) 0)
    ((TySubtype _) 0)
    ((TyDecl _) 0)))

; Which of rules (a), (b) and (c)/(d) this pass is asking for:
; 1 = declared by the referencing module, 2 = declared by nobody,
; 0 = any candidate at all.
(pub :: tyTierOk (-> TyEnt (Option String) Int Int))
(pub fn (tyTierOk te curMod tier)
  (if (== tier 0)
    1
    (if (== tier 2)
      (if (isNone te.module)
        1
        0)
      (if (isNone te.module)
        0
        (if (isNone curMod)
          0
          (typeModuleSame te.module curMod))))))

(pub :: tyScanFwd
  (-> (Vec TyEnt) Int String (Option StringRows) (Option String) Int Int (Option TyEnt)))
(pub fn (tyScanFwd v kind ref privs curMod tier i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    {
      (let te (vecGet v i))
      (if (&& (== (tyTierOk te curMod tier) 1)
            (&& (== (tyEntMatches te kind ref) 1) (== (tyVisible privs te curMod) 1)))
        (Some te)
        (tyScanFwd v kind ref privs curMod tier (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The LAST match rather than the first. `rfindCtor` is stage0's
; bare-value and saturation path and the difference is observable when
; two `data` declarations in one module name the same constructor, so
; the direction is preserved WITHIN each tier rather than dropped.
(pub :: tyScanRev
  (-> (Vec TyEnt) Int String (Option StringRows) (Option String) Int Int (Option TyEnt)))
(pub fn (tyScanRev v kind ref privs curMod tier i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let te (vecGet v i))
      (if (&& (== (tyTierOk te curMod tier) 1)
            (&& (== (tyEntMatches te kind ref) 1) (== (tyVisible privs te curMod) 1)))
        (Some te)
        (tyScanRev v kind ref privs curMod tier (- i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: tyPick
  (-> (Vec TyEnt) Int String (Option StringRows) (Option String) Int Int (Option TyEnt)))
(pub fn (tyPick v kind ref privs curMod last tier)
  (if (== last 0)
    (tyScanFwd v kind ref privs curMod tier 0)
    (tyScanRev v kind ref privs curMod tier (- (vecLen v) 1))))

; Rules (a) then (b) then (c)/(d), in that order. A QUALIFIED
; reference names one module, so it skips straight to the tier that
; asks nothing about the referencing module - `declMatches` has
; already pinned it.
;
; Rule (d) answers the FIRST candidate rather than 0, deliberately:
; the reference is refused by `checkAmbiguousType` at a site that has
; a span, and answering 0 here would turn one AX3044 into an AX3002
; cascade about a type that plainly exists.
(pub :: tyFind (-> TC Int String Int (Option TyEnt)))
(pub fn (tyFind tc kind name last)
  {
    (let entries (tyIdxBucket tc name))
    (let privs (tcPrivIdx tc))
    (let curMod (tcCurModule tc))
    (if (isSome (modOf name))
        (tyPick entries kind name privs curMod last 0)
      (let (Some own) (tyPick entries kind name privs curMod last 1))
        (Some own)
      (let (Some global) (tyPick entries kind name privs curMod last 2))
        (Some global)
      (tyPick entries kind name privs curMod last 0))
  })

; Existence, deliberately IGNORING visibility and the referencing
; module: reaching a type another module keeps private is AX3023's
; subject, and answering AX3002 for it would say "no such type" about
; one that exists.
(pub :: tyIdxHas (-> TC Int String Int))
(pub fn (tyIdxHas tc kind name)
  (if (isSome (tyScanFwd (tyIdxBucket tc name) kind name None None 0 0))
    1
    0))

; Does the bucket hold TWO OR MORE visible declarations of this name?
;
; Nothing but a `yes` here can produce an AX3044, and the answer is
; `no` for every name in every program that has no collision at all -
; which is every program in this repository. It exists so the tiered
; walk and its Vec below run only for a name that could possibly need
; them: `tyConAmbiguous` is asked of every capitalised name in every
; signature, annotation and field type, and building a module Vec
; there put one allocation per type reference on an arena that never
; frees. Measured on the scale probe (8,000 struct declarations,
; 32,000 type references): 0.29s with the Vec, 0.23s without.
(pub :: tyMultiDecl (-> TC Int String Int))
(pub fn (tyMultiDecl tc kind name)
  {
    (let v (tyIdxBucket tc name))
    (if (== (as v Int) 0)
      0
      (if (>= (tyMatchCount v kind name (tcPrivIdx tc) (tcCurModule tc) 0 0) 2)
        1
        0))
  })

(pub :: tyMatchCount (-> (Vec TyEnt) Int String (Option StringRows) (Option String) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (tyMatchCount v kind ref privs curMod acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    (tyMatchCount v kind ref privs curMod
      (if (&& (== (tyEntMatches (vecGet v i) kind ref) 1)
            (== (tyVisible privs (vecGet v i) curMod) 1))
        (+ acc 1)
        acc)
      (+ i 1))))

; The distinct modules that make `name` UNRESOLVABLE, or an empty Vec.
; Empty whenever rules (a), (b) or (c) answered - a module-local
; declaration, a module-less one, or a single definer - so this is
; exactly rule (d) and nothing else. The value namespace's
; `definingModules` answers the same question from a table built at
; collection; this one reads the bucket, which is the same one entry.
(pub :: tyAmbigMods (-> TC Int String Names))
(pub fn (tyAmbigMods tc kind name)
  {
    (let v (tyIdxBucket tc name))
    (if (== (as v Int) 0)
      vecNew
      (if (isSome (modOf name))
        vecNew
        {
          (let privs (tcPrivIdx tc))
          (let curMod (tcCurModule tc))
          (if (isSome (tyScanFwd v kind name privs curMod 1 0))
            vecNew
            (if (isSome (tyScanFwd v kind name privs curMod 2 0))
              vecNew
              (tyModsOf v kind name privs curMod vecNew 0)))
        }))
  })

(pub :: tyModsOf (-> (Vec TyEnt) Int String (Option StringRows) (Option String) Names Int Names))
(pub fn (tyModsOf v kind ref privs curMod acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    {
      (let te (vecGet v i))
      (if (&& (== (tyEntMatches te kind ref) 1) (== (tyVisible privs te curMod) 1))
        (if (let (Some moduleName) te.module)
          (if (== (strInVec acc moduleName 0) 0)
            (vecPush acc moduleName))))
      (tyModsOf v kind ref privs curMod acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; AX3044, the type namespace's AX3014. A separate code rather than a
; reuse, because the ESCAPE used to differ and a diagnostic's help is
; the half a reader acts on: AX3014 says "write `Mod::name`", and in
; type position that was a parse error (`parseTypeAtom` had no `::`
; arm), so answering AX3014 here would have handed the reader a fix
; that did not compile - and the help said to narrow the import
; instead. Since `Mod::Name` parses in type position the help says
; what AX3014's says, built by the same `joinQualified`.
;
; What DOES still work is narrowing the import: an import name list decides
; `exported` in `resolveDeclsPhase`, which is what `collectPrivates`
; reads, so `(import TeamA (fromA))` leaves TeamA's `Config` invisible
; outside TeamA and the reference resolves by rule (c).
(pub :: checkAmbiguousType (-> TC Int String Int Int))
(pub fn (checkAmbiguousType tc kind name span)
  (if (|| (== span 0) (== (tyMultiDecl tc kind name) 0))
    0
    {
      (let mods (tyAmbigMods tc kind name))
      (if (< (vecLen mods) 2)
        0
        {
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3044" "ambiguous-type" span
                (concat (concat (concat "ambiguous type `" (bareOf name)) "`: declared in ")
                  (joinModules mods))
                None (Some (concat "qualify the reference: " (joinQualified mods (bareOf name)))))
              (concat (concat "`" (bareOf name)) "` could mean more than one declaration")))
          1
        })
    }))

; Is this capitalised name unresolvable? Asked of TY_ANY, so a
; `struct` in one module and a `data` of the same name in another are
; the collision they are - per-kind questions would each answer "one
; definer" and let it through.
(pub :: tyConAmbiguous (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (tyConAmbiguous tc name)
  (if (typeKeywordKnown name)
    0
    (if (== (tyMultiDecl tc TY_ANY name) 0)
      0
      (if (>= (vecLen (tyAmbigMods tc TY_ANY name)) 2)
        1
        0))))

; The first ambiguous type constructor in `t`, or None. The walk
; preserves first-witness order and the declaration anchors its diagnostic.
(pub :: tyAmbigCon (-> TC ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (tyAmbigCon tc t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    None
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      (if (== (tyConAmbiguous tc (nodeAName t)) 1)
        (Some (nodeAName t))
        (tyAmbigConVec tc (nodeBChildren t) 0))
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
        {
          (let first (tyAmbigCon tc (nodeAChild t)))
          (if (let (Some name) first)
            (Some name)
            (tyAmbigCon tc (nodeBChild t)))
        }
        (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_TUP)
          (tyAmbigConVec tc (nodeAChildren t) 0)
          (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_PTR)
            (tyAmbigCon tc (nodeAChild t))
            None))))))

(pub :: tyAmbigConVec (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (tyAmbigConVec tc xs i)
  (if (vecIsNull xs)
    None
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      None
      (if (let (Some name) (tyAmbigCon tc (vecGet xs i)))
        (Some name)
        (tyAmbigConVec tc xs (+ i 1))))))

; A declaration's own signature or field types, refused when one of
; them names a type two modules declare. Asked BEFORE alias expansion,
; because expansion resolves an alias name and would otherwise pick
; one of the two colliding aliases and erase the evidence.
(pub :: checkAmbiguousSigTy (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkAmbiguousSigTy tc d t)
  {
    (let bad (tyAmbigCon tc t))
    (if (let (Some bad) bad)
      {
        (let _u (tcSetCurUnit tc (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) (nodeModuleOpt d))))
        {
          (let r (checkAmbiguousType tc TY_ANY bad (nodeSpan d)))
          {
            (tcSetCurUnit tc 0)
            r
          }
        }
      }
      0)
  })

; Does the constructor vector of one `data` hold `name`? The index
; above answers every ordinary lookup; this is the one caller that
; asks the question of a SINGLE declaration rather than of the
; program - `ctorHiddenModule`, which walks the datas a reference may
; NOT name in order to say "private" instead of "no such name".
(pub :: ctorInData (-> (Vec CtorEnt) (Option String) String Int (Option CtorEnt)))
(pub fn (ctorInData ctors declMod name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    None
    {
      (let c (vecGet ctors i))
      (if (== (declMatches declMod c.name name) 1)
        (Some c)
        (ctorInData ctors declMod name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Constructor lookup, FORWARD order - Option's are first. Used for
; type resolution and patterns (stage0's find_constructor).
(pub :: findCtor (-> TC String (Option CtorEnt)))
(pub fn (findCtor tc name)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFind tc TY_CTOR name 0))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyStruct entry) None)
      ((TyData entry) None)
      ((TyCtor entry _) (Some entry))
      ((TyAlias entry) None)
      ((TySubtype entry) None)
      ((TyDecl entry) None))
    None))

; The LAST matching constructor - stage0's bare-value and saturation
; paths use rfind, and the difference is observable when two datas
; declare the same constructor name.
(pub :: rfindCtor (-> TC String (Option CtorEnt)))
(pub fn (rfindCtor tc name)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFind tc TY_CTOR name 1))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyStruct entry) None)
      ((TyData entry) None)
      ((TyCtor entry _) (Some entry))
      ((TyAlias entry) None)
      ((TySubtype entry) None)
      ((TyDecl entry) None))
    None))

(pub :: findStruct (-> TC String (Option StructEnt)))
(pub fn (findStruct tc name)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFind tc TY_STRUCT name 0))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyStruct entry) (Some entry))
      ((TyData entry) None)
      ((TyCtor entry _) None)
      ((TyAlias entry) None)
      ((TySubtype entry) None)
      ((TyDecl entry) None))
    None))

(pub :: findData (-> TC String (Option DataEnt)))
(pub fn (findData tc name)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFind tc TY_DATA name 0))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyStruct entry) None)
      ((TyData entry) (Some entry))
      ((TyCtor entry _) None)
      ((TyAlias entry) None)
      ((TySubtype entry) None)
      ((TyDecl entry) None))
    None))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; WORD STRUCTS AND HANDLES (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-10a, MM-PAR-8).
;
; `(struct Ticket word shared (slot : Int))` declares a type that is ONE
; MACHINE WORD, not a heap block: `(Ticket s)` is the word `s` and
; `t.slot` is the word `t`. Three things follow, and this section is where the
; checker says each of them:
;
;   * IT IS A SCALAR to every question about ownership. `evClassOf`,
;     `tcFldClass`, `rgTyScalar` and codegen's `fldClass` all answer
;     "a word" for it, so it takes no share, has no bit in any reference
;     map, costs a polymorphic call no evidence and survives a region's
;     reset by value. A heap struct holding a word struct keeps its map.
;   * IT IS SEALED. Its constructor and its field belong to the module
;     that declares it, whatever `pub` says (AX3085, AX3086), and a
;     declared `Int` result may not answer one (`checkDeclaredReturn`),
;     so the only word a caller holds is one its module made. `pub`
;     exports the NAME, so an importer's signatures can speak of it.
;     A `cast` to one is a forging cast, which belongs to the unsafe
;     layer (`castForges`, AX3073).
;   * IT IS CAPTURED ONLY WHEN SHARED. A concurrent binding may capture
;     a word struct declared `shared` - its module built it for use
;     from every binding at once - and no other word struct (AX3064,
;     `capReport`).
;
; `Spawn`, the builtin handle a spawn answers, is a word too, and is
; never shared: a join from another thread is refused at run time (78,
; `MM-PAR-7`).
;
; What the word MEANS is its module's business. `Spawn` holds a slot in
; the runtime's handle table (`__handle_new`, MM-PAR-8), so a rejoined
; or forged one traps 85 instead of touching an unmapped page. The
; standard library's `Chan`, `Mutex` and `CancelToken` are not words but
; `sealed shared` owners (MM-PAR-16).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The declaration of `kind` a bare or qualified NAME denotes, IGNORING
; visibility: whether a type is a word, or a reference a cast can forge,
; is a fact about its declaration, not about who asks. Tiered exactly as
; `tyFind` is - the referencing module's own, then a module-less one,
; then any - with no privacy set.
(pub :: tyFindAnyVis (-> TC Int String (Option TyEnt)))
(pub fn (tyFindAnyVis tc kind name)
  (tyFindAnyVisFrom tc kind name 0))

(:: tyFindAnyVisFrom (-> TC Int String Int (Option TyEnt)))
(fn (tyFindAnyVisFrom tc kind name last)
  {
    (let entries (tyIdxBucket tc name))
    (let curMod (tcCurModule tc))
    (if (isSome (modOf name))
        (tyPick entries kind name None curMod last 0)
      (let (Some own) (tyPick entries kind name None curMod last 1))
        (Some own)
      (let (Some global) (tyPick entries kind name None curMod last 2))
        (Some global)
      (tyPick entries kind name None curMod last 0))
  })

(pub :: findStructAnyVis (-> TC String (Option StructEnt)))
(pub fn (findStructAnyVis tc name)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFindAnyVis tc TY_STRUCT name))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyStruct entry) (Some entry))
      ((TyData entry) None)
      ((TyCtor entry _) None)
      ((TyAlias entry) None)
      ((TySubtype entry) None)
      ((TyDecl entry) None))
    None))

; The `data` declaration a name denotes, ignoring visibility, as above.
(pub :: findDataAnyVis (-> TC String (Option DataEnt)))
(pub fn (findDataAnyVis tc name)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFindAnyVis tc TY_DATA name))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyStruct entry) None)
      ((TyData entry) (Some entry))
      ((TyCtor entry _) None)
      ((TyAlias entry) None)
      ((TySubtype entry) None)
      ((TyDecl entry) None))
    None))

; The word struct a type name denotes, or None when no declaration
; matches or the program declares no word structs.
(pub :: tcWordEnt (-> TC String (Option StructEnt)))
(pub fn (tcWordEnt tc n)
  (if (== (tcWordStructs tc) 0)
    None
    {
      (let ent (findStructAnyVis tc n))
      (if (== (structEntIsWord ent) 1)
        ent
        None)
    }))

; How many of `ds`, from `i`, are struct declarations marked `word`.
(pub :: countWordStructs (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (countWordStructs ds i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ds))
    acc
    {
      (let d (vecGet ds i))
      (countWordStructs ds (+ i 1)
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_STRUCT) (structIsWord d))
          (+ acc 1)
          acc))
    }))

; Is `n` a word type: a word struct, or `Spawn`?
(pub :: tcWordTyName (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (tcWordTyName tc n)
  (if (strEq n "Spawn")
    1
    (if (! (isNone (tcWordEnt tc n)))
      1
      0)))

; The same question of a type NODE: resolved, alias-expanded, and
; applied to no arguments (a word struct takes none).
(pub :: tyIsWordTy (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsWordTy tc ty0)
  (if (nodeSame ty0 nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve ty0) 0))
      (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
        0
        (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
          0
          (if (!= (vecLen (nodeBChildren ty)) 0)
            0
            (tcWordTyName tc (nodeAName ty)))))
    }))

; A word type a concurrent binding may NOT capture: `Spawn`, and a
; word struct its module did not declare `shared`.
(pub :: tyIsUnsharedWord (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsUnsharedWord tc ty0)
  (if (== (tyIsWordTy tc ty0) 0)
    0
    {
      (let n (nodeAName (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve ty0) 0)))
      (if (strEq n "Spawn")
        1
        (if (== (structEntIsShared (tcWordEnt tc n)) 1)
          0
          1))
    }))

; Two optional module names identify the same declaring module.
(pub :: tcSameModule (-> (Option String) (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (tcSameModule a b)
  (match a
    ((None)
      (if (isNone b)
        1
        0)
    )
    ((Some left)
      (if (let (Some right) b)
        (if (strEq left right)
          1
          0)
        0)
    )))

; Word structs and explicitly sealed heap structs expose operations,
; while their module owns construction and field access.
(pub :: wordSealedHere (-> TC (Option StructEnt) Int))
(pub fn (wordSealedHere tc ent)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    (if (== (wordEntKeepsFields ent) 0)
      0
      (if (== (tcSameModule entry.module (tcCurModule tc)) 1)
        0
        1))
    0))

; A sealed marker keeps the fields in the module. A `word` whose one
; field is `Int` does too: that word is a handle. A packed word is a
; data record, so its fields are readable everywhere unless it is
; also marked `sealed`.
(pub :: wordEntKeepsFields (-> (Option StructEnt) Int))
(pub fn (wordEntKeepsFields ent)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    (if (!= (& entry.repr 4) 0)
      1
      (if (&& (== (structEntIsWord ent) 1) (== (structEntIsLegacyWord ent) 1))
        1
        0))
    0))

; One `Int` field. Construction and field reads are that word.
(pub :: structEntIsLegacyWord (-> (Option StructEnt) Int))
(pub fn (structEntIsLegacyWord ent)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    {
      (let fs entry.fields)
      (if (!= (vecLen fs) 1)
        0
        (if (tyIsBareInt (nodeTyChild (vecGet fs 0)))
          1
          0))
    }
    0))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The declaration rules, AX3084. A marker is `word`, `sealed` or
; `shared`, each at most once. `shared` needs `word` or `sealed`: only a
; module that keeps the fields to itself can say every operation is safe
; from several bindings at once. A `sealed shared` heap struct is a
; counted block, and its count is safe because a `parallel` form lends
; it, frozen until the last join (MM-PAR-6b, MM-PAR-16); so it takes no
; `mut` field and no type parameters. And a word is ONE `Int`: one
; field, typed `Int`, not `mut` (a word has no storage a store could
; reach - `(set c.slot v)` would change a copy), and no type parameters
; (a word carries no type argument a `Vec`'s evidence could read).
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkStructMarkers (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkStructMarkers tc d)
  {
    (let ms (structMarkers d))
    (let seen vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen ms)
        {
          (let m (vecGet ms i))
          (let n (nodeAName (vecGet ms i)))
          (if (|| (strEq n "word") (|| (strEq n "shared") (strEq n "sealed")))
            (if (isSome (strVecIdx seen n))
              (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan m) (concat (concat "`" n) "` is written twice")
                "write each marker once"
                "a marker says one thing about the struct, and saying it twice says nothing more")
              (vecPushStr seen n))
            (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan m)
              (concat (concat "`" n) "` is not a struct marker")
              "a struct takes `word`, which makes it one machine word rather than a heap block, `sealed`, which keeps its constructor and fields in its module, and `shared`, which lets a `parallel` binding capture it; anything else after the struct's name is refused"
              "the markers come between the name and the first field: `(struct Ticket word shared (slot : Int))`"))
        })
      (if (&& (structIsShared d)
            (if (|| (structIsWord d) (structHasMarker d "sealed"))
              false
              true))
        (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan d)
          (concat (concat "struct `" (nodeAName d))
            "` is `shared` but neither a `word` nor `sealed`")
          "add `word` for a one-word handle or `sealed` for a counted one, or drop `shared`: only a module that keeps the fields to itself can promise every operation is safe from several bindings at once"
          "a `sealed shared` struct is lent to a `parallel` form's bindings with its count frozen until the last join, since `axiom_retain` is a plain load-add-store (MM-PAR-6b, MM-PAR-16)"))
      (if (&& (structIsShared d)
            (&&
              (if (structIsWord d)
                false
                true)
              (structHasMarker d "sealed")))
        (checkSharedHeapShape tc d))
      (if (structIsWord d)
        (checkWordStructShape tc d))
      0
    }
  })

; A `sealed shared` heap struct: no type parameters, and no `mut`
; field, since a lent block is read by every binding at once and a store
; to it would race (MM-PAR-16).
(pub :: checkSharedHeapShape (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkSharedHeapShape tc d)
  {
    (let fs (nodeBChildren d))
    (let name (nodeAName d))
    {
      (if (!= (nodeTy d) 0)
        (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan d)
          (concat (concat "shared struct `" name) "` takes type parameters")
          "drop the parameters: a shared struct's module states what every field is"
          "a `parallel` form lends a shared struct whole, and its module, not its caller, answers for every field's use from several bindings (MM-PAR-16)"))
      (for i in 0..(vecLen fs)
        {
          (let f (vecGet fs i))
          (if (== (fieldIsMut f) 1)
            (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (spanOf f)
              (concat
                (concat (concat (concat "field `" (nodeAName f)) "` of shared struct `") name)
                "` is `mut`")
              "drop `mut`: keep what changes in the memory the struct names, behind its module's atomics or lock"
              "a lent block is read by every binding at once, so a plain store to its field would race (MM-PAR-9)"))
        })
      0
    }
  })

(pub :: checkWordStructShape (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkWordStructShape tc d)
  {
    (let fs (nodeBChildren d))
    (let name (nodeAName d))
    (if (!= (nodeTy d) 0)
      (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan d)
        (concat (concat "word struct `" name) "` takes type parameters")
        "drop the parameters: a word carries no type argument"
        "a word takes no share and so has no evidence a type argument could describe")
      (if (== (vecLen fs) 0)
        (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan d)
          (concat (concat "word struct `" name) "` declares no fields")
          "give it one `Int` field, or fixed-width fields whose widths total 64 bits or fewer"
          "a word struct is one machine word, not a block (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-10a)")
        (wordCheckFields tc d fs name 0 0 1)))
  })

; `legacy` stays 1 only while every field seen is the single bare `Int`.
(:: wordCheckFields (-> TC ASTNode Nodes String Int Int Int Int))
(fn (wordCheckFields tc d fs name i total legacy)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fs))
    (if (== legacy 1)
      0
      (if (> total 64)
        (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan d)
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "word struct `" name) "` fields total ") (fmtInt total))
            " bits, and a word holds 64")
          "drop a field or narrow one until the widths fit in 64 bits"
          "a word struct is one machine word, not a block (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-10a)")
        (if (&& (structIsShared d)
              (if (structHasMarker d "sealed")
                false
                true))
          (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (nodeSpan d)
            (concat (concat "word struct `" name)
              "` is `shared` and its fields are readable everywhere")
            "add `sealed`, or drop `shared`: `shared` says the module keeps the fields"
            "a packed word is a data record, so mark `sealed` when its fields must stay in the module")
          0)))
    {
      (let f (vecGet fs i))
      (let code (wordWidthCode tc (nodeTyChild f)))
      (if (== (fieldIsMut f) 1)
        {
          (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (spanOf f)
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "field `" (nodeAName f)) "` of word struct `") name)
              "` is `mut`")
            "drop `mut`: a word struct is a value, and a store would change only the copy it was made on"
            "a word struct has no block a field store could reach")
          0
        }
        (if (== code 0)
          {
            (emitStructMarkerDiag tc (spanOf f)
              (concat (concat (concat (concat "field `" (nodeAName f)) "` of word struct `") name)
                "` is not a fixed-width field")
              "type it `Int8`, `Int16`, `Int32`, `Int64`, `UInt8`, `UInt16`, `UInt32`, `UInt64`, or a `Link` of width `UInt8`, `UInt16` or `UInt32`. One `Int` field is the whole word"
              "a `String`, a `Vec` or a record is a reference, and a word that held one would take no share of it")
            0
          }
          (wordCheckFields tc d fs name (+ i 1) (+ total (wordCodeBits code))
            (if (&& (== legacy 1) (&& (== i 0) (== code 1)))
              1
              0))))
    }))

; 1 is a bare `Int`, the handle form. Any other accepted code stores
; its width in the low 7 bits and sets bit 8 when the field is unsigned.
(pub :: wordWidthCode (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (wordWidthCode tc ty)
  (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
    0
    (wordWidthCodeNode tc (tyExpandAliases tc ty 0))))

(:: wordWidthCodeNode (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(fn (wordWidthCodeNode tc t)
  (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
    0
    (if (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0)
      (wordScalarCode (nodeAName t))
      (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName t) "Link") (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 2))
        (wordLinkWidthCode tc (vecGet (nodeBChildren t) 1))
        0))))

(:: wordScalarCode (-> String Int))
(fn (wordScalarCode name)
  (if (strEq name "Int")
    1
    (if (strEq name "Int8")
      8
      (if (strEq name "Int16")
        16
        (if (strEq name "Int32")
          32
          (if (strEq name "Int64")
            64
            (if (strEq name "UInt8")
              (+ 8 256)
              (if (strEq name "UInt16")
                (+ 16 256)
                (if (strEq name "UInt32")
                  (+ 32 256)
                  (if (strEq name "UInt64")
                    (+ 64 256)
                    0))))))))))

(:: wordLinkWidthCode (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(fn (wordLinkWidthCode tc ty)
  {
    (let w (wordWidthCode tc ty))
    (if (== (& w 256) 0)
      0
      (if (> (& w 127) 32)
        0
        w))
  })

(pub :: wordCodeBits (-> Int Int))
(pub fn (wordCodeBits code)
  (if (== code 1)
    64
    (& code 127)))

(:: wordCodeUnsigned (-> Int Int))
(fn (wordCodeUnsigned code)
  (if (== code 1)
    0
    (if (!= (& code 256) 0)
      1
      0)))

(:: wordBin (-> String ASTNode ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(fn (wordBin op left right span)
  (mkEApp (mkEApp (mkEVarAt op span) left) right))

(:: wordMask (-> Int Int))
(fn (wordMask bits)
  (if (== bits 8)
    255
    (if (== bits 16)
      65535
      (if (== bits 32)
        4294967295
        1))))

; The field's low `bits`, then shifted up to `shift`. A 64-bit field
; is the whole word.
(:: wordPlace (-> ASTNode Int Int Int ASTNode))
(fn (wordPlace value bits shift span)
  {
    (let masked
      (if (== bits 64)
        value
        (wordBin "&" value (nodeBuildEInt (wordMask bits) 0 0 span) span)))
    (if (== shift 0)
      masked
      (wordBin "<<" masked (nodeBuildEInt shift 0 0 span) span))
  })

(pub :: wordPackExpr (-> TC StructEnt Nodes Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (wordPackExpr tc ent args span)
  {
    (let fields (structEntFields ent))
    (let mut acc (nodeBuildEInt 0 0 0 span))
    (let mut shift 0)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen fields))
        {
          (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (nodeTyChild (vecGet fields i)) 0))
          (let code (wordWidthCodeNode tc ty))
          (let bits (wordCodeBits code))
          (let value (vecGet args i))
          ; Packing narrows a link just as a heap field store does.
          ; Check the original value before masking, evaluating it once.
          (let checked
            (if (> (linkWidthCode ty) 0)
              (setNodeTyChild
                (nodeBuildESubcheck value
                  (vecPush (vecPush nodesNew (nodeBuildEInt 0 0 0 span))
                    (nodeBuildEInt (+ (wordMask bits) 1) 0 0 span))
                  (nodeBuildEStr "\"axiom: link does not fit its field\\n\"" 0 0 span) span)
                ty)
              value))
          (let piece (wordPlace checked bits shift span))
          {
            (set acc (if (== i 0)
              piece
              (wordBin "|" acc piece span)))
            (set shift (+ shift bits))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          }
        })
      acc
    }
  })

; Sign-extend a signed field. An unsigned field is a shift and a mask.
(:: wordExtractBits (-> ASTNode Int Int Int Int ASTNode))
(fn (wordExtractBits recv shift bits unsigned span)
  (if (== bits 64)
    recv
    (if (== unsigned 1)
      (wordBin "&" (wordBin ">>" recv (nodeBuildEInt shift 0 0 span) span)
        (nodeBuildEInt (wordMask bits) 0 0 span) span)
      (wordBin ">>" (wordBin "<<" recv (nodeBuildEInt (- 64 (+ shift bits)) 0 0 span) span)
        (nodeBuildEInt (- 64 bits) 0 0 span) span))))

(pub :: wordExtractExpr (-> TC StructEnt String ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (wordExtractExpr tc ent fname recv span)
  {
    (let fields (structEntFields ent))
    (let mut shift 0)
    (let mut found recv)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen fields))
        {
          (let f (vecGet fields i))
          (let code (wordWidthCode tc (nodeTyChild f)))
          (let bits (wordCodeBits code))
          {
            (if (strEq (nodeAName f) fname)
              (set found (wordExtractBits recv shift bits (wordCodeUnsigned code) span)))
            (set shift (+ shift bits))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          }
        })
      found
    }
  })

(pub :: emitStructMarkerDiag (-> TC Int String String String Int))
(pub fn (emitStructMarkerDiag tc span msg help note)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3084" "struct-marker" span msg None (Some help) note)
          "refused here"))
      0
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The seal, AX3085 and AX3086: word structs and sealed heap structs
; keep their constructor and fields inside their declaring module.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: emitWordSealedBuild (-> TC (Option StructEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (emitWordSealedBuild tc ent span)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    (if (== span 0)
      0
      {
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3085" "word-struct-sealed" span
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat (concat "only module `" (wordEntModuleName entry)) "` can make a `")
                  (structEntName entry))
                "`")
              None
              (Some
                (concat (concat "get one from a function module `" (wordEntModuleName entry))
                  (if (!= (& (structEntRepr entry) 1) 0)
                    "` provides: a word struct's constructor stays in its module, `pub` or not"
                    "` provides: a sealed struct's constructor stays in its module, `pub` or not")))
              (if (!= (& (structEntRepr entry) 1) 0)
                "a word struct is sealed, so every value of its type is one its module made and no other code can forge one (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-10a)"
                "a sealed struct keeps ownership state private; use its module's functions (docs/reference.md Structs)"))
            "built outside its module"))
        0
      })
    0))

(pub :: emitWordSealedRead (-> TC (Option StructEnt) String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitWordSealedRead tc ent field span)
  (emitWordSealedAccess tc ent field span "read"))

(:: emitWordSealedAccess (-> TC (Option StructEnt) String Int String Int))
(fn (emitWordSealedAccess tc ent field span operation)
  (if (let (Some entry) ent)
    (if (== span 0)
      0
      {
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3086" "word-struct-sealed-field" span
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat
                    (concat
                      (concat (concat "only module `" (wordEntModuleName entry))
                        (concat "` can " (concat operation " field `")))
                      field)
                    "` of `")
                  (structEntName entry))
                "`")
              None
              (Some
                (concat (concat "use the functions module `" (wordEntModuleName entry))
                  (if (!= (& (structEntRepr entry) 1) 0)
                    "` provides: a word struct's field stays in its module, `pub` or not"
                    "` provides: a sealed struct's field stays in its module, `pub` or not")))
              (if (!= (& (structEntRepr entry) 1) 0)
                "a word struct is sealed: what its word means is its module's business, and code that read it could hand the same word to something else (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-10a)"
                "a sealed struct keeps ownership state private; use its module's functions (docs/reference.md Structs)"))
            (concat operation " outside its module")))
        0
      })
    0))

; The declaring module's name, for a message: its dotted path, or
; `main` for the entry file.
(pub :: wordEntModuleName (-> StructEnt String))
(pub fn (wordEntModuleName ent)
  (match ent.module
    ((None) "the entry file")
    ((Some name) name)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The lowering. A word struct's constructor and field read are the
; identity on one word, and the checker is the one phase that knows
; which applications and field reads those are. So each one it accepts
; is recorded here - the site and its operand - and `wordLowerApply`
; copies the operand's node over the site's once every pass has run.
; What reaches the emitter is the word expression itself: no
; constructor application, no field read, nothing for its thirty-six
; `lookupType` readers to mistake for a block. Codegen knows only that
; the type's NAME is a scalar (`fldClass`).
;
; A node copy, not a wrapper: an AST node is twelve words and every
; consumer reads it by index, so the site becomes its operand in every
; respect - tag, children, span and the checker's stamps. Children are
; shared, which is safe because nothing walks the tree as a graph.
; Recorded in post-order - an operand is checked, and so recorded,
; before the site that holds it - which is the order the copies need:
; `(Chan (Chan2 w))`'s inner site is already `w` when the outer copies
; it.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: wordLowerRecord (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (wordLowerRecord tc site operand)
  {
    (let v (tcWordLower tc))
    {
      (vecPush v site)
      (vecPush v operand)
      0
    }
  })

(pub :: wordLowerApply (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (wordLowerApply tc)
  {
    (let v (tcWordLower tc))
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< (+ i 1) (vecLen v))
        {
          (nodeCopyInto (vecGet v i) (vecGet v (+ i 1)))
          (set i (+ i 2))
        })
      0
    }
  })

; Every one of an AST node's twelve words, from `src` over `dst`.
(pub :: nodeCopyInto (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (nodeCopyInto dst src)
  (if (nodeSame dst src)
    0
    {
      (for k in 0..12
        (nodeCopyWord dst src k))
      0
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Type constructors named in a SIGNATURE.
;
; `parseTypeAtom` turns every capitalised name that is not one of
; `typeKeywordKnown`'s primitives into a bare `TAG_T_CON`, and nothing
; ever asked whether it named anything (`parser.ax:1261-1266`). So
;
;   (:: g (-> Nonexistent Int))
;   (fn (g x) 1)
;   (fn (main) (g 5))
;
; was accepted, and the diagnostic that eventually arrived was an
; AX3004 at the CALLER - blaming the caller's `Int` for the declarer's
; typo. AX3002 had exactly one construction site in this file and it
; was the `(struct Name ...)` expression, so no signature, annotation
; or field type had ever been resolved against anything.
;
; Type VARIABLES need no check: `lexStartsLower` sends every
; lowercase-initial name to `TAG_T_VAR` before this can see it. So the
; subject here is exactly the capitalised names.
;
; Anchoring: a type node carries NO span - `mkTCon` is `mkNode`, not
; `mkNodeAt` - so the diagnostic sits on the signature's own name span,
; and only the FIRST unknown constructor in a signature is reported.
; Pointing every one of them at that same span would print the same
; caret twice for `(-> Nope Nope)`, and this repository's rule for a
; shape it kept no span for is to say less rather than to mis-point.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: tcAliasFind (-> TC String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (tcAliasFind tc name)
  (if (typeKeywordKnown name)
    None
    (if (let (Some alias) (tyFindSyntax tc TY_ALIAS name 0))
      (if (isSome (modOf name))
        (Some alias)
        {
          (let here (tcCurModule tc))
          (let owner (nodeModuleOpt alias))
          (if (== (typeModuleSame here owner) 1)
            (Some alias)
            (if (== (typeNominalOwned tc name here 0) 1)
              None
              (if (isNone owner)
                (Some alias)
                (if (&& (isNone here) (== (typeNominalOwned tc name None 0) 1))
                  None
                  (Some alias)))))
        })
      None)))

; Aliases share the type namespace with nominal types. The declaration
; vector is available before field expansion registers data and structs,
; so it also resolves shadowing during that earlier pass.
(:: typeModuleSame (-> (Option String) (Option String) Int))
(fn (typeModuleSame a b)
  (match a
    ((None)
      (if (isNone b)
        1
        0)
    )
    ((Some left)
      (if (let (Some right) b)
        (if (strEq left right)
          1
          0)
        0)
    )))

(:: typeNominalOwned (-> TC String (Option String) Int Int))
(fn (typeNominalOwned tc name owner i)
  {
    (let decls (tcAllDeclsVec tc))
    (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
      0
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (let tag (nodeTag d))
        (if (&& (|| (== tag TAG_D_DATA) (|| (== tag TAG_D_STRUCT) (== tag TAG_D_SUBTYPE)))
              (&& (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName d)) (bareOf name))
                (== (typeModuleSame (nodeModuleOpt d) owner) 1)))
          1
          (typeNominalOwned tc name owner (+ i 1)))
      })
  })

; Existence, deliberately IGNORING visibility and the referencing
; module: reaching a type another module keeps private is AX3023's
; subject, answering AX3002 for it would say "no such type" about one
; that exists, and a name two modules declare is AX3044's subject and
; not "undefined".
;
; An ALIAS name counts as known. An alias is a declaration; saying
; "undefined type" about it would be false.
;
; It is also EXPANDED now - see `tcExpandSigAliases`. The note that
; used to stand here said expansion was a larger slice because
; `parseSigDecl` stores per-arrow float flags in the node's `b` slot,
; reconstructed at PARSE time from the tokens because float-ness is
; not observable at runtime, and codegen keys on that exact shape - so
; expanding `(type F Float)` in the checker alone would have the
; checker say `Float` where the emitter still says `Int`. That was
; right, and it is why expansion rewrites the FLAGS as well as the
; type.
(pub :: tyConKnown (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (tyConKnown tc name)
  (if (typeKeywordKnown name)
    1
    (if (== (tyIdxHas tc TY_ANY name) 1)
      1
      0)))

; The first unknown type constructor in `t`, or None when every
; constructor is known. A known head with an unknown argument still
; reports that argument, so `(Box Nope)` reports `Nope`.
(pub :: tyUnknownCon (-> TC ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (tyUnknownCon tc t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    None
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      (if (== (tyConKnown tc (nodeAName t)) 0)
        (Some (nodeAName t))
        (tyUnknownConVec tc (nodeBChildren t) 0))
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
        {
          (let first (tyUnknownCon tc (nodeAChild t)))
          (if (let (Some name) first)
            (Some name)
            (tyUnknownCon tc (nodeBChild t)))
        }
        (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_TUP)
          (tyUnknownConVec tc (nodeAChildren t) 0)
          (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_PTR)
            (tyUnknownCon tc (nodeAChild t))
            None))))))

; The help for an unknown type name: migration advice for the five
; removed builtins and for a `*` glued to a name, the generic
; sentence for anything else. `Integer` was the old spelling of
; `Int`; `Unit` and `Void` were the empty tuple, which is `()`;
; `Any` was the top type Axiom does not have, and a lowercase name
; generalises the way one was written for. `Linear` was the
; constructor `linear T` built, which outlived the keyword's refusal
; (AX2004) and enforced nothing. `*` is an identifier character, so
; `*Int` lexes as one name rather than a pointer.
(pub :: tyRemovedBuiltinHelp (-> String String))
(pub fn (tyRemovedBuiltinHelp n)
  (if (strEq n "Integer")
      "write `Int`: `Integer` is not a builtin type"
    (strEq n "Unit")
      "write `()`: `Unit` is not a builtin type"
    (strEq n "Void")
      "write `()`: `Void` is not a builtin type"
    (strEq n "Any")
      "`Any` is not a builtin type; a lowercase name in type position is a type variable"
    (strEq n "Linear")
      "linear types were removed and `Linear` is not a type: write the wrapped type itself"
    (strStartsWith n "*")
      "spell a pointer `(* T)`: `*` joins the name without the space"
    "check for typos, or a missing `data`/`struct`/`type` declaration"))

(pub :: tyUnknownConVec (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (tyUnknownConVec tc xs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    None
    (if (let (Some name) (tyUnknownCon tc (vecGet xs i)))
      (Some name)
      (tyUnknownConVec tc xs (+ i 1)))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `type` aliases, expanded.
;
; `docs/reference.md` says an alias "does not create a new type -
; `StringList` and `[String]` are interchangeable". The compiler
; answered two different ways depending on POSITION:
;
;   (type Count Int)
;   (:: h (-> Count Int))  (fn (h c) c)
;   (h 42)                     AX3004: expected Count, found Int
;   (:: mkc (-> Int Count)) (fn (mkc n) n)
;   (h (mkc 42))               exit 0, prints 42
;
; because argument checking compares constructor NAMES through
; `tyCompat` while the declared-return check names only `Bool` and
; `Float`. `tcAliases` was populated at collection and read by
; `symbols` and by nobody else, so the declaration existed and was
; never expanded.
;
; Expansion happens once, over the signature, and writes THREE places
; so no reader is left with the unexpanded spelling: the declaration
; node's type slot, its float-flag slot, and the `FnEnt`'s type - which
; is what `checkVar` hands a call site, what `bindFnParams` peels for
; the parameters and what `checkDeclaredReturn` compares the body
; against. One write per reader; the positions cannot disagree again
; because there is nothing left for them to disagree about.
;
; The FLAGS are the half that makes this sound rather than a swapped
; defect. They are one bit per arrow position - is it a bare `Float` -
; and codegen reads them because by emission time there is no checker
; to ask. Recomputing them from the expanded NODE reproduces
; `sigFloatFlags` at the definition's arity plus its result: an alias
; can hide the entire arrow spine from the parser. Position k is the
; k-th parameter and the last position is the declared result. A
; function returning another function keeps that result as one word.
;
; Alias expansion follows a declaration path. A repeated declaration
; is recursive; a long finite chain remains valid.
(pub :: tyHasAliasCon (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyHasAliasCon tc t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        (if (|| (isSome (tcAliasFind tc (nodeAName t)))
              (! (strEq (tyNominalName tc (nodeAName t)) (nodeAName t))))
          1
          (tyHasAliasConVec tc (nodeBChildren t) 0))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          (if (== (tyHasAliasCon tc (nodeAChild t)) 1)
            1
            (tyHasAliasCon tc (nodeBChild t)))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (tyHasAliasConVec tc (nodeAChildren t) 0)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
              (tyHasAliasCon tc (nodeAChild t))
              0))))
    }))

(pub :: tyHasAliasConVec (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tyHasAliasConVec tc xs i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      0
      (if (== (tyHasAliasCon tc (vecGet xs i)) 1)
        1
        (tyHasAliasConVec tc xs (+ i 1))))))

(pub :: tyExpandAliases (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyExpandAliases tc t depth)
  ; Canonical types already have the required names. Keep their tree:
  ; rebuilding it at each use retains redundant nodes and argument vectors.
  (if (== (tyHasAliasCon tc t) 0)
    t
    (tyExpandAliasPath tc t vecNew)))

; Expand arguments in the caller's scope, the target in the alias's
; scope, then substitute once. Inserted arguments are not revisited,
; so swapping `a` and `b` cannot capture either source variable.
(pub :: tyExpandAliasPath (-> TC ASTNode Nodes ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyExpandAliasPath tc t seen)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        {
          (let args (tyExpandAliasPathVec tc (nodeBChildren t) seen vecNew 0))
          (let al
            (if (== (tySourceAmbiguous tc (nodeAName t)) 1)
              None
              (tcAliasFind tc (nodeAName t))))
          (if (let (Some al) al)
            (if (|| (== (aliasPathHas seen al 0) 1) (!= (vecLen args) (aliasParamCount al)))
              mkTErr
              {
                (let saved (tcCurModule tc))
                (let next (vecAppendFrom seen vecNew 0))
                {
                  (vecPush next al)
                  (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt al))
                  {
                    (let target (tyExpandAliasPath tc (nodeBChild al) next))
                    {
                      (tcSetCurModule tc saved)
                      (tyEnvSubst target (aliasSubstEnv (nodeCNames al) args vecNew 0))
                    }
                  }
                }
              })
            (mkTCon (tyNominalName tc (nodeAName t)) args))
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          (mkTArr (tyExpandAliasPath tc (nodeAChild t) seen)
            (tyExpandAliasPath tc (nodeBChild t) seen))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (mkTTup (tyExpandAliasPathVec tc (nodeAChildren t) seen vecNew 0))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
              (mkTPtr (tyExpandAliasPath tc (nodeAChild t) seen))
              t))))
    }))

(pub :: aliasParamCount (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (aliasParamCount al)
  (if (== (nodeC al) 0)
    0
    (vecLen (nodeCNames al))))

(pub :: aliasSubstEnv (-> Names Nodes TypeEnv Int TypeEnv))
(pub fn (aliasSubstEnv names args env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    env
    {
      (vecPush env (TypeBinding :name (vecGet names i) :ty (vecGet args i)))
      (aliasSubstEnv names args env (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: aliasPathHas (-> Nodes ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (aliasPathHas seen al i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen seen))
    0
    (if (nodeSame (vecGet seen i) al)
      1
      (aliasPathHas seen al (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tyExpandAliasPathVec (-> TC Nodes Nodes Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (tyExpandAliasPathVec tc xs seen acc i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      acc
      {
        (vecPush acc (tyExpandAliasPath tc (vecGet xs i) seen))
        (tyExpandAliasPathVec tc xs seen acc (+ i 1))
      })))

; 1 when this type is a bare `Float`, which is the only thing a float
; flag is ever set for - an applied constructor, an arrow, a tuple, a
; list or a pointer is a word.
(pub :: tyIsBareFloat (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsBareFloat t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      0
      (if (!= (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0)
        0
        (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "Float")
          1
          0)))))

; One flag per emitted parameter and one for its result. An alias
; can hide arrows from the parser, so the definition supplies the arity.
(pub :: tyFloatFlags (-> ASTNode Int Ints))
(pub fn (tyFloatFlags t n)
  (tyFloatFlagsFrom t n 0 vecNew))

(pub :: tyFloatFlagsFrom (-> ASTNode Int Int Ints Ints))
(pub fn (tyFloatFlagsFrom t n i acc)
  (if (>= i n)
    acc
    (if (== (+ i 1) n)
      {
        (vecPush acc (tyIsBareFloat t))
        acc
      }
      (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
        ; An invalid signature can run out of arrows. Stop rather
        ; than read the parameter slot of a non-arrow.
        acc
        {
          (vecPush acc (tyIsBareFloat (nodeAChild t)))
          (tyFloatFlagsFrom (nodeBChild t) n (+ i 1) acc)
        }))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The same expansion, over RECORD FIELDS.
;
; `tcExpandSigAliases` above expands a `type` alias everywhere a
; SIGNATURE can name one. A struct field is a signature position too,
; and it was not in that sweep, so an alias written there stayed
; nominal - the exact defect `tests/selfhost/973-type-alias.ax` records
; for argument position, surviving in the one position that fix did not
; reach. Two things went wrong at once, and the quiet one is the
; expensive one:
;
;   (type NID Int)
;   (pub struct Aliased (nid : NID) (name : String))
;   (Aliased 1 s)            AX3004: expected NID, found Int
;
; and, once a `cast` gets past that, `fldClass` in the emitter answers
; 1 - unclassifiable - for the alias name, because it is neither a
; scalar nor a known `data`/`struct`. One unclassifiable field forces
; the WHOLE block to a leaf, and a leaf carries no reference map, so
; the `String` beside it is never walked when the block dies. Measured
; on 1,000 build-and-drop iterations: the `Int` spelling moves the
; allocator's bump 368 bytes, the `NID` spelling moves it 112,208.
; The field that leaks is not the field that was misspelled.
;
; Expanding here rather than in `fldClass` keeps the emitter's rule
; that a type name means what it says: by the time codegen runs there
; is no alias left to resolve, which is the same reason the signature
; half writes the node instead of teaching every reader about aliases.
; The alias list, ahead of every other kind of collection. `tcCollect`
; built it on the way past, which was fine while only signatures read
; it - a signature is checked in a later pass either way - and is not
; fine now that record fields are expanded before collection: a struct
; declared ABOVE its alias would have been expanded against a list that
; did not contain it yet. Declaration order in the source is not an
; ordering constraint on the types it mentions, anywhere else in this
; compiler, and it is not one here.
(pub :: tcCollectAliasDecls (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectAliasDecls tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_ALIAS)
          {
            (vecPush (tcAliasesVec tc) d)
            (tyIdxPut tc (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d) (TyAlias d))
          })
      }
      (tcCollectAliasDecls tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; Validate every alias, including unused declarations, before any
; field or signature is expanded. Invalid targets become poison, so
; their uses do not repeat the declaration's error.
(pub :: tcCheckAliasDecls (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckAliasDecls tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_ALIAS)
          {
            (let saved (tcCurModule tc))
            (let params (nodeCNames d))
            {
              (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
              (if (let (Some badParam) (aliasBadParam params 0))
                {
                  (emitTypeParameter tc d
                    (concat "invalid or repeated type parameter `"
                      (concat (vecGetStr params badParam) "`")))
                  (writeNodeChild d 2 mkTErr)
                }
                {
                  (let freeFound (aliasFreeVar (nodeBChild d) params))
                  (if (let (Some free) freeFound)
                    {
                      (emitTypeParameter tc d
                        (concat "undeclared type parameter `" (concat (nodeAName free) "`")))
                      (writeNodeChild d 2 mkTErr)
                    })
                })
              (if (!= (nodeTag (nodeBChild d)) TAG_T_ERR)
                (if (let (Some unused) (aliasUnusedParam (nodeBChild d) params))
                  {
                    (emitTypeParameter tc d
                      (concat "unused alias type parameter `"
                        (concat (vecGetStr params unused) "`")))
                    (writeNodeChild d 2 mkTErr)
                  }))
              (if (== (tyCheckAliasArity tc (nodeBChild d) (nodeSpan d)) 0)
                (writeNodeChild d 2 mkTErr))
              (tcSetCurModule tc saved)
            }
          })
      }
      (tcCheckAliasDecls tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: aliasBadParam (-> Names Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (aliasBadParam params i)
  (if (== (as params Int) 0)
    None
    (if (>= i (vecLen params))
      None
      {
        (let name (vecGetStr params i))
        (if (|| (== (lexStartsLower name) 0) (== (aliasParamBefore params name i 0) 1))
          (Some i)
          (aliasBadParam params (+ i 1)))
      })))

(pub :: aliasParamBefore (-> Names String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (aliasParamBefore params name end i)
  (if (>= i end)
    0
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr params i) name)
      1
      (aliasParamBefore params name end (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: aliasUnusedParam (-> ASTNode Names (Option Int)))
(pub fn (aliasUnusedParam target params)
  {
    (let used vecNew)
    {
      (sigTyVarsInto target used)
      (aliasFirstUnused params used 0)
    }
  })

(pub :: aliasFirstUnused (-> Names Names Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (aliasFirstUnused params used i)
  (if (== (as params Int) 0)
    None
    (if (>= i (vecLen params))
      None
      (if (== (strVecHas used (vecGetStr params i)) 0)
        (Some i)
        (aliasFirstUnused params used (+ i 1))))))

(pub :: aliasFreeVar (-> ASTNode Names (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (aliasFreeVar t params)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    None
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "")
          None
          (if (== (as params Int) 0)
            (Some t)
            (if (== (strInVec params (nodeAName t) 0) 1)
              None
              (Some t))))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (aliasFreeVarVec (nodeBChildren t) params 0)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            {
              (let badFound (aliasFreeVar (nodeAChild t) params))
              (if (let (Some bad) badFound)
                (Some bad)
                (aliasFreeVar (nodeBChild t) params))
            }
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (aliasFreeVarVec (nodeAChildren t) params 0)
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (aliasFreeVar (nodeAChild t) params)
                None)))))
    }))

(pub :: aliasFreeVarVec (-> Nodes Names Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (aliasFreeVarVec xs params i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    None
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      None
      (if (let (Some bad) (aliasFreeVar (vecGet xs i) params))
        (Some bad)
        (aliasFreeVarVec xs params (+ i 1))))))

(pub :: emitTypeParameter (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitTypeParameter tc d message)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3125" "type-parameter" (nodeSpan d) message None
        (Some
          "declare each lowercase type parameter once on the type; every variable in its target or fields must be declared there")))))

; The first recursive alias on a declaration path, or None. Arguments
; are walked before entering the target; `Id (Id Int)` is finite.
(pub :: aliasCycle (-> TC ASTNode Nodes (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (aliasCycle tc t seen)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    None
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        {
          (let alias (tcAliasFind tc (nodeAName t)))
          (if (if (let (Some alias) alias)
            (== (nodeTag (nodeBChild alias)) TAG_T_ERR)
            false)
            None
            {
              (let argCycle (aliasCycleVec tc (nodeBChildren t) seen 0))
              (if (let (Some argCycle) argCycle)
                (Some argCycle)
                {
                  (let al (tcAliasFind tc (nodeAName t)))
                  (if (let (Some al) al)
                    (if (== (aliasPathHas seen al 0) 1)
                      (Some al)
                      {
                        (let saved (tcCurModule tc))
                        (let next (vecAppendFrom seen vecNew 0))
                        {
                          (vecPush next al)
                          (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt al))
                          {
                            (let bad (aliasCycle tc (nodeBChild al) next))
                            {
                              (tcSetCurModule tc saved)
                              bad
                            }
                          }
                        }
                      })
                    None)
                })
            })
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          {
            (let bad (aliasCycle tc (nodeAChild t) seen))
            (if (let (Some bad) bad)
              (Some bad)
              (aliasCycle tc (nodeBChild t) seen))
          }
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (aliasCycleVec tc (nodeAChildren t) seen 0)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
              (aliasCycle tc (nodeAChild t) seen)
              None))))
    }))

(pub :: aliasCycleVec (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Nodes Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (aliasCycleVec tc xs seen i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    None
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      None
      {
        (let bad (aliasCycle tc (vecGet xs i) seen))
        (if (let (Some bad) bad)
          (Some bad)
          (aliasCycleVec tc xs seen (+ i 1)))
      })))

(pub :: tcCheckAliasCycles (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckAliasCycles tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_ALIAS)
          {
            (let saved (tcCurModule tc))
            (let seen vecNew)
            {
              (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
              (vecPush seen d)
              (if (isSome (aliasCycle tc (nodeBChild d) seen))
                (if (!= (nodeSpan d) 0)
                  (emitDiag tc
                    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3126" "recursive-alias" (nodeSpan d)
                      (concat "type alias `"
                        (concat (bareOf (nodeAName d)) "` expands recursively"))
                      None
                      (Some
                        "use a `data` or `struct` declaration for a recursive type; an alias must expand to a finite existing type")))))
              (tcSetCurModule tc saved)
            }
          })
      }
      (tcCheckAliasCycles tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; Check written uses before expansion erases their alias names.
(pub :: tyCheckAliasArity (-> TC ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (tyCheckAliasArity tc t span)
  (tyCheckArityIn tc t span false))

; A declaration's field types as written, before expansion: only the
; alias heads, which expansion is about to erase. Every other head is
; checked once, after collection (`tcCheckDeclaredTypeUses`), so a
; `(Vec Int Int)` field is one report and not two.
(pub :: tyCheckWrittenAliases (-> TC ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (tyCheckWrittenAliases tc t span)
  (tyCheckArityIn tc t span true))

(pub :: tyCheckArityIn (-> TC ASTNode Int Bool Int))
(pub fn (tyCheckArityIn tc t span aliasesOnly)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    1
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        {
          (let declared
            (:: (if (&& aliasesOnly (isNone (tcAliasFind tc (nodeAName t))))
              None
              (tyDeclaredArity tc (nodeAName t))) (Option Int)))
          (let arityOk
            (if (let (Some arity) declared)
              (if (!= (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) arity)
                {
                  (if (!= span 0)
                    (emitDiag tc
                      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3124" "type-argument-arity" span
                        (concat (concat "type `" (concat (srcSpelling (nodeAName t)) "` takes "))
                          (concat (fmtInt arity)
                            (concat " type argument(s), found "
                              (fmtInt (vecLen (nodeBChildren t))))))
                        None (Some "supply exactly the declared number of type arguments"))))
                  false
                }
                true)
              true))
          (if (! arityOk)
            0
            (if (&& (! aliasesOnly) (== (tyLinkWidthOk tc t span) 0))
              0
              (tyCheckArityVecIn tc (nodeBChildren t) span 0 aliasesOnly)))
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          {
            (let left (tyCheckArityIn tc (nodeAChild t) span aliasesOnly))
            (if (== left 0)
              0
              (tyCheckArityIn tc (nodeBChild t) span aliasesOnly))
          }
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (tyCheckArityVecIn tc (nodeAChildren t) span 0 aliasesOnly)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
              (tyCheckArityIn tc (nodeAChild t) span aliasesOnly)
              1))))
    }))

; AX3145: a link's width is `UInt8`, `UInt16` or `UInt32`, or a type
; variable a caller fills in (MM-VAL-10b). Asked after the arity, so a
; two-argument `Link` is the only shape it reads. An alias of a width
; is the width: field types are expanded before the emitter sizes them.
(pub :: tyLinkWidthOk (-> TC ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (tyLinkWidthOk tc t span)
  (if (|| (! (strEq (nodeAName t) "Link")) (!= (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 2))
    1
    {
      (let w (tyExpandAliases tc (vecGet (nodeBChildren t) 1) 0))
      (if (|| (== (nodeTag w) TAG_T_VAR)
            (&& (== (nodeTag w) TAG_T_CON) (sizedTyIn (nodeAName w) "UInt8 UInt16 UInt32")))
        1
        {
          (if (!= span 0)
            (emitDiag tc
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3145" "link-width" span
                (concat "a link's width is `UInt8`, `UInt16` or `UInt32`, found `"
                  (concat (tyRender w) "`"))
                None
                (Some
                  "write the narrowest of the three that counts the vector's elements plus one: `(Link Node UInt16)` reaches 65,535 elements"))))
          0
        })
    }))

; Declared arity, including empty data types and abstract builtins.
; Unknown constructors are left to AX3002's resolver.
(pub :: tyDeclaredArity (-> TC String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (tyDeclaredArity tc name)
  (if (let (Some alias) (tcAliasFind tc name))
    (Some (aliasParamCount alias))
    (if (let (Some data) (findDataAnyVis tc name))
      (Some (dataEntArity data))
      (if (let (Some record) (findStructAnyVis tc name))
        (Some
          (if (== (as record.tyvars Int) 0)
            0
            (vecLen record.tyvars)))
        (if (|| (typeKeywordKnown name) (isSome (tyFindSyntax tc TY_SUBTYPE name 0)))
          (Some 0)
          None)))))

(pub :: tyCheckAliasArityVec (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (tyCheckAliasArityVec tc xs span i)
  (tyCheckArityVecIn tc xs span i false))

(pub :: tyCheckArityVecIn (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Bool Int))
(pub fn (tyCheckArityVecIn tc xs span i aliasesOnly)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    1
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      1
      (if (== (tyCheckArityIn tc (vecGet xs i) span aliasesOnly) 0)
        0
        (tyCheckArityVecIn tc xs span (+ i 1) aliasesOnly)))))

; Preserve arrow shape while poisoning an invalid type use. Its
; parameters then suppress downstream mismatches at each call.
(pub :: tyPoisonBadArity (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyPoisonBadArity tc t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        {
          (let wrongArity
            (match (tyDeclaredArity tc (nodeAName t))
              ((Some arity) (!= arity (vecLen (nodeBChildren t))))
              ((None) false)))
          (if wrongArity
            mkTErr
            (mkTCon (nodeAName t) (tyPoisonBadArityVec tc (nodeBChildren t) vecNew 0)))
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          (mkTArr (tyPoisonBadArity tc (nodeAChild t)) (tyPoisonBadArity tc (nodeBChild t)))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (mkTTup (tyPoisonBadArityVec tc (nodeAChildren t) vecNew 0))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
              (mkTPtr (tyPoisonBadArity tc (nodeAChild t)))
              t))))
    }))

(pub :: tyPoisonBadArityVec (-> TC Nodes Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (tyPoisonBadArityVec tc xs out i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    out
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      out
      {
        (vecPush out (tyPoisonBadArity tc (vecGet xs i)))
        (tyPoisonBadArityVec tc xs out (+ i 1))
      })))

; Data and struct parameters are explicit binders too. Their fields
; cannot introduce a second, unwitnessed set of type variables.
(pub :: tcCheckTypeParams (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckTypeParams tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (|| (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_DATA) (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_STRUCT))
          {
            (let params (nodeTyNames d))
            (if (let (Some badParam) (aliasBadParam params 0))
              (emitTypeParameter tc d
                (concat "invalid or repeated type parameter `"
                  (concat (vecGetStr params badParam) "`")))
              {
                (let freeFound
                  (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_DATA)
                    (dataFreeVar (nodeBChildren d) params 0)
                    (structFreeVar (nodeBChildren d) params 0)))
                (if (let (Some free) freeFound)
                  (emitTypeParameter tc d
                    (concat "undeclared type parameter `" (concat (nodeAName free) "`"))))
              })
          })
      }
      (tcCheckTypeParams tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: dataFreeVar (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (dataFreeVar ctors params i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    None
    (if (let (Some bad) (aliasFreeVarVec (nodeTyChildren (vecGet ctors i)) params 0))
      (Some bad)
      (dataFreeVar ctors params (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: structFreeVar (-> (Vec ASTNode) Names Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (structFreeVar fields params i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    None
    (if (let (Some bad) (aliasFreeVar (nodeTyChild (vecGet fields i)) params))
      (Some bad)
      (structFreeVar fields params (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tcCheckDeclaredTypeUses (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckDeclaredTypeUses tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (let saved (tcCurModule tc))
        {
          (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
          (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_ALIAS)
            {
              (if (== (checkAmbiguousSigTy tc d (nodeBChild d)) 1)
                (writeNodeChild d 2 mkTErr))
              (tcCheckAliasTargetKnown tc d)
              (if (== (tyCheckAliasArity tc (nodeBChild d) (nodeSpan d)) 0)
                (writeNodeChild d 2 mkTErr))
            }
            (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_DATA)
              (tcCheckDataTypeUses tc (nodeBChildren d) 0)
              (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_STRUCT)
                (tcCheckStructTypeUses tc (nodeBChildren d) 0))))
          (tcSetCurModule tc saved)
        }
      }
      (tcCheckDeclaredTypeUses tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: tcCheckAliasTargetKnown (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckAliasTargetKnown tc d)
  (if (let (Some bad) (tyUnknownCon tc (nodeBChild d)))
    {
      (if (!= (nodeSpan d) 0)
        (emitDiag tc
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3002" "undefined-type" (nodeSpan d)
            (concat "undefined type `" (concat bad "`")) None (Some (tyRemovedBuiltinHelp bad)))))
      (writeNodeChild d 2 mkTErr)
    }
    0))

(pub :: tcCheckDataTypeUses (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckDataTypeUses tc ctors i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    0
    {
      {
        (let c (vecGet ctors i))
        (tyCheckAliasArityVec tc (nodeTyChildren c) (nodeSpan c) 0)
      }
      (tcCheckDataTypeUses tc ctors (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: tcCheckStructTypeUses (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckStructTypeUses tc fields i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    0
    {
      {
        (let f (vecGet fields i))
        (tyCheckAliasArity tc (nodeTyChild f) (nodeSpan f))
      }
      (tcCheckStructTypeUses tc fields (+ i 1))
    }))

; Collect TAG_D_SUBTYPE declarations into tcSubtypes for lookup by name.
; Registered in the type index like aliases so signatures may name
; them; the base/bounds stay on the declaration node itself.
; Constructor names share their module's value namespace. Keep the
; declarations in a typed table so comparisons use names and modules,
; without depending on an AST word offset.
(pub struct CtorDefinition
  (name : String)
  (module : (Option String))
  (span : Int))

(pub :: tcCheckDupCtors (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckDupCtors tc decls i)
  (tcCheckDupCtorsFrom tc decls i vecNew))

(pub :: tcCheckDupCtorsFrom (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec CtorDefinition) Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckDupCtorsFrom tc decls i seen)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_DATA)
        (tcCheckDupCtorsIn tc (nodeBChildren d) (nodeModuleOpt d) 0 seen))
      (tcCheckDupCtorsFrom tc decls (+ i 1) seen)
    }))

(pub :: tcCheckDupCtorsIn (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) (Option String) Int (Vec CtorDefinition) Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckDupCtorsIn tc ctors mod k seen)
  (if (>= k (vecLen ctors))
    0
    {
      (let c (vecGet ctors k))
      (let current (CtorDefinition :name (nodeAName c) :module mod :span (nodeSpan c)))
      (tcFindEarlierCtor tc seen current 0)
      (vecPush seen current)
      (tcCheckDupCtorsIn tc ctors mod (+ k 1) seen)
    }))

(pub :: tcFindEarlierCtor (-> TC (Vec CtorDefinition) CtorDefinition Int Int))
(pub fn (tcFindEarlierCtor tc seen current i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen seen))
    0
    {
      (let prior (vecGet seen i))
      (if (&& (== (tcSameModule prior.module current.module) 1) (strEq prior.name current.name))
        {
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3006" "duplicate-definition" current.span
                (concat (concat "duplicate constructor `" current.name) "`")
                (diagRelated prior.span (concat (concat "`" current.name) "` first declared here"))
                (Some "rename one of the constructors"))
              (concat (concat "`" current.name) "` redeclared here")))
          1
        }
        (tcFindEarlierCtor tc seen current (+ i 1)))
    }))

; An empty or reversed literal range admits no value, and every
; conversion into it then fails at run time. Refuse it where it is
; declared when both bounds are integer literals.
(pub :: tcCheckSubtypeRange (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckSubtypeRange tc d)
  {
    (let bounds (nodeCChildren d))
    (if (|| (== (as bounds Int) 0) (!= (vecLen bounds) 2))
      0
      {
        (let lo (vecGet bounds 0))
        (let hi (vecGet bounds 1))
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag lo) TAG_E_INT) (== (nodeTag hi) TAG_E_INT))
          (if (>= (nodeA lo) (nodeA hi))
            (emitDiag tc
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" (nodeSpan d)
                "this subtype range is empty: its lower bound is not below its upper bound" None
                (Some "give the range a lower bound below its upper bound")))
            0)
          0)
      })
  })

(pub :: tcCollectSubtypeDecls (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCollectSubtypeDecls tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SUBTYPE)
          {
            (vecPush (tcSubtypesVec tc) d)
            (tyIdxPut tc (nodeAName d) (nodeModuleOpt d) (TySubtype d))
            (tcCheckSubtypeRange tc d)
          })
      }
      (tcCollectSubtypeDecls tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: tcExpandFieldAliases (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcExpandFieldAliases tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    ; The declaring module, for the duration of ITS field types.
    ; Alias resolution is module-aware now, and this pass is one
    ; of the two that walk imported declarations with no function
    ; body around them to have set word 16 already - so without
    ; this a module's own `(type Amt Float)` would lose to
    ; another module's `Amt` in that module's own struct.
    ; A struct's fields are TAG_FIELD nodes carrying the type in
    ; their `ty` slot; a `data`'s are one type VECTOR per
    ; constructor, in the constructor node's. Two shapes, one
    ; rule.
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        {
          (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
          (let tg (nodeTag d))
          {
            (if (== tg TAG_D_STRUCT)
              (tcExpandFieldAliasesOf tc (nodeBChildren d) 0)
              (if (== tg TAG_D_DATA)
                (tcExpandCtorAliases tc (nodeBChildren d) 0)
                (if (== tg TAG_D_SUBTYPE)
                  (writeNodeChild d 2 (tyExpandAliases tc (nodeBChild d) 0))
                  (if (== tg TAG_D_EFFECT)
                    (tcResolveOperationTypes tc (nodeCChildren d) 0)))))
            (tcSetCurModule tc None)
          }
        }
      }
      (tcExpandFieldAliases tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; An operation's inline arrow lives in its field's b slot. Resolve it
; before the operation becomes a callable entry, in the effect module.
(:: tcResolveOperationTypes (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(fn (tcResolveOperationTypes tc fields i)
  (if (|| (== (as fields Int) 0) (>= i (vecLen fields)))
    0
    {
      {
        (let field (vecGet fields i))
        (if (!= (nodeB field) 0)
          (writeNodeChild field 2 (tyExpandAliases tc (nodeBChild field) 0)))
      }
      (tcResolveOperationTypes tc fields (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: tcExpandCtorAliases (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcExpandCtorAliases tc ctors i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    0
    {
      {
        (let c (vecGet ctors i))
        (if (! (== (nodeTy c) 0))
          {
            (tyCheckArityVecIn tc (nodeTyChildren c) (nodeSpan c) 0 true)
            (tcExpandTyVecAliases tc (nodeTyChildren c) 0)
          })
      }
      (tcExpandCtorAliases tc ctors (+ i 1))
    }))

; In place, because the vector a constructor node holds is the one
; `tcCollectCtors` will hand to its `CtorEnt` and to `ctorArrowOf`.
(pub :: tcExpandTyVecAliases (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcExpandTyVecAliases tc tys i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tys))
    0
    {
      {
        (let t (vecGet tys i))
        (if (! (nodeSame t nilNode))
          (if (! (== (tyHasAliasCon tc t) 0))
            (vecSet tys i (tyExpandAliases tc t 0))))
      }
      (tcExpandTyVecAliases tc tys (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: tcExpandFieldAliasesOf (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcExpandFieldAliasesOf tc fields i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    0
    {
      {
        (let f (vecGet fields i))
        (if (! (== (nodeTy f) 0))
          {
            (tyCheckWrittenAliases tc (nodeTyChild f) (nodeSpan f))
            (if (! (== (tyHasAliasCon tc (nodeTyChild f)) 0))
              (setNodeTyChild f (tyExpandAliases tc (nodeTyChild f) 0)))
          })
      }
      (tcExpandFieldAliasesOf tc fields (+ i 1))
    }))

; One pass over the resolved declaration list, after `tcCollect` has
; registered every `data`, `struct` and `type` - a signature may name
; a type declared later in the file, or in another module.
(pub :: tcCheckSigTypes (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckSigTypes tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    ; The declaring module, for the duration of ITS signature.
    ; A module's own `(:: f (-> Config Int))` names ITS `Config`,
    ; and this pass is where that is decided for every signature
    ; in the program.
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        {
          (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
          {
            (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG)
              ; Ambiguity FIRST, before expansion: expansion RESOLVES
              ; an alias name, so a signature naming a type two
              ; modules both alias would have had one of them
              ; substituted in and the collision erased before
              ; anything could report it.
              ;
              ; Then expansion, THEN the unknown-type check:
              ; `tcCheckSigTypeOf` poisons a signature naming an
              ; unknown type, and an alias is a known one.
              {
                (checkAmbiguousSigTy tc d (nodeTyChild d))
                (if (== (tyCheckAliasArity tc (nodeTyChild d) (nodeSpan d)) 0)
                  {
                    (let poisoned (tyPoisonBadArity tc (nodeTyChild d)))
                    (let entFound (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
                    {
                      (setNodeTyChild d poisoned)
                      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                        (fnEntSetTy ent poisoned)
                        ())
                    }
                  })
                (tcExpandSigAliases tc d)
                (tcCheckSigTypeOf tc d)
              }
              ; An `extern` item's type lives in the ITEM, not in the block
              ; node's `ty` slot, so the walk above cannot see it - and until
              ; this arm an extern signature could name any type at all. That
              ; is not a cosmetic hole: the whole point of writing the type
              ; inline is that it is the only description of the boundary the
              ; compiler has, and `axiom-bindgen` emits `Foreign`, `Slice` and
              ; `Outcome` into it.
              (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EXTERN)
                (tcCheckExternTypes tc (nodeCChildren d) 0)))
            (tcSetCurModule tc None)
          }
        }
      }
      (tcCheckSigTypes tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; Every item of an `extern` block, refused by name at its own span.
;
; And refused by SHAPE (AX3036, since 2026-08-22): the boundary is one
; machine word per argument and one back, so an item's signature may
; name only `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, `Char`, `String` and `Foreign`. A
; type variable, a tuple, a list, a function
; parameter, or a declared data type - `(Option Int)`, `(Result a e)`,
; `Handle`, a user struct - has no crossing the emitter could write,
; and used to pass into the signature unremarked, where a caller then
; trusted it. Such values cross as a `Foreign` handle or through the
; generated wrapper `axiom-bindgen` writes (docs/ffi.md).
(pub :: tcCheckExternTypes (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckExternTypes tc items i)
  (if (== (as items Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen items))
      0
      {
        {
          (let f (vecGet items i))
          {
            (let bad (tyUnknownCon tc (nodeBChild f)))
            (if (let (Some bad) bad)
              (if (! (== (nodeSpan f) 0))
                (emitDiag tc
                  (diagSetLabel
                    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3002" "undefined-type" (nodeSpan f)
                      (concat (concat "undefined type `" bad) "`") None
                      (Some
                        "an `extern` item's type is the only description of the boundary the compiler has; declare the type, or use `Foreign` for an opaque handle"))
                    (concat (concat "no type named `" bad) "` is visible here"))))
              {
                (let shape (externTypeRefusal (nodeBChild f) 1))
                (if (let (Some shape) shape)
                  (if (! (== (nodeSpan f) 0))
                    (emitDiag tc
                      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3036" "extern-type" (nodeSpan f)
                        (concat (concat "an `extern` item cannot carry " shape)
                          " across the boundary")
                        None
                        (Some
                          "an `extern` signature names only `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, `Char`, `String` and `Foreign` (one machine word each way); a Rust value of any other shape crosses as a `Foreign` handle, or through the wrapper `axiom-bindgen` generates (a `String` result, a `Result`, an `Option`, an opaque type)")))))
              })
          }
        }
        (tcCheckExternTypes tc items (+ i 1))
      })))

; The first part of an extern signature the boundary cannot carry,
; described in Some, or None when every leaf is a word. `top` is 1 along the
; signature's own arrow spine, where an arrow is the item's arity
; and not a function-typed argument.
; An arrow whose every leaf is a plain WORD - `Int`, `Float` (its
; bits), `Bool`, `Char` - and so a callback Rust calls through the
; record's code word with nothing to own or free. `String` is not
; such a leaf: a String argument or result carries a share, and the
; Rust side has no wrapper that would release it.
(pub :: allIntArrow (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (allIntArrow t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
      (if (== (allIntArrow (nodeAChild t)) 1)
        (allIntArrow (nodeBChild t))
        0)
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
            (&& (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0)
              (|| (strEq (nodeAName t) "Int")
                (|| (strEq (nodeAName t) "Float")
                  (|| (strEq (nodeAName t) "Bool") (strEq (nodeAName t) "Char"))))))
        1
        0))))

(pub :: externTypeRefusal (-> ASTNode Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (externTypeRefusal t top)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    None
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
        (if (== top 1)
          {
            (let a (externTypeRefusal (nodeAChild t) 0))
            (if (let (Some a) a)
              (Some a)
              (externTypeRefusal (nodeBChild t) 1))
          }
          ; a CALLBACK: an arrow whose leaves are all words is a
          ; closure record Rust calls through its code word
          ; (`AxFn1`..`AxFn3`, docs/ffi.md); an arrow with a
          ; String leaf has no crossing
          (if (== (allIntArrow t) 1)
            None
            (Some
              "a function-typed argument (a callback is an arrow over words only - `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, `Char` - such as `(-> Int Float)`)")))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
          (Some (concat (concat "the type variable `" (nodeAName t)) "`"))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
            (Some "a tuple")
            (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
              {
                (let n (nodeAName t))
                (if (||
                      (&& (== (evScalarName n) 1) (|| (== (integerTypeCode n) 0) (strEq n "Int")))
                      (|| (strEq n "String")
                        (|| (strEq n "Foreign") (|| (strEq n "Char") (strEq n "Bool")))))
                  (if (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0)
                    None
                    (Some (concat (concat "`" n) "` applied to type arguments")))
                  (Some (concat (concat "the type `" n) "`")))
              }
              (Some "this type")))))
    }))

(pub :: tcExpandSigAliases (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcExpandSigAliases tc d)
  (if (== (tyHasAliasCon tc (nodeTyChild d)) 0)
    0
    {
      (let ex (tyExpandAliases tc (nodeTyChild d) 0))
      (let entFound (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
      (let n
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (if (let (Some arity) (fnEntParamCount ent))
            (+ arity 1)
            (if (== (nodeB d) 0)
              0
              (vecLen (nodeBWords d))))
          (if (== (nodeB d) 0)
            0
            (vecLen (nodeBWords d)))))
      {
        (setNodeTyChild d ex)
        (if (! (== n 0))
          (writeNodeWords d 2 (tyFloatFlags ex n)))
        {
          (let entFound (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            (fnEntSetTy ent ex)
            0)
        }
      }
    }))

; AX3040: a type variable that appears in the RESULT and in no
; parameter.
;
; That is not parametric polymorphism. The CALLER picks the type,
; nothing witnesses the choice, and the callee cannot have produced a
; value of it - so the signature promises `forall a. ... -> a`, which
; in a sound system is inhabited only by non-termination. Here it is
; inhabited by a machine word, and the promise is an unchecked
; coercion wearing a polymorphic spelling.
;
; It reaches memory unsafety, measured rather than argued:
;
;     (:: conjure (-> Int a))
;     (fn (conjure n) (cast a n))
;     (strWrap (conjure 42) 8)
;
; `check` answers OK and the binary exits 139 - 42 dereferenced as a
; String. The same shape through `vecGet` does the same thing, which is
; how a `Vec` holding an Int reads back as a String.
;
; No declaration in this tree has it. A raw word read is an accessor
; whose declared result says what the word is, and a cast inside it
; says `effect(unsafe)`.
; THE PROMOTION, AND WHAT IT WAITED FOR. This was a WARNING from the
; day it was written, on a stated ground: the rule conflates two
; signatures and only one of them is unsound.
;
;     (:: conjure (-> Int a))     ; body CASTS a word out
;     (:: panic   (-> String a))  ; body NEVER RETURNS
;
; Measured on this tree, 2026-08-25, and the two are indistinguishable
; to everything the compiler said before this change: both check clean
; with the identical AX3040, and then `conjure` exits **139** while
; `panic` runs correctly and exits with the status it chose. A check
; that reports the same thing about a segfault and a correct program
; is not yet a check. Promoting it as it stood would have refused
; `panic`.
;
; SO THE ANALYSIS IS THE PROMOTION'S PREREQUISITE, and in this
; language it is exact rather than approximate. A function whose
; result is a type variable no parameter mentions can only PRODUCE
; that result two ways, because the type system admits no others:
;
;   1. a `cast`, which fabricates the value - unsound;
;   2. a call to another function whose result is also such a variable
;      - which is the same question one level down.
;
; There is no third way. A `while` loop's value is not `a`; a literal
; is not `a`; a parameter is not `a`, since no parameter mentions the
; variable. So the set of such functions splits by a FIXPOINT over
; their tail positions: assume they all diverge, then strike out any
; whose tail can produce a value - a `cast`, a call to a struck-out
; one, or anything that is not a call at all. What survives is a
; cycle of calls that never returns, which is exactly what "inhabited
; only by non-termination" means, and it is honestly typed. What is
; struck out fabricates a value and is refused.
;
; The greatest fixpoint is what makes `panic` work: its tail is its
; own recursive call, which returns only if `panic` returns, which is
; the question being asked. Starting from "diverges" and shrinking
; answers it; starting from "returns" and growing would never admit
; a self-call and would refuse every diverging function there is.
;
; THE SECOND SHAPE, closed 2026-08-25. Everything above asks about the
; RESULT, and that question misses the same unsoundness one level in:
;
;     (:: apply1 (-> (-> a Int) Int))
;     (fn (apply1 f) (f (cast a 42)))
;
; `a` is on a left side, so the rule called it witnessed and reported
; nothing; `check` answered OK and the binary exited 139. The fix is
; `sigSplitPolar` below - the spine split by VARIANCE rather than by
; side - and it feeds a second arm with no fixpoint behind it, because
; a function that fabricates on its way to returning an `Int` has no
; diverging reading to appeal to. Both arms are emitted by `tyvarEmit`
; in declaration order.
; The first result-only type variable of `d`'s signature, or "".
;
; The subtraction is the NEGATIVE set rather than "every variable on a
; left side" (`sigSplitPolar`, below). The two differ on exactly the
; shapes where a left side does not mean the caller supplies a value:
;
;   (:: f (-> (-> a Int) a))   ; `a` is on a left side and STILL
;                              ; unwitnessed - now reported
;   (:: g (-> Int (Holder (-> a Int))))
;                              ; `a` is in the result and witnessed by
;                              ; whoever calls the callback `g` hands
;                              ; back - a false positive, now silent.
;                              ; Spelled with a parameterised `data`
;                              ; because an arrow may not appear as a
;                              ; type ARGUMENT to anything else here
;
; Neither shape occurs in this tree, so the corpus does not move.
(pub :: resultOnlyVarOf (-> ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (resultOnlyVarOf d)
  ; ONLY A BARE RESULT VARIABLE, which is what this rule's own sentence
  ; says: "returns type variable `a`". A variable NESTED in the result
  ; - `(-> Int (Vec a))` - returns a Vec, not an `a`, and there is no
  ; value of type `a` anywhere in an empty container for a `cast` to
  ; have fabricated. The caller still chooses `a` and that is sound:
  ; `None` has had exactly this shape since `Option` was seeded.
  ;
  ; Before this narrowing every polymorphic empty constructor was
  ; refused, which is why `Vec` could not have an element type:
  ; `(pub :: vecNew (Vec a))` drew AX3040 and there was no way to
  ; declare the one function every generic container needs.
  {
    (let r (sigArrowResultTy (nodeTyChild d)))
    (if (nodeSame r nilNode)
      None
      (if (!= (nodeTag r) TAG_T_VAR)
        None
        {
          (let pos vecNew)
          (let neg vecNew)
          (let ps vecNew)
          (let rs vecNew)
          {
            (sigSplitPolar (nodeTyChild d) 1 pos neg)
            (sigSplitVars (nodeTyChild d) ps rs)
            (firstResultOnly neg rs 0)
          }
        }))
  })

; The result of an arrow spine: the first `nodeB` that is not itself an
; arrow, or the type when there is no arrow at all.
(pub :: sigArrowResultTy (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (sigArrowResultTy t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
      (sigArrowResultTy (nodeBChild t))
      t)))

; Spelled `neg` and not `ps`: what is subtracted here has not been "the
; type variables of the parameters" since the spine was split by
; variance. A variable on a left side can still be one the CALLEE
; produces, and one in the result can be witnessed by a function this
; signature hands back.
(pub :: firstResultOnly (-> Names Names Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (firstResultOnly neg rs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rs))
    None
    (if (== (strVecHas neg (vecGet rs i)) 1)
      (firstResultOnly neg rs (+ i 1))
      (Some (vecGet rs i)))))

; The first function declaration for a bare name, when present.
;
; A linear scan. It was asked about the result-only-tyvar signatures
; alone, a single-digit set, until R-B6's forging rule began asking the
; divergence walk about every cast that may forge (`castNoteForge`),
; and each call head there is a scan. The scan compares names in place
; (`bareNameIs`): it built `bareOf` of every declaration it passed,
; which was 11.6% of `check core/main.ax` and most of the 15% that
; check lost that day.
(pub :: findFnDecl (-> Nodes String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (findFnDecl decls name)
  {
    (let n (vecLen decls))
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut hit (:: None (Option ASTNode)))
    (while (< i n)
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN) (== (bareNameIs (nodeAName d) name) 1))
          {
            (set hit (Some d))
            (set i n)
          })
        (set i (+ i 1))
      })
    hit
  })

; `(strEq (bareOf full) name)`, without building the bare name. A
; bare name holds no `$`, so `full` answers to it when it IS `name`, or
; ends in `$` and `name`: the last `$` then sits just before `name`.
(pub :: bareNameIs (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (bareNameIs full name)
  (if (== (nameHasDollar name 0) 1)
    0
    {
      (let m (strLen full))
      (let n (strLen name))
      (if (== m n)
        (strEqAt full 0 name 0)
        (if (< m (+ n 1))
          0
          (if (== (strByte full (- (- m n) 1)) 36)
            (strEqAt full (- m n) name 0)
            0)))
    }))

(pub :: findFnBody (-> Nodes String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (findFnBody decls name)
  (if (let (Some declaration) (findFnDecl decls name))
    {
      (let body (nodeCChild declaration))
      (if (nodeSame body nilNode)
        None
        (Some body))
    }
    None))

; The name at the bottom of an application spine, when its head
; is a plain name. Spelled `divHeadName` and not `spineHeadName`
; because `codegen.ax` already has the latter, and a name two modules
; both declare is `AX3014` - which is how this one introduced itself. `(cast a w)` parses as `((cast a) w)`, so the
; spine bottoms out at the variable `cast` and this answers `"cast"` -
; which is the whole distinction this pass turns on.
(pub :: divHeadName (-> ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (divHeadName e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    None
    (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
      (divHeadName (nodeAChild e))
      (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
        (Some (bareOf (nodeAName e)))
        None))))

(:: divHeadIsCast (-> ASTNode Bool))
(fn (divHeadIsCast expression)
  (if (let (Some name) (divHeadName expression))
    (isValueCastForm name)
    false))

; Does evaluating `e` never yield a value?
;
; The tails of a form are the positions its value can come from: the
; last expression of a block, both arms of an `if`, every arm of a
; `match`, the body of a `let` or a `handle`. Everything else is its
; own tail.
;
; THREE WAYS TO NEVER RETURN, and the third one is what the
; repository's own control fixture needed. A call to `sysExitWith` is
; the base case: it is the exit syscall and there is no path past it.
; A call to a function whose every tail never returns inherits that -
; which is how `IO.exit` and `IO.die` qualify without being named
; here, since one ends in `sysExitWith` and the other ends in the
; first. And a `cast` whose ARGUMENT never returns never returns: the
; coercion is unreachable, so it fabricates nothing.
;
; That third arm is not a nicety. `tests/selfhost/976-diverging-tyvar.ax`
; - the fixture this repository wrote to record that the two shapes
; differ - spells its diverging `panic` as `(cast a (exit 70))`, and a
; rule that looked only at the cast would have refused the program the
; fixture exists to protect. The analysis was wrong and the fixture was
; right.
;
; A form this does not know is NOT a diverging tail, which is the safe
; direction for a check that refuses: it reports rather than accepts,
; and the three ways out are in the help.
(pub :: divTailOk (-> Nodes ASTNode Names Int Int))
(pub fn (divTailOk decls e names depth)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
        ; ANY statement, not only the last. A block whose second
        ; statement never returns never reaches its third, so the
        ; block never returns whatever its tail says. `pLoop` below is
        ; the shape that needs this: an endless `while`, and then a
        ; `cast` after it that no execution arrives at.
        (divAnyOk decls (nodeAChildren e) names depth 0)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
          (if (== (divTailOk decls (nodeBChild e) names depth) 1)
            (divTailOk decls (nodeCChild e) names depth)
            0)
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
            (divTailOk decls (nodeCChild e) names depth)
            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
              (divTailOk decls (nodeAChild e) names depth)
              (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                (divArmsOk decls (nodeBChildren e) names depth 0)
                ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds none, so a
                ; divergingly-tailed one cannot reach this walk.
                (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                  ; A loop whose test cannot become false. Only a
                  ; CONSTANT test is read: `(while true ...)` and
                  ; `(while 1 ...)` are the two spellings of "forever",
                  ; and deciding it for a computed test is the halting
                  ; problem. A loop this cannot prove endless is simply
                  ; not a diverging tail, which is the safe direction.
                  ; A region diverges exactly when its body does.
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                    (divTailOk decls (nodeBChild e) names depth)
                    (divConstTrue (nodeAChild e)))
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
                    (if (divHeadIsCast e)
                      ; The value being coerced. `(cast a x)` parses as
                      ; `((cast a) x)`, so it is this node's argument.
                      (divTailOk decls (nodeBChild e) names depth)
                      (if (let (Some name) (divHeadName e))
                        (divCall decls name names depth)
                        0))
                    0)))))))
    }))

; Does calling `name` never return?
;
; The depth bound is a refusal to recurse forever through a call chain,
; not an approximation of anything: a cycle among the candidate set is
; already answered by `names` (the fixpoint assumes it diverges), and a
; cycle outside it cannot end in `sysExitWith` at all. Eight is deeper
; than `die` -> `exit` -> `sysExitWith` by five.
(pub :: divCall (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Names Int Int))
(pub fn (divCall decls name names depth)
  (if (>= depth 8)
    0
    (if (strEq name "sysExitWith")
      1
      (if (== (strInVec names name 0) 1)
        1
        (if (let (Some body) (findFnBody decls name))
          (divTailOk decls body names (+ depth 1))
          0)))))

; Does ANY expression in `xs` never return?
(pub :: divAnyOk (-> Nodes Nodes Names Int Int Int))
(pub fn (divAnyOk decls xs names depth i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    0
    (if (== (divTailOk decls (vecGet xs i) names depth) 1)
      1
      (divAnyOk decls xs names depth (+ i 1)))))

; Is this test a constant the loop can never falsify?
(pub :: divConstTrue (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (divConstTrue t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_E_VAR)
      (if (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName t)) "true")
        1
        0)
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_E_INT)
        (if (== (nodeA t) 0)
          0
          1)
        0))))

(pub :: divArmsOk (-> Nodes (Vec ASTNode) Names Int Int Int))
(pub fn (divArmsOk decls arms names depth i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    ; No arm at all is not a diverging tail; an empty `match` produces
    ; nothing and is somebody else's diagnostic.
    (if (== i 0)
      0
      1)
    (if (== (divTailOk decls (nodeBChild (vecGet arms i)) names depth) 1)
      (divArmsOk decls arms names depth (+ i 1))
      0)))

; One round of the fixpoint: strike out every name whose body can
; produce a value. Answers the survivors.
(pub :: divRound (-> Nodes Names Names))
(pub fn (divRound decls names)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    (for name in names
      (if (let (Some body) (findFnBody decls name))
        (if (== (divTailOk decls body names 0) 1)
          (vecPush out name))))
    out
  })

; Iterate to a fixed point. Each round can only shrink the set, so it
; terminates in at most `|names|` rounds; this repository reaches it
; in one.
(pub :: divFixpoint (-> Nodes Names Names))
(pub fn (divFixpoint decls names)
  {
    (let next (divRound decls names))
    (if (== (vecLen next) (vecLen names))
      next
      (divFixpoint decls next))
  })

; The pass. Collect, solve, report.
(pub :: tcCheckResultOnly (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckResultOnly tc decls)
  ; No early return on an empty candidate list. There used to be one,
  ; and it was correct while the returned-variable arm was the only
  ; arm; with a second arm that has no candidates of its own it would
  ; have skipped the whole pass in exactly the file this closes -
  ; one whose only unsoundness is a callback parameter. `divFixpoint`
  ; of nothing is nothing, so the guard bought a comparison and cost
  ; a check.
  {
    (let cands (resultOnlyCandidates decls vecNew 0))
    {
      (let div (divFixpoint decls (candNames cands vecNew 0)))
      (tyvarEmit tc decls div 0)
    }
  })

; Every signature with a result-only type variable.
(pub :: resultOnlyCandidates (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (resultOnlyCandidates decls out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    out
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG) (isSome (resultOnlyVarOf d)))
          (vecPush out d))
        (resultOnlyCandidates decls out (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: candNames (-> Nodes Names Int Names))
(pub fn (candNames cands out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cands))
    out
    {
      (vecPush out (bareOf (nodeAName (vecGet cands i))))
      (candNames cands out (+ i 1))
    }))

; ONE sweep, in DECLARATION order, over both arms.
;
; The two arms were two sweeps until they were written: the returned
; variables first, then the demanded ones. Both were internally
; ordered and the concatenation was not, so a file holding one of
; each reported the later declaration first - `353-callback-tyvar.ax`
; showed line 57 above line 40. Nothing sorts diagnostics after the
; fact in this compiler; emission order IS report order, which is why
; the merge happens here rather than in the printer.
(pub :: tyvarEmit (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Names Int Int))
(pub fn (tyvarEmit tc decls div i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG)
          (emitTyvarFor tc decls div d))
        (tyvarEmit tc decls div (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; Both arms of one declaration. They are separate `if`s rather than an
; else-chain because a signature can hold one of each -
; `(:: f (-> (-> a Int) b))` fabricates an `a` for the callback AND a
; `b` for its caller - and those are two mistakes, not one seen twice.
; The sets are disjoint by construction (`firstDemanded` subtracts the
; result's variables), so the common shapes still draw exactly one.
(pub :: emitTyvarFor (-> TC Nodes Names ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (emitTyvarFor tc decls div d)
  {
    (let result (resultOnlyVarOf d))
    (let demanded (demandedVarOf d))
    (if (&& (isNone result) (isNone demanded))
      0
      (if (isNone (findFnBody decls (bareOf (nodeAName d))))
        0
        {
          (let diverges (strInVec div (bareOf (nodeAName d)) 0))
          (if (let (Some variable) result)
            (if (!= diverges 1)
              {
                (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
                (emitResultOnlyVar tc d variable)
              }))
          (if (let (Some variable) demanded)
            (if (if (let (Some returned) result)
              (&& (strEq variable returned) (== diverges 0))
              false)
              0
              {
                (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
                (emitDemandedVar tc d variable)
              })
            0)
        }))
  })

; Walk the arrow spine: every left side is a parameter, the last right
; side is the result.
(pub :: sigSplitVars (-> ASTNode Names Names Int))
(pub fn (sigSplitVars t ps rs)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
      {
        (sigTyVarsInto (nodeAChild t) ps)
        (sigSplitVars (nodeBChild t) ps rs)
      }
      (sigTyVarsInto t rs))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE SAME SIGNATURE, SPLIT BY VARIANCE.
;
; `sigSplitVars` above answers "which side of the spine is this
; variable on", and that is not the question. The question is who
; CHOOSES the type and who has to PRODUCE a value of it, and those
; two come apart the moment a parameter is itself a function:
;
;     (:: apply1 (-> (-> a Int) Int))
;     (fn (apply1 f) (f (cast a 42)))
;     (apply1 strLen)
;
; `a` is on a left side, so `sigSplitVars` files it under "parameter"
; and the result set comes out empty. Nothing was reported, `check`
; answered OK, and the binary exited 139 - 42 handed to `strLen` as a
; String pointer. Measured 2026-08-25 and recorded in `explain.ax` as
; what the rule did not catch; this is the walk that catches it.
;
; `pol` is 1 where the CALLEE must produce a value of the type and 0
; where the CALLER supplies one. A signature starts at 1 - a function
; produces its result. The LEFT of an arrow flips it, the right keeps
; it, and every other constructor carries it through. So in `apply1`
; the parameter `(-> a Int)` sits at 0, and `a`, on ITS left, flips
; back to 1: the callee produces it after all, one level in.
;
; The rule that falls out is one sentence. A type variable with a
; position at `pol` 1 and none at `pol` 0 is UNWITNESSED - nothing the
; caller hands over decides what it is, and the only ways to make one
; are a `cast` or a call to something that does the same.
;
; It is strictly a refinement, not a different rule. Every variable
; `sigSplitVars` called witnessed by a direct parameter is still at
; `pol` 0 here, and the corpus measures the difference at zero: 19
; signatures in this tree nest an arrow, and the four with variables
; in one - `(-> (-> a b) a b)` and `(-> (-> a a) a a)` and their
; copies - mention every variable on both sides and are untouched.
(pub :: sigSplitPolar (-> ASTNode Int Names Names Int))
(pub fn (sigSplitPolar t pol pos neg)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
        {
          (sigSplitPolar (nodeAChild t) (- 1 pol) pos neg)
          (sigSplitPolar (nodeBChild t) pol pos neg)
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
          (if (== pol 1)
            (sigTyVarPush pos t)
            (sigTyVarPush neg t))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
            (sigSplitPolarVec (nodeBChildren t) pol pos neg)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (sigSplitPolarVec (nodeAChildren t) pol pos neg)
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (sigSplitPolar (nodeAChild t) pol pos neg)
                0)))))
    }))

(pub :: sigSplitPolarVec (-> Nodes Int Names Names Int))
(pub fn (sigSplitPolarVec xs pol pos neg)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    0
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
        (sigSplitPolar (vecGet xs i) pol pos neg))
      0
    }))

; One source-written variable into one bucket, deduplicated. The same
; three lines `sigTyVarsInto` spells inline; shared so the two walks
; cannot disagree about what counts as a variable.
(pub :: sigTyVarPush (-> Names ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (sigTyVarPush acc t)
  (if (== (tyIsSrcVar t) 1)
    (if (== (strVecHas acc (nodeAName t)) 1)
      0
      {
        (vecPush acc (nodeAName t))
        0
      })
    0))

; The first unwitnessed variable that is not supplied by a negative position.
;
; Two sets are subtracted, and the second is what keeps this arm and
; the returned-variable arm from reporting one declaration twice: a
; variable in the result is `resultOnlyVarOf`'s, decided by the
; divergence fixpoint, because a function that never returns never
; produced it. That reading is not available here. `apply1` returns
; an `Int` perfectly well; it fabricates on the way, and a body that
; also looped forever would fabricate just the same.
(pub :: firstDemanded (-> Names Names Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (firstDemanded pos neg i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen pos))
    None
    {
      (let variable (vecGet pos i))
      (if (== (strVecHas neg variable) 1)
        (firstDemanded pos neg (+ i 1))
        (Some variable))
    }))

; The positive positions reachable through a PARAMETER: what this
; function must PRODUCE in order to use something it was handed.
;
; It is `sigSplitPolar` started at 0 on each top-level left side, and
; it deliberately does not look at the spine's TAIL. The tail's
; positives are the result, which is the other arm's question and the
; one a divergence fixpoint can answer; these are not.
;
; THE TWO SETS OVERLAP AND THAT OVERLAP IS A DECISION, NOT A
; SUBTRACTION. `(:: f (-> (-> a Int) a))` has `a` in both. The first
; version of this subtracted the result's variables here, which reads
; as "the other arm has it" - and left a hole, because the other arm
; does not always speak:
;
;     (:: divDemand (-> (-> a Int) a))
;     (fn (divDemand f) { (f (cast a 42)) (cast a (exit 70)) })
;     (divDemand strLen)
;
; That DIVERGES, so the result arm is honestly silent - `for all a` is
; the true type of a function that never returns. It still fabricates
; an `a` for `f` on the way there. Measured 2026-08-25: `check` OK,
; exit 139. `emitTyvarFor` decides the overlap instead, and yields only
; when the arm above actually reported the same variable.
(pub :: sigParamPos (-> ASTNode Names Names Int))
(pub fn (sigParamPos t pos neg)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
      {
        (sigSplitPolar (nodeAChild t) 0 pos neg)
        (sigParamPos (nodeBChild t) pos neg)
      }
      0)))

(pub :: demandedVarOf (-> ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (demandedVarOf d)
  {
    (let ppos vecNew)
    (let pneg vecNew)
    (let pos vecNew)
    (let neg vecNew)
    {
      (sigParamPos (nodeTyChild d) ppos pneg)
      ; `pneg` is discarded: the authoritative negative set is the
      ; whole signature's, and it is a superset of the parameters'.
      ; A variable the RESULT supplies negatively is witnessed just as
      ; well as one a parameter does.
      (sigSplitPolar (nodeTyChild d) 1 pos neg)
      (firstDemanded ppos neg 0)
    }
  })

(pub :: emitDemandedVar (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitDemandedVar tc d v)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3040" "result-only-tyvar" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat
          (concat
            (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` must produce a value of type variable `")
            v)
          "`, which no parameter supplies: it occurs inside a function-typed parameter, on the side this function fills in, so the caller chooses the type and an `as` conversion fabricates the value")
        None
        (Some
          "making the function diverge does not answer this one - the value is fabricated before it returns, or fails to; name a concrete word type in the callback's parameter and let callers write `as`")))))

(pub :: emitResultOnlyVar (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitResultOnlyVar tc d v)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3040" "result-only-tyvar" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` returns type variable `") v)
          "`, which no parameter mentions, and its body produces that result rather than never returning: the caller chooses the type and an `as` conversion fabricates the value")
        None
        (Some
          "two ways out: return a concrete word type and let callers write `as`; or make it DIVERGE, so every path ends in a call that never returns, which is what makes `for all a` honest")))))

; Does this declaration carry `;@axiom:<key>` exactly?
(pub :: declHasAxtag (-> ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (declHasAxtag d key)
  (declHasAxtagFrom (nodeAxtagsOf d) key 0))

(pub :: declHasAxtagFrom (-> Axtags String Int Int))
(pub fn (declHasAxtagFrom tags key i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    (if (strEq (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)) key)
      1
      (declHasAxtagFrom tags key (+ i 1)))))

; The first matching tag's value, including empty text, or None.
; Each tag owns its source offset and complete annotation text.
(pub :: axtagValueOf (-> ASTNode String (Option String)))
(pub fn (axtagValueOf d key)
  (axtagValueFrom (nodeAxtagsOf d) key 0))

; A tag's word 1 is the WHOLE text - `deprecated(use vecLen)`, not the
; bare key - which is why `declHasAxtag` works only for a key that
; takes no value and silently answers 0 for any key that does.
; Measured the hard way: the tag reached AXSYM as
; `#deprecated=use%20vecLen` while the lookup here saw nothing.
(:: axtagContent (-> AxtagEnt String))
(fn (axtagContent t)
  t.content)

(pub :: axtagKeyOf (-> AxtagEnt String))
(pub fn (axtagKeyOf t)
  {
    (let txt (axtagContent t))
    (if (let (Some op) (strFindByte txt 40 0))
      (strSlice txt 0 op)
      txt)
  })

; The value runs from the first `(` to the LAST `)` - `axtagCloseAt`,
; the rule `checkOneAxtag`, `symbols.ax`'s `saAxMeta` and `format.ax`'s
; `fpAxtag` all apply. This reader stopped at the FIRST `)` until
; 2026-08-29, so it was the one reader of four that truncated: measured,
; `;@axiom:deprecated(use (f x))` reached AX3048 as `use (f x` - the
; help said "the declaration says: use (f x" and the label quoted a tag
; with one paren fewer than the author wrote. A value with no `)` at
; all still answers "", as it always did.
(pub :: axtagTextValue (-> AxtagEnt String))
(pub fn (axtagTextValue t)
  {
    (let txt (axtagContent t))
    (if (let (Some op) (strFindByte txt 40 0))
      (if (let (Some closing) (axtagCloseAt txt))
        (if (<= closing op)
          ""
          (strSlice txt (+ op 1) (- closing (+ op 1))))
        "")
      "")
  })

(pub :: axtagValueFrom (-> Axtags String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (axtagValueFrom tags key i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    None
    (if (strEq (axtagKeyOf (vecGet tags i)) key)
      (Some (axtagTextValue (vecGet tags i)))
      (axtagValueFrom tags key (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tcCheckSigTypeOf (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckSigTypeOf tc d)
  {
    (let bad (tyUnknownCon tc (nodeTyChild d)))
    (if (let (Some bad) bad)
      {
        (if (! (== (nodeSpan d) 0))
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3002" "undefined-type" (nodeSpan d)
                (concat (concat "undefined type `" bad) "`") None (Some (tyRemovedBuiltinHelp bad)))
              (concat (concat "no type named `" bad) "` is visible here"))))
        {
          (let entFound (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            (fnEntSetTy ent (tyPoisonUnknown tc (nodeTyChild d)))
            0)
        }
      }
      0)
  })

; The same type with every unresolvable constructor replaced by
; poison, the structure kept. `tyCompat` treats poison as compatible
; with everything, so the one AX3002 at the declaration becomes the
; only thing reported: without this a signature naming an undefined
; type drew a further AX3004 at every CALL SITE - measured, one bad
; signature and four calls gave five errors, which is the cascade the
; poison discipline at the top of this file exists to prevent, and
; what the sibling AX3002 site already does by answering `TAG_T_ERR`.
;
; The arrow shape is preserved rather than poisoning the whole type,
; because `peelArrows` and `bindFnParams` read it and a NON-arrow
; signature means "every parameter is Int" to them - so poisoning
; wholesale would quietly retype the parameters instead of excusing
; them.
;
; A fresh tree is built rather than the nodes edited: the declaration's
; own `ty` slot is what `symbols` renders, and only the checker's copy
; in `FnEnt` word 1 should carry poison.
(pub :: tyPoisonUnknown (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyPoisonUnknown tc t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      (if (== (tyConKnown tc (nodeAName t)) 0)
        mkTErr
        (mkTCon (nodeAName t) (tyPoisonUnknownVec tc (nodeBChildren t) vecNew 0)))
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
        (mkTArr (tyPoisonUnknown tc (nodeAChild t)) (tyPoisonUnknown tc (nodeBChild t)))
        (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_TUP)
          (mkTTup (tyPoisonUnknownVec tc (nodeAChildren t) vecNew 0))
          (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_PTR)
            (mkTPtr (tyPoisonUnknown tc (nodeAChild t)))
            t))))))

(pub :: tyPoisonUnknownVec (-> TC Nodes Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (tyPoisonUnknownVec tc xs out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    out
    {
      (vecPush out (tyPoisonUnknown tc (vecGet xs i)))
      (tyPoisonUnknownVec tc xs out (+ i 1))
    }))

; A struct's declared field type, or None when the name is absent. Fields are the
; parser's TAG_FIELD nodes: name in a, parsed type in ty.
(pub :: structFieldTy (-> StructEnt String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (structFieldTy ent fname)
  (structFieldTyFrom (structEntFields ent) fname 0))

(pub :: structFieldTyFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (structFieldTyFrom fields fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    None
    {
      (let f (vecGet fields i))
      (if (strEq (nodeAName f) fname)
        (Some
          (if (== (nodeTy f) 0)
            mkSilentWild
            (nodeTyChild f)))
        (structFieldTyFrom fields fname (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Was the field declared `mut`? 1 yes, 0 no - and 0 for a name the
; struct does not declare at all, which `checkSetField` has already
; refused as AX3007 before it asks. The marker rides in word c of the
; parser's TAG_FIELD node (`parseOneField`).
(pub :: structFieldMut (-> StructEnt String Int))
(pub fn (structFieldMut ent fname)
  (structFieldMutFrom (structEntFields ent) fname 0))

(pub :: structFieldMutFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int))
(pub fn (structFieldMutFrom fields fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    0
    {
      (let f (vecGet fields i))
      (if (strEq (nodeAName f) fname)
        (fieldIsMut f)
        (structFieldMutFrom fields fname (+ i 1)))
    }))

; How many arguments a function's use must supply before it can be a
; value: the declared parameter count when a definition was seen, the
; arrow depth for builtins (their type is the whole truth), and `None`
; for a signature-only name (a one-parameter function
; returning a closure has arrow depth 2 but arity 1, so depth would
; wrongly flag legal code).
(pub :: repArity (-> FnEnt (Option Int)))
(pub fn (repArity ent)
  (if (let (Some arity) (fnEntParamCount ent))
    (Some arity)
    (if (== (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 1)
      (Some (arrowArity (fnEntTy ent)))
      None)))

; Unknown arity is never a known nullary or known positive-arity function.
(pub :: fnEntArityIs (-> FnEnt Int Bool))
(pub fn (fnEntArityIs ent expected)
  (match (fnEntParamCount ent)
    ((Some arity) (== arity expected))
    ((None) false)))

(pub :: fnEntArityPositive (-> FnEnt Bool))
(pub fn (fnEntArityPositive ent)
  (match (fnEntParamCount ent)
    ((Some arity) (> arity 0))
    ((None) false)))

; An unresolved signature may hold a function value; only known arity zero
; proves a bare reference is a nullary call rather than a callable value.
(pub :: fnEntMayTakeArgs (-> FnEnt Bool))
(pub fn (fnEntMayTakeArgs ent)
  (match (fnEntParamCount ent)
    ((Some arity) (!= arity 0))
    ((None) true)))

; A partial-call refusal needs a known definition parameter count.
(pub :: fnEntNeedsArgs (-> FnEnt Int Bool))
(pub fn (fnEntNeedsArgs ent supplied)
  (match (fnEntParamCount ent)
    ((Some arity) (< supplied arity))
    ((None) false)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Scope. One entry per binding: name, mutability, the binder's span
; (0 = unknown, suppresses AX3012's anchor), and now the TYPE.
; Entries below frameBase belong to the module walk (signatures); the
; frame region above it is the current function.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct SEnt
  (name : String)
  (mutable : Int)
  (bsp : Int)
  (ty : ASTNode)
  (mut used : Int))

(pub :: sEntName (-> SEnt String))
(pub fn (sEntName ent)
  ent.name)

(pub :: sEntMutable (-> SEnt Int))
(pub fn (sEntMutable ent)
  ent.mutable)

(pub :: sEntBsp (-> SEnt Int))
(pub fn (sEntBsp ent)
  ent.bsp)

(pub :: sEntUsed (-> SEnt Int))
(pub fn (sEntUsed ent)
  ent.used)

(pub :: pushScope (-> (Vec SEnt) String Int Int ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (pushScope scope name mutFlag bsp ty)
  {
    (vecPush scope (SEnt :name name :mutable mutFlag :bsp bsp :ty ty :used 0))
    0
  })

; AX3094 at a binding - or a top-level declaration, when `isDecl` -
; that takes the name `true` or `false`.
;
; The checker answers those spellings before it consults scope
; (`checkVar`), while the emitter reads a binding in scope before the
; literal (`emitVar`), so a program that binds `true` checks one
; thing and runs another: `(let ((true 0)) (while true ...))` said
; OK and the loop terminated at once, at exit 0. Patterns already
; refuse the other reading - a bare `true` there TESTS the literal
; and binds nothing - so reservation is the one rule left: the names
; are literals everywhere, and bindable nowhere.
(pub :: refuseReservedBinder (-> TC String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (refuseReservedBinder tc name span isDecl)
  (if (|| (strEq name "true") (strEq name "false"))
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3094" "reserved-literal" span
            (concat
              (concat
                (concat "cannot "
                  (if (== isDecl 1)
                    "declare `"
                    "bind `"))
                name)
              "` - it is a boolean literal")
            None (Some "choose another name")
            (if (== isDecl 1)
              "a top-level `true` would be dead: every reference answers the literal before the declaration is consulted"
              "a reference to this name answers the literal before scope is consulted, so the binding would check as `Bool` and run as the bound value"))
          "this name is a boolean literal"))
      1
    }
    0))

; The same refusal over a parameter vector's names, which carry no
; spans of their own: the declaration (or lambda) anchors every
; refusal, the way `checkDupParams` already anchors AX3006.
(pub :: refuseReservedParams (-> TC Names Int Int Int))
(pub fn (refuseReservedParams tc params span i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen params))
    0
    {
      (refuseReservedBinder tc (vecGetStr params i) span 0)
      (refuseReservedParams tc params span (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: sEntTy (-> SEnt ASTNode))
(pub fn (sEntTy ent)
  ent.ty)

; The innermost entry's index, when present. `checkSet` needs the position,
; not just the entry: whether the target sits below the innermost
; lambda's boundary is what decides the capture refusal (MM-MUT-1a).
(pub :: scopeFindIdx (-> (Vec SEnt) String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (scopeFindIdx scope name)
  (scopeFindIdxFrom scope name (- (vecLen scope) 1)))

(pub :: scopeFindIdxFrom (-> (Vec SEnt) String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (scopeFindIdxFrom scope name i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    (if (strEq (sEntName (vecGet scope i)) name)
      (Some i)
      (scopeFindIdxFrom scope name (- i 1)))))

; The innermost FRAME entry - value resolution reads only the current
; function's bindings; module-level names resolve through the
; function table (same types, stage0's route).
(pub :: scopeFindFrame (-> TC String (Option SEnt)))
(pub fn (scopeFindFrame tc name)
  (scopeFindFrameFrom (tcScopeVec tc) name (- (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)) 1) (tcFrameBase tc)))

(pub :: scopeFindFrameFrom (-> (Vec SEnt) String Int Int (Option SEnt)))
(pub fn (scopeFindFrameFrom scope name i base)
  (if (< i base)
    None
    {
      (let entry (vecGet scope i))
      (if (strEq entry.name name)
        (Some entry)
        (scopeFindFrameFrom scope name (- i 1) base))
    }))

(pub :: scopeTrunc (-> (Vec a) Int Int))
(pub fn (scopeTrunc scope n)
  (if (<= (vecLen scope) n)
    0
    {
      (vecPop scope)
      (scopeTrunc scope n)
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Regions, stage S2 of docs/memory-model.md §3.6: `(region r
; EXPR)` is a CHECKED SCOPE with no types yet.
;
; What the checker does with one, and the two things it refuses:
;
;   - It keeps the stack of open regions (`TC` word 35), pushed on
;     entry and popped on exit. MM-RGN-2 makes nesting the outlives
;     order, so the stack IS the order, and a nested region that
;     reuses an enclosing name is refused - AX3058 - because a name
;     that means two open extents would make diagnostics ambiguous.
;
;   - It refuses the two ways a value can leave a region that need no
;     region TYPES to see - AX3059, `region-escape`:
;       (a) the region's own VALUE, when its type is not a scalar. The
;           lowering (`emitRegion`) hands the body's value out past the
;           reset by VALUE, and only a scalar - Int, Bool, Char, Float,
;           `()` - survives that. A `String` is a 24-byte
;           descriptor over a second block; a struct is a block; a
;           closure is a record. Every one of them would point into
;           reclaimed memory.
;       (b) a `set` inside the region on a binding that was bound
;           OUTSIDE it, when the stored value is not a scalar. That is
;           MM-RGN-3's escape rule for the one channel a scope check
;           can see. `(set i (+ i 1))` on an outer counter is allowed,
;           which is what makes a region per loop iteration writable.
;
; What it does NOT see, stated rather than implied: a call that stores
; a region-allocated reference into an outer structure (`vecPush`,
; `memSetWord`), and a raw `Int` that is an address. Both are
; `MM-ALLOC-16`'s program obligation exactly as they are for a
; hand-written `__axiom_arena_mark`, and S3's typed regions are what
; close them. `tests/diagnostics/631-region-escape.ax` holds the
; refusals, `tests/stdlib/168-region.ax` what passes, and
; `scripts/check-region-scope.sh` the ablation that shows what (a)
; and (b) refuse reading freed memory when accepted.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct RgEnt
  (name : String)
  (mark : Int)
  (span : Int))

(pub :: tcRegionsVec (-> TC (Vec RgEnt)))
(pub fn (tcRegionsVec tc)
  tc.regions)

; The innermost open region's index in the stack, or -1 outside any.
(pub :: rgInner (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (rgInner tc)
  (- (vecLen (tcRegionsVec tc)) 1))

; The scope length when the innermost region opened, or None outside
; any: a scope entry below it was bound OUTSIDE the region.
(pub :: rgInnerMark (-> TC (Option Int)))
(pub fn (rgInnerMark tc)
  (if (< (rgInner tc) 0)
    None
    {
      (let entry (vecGet (tcRegionsVec tc) (rgInner tc)))
      (Some entry.mark)
    }))

(pub :: rgInnerName (-> TC String))
(pub fn (rgInnerName tc)
  {
    (let regions (tcRegionsVec tc))
    (let entry (vecGet regions (rgInner tc)))
    (let name entry.name)
    name
  })

; The index of the open region named `name`, or None.
(pub :: rgFind (-> TC String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (rgFind tc name)
  (rgFindFrom (tcRegionsVec tc) name (rgInner tc)))

(pub :: rgFindFrom (-> (Vec RgEnt) String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (rgFindFrom entries name index)
  (if (< index 0)
    None
    {
      (let entry (vecGet entries index))
      (if (strEq entry.name name)
        (Some index)
        (rgFindFrom entries name (- index 1)))
    }))

(pub :: rgPush (-> TC String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgPush tc name mark span)
  {
    (let regions (tcRegionsVec tc))
    (let entry (RgEnt :name name :mark mark :span span))
    (vecPush regions entry)
    0
  })

(pub :: rgPop (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (rgPop tc)
  {
    (let regions (tcRegionsVec tc))
    (let retired (vecPop regions))
    0
  })

; Is this a type whose VALUE survives a reset - a word that is not an
; address? The four scalar constructors and the empty tuple.
; A bare type variable answers 0: what it stands for is not known
; here, and the safe direction is to refuse.
;
; No type at all answers 0 for the same reason. `rgnKnownCall` handles
; a missing nthParamTy result conservatively before classification.
; The shared AST boundary still accepts nilNode here; it cannot prove
; a scalar type (tests/fuzz/region-nonarrow-sig.axfuzz).
(pub :: rgTyScalar (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgTyScalar tc ty0)
  (if (nodeSame ty0 nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve ty0) 0))
      (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
        (if (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren ty)) 0)
          {
            (let n (nodeAName ty))
            (if (|| (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
                  (|| (strEq n "Bool") (|| (strEq n "Char") (strEq n "Float"))))
              1
              ; A word struct's value is its word, and the handle table
              ; it names lives outside every arena (MM-PAR-8), so it
              ; survives a reset by value as an `Int` does.
              (tcWordTyName tc n))
          }
          ; A link is an index, not an address (MM-VAL-10b): it
          ; outlives a reset, and reaching the element again needs
          ; the vector, which the region still holds to its scope.
          (if (strEq (nodeAName ty) "Link")
            1
            0))
        (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_TUP)
          (if (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren ty)) 0)
            1
            0)
          0))
    }))

; A scalar leaves a region as a word. A `Result` leaves when both
; its payload and its error are scalars, so neither word points into
; the memory the region reclaims. A `String` error does not.
(:: rgResultEscapes (-> TC ASTNode Bool))
(fn (rgResultEscapes tc ty0)
  (if (nodeSame ty0 nilNode)
    false
    (if (== (rgTyScalar tc ty0) 1)
      true
      {
        (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve ty0) 0))
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
              (&& (strEq (nodeAName ty) "Err$Result") (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren ty)) 2)))
          (&& (== (rgTyScalar tc (vecGet (nodeBChildren ty) 0)) 1)
            (== (rgTyScalar tc (vecGet (nodeBChildren ty) 1)) 1))
          false)
      })))

(pub :: emitRegionShadow (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitRegionShadow tc name span)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3058" "region-name-shadowed" span
          (concat (concat "region `" name)
            "` is already open: a nested region cannot reuse an enclosing region's name")
          None
          (Some
            "rename the inner region: regions are ordered by nesting (MM-RGN-2), and a name that means two extents cannot be ordered")
          "lexical region names identify reset extents in diagnostics and macro expansion; reference origins are inferred from the body")
        (concat (concat "`" name) "` is already the name of an enclosing region")))
    0
  })

(pub :: emitRegionValueEscape (-> TC String ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (emitRegionValueEscape tc name ty span boundary)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3059" "region-escape" span
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat "region `" name) "` answers a value of type `") (tyRender ty))
            "`, which may point into the memory the region reclaims")
          (diagRelated boundary "this region reclaims the result's allocation when its body ends")
          (Some
            "compute a scalar such as (strLen value) inside the region and return it; if you need a reference result, allocate it outside the region")
          "a `String` is a descriptor over a second block, a struct is a block and a closure is a record; every one of them would point at reclaimed memory")
        (concat (concat "this has type `" (tyRender ty)) "`")))
    0
  })

(pub :: emitRegionStoreEscape (-> TC String String ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (emitRegionStoreEscape tc rname bname ty span)
  {
    ; The span joins the refused set S3's reporting walk suppresses:
    ; S2 owns the direct store, so S3 reporting the same store would
    ; draw the same refusal twice (AX3059 with AX3060 on 631). A zero
    ; span is not recorded - it renders nowhere, so nothing printable
    ; can collide with it.
    (if (!= span 0)
      (vecPush (tcRgnRefused tc) span))
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3059" "region-escape" span
          (concat
            (concat
              (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" bname) "` is bound outside region `") rname)
                "`, and the value stored into it has type `")
              (tyRender ty))
            "`")
          (diagRelated
            (if (< (rgInner tc) 0)
              0
              {
                (let entry (vecGet (tcRegionsVec tc) (rgInner tc)))
                entry.span
              })
            "this region ends before the outer binding is used")
          (Some
            "move the reference allocation and this store outside the region, or bind the destination inside it; an outer binding may receive a scalar computed inside the region")
          "a store is the one escape channel a scope check can see; a call that stores the value for you is refused too, where the facts walk sees it")
        "this store leaves the region"))
    0
  })

; `set` on the binding at scope index `idx`, inside a region: refused
; when the target was bound before the innermost region opened and the
; value is not a scalar. `suppressed` is `checkSet`'s own flag - an
; AX3012 already reported on this store - and a poisoned value type
; is silent, the cascade rule.
(pub :: rgCheckStore (-> TC Int ASTNode Int String Int Int))
(pub fn (rgCheckStore tc idx valTy span bname suppressed)
  (if (let (Some mark) (rgInnerMark tc))
    (if (|| (>= idx mark) (!= suppressed 0))
      0
      (if (|| (== (tyIsErr valTy) 1)
            (|| (== (tyIsSilentWild valTy) 1) (== (rgTyScalar tc valTy) 1)))
        0
        (emitRegionStoreEscape tc (rgInnerName tc) bname valTy span)))
    0))

; The scope index of the binding at the root of a field path -
; `a.b.c` is a store into `a` - or None when no binding is found.
(pub :: rgRootIdx (-> TC ASTNode (Option Int)))
(pub fn (rgRootIdx tc e)
  (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
    (scopeFindIdx (tcScopeVec tc) (nodeAName e))
    (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_FIELD)
      (rgRootIdx tc (nodeAChild e))
      None)))

(pub :: rgRootName (-> ASTNode String))
(pub fn (rgRootName e)
  (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_FIELD)
    (rgRootName (nodeAChild e))
    (nodeAName e)))

(pub :: rgCheckFieldStore (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (rgCheckFieldStore tc base vty span)
  (if (let (Some index) (rgRootIdx tc base))
    (rgCheckStore tc index vty span (rgRootName base) 0)
    0))

; The form itself. The name is checked against the stack BEFORE it is
; pushed, the body is checked with it open, and the value's type is
; judged after the pop so a diagnostic about the value names the
; region that just closed. The form's type is its body's.
(pub :: checkRegion (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkRegion tc e)
  {
    (let name (nodeAName e))
    {
      ; S4 slice 2b's trigger bit: a region form has been checked. O(1),
      ; set here because this walk already visits every such form.
      (tcSetRgnHasForm tc 1)
      (if (let (Some _) (rgFind tc name))
        (emitRegionShadow tc name (nodeC e)))
      (rgPush tc (nodeAName e) (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)) (nodeC e))
      {
        (let bodyTy (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0))
        {
          (rgPop tc)
          (if (!
                (|| (== (tyIsErr bodyTy) 1)
                  (|| (== (tyIsSilentWild bodyTy) 1) (rgResultEscapes tc bodyTy))))
            (emitRegionValueEscape tc name bodyTy
              (if (== (spanOf (nodeBChild e)) 0)
                (nodeSpan e)
                (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
              (nodeC e)))
          bodyTy
        }
      }
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; spanOf: stage0's Expr::span(), the anchor for every EXPRESSION-
; position diagnostic. An application's span is its ultimate head's;
; a field access or field store anchors at its BASE (the field-name
; span in the node's own slot belongs to AX3007 alone); a let's span
; is its BODY's. A shape with no span answers 0 and the caller
; suppresses - never mis-points.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: spanOf (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (spanOf e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        (spanOf (nodeAChild e))
        (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_FIELD) (== tag TAG_E_SETF))
          (spanOf (nodeAChild e))
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
            (spanOf (nodeCChild e))
            (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_IF) (== tag TAG_E_WHILE))
              (spanOf (nodeAChild e))
              (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                (spanOf (nodeAChild e))
                ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds none, so no
                ; span is ever read off one.
                (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                  (if (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren e)) 0)
                    0
                    (spanOf (vecGet (nodeAChildren e) 0)))
                  ; A handle's span is its BODY's, and recursively so - stage0's
                  ; `Expr::span()` takes the first field of an EHandle exactly
                  ; as it takes the function side of an EApp. Nested handles
                  ; therefore all report at the innermost body, and without
                  ; this arm the node's own zero span suppressed every
                  ; diagnostic anchored at a handle whose body is another one.
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                    (spanOf (nodeAChild e))
                    (nodeSpan e))))))))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Diagnostic emission helpers. EVERY emission guards its anchor: a
; zero span renders address zero, and a diagnostic stage1 cannot
; place is one it does not print.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Both types of one mismatch, named from one table - see `tyRender`.
(pub :: emitMismatchTy (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (emitMismatchTy tc want got span)
  (if (|| (refuseUnsignedParam tc want) (refuseUnsignedParam tc got))
    0
    {
      (let names vecNew)
      {
        (tyVarsInto want names)
        (tyVarsInto got names)
        (emitMismatch tc (tyRenderScoped want names (tcCurModule tc))
          (tyRenderScoped got names (tcCurModule tc)) span)
      }
    }))

; Unsigned parameters keep distinct default Int nodes. Aliases and
; generic payloads retain those nodes, so their identity records which
; assumption failed without confusing an unrelated Int literal with it.
; Once that assumption fails, poison every default in this definition:
; the missing signature is one cause, regardless of how many parameters
; or aliases need a type. Declared types and other expressions stay live.
(:: unsignedParamIn (-> Nodes ASTNode Bool))
(fn (unsignedParamIn params ty0)
  {
    (let ty (tyResolve ty0))
    (let mut found false)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen params)
      (if (nodeSame ty (vecGet params i))
        (set found true)))
    (if (|| found (nodeSame ty nilNode))
      found
      (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
        (unsignedParamInVec params (nodeBChildren ty))
        (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_ARR)
          (|| (unsignedParamIn params (nodeAChild ty)) (unsignedParamIn params (nodeBChild ty)))
          (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_TUP)
            (unsignedParamInVec params (nodeAChildren ty))
            (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_PTR)
              (unsignedParamIn params (nodeAChild ty))
              false)))))
  })

(:: unsignedParamInVec (-> Nodes Nodes Bool))
(fn (unsignedParamInVec params tys)
  {
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut found false)
    (while (&& (! found) (< i (vecLen tys)))
      {
        (set found (unsignedParamIn params (vecGet tys i)))
        (set i (+ i 1))
      })
    found
  })

(:: refuseUnsignedParam (-> TC ASTNode Bool))
(fn (refuseUnsignedParam tc ty)
  {
    (let state (tcCheckState tc))
    (let params state.unsignedParams)
    (if (! (unsignedParamIn params ty))
      false
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen params)
          (writeNodeWord (vecGet params i) 0 TAG_T_ERR))
        true
      })
  })

; Lambda grounding can discard a speculative pass's diagnostics. Keep
; the failed default in the type nodes, then report once after the whole
; function body, when the real diagnostic vector is restored.
(:: emitUnsignedSignature (-> TC Int))
(fn (emitUnsignedSignature tc)
  {
    (let state (tcCheckState tc))
    (let params state.unsignedParams)
    (if (|| (== (vecLen params) 0) (== (tyIsErr (vecGet params 0)) 0))
      0
      (if (let (Some d) (tcCurDeclNode tc))
        (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
          0
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3089" "signature-needed" (nodeSpan d)
                (concat "`"
                  (concat (bareOf (nodeAName d))
                    "` needs a signature to declare its parameter types"))
                None (Some "write a `(:: ...)` signature above this definition")
                "parameters of a function without a signature default to Int; this body needs a different parameter type")
              "declare this function's parameter types")))
        0))
  })

(pub :: emitMismatch (-> TC String String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitMismatch tc expected found span)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
            (concat (concat (concat "type mismatch: expected " expected) ", found ") found) None
            None)
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "this has type `" found) "`, expected `") expected) "`")))
      0
    }))

; `emitMismatch` with a help line. Separate from it rather than a
; flag on it: `mkDiagNote` with an empty note is not proven
; byte-identical to `mkDiag`, and every AX3004 in the corpus pins
; the difference.
(pub :: emitMismatchHelp (-> TC String String Int String Int))
(pub fn (emitMismatchHelp tc expected found span help)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" span
            (concat (concat (concat "type mismatch: expected " expected) ", found ") found) None
            (Some help))
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "this has type `" found) "`, expected `") expected) "`")))
      0
    }))

; The `for` desugar's length and element reads name `Vec$vecLen` and
; `Vec$vecGet` - spellings no program can write (`$` is AX1001 inside
; an identifier), owned on both ends the way `format#` is. A concrete
; non-`Vec` argument draws the tailored help naming what fits; a
; variable that may still instantiate to a `Vec`, poison a diagnostic
; elsewhere already owns, or a `Vec` itself falls through to the
; generic mismatch below. Answers 1 when it emitted.
(pub :: forVecMismatch (-> TC String Int ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (forVecMismatch tc hname headIsVar argTy wantArg span)
  (if (== headIsVar 0)
    0
    (if (|| (strEq hname "Vec$vecLen") (strEq hname "Vec$vecGet"))
      (if (|| (== (tyIsErr argTy) 1) (== (nodeTag argTy) TAG_T_VAR))
        0
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag argTy) TAG_T_CON) (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName argTy)) "Vec"))
          0
          {
            (let bn
              (if (== (nodeTag argTy) TAG_T_CON)
                (bareOf (nodeAName argTy))
                ""))
            {
              (let names vecNew)
              {
                (tyVarsInto wantArg names)
                (tyVarsInto argTy names)
                {
                  (let help
                    (if (strEq bn "Int")
                      (Some
                        "to count, use the range: `(for i in 0..n ...)` - a range counts, a container holds")
                      (if (strEq bn "String")
                        (Some
                          "to read bytes: `(for i in 0..(strLen s) ... (strByte s i) ...)` - `for` iterates `(Vec a)` directly")
                        (if (strEq bn "Map")
                          (Some
                            "to visit entries: `(for k in (mapKeys m) ... (mapGet m k default) ...)` - `for` iterates `(Vec a)` directly")
                          None))))
                  (if (let (Some help) help)
                    {
                      (if (! (refuseUnsignedParam tc argTy))
                        (emitMismatchHelp tc (tyRenderIn wantArg names) (tyRenderIn argTy names)
                          span help))
                      1
                    }
                    0)
                }
              }
            }
          }))
      0)))

; `tc`, not a bare `out`: a diagnostic carries the UNIT its span
; indexes, and these two pushed straight into the vector, so an
; `AX3012` raised while checking an imported MODULE's body was stamped
; unit 0 and rendered against the ENTRY file's bytes - measured on a
; three-line entry file that reported `p3.ax:4:45-48`, a line it does
; not have. Every other site in this checker goes through `emitDiag`,
; which is where the stamp lives.
(pub :: emitAX3012 (-> String Int Int TC Int))
(pub fn (emitAX3012 name tspan bspan tc)
  ; MAC-HYG-3a, binding-name half: `name` arrives renamed (`tmp.1`)
  ; when the `set` was generated by a macro template. Render the
  ; original spelling everywhere - message, label, helps and the
  ; replacement, which the expansion frame disarms anyway. Inlined so
  ; no new top-level function moves the effect-distribution pins.
  {
    (let noDot
      (if (let (Some d) (strFindByte name 46 0))
        (strSlice name 0 d)
        name))
    {
      (let disp
        (if (let (Some h) (strFindByte noDot 35 0))
          (strSlice noDot 0 h)
          noDot))
      {
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagFix2 SEV_ERROR "AX3012" "assign-to-immutable" tspan
              (concat (concat "cannot assign to immutable binding `" disp) "`")
              (diagRelated bspan (concat (concat "`" disp) "` is bound here"))
              (concat (concat "declare it mutable: `(let mut " disp) " value)`")
              (diagFix bspan (concat "mut " disp))
              (Some "only a local declared `(let mut name value)` may be the target of `set`"))
            (concat (concat "`" disp) "` cannot be assigned")))
        0
      }
    }
  })

; `set` on a PARAMETER. This passed `check` until 2026-08-14 - the
; refusal above was suppressed whenever the entry recorded no binder
; span, and parameters record none - and then died in codegen as
; AX4002, whose own note says "this is a compiler bug: the check that
; should have refused this program did not run" (docs/memory-model.md
; MM-MUT-1a). Same code as the immutable-binding refusal; no binder
; span to point at, and the `mut` fix would be wrong advice - a
; parameter list has no `mut`.
(pub :: emitSetOnParam (-> String Int TC Int))
(pub fn (emitSetOnParam name tspan tc)
  ; MAC-HYG-3a, binding-name half: see `emitAX3012`.
  {
    (let noDot
      (if (let (Some d) (strFindByte name 46 0))
        (strSlice name 0 d)
        name))
    {
      (let disp
        (if (let (Some h) (strFindByte noDot 35 0))
          (strSlice noDot 0 h)
          noDot))
      {
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3012" "assign-to-immutable" tspan
              (concat (concat "cannot assign to `" disp)
                "`: it is a parameter, and parameters are immutable")
              None
              (Some
                (concat
                  (concat
                    (concat
                      (concat "copy it into a mutable local inside a brace block: `(let mut " disp)
                      "2 ")
                    disp)
                  ")`"))
              "this used to pass `check` and fail inside codegen as AX4002; the check runs here now")
            (concat (concat "`" disp) "` is a parameter")))
        0
      }
    }
  })

; `set` on a name that is no binding at all: a built-in or an imported
; function. Answers poison, the type a refused target has everywhere
; else; nothing is emitted for a spanless target.
(pub :: emitSetNotBinding (-> TC String Int String ASTNode))
(pub fn (emitSetNotBinding tc name tspan what)
  {
    (if (! (== tspan 0))
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3012" "assign-to-immutable" tspan
            (concat
              (concat (concat (concat "cannot assign to `" (srcSpelling name)) "`: it is ") what)
              ", not a binding")
            None
            (Some
              "only a local declared `(let mut name value)` may be the target of `set`; bind a mutable local of your own")
            "this used to pass `check` and fail inside codegen as AX4002")
          (concat (concat "`" (srcSpelling name)) "` cannot be assigned"))))
    (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
  })

; `set` from inside a lambda on a binding OUTSIDE it. The binding may
; well be `mut` - but a closure captures the VALUE it saw when its
; record was built (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-16), so there is no
; cell this store could reach, and codegen refused it as AX4002 after
; `check` said OK (MM-MUT-1a).
(pub :: emitSetCaptured (-> String Int Int (Vec diag::Diag) Int))
(pub fn (emitSetCaptured name tspan bspan out)
  ; MAC-HYG-3a, binding-name half: see `emitAX3012`.
  {
    (let noDot
      (if (let (Some d) (strFindByte name 46 0))
        (strSlice name 0 d)
        name))
    {
      (let disp
        (if (let (Some h) (strFindByte noDot 35 0))
          (strSlice noDot 0 h)
          noDot))
      {
        (vecPush out
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3012" "assign-to-immutable" tspan
              (concat (concat "cannot assign to `" disp)
                "` from inside a lambda: the lambda captured its value, not the binding")
              (diagRelated bspan (concat (concat "`" disp) "` is bound outside the lambda, here"))
              (Some
                "return the new value instead, or restructure so the assignment happens where the binding lives")
              "a closure captures by value when its record is built, so a later `set` inside it could never be observed outside; this used to pass `check` and fail inside codegen as AX4002")
            (concat (concat "`" disp) "` was captured, not shared")))
        0
      }
    }
  })

; `set` on a STRUCT FIELD the declaration did not mark `mut`. Same
; code as the immutable-binding refusal, and the same rule: a field is
; immutable unless its declaration says otherwise, exactly as a `let`
; binding is.
;
; Until 2026-08-31 `mut` on a field was parsed and DROPPED - there was
; nothing here to read, and `(struct P (x : Int))` took `(set p.x 9)`
; in silence, `check` OK and the program returning 9. AX2004's explain
; text states the principle the marker was failing: "a marker that
; reads as an ownership guarantee and supplies none is worse than no
; marker, because a reader spends trust on it."
;
; No secondary span and no quickfix, for `emitSetOnParam`'s reason
; plus one of its own: the declaration that would carry them is often
; in ANOTHER UNIT (any imported struct), and a diagnostic's span
; indexes the unit it was stamped with - the mistake the comment above
; `emitAX3012` records. The help carries the field's own declared type
; so the suggested line is the one to write.
(pub :: emitSetImmutableField (-> String String String Int TC Int))
(pub fn (emitSetImmutableField fname tyname fty tspan tc)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3012" "assign-to-immutable" tspan
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat "cannot assign to field `" fname) "` of `")
              (srcSpelling tyname))
            "`: the field is not declared `mut`")
          None
          (Some
            (concat
              (concat
                (concat (concat "declare it mutable where `" (srcSpelling tyname))
                  "` is defined: `(mut ")
                fname)
              (concat (concat " : " fty) ")`")))
          "a struct field is immutable unless its declaration marks it `mut`, the rule `let` already follows; until 0.6.0 the marker was parsed and discarded and this store compiled in silence")
        (concat (concat "`" fname) "` is not a `mut` field")))
    0
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Operators, keywords and forms that are always in scope and are not
; declarations.
;
; Primitives are NOT here. Every `__` primitive the emitter lowers is
; registered in `tcNew`'s function table with its signature - that is
; how a call to one is typed at all - so a `__` name that reaches this
; function is one the table does not know. Until 2026-08-21 a `__`
; prefix answered 1 unconditionally, on the ground that "every
; primitive has it", and the consequence ran the other way: any
; misspelling passed `check` and failed in `opt` as `use of undefined
; value '@__thisPrimitiveDoesNotExist'` - a native-toolchain report
; about generated code, for a program the compiler had just called
; well-typed (the QA sweep's AX4003 finding). Now it is AX3001, with
; the real primitives in the suggestion pool. The REPL's synthesized
; `__repl_result` is a real `fn` and resolves through the table.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: isBuiltinName (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isBuiltinName n)
  (if (strEq n "+")
    1
    (if (strEq n "-")
      1
      (if (strEq n "*")
        1
        (if (strEq n "/")
          1
          (if (strEq n "%")
            1
            (if (strEq n "&")
              1
              (if (strEq n "|")
                1
                (if (strEq n "^")
                  1
                  (if (strEq n "<<")
                    1
                    (if (strEq n ">>")
                      1
                      (if (strEq n "==")
                        1
                        (if (strEq n "!=")
                          1
                          (if (strEq n "<")
                            1
                            (if (strEq n ">")
                              1
                              (if (strEq n "<=")
                                1
                                (if (strEq n ">=")
                                  1
                                  (if (strEq n "&&")
                                    1
                                    (if (|| (strEq n "||") (strEq n "!"))
                                      1
                                      (if (strEq n "true")
                                        1
                                        (if (strEq n "false")
                                          1
                                          ; The cast forms. As a HEAD each is claimed by
                                          ; `checkCastForm` before any name is resolved;
                                          ; named bare, `checkBareValue` refuses it
                                          ; (AX3013) before this list is asked.
                                          ;
                                          ; `handle` and the five built-in effect names
                                          ; `IO`, `Pure`, `Alloc`, `Mut`, `Div` were here
                                          ; until 2026-09-27, because a `handle` list used
                                          ; to be parsed as an APPLICATION and put each name
                                          ; where an argument goes. `parseHandleExpr` has
                                          ; built a TAG_E_HANDLE since, whose effect names
                                          ; no variable lookup ever reads - so the only
                                          ; references left reaching this answer were the
                                          ; ones in VALUE position, and each checked OK and
                                          ; emitted `%IO`, a register nothing defines
                                          ; (`tests/fuzz/builtin-name-value.axfuzz`). They
                                          ; are AX3001 now, as the sixth built-in effect
                                          ; `Unsafe` and the keywords `if`/`let`/`match`
                                          ; always were.
                                          (if (isValueCastForm n)
                                            1
                                            (if (strEq n "sizeof")
                                              1
                                              (if (strEq n "alignof")
                                                1
                                                0))))))))))))))))))))))))

(pub :: strInVec (-> (Vec a) String Int Int))
(pub fn (strInVec v n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr v i) n)
      1
      (strInVec v n (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: isMacroName (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (isMacroName tc n)
  {
    (let qm (modOf n))
    (if (isSome qm)
      ; A qualified reference splits its bare name and declaring module (MAC-LANG-12).
      (if (isSome
            (macroQualVisible (tcMacrosVec tc) (bareOf n) (modOfName n) (tcCurModule tc)
              (- (vecLen (tcMacrosVec tc)) 1)))
        1
        0)
      (if (let (Some macro) (isMacroVisible (tcMacrosVec tc) n (tcCurModule tc)
                              (- (vecLen (tcMacrosVec tc)) 1)))
        ; A bare function wins over an imported macro, unless the macro
        ; belongs to the module being checked (MAC-HYG-8).
        (if (&& (isSome (nodeModuleOpt macro))
              (&& (== (macroSameMod (nodeModuleOpt macro) (tcCurModule tc)) 0)
                (isSome (fnEntExact tc n))))
          0
          1)
        0))
  })

; Visible from the module being checked: exported, or the entry
; file's, or this module's own - answering the NODE, walked BACKWARD
; so a later declaration wins, exactly as `expFindMacroIn` walks. The
; same rule and the same veto apply in the expander - one side decides
; whether the name type-checks and the other whether it expands.
(pub :: isMacroVisible (-> (Vec ASTNode) String (Option String) Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (isMacroVisible ms n curMod i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let m (vecGet ms i))
      (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName m) n)
            (|| (== (nodeVis m) 1)
              (|| (isNone (nodeModuleOpt m)) (== (typeModuleSame (nodeModuleOpt m) curMod) 1))))
        (Some m)
        (isMacroVisible ms n curMod (- i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: macroQualVisible (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String (Option String) Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (macroQualVisible ms bare qm curMod i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    {
      (let m (vecGet ms i))
      (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName m) bare)
            (&& (== (typeModuleSame (nodeModuleOpt m) (Some qm)) 1)
              (|| (== (nodeVis m) 1) (== (typeModuleSame curMod (Some qm)) 1))))
        (Some m)
        (macroQualVisible ms bare qm curMod (- i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: macroSameMod (-> (Option String) (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (macroSameMod a b)
  (match a
    ((None)
      (if (isNone b)
        1
        0)
    )
    ((Some left)
      (if (let (Some right) b)
        (if (strEq left right)
          1
          0)
        0)
    )))

; Declared anywhere, visible or not - for telling "no such macro" from
; "a macro you may not name". Split-aware, so a QUALIFIED reference to
; a private macro routes to AX3023 rather than a false AX3001.
(pub :: isMacroAnywhere (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (isMacroAnywhere tc n)
  {
    (let qm (modOf n))
    (if (isSome qm)
      (isMacroQualNamedIn (tcMacrosVec tc) (bareOf n) (modOfName n) 0)
      (isMacroNamedIn (tcMacrosVec tc) n 0))
  })

(pub :: isMacroNamedIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int))
(pub fn (isMacroNamedIn ms n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ms))
    0
    (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet ms i)) n)
      1
      (isMacroNamedIn ms n (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: isMacroQualNamedIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String Int Int))
(pub fn (isMacroQualNamedIn ms bare qm i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ms))
    0
    {
      (let m (vecGet ms i))
      (if (&& (strEq (nodeAName m) bare)
            (&& (isSome (nodeModuleOpt m)) (strEq (nodeModuleName m) qm)))
        1
        (isMacroQualNamedIn ms bare qm (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The TAG_D_EFFECT node declaring `n`, or None. Its ops vector's length
; is what AX3017 reports.
(pub :: findEffectDecl (-> TC String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (findEffectDecl tc n)
  (findEffectDeclFrom (tcEffectsVec tc) n 0))

(pub :: findEffectDeclFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (findEffectDeclFrom effs n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    None
    {
      (let d (vecGet effs i))
      (if (strEq (nodeAName d) n)
        (Some d)
        (findEffectDeclFrom effs n (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The six built-in effect names a handle list may name, each with one
; spelling: `IO`, `Pure`, `Mut`, `Div`, `Alloc` and `Unsafe`, the last
; inferred at the raw-memory primitives. None of them is ever a
; `Custom` effect - and only `Custom` entries are subject to AX3016 and
; AX3017. A lowercase `alloc` is an ordinary name, like `mut`.
;
; `Err` WAS A SIXTH AND IS NOT ONE. It was accepted here, which made
; `(handle 5 (Err) 0)` check clean, and nothing in the compiler ever
; inferred it - so the name resolved and denoted nothing. Its own AXTAG
; spelling did not even reach it: the tag path lowercases the value, so
; `;@axiom:effect(err)` looked for a CUSTOM effect named `err` and drew
; `AX3010 missing err`. A name a handle list may write, that nothing
; produces, and that its own tag cannot name, is a hole in a table
; rather than an effect. `AX3016` is what a list naming something
; undeclared has always drawn, and that is what `(Err)` draws now.
;
; This set is also the reserved set `checkEffectNameReserved` refuses a
; declaration for, and deliberately the same function: a handle list
; resolves a built-in name to the built-in, so any name in here is a
; name an `effect` declaration could never be handled under.
(pub :: isBuiltinEffect (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isBuiltinEffect n)
  (if (strEq n "IO")
    1
    (if (strEq n "Pure")
      1
      (if (strEq n "Mut")
        1
        (if (strEq n "Div")
          1
          (if (strEq n "Alloc")
            1
            (if (strEq n "Unsafe")
              1
              (isConcEntropyEffect n))))))))

; The three built-in effects that refine `IO`: `Entropy` (the program
; drew randomness, so it is not deterministic), `Spawn` (it started
; another binding, thread or process) and `Block` (it waited for
; another binding, a lock, a child or time). Each is REQUIRED the way
; `IO` is - an undeclaring body is AX3042 - and each was measured to
; discriminate before it was made so: over one probe importing every
; standard-library module (1,044 functions, 235 performing IO), `Spawn`
; reaches 19 and `Block` 45; over `core/main.ax` (5,110 functions)
; they reach 10 and 19. A tag on that few says something `IO` alone
; does not.
;
; Their PRODUCERS are the spawn and join primitives, `__arm_rndr`, and
; any constant carrying `;@axiom:syscall(...)` - the syscall numbers in
; `Sys.Platform` that mean `getrandom`, `fork` or `futex`. The compiler
; cannot tell one `__syscallN` from another, because the number is a
; runtime value; the platform table that names the number is where its
; meaning is known, so that is where the effect is stated
; (`syscallTagEffects`).
(pub :: isConcEntropyEffect (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isConcEntropyEffect n)
  (if (strEq n "Entropy")
    1
    (if (strEq n "Spawn")
      1
      (if (strEq n "Block")
        1
        0))))

; AX3054: an effect declared with a built-in effect's name.
;
; `(effect IO (emit :: (-> Int Int)))` was ACCEPTED, and the acceptance
; was useless in a way nothing reported. Measured on this tree before
; the code existed, with `(handle (emit 1) (IO) h)` around its own call:
;
;   W AX3053  `main` reaches operation `emit` of effect `IO` ...
;   E AX3011  effect mismatch: unhandled effect `IO`
;
; Two diagnostics, neither of them about the name that caused both. A
; handle list resolves `IO` to the BUILT-IN, so the list names one
; effect while the body performs another that happens to share its
; spelling, and the custom effect can never be handled at all. Without
; the handle it is worse than a wrong message: an untagged `main`
; performing the custom `IO` reads `#effects=IO` with no `#effect=io`
; and draws nothing, so the row says the program reaches the outside
; world when it does nothing of the kind.
;
; An ERROR with no warning stage. There is no correct program on the
; other side of it - the declaration is unusable by construction, in
; every direction, and the fix is a rename.
(pub :: checkEffectNameReserved (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkEffectNameReserved tc d)
  (if (== (isBuiltinEffect (nodeAName d)) 0)
    0
    (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
      0
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3054" "effect-name-reserved" (nodeSpan d)
            (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName d))
              "` names a built-in effect, and an effect declared with that name can never be handled: a handle list naming it reads as the built-in")
            None
            (Some
              "choose another name - the built-in effect names are `IO`, `Pure`, `Alloc`, `Mut`, `Div` and `Unsafe`"))
          (concat (concat "`" (nodeAName d)) "` is a built-in effect"))))))

(pub :: isValueCastForm (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (isValueCastForm name)
  (|| (strEq name "cast") (|| (strEq name "__as_numeric") (strEq name "__ascribe"))))

; Numeric as uses the checked source and expanded target types. Every
; other explicit cast retains its reference and ownership checks.
(pub :: asNumericKind (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (asNumericKind tc ty)
  {
    (let t (castNormTy tc ty))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      0
      (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
        0
        (if (> (integerTypeCode (nodeAName t)) 0)
          1
          (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "Float")
            2
            0))))
  })

(pub :: rewriteNumericAs (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode (Option ASTNode) ()))
(pub fn (rewriteNumericAs tc e target source)
  {
    (let from
      (match source
        ((None) 0)
        ((Some sourceType) (asNumericKind tc sourceType))))
    (let to (asNumericKind tc target))
    (let primitive
      (if (&& (== from 1) (== to 2))
        "__intToFloat"
        (if (&& (== from 2) (== to 1))
          "__floatToInt"
          "")))
    (let head (spineHead e))
    (let fromInt
      (match source
        ((None) 0)
        ((Some sourceType) (tyIntegerCode tc sourceType))))
    (let toInt (tyIntegerCode tc target))
    (if (|| (&& (> fromInt 0) (!= fromInt 64)) (&& (> toInt 0) (!= toInt 64)))
      {
        (if (|| (== from 0) (== to 0))
          (emitMismatch tc "a numeric type"
            (match source
              ((None) "{unknown}")
              ((Some sourceType) (tyRender sourceType)))
            (spanOf (spineArgAt e 1))))
        (writeNodeWord head 3
          (+
            (*
              (if (== from 2)
                129
                fromInt)
              256)
            (if (== to 2)
              129
              toInt)))
        (writeNodeText head 1 "cast")
      }
      (if (== (strLen primitive) 0)
        {
          (if (&& (== to 2) (== from 0))
            (match source
              ((None) (emitMismatch tc "Float" "{unknown}" (spanOf (spineArgAt e 1))))
              ((Some sourceType)
                (emitMismatchTy tc mkFloatTy sourceType (spanOf (spineArgAt e 1)))
              )))
          (if (== to 2)
            (writeNodeChild (nodeAChild e) 2 (mkEVarAt "Float" (spanOf (spineArgAt e 0)))))
          (writeNodeText head 1 "cast")
        }
        {
          (let value (spineArgAt e 1))
          {
            (writeNodeWord e 0 TAG_E_APP)
            (writeNodeChild e 1 (mkEVarAt primitive (nodeSpan head)))
            (writeNodeChild e 2 value)
          }
        }))
  })

(pub :: isCastOperation (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (isCastOperation name)
  (|| (strEq name "cast")
    (|| (strEq name "__as_numeric") (|| (strEq name "__intToFloat") (strEq name "__floatToInt")))))

(pub :: isCastLike (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isCastLike n)
  (if (isValueCastForm n)
    1
    (if (strEq n "sizeof")
      1
      (if (strEq n "alignof")
        1
        0))))

; How many arguments an application spine supplies, and its head.
; The spine's arguments in SOURCE order.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The expression walk. checkExpr answers the expression's TYPE and
; pushes diagnostics in stage0's traversal order. `inHead` is true
; only while checking the function side of an application - the flag
; that lets a bare multi-parameter function name pass in head
; position (AX3013 elsewhere) and confines the saturation check to
; each spine's ROOT.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkExpr (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkExpr tc e)
  (checkExprH tc e 0))

(pub :: checkExprHCore (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkExprHCore tc e inHead)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    mkUnitTy
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (let disc (tcDiscard tc))
      (let _cleared (tcSetDiscard tc 0))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_INT)
        (if (isUnitLit e)
          mkUnitTy
          mkIntTy)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_FLT)
          mkFloatTy
          (if (== tag TAG_E_CHAR)
            mkCharTy
            (if (== tag TAG_E_STR)
              mkStringTy
              (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
                (checkVar tc e inHead)
                (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
                  (checkApp tc e inHead)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                    (deferLam tc e)
                    (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                      {
                        (let initTy (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0))
                        {
                          (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
                          {
                            (refuseReservedBinder tc (nodeAName e) (nodeSpan e) 0)
                            ; A `mut` slot's type, for codegen: a slot of a
                            ; reference type owns what it holds, so `set`
                            ; and the scope end release it (`emitLetMAt`,
                            ; MM-LIFE-2c event 3). The emitter cannot type
                            ; an initialiser that is not an application,
                            ; and a type variable in it resolves only later.
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_LETM)
                              (setNodeBinderTyChild e initTy)
                              e)
                            (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) (nodeAText e)
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_LETM)
                                1
                                0)
                              (nodeSpan e) initTy)
                            {
                              (let bodyTy
                                {
                                  (tcSetDiscard tc disc)
                                  (checkExprH tc (nodeCChild e) 0)
                                })
                              {
                                (if (&& (== tag TAG_E_LET)
                                      (&& (== (isDiscardedResultTy initTy) 1)
                                        (&& (!= (strByte (nodeAName e) 0) 95)
                                          (== (sEntUsed (vecGet (tcScopeVec tc) mark)) 0))))
                                  (emitDiag tc
                                    (diagSetLabel
                                      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3046" "discarded-result" (nodeSpan e)
                                        (concat (concat "a `Result` bound to `" (nodeAName e))
                                          "` is never read")
                                        None
                                        (Some
                                          "match on it, propagate it with `?`, or give it a name starting with `_` if the discard is deliberate"))
                                      "unused `Result`")))
                                (flushScopePending tc mark)
                                (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
                                bodyTy
                              }
                            }
                          }
                        }
                      }
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                        (checkIf tc e disc)
                        ; `cond` is AX2004 (see `checkIf`: the variadic
                        ; `if` desugars before this pass), so no arm
                        ; checks one.
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                          (checkExprSeq tc (nodeAChildren e) 0 mkUnitTy disc)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                            (checkMatch tc e disc)
                            (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                                (checkRegion tc e)
                                {
                                  (checkCondBool tc (nodeAChild e))
                                  ; The loop discards its body's value,
                                  ; tail included, so a pending lambda
                                  ; there is flushed like any statement's.
                                  {
                                    (let body (nodeBChildren e))
                                    {
                                      (let t (checkExprSeq tc body 0 mkUnitTy 1))
                                      (if (> (vecLen body) 0)
                                        {
                                          (let tail (vecGet body (- (vecLen body) 1)))
                                          (if (== (isDiscardedResultTy t) 1)
                                            (emitDiscardedResult tc tail))
                                          (flushPending tc t (spanOf tail))
                                        })
                                    }
                                  }
                                  mkIntTy
                                })
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                                (checkSet tc e)
                                (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
                                  (checkSetField tc e)
                                  (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                                    (checkField tc e)
                                    (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                                      (checkHandle tc e)
                                      (if (== tag TAG_E_SUBCHECK)
                                        {
                                          (checkExprH tc (nodeAChild e) 0)
                                          (checkSubtypeBounds tc (nodeBChildren e) 0)
                                          (if (!= (nodeC e) 0)
                                            (checkExprH tc (nodeCChild e) 0))
                                          {
                                            (let tt (nodeTyChild e))
                                            (if (! (nodeSame tt nilNode))
                                              tt
                                              mkSilentWild)
                                          }
                                        }
                                        ; A `?` the expander could not rewrite
                                        ; (AX3146, already reported): its operand
                                        ; is still checked, and the form answers
                                        ; poison, so nothing cascades from it.
                                        (if (== tag TAG_E_TRY)
                                          {
                                            (checkExprH tc (nodeAChild e) 0)
                                            mkTErr
                                          }
                                          mkSilentWild))))))))))))))))))
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The discard flag: is the value of the expression about to be
; checked thrown away? Set by the one who knows - a block for every
; statement but its last, a loop for its whole body - and read once,
; at `checkExprHCore`'s entry, which clears it so nothing inside the
; expression inherits it. A block's last expression and a `let`'s body
; inherit the block's or the `let`'s own flag.
;
; An `if` or a `match` whose value is dropped answers `()` whatever
; its branches answer: they need not agree, and none needs a filler
; `0` to agree with the others (`checkIf`, `checkMatch`). Each branch
; is dropped as a statement is, so a `Result` there is still AX3046.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: tcDiscard (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (tcDiscard tc)
  tc.unsafe.discard)

; The unknown callees (`UState` word 13).
(pub :: tcUnknownCallees (-> TC Names))
(pub fn (tcUnknownCallees tc)
  tc.unsafe.unknownCallees)

(pub :: tcNoteUnknownCallee (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (tcNoteUnknownCallee tc name)
  {
    (vecPushStr (tcUnknownCallees tc) name)
    0
  })

; Poison (ERR-DIAG-3): a call to a name with no body. Its row is not
; empty, it is unknown, and a claim checked against it has no answer.
(pub :: tcIsUnknownCallee (-> TC String Bool))
(pub fn (tcIsUnknownCallee tc name)
  {
    (let v (tcUnknownCallees tc))
    (if (== (vecLen v) 0)
      false
      (== (strVecHas v name) 1))
  })

(pub :: tcSetDiscard (-> TC Int Int))
(pub fn (tcSetDiscard tc v)
  {
    (set tc.unsafe.discard v)
    0
  })

(pub :: checkExprSeq (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkExprSeq tc v i last disc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    last
    {
      (let t
        {
          (tcSetDiscard tc
            (if (< i (- (vecLen v) 1))
              1
              disc))
          (checkExprH tc (vecGet v i) 0)
        })
      {
        ; ERR-DIAG-2: a `Result` answered where no one reads it. Every
        ; arm after this one runs whatever this one answered - which,
        ; for a `Result`, is nothing: an `Err` here vanishes as
        ; silently as a right answer. Only block sequences reach this
        ; walk (`{ }` bodies and `while` bodies), which is exactly the
        ; statement position the rule names; an `if` or `match` arm
        ; answering a `Result` answers its whole `if` or `match`, and
        ; what that answers is its own position's question.
        (if (&& (< i (- (vecLen v) 1)) (== (isDiscardedResultTy t) 1))
          (emitDiscardedResult tc (vecGet v i)))
        ; A non-tail element's value is discarded, so a pending
        ; lambda there escapes: flush it (verify standalone, drop
        ; only the `{unknown}` refusals no use witnesses).
        (if (< i (- (vecLen v) 1))
          (flushPending tc t (spanOf (vecGet v i))))
        (checkExprSeq tc v (+ i 1) t disc)
      }
    }))

; A type that is nominally a `Result` and known - not a poisoned
; unknown, which a diagnostic elsewhere already owns, and not a
; variable, which may instantiate to anything.
(pub :: isDiscardedResultTy (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (isDiscardedResultTy t)
  (if (|| (nodeSame t nilNode) (== (tyIsErr t) 1))
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      0
      (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "Err$Result")
        1
        0))))

; The span a discarded `Result` is reported at: through blocks and
; `let` binders, whose spans name the binder, not the value, to the tail
; expression that answers it. Anything else anchors where it stands.
; A descent that lands nowhere (a spanless tail) answers 0, and the
; caller falls back to the statement's own span rather than going
; quiet: the discard is real wherever it is pointed.
(pub :: discardSpanOf (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (discardSpanOf e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag e))
      (if (|| (== tg TAG_E_LET) (== tg TAG_E_LETM))
        {
          (let b (nodeCChild e))
          (if (nodeSame b nilNode)
            (nodeSpan e)
            (discardSpanOf b))
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_E_BEGIN)
          {
            (let v (nodeAChildren e))
            (if (== (vecLen v) 0)
              (nodeSpan e)
              (discardSpanOf (vecGet v (- (vecLen v) 1))))
          }
          (nodeSpan e)))
    }))

(pub :: emitDiscardedResult (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (emitDiscardedResult tc e)
  {
    (let sp (discardSpanOf e))
    {
      (let at
        (if (== sp 0)
          (nodeSpan e)
          sp))
      (if (== at 0)
        0
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3046" "discarded-result" at
              "a `Result` is discarded here, in statement position" None
              (Some
                "match on it, propagate it with `?`, or bind it to `_` if the discard is deliberate"))
            "unused `Result`")))
    }
  })

; A condition must be Bool. Skipped when the condition is already
; poisoned; a wildcard passes through tyCompat.
(pub :: checkCondBool (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkCondBool tc cnd)
  {
    (let cty (checkExprH tc cnd 0))
    {
      ; A pending lambda is compatible with everything, so the
      ; compatibility arm below would accept a function as a
      ; condition in silence. Name it instead.
      (if (== (isPendingNode cty) 1)
        (refusePendingOperand tc cty "Bool" (spanOf cnd))
        (if (&& (== (tyIsErr cty) 0) (== (tyCompat tc cty mkBoolTy) 0))
          (emitMismatchTy tc mkBoolTy cty (spanOf cnd))
          (refuseUnknownOperand tc "Bool" cty (spanOf cnd))))
      cty
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The unit type `()`, the empty tuple, and its one value `()`.
;
; A branch, an arm or a body whose type is `()` makes the form around
; it answer `()`, and the values of the others are dropped: an `if`
; with one `()` branch (which is every `if` written without an else,
; `parseIfTail`), a `match` with one `()` arm, and a function declared
; to answer `()`. Dropping is a rewrite of the dropped expression into
; `{ e () }` (`discardToUnit`), so the code generator meets a block it
; already handles: the value is released where it was a reference
; (`emitSeq`), and the whole answers the unit word 0.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: tyIsUnit (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (tyIsUnit t)
  {
    (let r (tyResolve t))
    (if (nodeSame r nilNode)
      false
      (&& (== (nodeTag r) TAG_T_TUP) (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren r)) 0)))
  })

; Rewrite `e`, whose type is `t`, in place into `{ e () }`. The node
; keeps its identity, so whatever holds it now holds the block; the
; expression moves to a copy. A `Result` dropped this way is AX3046
; and a pending lambda is flushed, as for any statement
; (`checkExprSeq`).
(pub :: discardToUnit (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (discardToUnit tc e t)
  (if (|| (nodeSame e nilNode) (tyIsUnit t))
    0
    {
      (if (== (isDiscardedResultTy t) 1)
        (emitDiscardedResult tc e))
      (flushPending tc t (spanOf e))
      {
        (let copy (nodeClone e))
        (let xs vecNew)
        {
          (for k in 5..12
            (nodeCopyWord copy e k))
          (vecPush xs copy)
          (vecPush xs (mkEUnitAt (nodeSpan e)))
          ; Built as a node first, so the block holds its share of
          ; `xs`; copying its mapped field gives `e` an owned share.
          ; The original children remain owned by `copy` until the
          ; replacement block itself is released.
          {
            (let blk (nodeBuildEBegin xs 0 0 (nodeSpan e)))
            {
              (writeNodeWord e 0 TAG_E_BEGIN)
              (nodeCopyWord e blk 1)
              (writeNodeWord e 2 0)
              (writeNodeWord e 3 0)
              (writeNodeWord e 5 0)
              (writeNodeWord e 6 0)
              (writeNodeWord e 7 0)
              (writeNodeWord e 9 0)
              (writeNodeWord e 10 0)
              (writeNodeWord e 11 0)
              0
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }))

(pub :: checkIf (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkIf tc e disc)
  {
    (checkCondBool tc (nodeAChild e))
    ; Branches must agree; the report anchors at the ELSE with
    ; the then-branch as the expectation - stage0's shape.
    ;
    ; A branch's value is dropped when the `if`'s is, and when the
    ; other branch is the unit value (an `if` written without an
    ; else), so the branch is checked with the discard flag set and an
    ; `if` or `match` nested in it may have branches that disagree.
    ; A `()` branch makes the `if` answer `()`, and the other
    ; branch's value is dropped: `(if t b)` is `(if t b ())`. So
    ; does a dropped `if` whose branches disagree: nobody reads
    ; the value, so neither branch owes the other a type.
    {
      (let thenTy
        {
          (tcSetDiscard tc
            (if (|| (== disc 1) (isUnitLit (nodeCChild e)))
              1
              0))
          (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0)
        })
      {
        (let elseTy
          {
            (tcSetDiscard tc
              (if (|| (== disc 1) (isUnitLit (nodeBChild e)))
                1
                0))
            (checkExprH tc (nodeCChild e) 0)
          })
        (if (|| (tyIsUnit thenTy)
              (|| (tyIsUnit elseTy)
                (&& (== disc 1)
                  (&& (== (tyIsErr thenTy) 0)
                    (&& (== (tyIsErr elseTy) 0) (== (tyCompat tc thenTy elseTy) 0))))))
          {
            (discardToUnit tc (nodeBChild e) thenTy)
            (discardToUnit tc (nodeCChild e) elseTy)
            mkUnitTy
          }
          {
            (if (&& (== (tyIsErr thenTy) 0)
                  (&& (== (tyIsErr elseTy) 0) (== (tyCompat tc thenTy elseTy) 0)))
              (emitMismatchTy tc thenTy elseTy (spanOf (nodeCChild e))))
            (if (== (tyIsErr thenTy) 1)
              elseTy
              thenTy)
          })
      }
    }
  })

; `cond` is AX2004: no arm checks one. A variadic `if` reaches
; `checkIf` as the nested chain `parseIfExpr` built, so every branch
; joins pairwise exactly as written `if`s do; the laxer join `cond`
; had (clause bodies never compared, only a real `else` against the
; last clause) goes with it.
(pub :: checkLamCore (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))

; The syntax-level half of `checkLam`, shared with the defer: a
; lambda with no parameters can never be called, so it is refused
; rather than built. `(f)` and `f` are the SAME expression - a
; one-element list is its head - which is how a bare reference to
; a nullary top-level `fn` calls it, and it leaves no syntax that
; applies a nullary closure. Before this it was accepted, lifted,
; and evaluated to the closure record's ADDRESS: `(let ((f (lambda
; () 42))) (f))` answered 16, at exit 0, and `((lambda () 42))`
; the same.
(pub :: checkLamValidate (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkLamValidate tc e)
  {
    (let params (nodeANames e))
    {
      (if (== (vecLen params) 0)
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3068" "nullary-lambda" (nodeSpan e)
              "a lambda with no parameters can never be called" None
              (Some
                "give it a parameter, or write a top-level `(fn (name) body)` - a bare reference to a nullary function does call it")
              "`(f)` and `f` are the same expression, so no syntax applies a nullary closure; it used to evaluate to the closure record's address")
            "this lambda takes no parameters")))
      (refuseReservedParams tc params (nodeSpan e) 0)
    }
  })

; No validation here: every caller checks a node the defer
; already validated (flush, grounding, and `checkLamAgainst`'s
; fallback all run post-defer), and validating twice would refuse
; twice - measured, a duplicated AX3094 on the reserved-literal
; fixture.
(pub fn (checkLamCore tc e)
  {
    (let params (nodeANames e))
    (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
    (let ptys vecNew)
    {
      (bindLamParams tc params ptys 0)
      ; The lambda boundary for `checkSet`: entries below `mark` are
      ; captured (by value, MM-VAL-16), and assigning one is refused.
      ; Saved and restored so nesting keeps the innermost boundary.
      ;
      ; The lambda-name stack rides the same save/restore, for the same
      ; reason: it names THIS lambda's parameter placeholder so that
      ; `evClassOf` can answer `EV_LAMARG` for it, and a nested lambda
      ; must shadow rather than inherit it. Set only for a
      ; one-parameter lambda - the parser curries, so that is the
      ; lifted shape, and a flat multi-parameter spelling would name
      ; the wrong placeholder.
      {
        (let savedLB (tcLamBase tc))
        (let lamMark
          (lamVarPush tc
            (if (== (vecLen ptys) 1)
              (nodeAName (vecGet ptys 0))
              "")))
        {
          (tcSetLamBase tc mark)
          ; A pending body (a lambda nested in this one) is
          ; inferred NOW, while this lambda's parameters are
          ; still in scope for it to close over: the arrow
          ; below must be a TYPE, never a pending node.
          {
            (let bodyTy0 (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0))
            {
              (let bodyTy
                (if (== (isPendingNode bodyTy0) 1)
                  (groundStandalone tc bodyTy0 (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                  bodyTy0))
              {
                (tcSetLamBase tc savedLB)
                (lamVarTrunc tc (- lamMark 1))
                (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
                (if (== (vecLen ptys) 0)
                  (mkTArr mkUnitTy bodyTy)
                  (if (== (vecLen ptys) 1)
                    (mkTArr (vecGet ptys 0) bodyTy)
                    (mkTArr (mkTTup ptys) bodyTy)))
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })

; A lambda checked AGAINST an arrow rather than inferred: the
; parameter is bound to the arrow's parameter type instead of a fresh
; variable, so inside a handler `s` IS the `String` the operation
; declared and a hole's rendering resolves on it - where the fresh
; variable drew AX3025 and forced `(cast String s)` at every use. Recurses through
; the curried chain: `(-> Int Int Int)` is `(Int -> (Int -> Int))`,
; and `(lambda (a b) ..)` is `(lambda (a) (lambda (b) ..))` by the
; parser (`curryLam`), so a flat two-parameter lambda for a
; two-argument operation is checked exactly as the nested spelling is.
; Falls back to `checkLam` when there is nothing to bind to - the
; arrow has run out (`(lambda (x y) ..)` for a one-argument operation:
; the inner lambda infers, and the whole is then refused against the
; arrow, `expected (Int -> Int), found (Int -> (_a -> ..))`) or the
; parameter vector is not one name.
;
; Nothing is INFERRED here, which is what makes it safe under the
; evidence-word hazard the type-inference note records: the arrow was
; written by the author, so every type it binds is one the author
; named rather than one this checker minted. A minted placeholder is
; what `evClassOf` answers -1 to, and -1 emits no `__retainref` at
; all - MM-LIFE-2g's hole, and a live use-after-free, not a leak.
;
; WHAT THE ARROW MAY CONTAIN, which changed on 2026-08-30 when a third
; caller arrived. The two original callers were both the handler at a
; `handle`, where AX3017 has already refused a type variable, so every
; type bound was GROUND and this comment said so flatly. A `fn` whose
; declared result is an arrow is under no such rule:
;
;     (:: mk (-> Int (-> String Int)))   binds `String`   - ground
;     (:: mk (-> Int (-> a Int)))        binds `a`        - a SOURCE
;                                                           variable
;
; Both are safe here, and neither is safe for the same reason. A
; ground type classifies 1 and the retain is unconditional. A source
; variable classifies `2+k`, and the emitted code is correct all the
; way down - measured on the second signature above, the lambda loads
; the evidence word its factory stored in the closure record, shifts
; out bit k and retains under it. What it is NOT is EFFECTIVE: `a`
; appears only in `mk`'s result, so nothing at `mk`'s call site
; witnesses it and the caller passes the constant 0. The value of type
; `a` arrives later, at the closure's own application, which is a site
; the evidence word does not reach today. So the second signature
; still under-retains, and under-retaining is the leaking direction
; rather than the freeing one.
;
; The rule to keep, therefore, is narrower than "the arrow is ground":
; bind what the author WROTE, whatever it is, and never a placeholder.
(pub :: checkLamAgainstCore (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkLamAgainstCore tc e arrow)
  {
    (let params (nodeANames e))
    (if (|| (!= (vecLen params) 1) (!= (nodeTag arrow) TAG_T_ARR))
      (checkLam tc e)
      {
        (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
        {
          (refuseReservedBinder tc (vecGetStr params 0) (nodeSpan e) 0)
          (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) (vecGetStr params 0) 0 0 (nodeAChild arrow))
          ; The lambda boundary for `checkSet`, saved and restored
          ; exactly as `checkLam` does it.
          ;
          ; The lambda-name stack records this parameter when the author
          ; wrote a TYPE VARIABLE for it, and is cleared when the
          ; author wrote a ground type.
          ;
          ; A ground type needs nothing: `evClassOf` answers `String`
          ; with 1 and `Int` with 0, and no application has to be
          ; consulted. A type variable is the case this exists for.
          ; `(:: mk (-> Int (-> a Int)))` emits a correct chain - `mk`
          ; takes its evidence word, stores it in the closure record,
          ; and the lambda loads it and retains under bit k - and still
          ; under-retains, because `a` appears only in `mk`'s RESULT,
          ; so nothing at `mk`'s call site witnesses it and the caller
          ; passes 0. The value of type `a` does not exist until the
          ; closure is APPLIED, which is the one place that knows.
          ;
          ; Cleared rather than inherited in the ground case, because
          ; an enclosing lambda's name left in place would still match
          ; wherever this body mentions that type, and stamp THIS
          ; lambda's application word onto a value this application
          ; does not witness.
          {
            (let savedLB (tcLamBase tc))
            (let lamMark
              (lamVarPush tc
                (if (== (nodeTag (nodeAChild arrow)) TAG_T_VAR)
                  (nodeAName (nodeAChild arrow))
                  "")))
            {
              (tcSetLamBase tc mark)
              ; A pending body THROUGH A WRAPPER (the direct
              ; lambda above already recursed): against an arrow
              ; codomain the declaration still binds it; against
              ; anything else it is inferred standalone, and the
              ; mismatch below owns the shape.
              {
                (let bodyTy
                  (if (== (nodeTag (nodeBChild e)) TAG_E_LAM)
                    (checkLamAgainst tc (nodeBChild e) (nodeBChild arrow))
                    (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0)))
                {
                  (let bodyTy2
                    (if (== (isPendingNode bodyTy) 1)
                      (if (== (nodeTag (nodeBChild arrow)) TAG_T_ARR)
                        (checkLamAgainst tc bodyTy (nodeBChild arrow))
                        (groundStandalone tc bodyTy (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))
                      bodyTy))
                  {
                    (tcSetLamBase tc savedLB)
                    (lamVarTrunc tc (- lamMark 1))
                    (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
                    (mkTArr (nodeAChild arrow) bodyTy2)
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          }
        }
      })
  })

(pub :: bindLamParams (-> TC Names Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (bindLamParams tc params ptys i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen params))
    0
    {
      (let t (freshTVar tc 1))
      {
        (vecPush ptys t)
        (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) (vecGetStr params i) 0 0 t)
        (bindLamParams tc params ptys (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Variable references, in stage0's check_var order. Frame-local wins
; silently. In VALUE position (not an application head), a name that
; cannot be a value is refused: an effect operation goes quiet with
; poison (stage0 emits AX3017 there and poisons; the poison is the
; part that keeps later bytes identical), a function of two or more
; parameters is AX3013 with 0 supplied, a fieldful constructor is
; AX3009 with 0 found (the LAST declaration of the name - rfind,
; stage0's order). Then the function table, then constructors, then
; the always-in-scope names, then AX3001.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; AX3014: a bare name two or more IMPORTED modules define, with no
; entry-file definition to win. Refused in every position, head or
; value; `Mod::name` picks explicitly.
;
; A DEFINER is a `fn` or an effect operation - a bare
; signature neither creates ambiguity nor outranks a real definer -
; plus a data constructor, because candidates are the union across
; BOTH namespaces: a function in one module and a constructor of the
; same name in another are exactly as unresolvable as two functions.
;
; Builtins remain globally visible. Entry-file definitions win only
; in the entry file; imported modules choose their own declaration or
; a unique visible imported definition.
(:: ownCtor (-> TC String (Option CtorEnt)))
(fn (ownCtor tc name)
  (if (let (Some ctor) (findCtor tc name))
    (if (== (typeModuleSame (modOf (nodeAName ctor.resTy)) (tcCurModule tc)) 1)
      (Some ctor)
      None)
    None))

(pub :: checkAmbiguous (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (checkAmbiguous tc name span)
  ; A QUALIFIED reference names one module, so it cannot be
  ; ambiguous - it is the escape this diagnostic's own help text
  ; tells the reader to write. The check asked `bareOf name`, which
  ; strips the qualifier that answers the question, so `AmbA::dup`
  ; reported `ambiguous name \`dup\`` and helpfully suggested
  ; writing `AmbA::dup`. The documented escape from the flat
  ; namespace did not work, which is why 149 imports still carry no
  ; name list and two `fmtInt`/`die` pairs are still duplicated by
  ; hand.
  {
    (let mods (definingModules tc (bareOf name)))
    (if (|| (isSome (modOf name))
          (|| (isSome (ownCtor tc name))
            (if (isNone (tcCurModule tc))
              false
              {
                (let localFound
                  (if (let (Some moduleName) (tcCurModule tc))
                    (fnEntQual tc moduleName name)
                    None))
                (if (let (Some local) localFound)
                  (! (signatureEntryOnly local))
                  false)
              })))
      0
      (if (< (vecLen mods) 2)
        0
        (if (== span 0)
          0
          {
            (emitDiag tc
              (diagSetLabel
                (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3014" "ambiguous-name" span
                  (concat
                    (concat (concat (concat "ambiguous name `" (bareOf name)) "`: defined in ")
                      (joinModules mods))
                    "")
                  None (Some (concat "qualify the reference: " (joinQualified mods (bareOf name)))))
                (concat (concat "`" (bareOf name)) "` could mean more than one definition")))
            1
          })))
  })

; The distinct modules defining `n`, in declaration order - or an
; empty Vec when anything module-less defines it.
; Built ONCE, at collection, instead of scanning every declaration at
; every reference. The scan was linear in the declaration list and
; the list is the whole program, so once the walk covered imported
; modules too it went quadratic and stage1 stopped terminating on any
; stdlib-sized input. stage0 keeps a map for the same reason.
(pub :: definingModules (-> TC String Names))
(pub fn (definingModules tc n)
  (if (let (Some entry) (ambFind tc n))
    (if (&& (== entry.moduleless 1) (rootNameVisibleHere tc n))
      vecNew
      entry.mods)
    vecNew))

(pub :: rootNameVisibleHere (-> TC String Bool))
(pub fn (rootNameVisibleHere tc name)
  (if (isNone (tcCurModule tc))
    true
    {
      (let eFound (fnEntExact tc name))
      (if (let (Some e) eFound)
        (== (fnEntOutOfModuleScope tc (tcCurModule tc) name e) 0)
        true)
    }))

(pub type AmbIndex (Vec (Vec AmbEnt)))

(pub struct AmbEnt
  (name : String)
  (mods : Names)
  (mut moduleless : Int))

(:: ambEntName (-> AmbEnt String))
(fn (ambEntName e)
  e.name)

(:: ambEntMods (-> AmbEnt Names))
(fn (ambEntMods e)
  e.mods)

(:: ambEntModuleless (-> AmbEnt Bool))
(fn (ambEntModuleless e)
  (== e.moduleless 1))

(:: ambSetModuleless (-> AmbEnt Int))
(fn (ambSetModuleless e)
  {
    (set e.moduleless 1)
    0
  })

; Buckets over the entries, keyed on the name. An entry is MUTATED
; after insert - a module appended to its list, or its module-less
; flag set - but its NAME never changes, which is the same
; precondition the exact-name index over `tcFns` records and relies
; on.
(pub :: ambIdxNew (-> Int AmbIndex))
(pub fn (ambIdxNew hint)
  {
    (let nb (fnIdxBuckets hint))
    (let bs vecNew)
    (for i in 0..nb
      (vecPush bs vecNew))
    bs
  })

(pub :: ambFind (-> TC String (Option AmbEnt)))
(pub fn (ambFind tc n)
  (if (let (Some bs) (tcAmbIdx tc))
    (ambFindIn (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash n) (vecLen bs))) n 0)
    None))

(pub :: ambFindIn (-> (Vec AmbEnt) String Int (Option AmbEnt)))
(pub fn (ambFindIn v n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    {
      (let entry (vecGet v i))
      (if (strEq entry.name n)
        (Some entry)
        (ambFindIn v n (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: ambRecord (-> TC String (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (ambRecord tc n m)
  (if (let (Some entry) (ambFind tc n))
    (match m
      ((None) (ambSetModuleless entry))
      ((Some moduleName)
        {
          (if (== (strInVec entry.mods moduleName 0) 0)
            (vecPush entry.mods moduleName))
          0
        }
      ))
    {
      (let modules vecNew)
      (if (let (Some moduleName) m)
        (vecPush modules moduleName))
      (let entry
        (AmbEnt
          :name n
          :mods modules
          :moduleless
            (if (isNone m)
              1
              0)))
      (vecPush (tcAmbTbl tc) entry)
      (if (let (Some bs) (tcAmbIdx tc))
        (ambIdxPut bs n entry))
      0
    }))

(pub :: ambIdxPut (-> AmbIndex String AmbEnt Int))
(pub fn (ambIdxPut bs n ent)
  {
    (vecPush (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash n) (vecLen bs))) ent)
    0
  })

; One pass over the merged list, recording every DEFINER's module.
(pub :: ambBuild (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (ambBuild tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (ambBuildDecl tc d)
        (ambBuild tc decls (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; A declaration nobody outside its module may name cannot make a bare
; reference ambiguous, so it is not recorded. Without this a module's
; private helper would collide with an unrelated public name in
; another module and report AX3014 about two definitions, only one of
; which the reference could ever have reached.
(pub :: ambBuildDecl (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (ambBuildDecl tc d)
  (if (== (nodeVis d) 0)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag d))
      (let m (nodeModuleOpt d))
      (if (== tag TAG_D_FN)
        (ambRecord tc (bareOf (nodeAName d)) m)
        (if (== tag TAG_D_EFFECT)
          (ambRecordAll tc (nodeBNames d) 0 m)
          (if (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
            (ambRecordCtors tc (nodeBChildren d) 0 m)
            ; Slot b, the bare NAMES - `ambRecordAll` wants names, and this is
            ; the one reader for which the parallel name vector exists.
            (if (== tag TAG_D_EXTERN)
              (ambRecordExtern tc (nodeCChildren d) 0 m)
              0))))
    }))

(pub :: ambRecordExtern (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (ambRecordExtern tc items i mod)
  (if (>= i (vecLen items))
    0
    {
      {
        (let f (vecGet items i))
        (if (== (nodeVis f) 1)
          (ambRecord tc (nodeAName f) mod))
      }
      (ambRecordExtern tc items (+ i 1) mod)
    }))

(pub :: ambRecordAll (-> TC Names Int (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (ambRecordAll tc v i m)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (ambRecord tc (vecGetStr v i) m)
      (ambRecordAll tc v (+ i 1) m)
    }))

(pub :: ambRecordCtors (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (ambRecordCtors tc ctors i m)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    0
    {
      (ambRecord tc (bareOf (nodeAName (vecGet ctors i))) m)
      (ambRecordCtors tc ctors (+ i 1) m)
    }))

(pub :: tcAllDeclsVec (-> TC Nodes))
(pub fn (tcAllDeclsVec tc)
  tc.allDecls)

; Every diagnostic records which source file its spans index. Stamped
; here rather than at the ~30 construction sites, so a new check
; cannot forget to do it.
; The unit a declaration's spans index: 0 (the entry file) when it
; carries no module stamp, else that module's entry.
(pub :: unitIndexFor (-> Units (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (unitIndexFor units m)
  (if (let (Some moduleName) m)
    (match (unitIndexForFrom units moduleName 0)
      ((None) 0)
      ((Some index) index))
    0))

(pub :: unitIndexForFrom (-> Units String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (unitIndexForFrom units m i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen units))
    None
    (if (unitIsEntry (vecGet units i))
      (unitIndexForFrom units m (+ i 1))
      (if (unitNameIs (vecGet units i) m)
        (Some i)
        (unitIndexForFrom units m (+ i 1))))))

(pub :: emitDiag (-> TC diag::Diag Int))
(pub fn (emitDiag tc d)
  {
    (diagSetUnit d (tcCurUnit tc))
    (vecPush (tcOutVec tc) d)
    0
  })

(:: joinModules (-> Names String))
(fn (joinModules v)
  {
    (let out strBuilderNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen v)
      {
        (if (> i 0)
          (strBuilderPush out ", "))
        (strBuilderPush out (vecGetStr v i))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

(:: joinQualified (-> Names String String))
(fn (joinQualified v n)
  {
    (let out strBuilderNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen v)
      {
        (if (> i 0)
          (strBuilderPush out " or "))
        (strBuilderPush out (concat (concat (concat "`" (vecGetStr v i)) "::") (concat n "`")))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

(pub :: checkVar (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkVar tc e inHead)
  ; `result` inside a contract, naming nothing: AX3050, not the
  ; AX3001 every other unresolved name gets. The cheap word test is
  ; FIRST and `&&` short-circuits, so outside a contract - which is
  ; every reference in every program in this repository today - this
  ; costs one load.
  {
    (let name (nodeAName e))
    (if (&& (isSome (tcContractTag tc))
          (&& (strEq name "result") (isNone (scopeFindFrame tc "result"))))
      {
        (emitContractResult tc e)
        (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
      }
      (if (|| (strEq name "true") (strEq name "false"))
        mkBoolTy
        ; A reference a macro template produced resolves where the
        ; template was written, which is top level, so the local scope
        ; is not consulted for it at all (MAC-HYG-9 / MAC-HYG-8.1).
        {
          (let found
            (if (!= (nodeDefScope e) 1)
              (scopeFindFrame tc name)
              None))
          (if (let (Some fr) found)
            ; A pending lambda answers through its STAMP: grounded
            ; reads the arrow, broken reads poison quietly (its
            ; diagnostics are out), in-progress names the too-early
            ; recursion (the first pass's swap discards it for
            ; honest self-recursion), and anything else answers the
            ; node itself - the use grounds it.
            {
              (set fr.used 1)
              (let ety fr.ty)
              (if (== (isPendingNode ety) 0)
                ety
                {
                  (let s (lamStamp ety))
                  (if (== (stampIsArrow s) 1)
                    s
                    (if (== (stampIsBroken s) 1)
                      (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                      (if (== (stampIsProg s) 1)
                        {
                          (emitDiag tc
                            (diagSetLabel
                              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" (nodeSpan e)
                                "type mismatch: cannot use this function while its parameter types are still being inferred"
                                None
                                (Some
                                  "use it after its definition is complete - a use through a `let` alias hides the recursion from inference")
                                "inference grounds a lambda's parameters from its uses; this use runs before the grounding finished")
                              "this use is too early to check"))
                          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                        }
                        ety)))
                })
            }
            (if (== (checkAmbiguous tc name (nodeSpan e)) 1)
              (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
              {
                (let refused
                  (if (== inHead 0)
                    (checkBareValue tc name (nodeSpan e))
                    0))
                (if (== refused 1)
                  (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                  {
                    (let entFound
                      (if (isNone (ownCtor tc name))
                        (findFnEnt tc name)
                        None))
                    (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                      {
                        (warnDeprecated tc name (nodeSpan e))
                        ; INSTANTIATED. A signature's type variables
                        ; are rigid where the body reads them and
                        ; fresh where a caller does; this is the
                        ; only place a declaration's type becomes a
                        ; reference's type, so it is the only place
                        ; that distinction has to be made - and the
                        ; only place an evidence stamp is born
                        ; (MM-LIFE-2d): the stamped variant also
                        ; hangs a TAG_EVSTAMP on this reference
                        ; when the declaration is polymorphic.
                        (tyInstStamped tc (fnEntTy ent) e)
                      }
                      {
                        (let ctorFound (findCtor tc name))
                        (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
                          {
                            (writeNodeText e 1 (ctorResolvedName ctor))
                            (tyInstStamped tc ctor.ctorTy e)
                          }
                          (if (|| (== (isBuiltinName name) 1)
                                (|| (== (isMacroName tc name) 1)
                                  (&& (== inHead 1) (showIsHead name))))
                            mkSilentWild
                            (emitUndefinedVar tc name (nodeSpan e))))
                      })
                  })
              }))
        }))
  })

; The bare-value refusals. Answers 1 when the reference was refused
; (poison the expression), 0 to continue resolving.
(pub :: checkBareValue (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (checkBareValue tc name span)
  {
    (let entFound (findFnEnt tc name))
    (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
      (if (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 1)
        ; An effect operation cannot be a bare value: it is
        ; dispatch, not data, so there is no closure to hand
        ; around. stage0 refuses with AX3017 and returns poison,
        ; and the poison is load-bearing independently of the
        ; message - without it the wildcard type would let a later
        ; check fire where stage0 is silent.
        {
          (if (! (== span 0))
            (emitDiag tc
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3017" "effect-handler-unsupported" span
                (concat
                  (concat
                    (concat
                      (concat (concat "effect operation `" (bareOf name))
                        "` cannot be used as a bare value; call it, or wrap it: `(lambda (x) (")
                      (bareOf name))
                    " x))`")
                  "")
                None None)))
          1
        }
        (if (let (Some ar) (repArity ent))
          (if (>= ar 2)
            (if (== span 0)
              1
              {
                (emitDiag tc
                  (diagSetLabel
                    (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3013" "partial-application" span
                      (concat
                        (concat (concat (concat "partial application of `" name) "`: it takes ")
                          (fmtInt ar))
                        " argument(s) and 0 were supplied")
                      None
                      (Some
                        "bind the missing arguments with a lambda: `(lambda (y) (f x y))` builds the value `(f x)` would mean")
                      "a partial application has to hold the arguments it was not given, and a top-level function has no closure record to hold them in")
                    (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" name) "` takes ") (fmtInt ar))
                      " arguments, so it cannot be used as a bare value")))
                1
              })
            ; A ONE-argument BUILT-IN (FnEnt word 3): a primitive is
            ; lowered in place at every call and has no function
            ; symbol, so unlike a one-parameter `fn` - whose bare name
            ; is a thunk - it has no value to take. `(__floatToInt)`,
            ; `(__alloc)` and eleven more checked OK and emitted
            ; `%__floatToInt`, a register nothing defines, which `llc`
            ; refused (`tests/fuzz/primitive-value.axfuzz`, found by
            ; the seeded fuzzer 2026-09-27). Every built-in of two or
            ; more took the arm above already.
            (if (&& (== ar 1) (== (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 1))
              (refuseBareForm tc name 1 span
                (concat
                  (concat "call it, or wrap it in a lambda to pass it as a value: `(lambda (x) ("
                    name)
                  " x))`")
                "a primitive is lowered in place at each call and has no function symbol, so a bare reference to one has no value to take")
              0))
          0))
      (if (== (isCastLike name) 1)
        (refuseBareForm tc name
          (if (isValueCastForm name)
            2
            1)
          span
          (if (isValueCastForm name)
            "write the value to convert before the target type - `(as e Int)` - or, for a function value, `(lambda (x) (as x Int))`"
            (concat (concat "write the type it measures: `(" name) " Int)`"))
          "`as`, `sizeof` and `alignof` require their operands; a bare form has no function value")
        {
          (let ctorFound (rfindCtor tc name))
          (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
            {
              (let fc (vecLen ctor.fieldTys))
              (if (== fc 0)
                0
                (if (== span 0)
                  1
                  {
                    (emitDiag tc
                      (diagSetLabel
                        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3009" "constructor-arity-mismatch" span
                          (concat
                            (concat
                              (concat (concat (concat "constructor `" name) "` expects ")
                                (fmtInt fc))
                              " argument(s), found ")
                            "0")
                          None
                          (Some
                            (concat (concat "this constructor takes exactly " (fmtInt fc))
                              " field(s); add or remove arguments to match")))
                        "0 argument(s) provided here"))
                    1
                  }))
            }
            0)
        }))
  })

; AX3013 for a bare name that takes `ar` arguments and has no value
; without them - a one-argument primitive, or one of the cast forms.
; Answers 1 (refused; the caller poisons), with nothing emitted for a
; spanless reference, as the arity arm above does.
(pub :: refuseBareForm (-> TC String Int Int String String Int))
(pub fn (refuseBareForm tc name ar span help note)
  {
    (if (! (== span 0))
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3013" "partial-application" span
            (concat
              (concat (concat (concat "partial application of `" name) "`: it takes ") (fmtInt ar))
              " argument(s) and 0 were supplied")
            None (Some help) note)
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" name) "` takes ") (fmtInt ar))
            " argument(s), so it cannot be used as a bare value"))))
    1
  })

; A name that IS defined, in a module that does not export it, is a
; different mistake from a name that is defined nowhere, and it used
; to be impossible to make: privacy was enforced by dropping the
; declaration, so the reference and the module's own calls failed
; together and the message was right about both by accident. Now the
; declaration is there and only the reference is refused, so the
; reference gets told why.
;
; The suggestion machinery is skipped for this branch on purpose -
; "did you mean `helper`?" about the very name that was written is
; noise.
(pub :: emitPrivateVar (-> TC String Int String ASTNode))
(pub fn (emitPrivateVar tc name span declName)
  {
    (if (&& (!= span 0)
          (if (let (Some rows) (tcPrivIdx tc))
            (== (privIdxHas rows (concat "internal " declName)) 1)
            false))
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX5002" "internal-module" span
            (concat
              (concat (concat (concat "`" (srcSpelling name)) "` belongs to `")
                (modOfName declName))
              "`, which is internal to the standard library")
            None
            (Some
              "use the library's typed interface instead, such as `vecGet` or `strSlice`; raw memory access belongs in your own function, written with the primitives under `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)`"))
          "internal to the standard library"))
      (if (!= span 0)
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3023" "private-name" span
              (concat
                (concat (concat (concat "`" (srcSpelling name)) "` is private to module `")
                  (modOfName declName))
                "`")
              None
              (Some
                "a name leaves its module when its declaration is written `pub`; if the import that brought the module in carries a name list, the name has to appear there too"))
            (concat (concat "`" (bareOf declName)) "` is not exported by its module")))))
    (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
  })

(pub :: emitUndefinedVar (-> TC String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (emitUndefinedVar tc name span)
  ; A WORD STRUCT's constructor, reached from another module: it is
  ; sealed whatever `pub` says (AX3085), and a PRIVATE one arrives
  ; here because the lookup filtered it - so the answer is the seal,
  ; not "undefined" about a name that plainly exists.
  {
    (let hidden
      (if (let (Some privateNames) (tcPrivates tc))
        (if (let (Some entry) (findFnEntAnywhere tc name))
          (if (== (strInVec privateNames entry.name 0) 1)
            (Some entry.name)
            None)
          None)
        None))
    (if (== (wordSealedHere tc (findStructAnyVis tc name)) 1)
        {
          (emitWordSealedBuild tc (findStructAnyVis tc name) span)
          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
        }
      (let (Some key) hidden)
        (emitPrivateVar tc name span key)
      (== (isMacroAnywhere tc name) 1)
        (emitPrivateVar tc name span
          (privKey (macroModuleOf (tcMacrosVec tc) name 0) (bareOf name)))
      {
        (let cm (ctorHiddenModule tc name))
        (if (isSome cm)
          (emitPrivateVar tc name span (privKey cm name))
          (emitUndefinedVarPlain tc name span))
      })
  })

; The module of an INVISIBLE `data` declaring a constructor of this
; name, or 0. A private type's constructors are already refused - the
; lookup filters them - but as `AX3001`/`AX3003`, which say the name
; does not exist when it plainly does.
(pub :: ctorHiddenModule (-> TC String (Option String)))
(pub fn (ctorHiddenModule tc name)
  (if (isNone (tcPrivates tc))
    None
    (ctorHiddenIn (tcDatasVec tc) (tcPrivIdx tc) (tcCurModule tc) name 0)))

(pub :: ctorHiddenIn
  (-> (Vec DataEnt) (Option StringRows) (Option String) String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (ctorHiddenIn datas privs curMod name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen datas))
    None
    {
      (let d (vecGet datas i))
      (if (&& (== (privBlocks privs (privKey d.module d.name) curMod) 1)
            (isSome (ctorInData d.ctors d.module name 0)))
        d.module
        (ctorHiddenIn datas privs curMod name (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: macroModuleOf (-> Nodes String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (macroModuleOf ms n i)
  {
    (let bare (bareOf n))
    (macroModuleOfBare ms bare i)
  })

(pub :: macroModuleOfBare (-> Nodes String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (macroModuleOfBare ms n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ms))
    None
    (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet ms i)) n)
      (nodeModuleOpt (vecGet ms i))
      (macroModuleOfBare ms n (+ i 1)))))

; `Some`/`None` need no import; `Ok`/`Err`/`Result` need `(import Err)`.
; The asymmetry stays - builtin `Result` would widen every prelude - so
; the use site steers instead: this names the three spellings whose
; fix is the import, and `emitUndefinedVarPlain` below says so.
(pub :: isErrImportName (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (isErrImportName n)
  (|| (strEq n "Ok") (|| (strEq n "Err") (strEq n "Result"))))

(pub :: emitUndefinedVarPlain (-> TC String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (emitUndefinedVarPlain tc name span)
  {
    (let best newBest)
    {
      (tcNoteUnknownCallee tc name)
      (offerScope best name (tcScopeVec tc) 0)
      (offerFnEnts best name (tcFnsVec tc) 0)
      (offerCtorEnts best name (tcDatasVec tc) 0)
      (if (!= span 0)
        (emitDiag tc
          {
            (let msg (concat (concat "undefined variable `" (srcSpelling name)) "`"))
            (diagSetLabel
              (if (isErrImportName name)
                (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3001" "undefined-variable" span msg None
                  (Some
                    (concat "`"
                      (concat (srcSpelling name)
                        "` is defined in the `Err` module; add `(import Err)`"))))
                (if (let (Some cand) best.cand)
                  (mkDiagFix SEV_ERROR "AX3001" "undefined-variable" span msg None
                    (concat (concat "a similarly named binding `" cand)
                      "` is in scope; did you mean this?")
                    (diagFix span cand))
                  (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3001" "undefined-variable" span msg None
                    (Some
                      "variables must be defined (via `fn`, a `let` binding, or a lambda parameter) before they are used; check for typos"))))
              (concat (concat "no binding named `" (srcSpelling name)) "` in scope"))
          }))
      (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; MM-PAR-6: what a `parallel` binding may CAPTURE.
;
; `(parallel p ((a e1) (b e2)) body)` is a PARSER desugaring - each
; binding becomes `(__par_spawn (lambda (parallel$_) e) 0)` - so the
; only thing the checker ever sees is an ordinary two-argument
; application with a lambda in argument 0. That is the whole reason
; this rule lives here and not in codegen: at `parScan` time the type
; table is not yet populated, so `fldClass` answers "not a reference"
; for every struct and every `data` - a check that passes on exactly
; the types it exists for. `codegen.ax`'s `emitParThread` header
; records the measurement.
;
; WHAT IS WRONG WITH A CAPTURE. Under `--threads` a binding's thunk
; runs on a real pthread while the parent keeps running, so a heap
; value the thunk names is reachable from two threads at once. Neither
; `axiom_retain` nor `axiom_release` is an `atomicrmw` - both are a
; plain load-add-store, by design and everywhere - so the two threads
; lose an increment and the block is freed while a live reference
; names it. Corruption, not a leak.
;
; UNCONDITIONAL, and not gated on `--threads`. The checker does not
; see codegen's flags, and a diagnostic that appears and disappears
; with a build flag is unlike anything else in the AX3xxx band. So the
; language rule is one rule and the PROCESS lowering is merely
; permitted to be laxer than it: `__proc_spawn` names that lowering
; explicitly - a separate address space, MM-PAR-3's isolation by
; construction - and is exempt for that reason and not because a flag
; was passed. `__par_spawn` (which is whichever lowering the build
; picks, so it must assume the sharper one) and `__thread_spawn` are
; refused alike.
;
; WHAT IS REFUSED IS EVERY CLASS BUT 0, and the width is a
; measurement rather than a preference - `capReport` carries the
; numbers. `evClassOf`'s 0 is the only answer that PROMISES no share
; is taken; 1, `2+k` and the negatives each mean "a reference, or
; possibly one", and the corpus contains none of them at a spawn, so
; the wide rule costs nothing and leaves no hole to come back to.
;
; `Vec` is a counted reference, class 1, and refused for that; its
; refusal names the second reason too: its handle names a mutable
; buffer, and a worker growing that buffer would publish thread-arena
; memory to the parent, then unmap it at thread exit (MM-PAR-6a).
; `tests/diagnostics/656-parallel-container-capture.ax` holds direct,
; aliased and nested containers, and `tests/stdlib/471-parallel-trap.ax`
; builds its vector inside the worker instead of capturing it.
;
; `Foreign` IS CLASS 0 FOR THE SAME REASON, and listed beside it for
; it: a bare `Foreign` word is skipped by the release walk
; (`tests/ffi/demo/410-foreign-not-walked.ax`), so no retain it races
; exists. The near miss is `Handle` - a COUNTED block carrying a
; destructor, class 1, refused - and
; `tests/diagnostics/655-parallel-capture-foreign.ax` pins the two
; answers side by side so no classifier change can silently flip
; either.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The two spawn primitives whose lowering SHARES an address space with
; the parent. `__proc_spawn` is deliberately not here: it names the
; forked lowering, whose isolation is MM-PAR-3's by construction, and
; exempting it is a statement about that primitive rather than about a
; build flag. `__par_spawn` is whichever lowering the build selects, so
; it has to assume the sharper one.
(pub :: capSpawnHead (-> String Int))
(pub fn (capSpawnHead nm)
  (if (|| (strEq nm "__par_spawn") (strEq nm "__thread_spawn"))
    1
    0))

(pub :: checkSpawnCaptures (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkSpawnCaptures tc lam)
  ; A bare TOP-LEVEL function reference - `(__par_spawn worker 0)` -
  ; is not a lambda and captures nothing: a top-level name is a
  ; symbol, not a record. `scopeFindFrame` reads the current frame
  ; only, so a name it cannot find is either top-level (safe) or
  ; undefined (some other diagnostic's: AX3001) - either way silent
  ; here.
  ;
  ; A FRAME-LOCAL name holding a function is the opposite: one hop the
  ; walk cannot see through. `(fn (viaHop f w) (__par_join
  ; (__par_spawn f w)))` spawns whatever function its caller passes,
  ; and a caller passing a lambda that captures a `String` compiled
  ; and ran under `--threads` with no diagnostic - the capture check
  ; ran at the spawn, where the thunk is the parameter `f`, and a
  ; parameter is not a lambda, so the old rule answered 0. The fix
  ; refuses the indirection itself: a spawn whose thunk is a
  ; frame-local name of arrow type draws AX3064, because its captures
  ; are not visible where it stands. Write the lambda at the spawn, or
  ; name a top-level function; both are checked where they stand.
  ;
  ; A frame-local of NON-arrow type is the argument checker's, not
  ; this rule's: the application is ill-typed without any capture
  ; question, and reporting it here too would say one mistake twice.
  ; Poison and the silent wildcard stay silent for the cascade reason
  ; `capReport` gives.
  ;
  ; Anything that is neither a lambda nor a bare name is walked
  ; STRUCTURALLY (`capWalkThunk`, `tests/diagnostics/644`): a
  ; conditional, a match, a `let` and a brace block choose or build
  ; the closure, so every lambda they can answer is scanned where it
  ; stands - while whatever they read to choose or build it is
  ; collected as a capture, because a `let`-bound value and a match
  ; scrutinee flow into the closure that names them. A `set` walks
  ; its value, a `handle` its body and a `(region r ...)` its body:
  ; the value is what each of those forms answers. What is none of
  ; a lambda, a name, or those transparent forms - a call result, a
  ; field - is refused outright (`emitSpawnOpaque`): its captures
  ; are not visible where the spawn stands, exactly as a frame-local
  ; name's are not. What answers a word - a literal, a constructor,
  ; an allocation, a loop, a field store - is the argument
  ; checker's: it cannot be the spawned closure. No opaque shape
  ; reaches a spawn from `parallel`'s desugaring, which always emits
  ; a literal lambda, and none is in the corpus - so the refusal
  ; costs nothing measured and leaves no shape accepted unseen.
  ;
  ; A `parallel` BINDING'S THUNK may borrow (MM-PAR-6b): the desugaring
  ; names its parameter `parallel$_`, which no program can spell, and
  ; wraps its spawns in a borrow scope, so a captured `String` is read
  ; under a frozen count. Every other spawn keeps the refusal.
  (if (== (nodeTag lam) TAG_E_LAM)
    (capReportFor tc (capScanInto (nodeBChild lam) (capBoundOf (nodeANames lam)) vecNew) 0 vecNew
      (capSpawnBorrowId lam))
    (if (!= (nodeTag lam) TAG_E_VAR)
      (capThunkCheck tc lam)
      (capThunkVar tc lam vecNew))))

; The thunk is neither a lambda literal nor a bare name: walk it to
; every closure it can answer, scanning each where it stands, and
; refuse it if any answer is opaque. `bound` holds the thunk's own
; binders - a `let` name, a match pattern - so a use of one is not
; mistaken for a capture of the parent; `acc` gathers the
; occurrences the values and scrutinees contribute, because those
; are evaluated in the parent and flow into the closure; `done`
; keeps the opaque refusal to one per spawn site.
(pub :: capThunkCheck (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (capThunkCheck tc e)
  {
    (let bound vecNew)
    (let acc vecNew)
    (let done vecNew)
    {
      (capWalkThunk tc e bound acc done)
      (capReport tc acc 0 vecNew)
    }
  })

; One spawn thunk's structure. The condition of an `if` and all but
; the last of a brace block run in the parent and choose nothing the
; child holds, so they are not walked; the
; branches, the bodies and the last are the closure. A `let`'s value
; is walked BEFORE its name is bound, as `capScanInto` walks it: the
; value cannot see the binding, and the body can. A match
; scrutinee IS walked, unlike a condition: the arm patterns bind
; parts of it, so a closure over a pattern holds the parent's block
; through the scrutinee (`rgnBindPat` reads patterns the same way).
(pub :: capWalkThunk (-> TC ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) Ints Int))
(pub fn (capWalkThunk tc e bound acc done)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
        {
          (let mark (vecLen bound))
          {
            (capPushAll (nodeANames e) 0 bound)
            (capScanInto (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
            (scopeTrunc bound mark)
          }
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          (capThunkVar tc e bound)
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
            {
              (capScanInto (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
              {
                (let mark (vecLen bound))
                {
                  (vecPush bound (nodeAName e))
                  (capWalkThunk tc (nodeCChild e) bound acc done)
                  (scopeTrunc bound mark)
                }
              }
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
              {
                (capWalkThunk tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc done)
                (capWalkThunk tc (nodeCChild e) bound acc done)
              }
              ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds none, so no arm
              ; walks one. A variadic `if` arrives as nested `if`s
              ; and walks through the arm above.
              (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                {
                  (capScanInto (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                  (capWalkMatchArms tc (nodeBChildren e) 0 bound acc done)
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                  {
                    (let v (nodeAChildren e))
                    (if (== (vecLen v) 0)
                      0
                      (capWalkThunk tc (vecGet v (- (vecLen v) 1)) bound acc done))
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                    (capWalkThunk tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc done)
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                      (capWalkThunk tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc done)
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                        (capWalkThunk tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc done)
                        (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_INT)
                              (|| (== tag TAG_E_FLT)
                                (|| (== tag TAG_E_STR)
                                  (|| (== tag TAG_E_CHAR)
                                    (|| (== tag TAG_E_QUOTE)
                                      (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_SETF)))))))
                          0
                          (capOpaqueOnce tc e tag done)))))))))))
    }))

; Every match arm's body under its own pattern's binders, as
; `capScanArms` binds them.
(pub :: capWalkMatchArms (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Names (Vec ASTNode) Ints Int))
(pub fn (capWalkMatchArms tc arms i bound acc done)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      {
        (let mark (vecLen bound))
        {
          (capPatBind (nodeAChild a) bound)
          (capWalkThunk tc (nodeBChild a) bound acc done)
          (scopeTrunc bound mark)
          (capWalkMatchArms tc arms (+ i 1) bound acc done)
        }
      }
    }))

; A bare name where a thunk stands: the indirection half of AX3064,
; shared by the top-level spawn and every transparent form above it.
; A name the thunk bound itself is not a capture. A name no frame
; holds is top-level (safe) or undefined (AX3001's) - either way
; silent here. Of the rest, an arrow may close over a reference the
; checker cannot see, and anything else is the argument checker's.
(pub :: capThunkVar (-> TC ASTNode Names Int))
(pub fn (capThunkVar tc e bound)
  (if (isSome (strVecIdx bound (nodeAName e)))
    0
    (if (let (Some ent) (scopeFindFrame tc (nodeAName e)))
      {
        (let ty ent.ty)
        (if (|| (== (tyIsErr ty) 1) (== (tyIsSilentWild ty) 1))
          0
          (if (== (tyIsArrow ty) 1)
            (emitSpawnIndirection tc (nodeAName e) ty (nodeSpan e))
            0))
      }
      0)))

; The opaque half of AX3064: the thunk is a shape whose captures no
; walk can see through - a call result, a field - so the spawn is
; refused at the shape rather than at a name. Same code because it
; is the same rule (MM-PAR-6); different message because there is
; no name to fix - the fix is to restructure. `done` answers
; whether this site already drew it.
(pub :: capOpaqueOnce (-> TC ASTNode Int Ints Int))
(pub fn (capOpaqueOnce tc e tag done)
  (if (>= (vecLen done) 1)
    0
    {
      (vecPush done 1)
      (emitSpawnOpaque tc
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          "a call result"
          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
            "a field"
            "an expression"))
        (nodeSpan e))
    }))

; The opaque half's diagnostic, beside `emitSpawnCapture` and
; `emitSpawnIndirection`.
(pub :: emitSpawnOpaque (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitSpawnOpaque tc shape span)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3064" "parallel-capture" span
          (concat (concat "a concurrent binding spawns " shape)
            ", whose captures this checker cannot see")
          None
          (Some
            "write the lambda at the spawn, or name a top-level function: a literal is checked where it stands and a top-level name captures nothing")
          "MM-PAR-6: a binding runs BESIDE its parent, and `axiom_retain`/`axiom_release` are a plain load-add-store rather than an `atomicrmw` - two threads touching one block's count lose an increment and free a block a live reference still names. The rule is the language's and not the lowering's, so it does not depend on `--threads`; `__proc_spawn` names the isolated lowering and is exempt")
        "the thunk's captures are not visible here"))
    0
  })

; The lambda's own parameters, COPIED. The walk pushes and truncates
; this vector as it enters and leaves binders, and the node's own
; parameter vector is read again by `checkLam` afterwards.
(pub :: capBoundOf (-> Names Names))
(pub fn (capBoundOf params)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen params)
        (vecPush v (vecGet params i)))
      v
    }
  })

; Every TAG_E_VAR under `e` that no binder BETWEEN `e` and the
; occurrence introduced, in source order, pushed into `acc`; answers
; `acc`. `castScanInto`'s twin, predicate swapped from "head named
; `cast`" to "a name this body did not bind", plus the binder arms
; that walker has no use for. Duplicated rather than shared for the
; reason `wrapScanInto` gives: this file already hand-duplicates a
; full tree walk per collected fact, and a generic walker would be a
; new abstraction for one rule.
;
; THE BINDER ARMS ARE THE WHOLE CORRECTNESS ARGUMENT. A name the
; thunk binds itself is not a capture, and a thunk that shadows an
; outer `String` with its own `let` would otherwise be refused for
; using its own binding - the one false positive this shape can
; have. `for` needs no arm of its own: the parser desugars it into
; `let`/`letm`/`while`, which are here.
(pub :: capScanInto (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (capScanInto e bound acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    acc
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      {
        (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          (if (isNone (strVecIdx bound (nodeAName e)))
            (vecPush acc e))
          (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
            {
              ; The HEAD too, unlike `castScanInto`: a head that names
              ; a local closure is a capture of that closure's record,
              ; and calling it from the child thread touches its count.
              ; A head naming a top-level function resolves to nothing
              ; in the frame and drops out at `capReport`.
              (capScanIn (spineHead e) bound acc)
              (capScanVec (spineArgVec e vecNew) 0 bound acc)
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
              {
                (capScanIn (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                (capScanIn (nodeCChild e) bound acc)
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                ; Word 2 is a Vec of TAG_E_VAR nodes naming EFFECTS,
                ; not values, and walking it would report an effect
                ; name as a captured variable.
                {
                  (capScanIn (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                  (capScanIn (nodeCChild e) bound acc)
                }
                (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                  {
                    (capScanIn (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                    {
                      (let mark (vecLen bound))
                      {
                        (vecPush bound (nodeAName e))
                        (capScanIn (nodeCChild e) bound acc)
                        (scopeTrunc bound mark)
                      }
                    }
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                    {
                      (let mark (vecLen bound))
                      {
                        (capPushAll (nodeANames e) 0 bound)
                        (capScanIn (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                        (scopeTrunc bound mark)
                      }
                    }
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                      {
                        (capScanIn (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                        (capScanIn (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                        (capScanIn (nodeCChild e) bound acc)
                      }
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                        (capScanVec (nodeAChildren e) 0 bound acc)
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                          {
                            (capScanIn (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                            (capScanVec (nodeBChildren e) 0 bound acc)
                          }
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                            ; Word 1 is the region's NAME, which
                            ; nothing refers to as a value.
                            (capScanIn (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                              ; The TARGET is `checkSet`'s: assigning
                              ; an outer binding from inside a lambda
                              ; is already MM-MUT-1a's refusal, and
                              ; reporting it again here would say the
                              ; same thing twice.
                              (capScanIn (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                                (capScanIn (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                                ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds
                                ; none, so no arm scans one.
                                (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                                  {
                                    (capScanIn (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                                    (capScanArms (nodeBChildren e) 0 bound acc)
                                  })))))))))))))
        acc
      }
    }))

; `capScanInto` for effect, answering 0 - the arms above sequence
; several of these in a block.
(pub :: capScanIn (-> ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (capScanIn e bound acc)
  {
    (capScanInto e bound acc)
    0
  })

(pub :: capScanVec (-> Nodes Int Names (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (capScanVec v i bound acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (capScanIn (vecGet v i) bound acc)
      (capScanVec v (+ i 1) bound acc)
    }))

; Each arm's PATTERN binds, and only inside that arm's body.
(pub :: capScanArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Names (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (capScanArms arms i bound acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      {
        {
          (let mark (vecLen bound))
          {
            (capPatBind (nodeAChild a) bound)
            (capScanIn (nodeBChild a) bound acc)
            (scopeTrunc bound mark)
          }
        }
        (capScanArms arms (+ i 1) bound acc)
      }
    }))

(pub :: capPushAll (-> Names Int Names Int))
(pub fn (capPushAll params i bound)
  (if (>= i (vecLen params))
    0
    {
      (vecPush bound (vecGet params i))
      (capPushAll params (+ i 1) bound)
    }))

; The names a pattern binds, on `checkPatternTyped`'s reading of the
; same nodes: a bare identifier binds (`_`, `true` and `false`
; excepted), a constructor spine and a named-field pattern bind
; through their sub-patterns, and a literal binds nothing.
(pub :: capPatBind (-> ASTNode Names Int))
(pub fn (capPatBind pat bound)
  (if (nodeSame pat nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag pat))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName pat) "_")
              (|| (strEq (nodeAName pat) "true") (strEq (nodeAName pat) "false")))
          0
          {
            (vecPush bound (nodeAName pat))
            0
          })
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (capPatBindVec (spineArgVec pat vecNew) 0 bound)
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (capPatBindVec (nodeCChildren pat) 0 bound)
            0)))
    }))

(pub :: capPatBindVec (-> Nodes Int Names Int))
(pub fn (capPatBindVec v i bound)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (capPatBind (vecGet v i) bound)
      (capPatBindVec v (+ i 1) bound)
    }))

; One diagnostic per NAME, at its first occurrence, so a thunk that
; reads the same string three times is refused once.
;
; `scopeFindFrame` and not `scopeFindIdx`: the scope vector holds the
; MODULE walk's entries below `frameBase` - every signature seen so
; far - and `scopeFindIdx` searches all of them. Measured: with that
; lookup, `tests/stdlib/470-parallel.ax` is refused at `spin`, because
; a top-level `(-> Int Int)` is a TAG_T_ARR and `evClassOf` answers 1
; for one. Value resolution reads the frame only, which is the rule
; this has to agree with.
(pub :: capReport (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Names Int))
(pub fn (capReport tc occ i seen)
  (capReportFor tc occ i seen None))

; `(parallel$lends b {})`, which only `parallel`'s desugaring writes
; (MM-PAR-6b): the borrow record is checked as any name is, the block is
; not checked at all - it is empty now, and what `capBorrowLend` puts in
; it later is a frame-local immutable value by construction - and the pair is
; recorded for the form's thunks to find.
(pub :: checkParLends (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkParLends tc e)
  {
    (let bvar (nodeBChild (nodeAChild e)))
    {
      (checkExprH tc bvar 0)
      (vecPush (tcParLends tc) bvar)
      (vecPush (tcParLends tc) (nodeBChild e))
      mkIntTy
    }
  })

; A `parallel` binding's thunk: a lambda whose parameter the desugaring
; named `parallel$_` and the form's number. Answers Some number, or None
; for any other lambda, whose captures keep AX3064's refusal.
(pub :: capSpawnBorrowId (-> ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (capSpawnBorrowId lam)
  {
    (let ps (nodeANames lam))
    (if (== (vecLen ps) 1)
      {
        (let pn (vecGet ps 0))
        (if (strStartsWith pn "parallel$_")
          (Some (strSlice pn 10 (- (strLen pn) 10)))
          None)
      }
      None)
  })

; The borrow block of the form numbered `id`: the most recent placeholder
; whose record is named `parallel$b` and that number, or None.
(pub :: capBorrowBlock (-> TC String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (capBorrowBlock tc id)
  {
    (let v (tcParLends tc))
    (let want (concat "parallel$b" id))
    (let mut i (- (vecLen v) 2))
    (let mut found (:: None (Option ASTNode)))
    {
      (while (&& (>= i 0) (isNone found))
        {
          (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet v i)) want)
            (set found (Some (vecGet v (+ i 1)))))
          (set i (- i 2))
        })
      found
    }
  })

; Lend `name` in the form numbered `id`: append it to that form's borrow
; block once. Answers 1 when the form's placeholder was found, and 0
; when it was not - then the capture is refused, since nothing would
; freeze its count.
(pub :: capBorrowLend (-> TC String String Int Int))
(pub fn (capBorrowLend tc id name span)
  (if (let (Some blk) (capBorrowBlock tc id))
    {
      (let xs (nodeAChildren blk))
      {
        (if (! (capBlockNames xs name 0))
          (vecPush xs (mkEVarAt name span)))
        1
      }
    }
    0))

(pub :: capBlockNames (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Bool))
(pub fn (capBlockNames xs name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
    false
    (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet xs i)) name)
      true
      (capBlockNames xs name (+ i 1)))))

; A scoped borrow may reach only immutable blocks: strings, data
; constructors whose instantiated fields are themselves borrowable, and
; `sealed shared` structs. Other structs, containers, closures, foreign
; resources and unknown types are refused. The runtime lends every mapped child before any spawn.
(pub :: capTyBorrowable (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (capTyBorrowable tc ty)
  (capBorrowableIn tc ty vecNew vecNew 0))

; `seen` keys fully instantiated types. `active` holds the data names
; on this path: recursive uses at the same instantiation are safe, but
; a recursive type that grows its arguments is refused before an
; unbounded expansion. Independent instantiations in sibling fields
; are checked separately. The depth bound also keeps this proof finite.
(pub :: capBorrowableIn (-> TC ASTNode Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (capBorrowableIn tc t seen active depth)
  {
    (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve t) 0))
    (if (|| (nodeSame ty nilNode) (>= depth 64))
      0
      (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
        0
        (if (== (showTyGround ty) 0)
          0
          {
            (let n (nodeAName ty))
            (if (|| (strEq n "String")
                  (|| (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
                    (|| (strEq n "Float") (|| (strEq n "Bool") (strEq n "Char")))))
              1
              ; A `sealed shared` struct (MM-PAR-16): its module built
              ; every operation for several bindings at once, and the
              ; lend freezes its block and its owner.
              (if (== (structEntIsSharedHeap (findStructAnyVis tc n)) 1)
                1
                {
                  (let dentFound (findData tc n))
                  (if (let (Some dent) dentFound)
                    {
                      (let ctors (dataEntCtors dent))
                      (if (== (vecLen ctors) 0)
                        0
                        {
                          (let key (showTyName ty))
                          (if (isSome (strVecIdx seen key))
                            1
                            (if (isSome (strVecIdx active n))
                              0
                              {
                                (vecPush seen key)
                                (vecPush active n)
                                {
                                  (let answer (capBorrowableCtors tc ctors ty seen active depth 0))
                                  {
                                    (vecPop active)
                                    answer
                                  }
                                }
                              }))
                        })
                    }
                    0)
                }))
          })))
  })

(pub :: capBorrowableCtors (-> TC (Vec CtorEnt) ASTNode Strings Strings Int Int Int))
(pub fn (capBorrowableCtors tc ctors ty seen active depth i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    1
    (if (==
          (capBorrowableFields tc (showCtorFieldTys tc (vecGet ctors i) ty) seen active (+ depth 1)
            0)
          0)
      0
      (capBorrowableCtors tc ctors ty seen active depth (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: capBorrowableFields (-> TC Nodes Strings Strings Int Int Int))
(pub fn (capBorrowableFields tc tys seen active depth i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tys))
    1
    (if (== (capBorrowableIn tc (vecGet tys i) seen active depth) 0)
      0
      (capBorrowableFields tc tys seen active depth (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: capReportFor (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Names (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (capReportFor tc occ i seen borrow)
  (if (>= i (vecLen occ))
    0
    {
      (let v (vecGet occ i))
      {
        {
          (let name (nodeAName v))
          (if (! (isSome (strVecIdx seen name)))
            {
              (let found (scopeFindFrame tc name))
              (if (let (Some ent) found)
                ; POISON AND THE SILENT WILDCARD SAY NOTHING, and a
                ; check that reported them would break the cascade
                ; rule this file keeps everywhere else - `rgCheckStore`
                ; spells the same guard one screen up. It matters
                ; here in particular because `evClassOf` answers a
                ; NEGATIVE class to the silent wildcard, and the arm
                ; below refuses every negative: without this line an
                ; unrelated earlier error would grow an AX3064 of its
                ; own on top of it.
                {
                  (let ty ent.ty)
                  (if (! (|| (== (tyIsErr ty) 1) (== (tyIsSilentWild ty) 1)))
                    ; EVERYTHING BUT CLASS 0. `evClassOf` answers 0
                    ; for a value that takes no share of a count -
                    ; the scalars and `Foreign`, each listed in
                    ; `evScalarName` with the ownership reason -
                    ; and that is the only answer which is a PROMISE
                    ; that no count is raced over. 1 is a reference
                    ; outright. `2+k`
                    ; is a type the caller chooses, so it may be a
                    ; reference at some call. And negative is two
                    ; things, neither of them a promise: `EV_LAMARG
                    ; - d` (-2 and down) is the enclosing lambda's
                    ; own parameter, which codegen retains under a
                    ; bit of the word the APPLICATION passes - a
                    ; reference exactly when that bit is set - and
                    ; -1 is a minted placeholder, which emits no
                    ; `__retainref` at all and is MM-LIFE-2g's known
                    ; hole rather than a classification.
                    ;
                    ; Refusing the last two is free, and that is a
                    ; measurement rather than a guess: swept over
                    ; every one of the 611 `.ax` files in the tree
                    ; on 2026-09-03, the corpus contains TWELVE
                    ; captures at a spawn, in four files
                    ; (`tests/stdlib/470-parallel.ax` x5,
                    ; `471-parallel-trap.ax` x1, and
                    ; `tests/fmt/syntax-zoo.ax` and its `.expected`
                    ; x3 each), and EVERY ONE classifies 0. Nothing
                    ; reaches `2+k` or a negative, so the wider rule
                    ; costs the corpus nothing and closes the hole
                    ; for the first `parallel` written inside a
                    ; generic function.
                    ;
                    ; `Vec` is class 1, a counted reference, and is
                    ; refused for a second reason as well, which its
                    ; message names: its handle names a MUTABLE
                    ; buffer, and two bindings that capture it can
                    ; grow it at once. Measured with the refusal off:
                    ; a binding that pushes onto a captured `Vec`
                    ; under `--threads` reallocates the parent's
                    ; buffer out of the THREAD's arena, and the
                    ; parent's handle names memory that goes away with
                    ; the thread (MM-PAR-6a). A `Foreign` stays
                    ; accepted: it names memory Axiom never allocated,
                    ; and its thread-safety is the foreign side's
                    ; (MM-FFI-7).
                    ;
                    ; A WORD STRUCT IS CLASS 0 AND STILL ASKED, the
                    ; third question beside the class and `Vec`: is it
                    ; `shared`? Its module says whether the word names
                    ; something built for use from every binding at
                    ; once - a channel's does - and a word struct not
                    ; declared so is refused like any other value the
                    ; binding has no business holding.
                    {
                      (let cls (evClassOf tc ty))
                      (if (&& (!= cls 0) (capNotBorrowed tc borrow name ty (nodeSpan v)))
                        {
                          (vecPush seen name)
                          (emitSpawnCapture tc name ty (nodeSpan v) cls)
                        }
                        (if (== (tyIsUnsharedWord tc ty) 1)
                          {
                            (vecPush seen name)
                            (emitCaptureUnshared tc name ty (nodeSpan v))
                          }))
                    })
                })
            })
        }
        (capReportFor tc occ (+ i 1) seen borrow)
      }
    }))

; A capture a `parallel` thunk may keep: an immutable value
; (`capTyBorrowable`) lent to its own form. True means refuse it.
(pub :: capNotBorrowed (-> TC (Option String) String ASTNode Int Bool))
(pub fn (capNotBorrowed tc borrow name ty span)
  (if (let (Some id) borrow)
    (if (== (strLen id) 0)
      true
      (if (== (capTyBorrowable tc ty) 0)
        true
        (== (capBorrowLend tc id name span) 0)))
    true))

; A captured type that is a `Vec`, after resolution: a counted
; reference whose handle is also a mutable heap buffer, which the
; refusal names (`emitSpawnCapture`).
(pub :: capTyIsVec (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (capTyIsVec ty)
  {
    (let rt (tyResolve ty))
    (if (nodeSame rt nilNode)
      0
      (if (== (nodeTag rt) TAG_T_CON)
        (if (strEq (nodeAName rt) "Vec")
          1
          0)
        0))
  })

(pub :: emitSpawnCapture (-> TC String ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (emitSpawnCapture tc name ty span cls)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3064" "parallel-capture" span
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat "a concurrent binding captures `" name) "`, which has type `")
              (tyRender ty))
            (if (== (capTyIsVec ty) 1)
              "` - a mutable container the parent also holds, which a binding could grow while another reads it"
              (if (== cls 1)
                "` - a reference the parent also holds"
                (if (>= cls 2)
                  "` - a type the caller chooses, so it may be a reference here"
                  "` - a type this checker cannot classify, so it cannot promise the block's count is untouched"))))
          None
          (Some
            "pass the value in through the thunk's word argument instead of capturing it, or build a copy of it inside the binding. A `parallel` binding may borrow a String or an immutable data graph its parent holds. Mutable, unknown and type-changing recursive payloads cannot be borrowed; the structural check examines at most 64 nested types. Use a shared word handle or explicit serialization for other input")
          "MM-PAR-6: a binding runs BESIDE its parent, and `axiom_retain`/`axiom_release` are a plain load-add-store rather than an `atomicrmw` - two threads touching one block's count lose an increment and free a block a live reference still names. The rule is the language's and not the lowering's, so it does not depend on `--threads`; `__proc_spawn` names the isolated lowering and is exempt")
        (concat (concat "`" name) "` is bound outside this binding")))
    0
  })

; A captured word struct its module did not declare `shared`, or a
; `Spawn`. Same code, because it is the same rule (MM-PAR-6: what a binding may share
; with its parent); its own message, because the fix is different - the
; value is a word, and the question is what its module built it for.
(pub :: emitCaptureUnshared (-> TC String ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (emitCaptureUnshared tc name ty span)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3064" "parallel-capture" span
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat "a concurrent binding captures `" name) "`, which has type `")
              (tyRender ty))
            (if (strEq (tyRender ty) "Spawn")
              "` - a spawn handle, which only the binding that spawned it may join"
              "` - a handle its module did not declare `shared`"))
          None
          (Some
            (if (strEq (tyRender ty) "Spawn")
              "join the handle in the binding that spawned it: a spawn handle belongs to the binding that made it, and a join from another thread is refused at run time"
              (concat
                (concat
                  "use the handle only in the binding that holds it, or have its module declare `(struct "
                  (tyRender ty))
                " word shared ...)` if every operation on it is safe from several bindings at once")))
          "MM-PAR-6: a word struct crosses into a concurrent binding only when its module declares it `shared`, which says every operation on it is safe from several bindings at once; `__proc_spawn` names the isolated lowering and is exempt")
        (concat (concat "`" name) "` is bound outside this binding")))
    0
  })

; The indirection half of AX3064: the spawned thunk is a frame-local
; NAME of arrow type, so its captures are not visible where it stands.
; Same code because it is the same rule (MM-PAR-6); different message
; because the name itself is not the captured reference - whatever
; function it holds may close over one, and the checker cannot tell
; from here.
(pub :: emitSpawnIndirection (-> TC String ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (emitSpawnIndirection tc name ty span)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3064" "parallel-capture" span
          (concat
            (concat
              (concat (concat "a concurrent binding spawns `" name)
                "`, a local name holding a function of type `")
              (tyRender ty))
            "` whose captures this checker cannot see")
          None
          (Some
            "write the lambda at the spawn, or name a top-level function: a literal is checked where it stands and a top-level name captures nothing")
          "MM-PAR-6: a binding runs BESIDE its parent, and `axiom_retain`/`axiom_release` are a plain load-add-store rather than an `atomicrmw` - two threads touching one block's count lose an increment and free a block a live reference still names. The rule is the language's and not the lowering's, so it does not depend on `--threads`; `__proc_spawn` names the isolated lowering and is exempt")
        (concat (concat "`" name) "` is bound outside this binding")))
    0
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Applications. At each spine's ROOT: the saturation check (AX3013 /
; AX3009 for under-application, with the refused spine's arguments
; still checked and the head left alone). At EVERY level - stage0
; does not gate these on the root: the numeric-operator intercept,
; the cast/sizeof/alignof forms (their first argument is a TYPE, not
; a variable reference), the macro-head skip, and struct
; construction. Then the general rule: an arrow consumes an argument
; (a mismatched argument reports and does NOT poison the result - a
; curried call reports every bad argument); poison propagates
; silently; a silent wildcard stays silent; anything else is
; "expected function type".
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A bare `__store64` of a reference-typed value through `cast`.
;
; `__store64` takes no share: it writes the word and nothing else,
; so storing a reference without retaining it leaves a share the
; release walk never counted, and the owner's release frees a block
; this word still names. The named wrapper is `memSetWord`, which
; retains first (`stdlib/Mem.ax`); `Ffi.ax`'s handle paths are its
; only other legitimate writers, and every use in this repository
; stores a scalar or goes through one of them.
;
; Runs at the spine root after this level's argument is checked, so
; the operand below was checked exactly once, by the normal flow -
; this arm reads types, it never checks. Only the cast-headed shape
; is read: a direct (uncast) reference against `Int` is AX3004's,
; decided below. Only what the checker can SEE is refused: a
; variable resolved without diagnostics (or a literal, whose type
; is its tag), and only a reference class (`evClassOf` 1, the same
; notion the release walk trusts). A call result, a field, poison,
; the untyped and the unclassifiable are skipped: missing those is
; the status quo, not a regression, and refusing them would need a
; type this site cannot honestly name.
(pub :: checkStoreWordRefusal (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkStoreWordRefusal tc e val)
  (if (!= (nodeTag val) TAG_E_APP)
    0
    (if (divHeadIsCast val)
      (if (let (Some oty) (storeOperandTy tc (storeCastOperand val)))
        (if (== (tyIsErr oty) 1)
          0
          (if (!= (evClassOf tc oty) 1)
            0
            (emitStoreWordUnretained tc e val oty)))
        0)
      0)))

; Through nested casts to the original value: `(cast Int (cast Int
; s))` stores `s`'s block all the same, so one level is not enough.
; Stops at surplus - `((cast Int f) x)` stores the application of
; the cast result, whose type the cast target already names, and
; that application node is not a shape below judgment calls. Always
; descends to a strict subnode, so it terminates with the source.
(pub :: storeCastOperand (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (storeCastOperand v)
  (if (!= (nodeTag v) TAG_E_APP)
    v
    (if (divHeadIsCast v)
      {
        (let args (spineArgVec v vecNew))
        (if (!= (vecLen args) 2)
          v
          (storeCastOperand (vecGet args 1)))
      }
      v)))

; The type of a store operand this site may judge: a variable
; resolved the way `checkVar` resolves it but silently (a name
; nothing declares already drew its diagnostic from the normal
; flow, and this arm adds none), or a literal, whose type is its
; tag. Anything else is None, the absence of an answer this site
; may act on.
(pub :: storeOperandTy (-> TC ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (storeOperandTy tc op)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag op))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
      (storeVarTy tc op)
      (if (== tag TAG_E_INT)
        (Some mkIntTy)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_FLT)
          (Some mkFloatTy)
          (if (== tag TAG_E_CHAR)
            (Some mkCharTy)
            (if (== tag TAG_E_STR)
              (Some mkStringTy)
              None)))))
  })

; `checkVar`'s resolution without its diagnostics: the local scope
; (never for a template-owned reference, which resolves where the
; template was written), then the function table, then the
; constructors. `true` and `false` are Bool spellings, not names,
; and a miss is None rather than a second AX3001.
(:: storeKnownTy (-> ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(fn (storeKnownTy ty)
  (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
    None
    (Some ty)))

(pub :: storeVarTy (-> TC ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (storeVarTy tc v)
  {
    (let name (nodeAName v))
    (if (|| (strEq name "true") (strEq name "false"))
      None
      (if (== (nodeDefScope v) 1)
        {
          (let entFound (findFnEnt tc name))
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            (storeKnownTy (fnEntTy ent))
            {
              (let ctorFound (findCtor tc name))
              (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
                (storeKnownTy ctor.ctorTy)
                None)
            })
        }
        {
          (let found (scopeFindFrame tc name))
          (if (let (Some fr) found)
            (storeKnownTy fr.ty)
            {
              (let entFound (findFnEnt tc name))
              (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                (storeKnownTy (fnEntTy ent))
                {
                  (let ctorFound (findCtor tc name))
                  (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
                    (storeKnownTy ctor.ctorTy)
                    None)
                })
            })
        }))
  })

(pub :: emitStoreWordUnretained (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (emitStoreWordUnretained tc e val oty)
  {
    (let sp (spanOf val))
    (if (== sp 0)
      0
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3071" "unretained-store" sp
            (concat (concat "storing a `" (tyRender oty))
              "` with a bare `__store64` takes no share: the owner's release frees a block this word still names")
            None
            (Some
              "take a share first with `(__retainref v)` and then store, or keep the value in a struct field or a `Vec`, which count their own shares")
            "a raw `__store64` writes the word and nothing else; the release walk never counted a share it cannot see")
          (concat (concat "storing `" (tyRender oty)) "`"))))
  })

; A reference smuggled through a field declared `Int`
; (`error-model.md` ERR-TYPE-5/`ERR-MEM-1`, `AX3043`
; `error-payload-untyped`). The release walk follows DECLARED types,
; so a reference word in an `Int` slot is invisible to reclamation
; and leaks. The type checker already refuses the direct shape - a
; `String` where an `Int` stands is `AX3004` - so what reaches here
; is the smuggled one: a `cast` laundering the operand's type while
; the operand stays a reference. Only what the checker can SEE is
; reported, exactly `AX3071`'s rule: a variable it resolves, or a
; literal, read through any nesting of casts (`storeCastOperand`),
; whose class is a reference (`evClassOf` 1). A call result, a
; field, poison, or a variable are skipped the way they always have
; been - missing those is the status quo, and a direct mismatch is
; owned by the error beside it, which is why the call sites run this
; only where compatibility already succeeded. AN ERROR: what it reports
; is a statically certain leak, not a limit of the analysis, and the
; answer is always available - declare the field's real type. It was
; a warning until 0.7.8, on the grounds that the cast was written on
; purpose; a cast written on purpose still leaks. Answers 1 when it
; emitted.
(pub :: emitPayloadSmuggled (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (emitPayloadSmuggled tc wantArg arg span)
  (if (nodeSame wantArg nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag wantArg) TAG_T_CON)
      (if (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName wantArg)) "Int")
        (if (if (== (nodeB wantArg) 0)
          true
          (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren wantArg)) 0))
          (if (let (Some oty) (storeOperandTy tc (storeCastOperand arg)))
            (if (== (tyIsErr oty) 1)
              0
              (if (== (evClassOf tc oty) 1)
                (if (== span 0)
                  0
                  {
                    (emitDiag tc
                      (diagSetLabel
                        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3043" "error-payload-untyped" span
                          (concat (concat "storing a `" (tyRender oty))
                            "` through a field declared `Int` hides it from the release walk, which follows declared types")
                          None (Some "declare the field's real type")
                          "an `Int` slot holding a reference is invisible to reclamation, and leaks (error-model.md ERR-TYPE-5/ERR-MEM-1)")
                        (concat (concat "this holds a `" (tyRender oty)) "`")))
                    1
                  })
                0))
            0)
          0)
        0)
      0)))

; `__addr` of anything but a string literal.
;
; `(__addr "text")` is the only spelling with interned bytes behind
; it: the literal is entered in the module's string table and the
; answer is the table's global. A non-literal argument reaches the
; emitter, which evaluates it like any expression and answers the
; value's address - the header, not bytes - so a caller asking for
; bytes gets an address it never asked for, silently. Every use in
; this repository spells the literal; the rule makes that
; universal. Runs beside the `__store64` refusal above, and only at
; the root of a complete application, for the same reason.
(pub :: checkAddrLitRefusal (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkAddrLitRefusal tc e arg)
  (if (== (nodeTag arg) TAG_E_STR)
    0
    {
      (let sp (spanOf arg))
      (if (== sp 0)
        0
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3072" "addr-nonliteral" sp
              "`__addr` answers a literal's bytes: a non-literal argument has no interned bytes, and the address it yields is the value's header, not its text"
              None
              (Some
                "spell the literal - `(__addr \"text\")` - or read bytes out of a value with `strData`")
              "the emitter evaluates a non-literal like any expression, so what comes back is an address the caller never asked for")
            "not a literal")))
    }))

; A trap the checker can see from the literals alone: division or
; remainder by a literal zero, a negative literal index into a `Vec`,
; and a literal outside the bounds of the subtype a parameter
; declares. Each traps on every run, so answering it at run time
; would only delay the same refusal. Only literal operands are
; judged; a computed operand is the run-time check's business.
(pub :: checkCertainTrap (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (checkCertainTrap tc e hname)
  {
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (if (|| (strEq hname "/") (strEq hname "%"))
      (if (&& (>= (vecLen args) 2)
            (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet args 1)) TAG_E_INT) (== (nodeA (vecGet args 1)) 0)))
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3140" "certain-trap" (spanOf (vecGet args 1))
              "this operation always traps: its divisor is the literal 0" None
              (Some "divide by a value that can be nonzero, or handle the zero case first")
              "division and remainder by zero trap with status 72 on every run, so the trap is part of what this expression means")
            "always traps"))
        0)
      (if (strEq (bareOf hname) "vecGet")
        (if (&& (>= (vecLen args) 2)
              (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet args 1)) TAG_E_INT) (< (nodeA (vecGet args 1)) 0)))
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3140" "certain-trap" (spanOf (vecGet args 1))
                "this `vecGet` always traps: its index is a negative literal" None
                (Some "index with a value from 0 to the vector's length")
                "`vecGet` traps with status 77 on an index outside the vector, and a negative index is outside every vector")
              "always traps"))
          0)
        (checkSubtypeLiteralTrap tc args hname 0)))
  })

(pub :: checkSubtypeLiteralTrap (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int))
(pub fn (checkSubtypeLiteralTrap tc args hname i)
  {
    (let entFound (fnEntExact tc hname))
    (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
      (checkSubtypeLiteralArgs tc args (fnEntTy ent) 0)
      0)
  })

; Each literal argument against the subtype its parameter declares.
; `ty` is the callee's arrow, walked one parameter per argument.
(pub :: checkSubtypeLiteralArgs (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (checkSubtypeLiteralArgs tc args ty i)
  (if (|| (>= i (vecLen args)) (|| (nodeSame ty nilNode) (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_ARR)))
    0
    {
      (let param (nodeAChild ty))
      (let arg (vecGet args i))
      {
        (if (== (nodeTag arg) TAG_E_INT)
          {
            (let bounds (tySubtypeBounds tc param))
            (if (if (let (Some bounds) bounds)
              (&& (> (vecLen bounds) 0)
                (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet bounds 0)) TAG_E_INT)
                  (|| (< (nodeA arg) (nodeA (vecGet bounds 0)))
                    (&& (== (vecLen bounds) 2)
                      (&& (== (nodeTag (vecGet bounds 1)) TAG_E_INT)
                        (>= (nodeA arg) (nodeA (vecGet bounds 1))))))))
              false)
              (emitDiag tc
                (diagSetLabel
                  (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3140" "certain-trap" (spanOf arg)
                    (concat (concat "this value always traps: the literal " (fmtInt (nodeA arg)))
                      (concat " is outside the range of `" (concat (tyRender param) "`")))
                    None (Some "pass a value inside the subtype's range")
                    "a value outside a subtype's range traps with status 80 on every run, so the trap is part of what this call means")
                  "always traps")))
          })
        (checkSubtypeLiteralArgs tc args (nodeBChild ty) (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: checkApp (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkApp tc e inHead)
  ; The hole rewrite happens FIRST: if the head is `format#`, the
  ; builtin resolves it from the argument's static type - splicing the
  ; call away, rewriting the head to a library function, or generating
  ; a renderer - and everything below (saturation, argument checking,
  ; and later the emitter, which reads these same nodes) proceeds as
  ; for any ordinary call. Until 0.6.0 this arm was trait dispatch and
  ; `show` was one of its cases.
  ; Only at the spine ROOT (`inHead` 0). `checkApp` runs at every
  ; level of a curried spine and `spineHead` answers the same node
  ; each time, so rewriting at each level reported the same refusal
  ; once per argument.
  ;
  ; THERE IS NO LONGER A "IS THE NAME FREE?" QUESTION HERE, and the
  ; history is worth keeping because the answer was once wrong twice
  ; over. The head was the writable name `show` until 0.7.4, so this
  ; hook had to ask whether the program had taken the spelling -
  ; `scopeFindFrame` for a parameter or `let` binding, `findFnEnt` for
  ; a declaration - the way `checkSaturation` twenty lines down has
  ; always asked. Asking NEITHER, which is how the hook shipped,
  ; captured every one of them at exit 0:
  ; `(let ((sz (lambda (q) 7))) (sz 5))` answered 111.
  ;
  ; `format#` cannot be spelled in source - `#` is not an identifier
  ; character, and the lexer answers `AX1001` - so both questions now
  ; have the constant answer "free", and a guard that cannot fire is
  ; this repository's most common defect. `showNameFree` is deleted
  ; rather than left as a comforting no-op; a reader who wants it back
  ; wants the writable name back with it. What replaces two scope
  ; lookups on EVERY application head is one `strEq`.
  ; `hname` is a NAME; the non-variable head has none, and the
  ; absent one is now spelled `""` rather than `0`. Every reader
  ; below guards on `headIsVar` first, so neither is ever read -
  ; but only one of them has the type the binding claims.
  ; Formatting checks its argument once, then checks the rewritten
  ; expression with that type available in `formatChecked`. Ordinary
  ; applications follow the argument walk below.
  {
    (let head (spineHead e))
    {
      (let headIsVar
        (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
          1
          0))
      {
        (let formatting
          (if (&& (== headIsVar 1) (== inHead 0))
            (showIsHead (nodeAName head))
            false))
        (if formatting
          (showBuiltin tc e head)
          ; MM-PAR-6, and it answers nothing: a SIDE CHECK, bound and
          ; ignored so the arms below decide this application's type
          ; exactly as they did before. `(spineArgs e 0)` is 2 only at
          ; the ROOT of `(__par_spawn thunk word)`, which is what stops
          ; it from running once per curried level, and argument 0 is
          ; `(nodeB (nodeA e))` - the reading `checkNumeric` above uses
          ; for the same shape.
          ; A recovery point is an extent an abort reclaims, so the
          ; region pass has to run over it (`rgnRecoverForm`), as it
          ; runs for a `region` form (TC word 39).
          {
            (let hname
              (if (== headIsVar 1)
                (nodeAName head)
                ""))
            {
              (let _cap
                (if (&& (== headIsVar 1) (&& (== (capSpawnHead hname) 1) (== (spineArgs e 0) 2)))
                  (checkSpawnCaptures tc (nodeBChild (nodeAChild e)))
                  0))
              (let _rec
                (if (&& (== headIsVar 1) (strEq hname "__axiom_recover"))
                  {
                    (tcSetRgnHasForm tc 1)
                    0
                  }
                  0))
              (let _trap
                (if (&& (== inHead 0) (== headIsVar 1))
                  (checkCertainTrap tc e hname)
                  0))
              (if (&& (== inHead 0) (&& (== headIsVar 1) (== (checkSaturation tc e head hname) 1)))
                (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                (if (&& (== headIsVar 1) (== (isNumericTwoArg e hname) 1))
                  (checkNumeric tc hname (nodeBChild (nodeAChild e)) (nodeBChild e) head)
                  ; `(__asm.sym f)` names `f` without using it as a value:
                  ; a function of two parameters is no bare value (AX3013),
                  ; and is still a function `bl` can reach (`checkAsmSym`).
                  (if (&& (== headIsVar 1)
                        (||
                          (&& (|| (strEq hname "__contract") (strEq hname "parallel$lends"))
                            (== (spineArgs e 0) 2))
                          (|| (&& (strEq hname "__asm.sym") (== (spineArgs e 0) 1))
                            (&& (isIntegerWordName hname) (== (spineArgs e 0) 1)))))
                    (if (isIntegerWordName hname)
                      (checkIntegerWord tc (nodeBChild e) hname)
                      (if (strEq hname "parallel$lends")
                        (checkParLends tc e)
                        (if (strEq hname "__asm.sym")
                          (checkAsmSym tc (nodeBChild e))
                          (checkContractForm tc e))))
                    (if (&& (== headIsVar 1)
                          (&& (== (isCastLike hname) 1)
                            (|| (! (isValueCastForm hname)) (<= (spineArgs e 0) 2))))
                      (checkCastForm tc e hname)
                      (if (&& (== headIsVar 1) (== (isMacroName tc hname) 1))
                        ; Expansion runs BEFORE this checker and walks every body
                        ; a declaration can carry (fn, impl method, trait default
                        ; - expandDecl), so a macro head that reaches this arm at
                        ; all means expansion did not rewrite it - which, on the
                        ; driver's path, means expansion already REFUSED and the
                        ; process exits before checking (MAC-EXP-1b). This arm is
                        ; for the consumers that keep checking anyway (the LSP on
                        ; a mid-edit file): going quiet stops a wrong AX3001 from
                        ; landing on top of the expansion's own diagnostic. Until
                        ; 2026-08-14 impl and trait-default bodies were NOT
                        ; walked, and this silence is what let a macro invocation
                        ; there pass `check` and die in `opt` (MAC-EXP-3a).
                        {
                          (checkSpineArgsOnly tc e)
                          mkSilentWild
                        }
                        (if (let (Some constructor) (if (&& (== headIsVar 1)
                                                          (|| (== (nodeDefScope head) 1)
                                                            (isNone (scopeFindFrame tc hname))))
                          (findStructIfConstruct tc hname)
                          None))
                          (checkStructCon tc e constructor)
                          ; MM-LIFE-2d: this level's argument witnesses the callee's
                          ; type parameters; meet into the head's stamp (no-op when
                          ; the head carries none).
                          ; The argument is checked FIRST (a lambda
                          ; literal defers: no check runs on it
                          ; yet), then the head grounds from it -
                          ; a pending head witnesses this level's
                          ; argument - and only then is a lambda
                          ; literal checked against a grounded
                          ; arrow. The order matters both ways:
                          ; grounding needs the argument's type,
                          ; and the literal needs the head's
                          ; arrow; deferring the literal (no
                          ; check) where the old order checked it
                          ; standalone (fresh parameters, spurious
                          ; refusals) is what lets a callback's
                          ; parameters carry the types the call
                          ; declares. Nothing is checked twice: a
                          ; deferred literal's first check is the
                          ; `checkLamAgainst` below (or a later
                          ; grounding, or a flush).
                          ; A lambda literal applied to a
                          ; declared (or grounded) arrow is
                          ; checked AGAINST it
                          ; (`checkLamAgainst`), so its
                          ; parameters carry the declared
                          ; types - the same rule
                          ; `checkHandler` holds handlers to.
                          ; A pending argument where an arrow is
                          ; declared grounds BY the declaration
                          ; (`groundArgByDecl`): its parameters
                          ; carry the types the author wrote.
                          ; A pending argument that survived the
                          ; grounding above faces a NON-arrow
                          ; parameter: against a variable one -
                          ; or an unknown head - it is a
                          ; generic payload, inferred
                          ; standalone (the pinning and the
                          ; evidence below require a TYPE,
                          ; never a pending node); against a
                          ; ground one it is a function where
                          ; a value belongs, and refuses.
                          {
                            (let argTy0 (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0))
                            {
                              (let funcTy (groundHead tc e argTy0))
                              {
                                (let argTy
                                  {
                                    (let argTy1
                                      (groundArgByDecl tc
                                        (if (&& (== (nodeTag (nodeBChild e)) TAG_E_LAM)
                                              (== (nodeTag funcTy) TAG_T_ARR))
                                          (checkLamAgainst tc (nodeBChild e) (nodeAChild funcTy))
                                          argTy0)
                                        (arrowParamTy funcTy 0) (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))
                                    (if (== (isPendingNode argTy1) 1)
                                      (if (nodeSame funcTy nilNode)
                                        (groundStandalone tc argTy1 (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                                        (if (== (nodeTag funcTy) TAG_T_ARR)
                                          (if (== (nodeTag (nodeAChild funcTy)) TAG_T_VAR)
                                            (groundStandalone tc argTy1 (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                                            {
                                              (refusePendingOperand tc argTy1
                                                (tyRender (nodeAChild funcTy))
                                                (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                                              argTy1
                                            })
                                          (groundStandalone tc argTy1 (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))))
                                      argTy1)
                                  })
                                {
                                  (evStampFill tc e funcTy argTy)
                                  ; THE CLOSURE-APPLICATION EVIDENCE, recorded on
                                  ; this application node for `walkAppChain` to
                                  ; read back. It answers the one question a call
                                  ; through a code pointer cannot ask a signature:
                                  ; is the word being handed over a reference?
                                  ;
                                  ; Written for every application, and read only
                                  ; for one through a closure - a direct call has
                                  ; `releaseOwnedArgs` and the callee's own
                                  ; declared types, and does not consult it. The
                                  ; node's `ty` slot is free for a `TAG_E_APP`:
                                  ; `resolveStructField` reads it for a field and
                                  ; `emitFnDef` for a declaration, and neither is
                                  ; an application.
                                  ;
                                  ; `EV_LAMARG` and "says nothing" both record 0.
                                  ; The first cannot be forwarded - this frame
                                  ; would have to know the class of a value its
                                  ; own caller has not named - and the second is
                                  ; not knowledge. Both under-retain, which
                                  ; leaks.
                                  {
                                    (let c (evClassOf tc argTy))
                                    {
                                      (setNodeTyWord e
                                        (if (< c 0)
                                          0
                                          c))
                                      (evNotePendingNode tc e argTy c)
                                    }
                                  }
                                  ; A bare `__store64` of a reference, refused
                                  ; here - after this level's argument is
                                  ; checked, so the operand below was checked
                                  ; exactly once, by the normal flow - and
                                  ; only at the root of a complete store, so
                                  ; one store draws one diagnostic. A side
                                  ; check: the arms below decide this
                                  ; application's type exactly as they did
                                  ; before.
                                  (if (&& (== inHead 0)
                                        (&& (== headIsVar 1)
                                          (&& (strEq hname "__store64") (== (spineArgs e 0) 3))))
                                    (checkStoreWordRefusal tc e (nodeBChild e)))
                                  ; `__addr` of anything but a literal, refused
                                  ; in the same place and for the same reason:
                                  ; only the root of a complete application,
                                  ; decided below exactly as before.
                                  (if (&& (== inHead 0)
                                        (&& (== headIsVar 1)
                                          (&& (strEq hname "__addr") (== (spineArgs e 0) 1))))
                                    (checkAddrLitRefusal tc e (nodeBChild e)))
                                  ; A staged curried application answers
                                  ; the REST node pending: the enclosing
                                  ; application grounds it in turn. It is
                                  ; not an arrow yet, so the arms below
                                  ; must not see it (the mismatch arm
                                  ; would refuse a sound program).
                                  (if (== (isPendingNode funcTy) 1)
                                    funcTy
                                    (if (== (nodeTag funcTy) TAG_T_ARR)
                                      {
                                        (if (== (tyCompat tc argTy (nodeAChild funcTy)) 0)
                                          {
                                            (let act
                                              (subtypeArgAction tc (nodeAChild funcTy) argTy))
                                            (if (! (== act 1))
                                              (if (== act 2)
                                                {
                                                  (let bh (tySubtypeBounds tc (nodeAChild funcTy)))
                                                  (if (let (Some bh) bh)
                                                    {
                                                      (checkSubtypeBounds tc bh 0)
                                                      (writeNodeChild e 2
                                                        (mkSubtypeCheckNode (nodeBChild e) bh
                                                          (nodeAChild funcTy)
                                                          (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))
                                                    })
                                                }
                                                ; A `for` desugar's length or element read
                                                ; against a concrete non-`Vec` names what
                                                ; fits instead (forVecMismatch answers 1
                                                ; when it emitted); anything else keeps
                                                ; the generic mismatch, whose answer -
                                                ; like this branch's - the `pinShape`
                                                ; below decides.
                                                (if (!
                                                      (==
                                                        (forVecMismatch tc hname headIsVar argTy
                                                          (nodeAChild funcTy)
                                                          (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                                                        1))
                                                  (emitMismatchTy tc (nodeAChild funcTy) argTy
                                                    (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))))
                                          }
                                          ; Compatibility succeeded, so a direct
                                          ; mismatch owns nothing here. An
                                          ; argument still unknown against a
                                          ; ground parameter refuses: the
                                          ; callee lowers for its declared
                                          ; representation and ignorance is
                                          ; not one (`(lambda (x) (g x))`
                                          ; applied to `1.5` ran `g`'s
                                          ; integer body on double bits).
                                          ; A data-constructor payload smuggled
                                          ; through an `Int` field still warns
                                          ; (AX3043): the head names a
                                          ; constructor, and this level's
                                          ; parameter is its payload's
                                          ; declared type.
                                          {
                                            (if (&& (== (tyIsUnknown argTy) 1)
                                                  (== (tyIsGround (nodeAChild funcTy)) 1))
                                              (refuseUnknownOperand tc
                                                (tyRender (nodeAChild funcTy)) argTy
                                                (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))
                                            (if (&& (== headIsVar 1)
                                                  (! (isNone (rfindCtor tc hname))))
                                              (emitPayloadSmuggled tc (nodeAChild funcTy)
                                                (nodeBChild e) (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))
                                          })
                                        ; The rest of the arrow, with this parameter's
                                        ; PLACEHOLDER pinned to the argument's type where the
                                        ; argument has one to offer (`pinArg`, 2026-08-21):
                                        ; `(Some 5)` is `Option Int`, not `Option _t7`, and a
                                        ; later parameter sharing the variable is held to
                                        ; the same type. Until then a placeholder stayed a
                                        ; placeholder for the whole spine, which is why a
                                        ; let-bound `(Some 5)` matched `(Some "x")` with no
                                        ; diagnostic and dereferenced 5 as a header under the
                                        ; content comparison (the QA sweep's refutation
                                        ; pass). Pinning mints nothing.
                                        (pinShape (nodeBChild funcTy) (nodeAChild funcTy) argTy)
                                      }
                                      (if (== (tyIsErr funcTy) 1)
                                        funcTy
                                        (if (== (tyIsSilentWild funcTy) 1)
                                          mkSilentWild
                                          {
                                            (if (! (refuseUnsignedParam tc funcTy))
                                              (if (== (signatureNeeded tc hname headIsVar) 1)
                                                (emitSignatureNeeded tc hname (spanOf e))
                                                (emitMismatch tc "function type" (tyRender funcTy)
                                                  (spanOf e))))
                                            (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                                          }))))
                                }
                              }
                            }
                          }))))))
            }
          })
      }
    }
  })

; `rest` with the minted placeholder `param` replaced by `argTy`
; throughout, when `param` is one and `argTy` says something - a
; constructor, an arrow, a tuple, a list, a pointer. A source
; variable is never pinned (it is rigid, and `tyCompat` has already
; judged it); a placeholder, poison or the silent wildcard on the
; argument side pins nothing, since it would only rename an unknown.
; `pinArg` through the parameter's SHAPE: a placeholder under an
; arrow or a constructor - `(-> (-> a b) a b)` applied to `inc`, whose
; type is `(-> Int Int)` - is pinned from the matching subtree of the
; argument's type. The walk stops where the shapes stop agreeing.
(pub :: pinShape (-> ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (pinShape rest param argTy)
  (if (|| (nodeSame param nilNode) (nodeSame argTy nilNode))
    rest
    (if (== (tyIsErr argTy) 1)
      ; Poison (ERR-DIAG-3): an argument whose diagnostic is already
      ; out makes every placeholder of its parameter poison in the
      ; rest of THIS call's arrow, so `(vecGet broken 0)` answers poison
      ; and a `println` of it stays quiet. `rest` is this call's own
      ; instantiation: nothing shared is bound, so rule 2 above holds.
      (poisonPlaceholders rest param)
      {
        (let pt (nodeTag param))
        (if (== pt TAG_T_VAR)
          (pinArg rest param argTy)
          (if (== pt TAG_T_ARR)
            (if (== (nodeTag argTy) TAG_T_ARR)
              (pinShape (pinShape rest (nodeAChild param) (nodeAChild argTy)) (nodeBChild param)
                (nodeBChild argTy))
              rest)
            (if (== pt TAG_T_CON)
              (if (&& (== (nodeTag argTy) TAG_T_CON)
                    (&& (strEq (nodeAName param) (nodeAName argTy))
                      (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren param)) (vecLen (nodeBChildren argTy)))))
                (pinShapeVec rest (nodeBChildren param) (nodeBChildren argTy) 0)
                rest)
              (if (== pt TAG_T_TUP)
                (if (&& (== (nodeTag argTy) TAG_T_TUP)
                      (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren param)) (vecLen (nodeAChildren argTy))))
                  (pinShapeVec rest (nodeAChildren param) (nodeAChildren argTy) 0)
                  rest)
                rest))))
      })))

; `rest` with every minted placeholder written in `param` replaced by
; poison. A source variable is rigid and stays.
(pub :: poisonPlaceholders (-> ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (poisonPlaceholders rest param)
  (if (|| (nodeSame param nilNode) (nodeSame rest nilNode))
    rest
    {
      (let pt (nodeTag param))
      (if (== pt TAG_T_VAR)
        (if (|| (== (tyIsSrcVar param) 1) (== (strLen (nodeAName param)) 0))
          rest
          (tyPinVar rest (nodeAName param) (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)))
        (if (== pt TAG_T_ARR)
          (poisonPlaceholders (poisonPlaceholders rest (nodeAChild param)) (nodeBChild param))
          (if (== pt TAG_T_CON)
            (poisonPlaceholdersVec rest (nodeBChildren param) 0)
            (if (== pt TAG_T_TUP)
              (poisonPlaceholdersVec rest (nodeAChildren param) 0)
              (if (== pt TAG_T_PTR)
                (poisonPlaceholders rest (nodeAChild param))
                rest)))))
    }))

(pub :: poisonPlaceholdersVec (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (poisonPlaceholdersVec rest ps i)
  (if (|| (== (vecLen ps) 0) (>= i (vecLen ps)))
    rest
    (poisonPlaceholdersVec (poisonPlaceholders rest (vecGet ps i)) ps (+ i 1))))

(pub :: pinShapeVec (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (pinShapeVec rest ps as i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    rest
    (pinShapeVec (pinShape rest (vecGet ps i) (vecGet as i)) ps as (+ i 1))))

(pub :: pinArg (-> ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (pinArg rest param argTy)
  (if (|| (nodeSame param nilNode) (nodeSame argTy nilNode))
    rest
    (if (!= (nodeTag param) TAG_T_VAR)
      rest
      (if (|| (== (tyIsSrcVar param) 1) (== (strLen (nodeAName param)) 0))
        rest
        {
          (let at (nodeTag argTy))
          (if (|| (== at TAG_T_VAR) (== at TAG_T_ERR))
            rest
            (tyPinVar rest (nodeAName param) argTy))
        }))))

; Every occurrence of the variable named `name` in `t` replaced by
; `rep`; nothing minted, nothing else rebuilt.
(pub :: tyPinVar (-> ASTNode String ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyPinVar t name rep)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (if (strEq (nodeAName t) name)
          rep
          t)
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (mkTCon (nodeAName t) (tyPinVarVec (nodeBChildren t) name rep vecNew 0))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            (mkTArr (tyPinVar (nodeAChild t) name rep) (tyPinVar (nodeBChild t) name rep))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (mkTTup (tyPinVarVec (nodeAChildren t) name rep vecNew 0))
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (mkTPtr (tyPinVar (nodeAChild t) name rep))
                t)))))
    }))

(pub :: tyPinVarVec (-> Nodes String ASTNode Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (tyPinVarVec xs name rep acc i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      acc
      {
        (vecPush acc (tyPinVar (vecGet xs i) name rep))
        (tyPinVarVec xs name rep acc (+ i 1))
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `format#` - the hole rendering, as a compiler-known head.
;
; `(format# x)` is what every `{hole}` and every `(println expr)`
; lowers to (`syntax/format`, expand.ax). Until 2026-08-29 the only
; thing that could answer it was a trait: `stdlib/Show.ax` declared
; `Show` with four instances and the dispatcher selected one from the
; argument's static type or refused with AX3025. That was the whole of
; what traits were used for in this repository - one trait, four
; impls, one file - and it is why they are gone (docs/reference.md,
; "Printing and Formatting"). This is a head the CHECKER resolves, the
; way `checkNumeric` already resolves `==` on two `String`s into
; `__streq` - by rewriting the head node in place from the operand's
; static type, with no table and no runtime tag.
;
; THE HEAD IS UNWRITABLE, since 0.7.4. It was the ordinary name `show`
; for as long as `Show.ax` had instances to dispatch to, and it stayed
; that way after they were deleted - so the compiler's own lowering
; and a user's function competed for one spelling, and the user's
; `(:: show (-> a String))` captured every hole in the program at exit
; 0. `#` is not an identifier character (`AX1001 unexpected character
; `#``), so no source file can name this head, no import can shadow
; it, `expQualify` is not consulted for it, and `(show 1)` is now the
; plain `AX3001` any other unknown name gets. The generated renderers
; below share the `#`: `show#Point` is a real emitted function with a
; type name after the mark, this is a bare head with nothing after it,
; and neither can be written either.
;
; The builtin ran SECOND while both existed: a written `impl` won, and
; this answered only where none covered the type. It is the whole of
; the resolution now. Measured before it landed, on the programs it
; was written to answer:
;
;   (println p) on a struct           AX3025 no implementation of `Show` for `Point`
;   (show 123) with only Fmt loaded   AX3001 undefined variable `show`
;
; WHAT THE BUILTIN ANSWERS, by the argument's static type:
;
;   String   the call is ELIDED - the argument's node stands in for
;            the application (`showSplice`), so 585 `(println s)`
;            sites stop paying for an identity call
;   Bool     `(if b "true" "false")`, spliced the same way
;   Int      `Fmt.fmtInt`      Float   `Fmt.fmtFloat`
;   Char     the character literal - `'x'`, `'\n'`, `'é'` - through a
;            generated helper, since no `Fmt` function renders one
;   data     a constructor application in the language's own
;            spelling: `(Some 3)`, `(Cons 1 (Cons 2 Nil))`, a nullary
;            constructor bare - `None`; a struct-variant constructor
;            positionally, which is how it is written
;   struct   `{x = 1, y = 2}`, in declaration order; `{}` for none
;
; and INSIDE a structure a `String` is quoted with the lexer's own
; escapes (`{name = "bob"}`, `"a\"b"`, `"\n"`) where at top level it
; is bare, because at top level `(println s)` means the bytes of `s`
; and always has. A `Char` is the literal in both positions.
;
; The structural renderer is GENERATED, once per concrete type, as an
; ordinary `::`/`fn` pair appended to the merged declaration list, for
; the reason the trait lowering appended `Trait#Type#method` before
; it: what is generated is then checked and emitted like anything
; written. Its name is `show#<type>` (`show#Point`,
; `show#(Option Int)`), and `#` is what keeps it out of `symbols`
; (`smGenerated`): a generated name is not a symbol. A parameterised
; type gets one renderer per INSTANTIATION, because `(Option Int)`
; and `(Option Bool)` render their field differently and a type
; variable cannot be rendered at all.
;
; TWO VISITED SETS, one at each time a walk can fail to end:
;
;   - at GENERATION, the registry itself: the renderer's signature is
;     registered before its body is built, so a recursive `data`
;     (`(Cons a (List a))`) meets its own name and calls it, and a
;     pair of mutually recursive types terminates for the same
;     reason. Keyed on the rendered type, not the call site.
;   - at RUN TIME, an ancestor stack: a struct field is assignable
;     (`(set c.next (Some c))`), so a value can reach itself, and a
;     renderer that followed it would not return. Every structural
;     renderer takes a `seen` vector of the blocks above it, pushes
;     its own before rendering its fields and pops after, and a
;     value already on the stack renders as `...`. Measured: the
;     cell above prints `{v = 1, next = (Some ...)}`. The stack is
;     fresh per top-level rendering - `(show#T x vecNew)` - and a
;     nullary-only type takes it and never reads it.
;
; WHAT IT REFUSES, all AX3025 so the code a reader already looks for
; still finds it: a type variable (the same refusal as before, since
; a rendering is chosen from the STATIC type and a variable is not
; one), a function value, a `Foreign`, a tuple, and a `data`/`struct`
; holding any of those - reported at the call, naming the offending
; field type, BEFORE anything is generated (`showDerivable`), so a
; generated body never has to report on the caller's behalf. And a
; `show` in a program that has not loaded the library module the
; rewrite needs - `Str` for `concat`, `Fmt` for `fmtInt` - is a
; diagnostic naming that module, not a call to a symbol that does not
; exist: the builtin is a rewrite to library functions, not a runtime.
;
; Free identifiers in a generated body are the library's QUALIFIED
; spellings (`Str$concat`), which is what makes them immune to the
; capture `tests/selfhost/383-format-capture.ax` still pins for the
; expander's own `concat`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The reserved head, by exact spelling.
;
; NOT `bareOf`, which this asked until 0.7.4 because the head went
; through `expQualify` and could arrive as `Mod$show`. `expFmtShow`
; does not qualify, so the head arrives bare or not at all; asking
; `bareOf` would additionally claim a `Mod$format#`, which no program
; can declare and no expansion emits, so it would widen the claim
; without widening what it can ever match.
(pub :: showIsHead (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (showIsHead hname)
  (strEq hname "format#"))

; Resolve `(format# x)`, `e` the application and `head` its var node.
;
; The argument's checked type and diagnostics are kept together. The
; rewritten expression is checked with the argument cached, so an
; already-grounded lambda need not report its body again.
(pub data ShowOutcome
  (ShowUnavailable)
  (ShowRefused)
  (ShowRewritten)
  (ShowSpliced))

(pub :: showBuiltin (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (showBuiltin tc e head)
  ; THERE IS NO ARITY ARM, and its absence is deliberate. This
  ; carried one - `(!= (vecLen args) 1)` under an AX3025 reading
  ; "`show` renders exactly one value" - for as long as `show` was a
  ; name a program could write and therefore call with two
  ; arguments. `format#` has exactly two producers, `expFmtHole` and
  ; `expEvalFormat` in `core/expand.ax`, and both build
  ; `(mkEApp (mkEVarAt "format#" sp) v)` - one argument, structurally.
  ; A guard against the other case could not be reached, and could
  ; not be given an ablation either, which is the argument for
  ; deleting it rather than keeping it for comfort. A third producer
  ; has to read this paragraph before it becomes the second thing
  ; that can put a node here.
  {
    (let arg (nodeBChild e))
    {
      (let at (checkExprH tc arg 0))
      (match (showResolve tc e head arg at)
        ((ShowUnavailable)
          {
            (showNoRenderingDiag tc arg at)
            (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
          }
        )
        ((ShowRefused) (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
        ((ShowRewritten) (showCheckRewritten tc e arg at))
        ((ShowSpliced) (showCheckRewritten tc e e at)))
    }
  })

; Keep the checked argument available while checking the rewrite. A
; direct String splice uses `e`, which now contains the argument.
(pub :: showCheckRewritten (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (showCheckRewritten tc e checked at)
  ; The reserved head must not be checked recursively if a rewrite
  ; leaves it unchanged. The refusal's diagnostic is already reported.
  (if (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
    {
      (let h (spineHead e))
      (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
        (showIsHead (nodeAName h))
        false)
    }
    false)
    (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
    {
      (let cache (tcFormatChecked tc))
      {
        (vecPush cache checked)
        (vecPush cache at)
        {
          (let answer (checkExprH tc e 0))
          {
            (vecPop cache)
            (vecPop cache)
            answer
          }
        }
      }
    }))

(pub :: showResolve (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ShowOutcome))
(pub fn (showResolve tc e head arg at0)
  ; RESOLVE FIRST. `at0` is whatever `checkExprH` checked, and that is
  ; routinely a MINTED PLACEHOLDER that is already BOUND - an
  ; un-annotated `let` over a container binds its element to a fresh
  ; var and pinning later binds that var to the element type
  ; (`scripts/check-type-pinning.sh`). This function read the
  ; placeholder itself, saw TAG_T_VAR, and answered AX3025 "its type is
  ; a type variable" about a type that was not a variable any more:
  ; measured, `(strLen y)` type-checked and `(println y)` on the SAME
  ; binding one line above was refused. Following the binding is not a
  ; widening - `tyResolve` walks bound placeholders only, so an UNBOUND
  ; var still reaches `showUnknownDiag` and all three refusals in
  ; `tests/diagnostics/620-show-refusals` still fire. Measured inert on
  ; the tree: `axiom emit-llvm core/main.ax` byte-identical at
  ; 202,021 lines, and zero diagnostic movement over 256 corpus files.
  {
    (let at (tyResolve at0))
    (if (nodeSame at nilNode)
      (showUnknownDiag tc arg at)
      (if (== (tyIsErr at) 1)
        ; poison: a diagnostic about this argument has already been
        ; reported (the cascade rule)
        ShowRefused
        ; A subtype renders as its base: a `Positive` prints like the
        ; `Int` it constrains, so `println` needs no per-subtype
        ; renderer. Chained subtypes walk to the root (v1 declares
        ; only `Int` bases, so one step in practice).
        {
          (let ty (showBaseOf tc (tyExpandAliases tc at 0)))
          (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
            (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_VAR)
              (showUnknownDiag tc arg ty)
              ShowUnavailable)
            (if (== (showTyGround ty) 0)
              (showUnknownDiag tc arg ty)
              {
                (let n (nodeAName ty))
                (if (strEq n "String")
                  (showSplice e arg)
                  (if (strEq n "Bool")
                    (showSpliceBool e arg)
                    (if (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
                      (if (strEq n "Int")
                        (showRewriteTo tc head "Fmt" "fmtInt" (spanOf e))
                        (if (let (Some rendered) (showIntegerExpr tc arg n (spanOf e)))
                          {
                            (showSplice e rendered)
                            ShowRewritten
                          }
                          ShowRefused))
                      (if (strEq n "Float")
                        (showRewriteTo tc head "Fmt" "fmtFloat" (spanOf e))
                        (if (strEq n "Char")
                          (showRewriteGen tc e head arg ty 0)
                          (if (|| (! (isNone (findData tc n))) (! (isNone (findStruct tc n))))
                            (showRewriteGen tc e head arg ty 1)
                            ShowUnavailable))))))
              }))
        }))
  })

; The argument's own node takes the application's place: every word
; copied, so the parent that points at `e` now sees the argument.
; The argument node itself is left as it was - nothing else points
; at it, and an AST node is never released (MM-LIFE-2c).
(pub :: showSplice (-> ASTNode ASTNode ShowOutcome))
(pub fn (showSplice e arg)
  {
    (for i in 0..12
      (nodeCopyWord e arg i))
    ShowSpliced
  })

; `(if b "true" "false")` in the application's place. Word 6 is the
; stamp the `format#` head may have been given and is cleared with it.
(pub :: showSpliceBool (-> ASTNode ASTNode ShowOutcome))
(pub fn (showSpliceBool e arg)
  {
    (let sp (nodeSpan e))
    {
      (writeNodeWord e 0 TAG_E_IF)
      (writeNodeChild e 1 arg)
      (writeNodeChild e 2 (showLit "true" sp))
      (writeNodeChild e 3 (showLit "false" sp))
      (writeNodeWord e 6 0)
      ShowRewritten
    }
  })

; The head renamed to a library function, resolved to the spelling the
; program actually holds (`showHelperName`).
(pub :: showRewriteTo (-> TC ASTNode String String Int ShowOutcome))
(pub fn (showRewriteTo tc head mod name sp)
  (if (let (Some full) (showHelperName tc mod name sp))
    {
      (writeNodeText head 1 full)
      (writeNodeWord head 6 0)
      ShowRewritten
    }
    ShowRefused))

; The head renamed to the generated renderer for `ty`, generating it
; first when this is the type's first use. `withSeen` adds the
; ancestor stack as a second argument: `(format# x)` becomes
; `(show#T x vecNew)`, the inner application built here and the outer
; node `e` re-pointed at it.
(pub :: showRewriteGen (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int ShowOutcome))
(pub fn (showRewriteGen tc e head arg ty withSeen)
  {
    (let sp (spanOf e))
    (if (let (Some nm) (showEnsure tc ty sp))
      (if (== withSeen 1)
        (if (let (Some fresh) (showHelperName tc "Vec" "vecNew" sp))
          {
            (writeNodeText head 1 nm)
            (writeNodeWord head 6 0)
            (let inner (nodeBuildEApp head arg nilWords (nodeSpan e)))
            (writeNodeChild e 1 inner)
            (writeNodeChild e 2 (mkEVarAt fresh (nodeSpan e)))
            ShowRewritten
          }
          ShowRefused)
        {
          (writeNodeText head 1 nm)
          (writeNodeWord head 6 0)
          ShowRewritten
        })
      ShowRefused)
  })

; The name a library function is registered under in THIS program:
; `Mod$name` for an imported module, the bare name when that module is
; the file being compiled, and `None` after a diagnostic when it is
; not loaded at all.
(pub :: showHelperName (-> TC String String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (showHelperName tc mod name sp)
  {
    (let full (concat (concat mod "$") name))
    (if (isSome (fnEntExact tc full))
      (Some full)
      (if (isSome (fnEntExact tc name))
        (Some name)
        {
          (showDiag tc sp
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`format` renders with `" mod) "`'s `") name)
              (concat (concat "`, and `" mod) "` is not loaded here"))
            (concat (concat "(import " mod)
              ") - `IO` imports it, so a program that prints already has it")
            "a rendering is a rewrite to the standard library's own functions, not a runtime of its own")
          None
        }))
  })

; Every renderer's refusal, under the code readers already know.
(pub :: showDiag (-> TC Int String String String Int))
(pub fn (showDiag tc sp msg help note)
  (if (== sp 0)
    0
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3025" "no-rendering" sp msg None (Some help) note)
          "no rendering derived"))
      0
    }))

; The argument's type is not a type: absent, a variable, or a
; constructor applied to one.
(pub :: showUnknownDiag (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ShowOutcome))
(pub fn (showUnknownDiag tc arg at)
  {
    (showDiag tc (spanOf arg)
      (concat "cannot render this argument with `format`: its type "
        (if (nodeSame at nilNode)
          "is not known here"
          (if (== (nodeTag at) TAG_T_VAR)
            "is a type variable, not a named type"
            (concat (concat "is `" (tyRender at)) "`, which mentions a type variable"))))
      "`format` renders by the STATIC type of its argument: bind it to a `let` with a signature, or write the type at the call - a generic function's own type variable cannot select a rendering either"
      "a rendering is chosen at compile time from the argument's static type, and a type variable is not a type")
    ShowRefused
  })

(pub :: showNoRenderingDiag (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (showNoRenderingDiag tc arg at)
  (showDiag tc (spanOf arg) (concat (concat "`format` has no rendering for `" (tyRender at)) "`")
    "`format` renders `Int`, `Float`, `Bool`, `Char` and `String`, and derives one for any `data` or `struct` whose fields it can render; for anything else write a function that renders it and interpolate its result"
    "a rendering is derived from the static type at compile time, and a type the compiler cannot take apart has none"))

; Does `t` mention no type variable at all? Only a ground type has a
; renderer: a variable's rendering would have to be chosen at run
; time from a tag no value carries.
(pub :: showTyGround (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (showTyGround t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        0
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (showTyGroundVec (nodeBChildren t) 0)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            (if (== (showTyGround (nodeAChild t)) 1)
              (showTyGround (nodeBChild t))
              0)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (showTyGroundVec (nodeAChildren t) 0)
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (showTyGround (nodeAChild t))
                1)))))
    }))

(pub :: showTyGroundVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (showTyGroundVec xs i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    1
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      1
      (if (== (showTyGround (vecGet xs i)) 0)
        0
        (showTyGroundVec xs (+ i 1))))))

; The generated renderer's name for a type: `Point`, `(Option Int)`,
; `(List (Option Int))` - parenthesised wherever a type is applied, so
; two instantiations cannot spell the same name. `tyRender` is not
; used because it drops the parentheses of a nested application.
(pub :: showTyName (-> ASTNode String))
(pub fn (showTyName t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    "?"
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
        (if (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0)
          (nodeAName t)
          (concat (concat "(" (showTyNameVec (nodeAName t) (nodeBChildren t))) ")"))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat "(-> " (showTyName (nodeAChild t))) " ")
              (showTyName (nodeBChild t)))
            ")")
          (tyRender t)))
    }))

(:: showTyNameVec (-> String (Vec ASTNode) String))
(fn (showTyNameVec head xs)
  {
    (let out (strBuilderPush strBuilderNew head))
    (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
      {
        (strBuilderPush out " ")
        (strBuilderPush out (showTyName (vecGet xs i)))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

; The constructor's field types at THIS instantiation of its type:
; `Some`'s `a` is `Int` over `(Option Int)`. `ctorPatEnv` pairs the
; declaration's parameters with the type's arguments, which is what a
; constructor pattern does for the same question.
(pub :: showCtorFieldTys (-> TC CtorEnt ASTNode Nodes))
(pub fn (showCtorFieldTys tc ctor ty)
  (tyInstVec tc ctor.fieldTys (ctorPatEnv ctor.resTy ty vecNew) vecNew 0))

; Can `ty` be rendered, transitively? Answers 0 when it can and the
; first type that cannot otherwise: a variable, an arrow, a `Foreign`,
; anything that is neither a scalar nor a declared `data`/`struct`.
; `seen` is the generation-time visited set, keyed on the rendered
; type, so a recursive type is asked once.
(pub :: showDerivable (-> TC ASTNode Strings (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showDerivable tc t seen)
  {
    (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc t 0))
    (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
      (Some (mkTVar "?"))
      (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
        (Some ty)
        (if (== (showTyGround ty) 0)
          (Some ty)
          {
            (let n (nodeAName ty))
            (if (|| (strEq n "String")
                  (|| (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
                    (|| (strEq n "Float") (|| (strEq n "Bool") (strEq n "Char")))))
              None
              {
                (let key (showTyName ty))
                (if (showSeenName seen key 0)
                  None
                  {
                    (let dentFound (findData tc n))
                    {
                      (vecPush seen key)
                      (if (let (Some dent) dentFound)
                        (showDerivableCtors tc (dataEntCtors dent) ty seen 0)
                        {
                          (let sentFound (findStruct tc n))
                          (if (let (Some sent) sentFound)
                            (showDerivableFields tc (structEntFields sent) seen 0)
                            (Some ty))
                        })
                    }
                  })
              })
          })))
  })

(pub :: showSeenName (-> Strings String Int Bool))
(pub fn (showSeenName seen key i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen seen))
    false
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr seen i) key)
      true
      (showSeenName seen key (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: showDerivableCtors (-> TC (Vec CtorEnt) ASTNode Strings Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showDerivableCtors tc ctors ty seen i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    None
    (if (let (Some bad) (showDerivableVec tc (showCtorFieldTys tc (vecGet ctors i) ty) seen 0))
      (Some bad)
      (showDerivableCtors tc ctors ty seen (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: showDerivableVec (-> TC Nodes Strings Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showDerivableVec tc tys seen i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tys))
    None
    (if (let (Some bad) (showDerivable tc (vecGet tys i) seen))
      (Some bad)
      (showDerivableVec tc tys seen (+ i 1)))))

; A struct's fields are the declaration's own TAG_FIELD nodes, typed
; as written; a field with no type kept is the silent wildcard, which
; has no rendering.
(pub :: showDerivableFields (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Strings Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showDerivableFields tc fields seen i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    None
    {
      (let f (vecGet fields i))
      (let badFound
        (if (== (nodeTy f) 0)
          (Some (mkTVar "?"))
          (showDerivable tc (nodeTyChild f) seen)))
      (if (let (Some bad) badFound)
        (Some bad)
        (showDerivableFields tc fields seen (+ i 1)))
    }))

; A renderer is registered before its body so recursive fields can name it.
; On refusal, discard only this generation's appended rows and declarations.
; Diagnostics and entries that existed before generation remain owned by TC.
(pub :: showRollback (-> TC Int Int ()))
(pub fn (showRollback tc declCount fnCount)
  {
    (let decls (tcAllDeclsVec tc))
    (let fns (tcFnsVec tc))
    (while (> (vecLen decls) declCount)
      {
        (vecPop decls)
        ()
      })
    (while (> (vecLen fns) fnCount)
      {
        (vecPop fns)
        ()
      })
    (if (isSome (tcFnIdx tc))
      (tcSetFnIdx tc (fnIdxBuildSized fns (vecLen decls))))
    (if (isSome (tcFnSufIdx tc))
      (tcSetFnSufIdx tc (fnSufIdxBuildSized fns (vecLen decls))))
    ()
  })

; The renderer for `ty`, by name - generated now if this is its first
; use, or `None` after a diagnostic when it cannot be. The derivability
; question is asked of the WHOLE type first, so that a body is only
; ever generated for a type every field of which renders, and the
; refusal lands at the call that asked rather than inside generated
; code nobody wrote.
(pub :: showEnsure (-> TC ASTNode Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (showEnsure tc ty sp)
  {
    (let nm (concat "show#" (showTyName ty)))
    (if (isSome (fnEntExact tc nm))
      (Some nm)
      (if (let (Some bad) (showDerivable tc ty vecNew))
        {
          (showDiag tc sp
            (concat (concat (concat "`format` cannot render `" (tyRender ty)) "`: it holds ")
              (if (== (nodeTag bad) TAG_T_VAR)
                "a value whose type is a type variable"
                (concat (concat "a `" (tyRender bad)) "`, which has no rendering")))
            "give the field a type `format` can render, or write a function that renders this type and interpolate its result"
            "a rendering is derived from the static type at compile time, field by field, and a type the compiler cannot take apart has none")
          None
        }
        {
          (let declCount (vecLen (tcAllDeclsVec tc)))
          (let fnCount (vecLen (tcFnsVec tc)))
          (if (showGenerate tc ty nm sp)
            (Some nm)
            {
              (showRollback tc declCount fnCount)
              None
            })
        }))
  })

; Append the pair `(:: nm ty)` / `(fn (nm params) 0)` to the merged
; declaration list and register both, the body to be filled in by the
; caller once it is built. Registration precedes the body on purpose:
; it is the generation-time visited set. `tcWalkDecls` reads the list's
; length afresh at every step, so a declaration appended during the
; walk is checked by the same walk - after every declaration the
; program wrote, which is where its spans (the call site's) can do no
; harm. Module 0, the entry file's, is what generated declarations
; carry; it is what lets the emitter record the name under
; itself.
(pub :: showDeclare (-> TC String ASTNode Strings Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showDeclare tc nm ty params sp)
  {
    (let sig (nodeBuildDSig nm nilWords 0 sp))
    {
      (writeNodeChild sig 6 ty)
      (writeNodeWord sig 5 1)
      (vecPush (tcAllDeclsVec tc) sig)
      (tcCollectSig tc sig)
      {
        (let fnd (nodeBuildDFn nm params nilNode sp))
        {
          (writeNodeWord fnd 5 1)
          (vecPush (tcAllDeclsVec tc) fnd)
          (tcCollectFn tc fnd)
          fnd
        }
      }
    }
  })

; The body, and then the row: `inferEffectsOne` is the fixpoint's own
; per-declaration step, run once here because the fixpoint has already
; finished. It writes the current-module and call-edge words for its
; own walk and clears them; both belong to the function being checked
; around this call, so they are put back.
(pub :: showFinish (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (showFinish tc fnd body)
  {
    (let m (tcCurModule tc))
    (let calls (tcCurCalls tc))
    (let may (tcMayCalls tc))
    {
      (writeNodeChild fnd 3 body)
      (inferEffectsOne tc fnd)
      (tcSetCurModule tc m)
      (tcSetCurCalls tc calls)
      (tcSetMayCalls tc may)
      0
    }
  })

(pub :: showParams (-> String String Strings))
(pub fn (showParams a b)
  {
    (let ps vecNew)
    {
      (vecPush ps a)
      (if (! (== (strLen b) 0))
        (vecPush ps b))
      ps
    }
  })

(pub :: showParams3 (-> String String String Strings))
(pub fn (showParams3 a b c)
  {
    (let ps (showParams a b))
    {
      (vecPush ps c)
      ps
    }
  })

; A string literal node spelled the way the lexer would have spelled
; it: `text` is the LEXEME's body, escapes included, and the quotes go
; on here (the emitter unescapes on the way out - see `expFmtLit`).
; Word 2 is 1 for the same reason as there: a literal this pass built
; is not source.
(pub :: showLit (-> String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showLit text sp)
  (nodeBuildEStr (concat (concat "\"" text) "\"") 1 0 sp))

(pub :: showVar (-> String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showVar n sp)
  (mkEVarAt n sp))

(pub :: showCall1 (-> String ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showCall1 f x sp)
  (mkEApp (mkEVarAt f sp) x))

(pub :: showCall2 (-> String ASTNode ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showCall2 f x y sp)
  (mkEApp (mkEApp (mkEVarAt f sp) x) y))

(pub :: showCall3 (-> String ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showCall3 f x y z sp)
  (mkEApp (mkEApp (mkEApp (mkEVarAt f sp) x) y) z))

; `(concat a b)`, through whatever `Str` is called here.
(pub :: showCat (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showCat tc a b sp)
  (if (let (Some concatFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "concat" sp))
    (Some (showCall2 concatFn a b sp))
    None))

; `(cast Int v)` - the block's address, or a nullary constructor's
; immediate tag, which can never be an ancestor of anything and so
; never matches.
(pub :: showAddr (-> String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showAddr v sp)
  (showCall2 "cast" (showVar "Int" sp) (showVar v sp) sp))

; What renders a value of type `fty` held INSIDE a structure, given
; the expression `x` that reads it. Scalars take the builtin's own
; rendering here rather than an instance - a `String` field is
; quoted - and a nested `data`/`struct` takes the ancestor stack.
(pub :: showIntegerExpr (-> TC ASTNode String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showIntegerExpr tc value name sp)
  (if (let (Some fmtFn) (showHelperName tc "Fmt"
                          (if (integerUnsigned (integerTypeCode name))
                            "fmtUIntWord"
                            "fmtInt")
                          sp))
    (Some (showCall1 fmtFn (showCall1 (concat "__integer.word." name) value sp) sp))
    None))

(pub :: showFieldExpr (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showFieldExpr tc fty x sp)
  {
    (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc fty 0))
    (let n (nodeAName ty))
    (if (strEq n "String")
      (if (let (Some name) (showEnsureString tc sp))
        (Some (showCall1 name x sp))
        None)
      (if (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
        (showIntegerExpr tc x n sp)
        (if (strEq n "Float")
          (if (let (Some name) (showHelperName tc "Fmt" "fmtFloat" sp))
            (Some (showCall1 name x sp))
            None)
          (if (strEq n "Bool")
            (Some (nodeBuildEIf x (showLit "true" sp) (showLit "false" sp) sp))
            (if (let (Some name) (showEnsure tc ty sp))
              (Some
                (if (strEq n "Char")
                  (showCall1 name x sp)
                  (showCall2 name x (showVar "seen" sp) sp)))
              None)))))
  })

; Dispatch on the kind of type a renderer is being generated for.
; `showDerivable` has already said yes to every field, so the three
; arms are exhaustive over what reaches here.
(pub :: showGenerate (-> TC ASTNode String Int Bool))
(pub fn (showGenerate tc ty nm sp)
  {
    (let n (nodeAName ty))
    (if (strEq n "Char")
      (showGenChar tc nm sp)
      {
        (let dentFound (findData tc n))
        (if (let (Some dent) dentFound)
          (showGenData tc ty nm sp dent)
          (if (let (Some sent) (findStruct tc n))
            (showGenStruct tc ty nm sp sent)
            false))
      })
  })

; The renderer of a `data` type:
;
;   (fn (show#T v seen)
;     (if (show#seen seen (cast Int v) 0)
;       "..."
;       { (vecPush seen (cast Int v))
;         (let ((r (match v ((C) "C") ((D f0 f1) (concat "(D" ...))))
;           { (vecPop seen) r }) }))
;
; and, for a type none of whose constructors carries a field, the
; `match` alone: nothing to recurse into, nothing to guard.
(pub :: showGenData (-> TC ASTNode String Int DataEnt Bool))
(pub fn (showGenData tc ty nm sp dent)
  {
    (let fnd
      (showDeclare tc nm (mkTArr ty (mkTArr mkVecIntTy mkStringTy)) (showParams "v" "seen") sp))
    (let ctors (dataEntCtors dent))
    (let arms vecNew)
    (if (showGenArms tc ctors ty arms sp 0)
      (if (let (Some body) (showGuarded tc (nodeBuildEMatch (showVar "v" sp) arms 0 sp)
                             (showCtorsHaveFields ctors 0) sp))
        {
          (showFinish tc fnd body)
          true
        }
        false)
      false)
  })

(pub :: showCtorsHaveFields (-> (Vec CtorEnt) Int Bool))
(pub fn (showCtorsHaveFields ctors i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    false
    (if (> (vecLen (ctorEntFieldTys (vecGet ctors i))) 0)
      true
      (showCtorsHaveFields ctors (+ i 1)))))

; The ancestor guard around a body, when the type has anything to
; recurse into.
(pub :: showGuarded (-> TC ASTNode Bool Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showGuarded tc body hasFields sp)
  (if (! hasFields)
    (Some body)
    (if (let (Some seenFn) (showEnsureSeen tc sp))
      (if (let (Some pushFn) (showHelperName tc "Vec" "vecPush" sp))
        (if (let (Some popFn) (showHelperName tc "Vec" "vecPop" sp))
          {
            (let after vecNew)
            (vecPush after (showCall1 popFn (showVar "seen" sp) sp))
            (vecPush after (showVar "r" sp))
            (let steps vecNew)
            (vecPush steps (showCall2 pushFn (showVar "seen" sp) (showAddr "v" sp) sp))
            (vecPush steps (mkELetAt "r" body (nodeBuildEBegin after 0 0 sp) sp))
            (Some
              (nodeBuildEIf (showCall3 seenFn (showVar "seen" sp) (showAddr "v" sp) (mkEInt 0) sp)
                (showLit "..." sp) (nodeBuildEBegin steps 0 0 sp) sp))
          }
          None)
        None)
      None)))

; One arm per constructor: `((C) "C")`, or `((D f0 f1) "(D " f0 " " f1 ")")`
; with each field rendered by `showFieldExpr` at this instantiation.
(pub :: showGenArms (-> TC (Vec CtorEnt) ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Bool))
(pub fn (showGenArms tc ctors ty arms sp i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    true
    {
      (let ctor (vecGet ctors i))
      (let cname ctor.name)
      (let ftys (showCtorFieldTys tc ctor ty))
      (if (== (vecLen ftys) 0)
        {
          (vecPush arms (nodeBuildMArm (nodeBuildPCon0 cname 0 0 sp) (showLit cname sp) 0 sp))
          (showGenArms tc ctors ty arms sp (+ i 1))
        }
        {
          (let pat (showBinderSpine (showVar cname sp) (vecLen ftys) sp 0))
          (if (let (Some body) (showCtorBody tc ftys (showLit (concat "(" cname) sp) sp 0))
            (if (let (Some closed) (showCat tc body (showLit ")" sp) sp))
              {
                (vecPush arms (nodeBuildMArm pat closed 0 sp))
                (showGenArms tc ctors ty arms sp (+ i 1))
              }
              false)
            false)
        })
    }))

; `(C f0 f1 ...)` as the parser builds a constructor pattern: a
; left-nested application spine of variable nodes.
(pub :: showBinderSpine (-> ASTNode Int Int Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showBinderSpine acc n sp i)
  (if (>= i n)
    acc
    (showBinderSpine (mkEApp acc (showVar (concat "f" (fmtInt i)) sp)) n sp (+ i 1))))

(pub :: showCtorBody (-> TC Nodes ASTNode Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showCtorBody tc ftys acc sp i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ftys))
    (Some acc)
    (if (let (Some spaced) (showCat tc acc (showLit " " sp) sp))
      (if (let (Some field) (showFieldExpr tc (vecGet ftys i) (showVar (concat "f" (fmtInt i)) sp)
                              sp))
        (if (let (Some joined) (showCat tc spaced field sp))
          (showCtorBody tc ftys joined sp (+ i 1))
          None)
        None)
      None)))

; The renderer of a struct: `{x = 1, y = 2}` in declaration order,
; each field read by name and rendered by its declared type, under
; the same ancestor guard.
;
; A WORD STRUCT renders as its type's name, `<Chan>`, and reads
; nothing. It is sealed (MM-VAL-10a): what its word means is its
; module's business, so a rendering outside the module that read the
; field would be refused as AX3086 at the format hole, over a field the
; program never wrote. The same answer inside the module keeps the one
; type rendering one way wherever it is printed.
(pub :: showGenStruct (-> TC ASTNode String Int StructEnt Bool))
(pub fn (showGenStruct tc ty nm sp sent)
  {
    (let fnd
      (showDeclare tc nm (mkTArr ty (mkTArr mkVecIntTy mkStringTy)) (showParams "v" "seen") sp))
    (let fields (structEntFields sent))
    (let rendered
      (if (!= (& (structEntRepr sent) 5) 0)
        (Some (showLit (concat "<" (concat (structEntName sent) ">")) sp))
        (if (== (vecLen fields) 0)
          (Some (showLit "{}" sp))
          (if (let (Some body) (showStructBody tc fields (showLit "{" sp) sp 0))
            (if (let (Some closed) (showCat tc body (showLit "}" sp) sp))
              (showGuarded tc closed true sp)
              None)
            None))))
    (if (let (Some body) rendered)
      {
        (showFinish tc fnd body)
        true
      }
      false)
  })

(pub :: showStructBody (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showStructBody tc fields acc sp i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    (Some acc)
    {
      (let f (vecGet fields i))
      (if (let (Some named) (showCat tc acc
                              (showLit
                                (concat
                                  (if (== i 0)
                                    ""
                                    ", ")
                                  (concat (nodeAName f) " = "))
                                sp)
                              sp))
        (if (let (Some field) (showFieldExpr tc (nodeTyChild f)
                                (nodeBuildEField (showVar "v" sp) (nodeAName f) 0 sp) sp))
          (if (let (Some joined) (showCat tc named field sp))
            (showStructBody tc fields joined sp (+ i 1))
            None)
          None)
        None)
    }))

; The ancestor test, one for the whole program:
;
;   (fn (show#seen seen x i)
;     (if (>= i (vecLen seen)) false
;       (if (== (vecGet seen i) x) true (show#seen seen x (+ i 1)))))
(pub :: showEnsureSeen (-> TC Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (showEnsureSeen tc sp)
  {
    (let nm "show#seen")
    (if (isSome (fnEntExact tc nm))
      (Some nm)
      (if (let (Some lenFn) (showHelperName tc "Vec" "vecLen" sp))
        (if (let (Some getFn) (showHelperName tc "Vec" "vecGet" sp))
          {
            (let fnd
              (showDeclare tc nm (mkTArr mkVecIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkIntTy mkBoolTy)))
                (showParams3 "seen" "x" "i") sp))
            {
              (showFinish tc fnd
                (nodeBuildEIf
                  (showCall2 ">=" (showVar "i" sp) (showCall1 lenFn (showVar "seen" sp) sp) sp)
                  (showVar "false" sp)
                  (nodeBuildEIf
                    (showCall2 "==" (showCall2 getFn (showVar "seen" sp) (showVar "i" sp) sp)
                      (showVar "x" sp) sp)
                    (showVar "true" sp)
                    (showCall3 nm (showVar "seen" sp) (showVar "x" sp)
                      (showCall2 "+" (showVar "i" sp) (mkEInt 1) sp) sp)
                    sp)
                  sp))
              (Some nm)
            }
          }
          None)
        None))
  })

; A `String` inside a structure, quoted with the lexer's seven escapes
; (`isEscapeChar`) minus the one a string never needs (`\'`), byte by
; byte so a multi-byte character passes through untouched:
;
;   (fn (show#String#quoted s) (concat (show#String#from s 0 "\"") "\""))
;   (fn (show#String#from s i acc)
;     (if (>= i (strLen s)) acc
;       (show#String#from s (+ i 1) (concat acc (show#String#byte (strByte s i))))))
;   (fn (show#String#byte b) (if (== b 34) "\\\"" ... (strFromByte b)))
(:: showGenerateString (-> TC String Int Bool))
(fn (showGenerateString tc nm sp)
  {
    (let fromNm "show#String#from")
    (let byteNm "show#String#byte")
    (let quoted (showDeclare tc nm (mkTArr mkStringTy mkStringTy) (showParams "s" "") sp))
    (let from
      (showDeclare tc "show#String#from"
        (mkTArr mkStringTy (mkTArr mkIntTy (mkTArr mkStringTy mkStringTy)))
        (showParams3 "s" "i" "acc") sp))
    (let byte
      (showDeclare tc "show#String#byte" (mkTArr mkIntTy mkStringTy) (showParams "b" "") sp))
    (if (let (Some concatFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "concat" sp))
      {
        (showFinish tc quoted
          (showCall2 concatFn (showCall3 fromNm (showVar "s" sp) (mkEInt 0) (showLit "\\\"" sp) sp)
            (showLit "\\\"" sp) sp))
        (if (let (Some lenFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "strLen" sp))
          (if (let (Some byteFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "strByte" sp))
            {
              (showFinish tc from
                (nodeBuildEIf
                  (showCall2 ">=" (showVar "i" sp) (showCall1 lenFn (showVar "s" sp) sp) sp)
                  (showVar "acc" sp)
                  (showCall3 fromNm (showVar "s" sp) (showCall2 "+" (showVar "i" sp) (mkEInt 1) sp)
                    (showCall2 concatFn (showVar "acc" sp)
                      (showCall1 byteNm (showCall2 byteFn (showVar "s" sp) (showVar "i" sp) sp) sp)
                      sp)
                    sp)
                  sp))
              (if (let (Some fromByteFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "strFromByte" sp))
                {
                  ; Each `text` is the LEXEME body that unescapes to the two
                  ; bytes wanted at run time: `\"` is spelled `\\\"` in a
                  ; literal, and that spelling is itself a literal here.
                  (showFinish tc byte
                    (showByteEscapes tc "b" 34 "\\\\\\\""
                      (showByteEscapes tc "b" 92 "\\\\\\\\"
                        (showByteEscapes tc "b" 10 "\\\\n"
                          (showByteEscapes tc "b" 9 "\\\\t"
                            (showByteEscapes tc "b" 13 "\\\\r"
                              (showByteEscapes tc "b" 0 "\\\\0"
                                (showCall1 fromByteFn (showVar "b" sp) sp) sp)
                              sp)
                            sp)
                          sp)
                        sp)
                      sp))
                  true
                }
                false)
            }
            false)
          false)
      }
      false)
  })

(pub :: showEnsureString (-> TC Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (showEnsureString tc sp)
  {
    (let nm "show#String#quoted")
    (if (isSome (fnEntExact tc nm))
      (Some nm)
      {
        (let declCount (vecLen (tcAllDeclsVec tc)))
        (let fnCount (vecLen (tcFnsVec tc)))
        (if (showGenerateString tc nm sp)
          (Some nm)
          {
            (showRollback tc declCount fnCount)
            None
          })
      })
  })

; `(if (== v code) "text" rest)` - one rung of an escape ladder.
(pub :: showByteEscapes (-> TC String Int String ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (showByteEscapes tc v code text rest sp)
  (nodeBuildEIf (showCall2 "==" (showVar v sp) (mkEInt code) sp) (showLit text sp) rest sp))

; A `Char` as its literal. A `Char` is a code point (reference.md), so
; the bytes are UTF-8 encoded here with the arithmetic `Utf8.utf8FromChar`
; would use - that module is not loaded by `IO` and the rewrite may not
; assume it:
;
;   (fn (show#Char c) (let ((n (cast Int c))) (concat "'" (concat (show#Char#esc n) "'"))))
;   (fn (show#Char#esc n) (if (== n 10) "\\n" ... (show#Char#utf8 n)))
;   (fn (show#Char#utf8 n) (if (< n 128) (strFromByte n) (if (< n 2048) ... )))
(pub :: showGenChar (-> TC String Int Bool))
(pub fn (showGenChar tc nm sp)
  {
    (let escNm "show#Char#esc")
    (let utfNm "show#Char#utf8")
    (let lit (showDeclare tc nm (mkTArr mkCharTy mkStringTy) (showParams "c" "") sp))
    (let esc (showDeclare tc "show#Char#esc" (mkTArr mkIntTy mkStringTy) (showParams "n" "") sp))
    (let utf (showDeclare tc "show#Char#utf8" (mkTArr mkIntTy mkStringTy) (showParams "n" "") sp))
    (if (let (Some concatFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "concat" sp))
      {
        (showFinish tc lit
          (mkELetAt "n" (showAddr "c" sp)
            (showCall2 concatFn (showLit "'" sp)
              (showCall2 concatFn (showCall1 escNm (showVar "n" sp) sp) (showLit "'" sp) sp) sp)
            sp))
        (showFinish tc esc
          (showByteEscapes tc "n" 10 "\\\\n"
            (showByteEscapes tc "n" 9 "\\\\t"
              (showByteEscapes tc "n" 13 "\\\\r"
                (showByteEscapes tc "n" 92 "\\\\\\\\"
                  (showByteEscapes tc "n" 39 "\\\\'"
                    (showByteEscapes tc "n" 0 "\\\\0" (showCall1 utfNm (showVar "n" sp) sp) sp) sp)
                  sp)
                sp)
              sp)
            sp))
        (if (let (Some utfBody) (showUtf8Ladder tc sp))
          {
            (showFinish tc utf utfBody)
            true
          }
          false)
      }
      false)
  })

; `(+ base (% (/ n div) 64))` as one continuation byte, or the lead
; byte when `mod` is 0 (no masking).
(:: showUtf8ByteWith (-> String Int Int Int Int ASTNode))
(fn (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn base div mask sp)
  {
    (let shifted
      (if (== div 1)
        (showVar "n" sp)
        (showCall2 "/" (showVar "n" sp) (mkEInt div) sp)))
    (showCall1 fromByteFn
      (showCall2 "+" (mkEInt base)
        (if (== mask 0)
          shifted
          (showCall2 "%" shifted (mkEInt 64) sp))
        sp)
      sp)
  })

(pub :: showUtf8Byte (-> TC Int Int Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showUtf8Byte tc base div mask sp)
  (if (let (Some fromByteFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "strFromByte" sp))
    (Some (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn base div mask sp))
    None))

(pub :: showUtf8Ladder (-> TC Int (Option ASTNode)))

; A continuation byte: `(+ 128 (% (/ n div) 64))`.
(pub :: showUtf8Cont (-> TC Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (showUtf8Cont tc div sp)
  (showUtf8Byte tc 128 div 1 sp))

(pub fn (showUtf8Ladder tc sp)
  (if (let (Some fromByteFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "strFromByte" sp))
    (if (let (Some concatFn) (showHelperName tc "Str" "concat" sp))
      (Some
        (nodeBuildEIf (showCall2 "<" (showVar "n" sp) (mkEInt 128) sp)
          (showCall1 fromByteFn (showVar "n" sp) sp)
          (nodeBuildEIf (showCall2 "<" (showVar "n" sp) (mkEInt 2048) sp)
            (showCall2 concatFn (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 192 64 0 sp)
              (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 128 1 1 sp) sp)
            (nodeBuildEIf (showCall2 "<" (showVar "n" sp) (mkEInt 65536) sp)
              (showCall2 concatFn (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 224 4096 0 sp)
                (showCall2 concatFn (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 128 64 1 sp)
                  (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 128 1 1 sp) sp)
                sp)
              (showCall2 concatFn (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 240 262144 0 sp)
                (showCall2 concatFn (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 128 4096 1 sp)
                  (showCall2 concatFn (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 128 64 1 sp)
                    (showUtf8ByteWith fromByteFn 128 1 1 sp) sp)
                  sp)
                sp)
              sp)
            sp)
          sp))
      None)
    None))

; Exactly `(op l r)` with a bare operator head - stage0's
; numeric_builtin_app is purely syntactic; no shadow check exists.
(pub :: isNumericTwoArg (-> ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (isNumericTwoArg e name)
  (if (!= (nodeTag (nodeAChild e)) TAG_E_APP)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag (nodeAChild (nodeAChild e))) TAG_E_VAR)
      0
      (if (== (isNumOp name) 0)
        0
        1))))

(pub :: isNumOp (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isNumOp n)
  (if (sizedTyIn n "% & | ^ << >>")
    1
    (if (strEq n "+")
      1
      (if (strEq n "-")
        1
        (if (strEq n "*")
          1
          (if (strEq n "/")
            1
            (if (strEq n "==")
              1
              (if (strEq n "!=")
                1
                (if (strEq n "<")
                  1
                  (if (strEq n ">")
                    1
                    (if (strEq n "<=")
                      1
                      (if (strEq n ">=")
                        1
                        0))))))))))))

(pub :: isNumCmpOp (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isNumCmpOp n)
  (if (strEq n "==")
    1
    (if (strEq n "!=")
      1
      (if (strEq n "<")
        1
        (if (strEq n ">")
          1
          (if (strEq n "<=")
            1
            (if (strEq n ">=")
              1
              0)))))))

; The numeric matrix, stage0's check_numeric_builtin: operands first
; (left then right, both always), poison silences everything (a
; comparison still answers Bool; arithmetic answers poison), a float
; on EITHER side selects the float path where every non-float operand
; must be compatible with Float, otherwise both operands must be
; compatible with Int. (A String no longer is - the stringly
; equality arm answers Bool ahead of this matrix since the fiat's
; deletion.)
;
; `head` is the spine node whose word 1 is the operator's own
; identifier, and it is here for one job: `==` and `!=` over two
; `String`s are rewritten in place into `__streq`/`__strne`, so the
; emitter compares CONTENT rather than the two handles. See
; `equalityIsStringly` for when that fires and why it cannot fire on an
; ordinary `Int`.
(pub :: tyIntegerCode (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIntegerCode tc ty)
  {
    (let t (castNormTy tc ty))
    (if (&& (! (nodeSame t nilNode)) (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON))
      (integerTypeCode (nodeAName t))
      0)
  })

; Both operands have one precise integer type. A parser-inserted unary
; zero adopts the operand's type; a written literal remains Int.
(pub :: checkIntegerOperands (-> TC String ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkIntegerOperands tc op l r lt0 rt head)
  {
    (let mut lt lt0)
    {
      (if (&& (strEq op "-") (&& (== (nodeTag l) TAG_E_INT) (== (nodeB l) 1)))
        (set lt rt))
      {
        (let code (tyIntegerCode tc lt))
        {
          (if (== code 0)
            (emitMismatch tc (tyRender rt) (tyRender lt) (spanOf l)))
          (if (== (tyCompat tc lt rt) 0)
            (emitMismatchTy tc lt rt (spanOf r)))
          (if (&& (&& (> code 0) (!= code 64)) (== code (tyIntegerCode tc rt)))
            (writeNodeWord head 3 code))
          (if (== (isNumCmpOp op) 1)
            mkBoolTy
            lt)
        }
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: checkNumeric (-> TC String ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkNumeric tc op l r head)
  ; Word 1 of the head VAR node is the operator's name. Writing
  ; `(nodeA head)` instead - the name STRING itself - corrupts the
  ; interned `"=="`, and the symptom is a comparison that stops
  ; answering `Bool` several checks later.
  {
    (let mut lt (checkExprH tc l 0))
    {
      (let rt (checkExprH tc r 0))
      {
        (writeNodeWord head 3 0)
        ; The parser marks only the zero it inserts for unary `(- x)`.
        ; Give that zero Float's tag when x is Float; a written Int zero
        ; remains an Int and cannot make mixed arithmetic well typed.
        (if (&& (strEq op "-")
              (&& (== (nodeTag l) TAG_E_INT) (&& (== (nodeB l) 1) (== (tyIsFloatTy rt) 1))))
          {
            (writeNodeWord l 0 TAG_E_FLT)
            (set lt mkFloatTy)
          })
        ; A function value where arithmetic needs a number is a shape
        ; error at the operand, not a `{unknown}` downstream: refuse
        ; it audibly (the body is still verified inside).
        (if (== (isPendingNode lt) 1)
          (refusePendingOperand tc lt "a number" (spanOf l)))
        (if (== (isPendingNode rt) 1)
          (refusePendingOperand tc rt "a number" (spanOf r)))
        (if (== (equalityIsStringly op lt rt) 1)
          (writeNodeText head 1
            (if (strEq op "==")
              "__streq"
              "__strne")))
        ; A STRINGLY equality answers Bool here, before the numeric
        ; matrix: its operands are legitimately Strings and the node was
        ; just rewritten to the content comparison. Under the fiat the
        ; Int arm accepted them anyway, so this arm is what makes string
        ; equality survive the fiat's deletion - the comparison the
        ; rewrite exists to serve must not be refused by the matrix it
        ; bypasses.
        (if (== (equalityIsStringly op lt rt) 1)
          mkBoolTy
          (if (|| (== (tyIsErr lt) 1) (== (tyIsErr rt) 1))
            (if (== (isNumCmpOp op) 1)
              mkBoolTy
              (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
            (if (&&
                  (|| (&& (> (tyIntegerCode tc lt) 0) (!= (tyIntegerCode tc lt) 64))
                    (&& (> (tyIntegerCode tc rt) 0) (!= (tyIntegerCode tc rt) 64)))
                  (&& (== (tyIsFloatTy lt) 0) (== (tyIsFloatTy rt) 0)))
              (checkIntegerOperands tc op l r lt rt head)
              (if (|| (== (tyIsFloatTy lt) 1) (== (tyIsFloatTy rt) 1))
                {
                  (if (sizedTyIn op "% & | ^ << >>")
                    (emitMismatch tc "an integer" "Float" (nodeSpan head)))
                  (if (&& (== (tyIsFloatTy lt) 0) (== (tyCompat tc lt mkFloatTy) 0))
                    (emitMismatchTy tc mkFloatTy lt (spanOf l))
                    (refuseUnknownOperand tc "Float" lt (spanOf l)))
                  (if (&& (== (tyIsFloatTy rt) 0) (== (tyCompat tc rt mkFloatTy) 0))
                    (emitMismatchTy tc mkFloatTy rt (spanOf r))
                    (refuseUnknownOperand tc "Float" rt (spanOf r)))
                  ; The one thing `restrict(no-wrap)` and
                  ; `restrict(no-trap)` need and cannot
                  ; find out for themselves: this `+`/`-`/`*`/`/` is a
                  ; FLOAT operation, so it lowers to
                  ; `fadd`/`fsub`/`fmul`/`fdiv` and has no wraparound
                  ; to refuse and no integer trap to require. Recorded
                  ; only when
                  ; BOTH operands are Float or compatible with it -
                  ; that is exactly the pair of tests above, so a
                  ; mixed-operand expression, which is a type error
                  ; here, is not also excused there.
                  (if (&& (|| (== (isWrapOp op) 1) (== (isTrapOp op) 1))
                        (&& (|| (== (tyIsFloatTy lt) 1) (== (tyCompat tc lt mkFloatTy) 1))
                          (|| (== (tyIsFloatTy rt) 1) (== (tyCompat tc rt mkFloatTy) 1))))
                    (tcFloatOpAdd tc head))
                  (if (== (isNumCmpOp op) 1)
                    mkBoolTy
                    mkFloatTy)
                }
                {
                  (if (== (subtypeCompatAllows tc mkIntTy lt) 0)
                    (emitMismatch tc "Int" (tyRender lt) (spanOf l))
                    (refuseUnknownOperand tc "Int" lt (spanOf l)))
                  (if (== (subtypeCompatAllows tc mkIntTy rt) 0)
                    (emitMismatch tc "Int" (tyRender rt) (spanOf r))
                    (refuseUnknownOperand tc "Int" rt (spanOf r)))
                  (if (== (isNumCmpOp op) 1)
                    mkBoolTy
                    mkIntTy)
                }))))
      }
    }
  })

; Does this comparison compare two STRINGS?
;
; All three conditions are load-bearing:
;
;   - the operator is `==` or `!=`. The orderings are left alone: `<`
;     on two strings would have to mean `strCmp`, a bigger decision
;     than fixing equality, and an address ordering is at least not a
;     WRONG answer to a question anyone asks.
;   - BOTH sides are exactly `String` (`tyIsStringTy`, not `tyCompat`).
;     One side a string and the other an `Int` was the shape the fiat
;     permitted; since its deletion that shape is a type error at the
;     numeric matrix, and this test's remaining job is keeping an
;     `Int`-vs-`Int` comparison integer.
;   - poison is excluded by the caller's own test, which runs after
;     this one only because a poisoned operand must not rewrite the
;     node and then be reported as a type error about it.
;
; The consequence worth stating: under the fiat most string-valued
; expressions are typed `Int`, so this fires where the author WROTE the
; types down and not otherwise. That is the same bargain `fadd` makes,
; and it is an argument for removing the fiat rather than against
; fixing equality.
(pub :: equalityIsStringly (-> String ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (equalityIsStringly op lt rt)
  (if (&& (|| (strEq op "==") (strEq op "!="))
        (&& (== (tyIsStringTy lt) 1) (== (tyIsStringTy rt) 1)))
    1
    0))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; FORGING CASTS (R-B6, `MM-EXEC-9d`). `(cast T x)` is a type-level
; no-op for the emitter: the word goes through unchanged. When `T` is a
; reference the runtime counts, frees or calls through - a `String`, a
; `Vec`, a `Handle`, a struct, a data type with a field, an arrow, a
; tuple - and `x` is not already that same reference, the cast MAKES
; one out of whatever word `x` was. `(cast Hid 7)` checked OK and read
; a field at address 7: exit 139 at run time, from a function with no
; tag at all. So a forging cast is an unsafe operation, like the raw
; primitives, and its declaration says `effect(unsafe)`.
;
; A cast that only OBSERVES is not one: a reference read as an `Int`,
; an `Int` as an `Int`, a `Foreign` as an `Int` or back, a number as
; another number, an `Int` as an enum-like `data` whose constructors
; are all nullary (a value of one IS its tag, and nothing dereferences
; it). Neither is a cast to the type the value already has, which is
; what a type ascription `(:: e T)` desugars to.
;
; The class of a type for that rule: 0 a word no one dereferences, 1 a
; reference, 2 a type variable (a caller may instantiate it at a
; reference, so a cast INTO one forges exactly as a cast into `String`
; does), 3 nothing is known - poison, the silent wildcard, a name no
; declaration introduces - which reports nothing, because the program
; has already drawn the diagnostic that made it unknown.
(pub :: castTyClass (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castTyClass tc t0)
  {
    (let t (castNormTy tc t0))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      3
      {
        (let tg (nodeTag t))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_ERR)
          3
          (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
            (if (== (strLen (nodeAName t)) 0)
              3
              2)
            (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
              1
              (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
                (if (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren t)) 0)
                  0
                  1)
                (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
                  (castConClass tc t)
                  ; `*T`, the unspellable pointer `alloc` answers: a raw
                  ; address, which nothing counts.
                  0)))))
      })
  })

(pub :: castConClass (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castConClass tc t)
  {
    (let n (nodeAName t))
    (if (|| (strEq n "String") (|| (strEq n "Vec") (strEq n "Handle")))
      1
      ; `Spawn` is a word the runtime opens through its handle table
      ; (MM-PAR-8): only a spawn primitive makes one, so a cast that
      ; makes one out of any other word forges a handle, exactly as a
      ; cast into a word struct does (MM-VAL-22).
      (if (strEq n "Spawn")
        1
        ; `evScalarName` spells the machine words and `Foreign`, a
        ; pointer into memory Axiom never allocated and never walks.
        (if (== (evScalarName n) 1)
          0
          (if (isSome (tySubtypeBase tc t))
            0
            ; Ignoring visibility: a module's private struct is still a
            ; reference in the module that names it - `Map` is private to
            ; `Map.ax`, and `(cast Map 4096)` in a user file forged one
            ; while this asked `findStruct` and answered 3, "unknown".
            (if (! (isNone (findStructAnyVis tc n)))
              1
              {
                (let deFound (findDataAnyVis tc n))
                (if (let (Some de) deFound)
                  (if (== (castDataAllNullary de.ctors 0) 1)
                    0
                    1)
                  3)
              })))))
  })

; A `data` whose every constructor is nullary is a set of tags, and a
; value of it is an immediate word: `(Empty)` allocates nothing
; (`ctorAllocArity`). `Vec`'s entry has no constructors at all and
; never reaches this - it is answered by name above.
(pub :: castDataAllNullary (-> (Vec CtorEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (castDataAllNullary ctors i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    1
    (if (> (vecLen (ctorEntFieldTys (vecGet ctors i))) 0)
      0
      (castDataAllNullary ctors (+ i 1)))))

; The checked type as the forging rule compares it: a pinned placeholder
; read as what it was pinned to, and `(-> A B C)` - which
; `exprToType` spells as a constructor named `->`, because a cast's
; type is written in expression position - as the arrow it names.
; Written types expand aliases when checked in their declaring scope.
; Re-expanding here would capture an imported nominal type with an
; unrelated alias in the caller's scope and hide a forging cast.
(pub :: castNormTy (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (castNormTy tc t0)
  {
    (let t (tyResolve t0))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      nilNode
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
            (&& (strEq (nodeAName t) "->") (>= (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 2)))
        (castArrowOf (nodeBChildren t) 0)
        t))
  })

(pub :: castArrowOf (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (castArrowOf parts i)
  (if (>= i (- (vecLen parts) 1))
    (vecGet parts i)
    (mkTArr (vecGet parts i) (castArrowOf parts (+ i 1)))))

; Are two types the same type? Structural, with every level normalised
; the way `castNormTy` normalises the top: no binding, no wildcard
; matching anything - `tyCompat` answers "may these meet", which is
; the wrong question for "is this cast a no-op".
(pub :: castSameTy (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castSameTy tc a0 b0)
  {
    (let a (castNormTy tc a0))
    (let b (castNormTy tc b0))
    (if (|| (nodeSame a nilNode) (nodeSame b nilNode))
      0
      ; An INSTANTIATION placeholder on the source side that nothing
      ; pinned says the value's type was never constrained: `vecNew`
      ; is `(Vec _i)` until something typed is pushed, and pinning
      ; binds `_i` the moment one is (`tyIsMintedVar`). This is asked
      ; once the whole body is typed (`unsafeSettleForges`), so a
      ; placeholder still unpinned then met nothing typed anywhere in
      ; the body. Casting it is the ascription `(:: vecNew Ints)`
      ; spells, which chooses the type rather than forging one. A `_t`
      ; placeholder - a value whose type the checker does not know -
      ; is not this, and neither is a variable the signature wrote.
      {
        (let ta (nodeTag a))
        (let tb (nodeTag b))
        (if (&& (== tb TAG_T_VAR) (== (tyIsMintedVar b) 1))
          1
          (if (!= ta tb)
            0
            (if (== ta TAG_T_VAR)
              (if (&& (> (strLen (nodeAName a)) 0) (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b)))
                1
                0)
              (if (== ta TAG_T_CON)
                (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b)) (== (tySameDecl tc a b) 1))
                  (castSameVec tc (nodeBChildren a) (nodeBChildren b) 0)
                  0)
                (if (== ta TAG_T_ARR)
                  (if (== (castSameTy tc (nodeAChild a) (nodeAChild b)) 1)
                    (castSameTy tc (nodeBChild a) (nodeBChild b))
                    0)
                  (if (== ta TAG_T_TUP)
                    (castSameVec tc (nodeAChildren a) (nodeAChildren b) 0)
                    (if (== ta TAG_T_PTR)
                      (castSameTy tc (nodeAChild a) (nodeAChild b))
                      0)))))))
      })
  })

(pub :: castSameVec (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (castSameVec tc xs ys i)
  (if (|| (== (as xs Int) 0) (== (as ys Int) 0))
    (if (&& (== (as xs Int) 0) (== (as ys Int) 0))
      1
      0)
    (if (!= (vecLen xs) (vecLen ys))
      0
      (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
        1
        (if (== (castSameTy tc (vecGet xs i) (vecGet ys i)) 1)
          (castSameVec tc xs ys (+ i 1))
          0)))))

; A word struct is one `Int` word, so `as` from one to any other type
; reads its field. Outside its module that is AX3086, as `w.slot` is,
; unless the declaration says `effect(unsafe)`: a trusted boundary may
; keep a sealed word in raw memory and forge it back, as AXQLite keeps
; its pager in a connection block, and forging is already its to vouch
; for. A conversion to the same type reads nothing and stays allowed.
(pub :: castCheckWordRead (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (castCheckWordRead tc tgt src span)
  {
    (let s (tyResolve src))
    (if (!= (nodeTag s) TAG_T_CON)
      0
      {
        (let entFound (findStructAnyVis tc (nodeAName s)))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (if (== (& (structEntRepr ent) 1) 0)
            0
            (if (|| (== (wordSealedHere tc (Some ent)) 0)
                  (|| (== (castSameTy tc tgt src) 1) (== (curDeclTrusted tc) 1)))
              0
              (emitWordSealedRead tc (Some ent) (nodeAName (vecGet (structEntFields ent) 0)) span)))
          0)
      })
  })

; 1 when the declaration being checked says `effect(unsafe)`: its
; FnEnt's word 9, which `tcUnsafeRoles` sets from its tags before any
; body is checked. Word 24 is the declaration, as `curFnSigTy` reads it.
(pub :: curDeclTrusted (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (curDeclTrusted tc)
  (if (let (Some d) (tcCurDeclNode tc))
    (if (let (Some ent) (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
      (fnEntUnsafe ent)
      0)
    0))

; 1 when `(cast tgt x)`, with `x` of type `src`, forges a reference.
; The target decides whether the cast can forge at all
; (`castMayForge`); the source only whether it already IS that
; reference.
(pub :: castForges (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castForges tc tgt src)
  (if (== (castMayForge tc tgt src) 0)
    0
    (if (== (castSameTy tc tgt src) 1)
      0
      1)))

; 1 when `(cast tgt x)` can forge at all: its target is a reference, or
; a type variable a caller may instantiate at one, and the source's
; type is known. A poisoned or unknown source forges nothing new to
; report: the diagnostic that made it unknown is already on the
; program.
;
; Whether it DOES forge - whether `x` already is that type - is asked
; only once the whole body is typed (`unsafeSettleForges`). Pinning
; binds a body's placeholders as its forms are checked, so the source's
; type at the cast is not yet its type:
;
;     (let ((v vecNew))
;       (let ((s (cast (Vec String) v)))
;         { (vecPush v 42) (vecGet s 0) }))
;
; reads `v` as `(Vec _i)` at the cast, which `castSameTy` takes for an
; ascription, and as `(Vec Int)` once the push is checked. Decided at
; the cast, that program checked OK and read 42 as a `String`: exit 139
; from a declaration with no tag.
(pub :: castMayForge (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castMayForge tc tgt src)
  {
    (let k (castTyClass tc tgt))
    (if (|| (== k 0) (== k 3))
      0
      (if (== (tyIsErr (tyResolve src)) 1)
        0
        1))
  })

; Record a cast that may forge, in the body being checked: the node,
; then the target and source types the message names. `forgeSites` is a Vec
; only while `tcWalkDecls` checks a function, so a cast checked
; anywhere else - a contract, a declaration the walk is not in -
; records nothing and has no declaration to answer for it. The body's
; check ends by keeping only the casts that do forge
; (`unsafeSettleForges`).
;
; A cast whose VALUE never returns forges nothing: there is no word to
; make a reference of, and the coercion is unreachable. That is the
; reading AX3040's divergence walk already gives the same shape
; (`divTailOk`'s `cast` arm), and it is how a diverging function types
; its result - `(cast a (exit 70))` in `IO.todo`,
; `tests/selfhost/976-diverging-tyvar.ax`'s `panic`. Asked only of a
; cast whose target is a reference, so the walk costs nothing on the
; observing casts that are most of them.
(pub :: castNoteForge (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castNoteForge tc e tgt src val)
  (if (let (Some sites) (tcForgeSites tc))
    (if (== (divTailOk (tcAllDeclsVec tc) val vecNew 0) 1)
      0
      (if (== (castMayForge tc tgt src) 1)
        {
          (vecPush sites e)
          (vecPush sites tgt)
          (vecPush sites src)
          0
        }
        ; A word target can erase a reference; keep the same body-local
        ; site order for eraseSettle, with an explicitly active collector.
        (if (let (Some sites) (tcEraseSites tc))
          (if (!= (castTyClass tc tgt) 0)
            0
            {
              (vecPush sites e)
              (vecPush sites tgt)
              (vecPush sites src)
              0
            })
          0)))
    0))

; `(cast Type v)`, `(sizeof Type)`, `(alignof Type)`: argument zero is
; a TYPE, and a type name is not a variable reference - checking it
; drew AX3001 `Int` on the compiler's own source, in three files. The
; remaining arguments are ordinary expressions. A cast's result is
; the named type; sizeof/alignof answer Int.
(pub :: checkCastForm (-> TC ASTNode String ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkCastForm tc e hname)
  {
    (let written (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (if (&& (> (vecLen written) 0)
          (== (tyCheckAliasArity tc (exprToType (vecGet written 0)) (spanOf (vecGet written 0))) 0))
      mkTErr
      ; The value's type is kept for the forging rule. `checkCastArgs`
      ; stamps nothing at index 1, so checking it here first and the
      ; surplus from index 2 checks the same nodes in the same order.
      ; The cast erases the value's type, so a pending lambda there
      ; is never grounded by a use: inferred standalone (the
      ; forging rule below requires a TYPE, never a pending node) -
      ; which also verifies the body (462: the cast spine's closure
      ; keeps its evidence).
      {
        (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
        ; None is a form with no source value, including sizeof/alignof.
        ; Some keeps a checked poison or inference type present.
        (let srcTy
          (:: {
            (let srcTy0
              (:: (if (>= (vecLen args) 2)
                (Some
                  {
                    (let value (vecGet args 1))
                    (let target (castTargetTy tc (vecGet args 0)))
                    (if (&& (== (nodeTag value) TAG_E_LAM) (== (nodeTag target) TAG_T_ARR))
                      (checkLamAgainst tc value target)
                      (groundArgByDecl tc (checkExprH tc value 0) (Some target) (spanOf value)))
                  })
                None) (Option ASTNode)))
            (match srcTy0
              ((None) None)
              ((Some sourceType)
                (Some
                  (if (== (isPendingNode sourceType) 1)
                    (groundStandalone tc sourceType (spanOf (vecGet args 1)))
                    sourceType))
              ))
          } (Option ASTNode)))
        {
          (checkCastArgs tc args e 2 (vecLen args))
          ; `sizeof` and `alignof` take one TYPE and answer a constant, so
          ; a surplus argument has nowhere to go: the emitter dropped it in
          ; silence and `(sizeof Int 99)` answered 8. `cast` is exempt -
          ; its result is routinely a function value, and a surplus
          ; argument there is an application of it.
          (if (&&
                (if (isValueCastForm hname)
                  false
                  true)
                (> (vecLen args) 1))
            (emitDiag tc
              (diagSetLabel
                (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3069" "sizeof-arity" (spanOf (vecGet args 1))
                  (concat (concat "`" hname)
                    "` takes one type and answers a constant, so this argument has nowhere to go")
                  None (Some "remove the extra argument") "it used to be discarded in silence")
                "extra argument")))
          ; The other side of the same count: `(cast T)` with its VALUE
          ; missing. `isCastLike` keeps `cast` out of `checkSaturation`,
          ; which is right for the surplus it exempts above and left the
          ; shortfall unasked - it checked OK and the emitter lowered the
          ; TYPE name as a variable, `%Int`, which `llc` refused as an
          ; undefined value (`tests/fuzz/cast-missing-operand.axfuzz`,
          ; found by the seeded fuzzer 2026-09-27). The code is the one
          ; a missing argument gets everywhere else. `sizeof`/`alignof`
          ; cannot reach this with none: `(sizeof)` is the bare name,
          ; which `checkBareValue` refuses. A lone LITERAL is not asked:
          ; AX3002 below already refuses it as "not a type", with the
          ; same `(cast Int e)` advice, and two reports would be one
          ; mistake said twice.
          (if (&& (isValueCastForm hname)
                (&& (< (vecLen args) 2)
                  (|| (== (vecLen args) 0) (== (castTypeIsLiteral (vecGet args 0)) 0))))
            (emitDiag tc
              (diagSetLabel
                (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3013" "partial-application" (spanOf (spineHead e))
                  (concat
                    (concat "partial application of `as`: it takes 2 argument(s) and "
                      (fmtInt (vecLen args)))
                    " were supplied")
                  None
                  (Some
                    "write the value to convert before the target type - `(as e Int)` - or, for a function value, `(lambda (x) (as x Int))`")
                  "`as` is a form and not a function: with its value missing there was nothing to convert, and the emitter read the type name as a variable the native toolchain refused")
                "`as` takes a value and a target type; an operand is missing")))
          (if (let (Some badPart) (if (>= (vecLen args) 1)
            (castTypeBadPart (vecGet args 0))
            None))
            ; A LITERAL cannot name a type, and `exprToType`
            ; answers the SILENT WILDCARD for every shape it
            ; cannot convert - a type that unifies with anything,
            ; so nothing downstream ever objects. Measured:
            ; `(cast "hello" 7)` and `(cast 4 helper)` both check
            ; clean, and the second returns a closure address
            ; from a function declaring `Int` - 32, whatever the
            ; argument. Type ascription `(:: e T)` desugars to
            ; `(cast T e)`, so `(::helper 4)` reached it too.
            ;
            ; The wildcard stays for shapes that MIGHT be a type
            ; this pass cannot resolve; a literal is not one of
            ; those, and saying so costs nothing that was working.
            ;
            ; `sizeof` and `alignof` share this refusal. They never
            ; reached it - the check sat inside the `cast` arm, and
            ; both answered `Int` for any operand, so `(alignof 00)`
            ; checked clean while `fmt` refused it (the seeded
            ; fuzzer's m00120). The operand was ignored, not
            ; wildcarded, so their help and note say that instead.
            {
              (emitDiag tc
                (diagSetLabel
                  (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3002" "undefined-type" (spanOf badPart)
                    (if (isValueCastForm hname)
                      (if (== (castTypeIsLiteral badPart) 1)
                        "a literal is not a type, and `as` takes a target type after the value"
                        "this expression is not a type, and `as` takes a target type after the value")
                      (if (== (castTypeIsLiteral badPart) 1)
                        (concat (concat "a literal is not a type, and `" hname) "` takes a type")
                        (concat (concat "this expression is not a type, and `" hname)
                          "` takes a type")))
                    None
                    (Some
                      (if (isValueCastForm hname)
                        "write the type to cast TO - `(as e Int)` - or, for an ascription, `(:: e Int)`"
                        (concat (concat "write the type to measure - `(" hname) " Int)`")))
                    (if (isValueCastForm hname)
                      "an unconvertible shape here used to answer a silent wildcard, which unifies with everything: the cast was performed, the result had no type anyone could object to, and a function returning it satisfied any declaration"
                      (concat (concat "the operand used to be ignored in silence: `(" hname)
                        " 99)` answered 8")))
                  "not a type"))
              (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
            }
            (if (isValueCastForm hname)
              (if (>= (vecLen args) 1)
                {
                  (let targetTy (castTargetTy tc (vecGet args 0)))
                  {
                    (if (let (Some sourceType) srcTy)
                      (if (&& (>= (vecLen args) 2) (|| (tyIsUnit targetTy) (tyIsUnit sourceType)))
                        (emitDiag tc
                          (diagSetLabel
                            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3139" "unit-cast" (spanOf (vecGet args 1))
                              (if (tyIsUnit targetTy)
                                "`as` cannot convert to `()`: it has one value and carries no information"
                                "`as` cannot convert from `()`: it has one value and carries no information")
                              None
                              (Some "convert a value that carries information, or drop the cast")
                              "a cast through `()` answers the unit word 0 whatever the value was, so the conversion could only lose what it was given")
                            "a cast through `()`"))))
                    (let subBase
                      (if (&& (! (nodeSame targetTy nilNode)) (== (nodeTag targetTy) TAG_T_CON))
                        (tySubtypeBase tc targetTy)
                        None))
                    (if (isNone subBase)
                      {
                        ; 3 is a proof of a type-preserving cast, distinct
                        ; from a word answer (1) and region freshness (2).
                        ; No proof is minted for surplus applications.
                        (setNodeResWord e
                          (match srcTy
                            ((None) 0)
                            ((Some sourceType)
                              (if (&& (== (vecLen args) 2)
                                    (== (castSameTy tc targetTy sourceType) 1))
                                3
                                0)
                            )))
                        ; Keep the target checked in this scope. Resolving
                        ; its written alias again after the body is checked
                        ; could capture a nominal type from another module.
                        (if (let (Some sourceType) srcTy)
                          (if (>= (vecLen args) 2)
                            {
                              (castNoteForge tc e targetTy sourceType (vecGet args 1))
                              (castCheckWordRead tc targetTy sourceType (spanOf (vecGet args 1)))
                            }))
                        (if (== (asNumericKind tc targetTy) 2)
                          (writeNodeChild (nodeAChild e) 2
                            (mkEVarAt "Float" (spanOf (vecGet args 0)))))
                        (if (strEq hname "__ascribe")
                          (if (let (Some sourceType) srcTy)
                            (if (== (tyCompat tc sourceType targetTy) 0)
                              (emitMismatchTy tc targetTy sourceType (spanOf (vecGet args 1)))))
                          (if (strEq hname "__as_numeric")
                            (rewriteNumericAs tc e targetTy srcTy)))
                        targetTy
                      }
                      ; `(cast Subtype v)` is a CHECKED conversion: the value
                      ; is evaluated once by SUBCHECK emission (value, bounds
                      ; and message travel separately, so a side-effecting
                      ; value is never emitted twice). `args` is
                      ; innermost-first [type, value, ...surplus], where
                      ; surplus is an application of the cast result.
                      (if (< (vecLen args) 2)
                        targetTy
                        {
                          (let boundsH (tySubtypeBounds tc targetTy))
                          (if (let (Some boundsH) boundsH)
                            (if (== (vecLen args) 2)
                              {
                                (writeNodeWord e 0 TAG_E_SUBCHECK)
                                (writeNodeChild e 1 (vecGet args 1))
                                (writeNodeChildren e 2 boundsH)
                                (writeNodeChild e 3
                                  (nodeBuildEStr "\"axiom: subtype check failed\\n\"" 0 0
                                    (spanOf e)))
                                (writeNodeChild e 6 targetTy)
                                targetTy
                              }
                              ; Surplus application of a subtype conversion:
                              ; wrap the VALUE in place and keep the spine.
                              {
                                (let inner (castAppNodeAt e (- (vecLen args) 2)))
                                {
                                  (writeNodeChild inner 2
                                    (mkSubtypeCheckNode (vecGet args 1) boundsH targetTy
                                      (spanOf (vecGet args 1))))
                                  targetTy
                                }
                              })
                            targetTy)
                        }))
                  }
                }
                mkSilentWild)
              mkIntTy))
        }
      })
  })

; `cast`'s arguments, and the SURPLUS ones stamped with the evidence
; class an ordinary application records for itself.
;
; `cast` takes two arguments and anything past them is an APPLICATION
; of its result - `((cast Int f) h.name)` is one spine, which
; `dispatchCall` splits and applies through `emitApplyChain`. Every
; other application stamps its own node with `evClassOf` of the
; argument's type (see `checkExprH`'s application arm), and that stamp
; is the only thing that can tell a lifted lambda whether the word it
; is being handed is a reference. This form does not reach that arm:
; `checkCastForm` claims the WHOLE spine at its outermost node, so the
; intermediate application nodes were never stamped and read back as
; class 0 - "not a reference".
;
; That was a live use-after-free, not the leak this was recorded as.
; Measured on 0.6.0 (`9116167`), a compiler built from this tree, the
; shape `tests/stdlib/461-curried-closure-arg.ax` uses:
;
;   ((cast Int f) h.name)   with f = (lambda (v) (vecPush log v))
;   (set h.name (strDup "bob"))   ; event 5 drops the only share
;   (strDup "zzzzz")              ; re-issued the freed block
;   (strByte (vecGetStr log 0) 0)
;
;   -> 122, the `z` of a string allocated after the free, where the
;      same lambda applied where it stands answers 97.
;
; The walk is by INDEX rather than by node because the two orders
; differ: `spineArgVec` answers innermost-first, so the argument at
; index `i` belongs to the application node `n - 1 - i` steps down
; from the outermost. Arguments are still checked in increasing index
; order, so no diagnostic moves.
(pub :: checkCastArgs (-> TC Nodes ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (checkCastArgs tc args e i n)
  (if (>= i n)
    0
    {
      ; A pending surplus argument is inferred standalone: the
      ; cast erased the only declaration that could ground it,
      ; and the evidence stamp below requires a TYPE (a pending
      ; node reads 0, "not a reference", and a stored closure
      ; would go unretained).
      {
        (let at
          {
            (let at0 (checkExprH tc (vecGet args i) 0))
            (if (== (isPendingNode at0) 1)
              (groundStandalone tc at0 (spanOf (vecGet args i)))
              at0)
          })
        (if (>= i 2)
          {
            (let site (castAppNodeAt e (- (- n 1) i)))
            (let c (evClassOf tc at))
            {
              (setNodeTyWord site
                (if (< c 0)
                  0
                  c))
              (evNotePendingNode tc site at c)
            }
          })
      }
      (checkCastArgs tc args e (+ i 1) n)
    }))

; The application node `k` steps down a spine from `e`, which is what
; turns an argument INDEX into the node `walkAppChain` will read the
; stamp off. Stops early at a non-application rather than walking off
; the end.
(pub :: castAppNodeAt (-> ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (castAppNodeAt e k)
  (if (<= k 0)
    e
    (if (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
      e
      (castAppNodeAt (nodeAChild e) (- k 1)))))

(pub :: checkArgsFromIndex (-> TC Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (checkArgsFromIndex tc args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (checkExprH tc (vecGet args i) 0)
      (checkArgsFromIndex tc args (+ i 1))
    }))

; A type spelled in expression position (cast's first argument),
; converted by the type grammar's atom rules. Unconvertible shapes
; answer the silent wildcard.
; Can this shape never be a type? Only literals answer yes: every
; other shape is either a type or a name this pass may not know yet.
(pub :: castTypeIsLiteral (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castTypeIsLiteral e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let t (nodeTag e))
      (if (|| (== t TAG_E_INT) (|| (== t TAG_E_STR) (|| (== t TAG_E_FLT) (== t TAG_E_CHAR))))
        1
        0)
    }))

; The first part of a type written in expression position that can't
; be one, or 0. A type is a name, or a name applied to types, and
; `exprToType` answers the silent wildcard for anything else: a
; literal, an `if`, a lambda, or a literal nested as an argument, as in
; `(:: e (Vec 2))`. The wildcard unifies with everything, so each of
; those typed a value as anything at all.
; A cast's target type, with its aliases expanded as a signature's
; are. `(cast Step f)` under `(type Step (-> Int Int))` answered
; `Step` itself, which no arrow unifies with, so a signature naming
; the alias's arrow refused the cast with AX3004 (AN-53).
(pub :: castTargetTy (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (castTargetTy tc e)
  (tyExpandedAliases tc (exprToType e)))

; `t` with its aliases expanded, or `t` itself when it names none.
(pub :: tyExpandedAliases (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyExpandedAliases tc t)
  (if (== (tyHasAliasCon tc t) 1)
    (tyExpandAliases tc t 0)
    t))

(pub :: isTypeNode (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (isTypeNode e)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag e))
    (|| (== tag TAG_T_VAR)
      (|| (== tag TAG_T_CON)
        (|| (== tag TAG_T_ARR) (|| (== tag TAG_T_TUP) (|| (== tag TAG_T_PTR) (== tag TAG_T_ERR))))))
  })

(pub :: castTypeBadPart (-> ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (castTypeBadPart e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    None
    {
      (let t (nodeTag e))
      (if (|| (isUnitLit e) (|| (isTypeNode e) (== t TAG_E_VAR)))
        None
        (if (== t TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (let head (spineHead e))
            (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
              (Some head)
              (castTypeBadArgs (spineArgVec e vecNew) 0))
          }
          (Some e)))
    }))

(pub :: castTypeBadArgs (-> Nodes Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (castTypeBadArgs args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    None
    (if (let (Some bad) (castTypeBadPart (vecGet args i)))
      (Some bad)
      (castTypeBadArgs args (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: exprToType (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (exprToType e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    mkSilentWild
    (if (isUnitLit e)
      mkUnitTy
      (if (isTypeNode e)
        e
        (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
          (typeNameToType (nodeAName e))
          (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
            {
              (let head (spineHead e))
              (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
                mkSilentWild
                {
                  (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
                  (let tys vecNew)
                  {
                    (exprsToTypes args tys 0)
                    (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "*")
                      (if (== (vecLen tys) 1)
                        (mkTPtr (vecGet tys 0))
                        mkSilentWild)
                      (if (strEq (nodeAName head) "->")
                        (if (>= (vecLen tys) 2)
                          (foldArrows tys (- (vecLen tys) 2) (vecGet tys (- (vecLen tys) 1)))
                          mkSilentWild)
                        (mkTCon (nodeAName head) tys)))
                  }
                })
            }
            mkSilentWild))))))

(pub :: exprsToTypes (-> Nodes Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (exprsToTypes args acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (vecPush acc (exprToType (vecGet args i)))
      (exprsToTypes args acc (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: typeNameToType (-> String ASTNode))
(pub fn (typeNameToType n)
  (if (typeKeywordKnown n)
    (mkTCon n vecNew)
    (if (== (lexStartsLower n) 1)
      (mkTVar n)
      (mkTCon n vecNew))))

; Saturation, once per spine at its root. A frame-local head is a
; function VALUE applied stepwise - not measurable. A resolved
; function with a measurable arity refuses under-application
; (AX3013); when no function matches, the LAST constructor of the
; name refuses through its arrow depth (AX3009). A refused spine's
; arguments are still checked, left to right; the head is not
; (reporting it again would double the diagnostic). Answers 1 when
; refused.
; A polymorphic result may itself be a function. Saturation cannot
; reject extra arguments until the application has grounded it.
(pub :: fnResultClosed (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (fnResultClosed ty)
  (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_ARR)
    (fnResultClosed (nodeBChild ty))
    (if (|| (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON) (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_PTR))
      1
      0)))

(pub :: tcCheckState (-> TC ExprChecks))
(pub fn (tcCheckState tc)
  tc.exprChecks)

(pub :: collectFnRestrictions (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (collectFnRestrictions tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let decl (vecGet decls i))
      (if (|| (== (nodeTag decl) TAG_D_FN) (== (nodeTag decl) TAG_D_SIG))
        {
          (let rawFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName decl)))
          (if (let (Some raw) rawFound)
            {
              (let entry raw)
              (restrictCollect (nodeAxtagsOf decl) entry.restrictions 0)
            }
            ())
        })
      (collectFnRestrictions tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: validateEffects (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings))
(pub fn (validateEffects tc expression scope)
  {
    (let state (tcCheckState tc))
    (set state.validatingEffects true)
    (let row (collectEffects tc expression scope))
    (set state.validatingEffects false)
    row
  })

; Restriction contracts follow callable parameters to their arguments.
; This runs during validation, after the inference fixpoint has settled.
(pub :: checkRestrictedArgument (-> TC FnEnt Int ASTNode BScope Int))
(pub fn (checkRestrictedArgument tc raw index arg bound)
  (if (let (Some use) (epUseFor raw index))
    (if (== use.def 0)
      0
      {
        (let entry raw)
        (let required (effCopy entry.restrictions))
        (effUnion required use.restrictions 0)
        (if (== (vecLen required) 0)
          0
          {
            (let row vecNew)
            (match (walkEffectsVal tc arg bound vecNew)
              ((None)
                {
                  (if (== (nodeTag arg) TAG_E_VAR)
                    (noteParamRestrictions bound
                      (bsAliasParam (bsParamAlias bound (nodeAName arg))) required))
                  (effAdd row effIncomplete)
                }
              )
              ((Some values)
                {
                  (fnValsCall tc values vecNew bound row 0)
                  (noteValueRestrictions bound values.members required 0)
                }
              ))
            (let state (tcCheckState tc))
            (if state.validatingEffects
              (checkArgumentRestrictions tc entry arg row required 0)
              0)
          })
      })
    0))

(pub :: noteParamRestrictions (-> BScope (Option Int) Strings Int))
(pub fn (noteParamRestrictions bound parameter required)
  (if (let (Some index) parameter)
    {
      (markEparamUse bound index vecNew 0)
      (if (let (Some use) (epUseFind (bsUsesVec bound) index 0))
        (effUnion use.restrictions required 0)
        0)
    }
    0))

(pub :: noteValueRestrictions (-> BScope (Vec FnVal) Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (noteValueRestrictions bound members required i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen members))
    0
    {
      (match (vecGet members i)
        ((NamedFn _) 0)
        ((LambdaFn latent) (noteLatentRestrictions bound latent.uses required 0)))
      (noteValueRestrictions bound members required (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: noteLatentRestrictions (-> BScope (Vec EpUse) Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (noteLatentRestrictions bound uses required i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen uses))
    0
    {
      (let use (vecGet uses i))
      (if (== use.def 1)
        (noteParamRestrictions bound (Some use.pidx) required))
      (noteLatentRestrictions bound uses required (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: restrictionEffectName (-> String (Option String)))
(pub fn (restrictionEffectName restriction)
  (if (strEq restriction "no-io")
      (Some "IO")
    (strEq restriction "no-alloc")
      (Some "Alloc")
    (strEq restriction "no-unsafe")
      (Some "Unsafe")
    (strEq restriction "no-entropy")
      (Some "Entropy")
    (strEq restriction "no-spawn")
      (Some "Spawn")
    (strEq restriction "no-block")
      (Some "Block")
    None))

(pub :: checkArgumentRestrictions (-> TC FnEnt ASTNode Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (checkArgumentRestrictions tc entry arg row required i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen required))
    0
    {
      (let restriction (vecGet required i))
      (match (restrictionEffectName restriction)
        ((None) 0)
        ((Some effect)
          (if (== (effMay row (builtinEff effect)) 1)
            (emitDiag tc
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3049" "restriction-violated" (nodeSpan arg)
                (concat (concat (concat "this callback may perform " effect) "; ")
                  (concat (concat (bareOf entry.name) " requires ") restriction))
                None (Some "pass a callback that keeps the restriction")))
            0)
        ))
      (checkArgumentRestrictions tc entry arg row required (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: checkSaturation (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (checkSaturation tc e head hname)
  (if (isSome (scopeFindFrame tc hname))
    0
    (if (== (isCastLike hname) 1)
      0
      (if (== (isMacroName tc hname) 1)
        0
        {
          (let entFound (findFnEnt tc hname))
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            (if (let (Some ar) (repArity ent))
              {
                (let arLim
                  (if (> (arrowArity (fnEntTy ent)) ar)
                    (arrowArity (fnEntTy ent))
                    ar))
                (let got (spineArgs e 0))
                (if (&& (> got arLim) (== (fnResultClosed (fnEntTy ent)) 1))
                  (if (|| (== (nodeSpan head) 0) (!= (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0))
                    0
                    {
                      (emitDiag tc
                        (diagSetLabel
                          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" (nodeSpan head)
                            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" hname) "` takes ") (fmtInt arLim))
                              (concat " argument(s), given " (fmtInt got)))
                            None
                            (Some
                              "remove the extra argument(s), or add a parameter to the definition"))
                          (concat (fmtInt got) " argument(s) provided here")))
                      (checkSpineArgsOnly tc e)
                      1
                    })
                  (if (>= got ar)
                    0
                    (if (== (nodeSpan head) 0)
                      0
                      {
                        (emitDiag tc
                          (diagSetLabel
                            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3013" "partial-application" (nodeSpan head)
                              (concat
                                (concat
                                  (concat
                                    (concat (concat "partial application of `" hname)
                                      "`: it takes ")
                                    (fmtInt ar))
                                  " argument(s) and ")
                                (concat (fmtInt got) " were supplied"))
                              None
                              (Some
                                "bind the missing arguments with a lambda: `(lambda (y) (f x y))` builds the value `(f x)` would mean")
                              "a partial application has to hold the arguments it was not given, and a top-level function has no closure record to hold them in")
                            (concat
                              (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" hname) "` takes ") (fmtInt ar))
                                " argument(s); ")
                              (concat (fmtInt (- ar got)) " more needed"))))
                        (checkSpineArgsOnly tc e)
                        1
                      })))
              }
              0)
            {
              (let ctorFound (rfindCtor tc hname))
              (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
                {
                  (let want (arrowArity ctor.ctorTy))
                  (let got (spineArgs e 0))
                  (if (== got want)
                    0
                    (if (== (nodeSpan head) 0)
                      0
                      {
                        (emitDiag tc
                          (diagSetLabel
                            (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3009" "constructor-arity-mismatch" (nodeSpan head)
                              (concat
                                (concat
                                  (concat (concat (concat "constructor `" hname) "` expects ")
                                    (fmtInt want))
                                  " argument(s), found ")
                                (fmtInt got))
                              None
                              (Some
                                (concat (concat "this constructor takes exactly " (fmtInt want))
                                  " field(s); add or remove arguments to match")))
                            (concat (fmtInt got) " argument(s) provided here")))
                        (checkSpineArgsOnly tc e)
                        1
                      }))
                }
                0)
            })
        }))))

(pub :: checkSpineArgsOnly (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkSpineArgsOnly tc e)
  {
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (checkArgsFromIndex tc args 0)
  })

; Struct construction: the head names a struct (any module - stage0
; ignores the module here). Wrong field count is AX3067 with the
; ARGUMENTS UNCHECKED and a poisoned result; the right count checks
; every argument against its field's declared type, reporting each
; mismatch and keeping the struct type as the result.
(pub :: findStructIfConstruct (-> TC String (Option StructEnt)))
(pub fn (findStructIfConstruct tc hname)
  (findStruct tc hname))

(pub :: checkStructCon (-> TC ASTNode StructEnt ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkStructCon tc e ent)
  (if (== (wordSealedHere tc (Some ent)) 1)
    {
      (emitWordSealedBuild tc (Some ent) (spanOf e))
      (checkSpineArgsOnly tc e)
      (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
    }
    {
      (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
      (let sourceName (nodeAName (spineHead e)))
      {
        (writeNodeText (spineHead e) 1 (nominalName (structEntName ent) (structEntModule ent)))
        {
          (let result (checkStructConAt tc args (spanOf e) ent sourceName))
          {
            (stampStructEvidence tc ent (spineHead e) result)
            (wordLowerIfWord tc ent e args result)
          }
        }
      }
    }))

; Use the same first-occurrence ordering as constructor code generation.
; The source type arguments remain invariant; evidence records only
; whether each declared variable denotes a counted reference.
(:: stampStructEvidence (-> TC StructEnt ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(fn (stampStructEvidence tc ent head result)
  (if (== (nodeTag result) TAG_T_CON)
    {
      (let vars vecNew)
      (let fields (structEntFields ent))
      {
        (for field in fields
          (sigTyVarsInto (nodeTyChild field) vars))
        (if (> (vecLen vars) 0)
          {
            (let w vecNew)
            (let env vecNew)
            (let pts vecNew)
            (let actuals vecNew)
            {
              (for name in vars
                {
                  (let pt (nodeBuildTVarPlain name 0 0))
                  (let actual (structSubstField ent pt (nodeBChildren result)))
                  {
                    (vecPush w -1)
                    (vecPush env (TypeBinding :name name :ty pt))
                    (vecPush pts pt)
                    (vecPush actuals actual)
                  }
                })
              {
                (let stamp
                  (setNodeTyNames (nodeBuildEvstampCallOptional (tcCurDeclNode tc) w env) vars))
                {
                  (setNodeTyChild head stamp)
                  (for i in 0..(vecLen vars)
                    (evWalk tc stamp (vecGet pts i) (vecGet actuals i) 0 0))
                }
              }
            }
          })
        0
      }
    }
    0))

; A word struct's construction, once it checks, is recorded for the
; lowering (`wordLowerRecord`): the site becomes its one argument.
; Answers the construction's type unchanged.
(pub :: wordLowerIfWord (-> TC StructEnt ASTNode Nodes ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (wordLowerIfWord tc ent site args t)
  {
    (if (&& (== (structEntIsWord (Some ent)) 1) (== (tyIsErr t) 0))
      (if (== (structEntIsLegacyWord (Some ent)) 1)
        (if (== (vecLen args) 1)
          (wordLowerRecord tc site (vecGet args 0)))
        (if (== (vecLen args) (vecLen (structEntFields ent)))
          (wordLowerRecord tc site (wordPackExpr tc ent args (nodeSpan site))))))
    t
  })

; The construction check proper for `(P 1 2)`, once `checkStructCon`
; above has ruled out a sealed word struct: an ambiguous name, the
; field count, then each argument against its field's declared type.
(pub :: checkStructConAt (-> TC Nodes Int StructEnt String ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkStructConAt tc args span ent sourceName)
  (if (== (checkAmbiguousType tc TY_ANY sourceName span) 1)
    ; Poison rather than "the first one" - `tyFind` answered a
    ; candidate so the AX3002 cascade stays shut, and the field count
    ; and field types below would otherwise be checked against a
    ; declaration this reference has just been told it cannot name.
    ; That is exactly the miscompile: an arity variant of the two-team
    ; probe reported `struct `Config` expects 3 field(s), found 2`
    ; inside the module that wrote 2, against a shape it never wrote.
    (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
    {
      (let fields (structEntFields ent))
      (if (!= (vecLen args) (vecLen fields))
        {
          (if (!= span 0)
            (emitDiag tc
              {
                (let msg
                  (concat
                    (concat
                      (concat
                        (concat (concat "struct `" (bareOf (structEntName ent))) "` expects ")
                        (fmtInt (vecLen fields)))
                      " field(s), found ")
                    (fmtInt (vecLen args))))
                (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3067" "struct-arity-mismatch" span msg None None)
              }))
          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
        }
        ; `0`, NOT an empty vector, is what an unparameterised struct
        ; stores - the `D_STRUCT` node's `ty` slot is left untouched
        ; for those, because the macro expander walks that slot as a
        ; type node. So the emptiness test has to admit both, and
        ; `vecLen` on 0 dereferences null: this arm segfaulted every
        ; macro-generated struct in the tree (exit 139) until the
        ; `(== tvs 0)` was put in front of it.
        {
          (let tvs (structEntTyvars ent))
          (if (|| (== (as tvs Int) 0) (== (vecLen tvs) 0))
            {
              (checkStructFields tc args fields 0)
              (mkTCon (nominalName (structEntName ent) (structEntModule ent)) vecNew)
            }
            ; PARAMETERISED. The declared field types mention the
            ; struct's variables, which are SOURCE variables and
            ; therefore rigid - `(ShowOf fmtInt)` against a field
            ; declared `(-> a String)` is a mismatch, not an
            ; instantiation, which is exactly what the first build of
            ; this feature reported.
            ;
            ; So the variables are instantiated ONCE for the whole
            ; construction, through one shared env, so that a variable
            ; used by two fields is the same placeholder in both and
            ; `(Pair 1 "x")` cannot answer `(Pair Int Int)`. Each field
            ; is then checked against its instantiated type, and
            ; `pinShape` - the same walker an application uses to pin a
            ; callee's placeholders from its arguments - reads the
            ; bindings back out of a carrier tuple. The result is
            ; `TCon(name, those bindings)`, which is what makes
            ; `(ShowOf Int)` a different type from `(ShowOf String)`.
            {
              (let env vecNew)
              {
                (let insts (structInstFieldTys tc fields env vecNew 0))
                {
                  (checkStructFieldsInst tc args insts 0)
                  (mkTCon (nominalName (structEntName ent) (structEntModule ent))
                    (structPinArgs tc args insts (structTvNodes env tvs vecNew 0) vecNew 0))
                }
              }
            })
        })
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Effect inference: stage0's `collect_effects` walk and the monotone
; fixpoint over it. What introduces an effect, and nothing else does:
;
;   calling `__syscallN`         IO
;   calling anything else        that callee's inferred effects
;   an effect operation          Custom(E), stored on its entry
;   `(set base.field v)`         Mut
;   a `handle` naming a resolved custom effect   Alloc
;
; What this deliberately does NOT model is effect POLYMORPHISM:
; stage0 marks a parameter effect-transparent when it is called, and
; instantiates the mark with each call site's argument. Omitting it
; makes stage1's sets a SUBSET of stage0's, so every diagnostic that
; reads them under-reports rather than inventing one - the direction
; this file is required to fail in. A call through a local or a
; parameter therefore contributes nothing here.
;
; `bound` is the list of names lexically bound at the walk's start
; plus everything bound inside it. Without it a local shadowing an
; effectful top-level name re-resolves globally and attributes
; effects the body cannot perform - the same shadowing bug stage0
; records having hit in its own mid-recursion check.
(pub :: collectEffects (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings))
(pub fn (collectEffects tc e bound)
  {
    (let acc vecNew)
    {
      (walkEffects tc e bound acc)
      acc
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE REGISTER-PAIR MIRROR.
;
; `core/codegen.ax` emits a function whose declared result is an
; `(Option T)` or `(Result T E)` of the right shape as `@F$pair`,
; answering `{ tag, payload }` in two registers, and boxes that pair
; only where a caller needs a block: a `let` that outlives the match,
; an argument, a field, a statement. A `match` on the direct call
; reads the registers and boxes nothing; a tail leaf of another pair
; function forwards them. So the block a `(Some v)` used to cost is
; built in the CALLER, at some sites and not others - and the effect
; row, which is what `restrict(no-alloc)` reads, has to say so, or the
; claim is checked against a block that is not there.
;
; Everything below is a MIRROR of a predicate in codegen.ax, named
; beside it, and the rule for the pair is that this side may refuse
; what the emitter accepts and never the reverse: a function this side
; calls boxed that the emitter unboxes is over-charged `Alloc`, which
; can refuse a true `no-alloc` claim; one this side calls unboxed that
; the emitter boxes is a claim checked against nothing, which is the
; defect `tests/diagnostics/384-restrict-no-alloc-ctor.ax` exists to
; keep out. `scripts/check-unboxed-sums.sh` section 5 holds the two
; sides to one answer over every function the compiler compiles into
; itself: a row without `Alloc` and a definition with an
; `axiom_alloc` in it is a failure there.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Resolve a leaf before comparing canonical constructor identities:
; effect collection runs before body checking stamps each use.
(pub :: tcPairCtorNamed (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (tcPairCtorNamed tc n)
  {
    (let ctorFound (rfindCtor tc n))
    (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
      {
        (let name (ctorResolvedName ctor))
        (if (|| (strEq name "None")
              (|| (strEq name "Some") (|| (strEq name "Err$Ok") (strEq name "Err$Err"))))
          1
          0)
      }
      0)
  })

; `scalarTyName`, the emitter's list of machine-word type names.
; `Foreign` is here for the ownership reason codegen.ax states: a
; pointer the block does not own is a word to the reference map.
(pub :: tcScalarTyName (-> String Int))
(pub fn (tcScalarTyName n)
  (if (|| (> (integerTypeCode n) 0)
        (|| (strEq n "Bool")
          (|| (strEq n "Char") (|| (strEq n "Float") (|| (strEq n "Foreign") (strEq n "Link"))))))
    1
    0))

; `fldClass`: 0 a machine word, 2 a reference, 1 unclassifiable. The
; SPELLING is classified, not the type it resolves to - an alias is 1
; here as it is there, because the emitter never expands one.
(pub :: tcFldClass (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcFldClass tc tn)
  (if (nodeSame tn nilNode)
    1
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag tn))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
        2
        (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
          (if (== (vecLen (nodeAChildren tn)) 0)
            0
            2)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
            {
              (let n (nodeAName tn))
              (if (== (tcScalarTyName n) 1)
                0
                (if (|| (strEq n "String")
                      (|| (strEq n "Option") (|| (strEq n "Handle") (strEq n "Vec"))))
                  2
                  (if (! (isNone (findDataAnyVis tc n)))
                    2
                    ; A word struct is codegen's scalar too (`fldClass`).
                    (if (== (tcWordTyName tc n) 1)
                      0
                      (if (! (isNone (findStructAnyVis tc n)))
                        2
                        1)))))
            }
            1)))
    }))

; `arrowResultTy`.
(pub :: tcArrowResultTy (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (tcArrowResultTy t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
      (tcArrowResultTy (nodeBChild t))
      t)))

; `pairRetOK`: `(Option T)` or `(Result T E)` whose arguments are
; each a word or a reference.
(pub :: tcPairRetOK (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcPairRetOK tc rt)
  (if (nodeSame rt nilNode)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag rt) TAG_T_CON)
      0
      {
        (let tn (nodeAName rt))
        (let args (nodeBChildren rt))
        (if (== (as args Int) 0)
          0
          (if (strEq tn "Option")
            (if (== (vecLen args) 1)
              (tcPairArgClassOK tc (vecGet args 0))
              0)
            (if (strEq tn "Err$Result")
              (if (== (vecLen args) 2)
                (if (== (tcPairArgClassOK tc (vecGet args 0)) 1)
                  (tcPairArgClassOK tc (vecGet args 1))
                  0)
                0)
              0)))
      })))

(pub :: tcPairArgClassOK (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcPairArgClassOK tc t)
  {
    (let c (tcFldClass tc t))
    (if (== c 0)
      1
      (if (== c 2)
        1
        0))
  })

; `cgTakesEvidence`: a source type variable in any PARAMETER position
; of the arrow means the function takes a hidden evidence word, which
; the emitter refuses to pair.
(pub :: tcTyHasSrcVar (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcTyHasSrcVar t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (tyIsSrcVar t)
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (tcTyVecHasSrcVar (nodeBChildren t) 0)
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            (if (== (tcTyHasSrcVar (nodeAChild t)) 1)
              1
              (tcTyHasSrcVar (nodeBChild t)))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (tcTyVecHasSrcVar (nodeAChildren t) 0)
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (tcTyHasSrcVar (nodeAChild t))
                0)))))
    }))

(pub :: tcTyVecHasSrcVar (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcTyVecHasSrcVar xs i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      0
      (if (== (tcTyHasSrcVar (vecGet xs i)) 1)
        1
        (tcTyVecHasSrcVar xs (+ i 1))))))

(pub :: tcParamPosVars (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcParamPosVars t)
  (if (&& (! (nodeSame t nilNode)) (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR))
    (if (== (tcTyHasSrcVar (nodeAChild t)) 1)
      1
      (tcParamPosVars (nodeBChild t)))
    0))

; `pairEntryOfArity`, asked of the checker's tables: is `name` a
; constructor of exactly `want` fields, of a data type that unboxes
; its nullary arms (mixed, rep 2) or has none (all fieldful, rep 0)?
; A type whose every arm is nullary is already immediate, and the
; emitter answers 0 for it.
(pub :: tcCtorOfArity (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (tcCtorOfArity tc name want)
  {
    (let cFound (rfindCtor tc name))
    (if (let (Some c) cFound)
      (if (!= (arrowArity c.ctorTy) want)
        0
        {
          (let res c.resTy)
          (if (nodeSame res nilNode)
            0
            (if (!= (nodeTag res) TAG_T_CON)
              0
              {
                (let dFound (findData tc (nodeAName res)))
                (if (let (Some d) dFound)
                  (if (== (tcDataAllNullary d.ctors 0) 1)
                    0
                    1)
                  0)
              }))
        })
      0)
  })

(pub :: tcDataAllNullary (-> (Vec CtorEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcDataAllNullary ctors i)
  (if (== (as ctors Int) 0)
    1
    (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
      1
      (if (> (arrowArity (ctorEntTy (vecGet ctors i))) 0)
        0
        (tcDataAllNullary ctors (+ i 1))))))

; `pairLeafEntry`: a bare `None`, or `(Some e)`, `(Ok e)`, `(Err e)`.
(pub :: tcPairLeafOK (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcPairLeafOK tc e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tg TAG_E_VAR)
        (if (== (tcPairCtorNamed tc (nodeAName e)) 1)
          (tcCtorOfArity tc (nodeAName e) 0)
          0)
        (if (== tg TAG_E_APP)
          (if (!= (nodeTag (nodeAChild e)) TAG_E_VAR)
            0
            (if (== (tcPairCtorNamed tc (nodeAName (nodeAChild e))) 1)
              (tcCtorOfArity tc (nodeAName (nodeAChild e)) 1)
              0))
          0))
    }))

; Is `full` - a declaration's own spelling, `Mod$name` for an import -
; in the pair set?
(pub :: tcIsPairName (-> Names String Int))
(pub fn (tcIsPairName pn full)
  (if (== (as pn Int) 0)
    0
    (strVecHas pn full)))

(pub :: tcIsPairEnt (-> TC FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (tcIsPairEnt tc ent)
  (tcIsPairName (tcPairFns tc) ent.name))

; `pairFwdOK`: a saturated direct call to a pair function whose head
; no `let` on the path and no parameter has bound.
(pub :: tcPairFwdOK (-> TC ASTNode Names Names Int))
(pub fn (tcPairFwdOK tc e bound pn)
  (if (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
    0
    {
      (let h (spineHead e))
      (if (!= (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
        0
        {
          (let n (nodeAName h))
          (if (isSome (strVecIdx bound n))
            0
            {
              (let entFound (findFnEnt tc n))
              (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                (if (== (tcIsPairName pn (fnEntName ent)) 0)
                  0
                  (if (== (spineArgs e 0) (arrowArity (fnEntTy ent)))
                    1
                    0))
                0)
            })
        })
    }))

; `pairTailsOK`, collecting: every tail leaf of `e` is a constructor
; leaf or a forwarding call, and each is pushed onto `acc`.
(pub :: tcPairTails (-> TC ASTNode Names Names (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (tcPairTails tc e bound pn acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tg TAG_E_BEGIN)
        {
          (let xs (nodeAChildren e))
          (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
            0
            (if (== (vecLen xs) 0)
              0
              (tcPairTails tc (vecGet xs (- (vecLen xs) 1)) bound pn acc)))
        }
        (if (== tg TAG_E_IF)
          (if (== (tcPairTails tc (nodeBChild e) bound pn acc) 1)
            (tcPairTails tc (nodeCChild e) bound pn acc)
            0)
          (if (|| (== tg TAG_E_LET) (== tg TAG_E_LETM))
            {
              (let _p (vecPush bound (nodeAName e)))
              {
                (let r (tcPairTails tc (nodeCChild e) bound pn acc))
                {
                  (vecPop bound)
                  r
                }
              }
            }
            (if (== (tcPairLeafOK tc e) 1)
              {
                (vecPush acc e)
                1
              }
              (if (== (tcPairFwdOK tc e bound pn) 1)
                {
                  (vecPush acc e)
                  1
                }
                0)))))
    }))

; `tailCallsSelf`, as the emitter asks it: does a tail of `e` call
; `name` with exactly `arity` arguments?
(pub :: tcTailCallsSelf (-> ASTNode String Int Int))
(pub fn (tcTailCallsSelf e name arity)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (let h (spineHead e))
          (if (&& (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
                (&& (strEq (nodeAName h) name) (== (spineArgs e 0) arity)))
            1
            0)
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
          {
            (let v (nodeAChildren e))
            (if (== (as v Int) 0)
              0
              (if (== (vecLen v) 0)
                0
                (tcTailCallsSelf (vecGet v (- (vecLen v) 1)) name arity)))
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
            (if (== (tcTailCallsSelf (nodeBChild e) name arity) 1)
              1
              (tcTailCallsSelf (nodeCChild e) name arity))
            (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
              (tcArmsCallSelf (nodeBChildren e) name arity 0)
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                (if (strEq (nodeAName e) name)
                  0
                  (tcTailCallsSelf (nodeCChild e) name arity))
                0)))))
    }))

(pub :: tcArmsCallSelf (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (tcArmsCallSelf arms name arity i)
  (if (== (as arms Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
      0
      (if (== (tcTailCallsSelf (nodeBChild (vecGet arms i)) name arity) 1)
        1
        (tcArmsCallSelf arms name arity (+ i 1))))))

; A fresh vector of the declaration's parameter names, for the walk
; to push binders onto.
(pub :: tcParamNames (-> ASTNode Names))
(pub fn (tcParamNames d)
  (tcParamNamesFrom (nodeBNames d) 0 vecNew))

(pub :: tcParamNamesFrom (-> Names Int Names Names))
(pub fn (tcParamNamesFrom ps i acc)
  (if (== (as ps Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
      acc
      (tcParamNamesFrom ps (+ i 1) (vecPush acc (vecGet ps i))))))

; `pairFnOK`, with the tail leaves it accepted left in `acc`. Field 16
; must name the declaration's module when this is asked, as
; `inferEffectsOne` arranges, because the callee lookups are
; visibility-scoped.
(pub :: tcPairFnOK (-> TC ASTNode Names (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (tcPairFnOK tc d pn acc)
  (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
    0
    {
      (let name (nodeAName d))
      (if (strEq name "main")
        0
        {
          (let entFound (findFnEnt tc name))
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            {
              (let ty (fnEntTy ent))
              (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
                0
                (if (== (tcParamPosVars ty) 1)
                  0
                  (if (== (tcPairRetOK tc (tcArrowResultTy ty)) 0)
                    0
                    (if (== (tcPairTails tc (nodeCChild d) (tcParamNames d) pn acc) 0)
                      0
                      (if (==
                            (tcTailCallsSelf (nodeCChild d) (bareOf name) (vecLen (nodeBNames d)))
                            1)
                        0
                        1)))))
            }
            0)
        })
    }))

; The pair set, to a fixpoint, exactly as `buildNameIndexes` builds
; the emitter's: a forwarding leaf makes one function's answer depend
; on another's, so the scan repeats until a pass adds nothing.
(pub :: tcPairFnsBuild (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Names))
(pub fn (tcPairFnsBuild tc decls)
  {
    (let pn vecNew)
    (let mut grew 1)
    {
      (while (== grew 1)
        {
          (set grew 0)
          {
            (let mut m 0)
            (while (< m (vecLen decls))
              {
                {
                  (let d (vecGet decls m))
                  (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
                    (if (== (tcIsPairName pn (nodeAName d)) 0)
                      {
                        (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
                        (if (== (tcPairFnOK tc d pn vecNew) 1)
                          {
                            (vecPush pn (nodeAName d))
                            (set grew 1)
                          })
                        (tcSetCurModule tc None)
                      }))
                }
                (set m (+ m 1))
              })
          }
        })
      pn
    }
  })

; The per-declaration site record: the nodes the emitter reads from
; registers - the tail leaves of a pair function, and every match
; scrutinee `tcPairMatchOK` admits, added as the walk meets it. Built
; at every `collectEffects` over a declaration body, so the
; restriction check's own re-walk sees the same sites the fixpoint
; did.
(pub :: tcPairSitesOf (-> TC ASTNode (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (tcPairSitesOf tc d)
  {
    (let sites vecNew)
    {
      (if (== (tcIsPairName (tcPairFns tc) (nodeAName d)) 1)
        (tcPairTails tc (nodeCChild d) (tcParamNames d) (tcPairFns tc) sites))
      sites
    }
  })

; Is node `e` recorded in the current declaration's site list?
(pub :: tcNodeIn (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcNodeIn v e)
  (tcNodeInFrom v e 0))

(pub :: tcNodeInFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (tcNodeInFrom v e i)
  (if (== (as v Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      0
      (if (nodeSame (vecGet v i) e)
        1
        (tcNodeInFrom v e (+ i 1))))))

(pub :: tcSiteIn (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcSiteIn tc e)
  (if (let (Some sites) (tcPairSites tc))
    (tcNodeIn sites e)
    0))

(pub :: tcSiteAdd (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcSiteAdd tc e)
  (if (let (Some sites) (tcPairSites tc))
    {
      (vecPush sites e)
      0
    }
    0))

; Word 37, the float-operator record: `tcFloatOpAdd` from
; `checkNumeric` as the operands are typed, `tcFloatOpIn` from
; `restrictEmitWraps` when it is deciding whether a `+` it found by
; spelling is one that can wrap. The KEY is the application's head
; node, which `checkApp` binds once per operator application
; (`spineHead e`) and `restrictEmitWraps` reads the same way, so the
; two agree on identity without either holding a type.
(pub :: tcFloatOpAdd (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcFloatOpAdd tc h)
  {
    (vecPush (tcFloatOps tc) h)
    0
  })

(pub :: tcFloatOpIn (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcFloatOpIn tc h)
  (tcNodeIn (tcFloatOps tc) h))

; `pairMatchOK`: the scrutinee is a saturated direct call to a pair
; function that no local shadows, and every arm is a shape the
; register test answers. Every such match is read from registers by
; one emitter or the other (`emitMatch`, or `emitMatchTail` with
; `pairTailDelegates` routing the case it cannot release to
; `emitMatch`), so there is no tail-position case here.
(pub :: tcPairMatchOK (-> TC ASTNode BScope Int))
(pub fn (tcPairMatchOK tc e bound)
  {
    (let s (nodeAChild e))
    (if (!= (nodeTag s) TAG_E_APP)
      0
      {
        (let h (spineHead s))
        (if (!= (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
          0
          (if (== (bsIsLocal bound (nodeAName h)) 1)
            0
            {
              (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName h)))
              (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                (if (== (tcIsPairEnt tc ent) 0)
                  0
                  (if (!= (spineArgs s 0) (arrowArity (fnEntTy ent)))
                    0
                    (tcPairArmsOK tc (nodeBChildren e) 0)))
                0)
            }))
      })
  })

; `pairArmsOK`.
(pub :: tcPairArmsOK (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcPairArmsOK tc arms i)
  (if (== (as arms Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
      1
      {
        (let pat (nodeAChild (vecGet arms i)))
        {
          (let tg (nodeTag pat))
          (if (== tg TAG_P_CON0)
            (if (&& (== (tcPairCtorNamed tc (nodeAName pat)) 1)
                  (== (tcCtorOfArity tc (nodeAName pat) 0) 1))
              (tcPairArmsOK tc arms (+ i 1))
              0)
            (if (== tg TAG_E_APP)
              (if (!= (nodeTag (nodeAChild pat)) TAG_E_VAR)
                0
                (if (== (tcPairCtorNamed tc (nodeAName (nodeAChild pat))) 0)
                  0
                  (if (== (tcCtorOfArity tc (nodeAName (nodeAChild pat)) 1) 0)
                    0
                    (if (== (nodeTag (nodeBChild pat)) TAG_E_VAR)
                      (tcPairArmsOK tc arms (+ i 1))
                      0))))
              0))
        }
      })))

; A one-effect row, for the possible spelling.
(pub :: tcAllocRow Strings)
(pub fn (tcAllocRow)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (vecPushStr v (builtinEff "Alloc"))
      v
    }
  })

; Which parameters can hold a callable value, from the declared type -
; stage0's `param_callables`. An arrow, a type variable or poison can;
; a concrete `Int` cannot, and marking one would claim a transparency
; the signature forbids.
(pub :: paramCallablesOf (-> (Option ASTNode) Int Ints))
(pub fn (paramCallablesOf ty count)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (paramCallablesInto v ty count 0)
      v
    }
  })

(pub :: paramCallablesInto (-> Ints (Option ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (paramCallablesInto v ty count i)
  (if (>= i count)
    0
    (match ty
      ((None)
        {
          (vecPush v 1)
          (paramCallablesInto v None count (+ i 1))
        }
      )
      ((Some signature)
        (if (&& (! (nodeSame signature nilNode)) (== (nodeTag signature) TAG_T_ARR))
          {
            (vecPush v (tyIsCallable (nodeAChild signature)))
            (paramCallablesInto v (bsTypeOpt (nodeBChild signature)) count (+ i 1))
          }
          {
            (vecPush v 1)
            (paramCallablesInto v None count (+ i 1))
          })
      ))))

(pub :: tyIsCallable (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsCallable t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    1
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
        1
        (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
          1
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ERR)
            1
            0)))
    }))

; The scope a function body is walked in.
;
; The signature travels with the scope, not just the per-parameter
; callability derived from it. `paramCallablesOf` answers "can THIS
; function's parameter `p` hold a callable value"; a call through `p`
; then asks the same question one level down - "can `p`'s own argument
; position `i` hold one" - and only the type node can answer that.
; `escapeArgs` is where it is asked.
(pub :: bodyScopeOf (-> TC ASTNode BScope))
(pub fn (bodyScopeOf tc d)
  {
    (let signature
      (:: (match (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d))
        ((None) None)
        ((Some entry) (bsTypeOpt entry.ty))) (Option ASTNode)))
    (bsMake vecNew (nodeBNames d) (paramCallablesOf signature (vecLen (nodeBNames d))) vecNew
      signature (bodyCtxReset tc))
  })

; The shared body context (`UState` word 11), emptied for a new walk.
(pub :: bodyCtxReset (-> TC (Vec BCtx)))
(pub fn (bodyCtxReset tc)
  {
    (let cv (walkCtxVec tc))
    {
      (let c (vecGet cv 0))
      {
        (vecClear c.handled)
        (vecClear c.uses)
        (vecClear c.vals)
        (set c.lambdaCast NoLambdaCast)
        cv
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: walkCtxVec (-> TC (Vec BCtx)))
(pub fn (walkCtxVec tc)
  tc.unsafe.walkCtx)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; FUNCTION VALUES THE WALK CAN FOLLOW.
;
; A custom effect is discharged by the `handle` around the place its
; operation RUNS. So a lambda's custom effects belong where the lambda
; is called, not where it is written: `(let ((g (lambda (x) (ask x))))
; (handle (g 2) (Ask) h))` is a correct program, and a closure built
; inside a `handle` and called after it pops traps 71. Built-in effects
; are never discharged, so they keep counting where the lambda is
; written, which is what every claim already reads.
;
; `FnVals` is what a function-valued expression is known to hold: a
; named function, or a lambda this body wrote (with the row of its body,
; `row`, and the parameter uses waiting in it, `uses`). `complete` is 0
; when the value may also be something the walk lost, and then a call
; through it is a hole like any other. A value the walk does not follow
; at all is `None`.
;
; A value is followed through a `let`, an `if`, a `match`, a block and
; a `handle` - the last is what makes the closure that escapes its
; handler visible. Everywhere else it ESCAPES: its custom effects
; arrive POSSIBLE, which every claim reads as an upper bound and which
; AX3053 does not report.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Callable values carry a checked variant: a resolved function entry
; or a lambda's latent effects and parameter uses.
(pub struct LambdaEffects
  (row : Strings)
  (uses : (Vec EpUse))
  (casts : Bool))

(pub data FnVal
  (NamedFn FnEnt)
  (LambdaFn LambdaEffects))

; Classification is separate from callable values: an unknown or scalar
; value cannot enter a set of callable members.
(pub struct FieldLambdaBinding
  (name : String)
  (initialiser : (Option ASTNode)))

(pub struct FieldLambda
  (owner : ASTNode)
  (expression : ASTNode)
  (bindings : (Vec FieldLambdaBinding))
  (row : Strings)
  (mut active : Bool)
  (mut casts : Bool))

(pub data StoredFnVal
  (KnownFn FnVal)
  (DeferredFn FieldLambda)
  (UnknownFn)
  (NonCallableFn))

(pub struct FnVals
  (complete : Int)
  (members : (Vec FnVal)))

(pub :: fnValsOne (-> FnVal FnVals))
(pub fn (fnValsOne m)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (vecPush v m)
      (FnVals :complete 1 :members v)
    }
  })

(pub :: fnValNamed (-> FnEnt FnVal))
(pub fn (fnValNamed ent)
  (NamedFn ent))

; Either value. A side the walk does not follow leaves the union
; incomplete.
(pub :: fnValsJoin (-> (Option FnVals) (Option FnVals) (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (fnValsJoin a b)
  (if (let (Some av) a)
    (match b
      ((None) (Some (FnVals :complete 0 :members av.members)))
      ((Some bv)
        {
          (let v vecNew)
          {
            (fnValCopyInto v av.members 0)
            (fnValCopyInto v bv.members 0)
            (Some
              (FnVals
                :complete
                  (if (&& (== av.complete 1) (== bv.complete 1))
                    1
                    0)
                :members v))
          }
        }
      ))
    (match b
      ((None) None)
      ((Some bv) (Some (FnVals :complete 0 :members bv.members))))))

(pub :: fnValCopyInto (-> (Vec FnVal) (Vec FnVal) Int Int))
(pub fn (fnValCopyInto dst src i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    0
    {
      (vecPush dst (vecGet src i))
      (fnValCopyInto dst src (+ i 1))
    }))

; `src` into `acc`, each entry as it is.
(pub :: effUnionRow (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effUnionRow acc src i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    0
    {
      (effAdd acc (vecGetStr src i))
      (effUnionRow acc src (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: effIsCustom (-> String Int))
(pub fn (effIsCustom e)
  (if (strEq (strSlice (effDefiniteOf e) 0 2) "c:")
    1
    0))

; A lambda written here: its body walked once, for its own row.
(pub :: walkLambdaLatent (-> TC ASTNode BScope LambdaEffects))
(pub fn (walkLambdaLatent tc lam bound)
  {
    (let lb (bsLambda bound (nodeANames lam)))
    {
      (let row (collectEffects tc (nodeBChild lam) lb))
      (LambdaEffects
        :row row
        :uses (bsUsesVec lb)
        :casts
          (|| (> (vecLen (castScanInto (nodeBChild lam) vecNew)) 0)
            (lambdaCastAny (bsLambdaCast lb))))
    }
  })

; What a lambda's row contributes where it is WRITTEN: everything but
; its custom effects, which wait for the call.
(pub :: latentBuiltinsInto (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (latentBuiltinsInto acc row i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen row))
    0
    {
      (if (! (== (effIsCustom (vecGetStr row i)) 1))
        (effAdd acc (vecGetStr row i)))
      (latentBuiltinsInto acc row (+ i 1))
    }))

; Its custom effects as possible: the value escaped.
(pub :: latentCustomPossibleInto (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (latentCustomPossibleInto acc row i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen row))
    0
    {
      (if (== (effIsCustom (vecGetStr row i)) 1)
        (effAdd acc (effPossibleOf (vecGetStr row i))))
      (latentCustomPossibleInto acc row (+ i 1))
    }))

; The parameter uses a lambda's body made, applied where the lambda
; runs: each call inside it gains `mask`, or every one escapes.
(pub :: latentApply (-> BScope (Vec EpUse) Strings Int Int Int))
(pub fn (latentApply b uses mask esc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen uses))
    0
    {
      (let r (vecGet uses i))
      {
        (if (== esc 1)
          (markEparamUse b r.pidx vecNew 1)
          {
            (if (== r.def 1)
              (markEparamUse b r.pidx (effJoined mask r.mask) 0))
            (if (== r.esc 1)
              (markEparamUse b r.pidx vecNew 1))
          })
        (latentApply b uses mask esc (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; ... handed to a callee position whose record is `u`.
(pub :: latentApplyU (-> BScope (Vec EpUse) (Option EpUse) Int))
(pub fn (latentApplyU b uses o)
  (if (let (Some u) o)
    {
      (if (== u.def 1)
        (latentApply b uses (effJoined (bsHandledVec b) u.mask) 0 0))
      (if (== u.esc 1)
        (latentApply b uses vecNew 1 0)
        0)
    }
    (latentApply b uses vecNew 1 0)))

; A row a value contributes by being handed to a callee position whose
; record is `u`. Built-in effects arrive as they are. A custom effect
; is definite when some call the callee makes is outside every handler
; for it, possible when the callee also lets the value escape, and
; absent when every call discharges it.
(pub :: addGated (-> Strings Strings (Option EpUse) Int Int))
(pub fn (addGated acc row o i)
  (if (let (Some u) o)
    (addGatedWith acc row u.def u.mask u.esc i)
    (addGatedWith acc row 0 row 1 i)))

; `def`, `mask` and `esc` are the position's record; a position nothing
; calls has `def` 0 and `esc` 1, and its mask is never read.
(pub :: addGatedWith (-> Strings Strings Int Strings Int Int Int))
(pub fn (addGatedWith acc row def mask esc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen row))
    0
    {
      (let e (vecGetStr row i))
      {
        (if (== (effIsCustom e) 0)
          (effAdd acc e)
          (if (&& (== def 1) (== (effIn mask (effDefiniteOf e)) 0))
            (effAdd acc e)
            (if (== esc 1)
              (effAdd acc (effPossibleOf e)))))
        (addGatedWith acc row def mask esc (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; One member, called with `args`.
(pub :: lambdaCastAny (-> LambdaCast Bool))
(pub fn (lambdaCastAny fact)
  (match fact
    ((NoLambdaCast) false)
    ((MaybeLambdaCast) true)
    ((DefiniteLambdaCast) true)))

(pub :: lambdaCastDefinite (-> LambdaCast Bool))
(pub fn (lambdaCastDefinite fact)
  (match fact
    ((NoLambdaCast) false)
    ((MaybeLambdaCast) false)
    ((DefiniteLambdaCast) true)))

(pub :: lambdaCastJoin (-> LambdaCast LambdaCast LambdaCast))
(pub fn (lambdaCastJoin a b)
  (if (|| (lambdaCastDefinite a) (lambdaCastDefinite b))
    DefiniteLambdaCast
    (if (|| (lambdaCastAny a) (lambdaCastAny b))
      MaybeLambdaCast
      NoLambdaCast)))

(pub :: bsLambdaCast (-> BScope LambdaCast))
(pub fn (bsLambdaCast bound)
  {
    (let context (bsCtx bound))
    context.lambdaCast
  })

(pub :: noteLambdaCast (-> BScope LambdaCast Bool Int))
(pub fn (noteLambdaCast bound fact certain)
  {
    (let context (bsCtx bound))
    (set context.lambdaCast (lambdaCastJoin context.lambdaCast
                              (if certain
                                fact
                                (if (lambdaCastAny fact)
                                  MaybeLambdaCast
                                  NoLambdaCast))))
    0
  })

(pub :: fnLambdaCast (-> FnEnt LambdaCast))
(pub fn (fnLambdaCast raw)
  raw.lambdaCast)

(pub :: mergeFnLambdaCast (-> FnEnt LambdaCast Int))
(pub fn (mergeFnLambdaCast raw fact)
  {
    (let entry raw)
    (let before entry.lambdaCast)
    (let after (lambdaCastJoin before fact))
    (if (|| (&& (! (lambdaCastAny before)) (lambdaCastAny after))
          (&& (! (lambdaCastDefinite before)) (lambdaCastDefinite after)))
      {
        (set entry.lambdaCast after)
        1
      }
      0)
  })

(pub :: fnValCall (-> TC FnVal Nodes BScope Strings Int Bool Int))
(pub fn (fnValCall tc value args bound acc unboxed certain)
  (match value
    ((NamedFn ent) (walkCallEnt tc ent.name ent args bound acc unboxed certain))
    ((LambdaFn latent)
      {
        (noteLambdaCast bound
          (if latent.casts
            DefiniteLambdaCast
            NoLambdaCast)
          certain)
        (effUnionRow acc latent.row 0)
        (latentApply bound latent.uses (bsHandledVec bound) 0 0)
      }
    )))

; A call through a value the walk follows. One member, known to be the
; only one, is the plain call. Otherwise each member's row is computed
; on its own: an effect every member performs definitely is definite,
; any other is possible, and a value the walk lost leaves the hole.
(pub :: fnValsCall (-> TC FnVals Nodes BScope Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (fnValsCall tc fv args bound acc unboxed)
  {
    (let ms fv.members)
    (let complete fv.complete)
    (if (&& (== complete 1) (== (vecLen ms) 1))
      (fnValCall tc (vecGet ms 0) args bound acc unboxed true)
      {
        (let rows vecNew)
        (let certain (tcCurCalls tc))
        {
          ; Each member's edge is one the body MAY take, recorded where
          ; the fixpoint follows it and the call-graph checks do not.
          (tcSetCurCalls tc
            (if (let (Some _) certain)
              (tcMayCalls tc)
              None))
          (fnValsRowsInto tc ms args bound rows unboxed 0)
          (tcSetCurCalls tc certain)
          (fnValsCombine acc rows complete 0)
          (if (== complete 1)
            0
            (effAdd acc effIncomplete))
        }
      })
  })

(pub :: fnValsRowsInto (-> TC (Vec FnVal) Nodes BScope StringRows Int Int Int))
(pub fn (fnValsRowsInto tc ms args bound rows unboxed i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ms))
    0
    {
      (let r vecNew)
      {
        (fnValCall tc (vecGet ms i) args bound r unboxed false)
        (vecPush rows r)
        (fnValsRowsInto tc ms args bound rows unboxed (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: fnValsCombine (-> Strings StringRows Int Int Int))
(pub fn (fnValsCombine acc rows complete i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rows))
    0
    {
      (fnValsCombineRow acc (vecGet rows i) rows complete 0)
      (fnValsCombine acc rows complete (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: fnValsCombineRow (-> Strings Strings StringRows Int Int Int))
(pub fn (fnValsCombineRow acc row rows complete i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen row))
    0
    {
      (let e (vecGetStr row i))
      {
        (if (|| (== (effIsMarker e) 1) (== (effIsPossible e) 1))
          (effAdd acc e)
          (if (&& (== complete 1) (== (effInAllRows rows e 0) 1))
            (effAdd acc e)
            (effAdd acc (effPossibleOf e))))
        (fnValsCombineRow acc row rows complete (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: effInAllRows (-> StringRows String Int Int))
(pub fn (effInAllRows rows e i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rows))
    1
    (if (== (effIn (vecGet rows i) e) 1)
      (effInAllRows rows e (+ i 1))
      0)))

; The value escaped: nothing here calls it, and something somewhere
; may. Each member's row arrives possible; a lambda written here
; already counted its built-in effects where it was written.
(pub :: fnValsEscape (-> TC FnVals BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (fnValsEscape tc fv bound acc)
  (fnValsEscapeFrom tc fv.members bound acc 0))

(pub :: fnValsEscapeFrom (-> TC (Vec FnVal) BScope Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (fnValsEscapeFrom tc ms bound acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ms))
    0
    {
      (match (vecGet ms i)
        ((NamedFn ent)
          {
            (noteLambdaCast bound ent.lambdaCast false)
            (tcNoteCall tc ent.name)
            (effUnionCallee acc ent 1)
            (if (== (isUnsafePrim ent.name) 1)
              (effAdd acc (effPossibleOf (builtinEff "Unsafe"))))
          }
        )
        ((LambdaFn latent)
          {
            (noteLambdaCast bound
              (if latent.casts
                DefiniteLambdaCast
                NoLambdaCast)
              false)
            (latentCustomPossibleInto acc latent.row 0)
            (latentApply bound latent.uses vecNew 1 0)
          }
        ))
      (fnValsEscapeFrom tc ms bound acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; The value handed to a callee position whose record is `u` (`None`:
; one the callee never calls). A single known member is
; gated by `u`; anything less certain escapes.
(pub :: fnValsHanded (-> TC FnVals BScope Strings (Option EpUse) Int))
(pub fn (fnValsHanded tc fv bound acc u)
  {
    (let ms fv.members)
    (if (|| (== (epUseAbsent u) 1) (|| (!= fv.complete 1) (!= (vecLen ms) 1)))
      (fnValsEscape tc fv bound acc)
      {
        (let row vecNew)
        (match (vecGet ms 0)
          ((NamedFn ent)
            {
              (noteLambdaCast bound ent.lambdaCast (epUseCalled u))
              (tcNoteCall tc ent.name)
              (effUnionCallee row ent 0)
              (if (== (isUnsafePrim ent.name) 1)
                (effAdd row (builtinEff "Unsafe")))
            }
          )
          ((LambdaFn latent)
            {
              (noteLambdaCast bound
                (if latent.casts
                  DefiniteLambdaCast
                  NoLambdaCast)
                (epUseCalled u))
              (effUnion row latent.row 0)
              (latentApplyU bound latent.uses u)
            }
          ))
        (addGated acc row u 0)
      })
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; What an expression's VALUE is, when it is a function the walk can
; follow. Walks `e` for its own effects into `acc` exactly as
; `walkEffects` does, and answers its `FnVals`, or `None`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: walkEffectsVal (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (walkEffectsVal tc e bound acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    None
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
        {
          (let m (walkLambdaLatent tc e bound))
          {
            ; emitLambda allocates the closure record at its creation site.
            (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc"))
            (latentBuiltinsInto acc m.row 0)
            (Some (fnValsOne (LambdaFn m)))
          }
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          (walkEffectsValVar tc e bound acc)
          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
            {
              (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
              (if (let (Some row) (fieldDeclRowAt tc e bound))
                (fieldDeclaredVals row)
                (fieldValsOf tc (nodeBName e)))
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
              {
                (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                (fnValsJoin (walkEffectsVal tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                  (walkEffectsVal tc (nodeCChild e) bound acc))
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                (walkEffectsValBegin tc (nodeAChildren e) 0 bound acc)
                (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                  (walkEffectsLet tc e bound acc 1)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                    (walkEffectsHandleVal tc e bound acc)
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                      {
                        (if (== (tcPairMatchOK tc e bound) 1)
                          (tcSiteAdd tc (nodeAChild e)))
                        (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                        (walkEffectsValArms tc (nodeBChildren e) 0 bound acc None 1)
                      }
                      {
                        (walkEffects tc e bound acc)
                        None
                      }))))))))
    }))

; A name: a local known to hold functions, or a top-level function
; named as a value. A nullary function is invoked by its name, so its
; name is a call and not a value.
(pub :: walkEffectsValVar (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (walkEffectsValVar tc e bound acc)
  {
    (let n (nodeAName e))
    (if (== (bsIsLocal bound n) 1)
      (bsValsOf bound n)
      {
        (let entFound (findFnEnt tc n))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (if (&& (== (isSyscallPrim n) 0)
                (&& (== (tyIsArrow (fnEntTy ent)) 1) (fnEntMayTakeArgs ent)))
            {
              (unsafeSinkNote tc e bound)
              (Some (fnValsOne (fnValNamed ent)))
            }
            {
              (walkEffects tc e bound acc)
              None
            })
          {
            (walkEffects tc e bound acc)
            None
          })
      })
  })

(pub :: walkEffectsValBegin (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int BScope Strings (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (walkEffectsValBegin tc v i bound acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    (if (== i (- (vecLen v) 1))
      (walkEffectsVal tc (vecGet v i) bound acc)
      {
        (walkEffects tc (vecGet v i) bound acc)
        (walkEffectsValBegin tc v (+ i 1) bound acc)
      })))

; The arms of a `match`: their values joined. `first` is 1 until one
; arm has answered, so a match whose every arm is followed is complete.
(pub :: walkEffectsValArms
  (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int BScope Strings (Option FnVals) Int (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (walkEffectsValArms tc arms i bound acc sofar first)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    sofar
    {
      (let v
        (walkEffectsVal tc (nodeBChild (vecGet arms i))
          (bsWithPattern bound (nodeAChild (vecGet arms i))) acc))
      (walkEffectsValArms tc arms (+ i 1) bound acc
        (if (== first 1)
          v
          (fnValsJoin sofar v))
        0)
    }))

; A `let`, in either position. `valMode` 1 answers the body's value.
; A name bound to a followed value carries it (`bsWithVals`); a `mut`
; one may be reassigned, so its first value escapes where it is bound.
(pub :: walkEffectsLet (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings Int (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (walkEffectsLet tc e bound acc valMode)
  {
    (let fv (walkEffectsVal tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc))
    {
      (let b2 (letScopeFor tc e bound acc fv))
      (if (== valMode 1)
        (walkEffectsVal tc (nodeCChild e) b2 acc)
        {
          (walkEffects tc (nodeCChild e) b2 acc)
          None
        })
    }
  })

; A `let` whose initialiser is a bare reference to a parameter is an
; ALIAS of it: calling the local is calling the parameter under another
; name, which is stage0's `param_alias_of` through `lookup_shadow`.
(pub :: letScopeFor (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings (Option FnVals) BScope))
(pub fn (letScopeFor tc e bound acc fv)
  (if (let (Some v) fv)
    (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LET)
      (bsWithVals bound (nodeAName e) v)
      {
        (fnValsEscape tc v bound acc)
        (bsWith bound (nodeAName e) bsOpaque)
      })
    (bsWith bound (nodeAName e)
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LET) (== (nodeTag (nodeBChild e)) TAG_E_VAR))
        (bsParamAlias bound (nodeAName (nodeBChild e)))
        bsOpaque))))

; A `handle`: its body walked with the handled effects in scope, so a
; parameter called inside records them; the body's own effects less the
; discharged ones; `Alloc` for installing evidence; and the handler,
; which the handle CALLS, outside the discharge. The body's VALUE is the
; form's value and passes out undischarged: a closure built in here and
; called after the handler pops performs its operations with no handler.
(pub :: walkEffectsHandleVal (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (walkEffectsHandleVal tc e bound acc)
  {
    (let inner vecNew)
    (let hb (bsWithHandled tc bound (nodeBChildren e)))
    {
      (let fv (walkEffectsVal tc (nodeAChild e) hb inner))
      {
        (effUnionUnhandled tc acc inner (nodeBChildren e) 0)
        (if (== (handleIsDynamic tc (nodeBChildren e) 0) 1)
          {
            (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc"))
            (walkHandler tc (nodeCChild e) bound acc)
          })
        fv
      }
    }
  })

; Installing a dynamic handler calls the supplied function whenever its
; operation runs. Follow its value as a call, including local aliases and
; effect-transparent parameters, in the installation's outer scope.
(pub :: walkHandler (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings ()))
(pub fn (walkHandler tc h bound acc)
  {
    (if (! (nodeSame h nilNode))
      (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_LAM)
        (walkCalledLambda tc h bound acc)
        (if (let (Some fv) (walkEffectsVal tc h bound acc))
          {
            (fnValsCall tc fv vecNew bound acc 0)
            (handlerOpaqueArgs acc fv.members)
          }
          (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
            (walkCallHead tc (nodeAName h) vecNew bound acc 0)
            (effAdd acc effIncomplete)))))
    ()
  })

; An operation can supply a callback to its handler. Its identity is
; unknown here, so a named handler that calls such an argument keeps an
; open row. Lambda parameters already carry bsOpaque in their own walk.
(pub :: handlerOpaqueArgs (-> Strings (Vec FnVal) ()))
(pub fn (handlerOpaqueArgs acc members)
  {
    (for i in 0..(vecLen members)
      (match (vecGet members i)
        ((NamedFn ent)
          (if (> (vecLen ent.eparams) 0)
            (effAdd acc effIncomplete))
        )
        ((LambdaFn _) 0)))
    ()
  })

; A lambda applied where it stands - a call head, or a handler - is
; walked in place: its parameters are bound opaque and everything its
; body performs is this point's. Anything else is walked as usual.
(pub :: walkCalledLambda (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (walkCalledLambda tc h bound acc)
  (if (nodeSame h nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_LAM)
      {
        (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc"))
        (walkEffects tc (nodeBChild h) (bsWithParams bound (nodeANames h) bsOpaque 0) acc)
      }
      (walkEffects tc h bound acc))))

; The arguments of a call to `ent`. Each value that is a function -
; a name, a lambda, a local holding one - contributes what calling it
; performs, gated by where `ent` calls that parameter (`addGated`):
; built-in effects as they are, a custom effect only where some call is
; outside its handler. Any other argument is walked for its own effects.
;
; A callee the fixpoint has not walked yet (`fnPending`) has no record,
; and reading that as an escape is an answer the monotone fixpoint can
; never take back: a lambda performing `Ask`, handed to a callee
; declared below that installs the `Ask` handler around it, kept `Ask`
; in the caller's row when the declarations were written in that order
; and not in the other. So a function value handed to a pending callee
; contributes nothing yet. The callee's first walk counts as a change
; (`inferEffectsOne`), so this call is walked again once the record
; exists, and the answer no longer depends on declaration order.
(pub :: walkEffectsArgVecAt (-> TC Nodes Int BScope Strings FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (walkEffectsArgVecAt tc v i bound acc ent)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet v i))
      (let u (epUseFor ent i))
      (let pend (fnPending ent))
      {
        (if (== pend 0)
          (checkRestrictedArgument tc ent i a bound))
        (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
          {
            (unsafeSinkNote tc a bound)
            (if (! (== pend 1))
              (if (== (bsIsLocal bound (nodeAName a)) 1)
                (if (let (Some fv) (bsValsOf bound (nodeAName a)))
                  (fnValsHanded tc fv bound acc u)
                  ())
                {
                  (let feFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName a)))
                  (if (let (Some fe) feFound)
                    (if (&& (== (tyIsArrow fe.ty) 1)
                          (&& (fnEntMayTakeArgs fe) (== (isSyscallPrim (nodeAName a)) 0)))
                      {
                        (let r vecNew)
                        {
                          (effRefSiteAt tc (nodeAName a) bound r 0)
                          (addGated acc r u 0)
                        }
                      }
                      (effRefSiteAt tc (nodeAName a) bound acc 0))
                    (effRefSiteAt tc (nodeAName a) bound acc 0))
                }))
          }
          (if (== (nodeTag a) TAG_E_LAM)
            {
              ; Creating an argument allocates even if the callee never calls it.
              (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc"))
              (if (! (== pend 1))
                {
                  (let m (walkLambdaLatent tc a bound))
                  {
                    (addGated acc m.row u 0)
                    (latentApplyU bound m.uses u)
                  }
                })
            }
            (walkEffects tc a bound acc)))
        (walkEffectsArgVecAt tc v (+ i 1) bound acc ent)
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; CAPABILITY RECORDS: what a function-typed field can hold.
;
; A call through a field, `((l.emit) s)`, is the language's dispatch.
; Before any body is walked, every value the program stores into a
; field whose declared type can hold a function is collected, by field
; name: each construction of a struct and each `(set e.f v)`. A stored
; top-level function or lambda becomes a member; an opaque parameter,
; call result or value read from raw memory leaves the
; field's set incomplete. A call through the field is then a call to
; each member (`fnValsCall`), with the member's edge recorded like any
; other call: an effect every member performs is definite, one some
; member performs is possible, and an incomplete set is still a hole.
;
; The set is the program's: a library's field is filled by whatever
; the program it is built into constructs. A value can also be written
; into a field without either form - through a forging `cast` or a raw
; store - and both are unsafe operations, outside what the check
; vouches for.
;
; Keyed by the field's NAME, since the walk runs before any body is
; typed and a field read does not yet know its struct: two structs
; with a callable field of the same name share one set, which can only
; make it larger. The stores live in `UState` word 9.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct FieldStore
  (name : String)
  (mut complete : Int)
  (members : (Vec StoredFnVal)))

(pub :: fieldStores (-> TC (Vec FieldStore)))
(pub fn (fieldStores tc)
  tc.unsafe.fieldVals)

(pub :: fieldValsOf (-> TC String (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (fieldValsOf tc fname)
  ; A field nothing in the program stores into was filled some
  ; other way - a raw store, a forging cast - and the walk cannot
  ; see what it holds.
  {
    (let stores (fieldStores tc))
    (if (let (Some index) (fieldStoreFind stores fname 0))
      {
        (let store (vecGet stores index))
        (Some (FnVals :complete store.complete :members (fieldCallableMembers tc store.members)))
      }
      None)
  })

; Stored closures are analysed against the bindings they captured.
; Their rows grow with the same named callees as the surrounding fixpoint.
(pub :: fieldLambdaBindings (-> FsStack Int (Vec FieldLambdaBinding)))
(pub fn (fieldLambdaBindings stack limit)
  {
    (let bindings vecNew)
    (for i in 0..limit
      (vecPush bindings
        (FieldLambdaBinding :name (vecGetStr stack.names i) :initialiser (vecGet stack.inits i))))
    bindings
  })

(pub :: fieldCallableMembers (-> TC (Vec StoredFnVal) (Vec FnVal)))
(pub fn (fieldCallableMembers tc members)
  {
    (let resolved vecNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen members)
      (match (vecGet members i)
        ((KnownFn callable) (vecPush resolved callable))
        ((DeferredFn closure) (vecPush resolved (LambdaFn (fieldLambdaEffects tc closure))))
        ((UnknownFn) resolved)
        ((NonCallableFn) resolved)))
    resolved
  })

(pub :: fieldLambdaScope (-> TC (Vec FieldLambdaBinding) BScope))
(pub fn (fieldLambdaScope tc bindings)
  {
    (let mut bound (bsMake vecNew vecNew vecNew vecNew None (bsCtxOf vecNew vecNew 0 vecNew)))
    (for i in 0..(vecLen bindings)
      {
        (let binding (vecGet bindings i))
        (let value
          (if (let (Some initialiser) binding.initialiser)
            (walkEffectsVal tc initialiser bound vecNew)
            None))
        (set bound (if (let (Some functions) value)
          (bsWithVals bound binding.name functions)
          (bsWith bound binding.name bsOpaque)))
      })
    bound
  })

(pub :: fieldLambdaEffects (-> TC FieldLambda LambdaEffects))
(pub fn (fieldLambdaEffects tc closure)
  {
    (if closure.active
      (effAdd closure.row effIncomplete)
      {
        (set closure.active true)
        (let module (tcCurModule tc))
        (let sink (tcUnsafeSink tc))
        (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt closure.owner))
        ; The closure's unsafe operations belong to its defining boundary.
        (tcClearUnsafeSink tc)
        (let validation (tcCheckState tc))
        (let checking validation.validatingEffects)
        (set validation.validatingEffects false)
        ; Capture initialisers ran when the closure was built. Resolve
        ; their values without attributing their calls to this caller.
        (let calls (tcCurCalls tc))
        (let possibleCalls (tcMayCalls tc))
        (tcSetCurCalls tc None)
        (tcSetMayCalls tc None)
        (let scope (fieldLambdaScope tc closure.bindings))
        (tcSetCurCalls tc calls)
        (tcSetMayCalls tc possibleCalls)
        (let latent (walkLambdaLatent tc closure.expression scope))
        (set validation.validatingEffects checking)
        (let row
          (if (==
                (tagsClaimUnsafe tc
                  (match (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName closure.owner))
                    ((None) vecNew)
                    ((Some tags) tags))
                  (nodeAxtagsOf closure.owner))
                1)
            (effDropUnsafe latent.row)
            latent.row))
        (effUnion closure.row row 0)
        (set closure.casts (|| closure.casts latent.casts))
        (tcSetCurModule tc module)
        (tcSetUnsafeSink tc sink)
        (set closure.active false)
      })
    (LambdaEffects :row closure.row :uses vecNew :casts closure.casts)
  })

(pub :: fieldStoreFind (-> (Vec FieldStore) String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (fieldStoreFind stores fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen stores))
    None
    {
      (let st (vecGet stores i))
      (if (strEq st.name fname)
        (Some i)
        (fieldStoreFind stores fname (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Collect every store, once, before the fixpoint.
(pub :: fieldStoresBuild (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (fieldStoresBuild tc decls)
  {
    (fieldStoresDecls tc (fieldStores tc) decls 0
      (FsStack :names vecNew :inits vecNew :envs vecNew))
    (tcSetCurModule tc None)
    0
  })

; `st` is the one stack every declaration's walk uses, empty between
; declarations.
(pub :: fieldStoresDecls (-> TC (Vec FieldStore) (Vec ASTNode) Int FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fieldStoresDecls tc t decls i st)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
          {
            (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
            (fsPush st (nodeBNames d) 0)
            (fsWalk tc t d (nodeCChild d) st)
            (fsPopTo st 0)
          })
        (fieldStoresDecls tc t decls (+ i 1) st)
      }
    }))

; The locals the collector knows, as three parallel columns: a local's
; name, its optional initialiser (`None` for a parameter or a binder
; whose value it cannot see), and the number of locals in scope where
; it was bound, where a name in the initialiser resolves. One stack serves
; every declaration, pushed on the way into a scope and popped on the
; way out: this pass reads the whole program before the walk, and a
; record per binding would cost it memory for nothing it keeps.
(pub struct FsStack
  (names : Names)
  (inits : (Vec (Option ASTNode)))
  (envs : (Vec Int)))

(pub :: fsLen (-> FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsLen st)
  (vecLen st.names))

(pub :: fsBind (-> FsStack String (Option ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (fsBind st n init env)
  {
    (vecPushStr st.names n)
    (vecPush st.inits init)
    (vecPush st.envs env)
    0
  })

(pub :: fsPush (-> FsStack Names Int Int))
(pub fn (fsPush st names i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    0
    {
      (fsBind st (vecGetStr names i) None (fsLen st))
      (fsPush st names (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: fsPopTo (-> FsStack Int Int))
(pub fn (fsPopTo st n)
  (if (<= (fsLen st) n)
    0
    {
      (vecPop st.names)
      (vecPop st.inits)
      (vecPop st.envs)
      (fsPopTo st n)
    }))

(pub :: fsFillCols (-> FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsFillCols st)
  (if (>= (vecLen st.inits) (vecLen st.names))
    0
    {
      (vecPush st.inits None)
      (vecPush st.envs 0)
      (fsFillCols st)
    }))

; The index of the innermost local named `n` at or below `i`, or `None`.
(pub :: fsFind (-> FsStack String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (fsFind st n i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr st.names i) n)
      (Some i)
      (fsFind st n (- i 1)))))

(pub :: fsWalk (-> TC (Vec FieldStore) ASTNode ASTNode FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsWalk tc t d e st)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (let head (spineHead e))
          {
            (if (&& (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
                  (isNone (fsFind st (nodeAName head) (- (fsLen st) 1))))
              (fsConstruction tc t d (nodeAName head) e st))
            (fsWalk tc t d head st)
            (fsWalkSpine tc t d e st)
          }
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
          {
            (if (== (fieldNameCallable tc (nodeBName e)) 1)
              (fsRecord t (nodeBName e) (fsClassify tc d (nodeCChild e) st (fsLen st))))
            (fsWalk tc t d (nodeAChild e) st)
            (fsWalk tc t d (nodeCChild e) st)
          }
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
            {
              (let n (fsLen st))
              {
                (fsWalk tc t d (nodeBChild e) st)
                (fsBind st (nodeAName e)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_LET)
                    (Some (nodeBChild e))
                    None)
                  n)
                (fsWalk tc t d (nodeCChild e) st)
                (fsPopTo st n)
              }
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
              {
                (let n (fsLen st))
                {
                  (fsPush st (nodeANames e) 0)
                  (fsWalk tc t d (nodeBChild e) st)
                  (fsPopTo st n)
                }
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                {
                  (fsWalk tc t d (nodeAChild e) st)
                  (fsWalkArms tc t d (nodeBChildren e) 0 st)
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                  {
                    (fsWalk tc t d (nodeAChild e) st)
                    (fsWalk tc t d (nodeBChild e) st)
                    (fsWalk tc t d (nodeCChild e) st)
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                    (fsWalkVec tc t d (nodeAChildren e) 0 st)
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                      {
                        (fsWalk tc t d (nodeAChild e) st)
                        (fsWalkVec tc t d (nodeBChildren e) 0 st)
                      }
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                        (fsWalk tc t d (nodeBChild e) st)
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                          {
                            (fsWalk tc t d (nodeAChild e) st)
                            (fsWalk tc t d (nodeCChild e) st)
                          }
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                            (fsWalk tc t d (nodeBChild e) st)
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                              (fsWalk tc t d (nodeAChild e) st)
                              0))))))))))))
    }))

; The arguments of an application, read off its spine in place.
(pub :: fsWalkSpine (-> TC (Vec FieldStore) ASTNode ASTNode FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsWalkSpine tc t d e st)
  (if (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
    0
    {
      (fsWalk tc t d (nodeBChild e) st)
      (fsWalkSpine tc t d (nodeAChild e) st)
    }))

(pub :: fsWalkVec (-> TC (Vec FieldStore) ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsWalkVec tc t d v i st)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (fsWalk tc t d (vecGet v i) st)
      (fsWalkVec tc t d v (+ i 1) st)
    }))

(pub :: fsWalkArms (-> TC (Vec FieldStore) ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsWalkArms tc t d arms i st)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      (let n (fsLen st))
      {
        ; The binders go straight onto the names column; the other two
        ; columns catch up, as values the collector cannot see.
        (patBinders (nodeAChild a) st.names)
        (fsFillCols st)
        (fsWalk tc t d (nodeBChild a) st)
        (fsPopTo st n)
        (fsWalkArms tc t d arms (+ i 1) st)
      }
    }))

; `(S a1 .. an)` for a struct `S`: argument i is stored into S's i-th
; field. The spine is read from its outermost application, which holds
; the last argument.
(pub :: fsConstruction (-> TC (Vec FieldStore) ASTNode String ASTNode FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsConstruction tc t d n e st)
  (if (isSome (findFnEnt tc n))
    0
    {
      (let sentFound (findStruct tc n))
      (if (let (Some sent) sentFound)
        (if (structEntNoFields sent)
          0
          (fsFields tc t d (structEntFields sent) e (- (spineArgs e 0) 1) st))
        0)
    }))

(pub :: fsFields (-> TC (Vec FieldStore) ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int FsStack Int))
(pub fn (fsFields tc t d fields e i st)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP))
    0
    {
      (if (< i (vecLen fields))
        {
          (let f (vecGet fields i))
          (if (== (tyIsCallable (nodeTyChild f)) 1)
            (fsRecord t (nodeAName f) (fsClassify tc d (nodeBChild e) st (fsLen st))))
        })
      (fsFields tc t d fields (nodeAChild e) (- i 1) st)
    }))

; Does any struct declare a field of this name a function could be
; stored in?
(pub :: fieldNameCallable (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (fieldNameCallable tc fname)
  (fieldNameCallableIn (tcStructsVec tc) fname 0))

(pub :: fieldNameCallableIn (-> (Vec StructEnt) String Int Int))
(pub fn (fieldNameCallableIn structs fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen structs))
    0
    {
      (let callable
        (match (structFieldTy (vecGet structs i) fname)
          ((None) 0)
          ((Some ty) (tyIsCallable ty))))
      (if (== callable 1)
        1
        (fieldNameCallableIn structs fname (+ i 1)))
    }))

; A stored value is a known function, a scalar or an unknown value.
(pub :: fsClassify (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode FsStack Int StoredFnVal))
(pub fn (fsClassify tc d v st lim)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag v))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
      (DeferredFn
        (FieldLambda
          :owner d
          :expression v
          :bindings (fieldLambdaBindings st lim)
          :row vecNew
          :active false
          :casts false))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (if (let (Some k) (fsFind st (nodeAName v) (- lim 1)))
          (if (let (Some initialiser) (vecGet st.inits k))
            (fsClassify tc d initialiser st (vecGet st.envs k))
            UnknownFn)
          {
            (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName v)))
            (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
              (if (&& (== (tyIsArrow (fnEntTy ent)) 1)
                    (&& (fnEntMayTakeArgs ent) (== (isSyscallPrim (nodeAName v)) 0)))
                (KnownFn (fnValNamed ent))
                UnknownFn)
              UnknownFn)
          })
        (if (== (notCallableShape v) 1)
          NonCallableFn
          UnknownFn)))
  })

(pub :: fsRecord (-> (Vec FieldStore) String StoredFnVal Int))
(pub fn (fsRecord table name value)
  {
    (let store
      (if (let (Some index) (fieldStoreFind table name 0))
        (vecGet table index)
        {
          (let added (FieldStore :name name :complete 1 :members vecNew))
          (vecPush table added)
          added
        }))
    (match value
      ((UnknownFn)
        {
          (set store.complete 0)
          0
        }
      )
      ((NonCallableFn) 0)
      ((KnownFn _)
        {
          (vecPush store.members value)
          0
        }
      )
      ((DeferredFn _)
        {
          (vecPush store.members value)
          0
        }
      ))
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; DECLARED FIELD EFFECTS: what a function-typed field promises.
;
; A field can say what anything stored in it performs, with the
; `;@axiom:effect(...)` lines a function claims its effects with,
; written above the field (`attachAxtagsFields`):
;
;     (struct Eq (a)
;       ;@axiom:effect(alloc)
;       (eq : (-> a a Bool)))
;
; The declaration is a contract with two halves.
;
; A call through the field is charged the declared row and nothing else
; (`fieldDeclaredCall`). The sets above join every function stored into
; any field of the name, so one record that prints made `IO` possible
; at every call through every `eq` in the program, and a
; `restrict(no-io,strict)` over a comparison could not be proven. The
; stored functions are still walked, for their call edges alone: the
; declaration speaks for effects, and a claim about the call graph
; (`no-recursion`, `no-foreign`, `no-cast:deep`, an `isr`) still follows
; what the field can hold. Where the walk cannot list that, the row
; carries `effGraphOpen`, a hole in the graph and not in the effects.
;
; Every value that enters the field is checked where it enters
; (`fieldStoreCheck`): each construction and each `(set e.f v)`, in the
; validation walk, when every row is final. A value whose effects the
; walk cannot determine is refused (AX3143) rather than trusted, so the
; row holds of everything safe code can put there. A raw store or a
; forging cast is the unsafe layer's, as it is for every other claim.
;
; The row is the named effects plus `Alloc` and `Mut`, which a function
; performs without declaring them; `effect(pure)` is the empty row. A
; stored function must not call its own function arguments
; (`#effect-params`): what those perform is the caller's, and a call
; through the field charges the declaration alone.
;
; Which struct a field read belongs to is a typing question, and the
; fixpoint runs before any body is typed. A read through a parameter
; whose declared type names the struct is charged that struct's row.
; Any other read is resolved by name, as `fieldValsOf` resolves it,
; and charged the union of the declared rows only when every struct
; with a callable field of that name declares one; otherwise the
; name-based set stands, and it covers the declared stores too. A
; program that declares no field effect takes none of these paths.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub struct FieldDecl
  (owner : StructEnt)
  (name : String)
  (row : Strings)
  (said : String))

(pub struct FieldDeclName
  (name : String)
  (all : Bool)
  (row : Strings))

(pub data FieldStoreVerdict
  (FieldStoreOk)
  (FieldStoreExceeds String Strings)
  (FieldStoreUnknown String))

; A hole in the call graph that is not one in the effect row. Effects
; read the declaration; a claim about the call graph reads this as a
; call it could not resolve (`effGraphUnresolved`).
(pub :: effGraphOpen String)
(pub fn (effGraphOpen)
  "?:graph")

(pub :: effGraphUnresolved (-> Strings Int))
(pub fn (effGraphUnresolved v)
  (if (== (effUnresolved v) 1)
    1
    (effIn v effGraphOpen)))

; Only the graph is open: no unresolved call stands beside the hole, so
; the message names the field rather than an unresolved value.
(pub :: effGraphOnly (-> Strings Bool))
(pub fn (effGraphOnly v)
  (&& (== (effUnresolved v) 0) (== (effIn v effGraphOpen) 1)))

(pub :: tcUState (-> TC UState))
(pub fn (tcUState tc)
  tc.unsafe)

; Every field's tags, read once before the fixpoint. A diagnostic lands
; in the declaring module's unit, as `strayAxtagsInUnit`'s does.
(pub :: fieldDeclsBuild (-> TC ()))
(pub fn (fieldDeclsBuild tc)
  {
    (let u (tcUState tc))
    (let was (tcCurUnit tc))
    (for s in (tcStructsVec tc)
      (for f in s.fields
        (if (isSome (nodeAxtagsOpt f))
          {
            (tcSetCurUnit tc (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) s.module))
            (if (let (Some decl) (fieldDeclRead tc s f))
              {
                (vecPush u.fieldDecls
                  (FieldDecl :owner s :name (nodeAName f) :row decl.row :said decl.said))
                ()
              }
              ())
          })))
    (tcSetCurUnit tc was)
    (for d in u.fieldDecls
      (if (let (Some entry) (fieldDeclNameAt u.fieldDeclNames d.name))
        {
          (effUnion entry.row d.row 0)
          ()
        }
        {
          (vecPush u.fieldDeclNames
            (FieldDeclName
              :name d.name
              :all (fieldNameAllDeclared tc u d.name)
              :row (effCopy d.row)))
          ()
        }))
  })

; One field's tags: its declaration, or `None` when it declares nothing
; or the declaration was refused. A key other than `effect` is answered
; as it is on any declaration that cannot carry it.
(pub :: fieldDeclRead (-> TC StructEnt ASTNode (Option FieldDecl)))
(pub fn (fieldDeclRead tc owner f)
  {
    (let named vecNew)
    (let said vecNew)
    (let mut seen false)
    (let mut pure false)
    (let mut good true)
    (for t in (nodeAxtagsOf f)
      {
        (let key (axtagKeyOf t))
        (if (strEq key "effect")
          {
            (set seen true)
            (let val (axtagTextValue t))
            (let names (effTagWords val))
            (if (> (vecLen names) 1)
              {
                (emitEffectTagList tc f val names)
                (set good false)
              }
              {
                (let word
                  (if (== (vecLen names) 1)
                    (vecGetStr names 0)
                    ""))
                (vecPushStr said (concat (concat "`effect(" word) ")`"))
                (if (strEq word "pure")
                  (set pure true)
                  (if (let (Some why) (fieldEffectRefusal tc word))
                    {
                      (emitFieldDeclBad tc f
                        (concat
                          (concat (concat (concat "`effect(" word) ")` on the field `")
                            (nodeAName f))
                          "` names no effect a field can declare")
                        why)
                      (set good false)
                    }
                    {
                      (effAdd named (axtagEffectOf tc word))
                      ()
                    }))
              })
          }
          {
            (if (== (axtagKnownKey key) 1)
              (emitAxtagStray tc f key)
              (axtagKeyTypo tc f key))
            ()
          })
      })
    (if (|| (! seen) (! good))
      None
      (if (&& (!= (nodeTy f) 0) (!= (nodeTag (nodeTyChild f)) TAG_T_ARR))
        {
          (emitFieldDeclBad tc f
            (concat
              (concat
                (concat (concat "the field `" (nodeAName f)) "` declares its effects but holds `")
                (tyRender (nodeTyChild f)))
              "`, which is not a function type")
            "an effect declaration describes the functions a field holds, so it needs an arrow type such as `(-> Int Int)`")
          None
        }
        (if (&& pure (> (vecLen named) 0))
          {
            (emitFieldDeclBad tc f
              (concat (concat "the field `" (nodeAName f))
                "` declares `effect(pure)` beside another effect")
              "`effect(pure)` says the field's functions perform nothing; keep it alone, or drop it and list the effects they may perform")
            None
          }
          (if (== (nodeTy f) 0)
            None
            (Some
              (FieldDecl
                :owner owner
                :name (nodeAName f)
                :row
                  (if pure
                    vecNew
                    {
                      (let row (effCopy named))
                      (effAdd row (builtinEff "Alloc"))
                      (effAdd row (builtinEff "Mut"))
                      row
                    })
                :said (fieldSaidJoin said)))))))
  })

(pub :: fieldSaidJoin (-> Strings String))
(pub fn (fieldSaidJoin said)
  {
    (let seen vecNew)
    (let mut out "")
    (for one in said
      (if (== (strInVec seen one 0) 0)
        {
          (vecPushStr seen one)
          (set out (if (strEq out "")
            one
            (concat (concat out " and ") one)))
        }))
    out
  })

; Why `word` is not an effect a field can declare, or `None` when it is.
; The names are a function's (`axtagEffectOf`), less the two that
; describe no stored function: `unsafe` marks a trusted encapsulation,
; and nothing infers `div`.
(pub :: fieldEffectRefusal (-> TC String (Option String)))
(pub fn (fieldEffectRefusal tc word)
  {
    (let want (axtagEffectOf tc word))
    (if (== (strLen want) 0)
      (Some "an `effect(...)` tag names one effect")
      (if (strEq want (builtinEff "Unsafe"))
        (Some
          "`effect(unsafe)` marks a trusted function; each function a field holds keeps its own unsafe boundary")
        (if (strEq want (builtinEff "Div"))
          (Some "nothing infers `Div`, so no stored function could be checked against it")
          (if (== (axtagCouldExist tc want) 0)
            (Some (concat (concat "no effect named `" word) "` is declared"))
            None))))
  })

(pub :: emitFieldDeclBad (-> TC ASTNode String String Int))
(pub fn (emitFieldDeclBad tc f msg note)
  (if (== (nodeSpan f) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3144" "field-effect-declaration" (nodeSpan f) msg None
          (Some
            "a function-typed field declares `;@axiom:effect(pure)`, or one line per effect its functions may perform: `io`, `alloc`, `mut`, `spawn`, `block`, `entropy` or a declared effect")
          note)
        "declaration not read"))))

(pub :: fieldDeclNameAt (-> (Vec FieldDeclName) String (Option FieldDeclName)))
(pub fn (fieldDeclNameAt names name)
  {
    (let mut found None)
    (for entry in names
      (if (strEq entry.name name)
        (set found (Some entry))))
    found
  })

(pub :: fieldDeclFor (-> UState StructEnt String (Option FieldDecl)))
(pub fn (fieldDeclFor u sent fname)
  {
    (let mut found None)
    (for d in u.fieldDecls
      (if (&& (== (as d.owner Int) (as sent Int)) (strEq d.name fname))
        (set found (Some d))))
    found
  })

; Does every struct with a callable field `name` declare that field's
; effects? Only then can a read whose struct the walk cannot see be
; charged the union of their rows.
(pub :: fieldNameAllDeclared (-> TC UState String Bool))
(pub fn (fieldNameAllDeclared tc u name)
  {
    (let mut all true)
    (for s in (tcStructsVec tc)
      (if (let (Some ty) (structFieldTy s name))
        (if (== (tyIsCallable ty) 1)
          (if (let (Some _) (fieldDeclFor u s name))
            ()
            (set all false)))))
    all
  })

; The declared row a read of the field `e` is charged, or `None` for the
; name-based set. See the section header for the two resolutions.
(pub :: fieldDeclRowAt (-> TC ASTNode BScope (Option Strings)))
(pub fn (fieldDeclRowAt tc e bound)
  {
    (let u (tcUState tc))
    (if (== (vecLen u.fieldDeclNames) 0)
      None
      (if (let (Some entry) (fieldDeclNameAt u.fieldDeclNames (nodeBName e)))
        (match (fieldParamStruct tc (nodeAChild e) bound)
          ((Some sent)
            (match (fieldDeclFor u sent (nodeBName e))
              ((None) None)
              ((Some d) (Some d.row)))
          )
          ((None)
            (if entry.all
              (Some entry.row)
              None)
          ))
        None))
  })

; The struct a field read's base names, when the base is a parameter
; (or a local aliasing one) whose declared type is that struct.
(pub :: fieldParamStruct (-> TC ASTNode BScope (Option StructEnt)))
(pub fn (fieldParamStruct tc base bound)
  (if (!= (nodeTag base) TAG_E_VAR)
    None
    (if (let (Some ty) (bsParamTy bound (bsParamAlias bound (nodeAName base))))
      (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
        (findStruct tc (nodeAName ty))
        None)
      None)))

; A call through a declared field: the row, and the stored functions'
; edges. The members are called with no arguments - no stored function
; calls its own (`fieldStoreVerdict`), so nothing of the caller's is
; handed to one - and into a row the call does not keep.
(pub :: fieldDeclaredCall (-> TC String Strings BScope Strings Int ()))
(pub fn (fieldDeclaredCall tc fname row bound acc unboxed)
  {
    (let scratch vecNew)
    (let listed
      (if (let (Some fv) (fieldValsOf tc fname))
        {
          (fnValsCall tc fv vecNew bound scratch unboxed)
          (&& (== fv.complete 1) (== (effPartial scratch) 0))
        }
        false))
    (effUnionRow acc row 0)
    (if (|| (! listed) (== (effIn scratch effGraphOpen) 1))
      {
        (effAdd acc effGraphOpen)
        ()
      })
  })

; A declared field read as a value: one function whose row is the
; declaration. Where it is called the walk cannot list what the field
; holds, so the graph is open there.
(pub :: fieldDeclaredVals (-> Strings (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (fieldDeclaredVals row)
  {
    (let r (effCopy row))
    (effAdd r effGraphOpen)
    (Some (fnValsOne (LambdaFn (LambdaEffects :row r :uses vecNew :casts false))))
  })

; The struct a construction head or a field node's stamp names. The
; checker stamps a field node with the struct's own name, or
; `Mod$Name` for one an import declared (`stampFieldStruct`), and the
; expander writes a construction's head the same way; a head a macro
; wrote bare is looked up as any type name is.
(pub :: fieldStructNamed (-> TC String (Option StructEnt)))
(pub fn (fieldStructNamed tc n)
  {
    (let mut found None)
    (for s in (tcStructsVec tc)
      (if (isNone found)
        (if (strEq (fieldStructSpelling s) n)
          (set found (Some s)))))
    (if (isSome found)
      found
      (findStruct tc n))
  })

(pub :: fieldStructSpelling (-> StructEnt String))
(pub fn (fieldStructSpelling s)
  (if (isNone s.module)
    s.name
    (concat (concat (structEntModuleName s) "$") s.name)))

; `(S a1 .. an)`: argument i enters S's i-th field. Asked in the
; validation walk only, when every row is final.
(pub :: fieldStoreCtorCheck (-> TC String Nodes BScope ()))
(pub fn (fieldStoreCtorCheck tc n args bound)
  {
    (let u (tcUState tc))
    (let state (tcCheckState tc))
    (if (&& state.validatingEffects (> (vecLen u.fieldDecls) 0))
      {
        (let sentFound (fieldStructNamed tc n))
        (if (let (Some sent) sentFound)
          {
            (let fields sent.fields)
            (if (== (vecLen fields) (vecLen args))
              {
                (let mut i 0)
                (while (< i (vecLen fields))
                  {
                    (if (let (Some d) (fieldDeclFor u sent (nodeAName (vecGet fields i))))
                      (fieldStoreCheck tc sent d (vecGet args i) bound)
                      ())
                    (set i (+ i 1))
                  })
              })
          })
      })
  })

; `(set e.f v)`: the struct is the one the checker stamped on the node.
(pub :: fieldStoreSetCheck (-> TC ASTNode BScope ()))
(pub fn (fieldStoreSetCheck tc e bound)
  {
    (let u (tcUState tc))
    (let state (tcCheckState tc))
    (if (&& state.validatingEffects (&& (> (vecLen u.fieldDecls) 0) (!= (nodeTy e) 0)))
      {
        (let sentFound (fieldStructNamed tc (nodeTyText e)))
        (if (let (Some sent) sentFound)
          (if (let (Some d) (fieldDeclFor u sent (nodeBName e)))
            (fieldStoreCheck tc sent d (nodeCChild e) bound)
            ()))
      })
  })

; One store, answered once: a value the walk meets twice - a lambda
; handed to a restricted callee is walked for the restriction and for
; the call - draws one diagnostic.
(pub :: fieldStoreCheck (-> TC StructEnt FieldDecl ASTNode BScope ()))
(pub fn (fieldStoreCheck tc sent d v bound)
  {
    (let u (tcUState tc))
    (let span (spanOf v))
    (if (&& (!= span 0) (== (intInVec u.fieldStoreSpans span 0) 0))
      (match (fieldStoreVerdict tc v d.row bound)
        ((FieldStoreOk) ())
        ((FieldStoreExceeds what effs)
          {
            (vecPush u.fieldStoreSpans span)
            (emitFieldStoreExceeds tc sent d span what effs)
          }
        )
        ((FieldStoreUnknown why)
          {
            (vecPush u.fieldStoreSpans span)
            (emitFieldStoreUnknown tc sent d span why)
          }
        )))
  })

; What the stored value is, and whether it keeps `row`. Its functions
; are found by the walk's own reading of a value (`walkEffectsVal`),
; with validation off so a store inside a stored lambda is answered
; once, where the main walk meets it. A field read whose node the
; checker stamped is resolved exactly, through the struct it names.
(pub :: fieldStoreVerdict (-> TC ASTNode Strings BScope FieldStoreVerdict))
(pub fn (fieldStoreVerdict tc v row bound)
  (if (let (Some d) (fieldStoreSourceDecl tc v))
    (fieldRowVerdict d.row row
      (concat (concat (concat "the field `" (bareOf d.owner.name)) ".") (concat (nodeBName v) "`")))
    {
      (let state (tcCheckState tc))
      (let checking state.validatingEffects)
      (let sink (tcUnsafeSink tc))
      (set state.validatingEffects false)
      (tcClearUnsafeSink tc)
      (let vals (walkEffectsVal tc v bound vecNew))
      (tcSetUnsafeSink tc sink)
      (set state.validatingEffects checking)
      (match vals
        ((None) (FieldStoreUnknown (fieldValueShape tc v bound)))
        ((Some fv)
          (if (!= fv.complete 1)
            (FieldStoreUnknown
              (if (== (nodeTag v) TAG_E_FIELD)
                (concat (concat "the field `" (fieldReadName tc v))
                  "`, which declares no effects and may hold a value the compiler can't follow")
                "a value that may be one the compiler can't follow"))
            (fieldMembersVerdict tc fv.members row bound 0))
        ))
    }))

; A field read as `S.f` when the checker stamped its struct, else `f`.
(pub :: fieldReadName (-> TC ASTNode String))
(pub fn (fieldReadName tc v)
  {
    (let src
      (:: (if (== (nodeTy v) 0)
        None
        (fieldStructNamed tc (nodeTyText v))) (Option StructEnt)))
    (if (let (Some entry) src)
      (concat (concat (bareOf entry.name) ".") (nodeBName v))
      (nodeBName v))
  })

; A stored field read the checker stamped, when its field declares.
(pub :: fieldStoreSourceDecl (-> TC ASTNode (Option FieldDecl)))
(pub fn (fieldStoreSourceDecl tc v)
  (if (|| (!= (nodeTag v) TAG_E_FIELD) (== (nodeTy v) 0))
    None
    (if (let (Some src) (fieldStructNamed tc (nodeTyText v)))
      (fieldDeclFor (tcUState tc) src (nodeBName v))
      None)))

(pub :: fieldMembersVerdict (-> TC (Vec FnVal) Strings BScope Int FieldStoreVerdict))
(pub fn (fieldMembersVerdict tc members row bound i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen members))
    FieldStoreOk
    {
      (let verdict
        (match (vecGet members i)
          ((NamedFn ent)
            (if (> (vecLen ent.eparams) 0)
              (FieldStoreUnknown
                (concat (concat "`" (bareOf ent.name))
                  "`, which calls a function it is given, and what that performs is chosen at each call"))
              (fieldRowVerdict (fieldCallRowOf tc ent) row
                (concat (concat "`" (bareOf ent.name)) "`")))
          )
          ((LambdaFn latent)
            (if (> (vecLen latent.uses) 0)
              (FieldStoreUnknown
                (concat
                  (concat "a lambda that calls the parameter `"
                    (fieldUseName bound (vecGet latent.uses 0)))
                  "`, which each caller chooses"))
              (fieldRowVerdict latent.row row "the lambda"))
          )))
      (match verdict
        ((FieldStoreOk) (fieldMembersVerdict tc members row bound (+ i 1)))
        (_ verdict))
    }))

(pub :: fieldUseName (-> BScope EpUse String))
(pub fn (fieldUseName bound use)
  (if (&& (>= use.pidx 0) (< use.pidx (vecLen (bsParamsVec bound))))
    (vecGetStr (bsParamsVec bound) use.pidx)
    "?"))

; What a call to `ent` adds to its caller's row, as `walkCallEnt` adds
; it: the row less a trusted callee's `Unsafe`, a raw primitive's own
; `Unsafe`, and the box a pair function's answer needs when it is
; called through a value.
(pub :: fieldCallRowOf (-> TC FnEnt Strings))
(pub fn (fieldCallRowOf tc ent)
  {
    (let r vecNew)
    (if (== ent.unsafe 1)
      (effUnionTrusted r ent.effects 0 0)
      (effUnion r ent.effects 0))
    (if (== (isUnsafePrim ent.name) 1)
      (effAdd r (builtinEff "Unsafe")))
    (if (== (tcIsPairName (tcPairFns tc) ent.name) 1)
      (effAdd r (builtinEff "Alloc")))
    r
  })

; Within `row`, or what is outside it. A row the walk could not close
; is no answer: its absences are the walk's, not the function's.
(pub :: fieldRowVerdict (-> Strings Strings String FieldStoreVerdict))
(pub fn (fieldRowVerdict have row what)
  (if (== (effUnresolved have) 1)
    (FieldStoreUnknown
      (concat what
        ", which calls a value the compiler could not resolve, so its effects are a lower bound"))
    {
      (let extra vecNew)
      (for e in have
        (if (== (effIsMarker e) 0)
          (if (== (effIn row (effDefiniteOf e)) 0)
            {
              (effAdd extra (effDefiniteOf e))
              ()
            })))
      (if (== (vecLen extra) 0)
        FieldStoreOk
        (FieldStoreExceeds what extra))
    }))

; Why a value the walk does not follow to a function is not one it can
; check. A parameter is named, because the fix is at the caller.
(pub :: fieldValueShape (-> TC ASTNode BScope String))
(pub fn (fieldValueShape tc value bound)
  (if (== (nodeTag value) TAG_E_VAR)
    (if (let (Some index) (bsAliasParam (bsParamAlias bound (nodeAName value))))
      (if (< index (vecLen (bsParamsVec bound)))
        (concat (concat "the parameter `" (vecGetStr (bsParamsVec bound) index))
          "`, which each caller chooses")
        "a value the compiler can't follow to a function, such as a pattern binder, a lambda's parameter or a mutable local")
      "a value the compiler can't follow to a function, such as a pattern binder, a lambda's parameter or a mutable local")
    (if (== (nodeTag value) TAG_E_APP)
      "a call's result"
      "a value the compiler can't follow to a function")))

; AX3142: a stored function performs an effect the field does not declare.
(pub :: emitFieldStoreExceeds (-> TC StructEnt FieldDecl Int String Strings ()))
(pub fn (emitFieldStoreExceeds tc sent d span what effs)
  {
    (let field (concat (concat (bareOf (structEntName sent)) ".") d.name))
    (let diag
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3142" "field-effect-exceeded" span
          (concat (concat (concat (concat what " performs ") (effJoin effs)) ", and the field `")
            (concat (concat field "` declares ") d.said))
          None
          (Some
            (concat
              "store a function that keeps the declaration, or declare the effect on the field with `;@axiom:effect("
              (concat (fieldEffectSpelling (fieldEffectToSuggest effs)) ")`")))
          (concat (concat "a call through `" field)
            "` is charged what the field declares and nothing else, so everything stored in it has to keep that"))
        (concat "performs " (effJoin effs))))
    ; A lambda of two or more parameters is curried: a call applies it
    ; one argument at a time, and every step but the last builds the
    ; closure for the next. That is the `Alloc`, and no body can avoid
    ; it.
    (if (&& (strEq what "the lambda")
          (&& (== (effIn effs (builtinEff "Alloc")) 1)
            (match (structFieldTy sent d.name)
              ((None) false)
              ((Some ty) (== (tyIsArrow (nodeBChild ty)) 1)))))
      (diagAddNote diag
        "a lambda of two or more parameters allocates the closure for each argument but the last, so calling it performs `Alloc`; a field of two or more parameters declares `effect(alloc)` at least"))
    (emitDiag tc diag)
    ()
  })

; The effect the help suggests declaring: one a function must declare
; when it has one, else the ambient pair's spelling.
(pub :: fieldEffectToSuggest (-> Strings String))
(pub fn (fieldEffectToSuggest effs)
  {
    (let mut pick (:: None (Option String)))
    ; Empty text has not selected an effect, matching the total Alloc fallback.
    (for e in (effSorted effs)
      (if (&& (isNone pick)
            (&& (> (strLen e) 0)
              (&& (! (strEq e (builtinEff "Alloc"))) (! (strEq e (builtinEff "Mut"))))))
        (set pick (Some e))))
    (match pick
      ((None) (builtinEff "Alloc"))
      ((Some effect) effect))
  })

; The tag spelling of an effect: a built-in's lower-case name, a
; declared effect's own.
(pub :: fieldEffectSpelling (-> String String))
(pub fn (fieldEffectSpelling e)
  (if (strEq (strSlice e 0 2) "b:")
    (strLower (effRender e))
    (effRender e)))

; AX3143: a stored value whose effects the compiler cannot determine.
(pub :: emitFieldStoreUnknown (-> TC StructEnt FieldDecl Int String ()))
(pub fn (emitFieldStoreUnknown tc sent d span why)
  {
    (let field (concat (concat (bareOf (structEntName sent)) ".") d.name))
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3143" "field-effect-unknown" span
          (concat
            (concat
              (concat (concat "the compiler can't show that the value stored in `" field)
                "` keeps ")
              d.said)
            (concat ": it is " why))
          None
          (Some
            "store a named function or a lambda whose body the compiler can see, or build the record where the function is known")
          (concat (concat "a call through `" field)
            "` is charged what the field declares and nothing else, so a value whose effects can't be determined is refused rather than trusted"))
        "effects unknown here"))
    ()
  })

; `#field-effects=` on a struct's AXSYM row: each declared field and
; what a call through it is charged, `pure` for nothing. "" when the
; struct declares none.
(pub :: fieldEffectsMeta (-> TC StructEnt String))
(pub fn (fieldEffectsMeta tc s)
  {
    (let u (tcUState tc))
    (let parts vecNew)
    (for f in s.fields
      (if (let (Some d) (fieldDeclFor u s (nodeAName f)))
        {
          (vecPushStr parts
            (concat (concat (nodeAName f) ":")
              (if (== (vecLen d.row) 0)
                "pure"
                (fieldRowPlus (effSorted d.row) 0 ""))))
          ()
        }
        ()))
    (if (== (vecLen parts) 0)
      ""
      (concat "field-effects=" (fieldJoinComma parts 0 "")))
  })

(pub :: fieldRowPlus (-> Strings Int String String))
(pub fn (fieldRowPlus v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    (fieldRowPlus v (+ i 1)
      (if (== i 0)
        (effRender (vecGetStr v i))
        (concat (concat acc "+") (effRender (vecGetStr v i)))))))

(pub :: fieldJoinComma (-> Strings Int String String))
(pub fn (fieldJoinComma v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    acc
    (fieldJoinComma v (+ i 1)
      (if (== i 0)
        (vecGetStr v i)
        (concat (concat acc ",") (vecGetStr v i))))))

(pub :: walkEffects (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (walkEffects tc e bound acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (walkEffectsSpine tc e bound acc)
          0
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          ; A bare reference to an effectful name is attributed
          ; exactly as a call is - stage0's reference-site rule,
          ; which is what keeps a function value's effects from
          ; escaping attribution entirely. Not a head, so a bound
          ; name here is an ordinary local read - unless it holds a
          ; function the walk follows, which escapes here.
          {
            (unsafeSinkNote tc e bound)
            (if (let (Some fv) (if (== (bsIsLocal bound (nodeAName e)) 1)
              (bsValsOf bound (nodeAName e))
              None))
              (fnValsEscape tc fv bound acc)
              (effRefSite tc (nodeAName e) bound acc))
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
            {
              (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Mut"))
              (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
              (walkEffects tc (nodeCChild e) bound acc)
              (fieldStoreSetCheck tc e bound)
              0
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
              (walkEffectsHandle tc e bound acc)
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                {
                  (walkEffectsLet tc e bound acc 0)
                  0
                }
                ; A lambda nothing here calls: it ESCAPES. Its built-in
                ; effects count where it is written; its custom ones
                ; arrive possible, and its parameter uses escape too.
                (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                  {
                    (let m (walkLambdaLatent tc e bound))
                    {
                      (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc"))
                      (latentBuiltinsInto acc m.row 0)
                      (latentCustomPossibleInto acc m.row 0)
                      (latentApply bound m.uses vecNew 1 0)
                    }
                  }
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                    {
                      (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                      (walkEffects tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                      (walkEffects tc (nodeCChild e) bound acc)
                    }
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                      (walkEffectsVec tc (nodeAChildren e) 0 bound acc)
                      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                          ; The row the three arena primitives carry:
                          ; a reset ends every block allocated since
                          ; its mark, which is heap MACHINERY whether
                          ; or not the body allocated.
                          {
                            (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc"))
                            (walkEffects tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                          }
                          {
                            (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                            (walkEffectsVec tc (nodeBChildren e) 0 bound acc)
                          })
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                          (walkEffects tc (nodeBChild e) bound acc)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                            ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds
                            ; none, so no arm walks one's effects.
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                              {
                                ; A match whose scrutinee is a direct
                                ; call the emitter answers in two
                                ; registers is recorded BEFORE the
                                ; scrutinee is walked, so the call
                                ; below is charged no box.
                                (if (== (tcPairMatchOK tc e bound) 1)
                                  (tcSiteAdd tc (nodeAChild e)))
                                (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild e) bound acc)
                                (walkEffectsArms tc (nodeBChildren e) 0 bound acc)
                              }
                              0))))))))))))
    }))

(pub :: walkEffectsVec (-> TC Nodes Int BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (walkEffectsVec tc v i bound acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (walkEffects tc (vecGet v i) bound acc)
      (walkEffectsVec tc v (+ i 1) bound acc)
    }))

; A match arm binds its pattern's variables, which must shadow.
(pub :: walkEffectsArms (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (walkEffectsArms tc arms i bound acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      {
        (walkEffects tc (nodeBChild a) (bsWithPattern bound (nodeAChild a)) acc)
        (walkEffectsArms tc arms (+ i 1) bound acc)
      }
    }))

; A handle subtracts what it handles from the BODY's effects only,
; then adds Alloc when it installs a real dynamic handler. The
; handler expression's own effects pass through undiminished. A
; function the body hands back is the form's value, and here nothing
; calls it: it escapes, past the handler (`walkEffectsHandleVal`).
(pub :: walkEffectsHandle (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (walkEffectsHandle tc e bound acc)
  (if (let (Some fv) (walkEffectsHandleVal tc e bound acc))
    (fnValsEscape tc fv bound acc)
    0))

(pub :: effUnionUnhandled (-> TC Strings Strings (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (effUnionUnhandled tc acc inner effs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen inner))
    0
    {
      (if (! (== (effDischargedBy tc effs (vecGetStr inner i) 0) 1))
        (effAdd acc (vecGetStr inner i)))
      (effUnionUnhandled tc acc inner effs (+ i 1))
    }))

; What a handle DISCHARGES, which is not what it NAMES.
;
; AX3011 asks whether the list names an effect, because the list has to
; be exhaustive: a body performing an effect the list omits is refused,
; and that is what makes the list a description of the region rather
; than a wish. Propagation asks a different question - whether the
; effect still happens to the CALLER - and for a built-in the answer is
; yes. `handleIsDynamic` is the reason: a handler is installed only
; when a name resolves to a declared effect, and for a built-in the
; form lowers to its body alone. Nothing intercepts the syscall.
;
; So `(handle BODY (IO) 0)` used to erase IO from everything above it.
; The author had to write `(IO)` for the form to compile at all, so it
; was never an accident, but the erasure was: a function could claim
; `;@axiom:effect(pure)`, acknowledge IO inside, and be reported `#pure` while
; writing to stdout - and the only diagnostic landed on its HONEST
; CALLER, for truthfully declaring the io the callee had hidden. A
; policy reading that build learns the opposite of the truth about both
; functions.
;
; A declared effect still discharges, because a handler really does
; intercept its operations - that is the whole of what `handle` does
; for the effects it can do it for.
;
; Both spellings discharge: a handler installed for `Ask` intercepts
; an operation whether the walk knew the body performs it or only
; that it might (`effDefiniteOf`).
(pub :: effDischargedBy (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int))
(pub fn (effDischargedBy tc effs e i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    0
    {
      (let n (nodeAName (vecGet effs i)))
      (if (&& (== (isBuiltinEffect n) 0) (isSome (findEffectDecl tc n)))
        (if (strEq (customEff n) (effDefiniteOf e))
          1
          (effDischargedBy tc effs e (+ i 1)))
        (effDischargedBy tc effs e (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Does this handle's effect-name list cover `e`? A name in the list
; is the built-in effect when it spells one and a custom effect
; otherwise - stage0's parser decides this, not the declarations.
; Compared on the NAME, so a possible effect must be listed too:
; AX3011 reads the upper bound, and a body that MAY reach `Ask`
; through a bare `ask` needs a handler for it as much as one that
; does (`340-effect-op-value.ax`).
(pub :: effHandledBy (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int))
(pub fn (effHandledBy effs e i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    0
    {
      (let n (nodeAName (vecGet effs i)))
      (if (strEq
            (if (== (isBuiltinEffect n) 1)
              (builtinEff n)
              (customEff n))
            (effDefiniteOf e))
        1
        (effHandledBy effs e (+ i 1)))
    }))

; A handle installs evidence - and so allocates - exactly when one of
; its names resolves to a declared effect, which mirrors the gate
; stage0's generator uses.
(pub :: handleIsDynamic (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (handleIsDynamic tc effs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    0
    {
      (let n (nodeAName (vecGet effs i)))
      (if (&& (== (isBuiltinEffect n) 0) (isSome (findEffectDecl tc n)))
        1
        (handleIsDynamic tc effs (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The spine: every argument is walked for its own effects, then the
; head decides what the call itself adds - and, when the callee is
; known to be effect-transparent somewhere, which argument escapes
; into that position.
(pub :: walkEffectsSpine (-> TC ASTNode BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (walkEffectsSpine tc e bound acc)
  {
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (let head (spineHead e))
    (let argEntFound (spineArgEnt tc (spineHead e) bound))
    {
      ; Arguments of a CALL are attributed exactly: a bare arrow-typed
      ; name there is handed to a body that may apply it, so its
      ; effects are this call's and not an over-approximation.
      ;
      ; A CONSTRUCTOR's arguments are not. `(Box doIO)` STORES the
      ; function; nothing applies it, and `install` does not perform
      ; what `doIO` performs. Measured: treating every argument as a
      ; call put a false AX3042 on `install`, `hold` and `main` in
      ; `tests/diagnostics/343-axtag-unverifiable.ax`, whose whole
      ; subject is a function value going into memory in one place and
      ; being called in another.
      ;
      ; `findFnEnt` answers 0 for a constructor, which is the same
      ; property `MM-EXEC-9a`'s note relies on - it answers 0 for
      ; `cast` and every struct/data constructor - so the head having
      ; an entry is exactly "this is a function that could apply it".
      ;
      ; A `cast`, `sizeof` or `alignof` head's FIRST argument is a
      ; type written in expression position, and a type is not a
      ; reference to anything. Walked as a value, the type variable in
      ; `Vec.ax`'s `(cast a (memGetWord ...))` resolved to whatever
      ; top-level function the entry file named `a`: a program
      ; declaring an IO-performing `a` drew 53 false errors inside
      ; `stdlib/`, and every file of `tests/stdlib/` stopped compiling
      ; once one was added (`tests/selfhost/1006-cast-type-operand.ax`).
      (if (let (Some argEnt) argEntFound)
        (walkEffectsArgVecAt tc args 0 bound acc argEnt)
        (walkEffectsVec tc args
          (if (&& (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
                (&& (== (isCastLike (nodeAName head)) 1) (== (bsIsLocal bound (nodeAName head)) 0)))
            1
            0)
          bound acc))
      (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
        {
          (unsafeSinkNote tc head bound)
          (walkCallHead tc (nodeAName head) args bound acc (tcSiteIn tc e))
        }
        ; A head that is not a name at all: a field holding a
        ; function, the result of a call, a `let`-bound value. Walking
        ; it attributes what EVALUATING the head performs and says
        ; nothing whatever about what CALLING it performs, and that
        ; difference is the whole of the hole `effIncomplete` was
        ; declared to record. `((b.f) x)` under a `;@axiom:effect(pure)` claim
        ; was ACCEPTED - not because the body was pure, but because
        ; the set the claim was checked against came back empty - and
        ; `symbols` then printed `#pure` with no `#effects=` beside
        ; it, which is the analysis asserting exactly what it had
        ; failed to look at.
        ;
        ; An immediately-applied lambda is the one shape here that IS
        ; resolvable: `walkEffects` walks its body, so it is
        ; attributed and must not be called incomplete.
        ;
        ; A FIELD head is resolved through what the program stores in
        ; that field (`fieldValsOf`), and stays a hole only when the
        ; stores include a value the walk cannot see. A field that
        ; declares its effects is charged the declaration instead
        ; (`fieldDeclaredCall`).
        (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_LAM)
          (walkCalledLambda tc head bound acc)
          (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_FIELD)
            {
              (walkEffects tc (nodeAChild head) bound acc)
              (if (let (Some row) (fieldDeclRowAt tc head bound))
                {
                  (fieldDeclaredCall tc (nodeBName head) row bound acc (tcSiteIn tc e))
                  0
                }
                (match (fieldValsOf tc (nodeBName head))
                  ((None) (effAdd acc effIncomplete))
                  ((Some fv) (fnValsCall tc fv args bound acc (tcSiteIn tc e)))))
            }
            {
              (walkEffects tc head bound acc)
              (effAdd acc effIncomplete)
            })))
    }
  })

; The function whose transparent positions a call's arguments land in,
; when the walk knows it: a top-level name, or a local or field known to
; hold exactly that one function. 0 otherwise.
(pub :: spineArgEnt (-> TC ASTNode BScope (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (spineArgEnt tc head bound)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag head))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
      (if (== (bsIsLocal bound (nodeAName head)) 1)
        (fnValsSoleEnt (bsValsOf bound (nodeAName head)))
        (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName head)))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
        (if (let (Some _) (fieldDeclRowAt tc head bound))
          None
          (fnValsSoleEnt (fieldValsOf tc (nodeBName head))))
        None))
  })

(pub :: fnValsSoleEnt (-> (Option FnVals) (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (fnValsSoleEnt value)
  (if (let (Some functions) value)
    (if (&& (== functions.complete 1) (== (vecLen functions.members) 1))
      (match (vecGet functions.members 0)
        ((NamedFn ent) (Some ent))
        ((LambdaFn _) None))
      None)
    None))

; Mark every parameter alias among `args` as escaping, for a call whose
; callee's own uses the walk cannot read.
(pub :: escapeArgsMark (-> BScope Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (escapeArgsMark bound args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (escapeValue bound (vecGet args i))
      (escapeArgsMark bound args (+ i 1))
    }))

; How many words a construction of `n` allocates a block for, or 0
; when `n` names no constructor at all.
;
; `findFnEnt` answers 0 for a `data` constructor, a struct and `cast`
; alike, so the effect walk needed a second question to tell the first
; two from the third. This is that question, asked of the same tables
; `checkSaturation` asks - `rfindCtor` for a `data` constructor's
; arrow depth, `findStruct` for a struct's declared field list - so a
; name the checker calls a constructor and a name this calls one
; cannot drift apart.
;
; ZERO IS BOTH ANSWERS on purpose: "not a constructor" and "a
; constructor that allocates nothing" have the same consequence here,
; and no caller needs to tell them apart.
(pub :: ctorAllocArity (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (ctorAllocArity tc n)
  {
    (let ctorFound (rfindCtor tc n))
    (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
      (arrowArity ctor.ctorTy)
      {
        (let sentFound (findStruct tc n))
        (if (let (Some sent) sentFound)
          (if (== (structEntIsWord (Some sent)) 1)
            0
            {
              (let fields (structEntFields sent))
              (if (== (as fields Int) 0)
                0
                (vecLen fields))
            })
          0)
      })
  })

; stage0's `walk_call`, by callee kind.
(pub :: walkCallHead (-> TC String Nodes BScope Strings Int Int))
; `unboxed` is 1 for exactly the call nodes `tcPairSitesOf` recorded:
; a tail leaf of a function the emitter writes as `@F$pair`, or a
; match scrutinee it reads from registers. Everywhere else a
; constructor application allocates its block and a call to a pair
; function boxes the pair it gets back - in THIS body, which is where
; the charge lands.
(pub fn (walkCallHead tc n args bound acc unboxed)
  (if (== (bsIsLocal bound n) 1)
    (if (let (Some fv) (bsValsOf bound n))
      {
        (if (isNone (fnValsSoleEnt (Some fv)))
          (escapeArgsMark bound args 0))
        (fnValsCall tc fv args bound acc unboxed)
      }
      {
        (markEparam bound (bsAliasParam (bsParamAlias bound n)))
        ; The callee is a parameter, or a local aliasing one, so the
        ; enclosing signature names its type and `escapeArgs` can ask
        ; which of its argument positions could hold a function at all.
        ; An opaque local answers 0 here and every position stays
        ; conservative, which is the right answer for a value whose
        ; identity the walk lost.
        (escapeArgs bound args 0 acc (bsParamTy bound (bsParamAlias bound n)))
        ; An OPAQUE local: the value came out of memory, so no call edge
        ; carries what it performs and `markEparam` had no parameter to
        ; mark. This is the `let`-shaped spelling of the same hole the
        ; spine walk records for a non-name head.
        (if (bsIsOpaque (bsParamAlias bound n))
          (effAdd acc effIncomplete)
          0)
      })
    (if (== (isSyscallPrim n) 1)
      ; The syscall is an EDGE as well as an effect. It short-circuits
      ; above `findFnEnt` - deliberately, `isSyscallPrim` is a literal
      ; seven-way match - so without this the bottom of every IO chain
      ; in the library carried `#effects=IO` and no edge explaining it,
      ; and a reader could not tell a syscall leaf from an `extern`
      ; whose row is constructed rather than walked. It is `Unsafe` as
      ; well (`isUnsafePrim`): the kernel reads and writes through its
      ; arguments, and the short-circuit skips the call-site rule below
      ; that adds `Unsafe` for every other primitive in the set.
      {
        (tcNoteCall tc n)
        (effAdd acc (builtinEff "IO"))
        (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Unsafe"))
      }
      {
        (let entFound (findFnEnt tc n))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (walkCallEnt tc n ent args bound acc unboxed true)
          {
            (if (&& (== unboxed 0) (> (ctorAllocArity tc n) 0))
              (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc")))
            ; A construction stores each argument into its field: one
            ; that declares its effects checks the value it is given.
            (fieldStoreCtorCheck tc n args bound)
            ; ...except that a name AX3001 refused does reach here:
            ; the effect walk runs after a body's type errors, not
            ; instead of them. Its call is unresolved (ERR-DIAG-3).
            (if (tcIsUnknownCallee tc n)
              (effAdd acc effPoison))
            0
          })
      })))

; One call to a function the walk resolved, `ent` under the name `n`:
; the edge, the pair box, the callee's row, a primitive's `Unsafe`,
; the transparent positions instantiated with `args`, and an
; over-application's hole. A call through a name and a call through a
; local known to hold that function (`FnVals`) are the same call.
(pub :: walkCallEnt (-> TC String FnEnt Nodes BScope Strings Int Bool Int))
(pub fn (walkCallEnt tc n ent args bound acc unboxed certain)
  {
    (noteLambdaCast bound (fnLambdaCast ent) certain)
    (tcNoteCall tc (fnEntName ent))
    ; A signature with no definition (AX3015) has no body to give it a
    ; row, so the call is unresolved, not effect-free (ERR-DIAG-3).
    (if (tcIsUnknownCallee tc n)
      (effAdd acc effPoison))
    ; A PAIR FUNCTION'S ANSWER IS BOXED BY ITS CALLER, at every
    ; call that is not a recorded site: the callee's row no
    ; longer carries the block its tail leaves used to build,
    ; so the block is charged here, where `emitPlainCall`
    ; builds it. A site the emitter reads from registers - a
    ; matched direct call, a forwarding leaf - is charged
    ; nothing.
    (if (&& (== unboxed 0) (== (tcIsPairEnt tc ent) 1))
      (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc")))
    ; The callee's row, less its `Unsafe` when it is a trusted
    ; encapsulation (`effUnionCallee`).
    (effUnionCallee acc ent 0)
    ; A raw-memory primitive performs `Unsafe` alongside whatever
    ; `Mut` or `Alloc` its seeded row already carries: `regFnEff`
    ; holds one effect, so the second is added here, where the
    ; row is read.
    (if (== (isUnsafePrim n) 1)
      (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Unsafe")))
    ; Instantiate the callee's transparent positions
    ; with what this call passes there.
    (instantiateEparams bound ent (fnEntEparams ent) args 0 acc)
    ; OVER-APPLICATION. More arguments than the callee's own
    ; parameters means the callee's RESULT is applied to the
    ; rest - `((handoff 1) n)` spines to `handoff` with two
    ; arguments against one - and that result is a value the
    ; walk cannot follow: what calling it performs is nobody's
    ; row. So the call is incomplete, the same hole a call
    ; through an opaque local records, and the row an honest
    ; lower bound. It used to inherit only the callee's row,
    ; which for `handoff` is `shout`'s effects as POSSIBLE -
    ; and an untagged `g` calling it read `#effects=IO
    ; #effects-overapprox`, checked clean, and printed.
    ; An unknown arity cannot establish over-application. Builtins use
    ; their declared arrow depth; unresolved signatures skip this test.
    (if (let (Some arity) (repArity ent))
      (if (> (vecLen args) arity)
        (effAdd acc effIncomplete)
        0)
      0)
  })

; BindingSource separates parameter aliases, attributed values, opaque
; locals and known function-value table entries. An opaque binding keeps
; its unresolved effect contribution when called or passed onward.
(pub :: bsOpaque BindingSource)
(pub fn (bsOpaque)
  BoundOpaque)

; Every argument handed to a call the walk could not resolve to a
; function. `acc` is 0 at the sites where the CALLEE is not known to
; call its arguments; where it is, an unaccounted value is a hole and
; the sentinel records it.
;
; `cty` IS THE CALLEE'S OWN DECLARED TYPE, and it is what decides
; whether an unfollowed argument is a hole at all. The rule is
; `paramCallablesOf`'s, asked one level down: "an arrow, a type
; variable or poison can hold a callable value; a concrete `Int`
; cannot, and marking one would claim a transparency the signature
; forbids." An `Int` position cannot be applied - the checker refuses
; it, `AX3004 expected function type` - so no effect can hide in a
; value handed there, however little the walk knows about the value.
;
; Without this the mark fired on the ARGUMENTS' shape alone, and the
; standard library's own sort was the casualty. Measured on this tree
; before the type was consulted:
;
;     F vecSiftDownBy ... #effects=Mut #effects-incomplete #effect-params=cmp
;     F vecSortBy     ... #effects=Mut #effects-incomplete #effect-params=cmp
;
; `vecSiftDownBy` calls `(cmp (memGetWord d r) (memGetWord d k))`, and
; `cmp` is declared `(-> Int Int Int)`. Both arguments are loads, so
; `escapeValue` answered 0 for both and the row was published as a
; LOWER BOUND - which is a claim that something might be hiding in two
; machine words the signature says are integers. A `pure` claim over
; anything reaching `vecSortBy` drew `AX3037` on the strength of it.
;
; `escapeValue` IS STILL CALLED for every argument, non-callable
; positions included, because it also marks a parameter alias
; effect-transparent and that mark is an over-approximation the walk
; is required to keep. Only the sentinel is gated.
;
; TYPE ALIASES WERE THE QUESTION WORTH PROBING, since a name in a
; signature could stand for an arrow while reading as a `TCon`. Both
; kinds were measured rather than argued:
;
;   (type Fn1 (-> Int Int))     expanded before checking, so the
;                                 signature already holds the arrow and
;                                 `tyIsCallable` sees it - the row is
;                                 still a lower bound;
;   (type Fn2 a (-> a a))       expanded at its type arguments;
;                                 the row follows the resulting arrow.
;
; A `data` or `struct` name is nominal: applying one is `AX3004
; expected function type`, and handing an arrow to one is `AX3004`
; too. So no well-typed program can put a function where a nominal
; type is declared, which is the same fact this whole rule rests on.
; Missing signature or argument-position evidence stays callable, so an
; unfollowed value at that position keeps the row incomplete.
(pub :: parameterMayBeCallable (-> (Option ASTNode) Int Bool))
(pub fn (parameterMayBeCallable signature position)
  (match signature
    ((None) true)
    ((Some declared)
      (match (arrowParamTy declared position)
        ((None) true)
        ((Some parameter) (== (tyIsCallable parameter) 1)))
    )))

(pub :: escapeArgs (-> BScope Nodes Int Strings (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (escapeArgs bound args i acc cty)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (if (&& (== (escapeValue bound (vecGet args i)) 0)
            (&& (!= (as acc Int) 0) (parameterMayBeCallable cty i)))
        (effAdd acc effIncomplete))
      (escapeArgs bound args (+ i 1) acc cty)
    }))

; The callee declared these positions effect-transparent, which is to
; say IT CALLS THEM. A value the walk cannot follow, landing in one, is
; called and unattributed - no guessing required about whether the
; position is a called one, because that is what the mark means.
(pub :: instantiateEparams (-> BScope FnEnt Ints Nodes Int Strings Int))
(pub fn (instantiateEparams bound ent marks args i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen marks))
    0
    {
      (if (< (vecGet marks i) (vecLen args))
        (if (==
              (escapeValueUse bound (vecGet args (vecGet marks i)) (epUseFor ent (vecGet marks i)))
              0)
          (effAdd acc effIncomplete)))
      (instantiateEparams bound ent marks args (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The call graph the effect fixpoint already walks.
;
; `inferEffects` resolves every reference site to a `FnEnt` and folds
; that entry's effect row into the caller's - `effRefSite` for a bare
; name, `walkCallHead` for an application head. The EDGE it resolved to
; get there was then dropped on the floor, which is why AXSYM could say
; `#effects=IO` and not which call put the IO there.
;
; This records it. The accumulator is a word on `tc` rather than a
; parameter threaded through the walk for the same reason the current
; module is (word 16): the walk is fifteen mutually recursive functions
; and a sixteenth parameter on every one of them would be a large
; diff to carry one fact that is constant for a whole declaration.
; Word 29 is 0 everywhere except inside `inferEffectsPass`, so the
; other `collectEffects` callers - `checkHandle`'s discharge check -
; record nothing, which is right: they are asking what an EXPRESSION
; performs, and an expression has no entry to hang an edge on.
;
; The name recorded is the RESOLVED entry's, not the spelling at the
; reference site: `(memGetWordStr ent 0)` is `Mod$name` where the
; checker mangled it, so an edge names which `writeStr` was meant
; rather than repeating an ambiguity AXSYM's bare `F` rows already
; have. It is also exactly the symbol codegen emits, so `#calls=` and
; the emitted `@Mod$name` cross-check.
;
; Deduplicated on the way in, because the fixpoint re-walks every body
; once per round until nothing grows - four rounds over this compiler -
; and an edge list that grew per round would be a set rendered as a
; multiset.
(pub :: tcNoteCall (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (tcNoteCall tc name)
  (if (let (Some acc) (tcCurCalls tc))
    (if (== (strInVec acc name 0) 1)
      0
      {
        (vecPush acc name)
        1
      })
    0))

; What naming `n` contributes when it is NOT a call head: stage0's
; reference-site rule, which is what keeps a function value's effects
; from escaping attribution entirely. A lexically bound name
; contributes nothing - its initialiser was walked where it was
; bound.
; Does this type take an argument? `TAG_T_ARR` is the only shape that
; does, and a `FnEnt`'s `paramCount` cannot answer it: every primitive
; is registered with `None` there, so `__argc` and `__syscall3` would be
; indistinguishable.
(pub :: tyIsArrow (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsArrow t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
      1
      0)))

; A bare reference in a position where the walk cannot see the value
; being CALLED. An arrow-typed referent's row arrives as POSSIBLE.
(pub :: effRefSite (-> TC String BScope Strings Int))
(pub fn (effRefSite tc n bound acc)
  (effRefSiteAt tc n bound acc 1))

; The same rule with the possible spelling SUPPRESSED - the referent's
; row arrives definite - for a reference the walk can see being handed
; to something that calls it.
;
; `mark` is 0 at exactly one place: an ARGUMENT of a call. Passing
; `shout` to `(apply shout n)` is not naming a function and walking
; away - it is handing it to a body that applies it, and `apply`'s own
; row says so (`#effect-params=f`). Reading that as possible
; suppressed AX3042 for the whole call chain: measured, `(fn (main)
; (sneaky 1))` compiled clean, printed "IO HAPPENED" at run time, and
; `symbols` reported `#effects=IO #effects-overapprox` on `main` - the
; walk KNEW, and the marker was the only reason nothing said so.
;
; The shape the possible spelling exists for survives: `(fn (handoff
; k) shout)` RETURNS an effectful function without calling it, and a
; bare name in that position still arrives possible. The two differ by
; whether the walk can see a call, which is exactly what argument
; position tells it.
(pub :: effRefSiteAt (-> TC String BScope Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effRefSiteAt tc n bound acc mark)
  (if (== (bsIsLocal bound n) 1)
    0
    (if (== (isSyscallPrim n) 1)
      {
        (tcNoteCall tc n)
        (effAdd acc (builtinEff "IO"))
        (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Unsafe"))
      }
      {
        (let entFound (findFnEnt tc n))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          {
            (tcNoteCall tc (fnEntName ent))
            ; NULLARY OR NOT is the whole question, and it is the
            ; difference between naming a function and calling one.
            ;
            ; This language has no argument list on a nullary call:
            ; `vecNew`, `sysArgc` and `__argc` are INVOKED by writing
            ; their name, so a bare reference to one is a call and its
            ; effects are this body's. That is why this rule unions
            ; them, and for a nullary referent it is exact.
            ;
            ; A referent that takes arguments is a different thing. A
            ; bare `shout` where `shout` is `(-> Int Int)` is a VALUE -
            ; nothing is performed, and unioning its effects reported
            ; `(fn (handoff k) shout)` as performing IO. That shape is
            ; the one `docs/agent-harness.md` §6 named as the reason
            ; `AX3010` could not be promoted to an error: "promoting
            ; that shape refuses correct programs". Routing it here is
            ; what removed that objection, and AX3010 was promoted on
            ; 2026-08-25.
            ;
            ; So an arrow-typed referent's row arrives as POSSIBLE
            ; (`effUnionPossible`): the value escaped, the walk cannot
            ; bound what is done with it, and each effect it carries
            ; is one the body MAY perform rather than one it does. A
            ; `pure` claim over it draws `AX3037` - cannot be checked -
            ; which is the diagnostic that was always right for it,
            ; and `AX3011` still sees the upper bound it needs.
            ;
            ; Per EFFECT, not per row. This used to add a row-global
            ; `?:byref` marker beside a definite union, and every
            ; reader of the marker excused the whole row - a definite
            ; IO from a call was excused by a reference that had
            ; nothing to do with it. The header above `effIsPossible`
            ; records the measurement.
            ;
            ; Measured over `stdlib/` and `core/` when the
            ; reference-site rule was corrected: ZERO rows lose an
            ; effect. Nothing in this repository names an arrow-typed
            ; function without calling it.
            (effUnionCallee acc ent
              (if (&& (== mark 1) (== (tyIsArrow (fnEntTy ent)) 1))
                1
                0))
            ; A raw-memory primitive named without being called carries
            ; `Unsafe` the way its seeded row carries `Mut` or `Alloc`:
            ; possible where the row arrives possible, definite where
            ; it arrives definite.
            (if (== (isUnsafePrim n) 1)
              (if (&& (== mark 1) (== (tyIsArrow (fnEntTy ent)) 1))
                (effAdd acc (effPossibleOf (builtinEff "Unsafe")))
                (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Unsafe"))))
            ; A pair function reached by NAME is reached through `@F`,
            ; the boxing wrapper: a nullary one called by its bare name
            ; boxes here, definitely; an arrow-typed one taken as a
            ; value boxes wherever it is later applied, which is the
            ; same possible/definite split the row above just took.
            (if (== (tcIsPairEnt tc ent) 1)
              (if (&& (== mark 1) (== (tyIsArrow (fnEntTy ent)) 1))
                (effUnionPossible acc (tcAllocRow) 0)
                (effAdd acc (builtinEff "Alloc")))
              0)
          }
          0)
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; AX3010: an AXTAG claim the body does not support. An ERROR since
; 2026-08-25 - a tag is a claim its author WROTE, and a build that
; ships a false one has published a guarantee it does not keep. It
; fires only where the walk has an ANSWER; the shapes it is not in a
; position to check are routed to AX3037 below, which stays a warning
; and is what made this promotion sound.
;
; Three forms, all of stage0's:
;   `;@axiom:effect(pure)`          -> "`pure` claim contradicted: body performs X, Y"
;   `;@axiom:effect(pure)`  -> "`effect(pure)` claim contradicted: ..."
;   `;@axiom:effect(v)`     -> "`effect(v)` claim unsupported: missing X"
; and the rendered message is prefixed "AXTAG mismatch on `name`: ".
; Effects are joined ", " SORTED BY THEIR DISPLAY NAME, because
; stage0 collects into a HashSet and sorts by `format!("{}")` before
; rendering - insertion order would be a different string.
;
; The gate that makes this sound: stage1 does not model effect
; polymorphism, so its effect sets are a SUBSET of stage0's, and a
; subset is fine for a per-effect diagnostic like AX3011 (fewer
; lines) but fatal here, where the set is rendered INTO the message -
; "body performs IO" against "body performs IO, Mut" is a
; mis-report, not an omission. So the walk records whether it saw a
; call it could not resolve: a head that is a lexically bound name,
; or a name with no entry at all. Those are precisely the calls that
; make stage0's `effect_params` non-empty, and when there are none,
; stage1's concrete set equals stage0's and its `effect_params` is
; empty - which is also the condition stage0 uses to decide whether a
; missing claimed effect is a lie. Anything else stays silent.
(pub :: effIncomplete String)
(pub fn (effIncomplete)
  "?:incomplete")

; The marker for a call to a name with no body: an undefined name
; (AX3001) or a signature with no definition (AX3015). Like
; `effIncomplete` it makes the row a lower bound, and unlike it, the
; diagnostic that explains the gap is already out (ERR-DIAG-3).
(pub :: effPoison String)
(pub fn (effPoison)
  "?:poison")

; Did the walk meet a call it could not resolve? Then this set is a
; LOWER BOUND on what the body performs, and every claim checked
; against it has to answer for the difference rather than read an
; absence as an acquittal.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; POSSIBLE effects. A row distinguishes what a body DEFINITELY performs
; from what it MAY perform through a value it named or a dispatch the
; walk could not resolve. The second kind is spelled with a `~` in
; front of the effect's own tag - `~b:IO`, `~c:Ask` - and rides the
; same Vec, so `effUnion` carries it into every caller unchanged: an
; effect that is only possible in a callee is only possible in the
; caller.
;
; This replaced the row-global `?:byref` marker on 2026-08-29, and the
; difference is the whole of the AX3042 bypass it closes. `?:byref`
; said "SOME of this row arrived by reference", and every consumer
; that read it excused the WHOLE row: `shout`, printing `"n={n}"`,
; carried the marker because `show` has four implementations, and the
; definite IO from `writeStr` in the same row was excused by an
; over-approximation about `Alloc` and `Mut` that had nothing to do
; with it. Measured: an untagged `relay` calling `shout` checked OK and
; printed at run time, while the same `relay` over a literal `println`
; drew AX3042. Per effect, an excuse reaches only the effect it is
; about.
;
; Two sites produce the spelling, exactly the two that produced the
; marker: `effRefSiteAt` for a bare arrow-typed name outside argument
; position, and `traitUnionEffects` for a method with more than one
; implementation. Both spellings of one effect may be present at once
; - a body that calls `shout` and also names it - and the predicates
; below are what every consumer asks instead of raw `effIn`:
;
;   effDefinite v X      `b:X`/`c:X` present - the body performs X
;   effPossible v X      `~b:X`/`~c:X` present - it may
;   effMay v X           either - the upper bound AX3011 needs
;   effPartial v         `?:incomplete` or `?:poison`, a LOWER bound
;   effUnresolved v      `?:incomplete` alone: a lower bound no
;                        diagnostic has explained yet
;
; `X` is always the DEFINITE spelling (`builtinEff`/`customEff`); the
; possible spelling is derived here and never written by a caller.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: effIsPossible (-> String Int))
(pub fn (effIsPossible e)
  (if (== (strByte e 0) 126)
    1
    0))

; The possible spelling of an entry. A marker has no possible form -
; a lower bound named without being called is still a lower bound on
; what calling it would do - and travels as itself.
(pub :: effPossibleOf (-> String String))
(pub fn (effPossibleOf e)
  (if (== (effIsPossible e) 1)
    e
    (if (== (effIsMarker e) 1)
      e
      (concat "~" e))))

(pub :: effDefiniteOf (-> String String))
(pub fn (effDefiniteOf e)
  (if (== (effIsPossible e) 1)
    (strSlice e 1 (- (strLen e) 1))
    e))

(pub :: effDefinite (-> Strings String Int))
(pub fn (effDefinite v x)
  (effIn v x))

(pub :: effPossible (-> Strings String Int))
(pub fn (effPossible v x)
  (effIn v (effPossibleOf x)))

(pub :: effMay (-> Strings String Int))
(pub fn (effMay v x)
  (if (== (effIn v x) 1)
    1
    (effPossible v x)))

; Is some member of the row possible and not definite? This is what
; `symbols` renders as `#effects-overapprox`, and `effPossibleNames`
; is the `#effects-possible=` list beside it.
(pub :: effAnyPossibleOnly (-> Strings Int))
(pub fn (effAnyPossibleOnly v)
  (effAnyPossibleOnlyFrom v 0))

(pub :: effAnyPossibleOnlyFrom (-> Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effAnyPossibleOnlyFrom v i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (let e (vecGetStr v i))
      (if (&& (== (effIsPossible e) 1) (== (effIn v (effDefiniteOf e)) 0))
        1
        (effAnyPossibleOnlyFrom v (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The definite entries alone, in row order: what a claim of absence is
; contradicted BY, and what `effJoinDefinite` renders into the AX3010
; message - a possible effect in that sentence would be a mis-report.
(pub :: effDefiniteOnly (-> Strings Strings))
(pub fn (effDefiniteOnly v)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (effDefiniteOnlyInto out v 0)
      out
    }
  })

(pub :: effDefiniteOnlyInto (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effDefiniteOnlyInto out v i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      {
        (let e (vecGetStr v i))
        (if (! (|| (== (effIsMarker e) 1) (== (effIsPossible e) 1)))
          (vecPushStr out e))
      }
      (effDefiniteOnlyInto out v (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: effHasDefinite (-> Strings Int))
(pub fn (effHasDefinite v)
  (if (> (vecLen (effDefiniteOnly v)) 0)
    1
    0))

(pub :: effJoinDefinite (-> Strings String))
(pub fn (effJoinDefinite v)
  (effJoin (effDefiniteOnly v)))

; Union `src` into `acc` as POSSIBLE: every definite entry arrives in
; its possible spelling, a possible one stays possible, and the
; incompleteness marker travels as itself. Answers 1 when anything was
; added, as `effUnion` does.
(pub :: effUnionPossible (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effUnionPossible acc src i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    0
    {
      (let rest (effUnionPossible acc src (+ i 1)))
      (if (== (effAdd acc (effPossibleOf (vecGetStr src i))) 1)
        1
        rest)
    }))

; A callee's row as a call site receives it (R-B6, `MM-EXEC-9d`):
; unioned whole, definite or `possible` as the site decides, EXCEPT
; that a TRUSTED encapsulation's `Unsafe` stays with it. That is what
; the bare `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)` tag means - the author vouches that
; every well-typed call is safe - and it is what lets
; `restrict(no-unsafe)` admit a body that pushes to a `Vec`: `vecPush`
; performs the raw store, its callers do not.
(pub :: effUnionCallee (-> Strings FnEnt Int Int))
(pub fn (effUnionCallee acc ent possible)
  (if (== (fnEntUnsafe ent) 1)
    (effUnionTrusted acc (fnEntEffects ent) possible 0)
    (if (== possible 1)
      (effUnionPossible acc (fnEntEffects ent) 0)
      (effUnion acc (fnEntEffects ent) 0))))

(pub :: effUnionTrusted (-> Strings Strings Int Int Int))
(pub fn (effUnionTrusted acc src possible i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    0
    {
      (let e (vecGetStr src i))
      {
        (if (! (strEq (effDefiniteOf e) (builtinEff "Unsafe")))
          (effAdd acc
            (if (== possible 1)
              (effPossibleOf e)
              e)))
        (effUnionTrusted acc src possible (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; Is this entry a MARKER rather than an effect?
;
; A marker rides the same Vec as effects, tagged `?:` where a builtin
; is `b:` and a custom one `c:`. The rule every consumer has to follow
; is in `checkUnhandled`'s own comment - "every consumer that renders
; an effect has to skip it". There is one marker since 2026-08-29:
; `?:byref` became the per-effect possible spelling above, which is
; an EFFECT to every renderer and is not skipped.
(pub :: effIsMarker (-> String Int))
(pub fn (effIsMarker e)
  (if (|| (strEq e effIncomplete) (|| (strEq e effPoison) (strEq e effGraphOpen)))
    1
    0))

; Is this row a lower bound? A call the walk could not resolve, or one
; to a name with no body (`effPoison`), may perform anything, so a claim
; read against the row has no answer for what is absent.
(pub :: effPartial (-> Strings Int))
(pub fn (effPartial v)
  (if (== (effIn v effIncomplete) 1)
    1
    (effIn v effPoison)))

; Is it a lower bound for a reason no diagnostic has named yet? Only
; then does "this cannot be checked" tell the reader something: a row
; that is partial through poison alone sits beside the error that made
; it so (ERR-DIAG-3).
(pub :: effUnresolved (-> Strings Int))
(pub fn (effUnresolved v)
  (effIn v effIncomplete))

; The concrete effects, sorted by display name, joined with ", ".
(pub :: effJoin (-> Strings String))
(pub fn (effJoin v)
  (effJoinFrom (effSorted v)))

(:: effJoinFrom (-> Strings String))
(fn (effJoinFrom v)
  {
    (let out strBuilderNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen v)
      {
        (if (> i 0)
          (strBuilderPush out ", "))
        (strBuilderPush out (effRender (vecGet v i)))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

; Insertion sort by rendered name - the sets are three or four
; entries, and this has to agree with stage0's `sort_by_key` on the
; same strings.
(pub :: effSorted (-> Strings Strings))
(pub fn (effSorted v)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (effSortInto out v 0)
      out
    }
  })

(pub :: effSortInto (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
; Deduplicated BY NAME: a row may carry both spellings of one effect
; (`b:IO` and `~b:IO`), and it is one effect to every sentence that
; renders this.
(pub fn (effSortInto out v i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (if (! (== (effIsMarker (vecGetStr v i)) 1))
        (if (! (== (effNameIn out (effRender (vecGetStr v i)) 0) 1))
          (effInsertSorted out (vecGet v i))))
      (effSortInto out v (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: effNameIn (-> Strings String Int Int))
(pub fn (effNameIn v name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    (if (strEq (effRender (vecGetStr v i)) name)
      1
      (effNameIn v name (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: effInsertSorted (-> Strings String Int))
(pub fn (effInsertSorted out e)
  {
    (let n (vecLen out))
    {
      (vecPush out e)
      (effShiftDown out n)
    }
  })

(pub :: effShiftDown (-> Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effShiftDown out i)
  (if (<= i 0)
    0
    (if (< (strCmp (effRender (vecGetStr out i)) (effRender (vecGetStr out (- i 1)))) 0)
      {
        {
          (let tmp (vecGet out i))
          {
            (vecSet out i (vecGet out (- i 1)))
            (vecSet out (- i 1) tmp)
          }
        }
        (effShiftDown out (- i 1))
      }
      0)))

; The syscall primitives are the IO ones; every other `__` primitive
; is pure. stage0 drives this off PRIMITIVES' `is_io` column.
(pub :: isSyscallPrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isSyscallPrim n)
  (if (strEq n "__syscall0")
    1
    (if (strEq n "__syscall1")
      1
      (if (strEq n "__syscall2")
        1
        (if (strEq n "__syscall3")
          1
          (if (strEq n "__syscall4")
            1
            (if (strEq n "__syscall5")
              1
              (if (strEq n "__syscall6")
                1
                0))))))))

; The raw-memory primitives are the Unsafe ones: every primitive that
; reads, writes, frees or jumps through a word the type system does not
; bound. `walkCallHead` adds `Unsafe` at a call to one, alongside
; whatever `Mut` or `Alloc` its seeded row already carries, and
; `graphSeeds` answers them the way it answers a syscall for `IO`.
;
; SEVEN NAMES WERE HERE UNTIL 2026-09-27, and this comment called the
; set "closed by construction: nothing else reads or writes memory the
; checker cannot see". It was not closed. Probed with one declaration
; per primitive under `restrict(no-unsafe)`, only `__load64` was
; refused; these passed, and each takes an arbitrary `Int`:
;
;   `__retain`, `__release`  write the count word 16 bytes below it,
;                            and a release that reaches zero FILES the
;                            block - a forged word becomes an address
;                            the allocator later hands out;
;   `__call_word`            calls it as a closure record's code;
;   `__atomic_load/_store/_add/_cas`  dereference it as a byte address;
;   `__axiom_arena_reset`, `__axiom_arena_reset_keeping`  rewind the
;                            allocator to it, reclaiming everything
;                            allocated since (MM-ALLOC-16's obligation).
;
; `pure` accepted `__retain` for the same reason. `__fence` touches no
; address and `__retainref` takes a TYPED value, so neither is here;
; `__axiom_arena_mark` only reads the position, and `__axiom_recover`
; arms at a mark its caller just took. `region` and `parallel` never
; reach this list: the region form is its own node and lowers to the
; runtime directly, and `parallel` desugars to the spawn primitives
; (`tests/diagnostics/1010-unsafe-primitives.ax`, MM-EXEC-9c).
;
; TEN MORE JOINED ON THE SAME DAY with the device primitives
; (`isUnsafeDevicePrim`, R-D2): twenty-six in all. They were Unsafe
; from their first commit rather than found outside the set later,
; and `1010-unsafe-primitives.ax` refuses each of them too.
;
; THE SEVEN SYSCALL PRIMITIVES JOINED with R-B10's third step (AN-58),
; which with the handle table's three (`isUnsafeHandlePrim`) makes
; thirty-six. A syscall reads and writes this process's memory through
; its arguments: `read` fills the buffer it is handed, `open` reads the
; path, `munmap` frees a mapping. Outside the set, a
; `restrict(no-unsafe)` function could write `(__syscall3 sysRandomNum
; 4096 64 0)` and have the kernel store 64 bytes at address 4096, and
; `pure` accepted a body whose only work was a syscall (the IO, not the
; memory, was what refused it). In the set, a syscall is an unsafe
; operation like `__store64`: its declaration says `effect(unsafe)`
; (AX3073), `no-unsafe` refuses it (AX3049), `pure` refuses it
; (AX3010), and its `Unsafe` supports an `effect(unsafe)` claim through
; the row, which is what `unsafeByKernel` answered separately while the
; seed could not accept the tags (`1080-unsafe-syscalls.ax`).
(pub :: isUnsafePrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isUnsafePrim n)
  (if (|| (strEq n "__resource_new")
        (|| (strEq n "__resource_get")
          (|| (strEq n "__resource_close")
            (|| (strEq n "__resource_disarm") (strEq n "__resource_unwinding")))))
    1
    (if (== (isUnsafeRawPrim n) 1)
      1
      (if (== (isAsmPrim n) 1)
        1
        (if (== (isUnsafeWordPrim n) 1)
          1
          (if (== (isUnsafeHandlePrim n) 1)
            1
            (if (== (isSyscallPrim n) 1)
              1
              (isUnsafeDevicePrim n))))))))

; The members of `isUnsafePrim` that take no argument, so that naming
; one performs it. A bare name of any other member has no value to take
; and is AX3013 already.
(pub :: isNullaryUnsafePrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isNullaryUnsafePrim n)
  (if (strEq n "__resource_unwinding")
    1
    0))

; Inline assembly (MM-FFI-9): the instructions may read, write or jump
; anywhere, which is this set's definition at its widest.
(pub :: isAsmPrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isAsmPrim n)
  (if (&& (strStartsWith n "__asm.")
        (&& (> (strLen n) 6) (&& (>= (strByte n 6) 48) (<= (strByte n 6) 57))))
    1
    0))

;; An `asm` form's `sym` operand, `(__asm.sym f)` (MM-FFI-9), which
;; names `f` for the instructions to call and answers the `Int` the
;; primitive takes. The name is looked up, never checked as a value:
;; a function of two parameters is no bare value (AX3013) and is still
;; one `bl` reaches. The expander has refused a local binding already.
;; What is refused here is a name that resolves to nothing (AX3001, or
;; AX3023 for a private one), to something with no symbol of its own -
;; an operator or primitive, an effect operation - or to a polymorphic
;; function, which takes a hidden evidence word the instructions would
;; have to supply.
(pub :: checkAsmSym (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkAsmSym tc a)
  (if (!= (nodeTag a) TAG_E_VAR)
    mkIntTy
    {
      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName a)))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        (if (|| (!= (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0) (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 1))
          (checkAsmSymBad tc a
            (concat (concat "`" (srcSpelling (nodeAName a)))
              "` has no symbol of its own; a `sym` operand names a function the program defines, or an `extern` item"))
          (if (== (tyHasSrcVar (fnEntTy ent)) 1)
            (checkAsmSymBad tc a
              (concat (concat "`" (srcSpelling (nodeAName a)))
                "` is polymorphic, so it takes a hidden evidence word the instructions would have to supply; name a function whose signature has no type variable"))
            mkIntTy))
        {
          (emitUndefinedVar tc (nodeAName a) (nodeSpan a))
          mkIntTy
        })
    }))

(pub :: checkAsmSymBad (-> TC ASTNode String ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkAsmSymBad tc a msg)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3091" "inline-asm" (nodeSpan a) msg None
        (Some
          "an `asm` form's `(sym name f)` passes the symbol of `f`, a top-level `fn` or an `extern` item, for instructions such as `bl {name}` or `call {name}`")
        "run `axiom explain AX3091` for the full form"))
    mkIntTy
  })

; The handle table's three (MM-PAR-8). Each takes or answers an
; ADDRESS its caller will dereference: the checker cannot see what that
; address holds, which is the whole of this set's definition. A library
; that wraps them in a word struct - `Chan`, `Mutex`, `CancelToken` -
; says `effect(unsafe)` on the private helpers that call them and
; offers a safe interface above.
(pub :: isUnsafeHandlePrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isUnsafeHandlePrim n)
  (if (strEq n "__handle_new")
    1
    (if (strEq n "__handle_get")
      1
      (if (strEq n "__handle_free")
        1
        0))))

; The original seven address and allocation primitives. The lexical
; declaration check and the transitive effect walk both read the full
; `isUnsafePrim` set; this helper only keeps the three families legible.
; The seed now recognizes the word primitives too, so stdlib wrappers
; can declare their direct raw operations without breaking bootstrap.
(pub :: isUnsafeRawPrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isUnsafeRawPrim n)
  (if (strEq n "__load8")
    1
    (if (strEq n "__store8")
      1
      (if (strEq n "__store8v")
        1
        (if (strEq n "__load64")
          1
          (if (strEq n "__store64")
            1
            (if (strEq n "__alloc")
              1
              (if (strEq n "__addr")
                1
                0))))))))

; The nine the 2026-09-27 probe found outside the set, kept in their
; own function so the list above reads as the seven it always was and
; this one as the nine that joined it.
(pub :: isUnsafeWordPrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isUnsafeWordPrim n)
  (if (|| (strEq n "__retain") (strEq n "__release"))
    1
    (if (strEq n "__call_word")
      1
      (if (|| (strEq n "__atomic_load")
            (|| (strEq n "__atomic_store") (|| (strEq n "__atomic_add") (strEq n "__atomic_cas"))))
        1
        (if (|| (strEq n "__axiom_arena_mark_into")
              (|| (strEq n "__axiom_arena_reset") (strEq n "__axiom_arena_reset_keeping")))
          1
          0)))))

; The ten device primitives of 2026-09-27 (R-D2, `tcRegDevicePrims`):
; the eight volatile accesses, which load or store at an arbitrary
; word exactly as `__load8`/`__store8v` do, and the two cache
; operations, which clean or invalidate the line holding one - an
; `__arm_dc_civac` of a word the program does not own discards nothing
; but still faults on an address nothing maps. The barriers, the
; counter and timer registers, the interrupt mask and the thread-ID
; register touch no address and are not here, for `__fence`'s reason.
; The seed does not know these names, so no module the compiler
; imports may spell one (`stdlib/Mmio.ax`, `Arm.ax` and `Dma.ax` are
; new modules and are never compiled by it).
(pub :: isUnsafeDevicePrim (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isUnsafeDevicePrim n)
  (if (|| (strEq n "__vload8")
        (|| (strEq n "__vload16") (|| (strEq n "__vload32") (strEq n "__vload64"))))
    1
    (if (|| (strEq n "__vstore8")
          (|| (strEq n "__vstore16") (|| (strEq n "__vstore32") (strEq n "__vstore64"))))
      1
      (if (|| (strEq n "__arm_dc_cvac") (strEq n "__arm_dc_civac"))
        1
        0))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The walk's scope. `names` is the shadow list - one entry per `let`,
; lambda parameter or match binder, carrying the PARAMETER INDEX it
; aliases or the binding source - while `params`, `callable` and `eparams` are
; constant for the whole walk and shared by every derived scope.
;
; Parameters are deliberately NOT in `names`: stage0 keeps them in a
; separate list so `param_index` can still find one, and a parameter
; masked as a shadow of itself would be invisible to the
; effect-transparency marks that are the whole point of tracking it.
(pub data BindingSource
  (BoundParam Int)
  (BoundAttributed)
  (BoundOpaque)
  (BoundValues Int))

(pub struct BEnt
  (name : String)
  (source : BindingSource))

(pub :: bEntName (-> BEnt String))
(pub fn (bEntName ent)
  ent.name)

(pub :: bEntSource (-> BEnt BindingSource))
(pub fn (bEntSource ent)
  ent.source)

; `ctx` is where the walk stands, in a one-element vector every scope a
; binding derives shares, so a `let` copies one word for it.
(pub struct BScope
  (names : (Vec BEnt))
  (mut params : Strings)
  (callable : Ints)
  (eparams : (Vec Int))
  (fnty : (Option ASTNode))
  (ctx : (Vec BCtx)))

; `handled` is the custom effects a `handle` around this point
; discharges, inside the body being walked. `uses` records, per
; effect-transparent parameter, where the body calls it (`EpUse`).
; `inLam` is 1 while a lambda's body is walked for its own row: its
; parameter uses wait in its own `uses` until the lambda is called or
; escapes, since only then is the `handle` around them known. `vals` is
; the walk's table of function values a name is known to hold: a
; binding that holds entry k records BoundValues k (`bsWithVals`).
(pub struct BCtx
  (handled : Strings)
  (uses : (Vec EpUse))
  (inLam : Int)
  (vals : (Vec FnVals))
  (mut lambdaCast : LambdaCast))

(pub :: bsMake (-> (Vec BEnt) Strings Ints Ints (Option ASTNode) (Vec BCtx) BScope))
(pub fn (bsMake names params callable eparams fnty ctx)
  (BScope :names names :params params :callable callable :eparams eparams :fnty fnty :ctx ctx))

(pub :: bsCtxVec (-> BScope (Vec BCtx)))
(pub fn (bsCtxVec b)
  b.ctx)

(pub :: bsCtx (-> BScope BCtx))
(pub fn (bsCtx b)
  (vecGet (bsCtxVec b) 0))

(pub :: bsCtxOf (-> Strings (Vec EpUse) Int (Vec FnVals) (Vec BCtx)))
(pub fn (bsCtxOf handled uses inLam vals)
  {
    (let v (vecWithCapacity 1))
    {
      (vecPush v
        (BCtx :handled handled :uses uses :inLam inLam :vals vals :lambdaCast NoLambdaCast))
      v
    }
  })

(pub :: bsTypeOpt (-> ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (bsTypeOpt ty)
  (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
    None
    (Some ty)))

(pub :: bsNew (-> Strings Ints Ints (Option ASTNode) BScope))
(pub fn (bsNew params callable eparams fnty)
  (bsMake vecNew params callable eparams fnty
    (bsCtxOf (vecWithCapacity 1) (vecWithCapacity 1) 0 (vecWithCapacity 1))))

(pub :: bsNames (-> BScope (Vec BEnt)))
(pub fn (bsNames b)
  b.names)

(pub :: bsNamesVec (-> BScope (Vec BEnt)))
(pub fn (bsNamesVec b)
  b.names)

(pub :: bsParams (-> BScope Strings))
(pub fn (bsParams b)
  b.params)

(pub :: bsParamsVec (-> BScope Strings))
(pub fn (bsParamsVec b)
  (bsParams b))

(pub :: bsCallable (-> BScope Ints))
(pub fn (bsCallable b)
  b.callable)

(pub :: bsCallableVec (-> BScope Ints))
(pub fn (bsCallableVec b)
  (bsCallable b))

(pub :: bsEparams (-> BScope Ints))
(pub fn (bsEparams b)
  b.eparams)

(pub :: bsEparamsVec (-> BScope Ints))
(pub fn (bsEparamsVec b)
  b.eparams)

; The enclosing function's declared type, or None when it has none. Every
; derived scope shares it, exactly as `params` and `callable` do.
(pub :: bsFnTy (-> BScope (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (bsFnTy b)
  b.fnty)

; The declared type of a parameter alias. Other binding sources and
; scopes without a signature have no parameter type.
(pub :: bsParamTy (-> BScope BindingSource (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (bsParamTy b source)
  (if (let (Some index) (bsAliasParam source))
    (if (let (Some signature) (bsFnTy b))
      (arrowParamTy signature index)
      None)
    None))

(pub :: bsWith (-> BScope String BindingSource BScope))
(pub fn (bsWith b name source)
  {
    (let names vecNew)
    (let previous (bsNamesVec b))
    (for entry in previous
      (vecPush names entry))
    (vecPush names (BEnt :name name :source source))
    (bsMake names (bsParams b) (bsCallable b) (bsEparamsVec b) (bsFnTy b) (bsCtxVec b))
  })

(pub :: bsWithVals (-> BScope String FnVals BScope))
(pub fn (bsWithVals b n fv)
  {
    (let table (bsValsTable b))
    {
      (vecPush table fv)
      (bsWith b n (BoundValues (- (vecLen table) 1)))
    }
  })

(pub :: bsHandledVec (-> BScope Strings))
(pub fn (bsHandledVec b)
  {
    (let c (bsCtx b))
    c.handled
  })

(pub :: bsValsTable (-> BScope (Vec FnVals)))
(pub fn (bsValsTable b)
  {
    (let c (bsCtx b))
    c.vals
  })

(pub :: bsUsesVec (-> BScope (Vec EpUse)))
(pub fn (bsUsesVec b)
  {
    (let c (bsCtx b))
    c.uses
  })

(pub :: bsInLam (-> BScope Int))
(pub fn (bsInLam b)
  {
    (let c (bsCtx b))
    c.inLam
  })

; The function values the innermost binding of `n` is known to hold, or
; none: a parameter, an opaque local, or a name bound to anything the
; walk does not follow.
(pub :: bsValsOf (-> BScope String (Option FnVals)))
(pub fn (bsValsOf b name)
  (match (bsParamAlias b name)
    ((BoundValues index) (Some (vecGet (bsValsTable b) index)))
    ((BoundParam _) None)
    ((BoundAttributed) None)
    ((BoundOpaque) None)))

; The scope a `handle` body is walked in: the same names, with the
; custom effects this handle discharges (`effDischargedBy`'s test)
; added to `handled`.
(pub :: bsWithHandled (-> TC BScope (Vec ASTNode) BScope))
(pub fn (bsWithHandled tc b effs)
  {
    (let h vecNew)
    {
      (effUnion h (bsHandledVec b) 0)
      (handledInto tc h effs 0)
      (bsMake (bsNamesVec b) (bsParams b) (bsCallable b) (bsEparamsVec b) (bsFnTy b)
        (bsCtxOf h (bsUsesVec b) (bsInLam b) (bsValsTable b)))
    }
  })

(pub :: handledInto (-> TC Strings (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (handledInto tc h effs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    0
    {
      (let n (nodeAName (vecGet effs i)))
      {
        (if (&& (== (isBuiltinEffect n) 0) (isSome (findEffectDecl tc n)))
          (effAdd h (customEff n)))
        (handledInto tc h effs (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; The scope a lambda's body is walked in for its own row: its
; parameters bound opaque, nothing handled yet, and a `uses` of its own.
(pub :: bsLambda (-> BScope Names BScope))
(pub fn (bsLambda b params)
  {
    (let b1 (bsWithParams b params bsOpaque 0))
    (bsMake (bsNamesVec b1) (bsParams b1) (bsCallable b1) (bsEparamsVec b1) (bsFnTy b1)
      (bsCtxOf (vecWithCapacity 1) (vecWithCapacity 1) 1 (bsValsTable b1)))
  })

(pub :: copyInto (-> (Vec a) (Vec a) Int Int))
(pub fn (copyInto dst src i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    0
    {
      (vecPush dst (vecGet src i))
      (copyInto dst src (+ i 1))
    }))

; The LAST entry for `n`, which is the innermost binding.
(pub :: bsFind (-> BScope String (Option BEnt)))
(pub fn (bsFind b n)
  (bsFindFrom (bsNamesVec b) n (- (vecLen (bsNamesVec b)) 1)))

(pub :: bsFindFrom (-> (Vec BEnt) String Int (Option BEnt)))
(pub fn (bsFindFrom names name index)
  (if (< index 0)
    None
    {
      (let entry (vecGet names index))
      (if (strEq entry.name name)
        (Some entry)
        (bsFindFrom names name (- index 1)))
    }))

(pub :: nameIndexIn (-> Strings String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (nameIndexIn v n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGet v i) n)
      (Some i)
      (nameIndexIn v n (+ i 1)))))

; Is this name lexically bound at all - a shadow entry or a parameter?
(pub :: bsIsLocal (-> BScope String Int))
(pub fn (bsIsLocal b name)
  (if (let (Some _) (bsFind b name))
    1
    (if (let (Some _) (bsParamIndex (bsParamsVec b) name 0))
      1
      0)))

; The innermost binding source, falling back to the parameter list.
; An unbound global is attributed where its reference is walked.
(pub :: bsParamAlias (-> BScope String BindingSource))
(pub fn (bsParamAlias b name)
  (if (let (Some entry) (bsFind b name))
    entry.source
    (if (let (Some index) (bsParamIndex (bsParamsVec b) name 0))
      (BoundParam index)
      BoundAttributed)))

(pub :: bsAliasParam (-> BindingSource (Option Int)))
(pub fn (bsAliasParam source)
  (match source
    ((BoundParam index)
      (if (< index 0)
        None
        (Some index))
    )
    ((BoundAttributed) None)
    ((BoundOpaque) None)
    ((BoundValues _) None)))

(pub :: bsIsOpaque (-> BindingSource Bool))
(pub fn (bsIsOpaque source)
  (match source
    ((BoundOpaque) true)
    ((BoundParam _) false)
    ((BoundAttributed) false)
    ((BoundValues _) false)))

(pub :: bsParamIndex (-> Names String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (bsParamIndex names name index)
  (if (>= index (vecLen names))
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGet names index) name)
      (Some index)
      (bsParamIndex names name (+ index 1)))))

; Mark parameter `i` effect-transparent, if it is one a value can
; arrive in. stage0 gates on `param_callable`: a parameter declared
; `Int` cannot be called, so marking it would claim a transparency
; the type forbids.
;
; A mark is also a USE, and the use records where the call is: inside
; which `handle`s (`mask`, the custom effects discharged around it), or
; somewhere the walk cannot see (`esc`). A caller reads it to decide
; what a value it passes here performs at the call: a custom effect
; every recorded call discharges is not the caller's, and one the
; parameter may be called with outside any handler is. That is what
; lets `(withAsk usesAsk)` - a callee that installs the handler around
; its callback - be the correct program it is (`EpUse`).
(pub :: markEparam (-> BScope (Option Int) Int))
(pub fn (markEparam b index)
  (if (let (Some i) index)
    (markEparamUse b i (bsHandledVec b) 0)
    0))

; A use of parameter `i` with `mask` discharged around it, or an escape
; when `esc` is 1. In a lambda's body the use waits in the lambda's own
; `uses` (`bsLambda`), and the eparam set is marked when the lambda is
; applied or escapes.
(pub :: markEparamUse (-> BScope Int Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (markEparamUse b i mask esc)
  (if (< i 0)
    0
    (if (== (paramIsCallable (bsCallableVec b) i) 0)
      0
      {
        (if (! (|| (== (bsInLam b) 1) (== (intInVec (bsEparamsVec b) i 0) 1)))
          (vecPush (bsEparamsVec b) i))
        (epUseNote (bsUsesVec b) i mask esc)
      })))

; Where a parameter is called. `mask` holds the custom effects every
; non-escaping call discharges, and is meaningful when `def` is 1;
; `esc` is 1 when the parameter also reaches a place the walk cannot
; follow - a value passed on to an unknown callee, or a lambda that
; escapes. A position nothing calls has no record, and what is handed
; there escapes.
(pub struct EpUse
  (pidx : Int)
  (mut mask : Strings)
  (mut def : Int)
  (mut esc : Int)
  (restrictions : Strings))

(pub :: epUseFind (-> (Vec EpUse) Int Int (Option EpUse)))
(pub fn (epUseFind uses i k)
  (if (>= k (vecLen uses))
    None
    {
      (let r (vecGet uses k))
      (if (== r.pidx i)
        (Some r)
        (epUseFind uses i (+ k 1)))
    }))

; Record one use. The masks of two calls intersect: an effect is
; discharged for the parameter only when it is discharged at both.
(pub :: epUseNote (-> (Vec EpUse) Int Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (epUseNote uses i mask esc)
  (if (let (Some r) (epUseFind uses i 0))
    (if (== esc 1)
      (if (== r.esc 1)
        0
        {
          (set r.esc 1)
          1
        })
      (if (== r.def 0)
        {
          (set r.mask (effCopy mask))
          (set r.def 1)
          1
        }
        (effIntersectInto r mask)))
    {
      (vecPush uses
        (EpUse
          :pidx i
          :mask
            (if (== esc 1)
              vecNew
              (effCopy mask))
          :def
            (if (== esc 1)
              0
              1)
          :esc esc
          :restrictions vecNew))
      1
    }))

(pub :: effCopy (-> Strings Strings))
(pub fn (effCopy v)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (effUnion out v 0)
      out
    }
  })

; Narrow `r`'s mask to the entries also in `other`. 1 when it shrank.
(pub :: effIntersectInto (-> EpUse Strings Int))
(pub fn (effIntersectInto r other)
  {
    (let cur r.mask)
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (effKeepIn out cur other 0)
      (if (== (vecLen out) (vecLen cur))
        0
        {
          (set r.mask out)
          1
        })
    }
  })

(pub :: effKeepIn (-> Strings Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effKeepIn out cur other i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen cur))
    0
    {
      (if (== (effIn other (vecGetStr cur i)) 1)
        (vecPush out (vecGet cur i)))
      (effKeepIn out cur other (+ i 1))
    }))

; `a` and `b` together, as a fresh set.
(pub :: effJoined (-> Strings Strings Strings))
(pub fn (effJoined a b)
  {
    (let out (effCopy a))
    {
      (effUnion out b 0)
      out
    }
  })

; Fold one walk's uses into the entry's, for the fixpoint: 1 when any
; record is new or changed. Masks only narrow and flags only rise, so
; the fold is monotone.
(pub :: epUsesMerge (-> (Vec EpUse) (Vec EpUse) Int Int))
(pub fn (epUsesMerge into from i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen from))
    0
    {
      (let r (vecGet from i))
      (let a
        (if (== r.def 1)
          (epUseNote into r.pidx r.mask 0)
          0))
      (let b
        (if (== r.esc 1)
          (epUseNote into r.pidx vecNew 1)
          0))
      (let contracts
        (if (let (Some prior) (epUseFind into r.pidx 0))
          (effUnion prior.restrictions r.restrictions 0)
          0))
      (let rest (epUsesMerge into from (+ i 1)))
      (if (|| (|| (== a 1) (== b 1)) (== (| rest contracts) 1))
        1
        0)
    }))

; The callee's record for its parameter `j`, or `None` when it never
; calls it.
(pub :: epUseCalled (-> (Option EpUse) Bool))
(pub fn (epUseCalled use)
  (if (let (Some record) use)
    (== record.def 1)
    false))

(pub :: epUseAbsent (-> (Option EpUse) Int))
(pub fn (epUseAbsent o)
  (if (let (Some u) o)
    0
    1))

; 1 while the fixpoint has yet to walk the function `ent` names, so it
; has no record of where it calls its parameters (`walkEffectsArgVecAt`).
(pub :: fnPending (-> FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (fnPending ent)
  ent.pending)

(pub :: epUseFor (-> FnEnt Int (Option EpUse)))
(pub fn (epUseFor ent j)
  (epUseFind (fnEntUses ent) j 0))

; A use of the current function's parameter `pidx`, made by handing it
; to a callee position whose own record is `o` (`None`: a position the
; walk cannot see called, which is an escape).
(pub :: epUseApply (-> BScope (Option Int) (Option EpUse) Int))
(pub fn (epUseApply b index use)
  (if (let (Some pidx) index)
    (if (let (Some record) use)
      {
        (if (== record.def 1)
          (markEparamUse b pidx (effJoined (bsHandledVec b) record.mask) 0))
        (if (== record.esc 1)
          (markEparamUse b pidx vecNew 1)
          0)
      }
      (markEparamUse b pidx vecNew 1))
    0))

(pub :: paramIsCallable (-> Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (paramIsCallable callable i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen callable))
    1
    (vecGet callable i)))

(:: fnEntryIn (-> (Vec FnEnt) FnEnt Int Int))
(fn (fnEntryIn entries entry i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen entries))
    0
    (if (== (as (vecGet entries i) Int) (as entry Int))
      1
      (fnEntryIn entries entry (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: intInVec (-> Ints Int Int Int))
(pub fn (intInVec v n i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    (if (== (vecGet v i) n)
      1
      (intInVec v n (+ i 1)))))

; stage0's `escape_value`: a value flowing somewhere it may be CALLED.
; Only a parameter alias needs marking - a named function or lambda
; was attributed where it was written. `escapeValueUse` is the same with
; the callee position's own record, so the alias's use inherits where
; that callee calls it.
(pub :: escapeValue (-> BScope ASTNode Int))
; Answers 1 when this value is ACCOUNTED FOR wherever it ends up, and 0
; when it may be a function whose identity the walk has lost. The
; caller decides what to do with a 0, because only the caller knows
; whether the position it landed in is one that gets CALLED.
;
;   a known name     its effects were attributed, or it aliases a
;                    parameter whose transparency is marked here;
;   a lambda literal `walkEffects` walked its body where it stands;
;   a literal, a struct construction, an `alloc`  cannot be called at
;                    all, so nothing can hide in one.
;
; An opaque local or an incomplete callable set remains unaccounted
; for through aliases, as does a field load, call result or `if`.
(pub fn (escapeValue b e)
  (escapeValueUse b e None))

(pub :: escapeValueUse (-> BScope ASTNode (Option EpUse) Int))
(pub fn (escapeValueUse b e u)
  {
    (let inner (stripGrouping e))
    (if (== (nodeTag inner) TAG_E_VAR)
      {
        (let alias (bsParamAlias b (nodeAName inner)))
        (epUseApply b (bsAliasParam alias) u)
        (if (bsIsOpaque alias)
          0
          (if (let (Some values) (bsValsOf b (nodeAName inner)))
            values.complete
            1))
      }
      (if (== (nodeTag inner) TAG_E_LAM)
        1
        (notCallableShape inner)))
  })

; Shapes no call can ever go through, so an unfollowed one hides
; nothing. Everything absent from this list is assumed callable, which
; is the conservative direction.
(pub :: notCallableShape (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (notCallableShape e)
  {
    (let t (nodeTag e))
    (if (|| (== t TAG_E_INT) (|| (== t TAG_E_STR) (|| (== t TAG_E_FLT) (== t TAG_E_CHAR))))
      1
      0)
  })

(pub :: stripGrouping (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (stripGrouping e)
  e)

; `source` is what every one of these binders records - see `bsOpaque`
; for the meanings. Pattern and lambda binders use BoundOpaque until
; the walk establishes which function values they carry.
(pub :: bsWithParams (-> BScope Names BindingSource Int BScope))
(pub fn (bsWithParams b params pidx i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen params))
    b
    (bsWithParams (bsWith b (vecGetStr params i) pidx) params pidx (+ i 1))))

; A pattern's binders are its non-head variables - the same rule the
; AX3013 scope walk uses. They bind values, never parameter aliases.
(pub :: bsWithPattern (-> BScope ASTNode BScope))
(pub fn (bsWithPattern b pat)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (patBinders pat v)
      (bsWithParams b v bsOpaque 0)
    }
  })

(pub :: patBinders (-> ASTNode Names Int))
(pub fn (patBinders pat acc)
  (if (nodeSame pat nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag pat))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        {
          (vecPush acc (nodeAName pat))
          0
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (patBinders (nodeBChild pat) acc)
            (patBindersHead (nodeAChild pat) acc)
          }
          ; A named pattern, `(Wrap {tag = t})`, keeps its sub-patterns
          ; in word c, as `capPatBind` and `rgnBindPat` read them. This
          ; walk alone missed them, so the arm's `t` was a free name:
          ; with an entry-file function `t` performing IO, a function
          ; matching on `{tag = t}` drew a false AX3042.
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (patBindersVec (nodeCChildren pat) 0 acc)
            0)))
    }))

(pub :: patBindersVec (-> Nodes Int Names Int))
(pub fn (patBindersVec pats i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen pats))
    0
    {
      (patBinders (vecGet pats i) acc)
      (patBindersVec pats (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

; The spine's head of a constructor pattern names the constructor,
; not a binder, so only the arguments' binders are collected.
(pub :: patBindersHead (-> ASTNode Names Int))
(pub fn (patBindersHead pat acc)
  (if (== (nodeTag pat) TAG_E_APP)
    {
      (patBinders (nodeBChild pat) acc)
      (patBindersHead (nodeAChild pat) acc)
    }
    0))

; The binder nodes of one pattern, in walk order. The wildcard and
; the two literal names are left out, because neither binds. This is
; `patBinders` over nodes instead of names, so a repeated binder can
; be reported at its own span.
(pub :: patBinderNodes (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (patBinderNodes pat acc)
  (if (nodeSame pat nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag pat))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        {
          (let name (nodeAName pat))
          {
            (if (&& (! (strEq name "_")) (&& (! (strEq name "true")) (! (strEq name "false"))))
              (vecPush acc pat))
            0
          }
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (patBinderNodesHead (nodeAChild pat) acc)
            (patBinderNodes (nodeBChild pat) acc)
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (patBinderNodesVec (nodeCChildren pat) 0 acc)
            0)))
    }))

(pub :: patBinderNodesVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (patBinderNodesVec pats i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen pats))
    0
    {
      (patBinderNodes (vecGet pats i) acc)
      (patBinderNodesVec pats (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

(pub :: patBinderNodesHead (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (patBinderNodesHead pat acc)
  (if (== (nodeTag pat) TAG_E_APP)
    {
      (patBinderNodes (nodeBChild pat) acc)
      (patBinderNodesHead (nodeAChild pat) acc)
    }
    0))

; How many of `nodes`' first `end` entries bind `name`.
(pub :: patBinderCountBefore (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (patBinderCountBefore nodes name i end)
  (if (>= i end)
    0
    (+
      (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet nodes i)) name)
        1
        0)
      (patBinderCountBefore nodes name (+ i 1) end))))

; The first node in `nodes` that binds `name`, or None.
(pub :: patBinderFirst (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (patBinderFirst nodes name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen nodes))
    None
    (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet nodes i)) name)
      (Some (vecGet nodes i))
      (patBinderFirst nodes name (+ i 1)))))

; A bare binder that starts with a capital letter reads as a
; constructor test and is not one: it binds every value the scrutinee
; can take, so the arm it opens matches everything and any arm after
; it is dead. A constructor test is written `(Name)` or `(Name ...)`.
; Reported per binder, at the binder.
(pub :: checkUpperPatBinders (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkUpperPatBinders tc pat)
  {
    (let nodes vecNew)
    {
      (patBinderNodes pat nodes)
      (checkUpperPatBindersAt tc nodes 0)
    }
  })

(pub :: checkUpperPatBindersAt (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkUpperPatBindersAt tc nodes i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen nodes))
    0
    {
      (let node (vecGet nodes i))
      (let name (nodeAName node))
      {
        (if (&& (> (strLen name) 0) (&& (>= (strByte name 0) 65) (<= (strByte name 0) 90)))
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3137" "uppercase-pattern-binder" (nodeSpan node)
                (concat (concat "pattern binder `" name) "` starts with a capital letter") None
                (Some
                  "rename the binder in lowercase to bind, or write `(Name)` or `(Name ...)` to test a constructor")
                "a bare name in pattern position binds every value, whatever its case, so this arm matches everything and the arms after it are dead; a constructor test needs the parentheses")
              (concat (concat "`" name) "` binds every value here"))))
        (checkUpperPatBindersAt tc nodes (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; One pattern binds each name once. A second binding of the same name
; shadows the first, so the arm body could only ever read one of the
; two values, and which one is a scope detail rather than part of the
; pattern's meaning. Reported once per repeated name, at its second
; binder, with the first as the related span.
(pub :: checkDupPatBinders (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkDupPatBinders tc pat)
  {
    (let nodes vecNew)
    {
      (patBinderNodes pat nodes)
      (checkDupPatBindersAt tc nodes 0)
    }
  })

(pub :: checkDupPatBindersAt (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkDupPatBindersAt tc nodes i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen nodes))
    0
    {
      (let node (vecGet nodes i))
      (let name (nodeAName node))
      {
        (if (== (patBinderCountBefore nodes name 0 i) 1)
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3136" "duplicate-pattern-binder" (nodeSpan node)
                (concat (concat "duplicate binder `" name) "` in one pattern")
                (diagRelated
                  (match (patBinderFirst nodes name 0)
                    ((Some first) (nodeSpan first))
                    ((None) 0))
                  (concat (concat "first binder `" name) "`"))
                (Some "give each binder in the pattern its own name")
                "one pattern binds each name once; the second binding shadows the first, so the arm body could only ever read one of the two values")
              (concat (concat "`" name) "` is bound twice in this pattern"))))
        (checkDupPatBindersAt tc nodes (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The fixpoint. Monotone: every round can only add, and one round per
; function suffices because each round propagates at least one
; call-graph edge. stage0 bounds it the same way, and for the same
; stated reason - termination must not depend on the walk staying
; monotone if it ever changes.
(pub :: inferEffects (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (inferEffects tc decls)
  {
    ; The pair set depends on signatures and syntax only, so it is
    ; complete before any row is, and the walk reads it from round 1.
    (tcSetPairFns tc (tcPairFnsBuild tc decls))
    ; What every function-typed field can hold depends on syntax alone,
    ; so it too is complete before any row is (`fieldStoresBuild`).
    (collectFnRestrictions tc decls 0)
    ; What a declared function-typed field may perform comes from its
    ; tags alone, so it too is read before any row is.
    (fieldDeclsBuild tc)
    (fieldStoresBuild tc decls)
    ; Every function the passes below will walk starts pending: no
    ; record yet of where it calls its parameters (`fnPending`).
    (effMarkPending tc decls 0)
    (inferEffectsRound tc decls 0 (+ (vecLen decls) 1))
  })

(pub :: effMarkPending (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (effMarkPending tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
          {
            (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
            (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
            (let _m0 (tcSetCurModule tc None))
            (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
              (fnEntSetPending ent 1)
              ())
          })
        (effMarkPending tc decls (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: inferEffectsRound (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
; ONE ROUND IS TWO PASSES, IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, and that is a
; complexity fix rather than a tidy-up.
;
; A round propagates an effect exactly one call edge when it meets the
; caller before the callee, and the WHOLE CHAIN when it meets them the
; other way round. The pass above visits declarations last-to-first, so
; which of those a program gets is decided by the order its
; declarations happen to be written in - and the conventional order,
; helpers above the functions that call them, is the losing one.
;
; Measured 2026-08-25 on one call graph written both ways, 2,000
; functions with an effect at the bottom of the chain:
;
;     callers declared first   0.052s
;     callees declared first   3.504s      67x, and 3.66x per doubling
;
; The second is the shape real code has. Running both directions costs
; at most one extra pass and collapses a linear chain in either order
; to a single round: the same file above goes 3.504s -> 0.057s.
;
; ORDER OF THE TWO: forward first, because a converged program then
; costs ONE pass rather than two - the reverse pass runs only when the
; forward one grew something. And either pass answering 0 ends the
; fixpoint, which is sound for the reason the round loop always rested
; on: a complete pass over every declaration that grows nothing has
; reached the fixed point, whichever direction it walked.
;
; This is not the complete answer. A graph that is not a chain can
; still need several rounds, and the general fix is a WORKLIST over the
; reverse call graph - when a row grows, re-examine that function's
; callers and nothing else. The edges are already recorded (`tcNoteCall`
; into word 29, resolved in round 1), so the reverse map is buildable
; from what is here. That is a larger change than this one and is not
; made here.
(pub fn (inferEffectsRound tc decls round limit)
  (if (== (inferEffectsPassFwd tc decls 0) 0)
    0
    (if (== (inferEffectsPass tc decls 0) 0)
      0
      ; Round 1 is over and every edge is recorded, so the reverse
      ; graph is buildable and rounds 2+ are a worklist. See THE
      ; WORKLIST below for the shape this costs when it is not one.
      (inferEffectsWork tc decls (callersIdxBuild tc decls) (allIndexes (vecLen decls))
        (flagsNew (vecLen decls)) (+ round 1) limit))))

; One pass over every `fn` in the program - imported modules
; included, since a call into one has to know what it performs.
; Answers 1 when anything grew.
(pub :: inferEffectsPass (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))

; ONE declaration's contribution. 1 when its effect row or its
; effect-parameter set grew.
;
; Split out of the pass below so the pass can be walked in EITHER
; direction over the same work - see `inferEffectsRound`.
(pub :: inferEffectsOne (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (inferEffectsOne tc d)
  (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
    0
    ; Set BEFORE the entry lookup, for the reason `tcCheckFn`
    ; states: `nodeA d` is this module's mangled name, and a
    ; declaration its own module does not export is invisible
    ; to a lookup made from nowhere. Looked up first, every
    ; private function's entry came back 0, its body's effects
    ; were never accumulated, and the whole standard library
    ; drew `AX3010 effect(io) claim unsupported` the moment one
    ; import carried a name list.
    {
      (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        {
          (let sc (bodyScopeOf tc d))
          {
            (let _c (tcSetCurCalls tc (Some (fnEntCalls ent))))
            (let _y (tcSetMayCalls tc (Some (fnEntMayCalls ent))))
            (let _s (tcSetPairSites tc (Some (tcPairSitesOf tc d))))
            (let got (collectEffects tc (nodeCChild d) sc))
            (let _s0 (tcClearPairSites tc))
            (let _c0 (tcSetCurCalls tc None))
            (let _y0 (tcSetMayCalls tc None))
            (let _m0 (tcSetCurModule tc None))
            {
              (let g1 (effUnion (fnEntEffects ent) got 0))
              (let g2 (intUnion (fnEntEparams ent) (bsEparamsVec sc) 0))
              (let g3 (effUnion (fnEntEffects ent) (syscallTagEffects tc d) 0))
              (let g4 (epUsesMerge (fnEntUses ent) (bsUsesVec sc) 0))
              (let g6 (mergeFnLambdaCast ent (bsLambdaCast sc)))
              (let g5
                (if (== (fnEntPending ent) 1)
                  {
                    (fnEntSetPending ent 0)
                    1
                  }
                  0))
              (if (|| (|| (== g1 1) (== g2 1)) (|| (|| (== g3 1) (== g4 1)) (== (| g5 g6) 1)))
                1
                0)
            }
          }
        }
        {
          (tcSetCurModule tc None)
          0
        })
    }))

; `;@axiom:syscall(entropy)`, `syscall(spawn)`, `syscall(block)`: this
; declaration is a syscall NUMBER, and the call it names draws entropy,
; starts a process or thread, or waits. The platform tables in
; `stdlib/Sys/Platform.*.ax` carry it on `sysRandomNum`, the fork and
; clone numbers and the wait numbers.
;
; The effect is seeded into the constant's own row, so every function
; that NAMES the number performs it, by the same propagation that
; carries IO up from a `__syscallN`. Naming is the right trigger: the
; kernel sees the number, and a function cannot make the call without
; first saying it. A function that names the number and never makes
; the call is over-approximated, which is the safe direction.
;
; The value is a comma list of the three names. This reader seeds no
; effect for a name outside that set.
(pub :: syscallTagEffects (-> TC ASTNode Strings))
(pub fn (syscallTagEffects tc d)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (syscallTagsInto out (nodeAxtagsOf d) 0)
      (if (let (Some tags) (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName d)))
        (syscallTagsInto out tags 0)
        0)
      out
    }
  })

(pub :: syscallTagsInto (-> Strings Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (syscallTagsInto out tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (let t (vecGet tags i))
      {
        (if (strEq (axtagKeyOf t) "syscall")
          (syscallNamesInto out (strSplit (axtagTextValue t) 44) 0))
        (syscallTagsInto out tags (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: syscallNamesInto (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (syscallNamesInto out names i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    0
    {
      (if (let (Some effect) (syscallEffectOf (strTrimBlanks (vecGetStr names i))))
        (effAdd out effect))
      (syscallNamesInto out names (+ i 1))
    }))

; The effect a `syscall(...)` name denotes, or `None` for a name it
; does not accept.
(pub :: syscallEffectOf (-> String (Option String)))
(pub fn (syscallEffectOf n)
  (if (strEq n "entropy")
    (Some (builtinEff "Entropy"))
    (if (strEq n "spawn")
      (Some (builtinEff "Spawn"))
      (if (strEq n "block")
        (Some (builtinEff "Block"))
        None))))

; LAST declaration to first. The recursive call is bound before the
; body runs, so `decls[i]` is processed after everything above it.
(pub fn (inferEffectsPass tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let rest (inferEffectsPass tc decls (+ i 1)))
      (if (== (inferEffectsOne tc (vecGet decls i)) 1)
        1
        rest)
    }))

; FIRST declaration to last: `decls[i]` is processed before the
; recursion, which is the whole difference.
(pub :: inferEffectsPassFwd (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (inferEffectsPassFwd tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let here (inferEffectsOne tc (vecGet decls i)))
      (if (== (inferEffectsPassFwd tc decls (+ i 1)) 1)
        1
        here)
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE WORKLIST.
;
; The two-direction round above collapses a linear chain in EITHER
; declaration order to one round, and its own note says what it does
; not do: "a graph that is not a chain can still need several rounds,
; and the general fix is a WORKLIST over the reverse call graph - when
; a row grows, re-examine that function's callers and nothing else."
; This is that.
;
; THE SHAPE IT COSTS, measured on this tree before the change. One
; chain of N functions, `f1` calling `f2` calling ... with the effect
; at the bottom, and only the DECLARATION ORDER varying:
;
;   n      callers first   callees first   pairs swapped
;   500        0.02 s          0.02 s          0.55 s
;   1000       0.02 s          0.02 s          1.32 s
;   2000       0.03 s          0.03 s          3.84 s
;   4000       0.06 s          0.06 s         14.53 s
;
; Both plain orders are one round and linear. The pair-swapped order -
; `f2 f1 f4 f3 f6 f5 ...`, which is the shape a generator produces
; when it emits a helper beside each of its callers - defeats both
; passes at once: the forward pass meets `f2` before `f3`, the reverse
; pass meets `f5` before `f6`, and each round advances the frontier by
; one PAIR while paying for a full walk of every body. 242x at n=4000,
; and 3.8x per doubling where the fixed orders are 2x.
;
; The rounds are the same rounds. What changes is who is IN them:
; round 1 is the pass pair unchanged - it is also what records every
; edge, since `tcNoteCall` writes into word 7 while `collectEffects`
; runs - and rounds 2+ walk only the DIRTY set, the callers of every
; function whose row or eparam set grew in the pass before. A frontier
; of one function per round then costs one body walk per round rather
; than N, which is the whole of the difference.
;
; Termination is the monotone argument unchanged: every pass can only
; ADD, the dirty set is empty exactly when nothing grew, and the
; `len(decls)+1` bound stays as the safety net it always was.
; Determinism does not rest on the order either: the dirty set is
; walked in DECLARATION order, `effSorted` sorts every rendered row,
; and `#calls=` is recorded in round 1 regardless - and the gate
; asserts byte-identical `symbols --calls` against a compiler with the
; filter ablated rather than taking that argument's word for it.
; A callee's callers, by the resolved name every edge already carries.
(pub type CallerIndex (Vec (Vec CallerEnt)))

(pub struct CallerEnt
  (name : String)
  (idxs : (Vec Int)))

; The reverse call graph, as buckets over `CallerEnt` on the same hash
; the other three indexes use.
;
; An INDEX rather than a field on `FnEnt`: the memory note prices a
; new FnEnt word at about 6% of a self-check, and this one is needed
; by a single walk that already holds the declaration list.
(pub :: callersIdxBuild (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) CallerIndex))
(pub fn (callersIdxBuild tc decls)
  {
    (let nb (fnIdxBuckets (vecLen decls)))
    (let bs vecNew)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< i nb)
        {
          (vecPush bs vecNew)
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (set i 0)
      (while (< i (vecLen decls))
        {
          (callersIdxAddDecl tc bs decls i)
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      bs
    }
  })

; Every edge decl `i` records, as an entry in the callee's caller list.
;
; `restrictOwnEnt` for the lookup, and for the reason
; `inferEffectsOne` states: a private function is invisible to a
; lookup made from nowhere, so the module has to be set first.
(pub :: callersIdxAddDecl (-> TC CallerIndex (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (callersIdxAddDecl tc bs decls i)
  {
    (let d (vecGet decls i))
    (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
      0
      {
        (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          {
            (callersIdxAddEdges bs (fnEntCalls ent) 0 i)
            (callersIdxAddEdges bs (fnEntMayCalls ent) 0 i)
          }
          0)
      })
  })

(pub :: callersIdxAddEdges (-> CallerIndex Names Int Int Int))
(pub fn (callersIdxAddEdges bs calls i who)
  (if (== (as calls Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen calls))
      0
      {
        (callersIdxPut bs (vecGetStr calls i) who)
        (callersIdxAddEdges bs calls (+ i 1) who)
      })))

(pub :: callersIdxPut (-> CallerIndex String Int Int))
(pub fn (callersIdxPut bs name who)
  (if (let (Some entry) (callersIdxFind bs name))
    {
      (intPushUnique entry.idxs who)
      0
    }
    {
      (let indexes vecNew)
      (vecPush indexes who)
      (callersIdxInsert bs name indexes)
    }))

(pub :: callersIdxInsert (-> CallerIndex String Ints Int))
(pub fn (callersIdxInsert bs name v)
  {
    (vecPush (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen bs))) (CallerEnt :name name :idxs v))
    0
  })

(pub :: callersIdxFind (-> CallerIndex String (Option CallerEnt)))
(pub fn (callersIdxFind bs name)
  (if (== (vecLen bs) 0)
    None
    (callersIdxFindIn (vecGet bs (% (fnIdxHash name) (vecLen bs))) name 0)))

(pub :: callersIdxFindIn (-> (Vec CallerEnt) String Int (Option CallerEnt)))
(pub fn (callersIdxFindIn v name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    None
    {
      (let entry (vecGet v i))
      (if (strEq entry.name name)
        (Some entry)
        (callersIdxFindIn v name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; A declaration index appended once. The edge list `tcNoteCall` builds
; is already deduplicated per caller, but one caller may reach the same
; callee under two spellings, so the guard is here rather than assumed.
(pub :: intPushUnique (-> Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (intPushUnique v x)
  (if (== (intInVec v x 0) 1)
    0
    {
      (vecPush v x)
      1
    }))

; A Vec of `n` zeroes: the membership marks the frontier is
; deduplicated through.
;
; THE DIRTY SET IS A LIST AND NOT A FLAG PER DECLARATION, and the
; difference is the whole complexity argument rather than a detail. A
; flag vector has to be allocated or cleared once per ROUND, which is
; O(N) of work in a round whose real content is one body walk - and a
; chain needs N rounds, so the quadratic comes straight back in the
; bookkeeping after being removed from the analysis. Measured on the
; pair-swapped chain with a flag vector per round: 0.06 / 0.17 / 0.60 s
; at n=1000 / 2000 / 4000, still 3.5x per doubling. The list costs one
; allocation per round proportional to the FRONTIER, and `marks` is
; allocated once and left all-zero at every round boundary, because the
; round that sets a mark is the round that clears it.
(pub :: flagsNew (-> Int Ints))
(pub fn (flagsNew n)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..n
        (vecPush v 0))
      v
    }
  })

; Every declaration index, ascending: round 2's frontier, because
; round 1 reported only THAT something grew and not what.
(pub :: allIndexes (-> Int Ints))
(pub fn (allIndexes n)
  {
    (let v vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..n
        (vecPush v i))
      v
    }
  })

; One dirty pass over `work`. Runs `inferEffectsOne` on each and collects the
; callers of every one that grew into `next`. Answers 1 when anything
; grew.
;
; Visit the frontier from its last element, preserving the recursive
; walk's unwind order. A loop keeps the stack independent of the
; frontier size: the initial pass can contain every declaration in
; the compiler. Processing the body after a recursive call retained
; one frame per entry, even at --opt 1.
(pub :: inferEffectsDirtyPass (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) CallerIndex Ints Ints Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (inferEffectsDirtyPass tc decls bs work marks next i)
  {
    (let mut j (- (vecLen work) 1))
    (let mut changed 0)
    (while (>= j i)
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls (vecGet work j)))
        (if (!= (inferEffectsOne tc d) 0)
          {
            (markCallers tc bs marks next d)
            (set changed 1)
          })
        (set j (- j 1))
      })
    changed
  })

; The callers of the function `d` declares, added to the next
; frontier. A declaration whose entry has gone is not an error here -
; the same `0` guard `inferEffectsOne` makes - it simply has no callers
; to wake.
(pub :: markCallers (-> TC CallerIndex Ints Ints ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (markCallers tc bs marks next d)
  {
    (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
    (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
      (if (let (Some entry) (callersIdxFind bs (fnEntName ent)))
        (markEach marks next entry.idxs 0)
        0)
      0)
  })

(pub :: markEach (-> Ints Ints Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (markEach marks next idxs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen idxs))
    0
    {
      {
        (let x (vecGet idxs i))
        (if (! (== (vecGet marks x) 1))
          {
            (vecSet marks x 1)
            (intPushSorted next x)
          })
      }
      (markEach marks next idxs (+ i 1))
    }))

; Insert `x` into an ascending list, in place. The frontier is small -
; the callers of what grew in one round - so an insertion is the right
; shape and a sort at the end would be the wrong one.
(pub :: intPushSorted (-> Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (intPushSorted v x)
  {
    (let mut i (vecLen v))
    {
      (vecPush v x)
      (while (if (> i 0)
        (> (vecGet v (- i 1)) x)
        false)
        {
          (vecSet v i (vecGet v (- i 1)))
          (set i (- i 1))
        })
      (vecSet v i x)
      0
    }
  })

; The marks this round set, put back to 0 so the next round starts on
; an all-zero vector without paying a walk of the whole declaration
; list to find out which entries to clear.
(pub :: clearMarks (-> Ints Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (clearMarks marks idxs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen idxs))
    0
    {
      (vecSet marks (vecGet idxs i) 0)
      (clearMarks marks idxs (+ i 1))
    }))

; WHICH DECLARATIONS THE NEXT ROUND WALKS, and this one line is the
; whole of the worklist: `next` is the frontier the pass just built,
; the callers of everything whose row grew.
;
; It is a function rather than the argument itself so that
; `scripts/check-effect-fixpoint.sh` has one line to ablate. Replacing
; the body with `(allIndexes (vecLen decls))` - every declaration is
; dirty, every round - is exactly the behaviour this replaced, and the
; gate requires the pair-swapped chain to go back over ten times the
; cost of the same call graph declared in order. A ratio that did not
; move under that ablation would mean the frontier was never what the
; measurement was measuring, which is the failure a bare timing gate
; cannot see.
(pub :: nextFrontier (-> Ints (Vec ASTNode) Ints))
(pub fn (nextFrontier next decls)
  next)

; Rounds 2..limit, each over the previous round's frontier.
(pub :: inferEffectsWork (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) CallerIndex Ints Ints Int Int Int))
(pub fn (inferEffectsWork tc decls bs work marks round limit)
  (if (>= round limit)
    0
    {
      (let next vecNew)
      (if (== (inferEffectsDirtyPass tc decls bs work marks next 0) 0)
        0
        {
          (clearMarks marks next 0)
          (inferEffectsWork tc decls bs (nextFrontier next decls) marks (+ round 1) limit)
        })
    }))

(pub :: intUnion (-> Ints Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (intUnion acc src i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen src))
    0
    {
      (let rest (intUnion acc src (+ i 1)))
      (if (== (intInVec acc (vecGet src i) 0) 1)
        rest
        {
          (vecPush acc (vecGet src i))
          1
        })
    }))

; The declaration of the ONE handleable effect a `handle` list names
; - declared, single-operation - or None: absent (a built-in-only list is
; a measurement, and its handler is never evaluated), or more than
; one (the shape `checkHandleEffects` refuses at the form with
; AX3017; there is no one arrow to hold a single handler to). Reports
; nothing: `checkHandleEffects` owns the list's diagnostics and runs
; AFTER the handler is checked, which keeps stage0's order - body,
; handler, list.
(pub :: handleOpDecl (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option ASTNode) (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (handleOpDecl tc effs i found)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    found
    {
      (let name (nodeAName (vecGet effs i)))
      (if (== (isBuiltinEffect name) 1)
        (handleOpDecl tc effs (+ i 1) found)
        (if (let (Some declaration) (findEffectDecl tc name))
          (if (!= (vecLen (nodeBNames declaration)) 1)
            (handleOpDecl tc effs (+ i 1) found)
            (if (isSome found)
              None
              (handleOpDecl tc effs (+ i 1) (Some declaration))))
          (handleOpDecl tc effs (+ i 1) found)))
    }))

; The declared arrow of the operation a handler answers, or None when
; there is none to hold it to: no single handleable effect
; (`handleOpDecl`), or an operation that declares no type (AX3055 at
; its declaration; its field is 0). The arrow rides in slot b of the
; TAG_FIELD node in the declaration's slot c - the same slot
; `tcAddEffectOp` registers the operation's callable type from.
(pub :: handleOpType (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (handleOpType tc effs)
  (if (let (Some declaration) (handleOpDecl tc effs 0 None))
    (if (== (nodeC declaration) 0)
      None
      {
        (let arrow (nodeBChild (vecGet (nodeCChildren declaration) 0)))
        (if (nodeSame arrow nilNode)
          None
          (Some arrow))
      })
    None))

; The handler, held to the operation's arrow. A lambda literal is
; checked AGAINST it (`checkLamAgainst`), so its parameters carry the
; declared types; any other expression - a top-level function passed
; bare, a closure a call built, a literal - is checked as it is and
; its type compared. A mismatch is AX3004, the type mismatch it is.
; Measured before this check existed: the integer `0` as a handler
; checked clean and SIGSEGVed when the dispatch applied it; a
; `String`-answering handler for an `Int` operation checked clean and
; handed its string's address plus one to the caller's arithmetic.
; With no arrow the handler is checked as an expression, exactly as
; before - `(handle BODY (Pure) 0)`, the agent-harness boundary, stays
; legal, and its `0` is never evaluated (`emitHandle`).
(pub :: checkHandler (-> TC ASTNode (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkHandler tc h arrow)
  (if (nodeSame h nilNode)
    {
      (checkExprH tc h 0)
      0
    }
    (if (let (Some arrow) arrow)
      {
        (let hty
          (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_LAM)
            {
              (checkLamValidate tc h)
              (checkLamAgainst tc h arrow)
            }
            (groundArgByDecl tc (checkExprH tc h 0) (Some arrow) (spanOf h))))
        (if (== (tyCompat tc hty arrow) 0)
          {
            (emitMismatchTy tc arrow hty (spanOf h))
            (setNodeBinderTyChild h (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
            0
          }
          0)
      }
      {
        (checkExprH tc h 0)
        0
      })))

; `(handle body (Effect...) handler)`. stage0's order is the whole
; rule and it is not the source order: the BODY is checked, then the
; HANDLER, and only then does the effect list produce diagnostics -
; so a mistake inside either expression is reported before AX3016 on
; a misspelled effect beside it.
;
; Only CUSTOM effects are subject to these checks. The seven built-in
; names never become `Custom` in stage0's parser, so they are skipped
; here rather than looked up and refused.
;
; The form's own type is its BODY's: an operation's result is its
; declared arrow's result, so `(handle (ask 1) (Ask) h)` is an `Int`
; and `(println (handle ..))` selects a rendering on it. It answered the
; silent wildcard until 2026-08-29, which is why every fixture and
; both stdlib effects spelled `(cast Int (handle ..))` and drew AX3025
; without it.
;
; The handler is held to the operation's declared arrow
; (`checkHandler`, AX3004). This comment recorded that check as
; omitted - "which needs the op type stage1's `effect` parser
; discards" - while the parser had kept the arrow in the TAG_FIELD
; beside the name since effects landed and `tcAddEffectOp` registered
; the callable from it. AX3011 is `checkUnhandled` below, over the
; fixpoint this checker does run.
(pub :: checkHandle (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkHandle tc e)
  {
    (let bodyTy (checkExprH tc (nodeAChild e) 0))
    {
      (checkHandler tc (nodeCChild e) (handleOpType tc (nodeBChildren e)))
      ; The form's own span is its BODY's - stage0's `Expr::span()`
      ; recurses into the first field of an EHandle exactly as it does
      ; for an application's head.
      (checkHandleEffects tc (nodeBChildren e) 0 0 (spanOf (nodeAChild e)))
      ; AX3011: every effect the body performs that the list does not
      ; name. Seeded with the names lexically bound HERE - the walk
      ; starts mid-function, and a local shadowing an effectful
      ; top-level name would otherwise re-resolve globally and produce
      ; an error the body contradicts.
      ;
      ; The seed starts at frameBase, NOT at 0. Everything below it is
      ; the module walk's own entries - every signature seen so far -
      ; and seeding with those makes every top-level
      ; function look lexically bound, so a call to one contributes
      ; nothing and the whole fixpoint's work is discarded at the only
      ; place that reads it. stage0 seeds from its `__scope__` marker
      ; for the same reason.
      {
        (let inner (collectEffects tc (nodeAChild e) (handleScopeOf tc)))
        {
          (checkUnhandled tc inner (nodeBChildren e) 0 (spanOf (nodeAChild e)))
          ; AX3011 is checked against a set that may be a LOWER BOUND, and
          ; it is an ERROR - so the silence on the other side of it was
          ; the compiler asserting the handled list is complete on the
          ; strength of a walk that had stopped early. An unhandled effect
          ; is not a soft failure: the runtime aborts the process with
          ; status 71 and no message, so the assertion was load-bearing.
          (checkHandleIncomplete tc inner (spanOf (nodeAChild e)))
        }
      }
      bodyTy
    }
  })

; AX3038: the handled-effects list could not be checked, because the
; body reaches a call the walk could not resolve. A WARNING and not an
; error, deliberately: AX3011 refuses a program KNOWN to reach an
; unhandled effect, and this one cannot know. Refusing here would make
; `(handle ((b.f) x) ...)` - dispatch through a stored function -
; unwriteable rather than unverified, which is a defect of its own.
;
; The gradient is the same one AX3010/AX3037 draw: "this is wrong" is
; an error, "this cannot be checked" informs.
(pub :: checkHandleIncomplete (-> TC Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (checkHandleIncomplete tc inner span)
  (if (&& (== (effUnresolved inner) 1) (!= span 0))
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_WARNING "AX3038" "effect-unverifiable" span
        "handled-effects list cannot be checked: the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve"
        None
        (Some
          "name the function at the call, or widen the handled list: an unresolved call may perform any effect, and reaching one with no handler aborts the process")))
    0))

(pub :: checkUnhandled (-> TC Strings (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (checkUnhandled tc inner effs i span)
  (if (>= i (vecLen inner))
    0
    ; The incompleteness sentinel travels in the same Vec and is
    ; NOT an effect: reporting it printed `unhandled effect
    ; `incomplete`` on a valid program - a handle whose body calls
    ; a local. Every consumer that renders an effect has to skip
    ; it, and no file in the swept trees calls through a local
    ; inside a handle body, so only the probe bank saw it.
    ;
    ; A POSSIBLE spelling is an effect and is checked - the list has to
    ; name what the body may reach - but not twice: when the definite
    ; twin is in the same set, that one reports it.
    {
      (if (! (== (effIsMarker (vecGetStr inner i)) 1))
        (if (!
              (&& (== (effIsPossible (vecGetStr inner i)) 1)
                (== (effIn inner (effDefiniteOf (vecGetStr inner i))) 1)))
          (if (&& (== (effHandledBy effs (vecGetStr inner i) 0) 0) (!= span 0))
            (emitDiag tc
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3011" "effect-mismatch" span
                (concat
                  (concat "effect mismatch: unhandled effect `" (effRender (vecGetStr inner i)))
                  "`")
                None None)))))
      (checkUnhandled tc inner effs (+ i 1) span)
    }))

; Every name currently in scope, which seeds the collector's `bound`
; list at a handle site.
; The scope a handle's body is walked in: the enclosing function's
; parameters and their callability, plus every frame binder live at
; this node as a plain shadow. Parameters are EXCLUDED from the
; shadow list - stage0 excludes them for the same reason, that a
; parameter masked as a shadow of itself is invisible to the
; transparency marks.
(pub :: handleScopeOf (-> TC BScope))
(pub fn (handleScopeOf tc)
  {
    (let base
      (bsMake vecNew (tcCurParams tc) (tcCurCallable tc) vecNew (curFnSigTy tc)
        (bsCtxOf (vecWithCapacity 1) (vecWithCapacity 1) 0 (vecWithCapacity 1))))
    (seedFrameBinders base (tcScopeVec tc) (tcFrameBase tc))
  })

; The declared type of the function being checked, or None without one.
;
; Word 24 is the current declaration - `tcCheckFn` sets it before the
; body and clears it after - so this is the same signature
; `bodyScopeOf` reads, arrived at from the other direction. A handle
; site inside a body needs it for the same reason the body does: a
; call through a parameter in there asks which of that parameter's
; argument positions could hold a function.
(pub :: curFnSigTy (-> TC (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (curFnSigTy tc)
  (if (let (Some declaration) (tcCurDeclNode tc))
    (if (let (Some entry) (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName declaration)))
      (bsTypeOpt (fnEntTy entry))
      None)
    None))

(pub :: seedFrameBinders (-> BScope (Vec SEnt) Int BScope))
(pub fn (seedFrameBinders b scope i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen scope))
    b
    {
      (let n (sEntName (vecGet scope i)))
      (seedFrameBinders
        (if (let (Some _) (nameIndexIn (bsParamsVec b) n 0))
          b
          (bsWith b n BoundAttributed))
        scope (+ i 1))
    }))

; One pass over the effect list, counting the custom effects that
; resolved to a one-operation declaration. stage0 reports AX3017 a
; second time, at the FORM rather than at a name, when more than one
; such effect survives - a dynamic handle installs one evidence
; record, so two is not a shape it can lower.
(pub :: checkHandleEffects (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (checkHandleEffects tc effs i dynN formSpan)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    (if (&& (> dynN 1) (!= formSpan 0))
      (emitDiag tc
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3017" "effect-handler-unsupported" formSpan
          (concat (fmtInt dynN) " custom effects in one handle; nest one `handle` per effect") None
          None))
      0)
    {
      (let nm (vecGet effs i))
      (if (== (isBuiltinEffect (nodeAName nm)) 1)
        (checkHandleEffects tc effs (+ i 1) dynN formSpan)
        {
          (let d (findEffectDecl tc (nodeAName nm)))
          (if (let (Some d) d)
            (if (!= (vecLen (nodeBNames d)) 1)
              {
                (if (!= (nodeSpan nm) 0)
                  (emitDiag tc
                    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3017" "effect-handler-unsupported" (nodeSpan nm)
                      (concat
                        (concat (concat (concat "effect `" (nodeAName nm)) "` declares ")
                          (fmtInt (vecLen (nodeBNames d))))
                        " operations; a dynamic handler covers exactly one")
                      None None)))
                (checkHandleEffects tc effs (+ i 1) dynN formSpan)
              }
              (checkHandleEffects tc effs (+ i 1) (+ dynN 1) formSpan))
            {
              (if (!= (nodeSpan nm) 0)
                (emitDiag tc
                  (diagSetLabel
                    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3016" "unknown-effect" (nodeSpan nm)
                      (concat (concat "unknown effect `" (nodeAName nm)) "`") None
                      (Some
                        "declare it with `(effect Name (op :: sig))`, import the module that does, or fix the spelling"))
                    (concat (concat "no effect named `" (nodeAName nm)) "` is declared"))))
              (checkHandleEffects tc effs (+ i 1) dynN formSpan)
            })
        })
    }))

(pub :: checkStructFields (-> TC Nodes (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))

; Each field's declared type, instantiated through ONE shared env so a
; variable used twice is one placeholder in both.
(pub :: structInstFieldTys (-> TC Nodes TypeEnv Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (structInstFieldTys tc fields env out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fields))
    out
    {
      (vecPush out
        (tyInstIn tc
          (if (== (nodeTy (vecGet fields i)) 0)
            mkSilentWild
            (nodeTyChild (vecGet fields i)))
          env))
      (structInstFieldTys tc fields env out (+ i 1))
    }))

; The placeholder each declared variable was instantiated to, in the
; declaration's order - the env is (name, node) pairs, and a variable
; the fields never mention has no entry, which answers a fresh
; wildcard so the arity of the TCon still matches the declaration.
(pub :: structTvNodes (-> TypeEnv Names Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (structTvNodes env tvs out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tvs))
    out
    {
      (vecPush out (structTvLookup env (vecGetStr tvs i) 0))
      (structTvNodes env tvs out (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: structTvLookup (-> TypeEnv String Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (structTvLookup env name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen env))
    mkSilentWild
    (if (strEq (typeBindingName (vecGet env i)) name)
      (typeBindingTy (vecGet env i))
      (structTvLookup env name (+ i 1)))))

; Check each argument against its INSTANTIATED field type.
(pub :: checkStructFieldsInst (-> TC Nodes (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkStructFieldsInst tc args insts i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    ; A pending lambda stored into a declared arrow field grounds
    ; BY the declaration (`groundArgByDecl`): its parameters carry
    ; the types the author wrote. Anything else flows through to
    ; the comparison, which a still-pending value skips (flushed:
    ; verified standalone, only the `{unknown}` refusals dropped).
    {
      (let aty
        (groundArgByDecl tc (checkExprH tc (vecGet args i) 0) (Some (vecGet insts i))
          (spanOf (vecGet args i))))
      {
        ; Still pending against a GROUND field is a function
        ; where a value belongs (refused, as the standalone
        ; arrow was before grounding); against a variable
        ; field it is a generic payload (flushed and skipped).
        (if (== (isPendingNode aty) 1)
          (if (== (tyIsGround (vecGet insts i)) 1)
            (refusePendingOperand tc aty (tyRender (vecGet insts i)) (spanOf (vecGet args i)))
            (flushPending tc aty (spanOf (vecGet args i))))
          (if (== (tyCompat tc aty (vecGet insts i)) 0)
            (emitMismatchTy tc (vecGet insts i) aty (spanOf (vecGet args i)))
            (emitPayloadSmuggled tc (vecGet insts i) (vecGet args i) (spanOf (vecGet args i)))))
        (checkStructFieldsInst tc args insts (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; The type arguments, read back by pinning each placeholder from the
; argument that witnessed it. `pinShape` is the application walker,
; used here for the same job: a declared position holding a minted
; placeholder takes the matching subtree of the actual type.
(pub :: structPinArgs (-> TC Nodes Nodes Nodes Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (structPinArgs tc args insts tvNodes out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    (structCarrierOut tvNodes out 0)
    {
      (structPinInto tvNodes (vecGet insts i) (checkExprH tc (vecGet args i) 0) 0)
      (structPinArgs tc args insts tvNodes out (+ i 1))
    }))

; `pinShape` answers a NEW tree rather than mutating, so the carrier is
; walked one slot at a time and each slot replaced in place.
(pub :: structPinInto (-> Nodes ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (structPinInto tvNodes param argTy i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tvNodes))
    0
    {
      (vecSet tvNodes i (pinShape (vecGet tvNodes i) param argTy))
      (structPinInto tvNodes param argTy (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: structCarrierOut (-> Nodes Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (structCarrierOut tvNodes out i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tvNodes))
    out
    {
      (vecPush out (vecGet tvNodes i))
      (structCarrierOut tvNodes out (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub fn (checkStructFields tc args fields i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    ; A pending lambda stored into a declared arrow field
    ; grounds BY the declaration first (`groundArgByDecl`);
    ; what is STILL pending escapes: flushed (verified
    ; standalone, only the `{unknown}` refusals dropped) and
    ; skipped by the comparison, which has no arrow to compare
    ; against.
    {
      (let aty0 (checkExprH tc (vecGet args i) 0))
      {
        (let fty
          (if (== (nodeTy (vecGet fields i)) 0)
            mkSilentWild
            (nodeTyChild (vecGet fields i))))
        {
          (let aty (groundArgByDecl tc aty0 (Some fty) (spanOf (vecGet args i))))
          {
            ; Still pending against a GROUND field is a function
            ; where a value belongs (refused, as the standalone
            ; arrow was before grounding); against a variable
            ; field it is a generic payload (flushed and
            ; skipped - the comparison has no arrow to compare
            ; against).
            (if (== (isPendingNode aty) 1)
              (if (== (tyIsGround fty) 1)
                (refusePendingOperand tc aty (tyRender fty) (spanOf (vecGet args i)))
                (flushPending tc aty (spanOf (vecGet args i))))
              (if (== (tyCompat tc aty fty) 0)
                (emitMismatchTy tc fty aty (spanOf (vecGet args i)))
                ; Compatibility succeeded, so a direct mismatch owns
                ; nothing here; a value still unknown against a
                ; ground field refuses, and a reference smuggled
                ; through an `Int` field is still refused (AX3043).
                {
                  (if (&& (== (tyIsUnknown aty) 1) (== (tyIsGround fty) 1))
                    (refuseUnknownOperand tc (tyRender fty) aty (spanOf (vecGet args i))))
                  (emitPayloadSmuggled tc fty (vecGet args i) (spanOf (vecGet args i)))
                }))
            (checkStructFields tc args fields (+ i 1))
          }
        }
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Field access and field store. The base must be a plain constructor
; type; its struct's field types answer reads and check writes. A
; miss is AX3007 anchored at the FIELD NAME token. stage0 has a
; data-named-field fallback here; stage1's parser keeps no named
; constructor fields, so that road is empty by construction. The
; result type is I64 on every path stage0 answers I64 - including
; setf's SUCCESS - and the non-constructor-base complaint fires even
; on poison (stage0 has no guard there; probed, `found {unknown}`
; cascades), but stays quiet for the silent wildcard, which marks
; what stage1 alone cannot know.
;
; A RESOLVED struct field also STAMPS the node with the struct it
; resolved through - `stampFieldStruct` - because codegen has no
; checker to ask. Until 2026-08-21 codegen resolved `base.field` by
; name across every struct in the program, first declaring struct
; winning, the receiver's type never consulted; two structs sharing a
; field name at different offsets therefore read and wrote each
; other's slots. Measured on the QA sweep's four-line repro:
;
;   (pub struct P (tag : Int) (name : String))
;   (pub struct Q (name : String) (tag : Int))
;   (let ((q (Q "hello" 999999999999))) (println q.name))
;
; `check` OK, `build` success, SIGSEGV - `Q.name` is word 0 and `P`
; answered word 1, so an Int was dereferenced as a String header. The
; compiler's own 44 structs declare 25 field names at conflicting
; indices and survived only by never using this syntax at all
; (1,047 hand-written `memGetWord` accessors, zero `.field` uses).
; The checker is the one place the receiver's type is known, so the
; resolution is decided here and carried to the emitter in the node's
; word 6, which no expression node used. tests/selfhost/995 pins it.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkField (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))

; The field type with the receiver's type arguments put in place of
; the struct's declared variables. The environment pairs the two lists
; positionally, and `tyEnvSubst` inserts each argument once.
; `(struct Pair (a b) ..)`
; against `(Pair Int String)` binds `a` to `Int` and `b` to `String`.
;
; Answers `fty` untouched when the struct declares no variables, when
; the receiver carries no arguments, or when the two disagree in
; count. The last is not a silent pass: a receiver whose argument
; count differs from the declaration is a type this checker did not
; build, and substituting positionally through it would invent a
; correspondence.
(struct ResolvedFieldType
  (owner : StructEnt)
  (ty : ASTNode))

(:: resolveStructFieldType (-> (Option StructEnt) String Nodes (Option ResolvedFieldType)))
(fn (resolveStructFieldType owner name args)
  (if (let (Some ent) owner)
    (if (let (Some ty) (structFieldTy ent name))
      (Some (ResolvedFieldType :owner ent :ty (structSubstField ent ty args)))
      None)
    None))

(pub :: structSubstField (-> StructEnt ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode))
(pub fn (structSubstField ent fty args)
  {
    (let tvs (structEntTyvars ent))
    (if (nodeSame fty nilNode)
      fty
      (if (== (as tvs Int) 0)
        fty
        (if (== (as args Int) 0)
          fty
          (if (!= (vecLen tvs) (vecLen args))
            fty
            (if (== (vecLen tvs) 0)
              fty
              (tyEnvSubst fty (structSubstEnv tvs args vecNew 0)))))))
  })

; Inserted arguments belong to the receiver's scope. Substituting them
; again would turn `(Pair b a)`'s first field from `b` into `a`.
(pub :: structSubstEnv (-> Names Nodes TypeEnv Int TypeEnv))
(pub fn (structSubstEnv tvs args env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tvs))
    env
    {
      (vecPush env (TypeBinding :name (vecGet tvs i) :ty (vecGet args i)))
      (structSubstEnv tvs args env (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub fn (checkField tc e)
  {
    (let baseTy (tyResolve (checkExprH tc (nodeAChild e) 0)))
    (if (tyIsBareInt baseTy)
      (refuseUnsignedParam tc baseTy))
    (if (== (nodeTag baseTy) TAG_T_CON)
      {
        (let entFound (findStruct tc (nodeAName baseTy)))
        (if (== (wordSealedHere tc (findStructAnyVis tc (nodeAName baseTy))) 1)
          ; A word struct's field outside its module: AX3086, whatever
          ; `pub` says - and for a private one too, where the lookup
          ; above filtered the declaration and AX3007 would call a field
          ; that exists missing.
          {
            (emitWordSealedRead tc (findStructAnyVis tc (nodeAName baseTy)) (nodeBName e)
              (nodeSpan e))
            (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
          }
          (if (== (checkAmbiguousType tc TY_ANY (nodeAName baseTy) (nodeSpan e)) 1)
            ; The receiver's type name is one that two modules declare,
            ; and the field offsets differ between them. Reading either
            ; is a guess, and this is the site the original defect was
            ; OBSERVED at: `c.port` inside TeamB answering TeamA's slot
            ; 0, at exit 0, with no diagnostic from any pass.
            (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
            ; SUBSTITUTED, since structs gained type parameters. The
            ; declared field type mentions the STRUCT's variables -
            ; `render` is declared `(-> a String)` - and the receiver's
            ; own type carries what those variables turned out to be:
            ; `(ShowOf Int)` says `a` is `Int`. Handing back the declared
            ; type unsubstituted answers `(-> a String)` for a value the
            ; checker already knows is `(-> Int String)`, which is the
            ; second of the two errors this feature had to close.
            ;
            ; A struct with no parameters substitutes nothing and takes
            ; the same path it always did.
            {
              (let fieldFound
                (:: (resolveStructFieldType entFound (nodeBName e)
                      (nodeBChildren baseTy)) (Option ResolvedFieldType)))
              (if (let (Some field) fieldFound)
                {
                  (let ent (:: field.owner StructEnt))
                  (let fty (:: field.ty ASTNode))
                  (stampFieldStruct e ent)
                  ; A legacy word's field is the word. A packed word's
                  ; field is the shift and mask that unpack it.
                  (if (== (structEntIsWord (Some ent)) 1)
                    (if (== (structEntIsLegacyWord (Some ent)) 1)
                      (wordLowerRecord tc e (nodeAChild e))
                      (wordLowerRecord tc e
                        (wordExtractExpr tc ent (nodeBName e) (nodeAChild e) (nodeSpan e)))))
                  fty
                }
                ; Not a struct field. When the receiver names a
                ; DATA type in scope, the field resolves IN THAT
                ; TYPE: it must be declared by every constructor at
                ; the same word with the same type, because the
                ; emitter loads one index for the name. A field
                ; another constructor does not declare, or declares
                ; elsewhere, loads its slot or a word past a shorter
                ; block - `check` said OK and the program answered a
                ; neighbour's field, or died of SIGSEGV. A `match`
                ; emits the guard; this form cannot, so those shapes
                ; are refused. When the receiver names no known data
                ; type, the stage0 program-wide first match stays:
                ; tightening that would refuse programs stage0
                ; accepts.
                {
                  (let dentFound (findData tc (nodeAName baseTy)))
                  (if (let (Some dent) dentFound)
                    {
                      (let ctors (dataEntCtors dent))
                      (if (isNone (dataFieldTyInCtors ctors (nodeBName e) 0))
                        {
                          (emitFieldNotFound tc (nodeBName e) (nodeAName baseTy) (nodeSpan e))
                          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                        }
                        ; The field exists on a DATA constructor - but a
                        ; type with a NULLARY constructor may hand this
                        ; access an immediate tag (MM-VAL-8, rep 2), and
                        ; field access emits an unguarded load: `check`
                        ; said OK and `(let ((x (E))) x.v)` died of
                        ; SIGSEGV (MM-VAL-9a). A `match` emits the guard;
                        ; this form cannot, so on such a type it is
                        ; refused.
                        (if (== (dataHasNullary dent) 1)
                          {
                            (emitFieldOnMixed tc (nodeBName e) (dataEntName dent) (nodeSpan e))
                            (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                          }
                          {
                            (let agreedFound (dataAgreedFieldTy ctors (nodeBName e)))
                            (if (let (Some agreed) agreedFound)
                              {
                                (stampFieldData e dent)
                                (tyEnvSubst agreed
                                  (ctorPatEnv (ctorEntResTy (vecGet ctors 0)) baseTy vecNew))
                              }
                              {
                                (emitFieldNotOnAllCtors tc (nodeBName e) (nodeAName baseTy)
                                  (nodeSpan e))
                                (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                              })
                          }))
                    }
                    {
                      (let dtyFound (dataFieldTy tc (nodeBName e)))
                      (if (let (Some dty) dtyFound)
                        {
                          (let ownFound (dataFieldOwner tc (nodeBName e)))
                          (if (let (Some own) (:: (if (let (Some owner) ownFound)
                            (if (== (dataHasNullary owner) 1)
                              (Some owner)
                              None)
                            None) (Option DataEnt)))
                            {
                              (emitFieldOnMixed tc (nodeBName e) own.name (nodeSpan e))
                              (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                            }
                            {
                              (setNodeTyText e "")
                              dty
                            })
                        }
                        {
                          (emitFieldNotFound tc (nodeBName e) (nodeAName baseTy) (nodeSpan e))
                          (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
                        })
                    })
                })
            }))
      }
      {
        ; A pending base would render its raw node as the "found"
        ; type below; name the shape instead (same expectation).
        (if (== (isPendingNode baseTy) 1)
          (refusePendingOperand tc baseTy "struct or data type" (spanOf (nodeAChild e)))
          (if (&& (== (tyIsSilentWild baseTy) 0) (== (tyIsErr baseTy) 0))
            (emitMismatch tc "struct or data type" (tyRender baseTy) (spanOf (nodeAChild e)))))
        ; Poison, like every other refused read above: the access
        ; has been reported, and a type made up here would be
        ; compared again wherever the read flows.
        (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
      })
  })

; Record on a TAG_E_FIELD / TAG_E_SETF node which struct its field
; resolved through, under the spelling codegen's type table is keyed
; by: the declared name, or `Mod$Name` for a struct that arrived
; through an import - the alias `addQualified` registers beside the
; bare entry, and the one that cannot collide with a same-named
; struct from another module. Word 6 (`ty`) is 0 on both node kinds
; from the parser and the expander, and nothing between the checker
; and the emitter rebuilds them (`lowerConds` answers the node it was
; given), so the stamp survives to `emitField`/`emitSetF`.
(pub :: stampFieldStruct (-> ASTNode StructEnt ASTNode))
(pub fn (stampFieldStruct e ent)
  {
    (let name (as (structEntName ent) String))
    (let m (structEntModule ent))
    (setNodeTyText e
      (if (isNone m)
        name
        (concat (concat (structEntModuleName ent) "$") name)))
  })

; Every constructor agrees on the field's slot and type here. Keeping
; one resolved constructor key lets the emitter use that declaration
; even when another module defines the same field or constructor name.
(:: stampFieldData (-> ASTNode DataEnt ASTNode))
(fn (stampFieldData e dent)
  {
    (setNodeTyText e "")
    (setNodeBinderTyText e (ctorResolvedName (vecGet (dataEntCtors dent) 0)))
  })

; The type of a named constructor field, searching every data type in
; declaration order, or 0. Mirrors stage0's `find_data_field_by_name`.
(pub :: dataFieldTy (-> TC String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (dataFieldTy tc fname)
  (dataFieldTyFrom (tcDatasVec tc) fname 0))

; The DataEnt whose constructor declares the field - the same walk,
; answering the owner instead of the type, for MM-VAL-9a's refusal.
(pub :: dataFieldOwner (-> TC String (Option DataEnt)))
(pub fn (dataFieldOwner tc fname)
  (dataFieldOwnerFrom (tcDatasVec tc) fname 0))

(pub :: dataFieldOwnerFrom (-> (Vec DataEnt) String Int (Option DataEnt)))
(pub fn (dataFieldOwnerFrom datas fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen datas))
    None
    (if (isSome (dataFieldTyInCtors (dataEntCtors (vecGet datas i)) fname 0))
      (Some (vecGet datas i))
      (dataFieldOwnerFrom datas fname (+ i 1)))))

; Does the type declare a nullary constructor? Under MM-VAL-8 its
; values may then be immediate tags, and a field load on an immediate
; dereferences a small integer.
(pub :: dataHasNullary (-> DataEnt Int))
(pub fn (dataHasNullary dent)
  (dataHasNullaryIn (dataEntCtors dent) 0))

(pub :: dataHasNullaryIn (-> (Vec CtorEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (dataHasNullaryIn ctors i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    0
    (if (== (vecLen (ctorEntFieldTys (vecGet ctors i))) 0)
      1
      (dataHasNullaryIn ctors (+ i 1)))))

; A DATA field read is only sound when every constructor of the
; RECEIVER's type declares the field at the same word with the same
; type: the emitter resolves a data field to one index
; (`findDataField`), so any disagreement loads another
; constructor's slot, or a word past a shorter block.
; `dataAgreedFieldTy` answers the field's declared type when that
; holds, else None. Type agreement compares renderings, which observe
; without unifying: `tyCompat` would pin where this must only look.
(pub :: dataAgreedFieldTy (-> (Vec CtorEnt) String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (dataAgreedFieldTy ctors fname)
  (if (let (Some idx) (dataFirstDeclIdx ctors fname 0))
    (if (let (Some ty0) (dataFieldTyInCtors ctors fname 0))
      (if (== (dataCtorsAgree ctors fname 0 idx ty0) 1)
        (Some ty0)
        None)
      None)
    None))

; The declared index in the first constructor that declares the field.
(pub :: dataFirstDeclIdx (-> (Vec CtorEnt) String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (dataFirstDeclIdx ctors fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    None
    (if (let (Some declared) (ctorEntFieldNames (vecGet ctors i)))
      (if (let (Some index) (declaredFieldIndex declared fname 0))
        (Some index)
        (dataFirstDeclIdx ctors fname (+ i 1)))
      (dataFirstDeclIdx ctors fname (+ i 1)))))

; Every constructor must declare the same field position and rendered type.
(pub :: dataCtorsAgree (-> (Vec CtorEnt) String Int Int ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (dataCtorsAgree ctors fname i idx ty0)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    1
    (if (let (Some declared) (ctorEntFieldNames (vecGet ctors i)))
      (if (let (Some index) (declaredFieldIndex declared fname 0))
        (if (!= index idx)
          0
          {
            (let tys (ctorEntFieldTys (vecGet ctors i)))
            (if (>= index (vecLen tys))
              0
              (if (strEq (tyRender (vecGet tys index)) (tyRender ty0))
                (dataCtorsAgree ctors fname (+ i 1) idx ty0)
                0))
          })
        0)
      0)))

(pub :: emitFieldOnMixed (-> TC String String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitFieldOnMixed tc fname tyname span)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3070" "unsafe-field-access" span
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat "field access `." fname) "` is unsafe on `")
              (srcSpelling tyname))
            "`: the type has a nullary constructor, so a value may be an immediate tag with no fields to read")
          None
          (Some
            "match on the value instead, and read the field in the fieldful arm - a `match` emits the immediate-or-address guard that field access cannot")
          "an unguarded load on an immediate dereferences a small integer: this passed `check` and died of SIGSEGV (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-9a)")
        "the value may not be a block at all"))
    0
  })

; The sibling refusal: the receiver's type has no nullary
; constructor, but not every constructor declares the field - so a
; value built by another constructor has no such word, and the
; unguarded load reads its slot or a word past its block. Same
; code, same remedy as the nullary arm above.
(pub :: emitFieldNotOnAllCtors (-> TC String String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitFieldNotOnAllCtors tc fname tyname span)
  {
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3070" "unsafe-field-access" span
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat "field access `." fname) "` is unsafe on `")
              (srcSpelling tyname))
            "`: not every constructor declares that field, so a value built by another constructor has no such word to read")
          None
          (Some
            "match on the value instead, and read the field in the arm that has it - a `match` emits the tag guard that field access cannot")
          "an unguarded load on a value of another constructor reads its slot, or a word past its block: this passed `check` and died of SIGSEGV")
        "only some constructors declare this field"))
    0
  })

(pub :: dataFieldTyFrom (-> (Vec DataEnt) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (dataFieldTyFrom datas fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen datas))
    None
    (if (let (Some ty) (dataFieldTyInCtors (dataEntCtors (vecGet datas i)) fname 0))
      (Some ty)
      (dataFieldTyFrom datas fname (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: dataFieldTyInCtors (-> (Vec CtorEnt) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (dataFieldTyInCtors ctors fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    None
    {
      (let ctor (vecGet ctors i))
      (if (let (Some declared) ctor.fieldNames)
        (if (let (Some index) (declaredFieldIndex declared fname 0))
          (Some
            (if (< index (vecLen ctor.fieldTys))
              (vecGet ctor.fieldTys index)
              mkTErr))
          (dataFieldTyInCtors ctors fname (+ i 1)))
        (dataFieldTyInCtors ctors fname (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: declaredFieldIndex (-> Names String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (declaredFieldIndex declared fname i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen declared))
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr declared i) fname)
      (Some i)
      (declaredFieldIndex declared fname (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: emitFieldNotFound (-> TC String String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitFieldNotFound tc fname tyName span)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3007" "field-not-found" span
            (concat
              (concat (concat (concat "field `" fname) "` not found on type `")
                (srcSpelling tyName))
              "`")
            None
            (Some
              (concat (concat "check the field name and the definition of `" (srcSpelling tyName))
                "`")))
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "no field `" fname) "` on `") (srcSpelling tyName)) "`")))
      0
    }))

(pub :: checkSetField (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkSetField tc e)
  {
    (let baseTy (tyResolve (checkExprH tc (nodeAChild e) 0)))
    {
      (if (tyIsBareInt baseTy)
        (refuseUnsignedParam tc baseTy))
      (if (== (nodeTag baseTy) TAG_T_CON)
        {
          (let entFound (findStruct tc (nodeAName baseTy)))
          (if (== (wordSealedHere tc (findStructAnyVis tc (nodeAName baseTy))) 1)
            (emitWordSealedAccess tc (findStructAnyVis tc (nodeAName baseTy)) (nodeBName e)
              (nodeSpan e) "write")
            {
              (let fieldFound
                (:: (resolveStructFieldType entFound (nodeBName e)
                      (nodeBChildren baseTy)) (Option ResolvedFieldType)))
              (if (let (Some field) fieldFound)
                ; Field found: the VALUE is checked, against the
                ; field's type. On a miss the value is NEVER checked
                ; - stage0's rule, probed; its diagnostics are lost.
                ; The store is stamped as the read is - the write
                ; path was the worse half of the by-name defect:
                ; `(set q.tag 123)` stored an integer over a live
                ; String pointer when `P` declared `tag` first.
                ; A pending value grounds against the field's
                ; declared arrow, is inferred standalone into a
                ; variable field, or is refused into any other
                ; ground field: the compatibility below is lenient
                ; to pending nodes, so without this a function
                ; stored into any field would pass in silence
                ; with its body unchecked.
                {
                  (let ent (:: field.owner StructEnt))
                  (let fty (:: field.ty ASTNode))
                  (let vty
                    {
                      (let vty1 (checkExprH tc (nodeCChild e) 0))
                      (if (== (isPendingNode vty1) 1)
                        (if (== (nodeTag fty) TAG_T_ARR)
                          (groundArgByDecl tc vty1 (Some fty) (spanOf (nodeCChild e)))
                          (if (== (nodeTag fty) TAG_T_VAR)
                            (groundStandalone tc vty1 (spanOf (nodeCChild e)))
                            {
                              (refusePendingOperand tc vty1 (tyRender fty) (spanOf (nodeCChild e)))
                              vty1
                            }))
                        vty1)
                    })
                  {
                    (stampFieldStruct e ent)
                    ; `(set a.b v)` inside a `region` is a store into
                    ; `a`; the same rule as `checkSet`'s (AX3059).
                    (rgCheckFieldStore tc (nodeAChild e) vty (nodeSpan e))
                    ; The MUTABILITY refusal runs beside the type
                    ; check, not instead of it: a write to an
                    ; immutable field with the wrong type is two
                    ; mistakes and reporting one hides the other.
                    (if (== (structFieldMut ent (nodeBName e)) 0)
                      (emitSetImmutableField (nodeBName e) (nodeAName baseTy) (tyRender fty)
                        (nodeSpan e) tc))
                    (if (== (tyCompat tc vty fty) 0)
                      (emitMismatchTy tc fty vty (spanOf (nodeCChild e))))
                  }
                }
                (emitFieldNotFound tc (nodeBName e) (nodeAName baseTy) (nodeSpan e)))
            })
        }
        ; A pending base would render its raw node as the "found"
        ; type below; name the shape instead (same expectation).
        (if (== (isPendingNode baseTy) 1)
          (refusePendingOperand tc baseTy "struct or data type" (spanOf (nodeAChild e)))
          (if (&& (== (tyIsSilentWild baseTy) 0) (== (tyIsErr baseTy) 0))
            (emitMismatch tc "struct or data type" (tyRender baseTy) (spanOf (nodeAChild e))))))
      mkIntTy
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `set`. VALUE first, target second - probed, golden 200. The target:
; the innermost binding ANYWHERE in scope (module signatures
; included - stage0's lookup_var has no frame restriction, which is
; exactly what makes a top-level target AX3012 with an AX3004 cascade
; through the binding's declared type). A binder stage1 has no span
; for suppresses BOTH halves. An unbound name that any signature,
; or imported definition covers stays silent (stage0 answers
; AX3012 anchored in another file - bytes stage1 cannot render);
; otherwise AX3001 with candidates from scope alone.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkSet (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkSet tc e)
  {
    (let mut valTy (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0))
    {
      (let name (nodeAName e))
      (if (let (Some idx) (scopeFindIdx (tcScopeVec tc) name))
        {
          (let ent (vecGet (tcScopeVec tc) idx))
          {
            (let suppressed
              (if (== (sEntMutable ent) 1)
                (if (&& (!= (tcLamBase tc) 0) (< idx (tcLamBase tc)))
                  {
                    (emitSetCaptured name (nodeSpan e) (sEntBsp ent) (tcOutVec tc))
                    1
                  }
                  0)
                (if (== (sEntBsp ent) 0)
                  {
                    (emitSetOnParam name (nodeSpan e) tc)
                    1
                  }
                  {
                    (emitAX3012 name (nodeSpan e) (sEntBsp ent) tc)
                    0
                  })))
            {
              ; The slot's type, for codegen, as the `mut` binder
              ; carries it: a store into a slot of a reference type
              ; may take a share of what it stores (`emitSet`,
              ; `slotStoreCounts`, MM-LIFE-2c event 3).
              (if (== (sEntMutable ent) 1)
                (setNodeBinderTyChild e (sEntTy ent))
                e)
              ; A pending lambda stored into a cell escapes the
              ; bindings: flush it (verify standalone, drop only
              ; the `{unknown}` refusals) and skip the comparison,
              ; which has no arrow to compare against. Loads flow
              ; the node and ground at their own uses.
              ; A pending value grounds against the binding's
              ; declared arrow, is inferred standalone into an
              ; untyped or variable binding, or is refused into
              ; any other ground binding - rebound, so the
              ; region check below judges the TYPE, never the
              ; node.
              (if (== (isPendingNode valTy) 1)
                (set valTy (if (== (isPendingNode (sEntTy ent)) 1)
                  ; The BINDING is itself unresolved (a `let`
                  ; holding a lambda): infer it standalone,
                  ; then ground the value by the inferred
                  ; arrow - or accept leniently when the
                  ; binding is no arrow.
                  {
                    (let ety (groundStandalone tc (sEntTy ent) (spanOf e)))
                    (if (== (nodeTag ety) TAG_T_ARR)
                      (groundArgByDecl tc valTy (Some ety) (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                      {
                        (flushPending tc valTy (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                        valTy
                      })
                  }
                  (if (nodeSame (sEntTy ent) nilNode)
                    {
                      (flushPending tc valTy (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                      valTy
                    }
                    (if (== (nodeTag (sEntTy ent)) TAG_T_ARR)
                      (groundArgByDecl tc valTy (Some (sEntTy ent)) (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                      (if (== (tyIsGround (sEntTy ent)) 1)
                        {
                          (refusePendingOperand tc valTy (tyRender (sEntTy ent))
                            (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                          valTy
                        }
                        (groundStandalone tc valTy (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))))))
                (if (&& (== suppressed 0)
                      (&& (== (tyIsErr valTy) 0)
                        (&& (== (tyIsErr (sEntTy ent)) 0) (== (tyCompat tc valTy (sEntTy ent)) 0))))
                  (emitMismatchTy tc (sEntTy ent) valTy (spanOf (nodeBChild e)))
                  (if (&& (== suppressed 0)
                        (&& (== (tyIsErr valTy) 0)
                          (&& (== (tyIsErr (sEntTy ent)) 0)
                            (&& (== (tyIsUnknown valTy) 1) (== (tyIsGround (sEntTy ent)) 1)))))
                    (refuseUnknownOperand tc (tyRender (sEntTy ent)) valTy
                      (spanOf (nodeBChild e))))))
              ; Inside a `region`, a store to a binding bound before
              ; it opened may not carry a reference out (AX3059).
              (rgCheckStore tc idx valTy (nodeSpan e) name suppressed)
              (sEntTy ent)
            }
          }
        }
        (if (== (isBuiltinName name) 1)
          (emitSetNotBinding tc name (nodeSpan e) "a built-in")
          (if (== (setTargetCovered tc name) 1)
            (emitSetNotBinding tc name (nodeSpan e) "an imported function")
            {
              (let best newBest)
              {
                (offerScope best name (tcScopeVec tc) 0)
                (if (!= (nodeSpan e) 0)
                  (emitDiag tc
                    {
                      (let msg (concat (concat "undefined variable `" (srcSpelling name)) "`"))
                      (diagSetLabel
                        (if (let (Some cand) best.cand)
                          (mkDiagFix SEV_ERROR "AX3001" "undefined-variable" (nodeSpan e) msg None
                            (concat (concat "a similarly named binding `" cand)
                              "` is in scope; did you mean this?")
                            (diagFix (nodeSpan e) cand))
                          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3001" "undefined-variable" (nodeSpan e) msg None
                            (Some
                              "variables must be defined (via `fn`, a `let` binding, or a lambda parameter) before they are used; check for typos")))
                        (concat (concat "no binding named `" (srcSpelling name)) "` in scope"))
                    }))
                (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0)
              }
            })))
    }
  })

; An IMPORTED declaration of the name - its stored name carries a
; `$`. stage0 resolves such a target through the scope entry the
; merged walk parked and answers AX3012 anchored in the OTHER file;
; stage1 renders every span against one source, so silence is the
; sound subset. ONLY imported declarations qualify: an entry-file
; signature is already a scope entry when the walk has passed it, and
; when it has NOT - the signature comes later in the file - stage0
; answers AX3001 (probed), which the fallthrough here preserves. An
; entry-file sig-less `fn` also stays AX3001: a plain `fn` never
; parks a scope entry in stage0 either.
(pub :: setTargetCovered (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (setTargetCovered tc name)
  (setTargetCoveredFrom (tcAllDeclsVec tc) name 0))

(pub :: setTargetCoveredFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int Int))
(pub fn (setTargetCoveredFrom decls name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      {
        (let tag (nodeTag d))
        (if (&& (|| (== tag TAG_D_SIG) (== tag TAG_D_FN))
              (&& (== (nameMatches (nodeAName d) name) 1)
                (if (strEq (nodeAName d) name)
                  false
                  true)))
          1
          (setTargetCoveredFrom decls name (+ i 1)))
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; match. The scrutinee first; each arm's pattern binds its variables
; at the constructor's DECLARED field types - tyvars as written,
; never substituted, which is why tyCompat treats them as wildcards -
; then the arm body. Concrete arm types are joined for diagnostics;
; the match keeps the last arm's type. After every arm: exhaustiveness,
; only when no catch-all arm exists and the scrutinee's type is a known data type;
; the missing list is the declaration order, and the anchor is the
; SCRUTINEE. Pattern errors therefore precede body errors, and AX3005
; comes after everything in its match - probed order.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkMatch (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))

; One slot per source arm. Rejected, unexpanded arms have no checked
; result; poison, wildcard and unit types remain present results.
(pub type ArmTypes (Vec (Option ASTNode)))

; Lift an unavailable parser or checked-type handle at this boundary.
(pub :: armTypeOpt (-> ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (armTypeOpt ty)
  (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
    None
    (Some ty)))

; ERR-PROP-4: a self-recursive call in the scrutinee of a `match`.
; Evaluating the scrutinee runs before any arm, so the call is never
; in tail position: every nesting level holds a frame, and deep inputs
; die of stack overflow (32 bytes a call, SIGSEGV at 262,144 nesting
; levels against 5,000,000 flat for the arm-tail shape -
; docs/error-model.md ERR-PROP-3). The walk reports the first such
; call in pre-order, once per match, as a warning: a shallow
; recursion is correct, and an error would refuse working programs.
;
; What counts is a CALL to the enclosing function - an application
; whose spine head spells it, or, when the function takes no
; parameters, a bare reference, which `parseArgs` leaves as the head
; alone and `emitVar` emits as `call i64 @...()`. A bare reference to
; a function that takes parameters is its closure record, not a call,
; and stays silent. Shadowing is lexical: a `let`, `lambda`
; parameter list or match pattern binding the name means the global
; from there down, so the walk stops reporting under it (its
; initialiser still counts - it evaluates outside the binding), and a
; binding in the match's own scope means no occurrence can be the
; global at all. Nodes from macro templates count: a generated
; self-call holds its frame like a written one, and its span is the
; invocation's. Tags the walk does not know answer None, so a missed
; exotic shape stays silent rather than misfiring.
(pub :: selfCallIn (-> ASTNode String String Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (selfCallIn e name bare nullary shadowed)
  (if (|| (nodeSame e nilNode) (== shadowed 1))
    None
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag e))
      (if (|| (== tg TAG_E_INT) (|| (== tg TAG_E_FLT) (|| (== tg TAG_E_STR) (== tg TAG_E_CHAR))))
        None
        (if (== tg TAG_E_VAR)
          (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName e) name) (strEq (nodeAName e) bare))
            (if (== nullary 1)
              (Some e)
              None)
            None)
          (if (== tg TAG_E_APP)
            {
              (let hd (spineHead e))
              (if (&& (== (nodeTag hd) TAG_E_VAR)
                    (|| (strEq (nodeAName hd) name) (strEq (nodeAName hd) bare)))
                (Some e)
                {
                  (let a (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                  (if (let (Some a) a)
                    (Some a)
                    (selfCallIn (nodeBChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                })
            }
            (if (|| (== tg TAG_E_LET) (== tg TAG_E_LETM))
              {
                (let a (selfCallIn (nodeBChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                (if (let (Some a) a)
                  (Some a)
                  (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName e) name) (strEq (nodeAName e) bare))
                    None
                    (selfCallIn (nodeCChild e) name bare nullary shadowed)))
              }
              (if (== tg TAG_E_LAM)
                (if (== (strsHasName (nodeANames e) name bare 0) 1)
                  None
                  (selfCallIn (nodeBChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                (if (== tg TAG_E_IF)
                  {
                    (let a (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                    (if (let (Some a) a)
                      (Some a)
                      {
                        (let b (selfCallIn (nodeBChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                        (if (let (Some b) b)
                          (Some b)
                          (selfCallIn (nodeCChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                      })
                  }
                  (if (== tg TAG_E_BEGIN)
                    (selfCallInVec (nodeAChildren e) name bare nullary shadowed 0)
                    (if (== tg TAG_E_MATCH)
                      {
                        (let a (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                        (if (let (Some a) a)
                          (Some a)
                          (selfCallInArms (nodeBChildren e) name bare nullary shadowed 0))
                      }
                      (if (== tg TAG_E_FIELD)
                        (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed)
                        (if (== tg TAG_E_SET)
                          (selfCallIn (nodeBChild e) name bare nullary shadowed)
                          (if (== tg TAG_E_SETF)
                            {
                              (let a (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                              (if (let (Some a) a)
                                (Some a)
                                (selfCallIn (nodeCChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                            }
                            (if (|| (== tg TAG_E_WHILE) (== tg TAG_E_REGION))
                              (if (== tg TAG_E_REGION)
                                (selfCallIn (nodeBChild e) name bare nullary shadowed)
                                {
                                  (let a (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                                  (if (let (Some a) a)
                                    (Some a)
                                    (selfCallInVec (nodeBChildren e) name bare nullary shadowed 0))
                                })
                              (if (== tg TAG_E_HANDLE)
                                {
                                  (let a (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                                  (if (let (Some a) a)
                                    (Some a)
                                    (selfCallIn (nodeCChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                                }
                                (if (== tg TAG_E_SUBCHECK)
                                  {
                                    (let a (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name bare nullary shadowed))
                                    (if (let (Some a) a)
                                      (Some a)
                                      {
                                        (let b
                                          (selfCallInVec (nodeBChildren e) name bare nullary
                                            shadowed 0))
                                        (if (let (Some b) b)
                                          (Some b)
                                          (if (== (nodeC e) 0)
                                            None
                                            (selfCallIn (nodeCChild e) name bare nullary shadowed)))
                                      })
                                  }
                                  None))))))))))))))
    }))

; The first self-call across a vector of expressions, in order, or None.
(pub :: selfCallInVec (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String Int Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (selfCallInVec v name bare nullary shadowed i)
  (if (|| (== (as v Int) 0) (>= i (vecLen v)))
    None
    {
      (let a (selfCallIn (vecGet v i) name bare nullary shadowed))
      (if (let (Some a) a)
        (Some a)
        (selfCallInVec v name bare nullary shadowed (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The first self-call across match arms, in order, or None. An arm whose
; pattern binds the name shadows it for that arm's body only; the
; scrutinee-side walk already ran above this.
(pub :: selfCallInArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) String String Int Int Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (selfCallInArms arms name bare nullary shadowed i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    None
    {
      (let bs vecNew)
      {
        (patBinders (nodeAChild (vecGet arms i)) bs)
        {
          (let a
            (selfCallIn (nodeBChild (vecGet arms i)) name bare nullary
              (if (== (strsHasName bs name bare 0) 1)
                1
                shadowed)))
          (if (let (Some a) a)
            (Some a)
            (selfCallInArms arms name bare nullary shadowed (+ i 1)))
        }
      }
    }))

; Whether a vector of name strings holds either spelling, or 0 for no
; vector at all. The vector is `(Vec Int)` - names are words, read
; with `vecGetStr` - whatever its elements were pushed as.
(pub :: strsHasName (-> Names String String Int Int))
(pub fn (strsHasName v name bare i)
  (if (|| (== (as v Int) 0) (>= i (vecLen v)))
    0
    (if (|| (strEq (vecGetStr v i) name) (strEq (vecGetStr v i) bare))
      1
      (strsHasName v name bare (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: checkRecursionInScrutinee (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (checkRecursionInScrutinee tc e scrutTy errsBefore)
  {
    (let d (tcCurDeclNode tc))
    (if (let (Some d) d)
      (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
        0
        {
          (let name (nodeAName d))
          (if (isSome (scopeFindFrame tc name))
            0
            {
              (let found
                (selfCallIn (nodeAChild e) name (bareOf name)
                  (if (== (vecLen (nodeBNames d)) 0)
                    1
                    0)
                  0))
              (if (let (Some found) found)
                (if (|| (!= errsBefore (countErrorDiags (tcOutVec tc))) (== (tyIsErr scrutTy) 1))
                  0
                  (if (== (nodeSpan found) 0)
                    0
                    (emitDiag tc
                      (diagSetLabel
                        (mkDiag SEV_WARNING "AX3045" "recursion-in-scrutinee" (nodeSpan found)
                          (concat (concat "`" (bareOf name))
                            "` calls itself here, in the scrutinee of a `match`")
                          None
                          (Some
                            "bind the scrutinee in a `let` and answer the recursive call from a match arm, where the self call is a tail call and the loop conversion fires"))
                        (concat (concat "recursive call to `" (bareOf name)) "`")))))
                0)
            })
        })
      0)
  })

; A bare pattern name that names a nullary constructor is that
; constructor's test, not a binder: `None` in an arm must not swallow
; `(Some 5)`. Retag in place before the arms are checked, recursively,
; so codegen - which reads the same tree after the checker - tests the
; tag too. A fieldful constructor's bare name stays a binder: it
; cannot test without its fields. `true`/`false` stay literal tests.
(pub :: normCtorPat (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (normCtorPat tc pat)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag pat))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
      {
        (let name (nodeAName pat))
        (if (&& (&& (! (strEq name "_")) (! (strEq name "true")))
              (&& (! (strEq name "false")) (== (tcCtorOfArity tc name 0) 1)))
          (writeNodeWord pat 0 TAG_P_CON0)
          0)
      }
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        (normCtorPatVec tc (spineArgVec pat vecNew) 0)
        (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
          (normCtorPatVec tc (nodeCChildren pat) 0)
          0)))
  })

(pub :: normCtorPatVec (-> TC Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (normCtorPatVec tc args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (normCtorPat tc (vecGet args i))
      (normCtorPatVec tc args (+ i 1))
    }))

; Whether two patterns are the same literal test, or the same
; constructor headed test whose arguments all bind.
(pub :: patSameTest (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (patSameTest a b)
  {
    (let ta (nodeTag a))
    (if (!= ta (nodeTag b))
      0
      (if (|| (== ta TAG_E_INT) (== ta TAG_E_CHAR))
        (if (== (nodeA a) (nodeA b))
          1
          0)
        (if (== ta TAG_E_STR)
          (if (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b))
            1
            0)
          (if (== ta TAG_P_CON0)
            (if (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b))
              1
              0)
            (if (== ta TAG_E_APP)
              (if (let (Some left) (patCtorName a))
                (if (let (Some right) (patCtorName b))
                  (if (strEq left right)
                    (patArgsAllBinders (spineArgVec a vecNew) 0)
                    0)
                  0)
                0)
              (if (== ta TAG_P_CONNAMED)
                (if (strEq (nodeAName a) (nodeAName b))
                  (patArgsAllBinders (nodeCChildren a) 0)
                  0)
                0))))))
  })

(pub :: patArgsAllBinders (-> Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (patArgsAllBinders args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    1
    (if (== (patIrrefutable (vecGet args i)) 1)
      (patArgsAllBinders args (+ i 1))
      0)))

; Warn AX3134 on an arm an earlier arm already covers: after a
; catch-all, or repeating a literal or constructor test.
(pub :: checkUnreachableArms (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (checkUnreachableArms tc arms i catchallSeen)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (if (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
        (checkUnreachableArms tc arms (+ i 1) catchallSeen)
        {
          (let pat (nodeAChild arm))
          {
            (if (|| (== catchallSeen 1) (== (armsRepeatTest arms pat 0 i) 1))
              (emitDiag tc
                (mkDiag SEV_WARNING "AX3134" "unreachable-arm" (nodeSpan pat)
                  "this arm can never match: an earlier arm already covers it" None
                  (Some "remove the arm, or move it above the arm that covers it"))))
            (checkUnreachableArms tc arms (+ i 1)
              (if (== (patIrrefutable pat) 1)
                1
                catchallSeen))
          }
        })
    }))

(pub :: armsRepeatTest (-> (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode Int Int Int))
(pub fn (armsRepeatTest arms pat j end)
  (if (>= j end)
    0
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms j))
      (if (&& (!= (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR) (== (patSameTest (nodeAChild arm) pat) 1))
        1
        (armsRepeatTest arms pat (+ j 1) end))
    }))

(pub :: normCtorArms (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (normCtorArms tc arms i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      {
        (if (!= (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
          (normCtorPat tc (nodeAChild arm)))
        (normCtorArms tc arms (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub fn (checkMatch tc e disc)
  {
    (normCtorArms tc (nodeBChildren e) 0)
    (checkUnreachableArms tc (nodeBChildren e) 0 0)
    (let errsBefore (countErrorDiags (tcOutVec tc)))
    (let scrutTy (checkExprH tc (nodeAChild e) 0))
    (let recWarn (checkRecursionInScrutinee tc e scrutTy errsBefore))
    (let covered vecNew)
    (let catchall (memAlloc 8))
    {
      (memSetWord catchall 0 0)
      ; Matching ON a function is a shape error at the scrutinee:
      ; no pattern can match it, and the arms below would compare
      ; against a pending node in silence.
      (if (== (isPendingNode scrutTy) 1)
        (refusePendingOperand tc scrutTy "a matchable value" (spanOf (nodeAChild e))))
      ; The type exhaustiveness is decided over, and the one every
      ; constructor arm is held to. The scrutinee's own when the
      ; checker concluded one; when it concluded only a placeholder
      ; (a minted `_tN`, the silent wildcard - a type no arm could
      ; clash with), the type of the first constructor the arms name,
      ; which is what the arms claim the value is. Until 2026-08-21 a
      ; placeholder scrutinee skipped the check outright, so a match
      ; over it with constructors missing fell through to the result
      ; cell's 0 exactly as MM-VAL-9b's literal matches once did.
      ; A match whose arms cover no constructor and bind no
      ; catch-all can FALL THROUGH: when nothing matched, the
      ; merge block answered the result cell's zeroed contents -
      ; 0, at exit 0, indistinguishable from a real result
      ; (MM-VAL-9b: `(match 7 ((1) 11) ((2) 22))` checked OK and
      ; answered 0). Constructor matches are exhaustiveness-
      ; checked above; `true`/`false` are literal TESTS that
      ; cover nothing in `covered`, so a match carrying BOTH is
      ; the one all-literal shape that cannot fall through.
      ; A `()` arm makes the `match` answer `()`, and the other arms'
      ; values are dropped (`armJoin` accepts the mix); so does a
      ; dropped `match` whose arms disagree.
      {
        (let exhTy
          (:: (if (== (nodeTag scrutTy) TAG_T_CON)
            (Some scrutTy)
            (if (&& (== (nodeTag scrutTy) TAG_T_VAR) (== (tyIsSrcVar scrutTy) 0))
              (armsCtorTy tc (nodeBChildren e) 0)
              None)) (Option ASTNode)))
        {
          (let tys (:: vecNew ArmTypes))
          (let resTy0
            (checkArms tc (nodeBChildren e) 0 covered catchall (freshTVar tc 0) scrutTy
              (match exhTy
                ((None) scrutTy)
                ((Some ty) ty))
              (:: None (Option ASTNode)) tys disc))
          {
            (let resTy
              (if (|| (matchArmsUnit tys 0)
                    (&& (== disc 1) (! (matchArmsAgree tc tys 0 (:: None (Option ASTNode))))))
                {
                  (discardArms tc (nodeBChildren e) tys 0)
                  mkUnitTy
                }
                resTy0))
            {
              ; Poison (ERR-DIAG-3): an arm naming a constructor
              ; that does not exist has drawn AX3003, and what
              ; it was meant to cover is unknown, so "missing
              ; Sq" beside it would be a guess about a typo.
              {
                (let ctorChecked
                  (if (let (Some exhaustiveTy) exhTy)
                    (if (== (memGetWord catchall 0) 0)
                      {
                        (let dentFound (findData tc (nodeAName exhaustiveTy)))
                        (if (let (Some dent) dentFound)
                          {
                            (if (! (armsNameUnknownCtor tc (nodeBChildren e) 0))
                              (checkExhaustiveMatrix tc (nodeBChildren e) exhaustiveTy
                                (nodeAChild e)))
                            1
                          }
                          0)
                      }
                      0)
                    0))
                (if (&& (== ctorChecked 0)
                      (&& (== (memGetWord catchall 0) 0)
                        (&& (== (vecLen covered) 0)
                          (&& (> (vecLen (nodeBChildren e)) 0)
                            (&& (== (armsBoolComplete (nodeBChildren e)) 0)
                              (&& (== (tyIsErr scrutTy) 0) (!= (spanOf (nodeAChild e)) 0)))))))
                  (emitDiag tc
                    (diagSetLabel
                      (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3005" "non-exhaustive-match" (spanOf (nodeAChild e))
                        "this match can fall through: no arm is a constructor and nothing catches the rest"
                        None (Some "add a `_` arm")
                        "a match no arm matched used to answer 0 silently at exit 0 - the result cell's zeroed contents, indistinguishable from a real answer (docs/memory-model.md MM-VAL-9b)")
                      "no arm catches a non-matching value")))
              }
              resTy
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  })

; The declared result type of the first constructor the arms name -
; `Color` for `((Red) 1)`, `Option a` for `((Some x) x)` - or None when
; no arm is a constructor pattern or none resolves. The shapes are
; the three `checkPatternTyped` dispatches as constructors.
(pub :: armsCtorTy (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (armsCtorTy tc arms i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    None
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (let cname
        (:: (if (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
          None
          (patCtorHeadName (nodeAChild arm))) (Option String)))
      (let ctor
        (:: (if (let (Some name) cname)
          (findCtor tc name)
          None) (Option CtorEnt)))
      (if (let (Some entry) ctor)
        {
          (let resTy entry.resTy)
          (if (== (nodeTag resTy) TAG_T_CON)
            (Some resTy)
            (armsCtorTy tc arms (+ i 1)))
        }
        (armsCtorTy tc arms (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The constructor a pattern names, or None for a binder, a literal, or
; a shape that names none.
(pub :: patCtorHeadName (-> ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (patCtorHeadName pat)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag pat))
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_P_CON0) (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED))
      (Some (nodeAName pat))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (let head (spineHead pat))
          (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
            (Some (nodeAName head))
            None)
        }
        None))
  })

; Every arm a literal `true`/`false` test, both present - the one
; all-literal match that cannot fall through. `recordCoverage` pushes
; nothing for these (they are tests, not binders - the hygiene slice
; measured why), so `covered` cannot make the distinction.
(pub :: armsBoolComplete (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (armsBoolComplete arms)
  {
    (let mut hasT 0)
    (let mut hasF 0)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen arms)
        {
          (let p (nodeAChild (vecGet arms i)))
          (if (== (nodeTag p) TAG_E_VAR)
            {
              (if (strEq (nodeAName p) "true")
                (set hasT 1))
              (if (strEq (nodeAName p) "false")
                (set hasF 1))
            })
        })
      (if (&& (== hasT 1) (== hasF 1))
        1
        0)
    }
  })

; `scrutTy` is threaded so a bare-identifier arm binds at the type the
; match is OVER.
;
; It used to pass a literal 0 here, and `checkPatternTyped`'s 0 case
; binds at `mkSilentWild` - `(mkTVar "")`, which `tyCompat` matches
; against everything. So the binder of a catch-all arm was a hole in
; the type system: `(fn (f n) (match n (x (strLen x))))` declared
; `(-> Int Int)` type-checked clean, because `x` was compatible with
; `String`. That is the same String/Int fiat that
; tests/diagnostics/555-string-int-distinct.ax pins as deleted,
; reachable through a match arm instead of through a parameter;
; tests/diagnostics/556-match-binder-typed.ax pins this half. A NESTED
; binder was never affected: `bindPatternArgs` already binds each one
; at its field type.
;
; Both callers provide a type: this walk uses the scrutinee's type,
; while nested patterns use an instantiated field type, a fresh variable
; for an excess argument, or poison for an unknown constructor. The nil
; guard remains for malformed shared AST input.
; `fitTy` is the type the arms' CONSTRUCTORS and literals are held to
; (`ctorPatFits`): the scrutinee's own, or, when that is only a
; placeholder, the type of the first constructor the arms name - so
; `((Red) 1) ((Big) 2)` over a lambda parameter refuses `Big` instead
; of checking exhaustiveness over whichever type came first. Binders
; still bind at `scrutTy`.
(pub :: checkArms
  (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Ints Int ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode (Option ASTNode) ArmTypes Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkArms tc arms i covered catchall resTy scrutTy fitTy joinTy tys disc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    resTy
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (if (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR)
        ; An UNEXPANDED `syntax/for` arm. The main pipelines never
        ; deliver one - phase E refuses it and substitutes a
        ; poison arm - but the REPL's bare-expression path types
        ; WITHOUT expansion (stage0 parity), and walking this
        ; node's words as a pattern and a body reads a Str handle
        ; as a node: for a one-byte binder the length word
        ; happens to mimic TAG_E_INT and the walk survives by
        ; luck; a longer one is garbage. Refuse it as the
        ; expander would, and skip the arm.
        {
          (emitDiag tc
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3028" "syntax-query" (nodeSpan arm)
              "syntax/for stands in a macro template's match, not in ordinary code" None
              (Some
                "iteration is an expansion-time facility: move the match into a declaration macro's template, or write the arms by hand")
              "the query vocabulary is CLOSED and compiler-implemented (macro-system.md MAC-CAP-5/6): every query is answered from the declaration list at expansion time"))
          (vecPush tys (:: None (Option ASTNode)))
          (checkArms tc arms (+ i 1) covered catchall resTy scrutTy fitTy joinTy tys disc)
        }
        {
          (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
          {
            (recordCoverage (nodeAChild arm) covered catchall)
            (checkDupPatBinders tc (nodeAChild arm))
            (checkUpperPatBinders tc (nodeAChild arm))
            (checkPatternTyped tc (nodeAChild arm) scrutTy fitTy)
            ; An arm's value is dropped when the match's is.
            {
              (let t
                {
                  (tcSetDiscard tc disc)
                  (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild arm) 0)
                })
              {
                (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
                (vecPush tys (armTypeOpt t))
                (checkArms tc arms (+ i 1) covered catchall t scrutTy fitTy
                  (armJoin tc joinTy t (nodeBChild arm) disc) tys disc)
              }
            }
          }
        })
    }))

; The arms of one match must agree, as an `if`'s branches do. `joinTy`
; is the first arm type that says something: not poisoned, and not a
; placeholder the checker minted, which is what `todo` and every other
; diverging call answer. Each later arm is held to it, and the report
; anchors at the arm that disagrees. The match still answers its last
; arm's type, so a program that already checked types the same way.
(pub :: armJoin (-> TC (Option ASTNode) ASTNode ASTNode Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (armJoin tc joinTy t body disc)
  (if (armTyOpen t)
    joinTy
    (if (let (Some first) joinTy)
      (if (|| (tyIsUnit first) (tyIsUnit t))
        (Some mkUnitTy)
        {
          ; A dropped match's arms need not agree (`checkMatch`).
          (if (&& (== disc 0) (== (tyCompat tc first t) 0))
            (emitMismatchTy tc first t (armTailSpan body)))
          joinTy
        })
      (Some t))))

; Do the arms' types agree with the first one that says something?
(pub :: matchArmsAgree (-> TC ArmTypes Int (Option ASTNode) Bool))
(pub fn (matchArmsAgree tc tys i first)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tys))
    true
    {
      (let found (vecGet tys i))
      (if (let (Some t) found)
        (if (armTyOpen t)
          (matchArmsAgree tc tys (+ i 1) first)
          (if (let (Some initial) first)
            (if (== (tyCompat tc initial t) 0)
              false
              (matchArmsAgree tc tys (+ i 1) first))
            (matchArmsAgree tc tys (+ i 1) (Some t))))
        (matchArmsAgree tc tys (+ i 1) first))
    }))

(pub :: matchArmsUnit (-> ArmTypes Int Bool))
(pub fn (matchArmsUnit tys i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tys))
    false
    (if (if (let (Some ty) (vecGet tys i))
      (tyIsUnit ty)
      false)
      true
      (matchArmsUnit tys (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: discardArms (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) ArmTypes Int Int))
(pub fn (discardArms tc arms tys i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (if (< i (vecLen tys))
        (if (let (Some ty) (vecGet tys i))
          (discardToUnit tc (nodeBChild (vecGet arms i)) ty)))
      (discardArms tc arms tys (+ i 1))
    }))

; Where an arm's value comes from: the last expression of a block or
; a `let` body, which is what a reader has to change.
(pub :: armTailSpan (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (armTailSpan e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag e))
      {
        (let inner
          (:: (if (|| (== tg TAG_E_LET) (== tg TAG_E_LETM))
            (armTypeOpt (nodeCChild e))
            (if (== tg TAG_E_BEGIN)
              {
                (let v (nodeAChildren e))
                (if (== (vecLen v) 0)
                  None
                  (armTypeOpt (vecGet v (- (vecLen v) 1))))
              }
              None)) (Option ASTNode)))
        (if (let (Some child) inner)
          {
            (let sp (armTailSpan child))
            (if (== sp 0)
              (spanOf e)
              sp)
          }
          (spanOf e))
      }
    }))

(pub :: armTyOpen (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (armTyOpen t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    true
    (if (== (tyIsErr t) 1)
      true
      (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_VAR)
        (== (tyIsSrcVar t) 0)
        false))))

; What an arm covers: a parenthesised nullary constructor or a
; fieldful pattern's head name. A bare identifier is a catch-all -
; except `true`/`false`, which are literal TESTS (codegen agrees),
; and literals, which cover nothing.
;
; This is deliberately SHALLOW, and no longer decides exhaustiveness:
; `covered` now feeds only the MM-VAL-9b fall-through guard above,
; which asks whether any arm is a constructor at all. Whether the
; constructor arms TOGETHER cover the type is `checkExhaustiveMatrix`'s
; question, and it has to look at sub-patterns to answer it.
; Whether any arm's pattern, at any depth, names a constructor no
; declaration introduces: the arm `checkCtorPattern` refused.
(pub :: armsNameUnknownCtor (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Bool))
(pub fn (armsNameUnknownCtor tc arms i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    false
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (if (&& (!= (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR) (patNamesUnknownCtor tc (nodeAChild arm)))
        true
        (armsNameUnknownCtor tc arms (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: patNamesUnknownCtor (-> TC ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (patNamesUnknownCtor tc pat)
  (if (nodeSame pat nilNode)
    false
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag pat))
      (if (|| (== tag TAG_P_CON0) (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED))
        (isNone (findCtor tc (nodeAName pat)))
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (let head (spineHead pat))
            (if (&& (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR) (isNone (findCtor tc (nodeAName head))))
              true
              (patArgsNameUnknownCtor tc (spineArgVec pat vecNew) 0))
          }
          false))
    }))

(pub :: patArgsNameUnknownCtor (-> TC Nodes Int Bool))
(pub fn (patArgsNameUnknownCtor tc args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    false
    (if (patNamesUnknownCtor tc (vecGet args i))
      true
      (patArgsNameUnknownCtor tc args (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: recordCoverage (-> ASTNode Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (recordCoverage pat covered catchall)
  ; A NAMED constructor arm covers its constructor exactly as a
  ; positional one does - stage0's exhaustiveness treats `PCon` and
  ; `PConNamed` in the same arm of the same match.
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag pat))
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_P_CON0) (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED))
      {
        (vecPush covered (nodeA pat))
        0
      }
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (let head (spineHead pat))
          (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
            {
              (vecPush covered (nodeA head))
              0
            }
            0)
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName pat) "true") (strEq (nodeAName pat) "false"))
            0
            {
              (memSetWord catchall 0 1)
              0
            })
          0)))
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Exhaustiveness, by SPECIALIZATION over a pattern matrix.
;
; The check used to be one shallow set difference: collect each arm's
; head constructor into `covered`, subtract from the type's
; declaration list, report the remainder. That answers the question
; only for a match whose arms are all one level deep. It reads
; `(Cons h (Cons h2 t))` as covering `Cons` ENTIRELY, because it never
; looks past the head - so
;
;     (match l ((Nil) 7) ((Cons h (Cons h2 t)) (+ h h2)))
;
; type-checked clean and then answered 0 for a one-element list: the
; one `Cons` shape no arm matched fell through to the merge block's
; zeroed result cell, at exit 0, indistinguishable from a real answer.
; That is MM-VAL-9b's silent 0 reached through a constructor arm
; instead of a literal one, and at a reference field type it is a null
; handle rather than a 0, so the same hole segfaults.
;
; The replacement is Maranget's usefulness check restricted to the
; exhaustiveness question ("Warnings for pattern matching", JFP 2007),
; which is the standard algorithm and is both SOUND and COMPLETE: it
; rejects exactly the matches that can fall through, so it neither
; misses `(Cons h (Cons h2 t))` nor wrongly refuses
; `((Wrap (Red)) ...) ((Wrap (Green)) ...)`, which a coarse
; "any refutable sub-pattern disqualifies" rule does refuse - fixtures
; 740, 810, 620, 270 and 120 are all that shape and are all total.
;
; The state is a MATRIX: one row per arm, one column per value still
; to be discriminated, plus a parallel vector of the columns' types.
; The recursion is the textbook one:
;
;   no columns left     -> exhaustive iff some row survives
;   column 0's type is
;     not a `data` type -> nothing to enumerate (Int, String, a
;                          tyvar), so only a binder covers it: recurse
;                          on the DEFAULT matrix, or fail
;     a `data` type     -> if every constructor appears in column 0,
;                          recurse into each one's SPECIALIZED matrix
;                          with its field types spliced in front;
;                          otherwise a binder must cover the rest, so
;                          recurse on the DEFAULT matrix, or fail with
;                          the missing constructors as the witness
;
; None means exhaustive. Some names is the first missing witness;
; Some empty means values are missing without enumerable names. The
; typed result preserves AX3005's message, label and witness order.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Does this pattern bind unconditionally? Only a bare identifier does
; - `_` and any binder - and `true`/`false` are the exception, since
; those are literal TESTS (codegen agrees, and `armsBoolComplete`
; above reads them the same way). Every other shape can fail to match.
(pub :: patIrrefutable (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (patIrrefutable pat)
  (if (== (nodeTag pat) TAG_E_VAR)
    (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName pat) "true") (strEq (nodeAName pat) "false"))
      0
      1)
    0))

; Some bare constructor name, or None for a binder or a literal. A literal tests something this algorithm cannot enumerate,
; so it names no constructor and is covered by neither branch - which
; is why a literal-only arm leaves its constructor missing.
(pub :: patCtorName (-> ASTNode (Option String)))
(pub fn (patCtorName pat)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag pat))
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_P_CON0) (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED))
      (Some (bareOf (nodeAName pat)))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (let head (spineHead pat))
          (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
            (Some (bareOf (nodeAName head)))
            None)
        }
        None))
  })

; A constructor pattern's sub-patterns, positionally. A NAMED pattern
; is reordered against the constructor's declared fields first, and an
; omitted field becomes `_` - the same spine `checkPatternTyped`
; builds, so the two agree on what each field position holds.
(pub :: patCtorArgs (-> TC ASTNode Nodes))
(pub fn (patCtorArgs tc pat)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag pat))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
      (spineArgVec pat vecNew)
      (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
        (if (let (Some ctor) (findCtor tc (nodeAName pat)))
          (if (let (Some declared) ctor.fieldNames)
            (spineArgVec
              (namedPatSpine (nodeAName pat) declared (nodeBChildren pat) (nodeCChildren pat)
                (nodeSpan pat))
              vecNew)
            vecNew)
          vecNew)
        vecNew))
  })

; Row helpers. A row is a Vec of patterns; `rowFrom` copies the tail
; from `i`, `vecAppendFrom` concatenates.
(pub :: rowTail (-> Nodes Nodes))
(pub fn (rowTail row)
  (vecAppendFrom row vecNew 1))

; `n` wildcards, for a binder row meeting a constructor of arity `n`.
(pub :: pushWilds (-> Nodes Int Int Nodes))
(pub fn (pushWilds acc n i)
  (if (>= i n)
    acc
    {
      (vecPush acc (mkEVar "_"))
      (pushWilds acc n (+ i 1))
    }))

; Any row whose first column binds unconditionally.
(pub :: colHasWild (-> NodesRows Int Int))
(pub fn (colHasWild matrix i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen matrix))
    0
    (if (== (patIrrefutable (vecGet (vecGetVec matrix i) 0)) 1)
      1
      (colHasWild matrix (+ i 1)))))

; Any row whose first column tests constructor `cn`.
(pub :: colHasCtor (-> NodesRows String Int Int))
(pub fn (colHasCtor matrix cn i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen matrix))
    0
    {
      (let found (patCtorName (vecGet (vecGet matrix i) 0)))
      (let same
        (match found
          ((None) false)
          ((Some name) (strEq name cn))))
      (if same
        1
        (colHasCtor matrix cn (+ i 1)))
    }))

; The constructors of this type that column 0 never tests.
(pub :: matMissing (-> NodesRows (Vec CtorEnt) Strings Int Strings))
(pub fn (matMissing matrix ctors acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    acc
    {
      {
        (let cn (bareOf (ctorEntName (vecGet ctors i))))
        (if (== (colHasCtor matrix cn 0) 0)
          (vecPush acc cn))
      }
      (matMissing matrix ctors acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; S(c, P): rows that can match `c`, with `c`'s fields spliced in.
; A binder row matches every constructor and contributes wildcards.
; An arity-mismatched pattern already drew AX3009; pad it so the
; matrix stays rectangular and the walk cannot read past a row.
(pub :: matSpecialize (-> TC NodesRows String Int NodesRows Int NodesRows))
(pub fn (matSpecialize tc matrix cn arity acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen matrix))
    acc
    {
      {
        (let row (vecGet matrix i))
        {
          (let p (vecGet row 0))
          (if (== (patIrrefutable p) 1)
            (vecPush acc (vecAppendFrom (rowTail row) (pushWilds vecNew arity 0) 0))
            (if (let (Some name) (patCtorName p))
              (if (strEq name cn)
                {
                  (let args (patCtorArgs tc p))
                  (vecPush acc
                    (vecAppendFrom (rowTail row)
                      (pushWilds (vecAppendFrom args vecNew 0) arity (vecLen args)) 0))
                })))
        }
      }
      (matSpecialize tc matrix cn arity acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; D(P): rows whose first column binds unconditionally, minus it.
; Is the column's type exactly `Bool`?
(pub :: tyIsBoolTy (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tyIsBoolTy t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      0
      (if (strEq (nodeAName t) "Bool")
        1
        0))))

; Any row whose first column is the literal `lit`.
(pub :: colHasBoolLit (-> NodesRows String Int Int))
(pub fn (colHasBoolLit matrix lit i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen matrix))
    0
    {
      (let p (vecGet (vecGetVec matrix i) 0))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag p) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName p) lit))
        1
        (colHasBoolLit matrix lit (+ i 1)))
    }))

; S(lit, P) for a Bool literal: the rows that test `lit`, and the
; binder rows, minus the column.
(pub :: matBoolRows (-> NodesRows String NodesRows Int NodesRows))
(pub fn (matBoolRows matrix lit acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen matrix))
    acc
    {
      {
        (let row (vecGet matrix i))
        {
          (let p (vecGet row 0))
          (if (|| (== (patIrrefutable p) 1)
                (&& (== (nodeTag p) TAG_E_VAR) (strEq (nodeAName p) lit)))
            (vecPush acc (rowTail row)))
        }
      }
      (matBoolRows matrix lit acc (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: matDefault (-> NodesRows NodesRows Int NodesRows))
(pub fn (matDefault matrix acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen matrix))
    acc
    {
      {
        (let row (vecGet matrix i))
        (if (== (patIrrefutable (vecGet row 0)) 1)
          (vecPush acc (rowTail row)))
      }
      (matDefault matrix acc (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: matExhaustive (-> TC NodesRows Nodes (Option Names)))
(pub fn (matExhaustive tc matrix types)
  (if (== (vecLen types) 0)
    (if (> (vecLen matrix) 0)
      None
      (Some vecNew))
    {
      (let ty (vecGet types 0))
      (let rest (rowTail types))
      (let dent
        (if (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
          (findData tc (nodeAName ty))
          None))
      (if (let (Some dataEntry) dent)
        {
          (let missing (matMissing matrix (dataEntCtors dataEntry) vecNew 0))
          (if (> (vecLen missing) 0)
            (if (== (colHasWild matrix 0) 1)
              (matExhaustive tc (matDefault matrix vecNew 0) rest)
              (Some missing))
            (matCtorsExh tc matrix rest (dataEntCtors dataEntry) ty 0))
        }
        (if (== (tyIsBoolTy ty) 1)
          (if (== (colHasWild matrix 0) 1)
            (matExhaustive tc (matDefault matrix vecNew 0) rest)
            (if (&& (== (colHasBoolLit matrix "true" 0) 1) (== (colHasBoolLit matrix "false" 0) 1))
              {
                (let missing (matExhaustive tc (matBoolRows matrix "true" vecNew 0) rest))
                (if (let (Some names) missing)
                  (Some names)
                  (matExhaustive tc (matBoolRows matrix "false" vecNew 0) rest))
              }
              {
                (let missing vecNew)
                (if (== (colHasBoolLit matrix "true" 0) 0)
                  (vecPush missing "true"))
                (if (== (colHasBoolLit matrix "false" 0) 0)
                  (vecPush missing "false"))
                (Some missing)
              }))
          (if (== (colHasWild matrix 0) 1)
            (matExhaustive tc (matDefault matrix vecNew 0) rest)
            (Some vecNew))))
    }))

; `ty` is the column's own type, and the constructor's field types
; are spliced in AT ITS ARGUMENTS: over `(Option (Option Int))` the
; field of `Some` is `(Option Int)`, an enumerable column, not the
; declaration's `a`. Spliced as declared until 2026-08-21, the inner
; column was a type variable nothing but a binder could cover, so
; `((Some (Some x)) ..) ((Some (None)) ..) ((None) ..)` was AX3005
; "some values are not covered" - the QA sweep's lead, confirmed by
; its refutation pass. `tyEnvSubst` replaces what the column pins and
; leaves the rest as written; it mints nothing, so the placeholder
; numbering every later diagnostic in the file renders is untouched.
(pub :: matCtorsExh (-> TC NodesRows Nodes (Vec CtorEnt) ASTNode Int (Option Names)))
(pub fn (matCtorsExh tc matrix rest ctors ty i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    None
    {
      (let c (vecGet ctors i))
      (let fieldTys (tyEnvSubstVec c.fieldTys (ctorPatEnv c.resTy ty vecNew) vecNew 0))
      (let missing
        (matExhaustive tc (matSpecialize tc matrix (bareOf c.name) (vecLen fieldTys) vecNew 0)
          (vecAppendFrom rest (vecAppendFrom fieldTys vecNew 0) 0)))
      (if (let (Some names) missing)
        (Some names)
        (matCtorsExh tc matrix rest ctors ty (+ i 1)))
    }))

; `tyInstIn` without the minting: a source variable the environment
; maps is replaced, one it does not is answered as written.
(pub :: tyEnvSubst (-> ASTNode TypeEnv ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyEnvSubst t env)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    nilNode
    {
      (let tg (nodeTag t))
      (if (== tg TAG_T_VAR)
        (if (== (tyIsSrcVar t) 0)
          t
          (tyEnvSubstVar t (nodeAName t) env 0))
        (if (== tg TAG_T_CON)
          (mkTCon (nodeAName t) (tyEnvSubstVec (nodeBChildren t) env vecNew 0))
          (if (== tg TAG_T_ARR)
            (mkTArr (tyEnvSubst (nodeAChild t) env) (tyEnvSubst (nodeBChild t) env))
            (if (== tg TAG_T_TUP)
              (mkTTup (tyEnvSubstVec (nodeAChildren t) env vecNew 0))
              (if (== tg TAG_T_PTR)
                (mkTPtr (tyEnvSubst (nodeAChild t) env))
                t)))))
    }))

(pub :: tyEnvSubstVar (-> ASTNode String TypeEnv Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (tyEnvSubstVar t name env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen env))
    t
    (if (strEq (typeBindingName (vecGet env i)) name)
      (typeBindingTy (vecGet env i))
      (tyEnvSubstVar t name env (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: tyEnvSubstVec (-> Nodes TypeEnv Nodes Int Nodes))
(pub fn (tyEnvSubstVec xs env acc i)
  (if (== (as xs Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen xs))
      acc
      {
        (vecPush acc (tyEnvSubst (vecGet xs i) env))
        (tyEnvSubstVec xs env acc (+ i 1))
      })))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The fix AX3005 and AX3042 carry, and the bytes it is written against.
;
; A machine-applicable fix is a byte-range substitution in the file
; the diagnostic names (docs/diagnostics.md, `~>`), and the checker
; holds every unit's source in its typed unit table. Only the entry
; file is ever offered one from here. A
; diagnostic on an imported module's declaration renders against that
; module's bytes, and a fix into a file the request did not name is an
; edit the editor would apply to the wrong document - lsp.ax's
; `lspQuickfixes` keeps unit 0 for the same reason. `tcEntrySrc`
; answers None outside unit 0 or without an entry unit. An empty file
; remains Some empty text; the source scanners find no edit position.
;
; The scanners below are `lspFormEnd`'s, with the same string, char,
; comment and identifier skips, so a `)` inside a literal or a comment
; is not a paren. They live here rather than in lsp.ax because the
; checker is what knows WHICH form: the language server only carries
; what the diagnostic already says.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: tcEntrySrc (-> TC (Option String)))
(pub fn (tcEntrySrc tc)
  (if (!= (tcCurUnit tc) 0)
    None
    {
      (let units (tcUnits tc))
      (if (== (vecLen units) 0)
        None
        (Some (unitSrc (vecGet units 0))))
    }))

; The byte just past the `)` closing the form that opens at `o`, or
; None when no complete form opens at that position.
(pub :: formEndFrom (-> String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (formEndFrom src o)
  (if (|| (< o 0) (>= o (strLen src)))
    None
    (if (!= (strByte src o) 40)
      None
      {
        (let n (strLen src))
        (let mut i o)
        (let mut depth 0)
        (let mut stop (:: None (Option Int)))
        {
          (while (if (< i n)
            (isNone stop)
            false)
            {
              (let ch (strByte src i))
              (if (== ch 34)
                (set i (skipStringBody src (+ i 1) n))
                (if (isIdentStart ch)
                  (set i (skipIdent src (+ i 1) n))
                  (if (== ch 39)
                    (set i (skipCharBody src (+ i 1) n))
                    (if (&& (== ch 35) (isBlockOpen src i n))
                      (set i (skipBlockComment src (+ i 2) n))
                      (if (== ch 59)
                        (set i (findNewline src i n))
                        {
                          (if (== ch 40)
                            (set depth (+ depth 1)))
                          (if (== ch 41)
                            {
                              (set depth (- depth 1))
                              (if (<= depth 0)
                                (set stop (Some (+ i 1))))
                            })
                          (set i (+ i 1))
                        })))))
            })
          stop
        }
      })))

; The first byte at or after `i` that is neither whitespace nor a
; comment, or None when the cursor is invalid or only trivia remains.
(pub :: skipTrivia (-> String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (skipTrivia src i)
  {
    (let n (strLen src))
    (let mut k i)
    (let mut go 1)
    {
      (while (&& (== go 1) (&& (>= k 0) (< k n)))
        {
          (let ch (strByte src k))
          (if (|| (== ch 32) (|| (== ch 9) (|| (== ch 10) (== ch 13))))
            (set k (+ k 1))
            (if (== ch 59)
              (set k (findNewline src k n))
              (if (&& (== ch 35) (isBlockOpen src k n))
                (set k (skipBlockComment src (+ k 2) n))
                (set go 0))))
        })
      (if (&& (>= k 0) (< k n))
        (Some k)
        None)
    }
  })

(pub :: isBlankByte (-> Int Bool))
(pub fn (isBlankByte b)
  (|| (== b 32) (== b 9)))

; The `(` opening the OUTERMOST form whose head token starts at `p`:
; back over the run of `(` and blanks before `p`, then forward to the
; first `(` of that run. None when no `(` sits between the previous
; token and `p` - a bare token, which is its own form.
(pub :: formOpenBefore (-> String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (formOpenBefore src p)
  (if (|| (< p 0) (> p (strLen src)))
    None
    {
      (let mut i (- p 1))
      {
        (while (if (>= i 0)
          (|| (== (strByte src i) 40) (isBlankByte (strByte src i)))
          false)
          (set i (- i 1)))
        {
          (let mut j (+ i 1))
          {
            (while (if (< j p)
              (isBlankByte (strByte src j))
              false)
              (set j (+ j 1)))
            (if (&& (< j p) (== (strByte src j) 40))
              (Some j)
              None)
          }
        }
      }
    }))

; The `(` opening the form `(kw ...)` whose second token starts at
; `p` - `(match` before a scrutinee, `(import` before a module path -
; or None when the word before `p` is not `kw` or no `(` precedes it.
(pub :: headOpenBefore (-> String Int String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (headOpenBefore src p kw)
  (if (|| (< p 0) (> p (strLen src)))
    None
    {
      (let mut i (- p 1))
      {
        (while (if (>= i 0)
          (|| (isBlankByte (strByte src i)) (|| (== (strByte src i) 10) (== (strByte src i) 13)))
          false)
          (set i (- i 1)))
        {
          (let mut j i)
          {
            (while (if (>= j 0)
              (isIdentChar (strByte src j))
              false)
              (set j (- j 1)))
            (if (strEq (strSlice src (+ j 1) (- i j)) kw)
              {
                (let mut k j)
                {
                  (while (if (>= k 0)
                    (isBlankByte (strByte src k))
                    false)
                    (set k (- k 1)))
                  (if (&& (>= k 0) (== (strByte src k) 40))
                    (Some k)
                    None)
                }
              }
              None)
          }
        }
      }
    }))

(pub :: spacesOf (-> Int String))
(pub fn (spacesOf n)
  (if (<= n 0)
    ""
    (concat " " (spacesOf (- n 1)))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; AX3005's fix: the missing arms, written.
;
; One arm per missing constructor, in declaration order, each
; `((Ctor _ ...) (todo "Ctor"))` with one `_` per field. `IO.todo`
; types as anything and never returns, so the arm checks against
; whatever the other arms answer and the program says where it
; stopped. The arms go before the match's closing `)`, each on its
; own line at the column the first written arm holds; a match with no
; arms yet takes the `(match` column plus two.
;
; WHERE THE CLOSING `)` IS. The match node carries no span (it is
; `TAG_E_MATCH` with span 0) and the anchor is the scrutinee's head
; token, so the paren is found from the LAST ARM: its pattern's head
; token, back to the `(` that opens the arm, forward over the arm's
; form, then past trivia - and the next byte must be `)`. With no
; arms the scrutinee's own form is walked the same way, but only for
; a scrutinee whose head token opens its own form (a name, a literal,
; a call, a nullary constructor): `spanOf` answers a `let`'s BODY and
; a field access's BASE, and walking forward from those lands on the
; `)` of the inner form - probed, `(match (let ((x 1)) x))` would have
; put the arms inside the `let`.
;
; REFUSED, with the prose help kept alone, when
;   * the diagnostic is not on the entry file, or `missing` is empty;
;   * a missing name is not a constructor of the scrutinee's own type,
;     or column 0 already tests it. The witness `matExhaustive` hands
;     back can come from a NESTED column - probed: `((Some (Some x))
;     ..) ((None) ..)` over `(Option (Option Int))` reports `missing
;     None`, the inner one - and an arm for it at the top level would
;     be a duplicate, or a constructor of the wrong type;
;   * the closing `)` or the arms' column cannot be read off the bytes.
;
; A SECOND FIX HELP brings `todo` into scope when the resolver cannot
; see it. Measured before writing it: a name outside an import's list
; is AX3023, and a second `(import IO ...)` beside a listed one is
; refused too - so an existing `(import IO (...))` has ` todo` added
; to its list, and only a file that imports IO nowhere gets a fresh
; `(import IO (todo))`, after its last import or at byte 0 when it has
; none.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: matchFixHelps (-> TC diag::Diag (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode ASTNode NodesRows Names ()))
(pub fn (matchFixHelps tc d arms scrutTy scrut matrix missing)
  {
    (let mut offered false)
    (if (let (Some src) (tcEntrySrc tc))
      (if (let (Some close) (matchCloseAt src scrut arms))
        (if (let (Some indent) (matchArmIndent src scrut arms))
          (if (let (Some text) (missingArmsText tc scrutTy matrix missing indent))
            (if (> (strLen text) 0)
              {
                (diagAddFixHelp d "add the missing arms, each a `todo` until it is written"
                  (mkSpan close close) text)
                (set offered true)
                (if (isNone (findFnEnt tc "todo"))
                  (importTodoHelp tc d src))
              })))))
    (if (! offered)
      (diagAddHelp d "add the missing arms, or a wildcard `_` arm to catch the rest"))
    ()
  })

; The byte of the `)` closing the match, or None.
(pub :: matchCloseAt (-> String ASTNode Nodes (Option Int)))
(pub fn (matchCloseAt src scrut arms)
  {
    (let anchor
      (if (== (vecLen arms) 0)
        (scrutFormEnd src scrut)
        (armFormEnd src (vecGet arms (- (vecLen arms) 1)))))
    (if (let (Some end) anchor)
      (if (let (Some next) (skipTrivia src end))
        (if (== (strByte src next) 41)
          (Some next)
          None)
        None)
      None)
  })

; The `(` opening an arm, from its pattern's head token; None for a
; `syntax/for` arm or a pattern with no span.
(pub :: armOpenOf (-> String ASTNode (Option Int)))
(pub fn (armOpenOf src arm)
  (if (!= (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_ARM)
    None
    {
      (let sp (spanOf (nodeAChild arm)))
      (if (== sp 0)
        None
        (formOpenBefore src (spanStart sp)))
    }))

(pub :: armFormEnd (-> String ASTNode (Option Int)))
(pub fn (armFormEnd src arm)
  (if (let (Some opening) (armOpenOf src arm))
    (formEndFrom src opening)
    None))

; Whether `spanOf` this scrutinee is the head token of its own
; outermost form - the shapes the zero-arm walk may start from.
(pub :: scrutHeadIsOwn (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (scrutHeadIsOwn scrut)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag scrut))
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          (|| (== tag TAG_E_INT)
            (|| (== tag TAG_E_STR)
              (|| (== tag TAG_E_CHAR) (|| (== tag TAG_E_FLT) (== tag TAG_P_CON0))))))
      true
      (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
        {
          (let h (spineHead scrut))
          (|| (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR) (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_LAM))
        }
        false))
  })

; The byte past the scrutinee's whole form, or None.
(pub :: scrutFormEnd (-> String ASTNode (Option Int)))
(pub fn (scrutFormEnd src scrut)
  (if (scrutHeadIsOwn scrut)
    {
      (let sp (spanOf scrut))
      (if (== sp 0)
        None
        (if (|| (< (spanStart sp) 0)
              (|| (< (spanEnd sp) (spanStart sp)) (> (spanEnd sp) (strLen src))))
          None
          (if (let (Some opening) (formOpenBefore src (spanStart sp)))
            (formEndFrom src opening)
            (Some (spanEnd sp)))))
    }
    None))

; The column the inserted arms take, or None.
(pub :: matchArmIndent (-> String ASTNode Nodes (Option Int)))
(pub fn (matchArmIndent src scrut arms)
  (if (> (vecLen arms) 0)
    (if (let (Some opening) (armOpenOf src (vecGet arms 0)))
      (Some (- opening (lineStartOf src opening)))
      None)
    {
      (let sp (spanOf scrut))
      (if (== sp 0)
        None
        {
          (let position (spanStart sp))
          (let head
            (match (formOpenBefore src position)
              ((None) position)
              ((Some opening) opening)))
          (if (let (Some opening) (headOpenBefore src head "match"))
            (Some (+ 2 (- opening (lineStartOf src opening))))
            None)
        })
    }))

; The arms, or None when one of the missing names is not this type's
; own top-level hole.
(pub :: missingArmsText (-> TC ASTNode NodesRows Names Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (missingArmsText tc scrutTy matrix missing indent)
  {
    (let dentFound (findData tc (nodeAName scrutTy)))
    (if (let (Some dent) dentFound)
      {
        (let ctors (dataEntCtors dent))
        (let pad (spacesOf indent))
        (let parts vecNew)
        (let mut i 0)
        (let mut ok 1)
        {
          (while (&& (== ok 1) (< i (vecLen missing)))
            {
              (let cn (vecGet missing i))
              {
                (if (let (Some arity) (ctorArityIn ctors cn 0))
                  (if (== (colHasCtor matrix cn 0) 1)
                    (set ok 0)
                    (vecPush parts (concat "\n" (concat pad (todoArmText cn arity)))))
                  (set ok 0))
                (set i (+ i 1))
              }
            })
          (if (== ok 1)
            (Some (catStrs parts))
            None)
        }
      }
      None)
  })

; The field count of the constructor `cn` names among `ctors`
; (CtorEnt word 1 is its field types), or None.
(pub :: ctorArityIn (-> (Vec CtorEnt) String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (ctorArityIn ctors cn i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    None
    {
      (let c (vecGet ctors i))
      (if (strEq (bareOf c.name) cn)
        (Some (vecLen c.fieldTys))
        (ctorArityIn ctors cn (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: todoArmText (-> String Int String))
(pub fn (todoArmText cn arity)
  (concat (concat (concat "((" cn) (wildsOf arity)) (concat (concat ") (todo \"" cn) "\"))")))

(pub :: wildsOf (-> Int String))
(pub fn (wildsOf n)
  (if (<= n 0)
    ""
    (concat " _" (wildsOf (- n 1)))))

; The import that brings `todo` in, as a second fix help on the same
; diagnostic. The search selects IO first, then the last import of any module.
(pub :: importTodoHelp (-> TC diag::Diag String ()))
(pub fn (importTodoHelp tc d src)
  (if (let (Some imp) (lastImportOf (tcEntryDecls tc) (Some "IO") 0 None))
    (if (let (Some opening) (importListOpen src imp))
      (if (let (Some end) (formEndFrom src opening))
        (diagAddFixHelp d "add `todo` to the names imported from IO" (mkSpan (- end 1) (- end 1))
          " todo")))
    (if (let (Some last) (lastImportOf (tcEntryDecls tc) None 0 None))
      (if (let (Some opening) (headOpenBefore src (spanStart (nodeSpan last)) "import"))
        (if (let (Some end) (formEndFrom src opening))
          (diagAddFixHelp d "import `todo` from IO" (mkSpan end end) "\n(import IO (todo))")))
      (diagAddFixHelp d "import `todo` from IO" (mkSpan 0 0) "(import IO (todo))\n"))))

; The entry file's LAST `(import ...)` by position - of module `mod`,
; or of any module when `mod` is None.
(pub :: lastImportOf (-> Nodes (Option String) Int (Option ASTNode) (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (lastImportOf decls mod i best)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    best
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (lastImportOf decls mod (+ i 1)
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_IMPORT)
              (&& (!= (nodeSpan d) 0)
                (if (let (Some name) mod)
                  (strEq (nodeAName d) name)
                  true)))
          (if (let (Some previous) best)
            (if (> (spanStart (nodeSpan d)) (spanStart (nodeSpan previous)))
              (Some d)
              best)
            (Some d))
          best))
    }))

; The `(` of an import's name list: past the rest of a dotted path
; (the span covers its first segment only), then blanks. None for a
; bare import, which has no list to extend.
(pub :: importListOpen (-> String ASTNode (Option Int)))
(pub fn (importListOpen src imp)
  (if (== (nodeSpan imp) 0)
    None
    (if (|| (< (spanEnd (nodeSpan imp)) 0) (> (spanEnd (nodeSpan imp)) (strLen src)))
      None
      {
        (let n (strLen src))
        (let mut i (spanEnd (nodeSpan imp)))
        {
          (while (if (< i n)
            (|| (isIdentChar (strByte src i)) (== (strByte src i) 46))
            false)
            (set i (+ i 1)))
          (while (if (< i n)
            (isBlankByte (strByte src i))
            false)
            (set i (+ i 1)))
          (if (&& (< i n) (== (strByte src i) 40))
            (Some i)
            None)
        }
      })))

; One column wide, one row per arm - the entry point from checkMatch.
; `scrut` is the scrutinee NODE: its span is the anchor, and its shape
; is what the fix reads when the match has no arms yet.
(pub :: checkExhaustiveMatrix (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkExhaustiveMatrix tc arms scrutTy scrut)
  {
    (let span (spanOf scrut))
    (if (== span 0)
      0
      {
        (let matrix (armsMatrix arms vecNew 0))
        (let types (vecPush vecNew scrutTy))
        (if (let (Some missing) (matExhaustive tc matrix types))
          {
            (let d
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3005" "non-exhaustive-match" span
                (if (== (vecLen missing) 0)
                  (concat "non-exhaustive pattern match: some `"
                    (concat (tyRender scrutTy) "` values are not covered"))
                  (concat "non-exhaustive pattern match: missing " (joinComma missing)))
                None None))
            {
              (diagSetLabel d
                (if (== (vecLen missing) 0)
                  (concat "this `match` does not cover every value of `"
                    (concat (tyRender scrutTy) "`"))
                  (concat "this `match` does not cover: " (joinComma missing))))
              (matchFixHelps tc d arms scrutTy scrut matrix missing)
              (emitDiag tc d)
              0
            }
          }
          0)
      })
  })

(pub :: armsMatrix (-> (Vec ASTNode) NodesRows Int NodesRows))
(pub fn (armsMatrix arms acc i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    acc
    {
      {
        (let arm (vecGet arms i))
        (if (! (== (nodeTag arm) TAG_M_FOR))
          (vecPush acc (vecPush nodesNew (nodeAChild arm))))
      }
      (armsMatrix arms acc (+ i 1))
    }))

; `missingCtors` and `coveredHas` lived here: the shallow set
; difference over `covered` that decided exhaustiveness before
; `matExhaustive` replaced it. `matMissing` asks the same question of
; a matrix COLUMN, which is the only place the answer was ever
; correct, so the pair had no remaining caller.
(:: joinComma (-> Names String))
(fn (joinComma xs)
  {
    (let out strBuilderNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen xs)
      {
        (if (> i 0)
          (strBuilderPush out ", "))
        (strBuilderPush out (vecGet xs i))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Patterns, typed. A bare identifier binds at the expected type -
; `_` excepted: stage0 parses it as a wildcard that binds nothing,
; and a bound `_` would join the suggestion pool. A constructor
; pattern checks existence (AX3003, suggestions from constructors
; alone) and arity (AX3009), then binds each argument at the
; constructor's declared field type; arguments past the declared
; fields, and every argument of an UNKNOWN constructor, bind at fresh
; `_tN` wildcards - and each such fallback bumps the counter, stage0's
; check_pattern discipline, literals and wildcards included.
;
; A top-level arm receives the scrutinee's type. A nested pattern
; receives its field type from `bindOnePatArg`; missing fields receive
; fresh variables and an unknown constructor's arguments receive poison.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkPatternTyped (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkPatternTyped tc pat scrutTy fitTy)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag pat))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
      (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName pat) "true") (strEq (nodeAName pat) "false"))
        0
        (if (strEq (nodeAName pat) "_")
          0
          {
            (stampPatBinderTy pat scrutTy)
            (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) (nodeAText pat) 0 (nodeSpan pat)
              (if (nodeSame scrutTy nilNode)
                mkSilentWild
                scrutTy))
            0
          }))
      ; A string-literal pattern is lowered to a CONTENT comparison
      ; (`emitPatMatch`, 2026-08-21), which reads the scrutinee as a
      ; String header. The checker is what makes that read safe: a
      ; literal against a scrutinee the checker knows is not a String
      ; is AX3004 here, where the old address comparison would have
      ; silently never matched.
      (if (&& (> (tyIntegerCode tc fitTy) 0) (!= (tyIntegerCode tc fitTy) 64))
        (if (== tag TAG_E_INT)
          (checkIntegerPattern tc pat fitTy)
          (if (== tag TAG_E_FLT)
            (emitMismatchTy tc fitTy mkFloatTy (nodeSpan pat))
            (checkPatternIntegerRest tc pat scrutTy fitTy)))
        (checkPatternIntegerRest tc pat scrutTy fitTy)))
  })

(pub :: checkIntegerPattern (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkIntegerPattern tc pat ty)
  {
    (let code (tyIntegerCode tc ty))
    (let bits (integerBits code))
    (let value (nodeA pat))
    (let minimum
      (if (integerUnsigned code)
        0
        (integerMinPattern bits)))
    (let maximum
      (if (integerUnsigned code)
        (if (== bits 64)
          9223372036854775807
          (- (<< 1 bits) 1))
        (- (- 0 (integerMinPattern bits)) 1)))
    (if (|| (< value minimum) (> value maximum))
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3004" "type-mismatch" (nodeSpan pat)
            (concat "integer pattern `"
              (concat (fmtInt value)
                (concat "` is outside the range of `" (concat (tyRender ty) "`"))))
            None
            (Some
              "Use an integer literal within the scrutinee type's range. Patterns test the mathematical value; they do not truncate or reinterpret its bits."))
          "pattern is outside the type's range"))
      0)
  })

(pub :: integerMinPattern (-> Int Int))
(pub fn (integerMinPattern bits)
  (if (== bits 64)
    (- (- 0 9223372036854775807) 1)
    (- 0 (<< 1 (- bits 1)))))

(pub :: checkPatternIntegerRest (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkPatternIntegerRest tc pat scrutTy fitTy)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag pat))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_STR)
      (checkLitPatternTy tc mkStringTy fitTy (nodeSpan pat))
      (if (== tag TAG_P_CON0)
        {
          (stampCtorName tc pat)
          (checkCtorPattern tc (nodeAName pat) vecNew (nodeSpan pat) fitTy scrutTy)
        }
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (let head (spineHead pat))
            (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
              {
                (let span
                  (if (== (nodeSpan head) 0)
                    (nodeSpan pat)
                    (nodeSpan head)))
                (emitDiag tc
                  (diagSetLabel
                    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3003" "undefined-constructor" span
                      "this pattern head does not name a data constructor" None
                      (Some
                        "Write a declared data constructor before its field patterns, or match a literal directly."))
                    "expected a constructor name"))
                (bindPatternArgsPoison tc (spineArgVec pat vecNew) 0)
                0
              }
              {
                (stampCtorName tc head)
                (checkCtorPattern tc (nodeAName head) (spineArgVec pat vecNew) (nodeSpan head)
                  fitTy scrutTy)
              })
          }
          ; A NAMED constructor pattern. Every field it mentions must be one
          ; the constructor declares - a miss is AX3007, anchored at the
          ; FIELD NAME, which is why the parser keeps each name as a spanned
          ; node. The pattern is then reordered into the positional spine
          ; and checked by the ordinary path, so field types, binding and
          ; AX3003 all behave as they do for `(Rect w h)`.
          ;
          ; There is deliberately no arity check: stage0 fills an omitted
          ; field with a wildcard, so a partial named pattern is legal.
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            {
              (let _resolved (stampCtorName tc pat))
              (let ctorFound (findCtor tc (nodeAName pat)))
              (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
                {
                  (let declared ctor.fieldNames)
                  {
                    (checkNamedPatFields tc (nodeBChildren pat) declared (bareOf (nodeAName pat)) 0)
                    (if (let (Some fields) declared)
                      (checkCtorPattern tc (nodeAName pat)
                        (spineArgVec
                          (namedPatSpine (nodeAName pat) fields (nodeBChildren pat)
                            (nodeCChildren pat) (nodeSpan pat))
                          vecNew)
                        (nodeSpan pat) fitTy scrutTy)
                      0)
                  }
                }
                (checkCtorPattern tc (nodeAName pat) vecNew (nodeSpan pat) fitTy scrutTy))
            }
            0))))
  })

; The instantiation environment for a constructor pattern: the
; declaring type's parameters, paired positionally with the
; scrutinee's arguments when the scrutinee is the same constructor
; with as many. `env` is `tyInstIn`'s typed substitution vector; a name
; it does not hold mints a placeholder as before.
(pub :: ctorPatEnv (-> ASTNode ASTNode TypeEnv TypeEnv))
(pub fn (ctorPatEnv resTy scrutTy env)
  (if (|| (nodeSame resTy nilNode) (nodeSame scrutTy nilNode))
    env
    (if (|| (!= (nodeTag resTy) TAG_T_CON) (!= (nodeTag scrutTy) TAG_T_CON))
      env
      (if (if (strEq (nodeAName resTy) (nodeAName scrutTy))
        (!= (vecLen (nodeBChildren resTy)) (vecLen (nodeBChildren scrutTy)))
        true)
        env
        (ctorPatEnvFrom (nodeBChildren resTy) (nodeBChildren scrutTy) env 0)))))

(pub :: ctorPatEnvFrom (-> Nodes Nodes TypeEnv Int TypeEnv))
(pub fn (ctorPatEnvFrom params args env i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen params))
    env
    {
      (let p (vecGet params i))
      (let a (vecGet args i))
      {
        (if (&& (== (tyIsSrcVar p) 1) (! (nodeSame a nilNode)))
          (vecPush env (TypeBinding :name (nodeAName p) :ty a)))
        (ctorPatEnvFrom params args env (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; A literal pattern's type against the scrutinee's. Quiet when the
; scrutinee's type is unknown, poisoned, or a placeholder - `tyCompat`
; already says yes to those; AX3004 when the two are known and
; disagree, anchored at the literal.
(pub :: checkLitPatternTy (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (checkLitPatternTy tc litTy scrutTy span)
  (if (|| (nodeSame scrutTy nilNode) (== span 0))
    0
    (if (== (tyCompat tc litTy scrutTy) 1)
      0
      {
        (emitMismatchTy tc litTy scrutTy span)
        0
      })))

; Does a constructor pattern of `resTy` (the constructor's declared
; result, `Option a` for `Some`) fit a scrutinee of `scrutTy`?
;
; Head names only, deliberately: `Option a`'s rigid `a` against a
; scrutinee `Option Int` is not a clash, and the arguments are what
; `bindPatternArgs` instantiates and binds. A scrutinee the checker
; does not know - 0, poison, the silent wildcard, a minted `_tN` -
; fits anything, as it does everywhere else. A SOURCE type variable
; does not: inside `(:: g (-> a Int))` the parameter stands for one
; unknown type, and `(match v ((Red) 1))` claims it is `Color`. A
; known constructor type with a different name does not either, and
; nor does an arrow, a tuple or a pointer. Checked aliases have
; already resolved to their target declaration.
(pub :: ctorPatFits (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (ctorPatFits tc resTy scrutTy)
  (if (|| (nodeSame scrutTy nilNode) (nodeSame resTy nilNode))
    1
    {
      (let st (nodeTag scrutTy))
      (if (== st TAG_T_ERR)
        1
        (if (== st TAG_T_VAR)
          (if (== (tyIsSrcVar scrutTy) 1)
            0
            1)
          (if (!= (nodeTag resTy) TAG_T_CON)
            1
            (if (== st TAG_T_CON)
              (tySameDecl tc scrutTy resTy)
              0))))
    }))

; Each field the pattern names must be declared. AX3007's message and
; help are the field-access ones, byte for byte.
(pub :: checkNamedPatFields (-> TC Nodes (Option Names) String Int Int))
(pub fn (checkNamedPatFields tc names declared tyName i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    0
    {
      {
        (let nm (vecGet names i))
        (if (!
              (match declared
                ((None) false)
                ((Some fields) (== (declaredHasField fields (nodeAName nm)) 1))))
          (emitFieldNotFound tc (nodeAName nm) tyName (nodeSpan nm)))
      }
      (checkNamedPatFields tc names declared tyName (+ i 1))
    }))

; `scrutTy` is what the match is over, and the constructor must
; belong to it. Until 2026-08-21 nothing compared the two: a
; constructor of any type matched a scrutinee of any other - `Int`,
; `Shape`, or a signature's rigid `a` - and with the scrutinee's type
; never a data type, exhaustiveness never ran either, so
;
;   (:: g (-> a Int))  (fn (g v) (match v ((Red) 1)))
;   (g 42) (g "hello")
;
; checked OK and answered 0 twice through the fall-through cell
; (the QA sweep's tyvar-shaped sibling of MM-VAL-9b). A clash is
; AX3004 at the constructor's name, expected the constructor's type;
; the arguments are still bound so the arm's body checks once.
; Constructor references and patterns retain the same resolved declaration
; the checker used. The emitter's qualified registry entries carry its
; tag, field offsets, Float flags and ownership map.
(:: ctorResolvedName (-> CtorEnt String))
(fn (ctorResolvedName ctor)
  (nominalName ctor.name (modOf (nodeAName ctor.resTy))))

(:: stampCtorName (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(fn (stampCtorName tc node)
  {
    (let ctorFound (findCtor tc (nodeAName node)))
    (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
      (writeNodeText node 1 (ctorResolvedName ctor))
      0)
  })

(pub :: checkCtorPattern (-> TC String Nodes Int ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkCtorPattern tc cname args span scrutTy envTy)
  {
    (let ctorFound (findCtor tc cname))
    (if (let (Some ctor) ctorFound)
      {
        (let fieldTys (tyInstVec tc ctor.fieldTys (ctorPatEnv ctor.resTy envTy vecNew) vecNew 0))
        {
          (if (&& (!= span 0) (== (ctorPatFits tc ctor.resTy scrutTy) 0))
            (emitMismatchTy tc ctor.resTy scrutTy span))
          (if (!= (vecLen args) (vecLen fieldTys))
            (if (! (== span 0))
              (emitDiag tc
                (diagSetLabel
                  (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3009" "constructor-arity-mismatch" span
                    (concat
                      (concat
                        (concat (concat (concat "constructor `" cname) "` expects ")
                          (fmtInt (vecLen fieldTys)))
                        " argument(s), found ")
                      (fmtInt (vecLen args)))
                    None
                    (Some
                      (concat (concat "this constructor takes exactly " (fmtInt (vecLen fieldTys)))
                        " field(s); add or remove arguments to match")))
                  (concat (fmtInt (vecLen args)) " argument(s) provided here")))))
          (bindPatternArgs tc args fieldTys 0)
          0
        }
      }
      {
        (emitUndefinedCtor tc cname span)
        (bindPatternArgsPoison tc args 0)
        0
      })
  })

(pub :: bindPatternArgs (-> TC Nodes Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (bindPatternArgs tc args fieldTys i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (let arg (vecGet args i))
      {
        (if (< i (vecLen fieldTys))
          (bindOnePatArg tc arg (vecGet fieldTys i))
          (bindOnePatArg tc arg (freshTVar tc 1)))
        (bindPatternArgs tc args fieldTys (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; The arguments of a constructor pattern that names no constructor,
; bound as poison (ERR-DIAG-3): AX3003 is out, the fields' types are
; unknown, and the arm's body checks without comparing them again.
(pub :: bindPatternArgsPoison (-> TC Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (bindPatternArgsPoison tc args i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen args))
    0
    {
      (bindOnePatArg tc (vecGet args i) (nodeBuildTErrPlain 0 0 0))
      (bindPatternArgsPoison tc args (+ i 1))
    }))

; Record on the binder what type it stands for, so codegen can ask a
; question it has no other way to ask: can this name carry a reference
; out of the block the match is over?
;
; `ty` here is the constructor's field type ALREADY INSTANTIATED
; against the scrutinee (`ctorPatEnv`, `tyInstVec`), which is the
; whole value of stamping it here rather than deriving it there:
; `Ok`'s field is declared `a`, and only the match site knows that
; this `Ok` came out of a `(Result Int String)`.
;
; Only a `TAG_T_CON` is recorded, and only its NAME. A type variable,
; an arrow or an array poisons the word, which is what codegen already
; treats as "assume it can alias" - so an unresolved type costs the old
; answer and never a wrong one. The name is a snapshot rather than the
; node, because the unifier writes through type nodes in place and a
; pointer kept across phases can be overwritten; `stampFieldStruct`
; records a name for the same reason.
;
; THE WORD HAS THREE STATES, AND THE THIRD IS WHY. `0` is "never
; stamped", a NAME is "every check that reached this node agreed it is
; that type", and the EMPTY STRING is "two checks disagreed". Codegen
; reads the last two the same way - `scalarTyName ""` is 0, which is
; conservative - so the poison state costs nothing to read. It exists
; because a binder node can in principle be checked more than once at
; DIFFERENT types, and last-write-wins would then hand codegen an `Int`
; for a binder that is really a `String`. That is not a leak; it is a
; release of a block the binder still points into.
;
; IT WAS REACHABLE, and it is not any more. Both halves of that were
; measured rather than argued for, and the second half is why this
; comment is long.
;
; The path was a trait DEFAULT body, which `checkImplComplete`
; synthesized into every impl that omitted the method WITHOUT copying
; the body's nodes - so two impls at two types checked one AST, and
; `impl` declaration order alone decided which stamp survived. Measured
; 2026-08-25: with this arm removed, that program's `Ident#String#ident`
; contained `call void @axiom_release(i64 %.t0)` - a release of the
; block the returned String still lived in - and with it present, none.
;
; Traits were removed in 0.6.0 and took the path with them. Re-measured
; 2026-08-31 by building the last-write-wins compiler and diffing
; emitted IR: 278 fixtures, every stdlib module, and core/main.ax
; - the whole compiler - are BYTE-IDENTICAL. Nothing Axiom can now
; express checks one pattern binder twice at two types.
;
; The arm is kept anyway. It costs one comparison, it reads back
; conservative, and the hazard it covers is a property of how the
; checker walks shared nodes rather than of any one construct - the
; next feature that re-checks a body per instantiation reintroduces it.
; `scripts/check-fallible-reclaim.sh` now asserts the unreachability
; directly, so this is a guard with a live check on it and not
; untested defensive code: if a program ever reaches the arm again,
; that gate goes red and says so.
(pub :: stampPatBinderTy (-> ASTNode ASTNode Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (stampPatBinderTy arg ty)
  {
    (let prev (nodeBinderTy arg))
    (let now
      (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
        ""
        (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON)
          ""
          (nodeAText ty))))
    (if (== prev 0)
      {
        (setNodeBinderTyText arg now)
        0
      }
      (if (strEq (as prev String) (as now String))
        0
        {
          (setNodeBinderTyText arg "")
          0
        }))
  })

; One argument position: a bare name binds at the given type, a
; nested constructor pattern recurses with its own checks, a literal
; binds nothing.
(pub :: bindOnePatArg (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (bindOnePatArg tc arg ty)
  {
    (let tag (nodeTag arg))
    (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
      (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName arg) "true") (strEq (nodeAName arg) "false"))
        0
        (if (strEq (nodeAName arg) "_")
          0
          {
            (stampPatBinderTy arg ty)
            (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) (nodeAText arg) 0 (nodeSpan arg) ty)
            0
          }))
      ; A nested constructor pattern recurses, and a NAMED one is a
      ; constructor pattern: without this arm `(Wrap { inner =
      ; (Rect { w, h }) })` bound nothing for `w` or `h` and every
      ; use of them was an undefined variable.
      ; A nested constructor pattern is checked against the FIELD
      ; type it sits in - fit and instantiation both - so `(Some
      ; (Some x))` over `(Option (Option Int))` binds `x` at `Int`
      ; rather than at a fresh placeholder, and a constructor of
      ; the wrong type in a field is refused where it stands.
      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_APP) (|| (== tag TAG_P_CON0) (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)))
        (checkPatternTyped tc arg ty ty)
        ; A nested string literal is the same content comparison
        ; the top-level one is, and needs the same guard: against
        ; an `Int` field - `((MkU "x") 1)` over `(MkU Int)` - the
        ; header read would dereference the field's integer.
        (if (== tag TAG_E_STR)
          (checkLitPatternTy tc mkStringTy ty (nodeSpan arg))
          0)))
  })

(pub :: emitUndefinedCtor (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitUndefinedCtor tc cname span)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    {
      (let best newBest)
      {
        (offerCtorEnts best cname (tcDatasVec tc) 0)
        ; A constructor of a type its module does not export is
        ; refused either way - the lookup filters it - but saying it
        ; does not exist is wrong about a name that plainly does.
        (if (isSome (ctorHiddenModule tc cname))
          (emitPrivateVar tc cname span (privKey (ctorHiddenModule tc cname) cname))
          (emitDiag tc
            {
              (let msg (concat (concat "undefined constructor `" cname) "`"))
              (diagSetLabel
                (if (let (Some cand) best.cand)
                  (mkDiagFix SEV_ERROR "AX3003" "undefined-constructor" span msg None
                    (concat (concat "a similarly named constructor `" cand)
                      "` is visible; did you mean this?")
                    (diagFix span cand))
                  (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3003" "undefined-constructor" span msg None
                    (Some "check the `data` declaration for the correct constructor name")))
                (concat (concat "no constructor named `" cname) "` is visible here"))
            }))
        0
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The suggestion. Byte-identical output means reproducing stage0's
; `suggest_closest` exactly: Levenshtein distance, a threshold of
; `(min(len)/2)` clamped to [1,2], candidates equal to the name or
; beginning `__` skipped, and the FIRST strictly-smaller distance
; winning - so the order candidates are visited in decides ties.
; stage0 visits scope, then the function TABLE - operators and
; primitives included, struct names absent (probed: a typo near a
; struct name draws no suggestion) - then every constructor, the
; builtin Option's first.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: levenshtein (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (levenshtein a b)
  {
    (let la (strLen a))
    (let lb (strLen b))
    {
      (let prev (memAlloc (* 8 (+ lb 1))))
      (let cur (memAlloc (* 8 (+ lb 1))))
      {
        (levInitRow prev lb 0)
        (levRows a b la lb prev cur 1)
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: levInitRow (-> Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (levInitRow row lb j)
  (if (> j lb)
    0
    {
      (memSetWord row j j)
      (levInitRow row lb (+ j 1))
    }))

(pub :: levRows (-> String String Int Int Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (levRows a b la lb prev cur i)
  (if (> i la)
    (memGetWord prev lb)
    {
      (memSetWord cur 0 i)
      (levCols a b lb prev cur i 1)
      (levCopyRow cur prev lb 0)
      (levRows a b la lb prev cur (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: levCols (-> String String Int Int Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (levCols a b lb prev cur i j)
  (if (> j lb)
    0
    {
      (let cost
        (if (== (strByte a (- i 1)) (strByte b (- j 1)))
          0
          1))
      {
        (let del (+ (memGetWord prev j) 1))
        (let ins (+ (memGetWord cur (- j 1)) 1))
        (let sub (+ (memGetWord prev (- j 1)) cost))
        {
          (memSetWord cur j (minOf3 del ins sub))
          (levCols a b lb prev cur i (+ j 1))
        }
      }
    }))

(pub :: minOf3 (-> Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (minOf3 a b c)
  {
    (let m
      (if (< a b)
        a
        b))
    (if (< m c)
      m
      c)
  })

(pub :: levCopyRow (-> Int Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (levCopyRow src dst lb j)
  (if (> j lb)
    0
    {
      (memSetWord dst j (memGetWord src j))
      (levCopyRow src dst lb (+ j 1))
    }))

; `(min(len)/2)` clamped into [1,2], stage0's rule verbatim.
(pub :: suggestThreshold (-> Int Int Int))
(pub fn (suggestThreshold la lb)
  {
    (let m
      (if (< la lb)
        la
        lb))
    {
      (let t (/ m 2))
      (if (< t 1)
        1
        (if (> t 2)
          2
          t))
    }
  })

; Keep the candidate optional throughout the search. The distance is
; an upper bound until a candidate wins; it is never a reference.
(pub struct Suggest
  (mut cand : (Option String))
  (mut dist : Int))

(pub :: newBest Suggest)
(pub fn (newBest)
  (Suggest :cand None :dist 999))

; A `__` primitive is offered only to a `__` query: `__stor8` should
; hear about `__store8`, and `store` should not.
(pub :: offerCandidate (-> Suggest String String Int))
(pub fn (offerCandidate best name cand)
  (if (|| (strEq cand name)
        (&& (strStartsWith cand "__")
          (if (strStartsWith name "__")
            false
            true)))
    0
    {
      (let d (levenshtein name cand))
      (if (&& (<= d (suggestThreshold (strLen name) (strLen cand))) (< d best.dist))
        {
          (set best.cand (Some cand))
          (set best.dist d)
          0
        }
        0)
    }))

(pub :: offerScope (-> Suggest String (Vec SEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (offerScope best name scope i)
  ; MAC-HYG-3a: a scope entry introduced by a macro template is
  ; gensym-renamed (`tmpvar.0`), which no source can spell. Offering
  ; the raw spelling computes the distance against bytes the author
  ; never wrote and reports them back as a suggestion. Strip at the
  ; first `.` (46) and `#` (35) - the two bytes `lspDeHygiene` maps -
  ; so the candidate, the distance and the stored best are all the
  ; original spelling. Names without either byte are untouched, so a
  ; macro-free program suggests byte-identically. Inlined here rather
  ; than factored so no new top-level function moves the
  ; effect-distribution pins; this function already carries
  ; `Alloc,Mut`.
  (if (>= i (vecLen scope))
    0
    {
      (let raw (sEntName (vecGet scope i)))
      {
        (let noDot
          (if (let (Some d) (strFindByte raw 46 0))
            (strSlice raw 0 d)
            raw))
        {
          (let disp
            (if (let (Some h) (strFindByte noDot 35 0))
              (strSlice noDot 0 h)
              noDot))
          {
            (offerCandidate best name disp)
            (offerScope best name scope (+ i 1))
          }
        }
      }
    }))

(pub :: offerFnEnts (-> Suggest String (Vec FnEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (offerFnEnts best name fns i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen fns))
    0
    {
      (offerCandidate best name (bareOf (fnEntName (vecGet fns i))))
      (offerFnEnts best name fns (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: offerCtorEnts (-> Suggest String (Vec DataEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (offerCtorEnts best name datas i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen datas))
    0
    {
      (offerCtorsOf best name (dataEntCtors (vecGet datas i)) 0)
      (offerCtorEnts best name datas (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: offerCtorsOf (-> Suggest String (Vec CtorEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (offerCtorsOf best name ctors i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ctors))
    0
    {
      (offerCandidate best name (bareOf (ctorEntName (vecGet ctors i))))
      (offerCtorsOf best name ctors (+ i 1))
    }))

; A declaration's source-spelled name: import resolution rewrites an
; imported one to `Mod$name` in place, and a suggestion has to read
; back as something the programmer could type.
; The MODULE part of a qualified name `Mod$name`, or 0 when there is
; none. The mirror of `bareOf`, which answers the other half.
; A name as the PROGRAMMER wrote it. A qualified reference is parsed
; into the flat `Mod$name` spelling the declarations use, and a
; diagnostic that quotes it back reports a name that appears nowhere in
; the source: `undefined variable `Nope$Red`` for a line reading
; `Nope::Red`. stage0 reports the source spelling; so does this now.
(pub :: srcSpelling (-> String String))
(pub fn (srcSpelling s)
  (if (let (Some moduleName) (modOf s))
    (concat (concat moduleName "::") (bareOf s))
    s))

; The module prefix of a mangled `Mod$name`. A missing separator is
; None; a present separator retains its prefix, including an empty one.
(pub :: modOf (-> String (Option String)))
(pub fn (modOf s)
  (modOfFrom s (- (strLen s) 1)))

(pub :: modOfFrom (-> String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (modOfFrom s i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    (if (== (strByte s i) 36)
      (Some (strSlice s 0 i))
      (modOfFrom s (- i 1)))))

; The module name for diagnostic text; an unqualified name renders
; an empty prefix.
(pub :: modOfName (-> String String))
(pub fn (modOfName s)
  (match (modOf s)
    ((None) "")
    ((Some name) name)))

; Does a type or constructor named `declName`, declared by module
; `declMod`, answer to the reference `ref`?
;
; A BARE reference behaves exactly as it always did - `nameMatches`,
; which also lets a bare reference reach a declaration `mangleDecl`
; rewrote. A QUALIFIED one now has its module CHECKED against the
; declaring module rather than ignored, and that is the whole fix:
; `mangleDecl` rewrites `fn` and `::` declarations, so `Mod::f`
; resolved by matching a rewritten name, and it does not rewrite
; `data` or `struct`, so `Mod::Ctor` matched nothing at all and every
; qualified constructor reference was a false AX3001 on a program
; stage0 compiles and runs.
;
; stage0 checks the module for data constructors and for functions and
; does NOT for struct constructors - `(Nope::Pt 1 2)` is accepted
; there, measured 2026-08-08. This is the stricter of the two answers,
; applied uniformly: a qualified reference names a module or it does
; not resolve.
(pub :: declMatches (-> (Option String) String String Int))
(pub fn (declMatches declMod declName ref)
  (if (let (Some moduleName) (modOf ref))
    (if (let (Some owner) declMod)
      (if (&& (strEq owner moduleName) (strEq declName (bareOf ref)))
        1
        0)
      0)
    (nameMatches declName ref)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The declaration walk: signatures park scope entries AS
; THE WALK REACHES THEM (order-sensitive, probed: a `set` in an
; earlier body does not see a later signature), and every entry-file
; function body is checked with its parameters typed by peeling the
; signature's arrows - a missing or non-arrow signature types every
; parameter Int; parameters past the arrows bind at fresh `_tN`. The
; inferred arrow is recorded only over a still-placeholder type: a
; declared signature is authoritative and never overwritten.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Primitive representations are compiler contracts, so a declaration
; cannot replace one with a heap object under the same spelling.
(pub :: checkBuiltinTypeDefinition (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkBuiltinTypeDefinition tc d)
  (if (&& (== (declNamespace (nodeTag d)) NS_TYPE)
        (|| (typeKeywordKnown (nodeAName d)) (sizedTyIn (nodeAName d) "Vec Option Link")))
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3006" "duplicate-definition" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat "cannot redefine built-in type `" (concat (nodeAName d) "`")) None
        (Some
          "Choose another type name. Primitive types and built-in containers keep the representation and ownership rules the compiler assigns them.")))
    0))

(pub :: tcWalkDecls (-> TC Int Int))
; The MERGED list, not the entry file's: stage0 type-checks the
; bodies of imported modules too, and renders each diagnostic against
; its own filename. `resolveDecls` accumulates depth-first, so the
; order is dependencies first and the entry file last - which is the
; order stage0 reports in (probed with two bad modules and a bad
; entry). Each declaration's `module` stamp picks the unit its spans
; index.
(pub fn (tcWalkDecls tc i)
  {
    (let decls (tcAllDeclsVec tc))
    (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
      0
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        {
          (tcSetCurUnit tc (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) (nodeModuleOpt d)))
          (checkBuiltinTypeDefinition tc d)
          {
            (let tag (nodeTag d))
            (if (== tag TAG_D_SIG)
              {
                (refuseReservedBinder tc (nodeAName d) (nodeSpan d) 1)
                (checkImportedSignature tc d)
                (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) (nodeAText d) 0 (nodeSpan d)
                  (if (== (nodeTy d) 0)
                    mkSilentWild
                    (nodeTyChild d)))
              }
              ; The body first, then its tags - stage0's
              ; `check_decls` runs `check_expr(body)` and only
              ; then walks `axtags`, so every diagnostic from a
              ; function's body precedes its AX3010.
              (if (== tag TAG_D_FN)
                {
                  (refuseReservedBinder tc (nodeAName d) (nodeSpan d) 1)
                  ; `forgeSites` collects the body's casts that may forge
                  ; while it is checked (`castNoteForge`), keeps the ones
                  ; that do once it is typed (`unsafeSettleForges`), for
                  ; `checkAxtags` to answer and `unsafeLatePass` to
                  ; follow.
                  (tcSetForgeSites tc (Some nodesNew))
                  (tcSetEraseSites tc (Some nodesNew))
                  (tcCheckFn tc d)
                  (unsafeSettleForges tc)
                  (eraseSettle tc d)
                  (checkAxtags tc d)
                  (unsafeNoteForgeDecl tc d)
                  (tcClearForgeSites tc)
                  (tcClearEraseSites tc)
                }
                ; A subtype declaration carries no body: validate the
                ; base names a known type (v1: exactly `Int`) and the
                ; bounds are Int expressions.
                (if (== tag TAG_D_SUBTYPE)
                  {
                    {
                      (let bad (tyUnknownCon tc (nodeBChild d)))
                      (if (let (Some bad) bad)
                        (if (! (== (nodeSpan d) 0))
                          (emitDiag tc
                            (diagSetLabel
                              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3002" "undefined-type" (nodeSpan d)
                                (concat (concat "undefined type `" bad) "`") None
                                (Some (tyRemovedBuiltinHelp bad)))
                              (concat (concat "no type named `" bad) "` is visible here"))))
                        (if (! (tyIsBareInt (nodeBChild d)))
                          (if (! (== (nodeSpan d) 0))
                            (emitMismatchTy tc mkIntTy (nodeBChild d) (nodeSpan d)))))
                    }
                    (checkSubtypeBounds tc (nodeCChildren d) 0)
                  })))
          }
          (tcWalkDecls tc (+ i 1))
        }
      })
  })

; Redeclarations may rename type parameters, but cannot alter a callee's
; signature. Both type trees use the same rigid names in first-use order;
; repeated variables therefore remain repeated, rather than unifying away.
(:: signatureCanonicalType (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(fn (signatureCanonicalType tc ty)
  {
    (let names vecNew)
    (sigTyVarsInto ty names)
    (let env vecNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen names)
      (vecPush env
        (TypeBinding :name (vecGet names i) :ty (mkTVar (concat "signaturevar" (fmtInt i))))))
    (tyInstIn tc ty env)
  })

(:: checkImportedSignature (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(fn (checkImportedSignature tc d)
  {
    (if (let (Some declared) (fnEntExact tc (nodeAName d)))
      (if (signatureEntryOnly declared)
        {
          (let saved (tcCurModule tc))
          (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
          (if (let (Some definition) (resolveSignatureEntry tc (Some declared)))
            (if (!= (as definition Int) (as declared Int))
              {
                (let ambiguous (checkAmbiguous tc (bareOf (nodeAName d)) (nodeSpan d)))
                (if (== ambiguous 0)
                  {
                    (let expected definition.ty)
                    (let actual (nodeTyChild d))
                    (if (==
                          (tyCompat tc (signatureCanonicalType tc expected)
                            (signatureCanonicalType tc actual))
                          0)
                      (emitMismatchTy tc expected actual (nodeSpan d)))
                  })
              }))
          (tcSetCurModule tc saved)
        }))
    0
  })

; Every AXTAG belonging to one function.
;
; A tag attaches to the declaration that FOLLOWS it, and a function has
; two: its `(:: f ...)` and its `(fn (f ...) ...)`. `checkAxtags` runs
; only for a `fn`, so a tag above the SIGNATURE was collected, printed
; by `symbols` on the function's line, and never checked - `#pure`
; beside `#effects=IO` while `check` said OK. `symbols` keys tags by
; name and was right: a signature has no body to contradict a claim.
(pub :: checkAxtags (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkAxtags tc d)
  ; THERE IS NO EARLY RETURN FOR AN UNTAGGED DECLARATION, and its
  ; absence is the point. `(if (&& (== own 0) (== sig 0)) 0 ...)`
  ; stood here and made the whole effect system opt-in: a function
  ; that volunteered no tag was never asked what it performed, so
  ; silence meant "not checked". Silence now means "performs no IO,
  ; and its own code touches no raw memory",
  ; which is a claim like any other and is checked like one. The
  ; effects are therefore collected for EVERY function, not only for
  ; the ones that volunteered.
  ; `unsafeSink` is live for exactly this walk: every reference it
  ; resolves to an extern lands in `sink`, which is where AX3073
  ; finds the call to name.
  ; A forging cast is this body's `Unsafe` as definitely as a
  ; raw store is. The walk cannot see one - it runs before any
  ; body is typed, and a cast forges by the type of what it is
  ; given - so the sites `castNoteForge` recorded while this
  ; body was checked are added here, before any claim is read.
  ; Does THIS body perform an unsafe operation itself - the three
  ; AX3073 asks about? `Unsafe` is required lexically
  ; (`MM-EXEC-9d`), so this, and not the row, is what an
  ; `effect(unsafe)` claim answers to (`axtagUnsupported`).
  {
    (let own (nodeAxtagsOf d))
    ; An absent signature contributes the empty set to this union.
    (let sig
      (match (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName d))
        ((None) vecNew)
        ((Some tags) tags)))
    {
      (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
      (let sc (bodyScopeOf tc d))
      {
        (let _s (tcSetPairSites tc (Some (tcPairSitesOf tc d))))
        (let sink nodesNew)
        (let _k (tcSetUnsafeSink tc (Some sink)))
        (let eff (validateEffects tc (nodeCChild d) sc))
        (let _k0 (tcClearUnsafeSink tc))
        (let _s0 (tcClearPairSites tc))
        (let _m0 (tcSetCurModule tc None))
        (let forges (unsafeForgeSitesOf tc))
        (let _f
          (if (> (vecLen forges) 0)
            (effAdd eff (builtinEff "Unsafe"))
            0))
        (let lex (unsafeLexical (nodeCChild d) sink forges))
        {
          ; The signature's first: it is written first.
          (if (! (== (as sig Int) 0))
            (checkAxtagsFrom tc d sig 0 eff (bsEparamsVec sc) lex))
          ; The signature's claims first (above), the function's own
          ; minus the contents already checked, so one claim draws one
          ; diagnostic no matter which declarations carry it.
          (if (! (== (as own Int) 0))
            (checkAxtagsFromSkipping tc d own 0 eff (bsEparamsVec sc) sig lex))
          ; `restrict(...)` on either declaration, as a union - the
          ; same two sets, read once more for a different kind of
          ; claim. Runs on the same `eff` AX3010 and AX3042 read.
          (checkRestricts tc d own sig eff)
          ; A `restrict(no-unsafe)` this row could not refute is asked
          ; once more after every body is typed (`unsafeLatePass`).
          (unsafeNotePending tc d own sig eff)
          ; `;@axiom:ct(...)` holds of the body (AX3092, AX3093).
          (checkCtClaim tc d)
          ; Each required effect needs its own declaration. A purity
          ; claim is answered by AX3010; other claims cannot excuse IO.
          (if (== (tagsClaimPure sig own) 0)
            (checkUndeclaredOne tc d eff own sig "IO"))
          ; `Entropy`, `Spawn` and `Block` are asked of every
          ; declaration, claims or none: `effect(io)` says the body
          ; reaches the outside world, and says nothing about whether it
          ; draws randomness, starts a binding or waits for one.
          (checkUndeclaredRefined tc d eff own sig)
          ; AX3073 asks every declaration that did not SAY
          ; `effect(unsafe)`, whatever else it claimed: an `effect(io)`
          ; claim is about IO, and it used to buy silence about the raw
          ; memory the same body touched. A purity claim is the one
          ; exception - an unsafe operation under it is AX3010 already,
          ; and one mistake is reported once.
          (if (! (|| (== (tagsClaimUnsafe tc sig own) 1) (== (tagsClaimPure sig own) 1)))
            (checkUndeclaredUnsafe tc d (nodeCChild d) sink forges))
          0
        }
      }
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `;@axiom:restrict(...)`: a claim that this declaration does NOT do
; something, answered from analysis the checker already performs and
; used to throw away.
;
; The value is a comma-separated list of restriction names. Nine are
; checked here:
;
;   no-io        `IO` is not in the effect row                 transitive
;   no-alloc     `Alloc` is not in the effect row              transitive
;   no-unsafe    `Unsafe` is not in the effect row             transitive
;   no-foreign   no call-graph path reaches an `extern` item   transitive
;   no-cast      no `cast` head in THIS body                   local
;   no-cast:deep no `cast` head in this body or any reachable  transitive,
;                one                                           opt-in
;   no-recursion no cycle in the call graph reachable from     transitive
;                this declaration
;   no-wrap      no INTEGER `+`, `-` or `*` head in THIS body   local
;   no-trap           no INTEGER `/`, `%`, `<<` or `>>` head in     local
;                THIS body
;
; `no-wrap` is `docs/reference.md`'s slice of the
; Ada-hardening work: `+`/`-`/`*` lower to plain `add`/`sub`/`mul`
; with no `nsw` (silent wraparound on overflow), and `stdlib/Err.ax`'s
; `addChecked`/`subChecked`/`mulChecked` are the alternative this
; restriction forces a claiming body to use instead.
;
; `no-trap` is the same design's named follow-up, one restriction
; over the four operators that trap: `/`/`%` on a zero divisor (exit
; 72) and on the `INT_MIN / -1` corner (exit 83), and `<<`/`>>` on
; an out-of-range shift amount (exit 84). Called `no-untrapped` until
; 0.8.0, when those corners were undefined; the guards landed and the
; rename followed them. `stdlib/Err.ax`'s
; `divChecked`/`remChecked`/`shlChecked`/`shrChecked` are the
; sanctioned alternative, every one `(-> Int Int (Result Int
; Error))`, and calling one pulls `Alloc` into the row exactly as
; the `no-wrap` alternatives do.
;
; INTEGER, and that word is the 2026-09-04 correction. The check
; matches the operator's SPELLING, and two things wearing those
; spellings cannot wrap: the same three on `Float` operands, which
; lower to `fadd`/`fsub`/`fmul` (`restrictEmitWraps`, over the sites
; `checkNumeric` recorded in word 37), and the `for` keyword's own
; counter increment, which the parser generates and the loop guard
; bounds (`isForBump`). Refusing either reported a violation the
; program could not commit; the `for` one additionally underlined the
; word `for` while naming a `+` the source did not contain.
;
; `no-recursion` is a cycle, which is global by definition: a
; depth-first walk over the same graph (`graphCycle`), rendered from
; the declaration to the function the walk met twice - `ping -> pong
; -> pang -> ping`, or `entry -> countdown -> countdown` when the
; cycle is below the claim. A `while` loop is not recursion. It is the
; static half of what `scripts/check-stack-depth.sh` measures
; dynamically: a region under it has a stack need bounded by its depth
; rather than by its input.
;
; EVERY TRANSITIVE VIOLATION NAMES ITS PATH. `graphWitness` walks the
; call graph breadth-first from the claiming declaration to the
; nearest entry where the effect ENTERS - a syscall primitive, an
; extern, a builtin whose row is seeded (`__argc`, `__alloc`), or a
; function whose row carries the effect while no callee does, which is
; a form in its own body - and the message renders the hops with the
; RESOLVED spellings `tcNoteCall` recorded (`Mod$name`,
; `Trait#Type#method`), so a path cross-checks against `#calls=` hop
; for hop. The path is the whole diagnostic: a restriction on a leaf
; is easy to check by reading, a restriction on a call chain is what a
; call graph is for, and printing the chain is the difference between
; "somewhere below you" and a place to put the fix.
;
; THREE ARE TRANSITIVE BY CONSTRUCTION AND ONE IS NOT, and the
; difference is not a policy choice. `no-io` and `no-alloc` read the
; effect row, which is already a transitive fixpoint (`inferEffects`):
; a function calling an IO-performing function HAS `IO` in its row,
; and a check that reads the row has no local reading available to
; it. `no-foreign` walks the call graph the same fixpoint records
; (`tcNoteCall`, FnEnt word 7), because the row cannot tell IO through
; a syscall from IO through an `extern` and the graph can: an extern's
; entry is the one `tcAddExtern` seeds with word 4 = 2, the
; discriminator it writes for exactly this purpose. `no-cast` is
; LEXICAL - a cast is an act performed by the body that writes it, not
; a property that propagates - so it is a walk over this body's
; expression tree, and a callee's casts are that callee's claim to
; make. Measured: 653 `(cast ` in `core/` and `stdlib/` against
; 3,196 `fn` declarations, so a transitive `no-cast` would refuse
; nearly every program that reaches the standard library - which is
; why the transitive reading is the OPT-IN spelling `no-cast:deep`: the
; body's own casts at their spans, and the nearest reachable function
; whose body casts, once, at the declaration, with the path (a span in
; another unit cannot be rendered against this one's source).
;
; `sizeof` and `alignof` share `cast`'s arity exemption in
; `checkCastForm` and nothing else: they read a type's layout and
; reinterpret no value. `isCastLike` groups the three for the
; type-as-first-argument rule; this check asks the narrower question.
;
; A restriction is a PER-DECLARATION claim. A tag attaches to the
; declaration it is written above, and a function has two - the `::`
; and the `fn` - so the set checked is the union of both, exactly as
; `checkAxtags` reads both for `pure`. There is no module-wide form:
; measured, a tag above `(import IO)` attaches to the import and to no
; function's row. Module scope is deferred (`docs/reference.md`, AXTAG
; Keys); a module-wide claim is written on each declaration, and
; `symbols` shows it there.
;
; THE VERDICT READS THE ROW THE WAY `axtagContradicted` DOES, per
; effect and per direction. An effect that is DEFINITE in the row
; (`effDefinite`) is one the body performs, and the claim is
; violated. One that is only POSSIBLE (`effPossible`, not definite) -
; it arrived by NAMING an arrow-typed function without calling it -
; which is not evidence that the body performs it. `effIncomplete`
; says the row is
; a LOWER bound - the walk met a call it could not resolve - so an
; effect ABSENT under that mark is not evidence that the body does
; not. Each of those two readings is unverifiable in exactly one
; direction: present-only-as-possible, absent-and-incomplete.
; Present-and-incomplete is a VIOLATION, because a lower bound that
; already contains the effect is the body performing it whatever the
; unresolved call adds.
;
; A THIRD READING was added on 2026-08-31 and is NOT about a row the
; walk failed to close - it is about a row the walk closed, over a
; body whose answer is not this body's to give. See
; `restrictTransparent` below. All three answers are AX3051, a
; WARNING, for AX3037's reason: a limit of the analysis is not a
; defect in the program, and refusing `((b.f) 7)` under
; `restrict(no-io)` would make dispatch through a stored function
; unwriteable rather than unverified.
;
; `no-cast` is checked under either marker, because a cast in this
; body is this body's whether or not the walk resolved a call
; elsewhere in it.
;
; A violation is AX3049, an ERROR with no warning stage, for the
; argument that promoted AX3010 on 2026-08-25: the tag is a claim the
; author WROTE, and a build shipping a false one publishes a guarantee
; the program does not keep. Unlike AX3010's history there is no
; conflated second shape to disentangle first - the row contains the
; effect or it does not, the graph contains the edge or it does not.
; Deleting the tag silences it and WITHDRAWS the claim; an
; unrestricted function is never asked.
;
; A name that is not a restriction is AX3052, also an ERROR. The list
; of restrictions is CLOSED, unlike the key namespace AX3039 guards,
; so `no-fo` inside `restrict(...)` is not metadata: it is a claim that
; reads like a guarantee and buys silence, in the one place the
; compiler has said it checks. AX3016 refuses an unknown effect name
; in a `handle` list for the same reason.
(pub :: checkRestricts (-> TC ASTNode Axtags Axtags Strings Int))
(pub fn (checkRestricts tc d own sig eff)
  {
    (let rs vecNew)
    {
      (restrictCollect sig rs 0)
      (restrictCollect own rs 0)
      ; `;@axiom:isr` marks an interrupt entry point and implies
      ; `restrict(no-alloc)`: a handler runs where no heap can grow,
      ; so a body that allocates is refused here rather than
      ; corrupting memory at 3 a.m. The implication is pushed into
      ; this same set - not checked twice elsewhere - so the
      ; violation, the unverifiable warning and `strict` all read
      ; exactly as if the author had written the restriction: an
      ; `isr` that allocates draws AX3049 naming `no-alloc`, which
      ; the tag means. `strInVec` dedups for the author who wrote
      ; both spellings, so the claim is still checked once. The
      ; parameter half lives beside it (`checkIsrParams`): an entry
      ; point the hardware calls by name takes no arguments.
      ;
      ; `no-recursion` is implied the same way: a handler runs on a
      ; stack it does not own - the interrupted code's, or the fault
      ; stack - on top of whatever depth that stack had reached, so its
      ; own depth must be bounded, and a call graph with no cycle is
      ; what the stack bound (RP-7) can bound. A handler bound to a
      ; vector (`isr(irq)`, `isr(fault)`) is also refused an operation
      ; that waits (`checkIsrWaits`, AX4009).
      (if (== (tagsHaveIsr sig own) 1)
        {
          (if (! (== (strInVec rs "no-alloc" 0) 1))
            (vecPush rs "no-alloc"))
          (if (! (== (strInVec rs "no-recursion" 0) 1))
            (vecPush rs "no-recursion"))
          ; and `no-block`: a handler that waits for another binding,
          ; a lock or a child waits with the interrupted code stopped
          ; underneath it, which is how an interrupt deadlocks.
          (if (! (== (strInVec rs "no-block" 0) 1))
            (vecPush rs "no-block"))
          (let vector (isrVectorOf sig own))
          (checkIsrParams tc d vector)
          (checkIsrWaits tc d vector eff)
        })
      ; `strict` is read off the whole SET, before any name in it is
      ; checked, because it modifies how every one of them answers.
      (tcSetRestrictionsStrict tc (== (restrictHasStrict rs 0) 1))
      (checkRestrictsFrom tc d rs 0 eff)
      (tcSetRestrictionsStrict tc false)
    }
    0
  })

; Whether either tag set carries an `isr` claim. The key ends at
; a present `(`, so bare `isr` and `isr(foo)` both claim it.
(pub :: tagsHaveIsr (-> Axtags Axtags Int))
(pub fn (tagsHaveIsr sig own)
  (if (== (tagsHaveIsrIn sig 0) 1)
    1
    (tagsHaveIsrIn own 0)))

(pub :: tagsHaveIsrIn (-> Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (tagsHaveIsrIn tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (let content (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)))
      {
        (let op (strFindByte content 40 0))
        (if (strEq
              (if (let (Some opening) op)
                (strSlice content 0 opening)
                content)
              "isr")
          1
          (tagsHaveIsrIn tags (+ i 1)))
      }
    }))

; An interrupt entry point takes no arguments: the hardware calls it
; by name. Anything else is the tag contradicting the declaration,
; which is AX3010's whole subject - the walk HAS an answer here, so
; it is not AX3037. Only functions are asked (`d` is one where this
; runs, and the guard says so rather than assuming it); a `::` with
; no `fn` under it never reaches `checkRestricts` with parameters to
; count.
;
; `isr(fault)` is the one exception: the fault hook is called by the
; runtime, not the hardware, with five `Int`s - the status the fixed
; exit would answer, the vector offset, ESR_EL1, ELR_EL1 and FAR_EL1 -
; and its `Int` answer is the exit status. The dispatch passes five
; words whatever the declaration says, so the declaration must say
; exactly that: five parameters, each `Int`, and an `Int` result.
(pub :: checkIsrParams (-> TC ASTNode (Option String) Int))
(pub fn (checkIsrParams tc d v)
  (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
    0
    (if (match v
      ((None) false)
      ((Some vector) (strEq vector "fault")))
      (if (== (isrFaultSigOk tc d) 1)
        0
        (emitAxtag tc d
          "`isr(fault)` binds the fault hook, which the runtime calls with five `Int`s - the status the fixed exit would answer, the vector offset (-1 for a software trap), ESR_EL1, ELR_EL1 and FAR_EL1 - and whose `Int` answer is the exit status. Declare it `(-> Int Int Int Int Int Int)`"))
      {
        (let ps (nodeBNames d))
        (if (== (as ps Int) 0)
          0
          (if (== (vecLen ps) 0)
            0
            (emitAxtag tc d
              "`isr` marks an interrupt entry point, which the hardware calls by name with no arguments - this declaration takes parameters, so it cannot be one. Remove the parameters, or drop the tag")))
      })))

; Five parameters, and a declared type of six `Int`s: `(-> Int Int Int
; Int Int Int)`, which the checker holds as five nested arrows
; (`mkTArr`), each from `Int`, ending in `Int`. A declaration the table
; has no entry for is not asked: there is nothing to compare.
(pub :: isrFaultSigOk (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (isrFaultSigOk tc d)
  {
    (let ps (nodeBNames d))
    (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
    (if (|| (== (as ps Int) 0) (!= (vecLen ps) 5))
      0
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        (isrIntArrows (fnEntTy ent) 5)
        1))
  })

; `t` is `n` arrows from `Int`, ending in `Int`.
(pub :: isrIntArrows (-> ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (isrIntArrows t0 n)
  {
    (let t (tyResolve t0))
    (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
      0
      (if (== n 0)
        (isrIsInt t)
        (if (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
          (if (== (isrIsInt (tyResolve (nodeAChild t))) 1)
            (isrIntArrows (nodeBChild t) (- n 1))
            0)
          0)))
  })

(pub :: isrIsInt (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (isrIsInt t)
  (if (&& (! (nodeSame t nilNode))
        (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
          (&& (strEq (nodeAName t) "Int") (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0))))
    1
    0))

; Some vector text for an `isr(...)` tag, including explicit empty
; text, or None for a bare `isr` or no binding. A nonempty signature
; binding wins; otherwise the function's own binding is used.
(pub :: isrVectorOf (-> Axtags Axtags (Option String)))
(pub fn (isrVectorOf sig own)
  {
    (let signature (isrVectorIn sig 0))
    (if (let (Some vector) signature)
      (if (> (strLen vector) 0)
        (Some vector)
        (isrVectorIn own 0))
      (isrVectorIn own 0))
  })

(pub :: isrVectorIn (-> Axtags Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (isrVectorIn tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    None
    {
      (let content (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)))
      (if (&& (strStartsWith content "isr(") (== (strByte content (- (strLen content) 1)) 41))
        (Some (strTrim (strSlice content 4 (- (strLen content) 5))))
        (isrVectorIn tags (+ i 1)))
    }))

; AX4009: a handler bound to a vector reaches an operation that waits.
; Nothing the interrupted code does can happen while a handler runs -
; it resumes only when the handler returns, and never after a fault -
; so a wait inside one waits on nothing that can come.
;
; Two operations wait, and the call graph reaches both (`graphSeeds`):
;   syscall  a `__syscallN` primitive, where every blocking library
;            call ends - a lock, a channel, a join, a sleep. On
;            baremetal-aarch64 there is no kernel, and it traps 74.
;   wfi      `__arm_wfi`, which waits for an interrupt. In an IRQ
;            handler the core has masked IRQs; the fault hook MAY halt
;            in `wfi`, because a hook that never returns is one of the
;            answers it exists to give, and a low-power halt is the
;            usual safe state.
; The walk follows the edges a call through a field, or through a local
; that holds one of several functions, may take (`graphWitnessAny`): a
; handler that MAY wait is refused, as one that certainly waits is.
; A poll of a volatile word the handler waits on is a wait this walk
; can't see: that stays the program's obligation (MM-EXEC-18).
(pub :: checkIsrWaits (-> TC ASTNode (Option String) Strings Int))
(pub fn (checkIsrWaits tc d vector eff)
  (if (let (Some v) vector)
    (if (|| (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
          (if (strEq v "irq")
            false
            (if (strEq v "fault")
              false
              true)))
      0
      {
        (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          {
            {
              (let sysFound (graphWitnessAny tc (Some ent) "syscall"))
              (if (let (Some sys) sysFound)
                (emitIsrWaits tc d v
                  (concat
                    (concat
                      "reaches a system call, where every blocking library call ends, and baremetal-aarch64 has no kernel to answer it: "
                      (graphRender sys))
                    "")
                  sys)
                ())
            }
            (if (strEq v "irq")
              {
                (let wFound (graphWitnessAny tc (Some ent) "wfi"))
                (if (let (Some w) wFound)
                  (emitIsrWaits tc d v
                    (concat "waits for an interrupt with IRQs masked: " (graphRender w)) w)
                  0)
              }
              0)
          }
          0)
      })
    0))

(pub :: emitIsrWaits (-> TC ASTNode String String (Vec FnEnt) Int))
(pub fn (emitIsrWaits tc d v why path)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (restrictPathSecs tc path 1
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX4009" "isr-waits" (nodeSpan d)
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` is bound by `isr(") v)
            (concat ")` and " why))
          None
          (Some
            "a handler bound to a vector runs while the code it interrupted is stopped - until it returns, for `isr(irq)`, and for good, for `isr(fault)` - so anything it waits for can't come. Every blocking library call ends in a system call, which baremetal-aarch64 answers with trap 74, and `__arm_wfi` in an IRQ handler sleeps with the interrupt that would wake it masked. Set a flag the main loop reads instead, and do the waiting there. A fault hook may halt in `wfi`: never returning is one of its answers"))))))

(pub :: restrictHasStrict (-> Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (restrictHasStrict rs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rs))
    0
    (if (strEq (vecGetStr rs i) "strict")
      1
      (restrictHasStrict rs (+ i 1)))))

; Every name in every `restrict(...)` tag of `tags`, deduplicated, in
; source order - the signature's first, because `checkRestricts` reads
; it first. The value runs to the LAST `)` (`axtagCloseAt`, the rule
; every reader applies since phase 0 of this work), and the names are
; split on `,` with the blanks around each trimmed: a space is not a
; restriction name, and reporting ` no-alloc` as unknown would be a
; diagnostic about the author's typing rather than their claim.
(pub :: restrictCollect (-> Axtags Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (restrictCollect tags rs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (let content (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)))
      {
        (let op (strFindByte content 40 0))
        {
          (if (strEq
                (if (let (Some opening) op)
                  (strSlice content 0 opening)
                  content)
                "restrict")
            (restrictSplit (axtagListValue content op) rs)
            ())
          (restrictCollect tags rs (+ i 1))
        }
      }
    }))

; The bytes between a present opening delimiter and the last `)`.
; Missing or invalid delimiters normalise to empty text.
(pub :: axtagListValue (-> String (Option Int) String))
(pub fn (axtagListValue content opening)
  (if (let (Some op) opening)
    (if (let (Some closing) (axtagCloseAt content))
      (if (|| (< op 0) (<= closing op))
        ""
        (strSlice content (+ op 1) (- closing (+ op 1))))
      "")
    ""))

; Split on `,`, trimming blanks, pushing each name once. A tag naming
; NOTHING - `restrict` or `restrict()` - pushes the empty name, so
; that it is reported (AX3052) rather than read as a claim of
; nothing.
(pub :: restrictSplit (-> String Strings Strings))
(pub fn (restrictSplit val rs)
  {
    (let mut start 0)
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut any 0)
    {
      (while (<= i (strLen val))
        {
          (if (|| (== i (strLen val)) (== (strByte val i) 44))
            {
              {
                (let name (strTrimBlanks (strSlice val start (- i start))))
                (if (> (strLen name) 0)
                  {
                    (set any 1)
                    (if (! (== (strInVec rs name 0) 1))
                      (vecPush rs name))
                  })
              }
              (set start (+ i 1))
            })
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (if (== any 0)
        (vecPush rs ""))
      rs
    }
  })

(pub :: strTrimBlanks (-> String String))
(pub fn (strTrimBlanks s)
  {
    (let mut a 0)
    (let mut b (strLen s))
    {
      (while (if (< a b)
        (isBlankByte (strByte s a))
        false)
        (set a (+ a 1)))
      (while (if (< a b)
        (isBlankByte (strByte s (- b 1)))
        false)
        (set b (- b 1)))
      (strSlice s a (- b a))
    }
  })

(pub :: checkRestrictsFrom (-> TC ASTNode Strings Int Strings Int))
(pub fn (checkRestrictsFrom tc d rs i eff)
  (if (>= i (vecLen rs))
    0
    {
      ; THE THIRD READING OF "UNVERIFIABLE", asked only when the two
      ; the header already describes said nothing. `checkOneRestrict`
      ; either refutes the claim (AX3049), reports it unverifiable
      ; (AX3051) or is silent; silence is the only case where a body
      ; that CALLS one of its own parameters has not been accounted
      ; for, and asking after the fact rather than before is what
      ; keeps a definite violation reported once, as a violation.
      ; Counting the diagnostics vector is how "said nothing" is
      ; answered without threading a return value through six checks.
      (if (strEq (vecGetStr rs i) "strict")
        ()
        ; the modifier itself restricts nothing, so it is neither
        ; checked nor unknown
        {
          (let before (vecLen (tcOutVec tc)))
          {
            (checkOneRestrict tc d (vecGetStr rs i) eff)
            (if (== (vecLen (tcOutVec tc)) before)
              (restrictTransparent tc d (vecGetStr rs i)))
          }
        })
      (checkRestrictsFrom tc d rs (+ i 1) eff)
    }))

; A TRANSITIVE RESTRICTION OVER AN EFFECT-TRANSPARENT BODY CANNOT BE
; CHECKED, and until 2026-08-31 it was not merely unchecked but SILENT.
;
; `docs/reference.md` (Effect Polymorphism) records the reading that
; makes `;@axiom:effect(pure)` on `(fn (apply f x) (f x))` stand: a
; higher-order function's summary has two halves, the effects its own
; body performs and the parameters it CALLS, and claims are validated
; against the concrete half. That is right for `pure`, which is a claim
; about a BODY. A restriction is not read that way by anyone: `no-io`
; on a declaration reads as "calling this performs no IO", and for a
; body whose work is its argument's that is the caller's business.
;
; MEASURED BEFORE THIS EXISTED, on the merged tree: `(fn (runIt f n)
; (f n))` under each of the five transitive restrictions checked `OK`
; with no diagnostic at all, and `restrict(no-io)` over `{ (f "x") n }`
; printed to stdout at run time. `no-recursion` was silent over a
; program that really does recurse - `runIt -> back -> runIt`, a cycle
; whose middle edge is a parameter the call graph has no edge for.
;
; The compiler KNEW. `symbols` renders `#effect-params=f` on exactly
; these rows (`FnEnt` word 6), and nothing in this file read it. That
; is the same shape as `restrict(no-alloc)` before `MM-EXEC-9a`'s
; constructor row closed: a marker that exists, a check that does not
; consult it, and a claim that therefore cannot fail.
;
; AX3051 AND NOT AX3049, because the claim is not refuted - it is
; undecidable here, and which way it falls is chosen at every call
; site. That is AX3037's argument, and it is the same warning the two
; existing readings get.
;
; THE LEXICAL THREE ARE EXCLUDED, and that is the same line the header
; draws: `no-cast`, `no-wrap` and `no-trap` are properties of the
; bytes of THIS body, which a parameter cannot change.
;
; BLAST RADIUS, measured over `core/main.ax`: 347 rows carry a
; restriction, 5 rows carry `#effect-params=`, and the intersection is
; **0**. Nothing in this tree changes; the check costs one `vecLen`
; per restriction claim on the silent path.
(pub :: restrictTransparent (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (restrictTransparent tc d r)
  (if (== (restrictIsTransitive r) 0)
    0
    {
      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        {
          (let eps (fnEntEparams ent))
          (if (== (as eps Int) 0)
            0
            (if (== (vecLen eps) 0)
              0
              (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
                (concat
                  (concat "the body CALLS its own parameter " (restrictEparamNames d eps 0 ""))
                  ", so what it performs is decided by the argument each caller passes - the effect row is complete about this body and says nothing about that call")
                vecNew)))
        }
        0)
    }))

; The five that are decided by walking somewhere else - the effect row,
; which is a transitive fixpoint, or the call graph. `no-cast`,
; `no-wrap` and `no-trap` are lexical and are deliberately not
; here; keep this in step with the Scope column of
; `docs/reference.md`'s restriction table, which is where the
; distinction is stated to the author.
(pub :: restrictIsTransitive (-> String Int))
(pub fn (restrictIsTransitive r)
  (if (strEq r "no-io")
    1
    (if (strEq r "no-alloc")
      1
      (if (strEq r "no-foreign")
        1
        (if (strEq r "no-recursion")
          1
          (if (strEq r "no-cast:deep")
            1
            (if (strEq r "no-unsafe")
              1
              ; `no-escape` reads the region facts, a fixpoint over
              ; every callee's stores (stage S3, `restrictNoEscape`).
              (if (strEq r "no-escape")
                1
                ; the three refinements of `IO`, read off the row as
                ; `no-io` is
                (if (strEq r "no-entropy")
                  1
                  (if (strEq r "no-spawn")
                    1
                    (if (strEq r "no-block")
                      1
                      0)))))))))))

; The effect-transparent parameters by NAME, backticked and comma
; joined. `FnEnt` word 6 holds indices, and `symbols` renders them the
; same way (`saJoinComma` over the header's names); a diagnostic that
; said `parameter 0` would make the author count.
(pub :: restrictEparamNames (-> ASTNode Ints Int String String))
(pub fn (restrictEparamNames d eps i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen eps))
    acc
    (restrictEparamNames d eps (+ i 1)
      (concat
        (if (== (strLen acc) 0)
          ""
          (concat acc ", "))
        (concat (concat "`" (restrictParamName d (vecGet eps i))) "`")))))

; The `k`th parameter's spelling, from the `fn` header. A `::` with no
; `fn` under it never reaches `checkRestricts`, and an index the header
; is too short for is impossible unless the two disagree - answered
; with the index rather than a crash, because a diagnostic is not the
; place to discover that.
(pub :: restrictParamName (-> ASTNode Int String))
(pub fn (restrictParamName d k)
  {
    (let ps (nodeBNames d))
    (if (== (as ps Int) 0)
      "?"
      (if (>= k (vecLen ps))
        "?"
        (vecGetStr ps k)))
  })

; The closed list. A name not matched here is AX3052; keep the help in
; `emitRestrictUnknown` in step with these arms, because it is the
; only place the list is stated to the author. `no-cast:deep` is a
; separate name and not a modifier: `no-cast` alone is local by
; design, and the deep reading is opted into by spelling it.
(pub :: checkOneRestrict (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Int))
(pub fn (checkOneRestrict tc d r eff)
  (if (strEq r "no-io")
    (restrictEffect tc d r eff "IO")
    (if (strEq r "no-alloc")
      (restrictEffect tc d r eff "Alloc")
      (if (strEq r "no-unsafe")
        (restrictEffect tc d r eff "Unsafe")
        (if (strEq r "no-cast")
          (restrictNoCast tc d r (nodeCChild d))
          (if (strEq r "no-cast:deep")
            (restrictNoCastDeep tc d r eff)
            (if (strEq r "no-foreign")
              (restrictNoForeign tc d r eff)
              (if (strEq r "no-recursion")
                (restrictNoRecursion tc d r eff)
                (if (strEq r "no-wrap")
                  (restrictNoWrap tc d r (nodeCChild d))
                  (if (strEq r "no-trap")
                    (restrictNoTrap tc d r (nodeCChild d))
                    (if (strEq r "no-escape")
                      (restrictNoEscape tc d r)
                      (if (strEq r "no-entropy")
                        (restrictEffect tc d r eff "Entropy")
                        (if (strEq r "no-spawn")
                          (restrictEffect tc d r eff "Spawn")
                          (if (strEq r "no-block")
                            (restrictEffect tc d r eff "Block")
                            (emitRestrictUnknown tc d r)))))))))))))))

; `no-io`, `no-alloc` and `no-unsafe`: the effect row, read per
; direction as the header explains.
(pub :: restrictEffect (-> TC ASTNode String Strings String Int))
(pub fn (restrictEffect tc d r eff e)
  (if (== (effDefinite eff (builtinEff e)) 1)
    ; A forging cast in this body is the nearest witness there is, and
    ; the call graph cannot name it: it is not a call. So it is named
    ; here, before any path is walked.
    (if (&& (strEq e "Unsafe") (> (vecLen (unsafeForgeSitesOf tc)) 0))
      (emitRestrictViolated tc d r
        (concat "the body performs Unsafe: it " (unsafeForgeText (unsafeForgeSitesOf tc) 0)) vecNew)
      ; The path is walked ONCE and handed to both halves - the prose
      ; and the related locations - so the two cannot come to disagree
      ; about which hops there are.
      {
        (let path (graphWitness tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d) e))
        (emitRestrictViolated tc d r
          (concat (concat "the body performs " e) (witnessTextOf tc path e))
          (witnessSecPath tc path e))
      })
    (if (== (effPossible eff (builtinEff e)) 1)
      (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
        (concat (concat "`" e)
          "` reaches the effect row only as a possible effect - the body names a function without calling it, or calls a field whose stored functions do not all perform it - so its presence is not evidence the body performs it")
        vecNew)
      (if (== (effUnresolved eff) 1)
        (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
          (concat
            (concat
              "the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve, so the effect row is a lower bound and the absence of `"
              e)
            "` from it is not evidence")
          vecNew)
        0))))

; `no-foreign`: `graphWitness` over FnEnt word 7 from this
; declaration's own entry, for an entry whose word 4 is 2. The graph
; has an edge for every resolved call and every syscall primitive.
; It also has an edge for a function the body merely NAMES (`effRefSiteAt`
; records the edge before deciding whether the reference is a call),
; and the graph does not say which edges those are. The row does, for
; the one effect an extern carries: an extern is seeded `IO`, so an
; extern reached through a named-not-called function puts IO into
; this row as POSSIBLE (`effPossible`), and an extern found while IO
; is possible here is unverifiable, not violated - whether or not IO
; is also definite, because the graph cannot say which path the hit
; is on. IO nowhere possible means every edge to the hit is a call.
; What the graph does NOT have is the call the effect walk could not
; resolve, the hole `effIncomplete` marks, so an extern NOT found under
; that mark is unverifiable too.
(pub :: restrictNoForeign (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Int))
(pub fn (restrictNoForeign tc d r eff)
  {
    (let pathFound (graphWitness tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d) "extern"))
    (if (let (Some path) pathFound)
      {
        (let hit (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)))
        (if (== (effPossible eff (builtinEff "IO")) 1)
          (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
            (concat
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat "the call graph reaches the `extern` item `" (bareOf (fnEntName hit)))
                  "` through a function the body names without calling, so the edge is not evidence of a call: ")
                (graphRender path))
              "")
            path)
          (emitRestrictViolated tc d r
            (concat
              (concat (concat "the body reaches the `extern` item `" (bareOf (fnEntName hit)))
                "`: ")
              (graphRender path))
            path))
      }
      (if (== (effGraphUnresolved eff) 1)
        (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
          (if (effGraphOnly eff)
            "the body calls through a field that declares its effects, and the compiler could not list the functions the field holds, so the call graph is incomplete there"
            "the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve, and the call graph has no edge for it")
          vecNew)
        {
          (let mayPathFound (graphWitnessMay tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d) "extern"))
          (if (let (Some mayPath) mayPathFound)
            (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat "the body may reach the `extern` item `"
                    (bareOf (fnEntName (vecGet mayPath (- (vecLen mayPath) 1)))))
                  "` through a call through a field or a local that holds one of several functions: ")
                (graphRender mayPath))
              mayPath)
            0)
        }))
  })

; `no-recursion`: `graphCycle` from this declaration's own entry. A
; cycle found through an edge the body only NAMES (`effByRef`) is not
; a call cycle the walk can vouch for, so it is unverifiable, as for
; `no-foreign`; no cycle found beside a call the walk could not resolve
; is unverifiable too, because the unresolved call may close one.
(pub :: restrictNoRecursion (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Int))
(pub fn (restrictNoRecursion tc d r eff)
  {
    (let pathFound (graphCycle tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d)))
    (if (let (Some path) pathFound)
      {
        (let again (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)))
        (if (== (effAnyPossibleOnly eff) 1)
          (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
            (concat
              (concat (concat "the call graph closes a cycle at `" (bareOf (fnEntName again)))
                "` through a function the body names without calling, so the edge is not evidence of a call: ")
              (graphRender path))
            path)
          (emitRestrictViolated tc d r
            (concat
              (concat (concat "the call graph closes a cycle at `" (bareOf (fnEntName again)))
                "`: ")
              (graphRender path))
            path))
      }
      (if (== (effGraphUnresolved eff) 1)
        (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
          (if (effGraphOnly eff)
            "the body calls through a field that declares its effects, and the compiler could not list the functions the field holds, so the call graph is incomplete there"
            "the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve, and that call may close a cycle the graph does not show")
          vecNew)
        {
          (let mayPathFound (graphCycleMay tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d)))
          (if (let (Some mayPath) mayPathFound)
            (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat "the call graph may close a cycle at `"
                    (bareOf (fnEntName (vecGet mayPath (- (vecLen mayPath) 1)))))
                  "` through a call through a field or a local that holds one of several functions: ")
                (graphRender mayPath))
              mayPath)
            0)
        }))
  })

; The first cycle reachable from `start`, as the walk's own path: every
; entry from `start` down to the one met twice, and that entry once
; more at the end. None means no reachable cycle. The path includes
; the start even when the cycle is below it.
;
; Depth-first with an explicit stack of the entries on the current
; path (`vecPush`/`vecPop`) and a `done` set of names whose subtrees
; are closed, so the walk is linear in the graph and a node reached
; twice by different paths is not searched twice. A callee ON THE
; STACK is the cycle; a callee in `done` was searched from elsewhere
; and closed no cycle through the stack as it stands - which is
; sufficient, because any cycle reachable from it would have been
; found when it was searched.
(pub :: graphCycle (-> TC (Option FnEnt) (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphCycle tc start)
  (if (let (Some entry) start)
    (graphCycleVia tc entry 0)
    None))

; The first cycle through certain and may edges alike, for the reason
; `graphWitnessMay` gives.
(pub :: graphCycleMay (-> TC (Option FnEnt) (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphCycleMay tc start)
  (if (let (Some entry) start)
    (graphCycleVia tc entry 1)
    None))

(pub :: graphCycleVia (-> TC FnEnt Int (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphCycleVia tc start withMay)
  (graphCycleFrom tc start vecNew vecNew withMay))

(pub :: graphCycleFrom (-> TC FnEnt (Vec FnEnt) Strings Int (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphCycleFrom tc ent stack done withMay)
  {
    (vecPush stack ent)
    (let certain (graphCycleEdges tc ent.calls 0 stack done withMay))
    (let found
      (if (|| (isSome certain) (== withMay 0))
        certain
        (graphCycleEdges tc ent.mayCalls 0 stack done withMay)))
    (vecPop stack)
    (if (isNone found)
      (vecPush done ent.name))
    found
  })

(pub :: graphCycleEdges (-> TC Names Int (Vec FnEnt) Strings Int (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphCycleEdges tc calls i stack done withMay)
  (if (>= i (vecLen calls))
    None
    {
      (let name (vecGetStr calls i))
      (if (let (Some on) (graphStackIndex stack name))
          (Some (graphCyclePath stack on (vecGet stack on) vecNew))
        (== (strInVec done name 0) 1)
          (graphCycleEdges tc calls (+ i 1) stack done withMay)
        {
          (let found
            (if (let (Some e) (fnEntExact tc name))
              (graphCycleFrom tc e stack done withMay)
              None))
          (if (isSome found)
            found
            (graphCycleEdges tc calls (+ i 1) stack done withMay))
        })
    }))

; Where `name` sits on the walk stack, or `None`.
;
; `Option` rather than -1: the caller reads the answer as
; `(vecGet stack on)`, so a miss that reached the index is a wild read.
(pub :: graphStackIndex (-> (Vec FnEnt) String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (graphStackIndex stack name)
  (graphStackIndexFrom stack name 0))

(pub :: graphStackIndexFrom (-> (Vec FnEnt) String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (graphStackIndexFrom stack name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen stack))
    None
    (if (strEq (fnEntName (vecGet stack i)) name)
      (Some i)
      (graphStackIndexFrom stack name (+ i 1)))))

; The whole stack, root first, then the repeated entry once more.
; The whole stack and not the stack from `on`: the hops above the
; cycle are the path from the claim to it, which is what the reader
; needs when the cycle is below the declaration that claimed.
(pub :: graphCyclePath (-> (Vec FnEnt) Int FnEnt (Vec FnEnt) (Vec FnEnt)))
(pub fn (graphCyclePath stack on again acc)
  {
    (for i in 0..(vecLen stack)
      (vecPush acc (vecGet stack i)))
    (vecPush acc again)
    acc
  })

; The suffix a `no-io`/`no-alloc` violation carries: `: a -> b -> c`,
; ending at the entry where the effect enters; or, when the walk ends
; at a function whose row carries the effect and whose callees do not,
; the note that it is a form in that body. `graphWitness` cannot fail
; to find a path for an effect the row carries - every source of `IO`
; is a seed and every source of `Alloc` is a seed or a form - but the
; fallback says so rather than rendering nothing, because a message
; that silently lost its evidence is the AX3039 shape in another coat.
;
; THE FALLBACK NAMES A CONSTRUCTOR FIRST since 2026-08-31, because it
; is now much the commonest way to reach this arm and it is the one
; with no keyword to search for. The line used to offer `alloc` and
; `handle` alone, and against
;
;     (fn (mk n) (match (Wrap n) ((Wrap x) x) ((Empty) 0)))
;
; that sends the reader hunting for an `alloc` form the body does not
; contain. A message that lists every source but the one in front of
; the reader is the same defect as one that lists none.
; TAKES THE PATH rather than walking for one, so that the related
; locations beside the message are built from the very vector the
; prose was rendered from. It used to call `graphWitness` itself and
; throw the vector away, which is exactly why the `->` chain lived
; only inside the quoted string.
(pub :: witnessTextOf (-> TC (Option (Vec FnEnt)) String String))
(pub fn (witnessTextOf tc pathFound e)
  (match pathFound
    ((None) " (the walk found no path to a source of it)")
    ((Some path)
      (if (== (vecLen path) 0)
        " (the walk found no path to a source of it)"
        {
          (let last (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)))
          (if (== (graphSeeds tc last e 1) 1)
            (concat ": " (graphRender path))
            (if (== (vecLen path) 1)
              " in its own body (a constructor or closure, an `alloc` form, a `handle` installing a handler, or a call through a field that declares the effect)"
              (concat (concat (concat ": " (graphRender path)) ", in ")
                (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (fnEntName last))) "`'s own body"))))
        })
    )))

; The path the RELATED LOCATIONS are built from - which is the path
; the prose rendered, and 0 in the one arm above that renders no chain
; at all. The two are decided by the same three tests, in the same
; order, because a `^` field for a hop the message never named would
; be a location with nothing to explain it.
(pub :: witnessSecPath (-> TC (Option (Vec FnEnt)) String (Vec FnEnt)))
(pub fn (witnessSecPath tc pathFound e)
  (match pathFound
    ((None) vecNew)
    ((Some path)
      (if (== (vecLen path) 0)
        vecNew
        (if (== (graphSeeds tc (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)) e 1) 1)
          path
          (if (== (vecLen path) 1)
            vecNew
            path)))
    )))

; This declaration's own entry, looked up under its own module -
; `inferEffectsOne` states why: a private function is invisible to a
; lookup made from nowhere.
(pub :: restrictOwnEnt (-> TC ASTNode (Option FnEnt)))
(pub fn (restrictOwnEnt tc d)
  {
    (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
    {
      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
      {
        (tcSetCurModule tc None)
        entFound
      }
    }
  })

; An effect KIND as the row spells it. The restriction checks ask this
; walk for "IO" and "Alloc", which are built-in and tagged `b:`; item
; 3's `checkUnhandledAtMain` asks it for a CUSTOM effect's name, which
; is tagged `c:`. `isBuiltinEffect` is the same question the handle
; list asks, so the two readings cannot drift apart.
;
; "extern" and "cast" never reach here - `graphSeeds` answers them
; before the row is consulted - and neither names an effect, so an
; encoding for them would be a fiction.
(pub :: graphEffKey (-> String String))
(pub fn (graphEffKey kind)
  (if (== (isBuiltinEffect kind) 1)
    (builtinEff kind)
    (customEff kind)))

; The witness: the nearest entry reachable from `start` at which
; `kind` ENTERS the graph, with the path to it. `kind` is "IO",
; "Alloc", "extern", "cast", or the NAME of a declared custom effect.
; Some retains the path from `start` to the witness inclusive. None
; means nothing reachable qualifies; a hit always includes the start.
;
; A custom effect has no seed of its own: an operation is not a
; function and `tcNoteCall` records no edge for it, so the entry that
; PERFORMS one is exactly the entry `graphOwnSource` describes - the
; row carries the effect and no callee does. That is why the custom
; case needs no arm in `graphSeeds`: it is already the shape the
; `handle` in this body was written for.
;
; Breadth-first, so the witness is the one at the fewest hops; `seen`
; is keyed by the resolved name every edge already carries; a parent
; index is kept per queue slot so the path reads back from the hit.
; Every entry's word 7 is a Vec - `regFn`, `regFnEff`, `tcAddExtern`
; and `tcCollectFn` all construct one - so no edge list needs a guard.
(pub :: graphWitness (-> TC (Option FnEnt) String (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphWitness tc start kind)
  (if (let (Some entry) start)
    (graphWitnessVia tc entry kind 0)
    None))

; The witness through the edges a body MAY take as well as the certain
; ones. `FnEnt` word 11 holds the functions a call through a field, or
; through a local that holds one of several functions, may reach. The
; effect fixpoint follows those edges and records what they bring as
; POSSIBLE, so a check that reads the graph follows them too: a path
; that needs one shows where a claim may fail, and no path through
; either kind proves the claim as far as the graph goes. A call the
; walk could not resolve has no edge of either kind, which is why
; every caller tests `effUnresolved` first.
(pub :: graphWitnessMay (-> TC (Option FnEnt) String (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphWitnessMay tc start kind)
  (if (let (Some entry) start)
    (graphWitnessVia tc entry kind 1)
    None))

; A certain path when there is one, else a path through may edges: what
; a check asks when it must refuse anything the body may reach, and
; should still name the certain route.
(pub :: graphWitnessAny (-> TC (Option FnEnt) String (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphWitnessAny tc start kind)
  (if (let (Some certain) (graphWitness tc start kind))
    (Some certain)
    (graphWitnessMay tc start kind)))

(pub :: graphWitnessVia (-> TC FnEnt String Int (Option (Vec FnEnt))))
(pub fn (graphWitnessVia tc start kind withMay)
  {
    (let queue (:: vecNew (Vec FnEnt)))
    (let parent (:: vecNew (Vec (Option Int))))
    (let seen (:: vecNew Names))
    (let mut qi 0)
    (let mut hit (:: None (Option Int)))
    (vecPush queue start)
    (vecPush parent None)
    (vecPush seen start.name)
    (while (&& (isNone hit) (< qi (vecLen queue)))
      {
        (let ent (vecGet queue qi))
        (if (== (graphSeeds tc ent kind qi) 1)
          (set hit (Some qi))
          (if (== (graphOwnSource tc ent kind) 1)
            (set hit (Some qi))
            {
              (graphEnqueue tc ent.calls 0 queue parent seen qi (graphStopsAtTrusted kind))
              (if (== withMay 1)
                (graphEnqueue tc ent.mayCalls 0 queue parent seen qi (graphStopsAtTrusted kind)))
            }))
        (set qi (+ qi 1))
      })
    (match hit
      ((None) None)
      ((Some index) (Some (graphReadBack queue parent (Some index) vecNew))))
  })

; Where `kind` ENTERS rather than being inherited:
;
;   extern  an entry `tcAddExtern` marked, word 4 = 2;
;   IO      a syscall primitive - its row is EMPTY, `walkCallHead` adds
;           the effect at the call site, so `isSyscallPrim` and not the
;           row is the test - or an extern, or a builtin whose seeded
;           row carries it (`__argc`, `__argv`);
;   Alloc   a builtin whose seeded row carries it (`__alloc`, the
;           arena primitives);
;   Unsafe  an extern, a raw-memory primitive or a syscall - its row carries `Mut`
;           or `Alloc` (a syscall's is empty) but never `Unsafe` itself,
;           `walkCallHead` adds that at the call site, so `isUnsafePrim`
;           and not the row is the test;
;   cast    a function other than the start (`depth` > 0; the start's
;           own casts are reported at their spans by the local walk)
;           whose declaration's body holds a `cast` head;
;   forge   a function other than the start whose body forges a
;           reference with `cast` and whose declaration does not say
;           `effect(unsafe)` - the one `Unsafe` the fixpoint cannot
;           see, because a cast forges by a type the walk runs before
;           (`unsafeLatePass`).
(pub :: graphSeeds (-> TC FnEnt String Int Int))
(pub fn (graphSeeds tc ent kind depth)
  (if (strEq kind "extern")
    (if (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 2)
      1
      0)
    ; The two operations that wait (`checkIsrWaits`): a system call, and
    ; `wfi`. Both are primitives reached by an edge of their own.
    (if (strEq kind "syscall")
      (isSyscallPrim (fnEntName ent))
      (if (strEq kind "wfi")
        (if (strEq (fnEntName ent) "__arm_wfi")
          1
          0)
        (if (isValueCastForm kind)
          (if (> depth 0)
            (graphDeclCasts tc ent)
            0)
          (if (strEq kind "forge")
            (if (&& (> depth 0) (== (fnEntUnsafe ent) 0))
              (if (== (fnEntryIn (tcForgeDecls tc) ent 0) 1)
                1
                0)
              0)
            (if (&& (strEq kind "IO")
                  (|| (== (isSyscallPrim (fnEntName ent)) 1) (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 2)))
              1
              (if (&& (strEq kind "Unsafe")
                    (|| (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 2) (== (isUnsafePrim (fnEntName ent)) 1)))
                1
                (if (&& (== (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 1)
                      (== (effIn (fnEntEffects ent) (graphEffKey kind)) 1))
                  1
                  0)))))))))

; `Unsafe` does not travel through a trusted encapsulation
; (`effUnionCallee`), so a walk looking for where it enters does not go
; into one either: `vecPush`'s own raw store is not its caller's.
(pub :: graphStopsAtTrusted (-> String Int))
(pub fn (graphStopsAtTrusted kind)
  (if (|| (strEq kind "Unsafe") (strEq kind "forge"))
    1
    0))

; A function whose row carries the effect while NO callee seeds or
; carries it: the effect came from a form in its own body - a
; `handle` installing a handler - which the row cannot name
; and the graph cannot reach past. Only an effect kind has this shape;
; an extern is reached or it is not, and a cast is a seed by
; definition.
(pub :: graphOwnSource (-> TC FnEnt String Int))
(pub fn (graphOwnSource tc ent kind)
  (if (|| (strEq kind "extern")
        (|| (isValueCastForm kind)
          (|| (strEq kind "forge") (|| (strEq kind "syscall") (strEq kind "wfi")))))
    0
    (if (!= (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0)
      0
      (if (!= (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0)
        0
        (if (== (effIn (fnEntEffects ent) (graphEffKey kind)) 0)
          0
          (if (== (graphAnyCalleeCarries tc (fnEntCalls ent) 0 kind) 1)
            0
            1))))))

; AN EFFECT OPERATION IS NOT A CALLEE THAT CARRIES THE EFFECT - it is
; the effect ENTERING, which is the distinction this whole predicate
; exists to draw. `tcAddEffectOp` gives an operation an FnEnt with word
; 4 = 1 and its own effect already seeded in word 5, so `(log "x")`
; records a `#calls=log` edge to a stub with `c:Console` in its row and
; no body behind it. Counted as a callee, that stub made every function
; performing an operation NOT the source of it, the walk ran off the
; end of the graph, and `graphWitness` answered 0 for every custom
; effect - measured: `main -> mid -> deep` came back as no path at all.
;
; The three kinds that predate this are unaffected either way: an
; operation's seeded row holds `c:E` and never `b:IO` or `b:Alloc`, and
; `graphSeeds` answers 0 for it under every kind, so before this guard
; an operation was already counted by neither arm for IO, Alloc,
; `extern` or `cast`. It changes exactly the custom case.
(pub :: graphAnyCalleeCarries (-> TC Names Int String Int))
(pub fn (graphAnyCalleeCarries tc calls i kind)
  (if (>= i (vecLen calls))
    0
    {
      (let cFound (fnEntExact tc (vecGetStr calls i)))
      (if (let (Some c) cFound)
        (if (|| (== c.isEffectOp 1) (&& (== (graphStopsAtTrusted kind) 1) (== c.unsafe 1)))
          (graphAnyCalleeCarries tc calls (+ i 1) kind)
          (if (== (graphSeeds tc c kind 1) 1)
            1
            (if (== (effIn c.effects (graphEffKey kind)) 1)
              1
              (graphAnyCalleeCarries tc calls (+ i 1) kind))))
        (graphAnyCalleeCarries tc calls (+ i 1) kind))
    }))

; Enqueue every callee of `calls` not yet seen, each with `from` as
; its parent slot. `fnEntExact` and not `findFnEnt`: an edge names the
; RESOLVED entry (`Mod$name`), which is the exact-name index's key,
; and visibility was decided when the edge was recorded.
(pub :: graphEnqueue (-> TC Names Int (Vec FnEnt) (Vec (Option Int)) Strings Int Int Int))
(pub fn (graphEnqueue tc calls i queue parent seen from skipTrusted)
  (if (>= i (vecLen calls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let name (vecGetStr calls i))
        (if (! (== (strInVec seen name 0) 1))
          {
            (vecPush seen name)
            {
              (let eFound (fnEntExact tc name))
              (if (let (Some e) eFound)
                (if (! (&& (== skipTrusted 1) (== (fnEntUnsafe e) 1)))
                  {
                    (vecPush queue e)
                    (vecPush parent (Some from))
                  }
                  ())
                ())
            }
          })
      }
      (graphEnqueue tc calls (+ i 1) queue parent seen from skipTrusted)
    }))

; The path from the start to slot `i`, start first: recurse to the
; parent, then push.
(pub :: graphReadBack (-> (Vec FnEnt) (Vec (Option Int)) (Option Int) (Vec FnEnt) (Vec FnEnt)))
(pub fn (graphReadBack queue parent i acc)
  (if (let (Some index) i)
    {
      (graphReadBack queue parent (vecGet parent index) acc)
      (vecPush acc (vecGet queue index))
      acc
    }
    acc))

; `a -> b -> c`, in the resolved spellings the edges carry, so a path
; cross-checks `#calls=` hop for hop.
(pub :: graphRender (-> (Vec FnEnt) String))
(pub fn (graphRender path)
  (graphRenderFrom path 0 ""))

(pub :: graphRenderFrom (-> (Vec FnEnt) Int String String))
(pub fn (graphRenderFrom path i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen path))
    acc
    (graphRenderFrom path (+ i 1)
      (if (== i 0)
        (graphShownName (fnEntName (vecGet path i)))
        (concat (concat acc " -> ") (graphShownName (fnEntName (vecGet path i))))))))

; A name as a message shows it: `__asm.N` is the `asm` form the author
; wrote, since no source can spell the primitive (MM-FFI-9).
(pub :: graphShownName (-> String String))
(pub fn (graphShownName n)
  (if (== (isAsmPrim n) 1)
    "asm"
    n))

; Does the declaration behind `ent` hold a `cast`? The entry is found
; back in the declaration list by its resolved name - a lowered impl
; method is a top-level `fn` by the time this runs, so
; `Trait#Type#method` is found like any other - and a builtin or an
; extern has no declaration and casts nothing.
(pub :: graphDeclCasts (-> TC FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (graphDeclCasts tc ent)
  (if (!= (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0)
    0
    (if (!= (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0)
      0
      {
        (let dd (declByName tc.allDecls (fnEntName ent) 0))
        (if (let (Some dd) dd)
          (if (> (vecLen (castScanInto (nodeCChild dd) vecNew)) 0)
            1
            0)
          0)
      })))

(pub :: declByName (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (declByName decls name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    None
    {
      (let dd (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag dd) TAG_D_FN) (strEq (nodeAName dd) name))
        (Some dd)
        (declByName decls name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; `no-cast`: every `cast` head in this body, each reported at its own
; span. `castScanInto` collects them; the arms mirror `walkEffects`
; form for form, because a walk that skips a form skips every cast
; under it; the literal leaves and a bare name hold nothing. The head
; test is the checker's own (`headIsVar` and the spelling `cast`), so
; what this refuses is exactly what `checkCastForm` checks. An
; application's span is its head's (`parser.ax`, `TAG_E_APP`), so the
; span reported covers the word `cast` and the message leads with it.
(pub :: restrictNoCast (-> TC ASTNode String ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (restrictNoCast tc d r e)
  (restrictEmitCasts tc d r (castScanInto e vecNew) 0))

(pub :: restrictEmitCasts (-> TC ASTNode String (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (restrictEmitCasts tc d r casts i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen casts))
    0
    {
      (emitRestrictCast tc d r (nodeSpan (vecGet casts i)))
      (restrictEmitCasts tc d r casts (+ i 1))
    }))

; `no-cast:deep`: this body's casts at their spans, exactly as
; `no-cast`; then the closure, for the NEAREST reachable function
; whose body casts, reported once at the declaration with the path -
; a span in another unit cannot be rendered against this one's
; source, and one path is what an author needs to decide whether the
; tag or the callee is wrong. A cast found is a refutation whatever
; the walk could not close; only a closure with no cast in it and a
; call the walk could not resolve is unverifiable, and a cast reached
; only through a function the body names without calling (`effByRef`)
; is unverifiable for the reason `no-foreign` gives.
(pub :: restrictNoCastDeep (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Int))
(pub fn (restrictNoCastDeep tc d r eff)
  {
    (let local (castScanInto (nodeCChild d) vecNew))
    {
      (restrictEmitCasts tc d r local 0)
      {
        (let lambdaFact
          (if (let (Some entry) (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
            entry.lambdaCast
            NoLambdaCast))
        (if (&& (== (vecLen local) 0) (lambdaCastAny lambdaFact))
          (if (lambdaCastDefinite lambdaFact)
            (emitRestrictViolated tc d r "the body calls a lambda containing a cast" vecNew)
            (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
              "a reachable function value may call a lambda containing a cast" vecNew))
          {
            (let pathFound (graphWitness tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d) "cast"))
            (if (let (Some path) pathFound)
              {
                (let hit (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)))
                (if (== (effAnyPossibleOnly eff) 1)
                  (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
                    (concat
                      (concat (concat "the closure reaches a cast in `" (bareOf (fnEntName hit)))
                        "` through a function the body names without calling, so the edge is not evidence of a call: ")
                      (graphRender path))
                    path)
                  (emitRestrictViolated tc d r
                    (concat
                      (concat
                        (concat "the closure reaches a cast in the body of `"
                          (bareOf (fnEntName hit)))
                        "`: ")
                      (graphRender path))
                    path))
              }
              (if (&& (== (vecLen local) 0) (== (effGraphUnresolved eff) 1))
                (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
                  (if (effGraphOnly eff)
                    "the body calls through a field that declares its effects, and the compiler could not list the functions the field holds, so the call graph is incomplete there"
                    "the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve, so its closure cannot be enumerated")
                  vecNew)
                (if (== (vecLen local) 0)
                  {
                    (let mayPathFound (graphWitnessMay tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d) "cast"))
                    (if (let (Some mayPath) mayPathFound)
                      (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
                        (concat
                          (concat
                            (concat "the closure may reach a cast in `"
                              (bareOf (fnEntName (vecGet mayPath (- (vecLen mayPath) 1)))))
                            "` through a call through a field or a local that holds one of several functions: ")
                          (graphRender mayPath))
                        mayPath)
                      0)
                  }
                  0)))
          })
      }
    }
  })

; Every `cast` application under `e`, in source order, pushed into
; `acc`; answers `acc`.
(pub :: castScanInto (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (castScanInto e acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    acc
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      {
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (let head (spineHead e))
            {
              (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
                (if (isCastOperation (nodeAName head))
                  (vecPush acc e))
                (castScanInto head acc))
              (castScanVec (spineArgVec e vecNew) 0 acc)
            }
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
            {
              (castScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
              (castScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
              {
                (castScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                (castScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
              }
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                {
                  (castScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (castScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                  (castScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                    {
                      (castScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                      (castScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                      (castScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                    }
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                      (castScanVec (nodeAChildren e) 0 acc)
                      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                          (castScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                          {
                            (castScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            (castScanVec (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                          })
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                          (castScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            (castScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds
                            ; none, so no arm scans one for casts.
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                              {
                                (castScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                                (castScanArms (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                              })))))))))))
        acc
      }
    }))

; `castScanInto` for effect, answering 0 - the arms above sequence
; several of these in a block.
(pub :: castScanIn (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (castScanIn e acc)
  {
    (castScanInto e acc)
    0
  })

(pub :: castScanVec (-> Nodes Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (castScanVec v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (castScanIn (vecGet v i) acc)
      (castScanVec v (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

(pub :: castScanArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (castScanArms arms i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      {
        (castScanIn (nodeBChild a) acc)
        (castScanArms arms (+ i 1) acc)
      }
    }))

; Every raw-memory-primitive application under `e`, in source order,
; pushed into `acc`; answers `acc`. `castScanInto`'s twin, predicate
; swapped from "head named `cast`" to "head named a raw-memory
; primitive" (`isUnsafePrim`), collecting the sites AX3073 refuses -
; the row is the wrong input for that question, because the row is
; transitive and the obligation is lexical. Duplicated rather than
; shared for the reason `wrapScanInto` gives: this file already
; hand-duplicates a full tree walk per collected fact.
(pub :: unsafeScanInto (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (unsafeScanInto e acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    acc
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      {
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (let head (spineHead e))
            {
              (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
                (if (== (isUnsafePrim (nodeAName head)) 1)
                  (vecPush acc e))
                (unsafeScanInto head acc))
              (unsafeScanVec (spineArgVec e vecNew) 0 acc)
            }
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
            {
              (unsafeScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
              (unsafeScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
              {
                (unsafeScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                (unsafeScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
              }
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                {
                  (unsafeScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (unsafeScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                  (unsafeScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                    {
                      (unsafeScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                      (unsafeScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                      (unsafeScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                    }
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                      (unsafeScanVec (nodeAChildren e) 0 acc)
                      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                          (unsafeScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                          {
                            (unsafeScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            (unsafeScanVec (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                          })
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                          (unsafeScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            (unsafeScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds
                            ; none, so no arm scans one for raw-memory
                            ; calls.
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                              {
                                (unsafeScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                                (unsafeScanArms (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                              }
                              ; A nullary primitive is read by naming
                              ; it, `(== __resource_unwinding 1)`:
                              ; the name is the operation, as the
                              ; head of an application is.
                              (if (&& (== tag TAG_E_VAR) (== (isNullaryUnsafePrim (nodeAName e)) 1))
                                (vecPush acc e)))))))))))))
        acc
      }
    }))

; `unsafeScanInto` for effect, answering 0 - the arms above sequence
; several of these in a block.
(pub :: unsafeScanIn (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (unsafeScanIn e acc)
  {
    (unsafeScanInto e acc)
    0
  })

(pub :: unsafeScanVec (-> Nodes Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (unsafeScanVec v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (unsafeScanIn (vecGet v i) acc)
      (unsafeScanVec v (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

(pub :: unsafeScanArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (unsafeScanArms arms i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      {
        (unsafeScanIn (nodeBChild a) acc)
        (unsafeScanArms arms (+ i 1) acc)
      }
    }))

; `no-wrap`: every `+`, `-` or `*` head in this body, each reported at
; its own span - `wrapScanInto` is `castScanInto`'s twin, predicate
; swapped from "head named `cast`" to "head named a wrapping
; operator" (`isWrapOp`). Duplicated rather than shared: this file
; already hand-duplicates a full tree walk per collected fact
; (`castScanInto`'s own comment: its arms "mirror `walkEffects` form
; for form"), and a generic walker would be a new abstraction the
; file does not otherwise use for one restriction.
;
; LOCAL, for the same reason `no-cast` is: `+`/`-`/`*` lower to plain
; `add`/`sub`/`mul` with no `nsw` (measured: `grep -c 'with\.overflow'
; core/codegen.ax` is 0), so a wraparound is an act THIS BODY
; performs by writing the bare operator, not a fact the effect row or
; call graph carries. `docs/reference.md` defines `no-wrap`; `stdlib/Err.ax`'s `addChecked`/`subChecked`/
; `mulChecked` are the sanctioned alternative, and calling one pulls
; `Alloc` into this body's row (constructing the `Result` allocates),
; which is why `no-wrap` and `no-alloc`, or `no-wrap` and `pure`, are
; not satisfiable together for a body that needs arithmetic - stated
; in `emitRestrictWrap`'s help rather than left to be discovered.
(pub :: isWrapOp (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isWrapOp n)
  (if (strEq n "+")
    1
    (if (strEq n "-")
      1
      (if (|| (strEq n "*") (strEq n "<<"))
        1
        0))))

(pub :: restrictNoWrap (-> TC ASTNode String ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (restrictNoWrap tc d r e)
  (restrictEmitWraps tc d r (wrapScanInto e vecNew) 0))

(pub :: restrictEmitWraps (-> TC ASTNode String (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (restrictEmitWraps tc d r wraps i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen wraps))
    0
    {
      (let w (vecGet wraps i))
      {
        ; THE SPELLING IS NOT THE OPERATION. `wrapScanInto` matches
        ; `+`, `-` and `*` by name, and on `Float` operands those
        ; three lower to `fadd`/`fsub`/`fmul`, which do not wrap -
        ; `checkNumeric` recorded which heads those were while it had
        ; the operand types (word 37), and this is the only reader.
        ; Refusing one was a violation the program could not commit,
        ; carrying a fix (`addChecked`, `(-> Int Int (Result Int
        ; Error))`) that does not typecheck against a `Float`.
        (if (! (== (tcFloatOpIn tc (spineHead w)) 1))
          (if (> (nodeIntegerOp w) 0)
            (emitRestrictWidth tc d r w false)
            (emitRestrictWrap tc d r (nodeSpan w) (nodeAName (spineHead w)))))
        (restrictEmitWraps tc d r wraps (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; The `for` keyword's own counter bump, and nothing else.
;
; `for` is DESUGARED IN THE PARSER (`forWhileBody`) into
; `(set for$i (+ for$i 1))` beneath a `(< for$i for$n)` guard, and
; every generated node carries the KEYWORD's span. Under `no-wrap`
; that produced `` `+` in the body of `f` `` underlining the word
; `for` - an operator the body does not contain, at a span that spells
; something else, with no fix available: a loop counter cannot be a
; `Result`. The keyword landed 2026-09-03, three days after `no-wrap`,
; and `docs/reference.md`'s phase 1 could not have
; seen it.
;
; Skipping it is SOUND and not merely convenient: the body is entered
; only while `for$i < for$n` and both are `Int`, so `for$i + 1` is at
; most `INT_MAX` and the increment cannot overflow. The claim
; `no-wrap` makes is kept.
;
; The shape is the parser's alone. `$` is `AX1001 unexpected
; character` inside an identifier (measured), so `for$i` is a name no
; author can write and no author's `(set i (+ i 1))` can be mistaken
; for - a hand-rolled `while` counter is still refused, which is what
; `394-restrict-no-wrap-exempt.ax`'s `handRolled` pins. Matched
; whole - target, head, both operands - so that a desugar which drifts
; stops matching and the fixture goes red rather than quiet.
(pub :: isForBump (-> ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (isForBump e)
  (if (strEq (nodeAName e) "for$i")
    {
      (let v (nodeBChild e))
      (if (!= (nodeTag v) TAG_E_APP)
        0
        {
          (let h (spineHead v))
          (if (!= (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
            0
            (if (strEq (nodeAName h) "+")
              {
                (let as (spineArgVec v vecNew))
                (if (!= (vecLen as) 2)
                  0
                  {
                    (let lhs (vecGet as 0))
                    (let rhs (vecGet as 1))
                    (if (!= (nodeTag lhs) TAG_E_VAR)
                      0
                      (if (strEq (nodeAName lhs) "for$i")
                        (if (|| (!= (nodeTag rhs) TAG_E_INT) (!= (nodeA rhs) 1))
                          0
                          1)
                        0))
                  })
              }
              0))
        })
    }
    0))

(pub :: wrapScanInto (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (wrapScanInto e acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    acc
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      {
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (let head (spineHead e))
            {
              (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
                (if (== (isWrapOp (nodeAName head)) 1)
                  (vecPush acc e))
                (wrapScanInto head acc))
              (wrapScanVec (spineArgVec e vecNew) 0 acc)
            }
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
            {
              (wrapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
              (wrapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
              {
                (wrapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                (wrapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
              }
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                {
                  (wrapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (wrapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                  (wrapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                    {
                      (wrapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                      (wrapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                      (wrapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                    }
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                      (wrapScanVec (nodeAChildren e) 0 acc)
                      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                          (wrapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                          {
                            (wrapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            (wrapScanVec (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                          })
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                          ; The one arm that does not walk what it
                          ; is given: `isForBump` is the `for`
                          ; keyword's own counter increment, which
                          ; the author did not write and which
                          ; cannot overflow. Everything else the
                          ; loop contains - the guard, the user's
                          ; binder, the body - is reached through
                          ; the `while` arm above and is refused
                          ; normally.
                          (if (! (== (isForBump e) 1))
                            (wrapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc))
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            (wrapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds
                            ; none, so no arm scans one for wraps.
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                              {
                                (wrapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                                (wrapScanArms (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                              })))))))))))
        acc
      }
    }))

; `wrapScanInto` for effect, answering 0 - the arms above sequence
; several of these in a block.
(pub :: wrapScanIn (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (wrapScanIn e acc)
  {
    (wrapScanInto e acc)
    0
  })

(pub :: wrapScanVec (-> Nodes Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (wrapScanVec v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (wrapScanIn (vecGet v i) acc)
      (wrapScanVec v (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

(pub :: wrapScanArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (wrapScanArms arms i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      {
        (wrapScanIn (nodeBChild a) acc)
        (wrapScanArms arms (+ i 1) acc)
      }
    }))

; `no-trap`: every `/`, `%`, `<<` or `>>` head in this body,
; each reported at its own span - `trapScanInto` is
; `wrapScanInto`'s twin, predicate swapped from "head named a
; wrapping operator" to "head named a trapping operator"
; (`isTrapOp`). Duplicated rather than shared, for the reason
; `wrapScanInto` gives: this file already hand-duplicates a full tree
; walk per collected fact rather than sharing one generic walker.
;
; LOCAL, for the same reason `no-cast` and `no-wrap` are: `/`/`%`
; trap on a zero divisor (exit 72) and on `INT_MIN / -1` (exit 83),
; and `<<`/`>>` trap on an out-of-range shift amount (exit 84) -
; so the trapping use is an act THIS BODY performs by writing the
; bare operator, not a fact the effect row or call graph carries.
; `docs/reference.md` names this the follow-up its
; phase 1 deliberately left out; `stdlib/Err.ax`'s
; `divChecked`/`remChecked`/`shlChecked`/`shrChecked` are the
; sanctioned alternative, and calling one pulls `Alloc` into this
; body's row (constructing the `Result` allocates), which is why
; `no-trap` and `no-alloc`, or `no-trap` and `pure`, are
; not satisfiable together for a body that needs these operations -
; stated in `emitRestrictTrap`'s help rather than left to be
; discovered. There is no `for`-bump exemption to carry: `for`
; desugars to `+` and `<` only, so no generated node wears one of
; these four spellings.
(pub :: isTrapOp (-> String Int))
(pub fn (isTrapOp n)
  (if (strEq n "/")
    1
    (if (strEq n "%")
      1
      (if (strEq n "<<")
        1
        (if (strEq n ">>")
          1
          0)))))

(pub :: restrictNoTrap (-> TC ASTNode String ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (restrictNoTrap tc d r e)
  (restrictEmitTraps tc d r (trapScanInto e vecNew) 0))

(pub :: restrictEmitTraps (-> TC ASTNode String (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (restrictEmitTraps tc d r found i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen found))
    0
    {
      (let w (vecGet found i))
      {
        ; THE SPELLING IS NOT THE OPERATION. `trapScanInto`
        ; matches `/`, `%`, `<<` and `>>` by name, and on `Float`
        ; operands `/` lowers to `fdiv`, which has no integer trap -
        ; `checkNumeric` recorded which heads those were while it had
        ; the operand types (word 37), and this is the second reader.
        ; Refusing one was a violation the program could not commit,
        ; carrying a fix (`divChecked`, `(-> Int Int (Result Int
        ; Error))`) that does not typecheck against a `Float`.
        (if (! (== (tcFloatOpIn tc (spineHead w)) 1))
          (if (> (nodeIntegerOp w) 0)
            (emitRestrictWidth tc d r w true)
            (emitRestrictTrap tc d r (nodeSpan w) (nodeAName (spineHead w)))))
        (restrictEmitTraps tc d r found (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

(pub :: trapScanInto (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (trapScanInto e acc)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    acc
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      {
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (let head (spineHead e))
            {
              (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
                (if (== (isTrapOp (nodeAName head)) 1)
                  (vecPush acc e))
                (trapScanInto head acc))
              (trapScanVec (spineArgVec e vecNew) 0 acc)
            }
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
            {
              (trapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
              (trapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
              {
                (trapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                (trapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
              }
              (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
                {
                  (trapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (trapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                  (trapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                    {
                      (trapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                      (trapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                      (trapScanIn (nodeCChild e) acc)
                    }
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                      (trapScanVec (nodeAChildren e) 0 acc)
                      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_WHILE) (== tag TAG_E_REGION))
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                          (trapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                          {
                            (trapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            (trapScanVec (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                          })
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                          (trapScanIn (nodeBChild e) acc)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            (trapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                            ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds
                            ; none, so no arm scans one for traps.
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                              {
                                (trapScanIn (nodeAChild e) acc)
                                (trapScanArms (nodeBChildren e) 0 acc)
                              })))))))))))
        acc
      }
    }))

; `trapScanInto` for effect, answering 0 - the arms above
; sequence several of these in a block.
(pub :: trapScanIn (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (trapScanIn e acc)
  {
    (trapScanInto e acc)
    0
  })

(pub :: trapScanVec (-> Nodes Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (trapScanVec v i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (trapScanIn (vecGet v i) acc)
      (trapScanVec v (+ i 1) acc)
    }))

(pub :: trapScanArms (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (trapScanArms arms i acc)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    0
    {
      (let a (vecGet arms i))
      {
        (trapScanIn (nodeBChild a) acc)
        (trapScanArms arms (+ i 1) acc)
      }
    }))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE CALL CHAIN AS RELATED LOCATIONS.
;
; `AX3049`'s message has named its path since the graph walk landed -
; `parseConfig -> readSection -> IO$writeStr -> ...` - and named it in
; PROSE, inside the quoted string, where nothing but a reader could
; use it. These three functions turn the same vector into the
; diagnostic's `secs`, which is what makes all three surfaces carry it
; at once: AXDL's `^` field, the human renderer's note or underline,
; and the JSON `related` array.
;
; ONE FIELD PER HOP AFTER THE FIRST, and no exceptions - that is the
; rule `scripts/check-diagnostics.sh` checks the corpus against, and
; it is exact only because the two awkward cases below are spelled
; rather than skipped:
;
;   * The FIRST hop is this declaration. Its span is already the
;     diagnostic's primary span, so a field for it would be a second
;     underline in the same place.
;   * A hop with NO DECLARATION - a builtin like `__syscall3`, an
;     `extern` item, an effect operation - gets a field with no
;     location, `^-:"name"`. It is a hop, it is in the message, and
;     omitting it would leave a field list that looks like the whole
;     chain and is not. That failure mode is the one this whole
;     change exists to answer, so it is not repeated one level down.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: restrictPathSecs (-> TC (Vec FnEnt) Int diag::Diag diag::Diag))
(pub fn (restrictPathSecs tc path i dg)
  (if (== (vecLen path) 0)
    dg
    (if (>= i (vecLen path))
      dg
      {
        (restrictHopSec tc dg (vecGet path i))
        (restrictPathSecs tc path (+ i 1) dg)
      })))

; One hop's related location. The message is the hop's RESOLVED name,
; the spelling `graphRender` puts in the chain and `symbols --calls`
; puts in its rows - three places, one spelling, so the field and the
; prose can be compared hop for hop by a gate that knows neither. The
; one exception is an `asm` form's primitive, which the prose and this
; field both show as `asm` (`graphShownName`) and `symbols --calls`
; keeps as `__asm.N`, since a program may name a function `asm`.
(pub :: restrictHopSec (-> TC diag::Diag FnEnt diag::Diag))
(pub fn (restrictHopSec tc dg ent)
  {
    (let dd (restrictHopDecl tc ent))
    (if (let (Some dd) dd)
      (diagAddSecondaryIn dg (nodeSpan dd) (graphShownName (fnEntName ent))
        (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) (nodeModuleOpt dd)))
      (diagAddSecondaryIn dg 0 (graphShownName (fnEntName ent)) 0))
  })

; The declaration behind an entry, or None. `graphDeclCasts` asks the
; same question the same way and for the same reason - a builtin
; (word 3) and an effect operation (word 4) have no declaration node,
; and neither does an `extern` item, which is why the name lookup can
; still come back empty after both guards pass. Word 10 is `allDecls`,
; every unit's declarations, so a hop in `stdlib/` is found here as
; readily as one in the entry file; word 13 is the unit table the
; caller turns `nodeModule` into an index against.
(pub :: restrictHopDecl (-> TC FnEnt (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (restrictHopDecl tc ent)
  (if (!= (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0)
    None
    (if (!= (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0)
      None
      (declByName tc.allDecls (fnEntName ent) 0))))

; AX3049 at the DECLARATION, for the transitive three: the violation
; may be anywhere below this body, and the declaration is where the
; claim was written.
; `path` is the call chain the message names, or 0 when it names none.
; Every hop after the FIRST becomes a related location - the first is
; this declaration, whose span is already the diagnostic's primary
; one, so a `^` field for it would underline the caret a second time
; and say nothing. `restrictPathSecs` is where that rule lives.
(pub :: emitRestrictViolated (-> TC ASTNode String String (Vec FnEnt) Int))
(pub fn (emitRestrictViolated tc d r why path)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (restrictPathSecs tc path 1
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3049" "restriction-violated" (nodeSpan d)
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` claims `restrict(") r)
            (concat ")` and " why))
          None
          (Some
            "a restriction is a CLAIM the author wrote, and this is the compiler answering it. `no-io`, `no-alloc`, `no-unsafe`, `no-foreign`, `no-cast:deep` and `no-recursion` are transitive - the effect row and the call graph are fixpoints over every callee - and the path in the message is the chain of resolved calls (`symbols --calls` spells each hop the same way) from this declaration to where the effect enters, or around the cycle, so the fix is at a named place rather than somewhere below. Make the body keep the claim, or delete the tag, which WITHDRAWS the claim rather than answering it; an unrestricted function is never asked"))))))

; AX3049 at the CAST, for the local one: it has a span, and the fix
; is where the span is.
(pub :: emitRestrictCast (-> TC ASTNode String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitRestrictCast tc d r span)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3049" "restriction-violated" span
        (concat
          (concat
            (concat (concat "`as` in the body of `" (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
              "`, which claims `restrict(")
            r)
          ")`")
        None
        (Some
          "`no-cast` rejects explicit conversions in this body. Use an operation that preserves the required type, or remove the restriction. `no-cast:deep` also follows resolved callees; an unresolved path cannot establish the claim.")))))

(pub :: emitRestrictWidth (-> TC ASTNode String ASTNode Bool Int))
(pub fn (emitRestrictWidth tc d restriction expression trapping)
  (emitDiag tc
    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3049" "restriction-violated" (nodeSpan expression)
      (concat "`"
        (concat (nodeAName (spineHead expression))
          (concat "` in the body of `"
            (concat (bareOf (nodeAName d))
              (concat "`, which claims `restrict(" (concat restriction ")`"))))))
      None
      (Some
        (if trapping
          "Fixed-width division and remainder trap on zero; signed minimum divided by -1 also traps. Shifts trap when the count is negative or reaches the type's width. Remove this restriction when these operations are intended."
          "Fixed-width addition, subtraction, multiplication and left shifts wrap at the declared width. Use explicit widening and checked Int arithmetic when the range fits Int, or remove this restriction when wrapping is intended.")))))

; AX3049 at the OPERATOR, for `no-wrap`, LOCAL like `no-cast`: it has
; a span, and the fix is where the span is. Unlike `no-cast` there are
; three possible fixes rather than one, so the message names which
; operator was found (`op`, from `wrapScanInto`'s recorded head).
(pub :: emitRestrictWrap (-> TC ASTNode String Int String Int))
(pub fn (emitRestrictWrap tc d r span op)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3049" "restriction-violated" span
        (concat
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" op) "` in the body of `") (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
              "`, which claims `restrict(")
            r)
          ")`")
        None
        (Some
          "Left shifts can discard high bits and are refused by `no-wrap`. `no-wrap` is LOCAL, like `no-cast`: on `Int` operands `+`, `-` and `*` lower to plain `add`/`sub`/`mul` with no overflow check, so a silent wraparound is an act this body performs by writing the raw operator, not a fact the effect row or call graph carries - it is refused where it is written, and a callee's arithmetic is the callee's claim to make. `stdlib/Err.ax` has the checked alternative for each: `addChecked`, `subChecked`, `mulChecked`, every one `(-> Int Int (Result Int Error))`. Calling one pulls `Alloc` into this body's effect row (constructing the `Result` allocates), so `no-wrap` cannot be satisfied together with `no-alloc` or `pure` by a body that needs arithmetic - write the checked call and match its `Err`, accept the row, or delete the tag. TWO OPERATORS ARE NOT REFUSED, because neither can wrap: the same three spellings on `Float` operands, which lower to `fadd`/`fsub`/`fmul`, and the `for` keyword's own counter increment, which the parser writes and whose loop guard bounds it")))))

; AX3049 at the OPERATOR, for `no-trap`, LOCAL like `no-cast`
; and `no-wrap`: it has a span, and the fix is where the span is.
; Unlike `no-cast` there are four possible fixes rather than one, so
; the message names which operator was found (`op`, from
; `trapScanInto`'s recorded head).
(pub :: emitRestrictTrap (-> TC ASTNode String Int String Int))
(pub fn (emitRestrictTrap tc d r span op)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3049" "restriction-violated" span
        (concat
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" op) "` in the body of `") (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
              "`, which claims `restrict(")
            r)
          ")`")
        None
        (Some
          "`no-trap` is LOCAL, like `no-cast` and `no-wrap`: on `Int` operands `/` and `%` trap on a zero divisor (exit 72) and on the `INT_MIN / -1` corner (exit 83), and `<<` and `>>` trap on an out-of-range shift amount (exit 84) - so the trapping use is an act this body performs by writing the raw operator, not a fact the effect row or call graph carries. `stdlib/Err.ax` has the checked alternative for each: `divChecked`, `remChecked`, `shlChecked`, `shrChecked`, every one `(-> Int Int (Result Int Error))`. Calling one pulls `Alloc` into this body's effect row (constructing the `Result` allocates), so `no-trap` cannot be satisfied together with `no-alloc` or `pure` by a body that needs these operations - write the checked call and match its `Err`, accept the row, or delete the tag. ONE SPELLING IS NOT REFUSED: `/` on `Float` operands, which lowers to `fdiv` with no integer trap to require, and whose checked fix is `(-> Int Int (Result Int Error))` and does not typecheck against a `Float`")))))

; AX3051: unverifiable, a warning - the claim is neither refuted nor
; acquitted, and the reason is the analysis's rather than the body's.
(pub :: emitRestrictUnverifiable (-> TC ASTNode String String (Vec FnEnt) Int))
(pub fn (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r why path)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    ; UNDER `strict`, AN UNPROVEN CLAIM IS A FAILED ONE. The default
    ; stays a warning for the reason `severity.policy` records - a body
    ; dispatching through a stored function is a correct program the
    ; walk cannot follow, and refusing it would make that unwriteable
    ; under any restriction. `strict` is the author saying the opposite
    ; for THIS declaration: on a sensitive operation, "the compiler
    ; could not tell" is not the guarantee that was asked for, and a
    ; guarantee that might not hold is worth less than no claim at all.
    (if (tcRestrictionsStrict tc)
      (emitDiag tc
        (restrictPathSecs tc path 1
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3057" "restriction-unproven" (nodeSpan d)
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` claims `restrict(") r)
              (concat ", strict)` and the claim cannot be PROVEN: " why))
            None
            (Some
              "`strict` asks for a guarantee and not a best effort: where the walk cannot settle the claim, it is refused rather than warned. The four readings are AX3051's - an effect present only as POSSIBLE, a row the walk marked incomplete, a body that CALLS one of its own parameters (`#effect-params=`), whose answer is chosen by each caller, and a region fixpoint that hit its round limit. Name the function at the call site, stop calling the parameter, shorten the chain, or drop `strict` to keep the claim as a checked best effort (AX3051)"))))
      (emitDiag tc
        (restrictPathSecs tc path 1
          (mkDiag SEV_WARNING "AX3051" "restriction-unverifiable" (nodeSpan d)
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` claims `restrict(") r)
              (concat ")` and the claim cannot be checked: " why))
            None
            (Some
              "a limit of the ANALYSIS rather than a defect in the program - AX3037's argument at one remove. There are four readings: an effect present only as POSSIBLE, a row the walk marked incomplete, a body that CALLS one of its own parameters (`#effect-params=`), whose answer is chosen by each caller and cannot be settled here at all, and - for `no-escape` alone - a region fixpoint that hit its round limit before the facts settled, which is a depth limit rather than anything about this declaration. Name the function at the call, stop calling the parameter, shorten the chain, or drop the claim; `no-cast`, `no-wrap` and `no-trap` are checked regardless, because all three are lexical and need no call resolved")))))))

; AX3052: a name outside the closed list. The help states the list,
; and it is the one place the list is stated to the author - keep it
; in step with `checkOneRestrict`'s arms.
(pub :: emitRestrictUnknown (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitRestrictUnknown tc d r)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3052" "restriction-unknown" (nodeSpan d)
        (if (== (strLen r) 0)
          (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
            "` claims `restrict()`, which names no restriction")
          (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` claims `restrict(") r)
            (concat (concat ")`, and `" r) "` names no restriction")))
        None
        (Some
          "the restrictions this compiler checks are `no-cast`, `no-cast:deep`, `no-io`, `no-alloc`, `no-unsafe`, `no-foreign`, `no-recursion`, `no-wrap`, `no-trap`, `no-escape`, `no-entropy`, `no-spawn` and `no-block`, comma-separated inside one `restrict(...)`. The list is closed, so a name outside it is not metadata - it is a claim in the one place the compiler has said it checks, and it would otherwise read like a guarantee and buy silence")))))

; ==================================================================
; `;@axiom:pre(...)` and `;@axiom:post(...)`: AX3050, the static half.
;
; WHAT A CONTRACT IS, AND WHY IT IS BOTH HALVES. `restrict(...)` is a
; claim the compiler REFUSES statically because the analysis that
; answers it already exists - the effect row and the call graph are
; fixpoints this checker computes anyway. A contract is not that kind
; of claim. `(> n 0)` is a statement about a VALUE, and there is no
; value analysis in this compiler at all - measured, `grep -v '^ *;'
; FILE | grep -c 'constFold\|constantFold\|interval\|rangeOf\|
; abstractVal'` over `typecheck.ax`, `codegen.ax` and `expand.ax`
; answers 0, 0, 0. The comment lines are excluded because THIS
; sentence matches the pattern it quotes: without the exclusion the
; same grep answers 1, 1, 1 and all three hits are a note saying there
; are none - so
; the checker cannot decide it for a caller it has not seen, and a
; refusal it cannot justify is not available.
;
; This repository's rule is that a claim nothing checks is a comment,
; and it refuses those. So a contract is enforced at RUN TIME, which
; is Ada's answer, and `expLowerContracts` compiles the check into the
; body. It is not behind a flag: a check that is off by default is a
; comment by default, and the whole point of the AXTAG namespace's
; `pure`/`effect`/`restrict` neighbours is that writing one buys an
; answer rather than silence.
;
; WHAT IS STILL STATIC IS EVERYTHING ABOUT THE CONTRACT ITSELF, and
; that is what AX3050 is:
;
;   1. it must PARSE, as exactly one expression;
;   2. it must type as `Bool` against this declaration's signature,
;      with the parameters in scope;
;   3. `result` must name something: the declared result in a `post`,
;      and nothing at all in a `pre` or on a declaration with no
;      signature to declare one (`checkVar`, guarded by `inContract`);
;   4. it must PERFORM NOTHING. A contract is evaluated on every call,
;      so a contract that writes, allocates or performs IO changes the
;      program by being stated.
;
; ON (4) AND WHY `Alloc` IS EXPRESSIBLE HERE. The effect-enforcement
; design left `Alloc` AMBIENT - only `IO` is declarable, so "this
; function does not allocate" is not a claim an author can write. That
; is a fact about DECLARABILITY and not about the row: `Alloc` is in
; the effect row exactly as `IO` is, `restrict(no-alloc)` already reads
; it, and this check reads it the same way. So no part of the effect
; system has to move for a contract to be allocation-free.
;
; AND IT IS NOT A BLANKET REFUSAL, which is the measurement that
; matters. The vocabulary a predicate needs is allocation-free
; already - measured from `symbols --calls` over `core/main.ax`:
; `vecLen`, `vecGet`, `strLen`, `strEq`, `strByte` and `memGetWord`
; all render `#restrict=no-io,no-alloc,no-foreign` with `#effects=Unsafe`
; and nothing else (a read, which `effDropUnsafe` exempts),
; while `concat`, `fmtInt` and `vecNew` carry `Alloc,Mut`.
; A contract may compare, index, measure and test; it may not build.
;
; ONLY A DEFINITE EFFECT IS REFUSED. An effect that reaches the row
; only as POSSIBLE arrived because the expression NAMES a function
; without calling it, and naming one performs nothing - the same
; reading `restrictEffect` applies, and here the silence is correct
; rather than merely conservative. A row marked incomplete is a lower
; bound and says nothing either way, and AX3037's argument applies:
; refusing a program over a limit of the analysis is worse than not
; noticing.
; ==================================================================
; The `checkApp` arm for the lowered form. It checks the MESSAGE and
; not the condition, because `checkContracts` has already checked the
; condition once, in the same scope, against `Bool`, with the
; contract's own span and the contract's own diagnostic. Without this
; the condition would be walked twice and every diagnostic inside a
; contract would be reported twice.
(pub :: checkContractForm (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkContractForm tc e)
  {
    (checkExprH tc (nodeBChild e) 0)
    mkIntTy
  })

; Every contract on this declaration, checked in the body's scope.
; Called from `tcCheckFn` while the parameters are still bound and the
; declared result type is in hand - `checkAxtags` runs after the frame
; is truncated and has neither.
(pub :: checkContracts (-> TC ASTNode (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkContracts tc d resTy)
  {
    (let tags vecNew)
    {
      ; The signature's first, because it is written first - the order
      ; `checkRestricts` reads its own two sets in, and the order
      ; `expLowerContracts` lowers them in.
      (if (let (Some signature) (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName d)))
        (contractTagsInto tags signature)
        0)
      (contractTagsInto tags (nodeAxtagsOf d))
      (checkContractsFrom tc d tags 0 resTy)
    }
  })

(pub :: checkContractsFrom (-> TC ASTNode Axtags Int (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkContractsFrom tc d tags i resTy)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (checkOneContract tc d (vecGet tags i) resTy)
      (checkContractsFrom tc d tags (+ i 1) resTy)
    }))

(pub :: checkOneContract (-> TC ASTNode AxtagEnt (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkOneContract tc d t resTy)
  (if (let (Some p) (contractExprOf t))
    {
      (let mark (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
      (let _r
        (if (strEq (contractKeyOf t) "post")
          (if (let (Some resultType) resTy)
            (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) "result" 0 (contractSpanOf t) resultType)
            0)
          0))
      (let _i
        (tcSetContractTag tc
          (if (strEq (contractKeyOf t) "pre")
            (Some ContractPre)
            (Some ContractPost))))
      {
        (let ty (checkExprH tc p 0))
        {
          (tcSetContractTag tc None)
          (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) mark)
          (if (== (tyCompat tc mkBoolTy ty) 0)
            (emitContractMalformed tc t (contractSpanOf t)
              (concat
                (concat (concat (concat "is the `" (contractKeyOf t)) "` on `")
                  (concat (bareOf (nodeAName d)) "` and has type `"))
                (concat (tyRender ty) "`, not `Bool`"))))
          (checkContractPurity tc d t p resTy)
        }
      }
    }
    (emitContractMalformed tc t (contractSpanOf t)
      (if (== (strLen (contractTextOf t)) 0)
        "states a contract with no expression"
        "is not one expression, and a contract is exactly one"))))

; (4): the contract's OWN effect row, walked with `result` bound so
; that a `post` naming it is a local read and not a reference to some
; global of that name.
;
; It is bound as an extra PARAMETER, on a COPY of the parameter list -
; `bsNames` holds shadow RECORDS and not strings (`bsFindFrom` reads
; word 0 of each element), and `bsParams` is the declaration's own
; vector, which this must not grow. `bodyScopeOf` mints a fresh
; `BScope` per call, so replacing its word 1 with the copy and
; extending its callable list by one is local to this check.
(pub :: checkContractPurity (-> TC ASTNode AxtagEnt ASTNode (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkContractPurity tc d t p resTy)
  {
    (let sc (bodyScopeOf tc d))
    (let ps vecNew)
    {
      (copyInto ps (nodeBNames d) 0)
      (vecPushStr ps "result")
      (set sc.params ps)
      (vecPush (bsCallableVec sc)
        (match resTy
          ((Some resultType) (tyIsCallable resultType))
          ((None) 0)))
      {
        (let did (effDropUnsafe (effDefiniteOnly (collectEffects tc p sc))))
        (if (== (vecLen did) 0)
          0
          (emitContractMalformed tc t (contractSpanOf t)
            (concat
              (concat (concat (concat "is the `" (contractKeyOf t)) "` on `")
                (concat (bareOf (nodeAName d)) "` and performs "))
              (effJoin did))))
      }
    }
  })

; The row without `Unsafe`: what a contract is refused for. A contract
; may compare, index, measure and test, and that vocabulary - `vecLen`,
; `vecGet`, `strLen`, `strEq`, `strByte`, `memGetWord` - reads raw
; memory to do it, so every one of those carries definite `Unsafe` and
; refusing it would make a contract unwriteable. A read does not change
; the program by being stated; a write still draws this code through
; `Mut` (`memSetWord` carries both), an allocation through `Alloc`,
; and outside reach through `IO`.
(pub :: effDropUnsafe (-> Strings Strings))
(pub fn (effDropUnsafe v)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (effDropUnsafeInto out v 0)
      out
    }
  })

(pub :: effDropUnsafeInto (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effDropUnsafeInto out v i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      (if (! (strEq (vecGetStr v i) (builtinEff "Unsafe")))
        (vecPushStr out (vecGetStr v i)))
      (effDropUnsafeInto out v (+ i 1))
    }))

; The span a contract diagnostic points at: the EXPRESSION as written,
; or - when the tag holds no expression at all - the whole tag, so the
; refusal has somewhere to point and the first backticked text in the
; message is what the source spells there (`tests/lsp/drive.py`
; `check_range`).
;
; The 8 is `;` plus the seven bytes of `@axiom:`, which `scanAxtags`
; slices off the content it records - the same 8 `contractBaseOf`
; adds back. Spanning `off .. off+1+len` instead underlined `;@ax`
; and quoted `;pre`, which is a diagnostic pointing one place and
; naming another - AX3039's own recorded defect, one code over.
(pub :: contractSpanOf (-> AxtagEnt Int))
(pub fn (contractSpanOf t)
  {
    (let txt (contractTextOf t))
    (if (== (strLen txt) 0)
      (mkSpan t.off (+ t.off (+ 8 (strLen t.content))))
      (mkSpan (contractBaseOf t) (+ (contractBaseOf t) (strLen txt))))
  })

; The source text a contract diagnostic leads with, backticked: the
; expression, or the whole tag when there is none.
(pub :: contractLead (-> AxtagEnt String))
(pub fn (contractLead t)
  {
    (let txt (contractTextOf t))
    (if (== (strLen txt) 0)
      (concat ";@axiom:" t.content)
      txt)
  })

(pub :: emitContractMalformed (-> TC AxtagEnt Int String Int))
(pub fn (emitContractMalformed tc t span why)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3050" "contract-malformed" span
        (concat (concat (concat "`" (contractLead t)) "` ") why) None
        (Some
          "a `;@axiom:pre(...)` or `;@axiom:post(...)` is a claim the COMPILER cannot decide - there is no value analysis here - so it is compiled into the body and checked at run time, on every call, and a failure writes `axiom: precondition failed in `f`: ...` on fd 2 and exits 80. That is why the expression has to parse, has to be `Bool`, and has to PERFORM nothing: a contract that allocates or writes changes the program by being stated. Comparison, indexing, `strEq`, `strLen`, `vecLen` and `vecGet` are allocation-free and are what a predicate is written from; `concat`, `fmtInt` and `vecNew` are not. `result` names the declared result of a `post` and nothing anywhere else")))))

; AX3050 for `result` naming nothing, reported from `checkVar` so that
; the question is total - see the `inContract` field's own note.
(pub :: emitContractResult (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (emitContractResult tc e)
  (if (let (Some contract) (tcContractTag tc))
    {
      (let context contract)
      (if (== (nodeSpan e) 0)
        0
        (emitDiag tc
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3050" "contract-malformed" (nodeSpan e)
            (if (match context
              ((ContractPre) true)
              ((ContractPost) false))
              "`result` names nothing in a `pre`: a precondition is checked BEFORE the body runs, so there is no result yet"
              "`result` names nothing here: this declaration has no `::` signature, so it declares no result type for `result` to have")
            None
            (Some
              "a `;@axiom:pre(...)` or `;@axiom:post(...)` is a claim the COMPILER cannot decide - there is no value analysis here - so it is compiled into the body and checked at run time, on every call, and a failure writes `axiom: precondition failed in `f`: ...` on fd 2 and exits 80. That is why the expression has to parse, has to be `Bool`, and has to PERFORM nothing: a contract that allocates or writes changes the program by being stated. Comparison, indexing, `strEq`, `strLen`, `vecLen` and `vecGet` are allocation-free and are what a predicate is written from; `concat`, `fmtInt` and `vecNew` are not. `result` names the declared result of a `post` and nothing anywhere else"))))
    }
    0))

; AX3042 for the three effects that refine `IO` (`isConcEntropyEffect`).
; A purity claim is answered by AX3010 instead, as it is for IO, so one
; mistake draws one diagnostic.
(pub :: checkUndeclaredRefined (-> TC ASTNode Strings Axtags Axtags Int))
(pub fn (checkUndeclaredRefined tc d eff own sig)
  (if (== (tagsClaimPure sig own) 1)
    0
    {
      (checkUndeclaredOne tc d eff own sig "Entropy")
      (checkUndeclaredOne tc d eff own sig "Spawn")
      (checkUndeclaredOne tc d eff own sig "Block")
    }))

(pub :: checkUndeclaredOne (-> TC ASTNode Strings Axtags Axtags String Int))
(pub fn (checkUndeclaredOne tc d eff own sig e)
  (if (== (effDefinite eff (builtinEff e)) 0)
    0
    (if (|| (== (tagsClaimEffectIn tc sig (builtinEff e) 0) 1)
          (== (tagsClaimEffectIn tc own (builtinEff e) 0) 1))
      0
      (emitUndeclaredEffect tc d e))))

; A trait-method IMPLEMENTATION draws this like anything else, and
; getting there needed a parser fix rather than an exemption.
; `attachAxtags` walked only the top-level declaration vector, so a
; `;@axiom:effect(io)` written above `(emit ...)` inside an `(impl
; ...)` was attached to the next TOP-LEVEL declaration or to nothing -
; measured: silently ignored, with AX3042 firing on a method no edit
; could satisfy. A diagnostic no edit can satisfy is worse than none,
; and the honest fix is a place to put the answer, not silence about
; the question. `attachAxtags` now threads its cursor through an
; impl's methods and a trait's defaults, so the positional rule holds
; at both depths.
;
; The effect would reach a caller either way - the walk unions every
; impl at a trait-method call site, which is what dynamic dispatch
; means - but "the caller is told" is not the same as "the
; implementation declared it", and only the second lets a reader learn
; what an impl does without reading its body.
; The help carries the FIX since 2026-08-29: `;@axiom:effect(io)` on a
; line of its own, inserted at the START OF THE LINE that holds the
; declaration. A tag attaches to the next declaration after it, so
; above the `fn` is right even when a `::` signature precedes it
; (measured: the result checks clean), and a line start is right for
; an indented declaration too - an `impl` member gets the tag inside
; the impl, where `attachAxtags` reads it (358 pins that shape).
;
; Offered for the ENTRY file only (`tcEntrySrc`), and only when the
; name's span sits where a declaration is WRITTEN: the byte before it
; is `(`, or the word before it is `fn` (the bare nullary `(fn answer
; 42)`). The other shape is a generated declaration named by a macro
; ARGUMENT - `(pair alpha beta quiet loud)` in 361 anchors `loud`'s
; AX3042 on that token - and a tag line above an invocation attaches
; to nothing the diagnostic is about. The prose stays either way.
(pub :: emitUndeclaredEffect (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitUndeclaredEffect tc d name)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    {
      (let dg
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3042" "undeclared-effect" (nodeSpan d)
          (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` performs ")
            (concat name " and its declaration does not say so"))
          None None))
      {
        (let at
          (if (let (Some src) (tcEntrySrc tc))
            (effectTagInsertAt src (spanStart (nodeSpan d)))
            None))
        {
          {
            (let tag (concat ";@axiom:effect(" (concat (strLower name) ")")))
            (if (let (Some position) at)
              (diagAddFixHelp dg (concat "write `" (concat tag "` above the declaration"))
                (mkSpan position position) (concat tag "\n"))
              (diagAddHelp dg (concat "write `" (concat tag "` above the declaration"))))
          }
          (diagAddNote dg
            "effects are INFERRED transitively, so this fires on every function up the call chain, and each one is a place a reader would otherwise have to open the callee to learn it. If the effect is not wanted here the fix is to stop performing it, not to stop saying so. `IO`, `Entropy`, `Spawn` and `Block` are required; `Alloc` and `Mut` are ambient and never need declaring")
          (emitDiag tc dg)
        }
      }
    }))

; The byte the tag line is inserted at for a declaration whose name
; starts at `p`, or None when `p` is not where a declaration is written.
(pub :: effectTagInsertAt (-> String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (effectTagInsertAt src p)
  (if (|| (< p 0) (>= p (strLen src)))
    None
    (if (declNameWritten src p)
      (Some (lineStartOf src p))
      None)))

(pub :: declNameWritten (-> String Int Bool))
(pub fn (declNameWritten src p)
  {
    (let mut i (- p 1))
    {
      (while (if (>= i 0)
        (isBlankByte (strByte src i))
        false)
        (set i (- i 1)))
      (if (< i 0)
        false
        (if (== (strByte src i) 40)
          true
          {
            (let mut j i)
            {
              (while (if (>= j 0)
                (isIdentChar (strByte src j))
                false)
                (set j (- j 1)))
              (strEq (strSlice src (+ j 1) (- i j)) "fn")
            }
          }))
    }
  })

; AX3073: a body performs an unsafe operation and its declaration does
; not say so.
;
; THREE OPERATIONS ARE UNSAFE (R-B6, `MM-EXEC-9d`):
;
;   a raw primitive     the thirty-six `isUnsafePrim` names, `__load64`,
;                       `__retain` and `__syscall3` among them;
;   an extern call      a call to, or a reference to, an `extern` item,
;                       whose implementation the checker cannot see;
;   a forging cast      `(cast T x)` into a reference `x` is not already
;                       (`castForges`): `(cast Hid 7)` checked OK here
;                       and died at run time with SIGSEGV.
;
; LEXICAL, where AX3042 is transitive - and deliberately. A transitive
; closure through `strData`, `strByte`, `memGetWord` and `memAlloc`
; would tag 3,910 of the 4,021 effectful functions in this tree
; (`scripts/check-effect-distribution.sh`); the obligation sits where
; the operation is performed instead, the way AX3071 and AX3072 refuse
; a prim call's shape rather than its callers. The tag makes a TRUSTED
; encapsulation: its author vouches that every well-typed call is
; safe, and its callers inherit nothing (`effUnionCallee`).
;
; EVERY DECLARATION THAT DID NOT SAY `effect(unsafe)` IS ASKED, whatever
; else it claimed. Only the unclaimed were until R-B6, the way only the
; unclaimed draw AX3042, and that left `effect(io)` over a body that
; also touched raw memory silent: a claim about IO bought silence about
; memory. With trusted callees the difference is no longer cosmetic -
; an undeclared unsafe operation would reach every caller's row and
; refuse `restrict(no-unsafe)` on code that never asked for it. A
; purity claim is the one exception: an unsafe operation under it is
; AX3010 already, and one mistake is reported once.
;
; Reported ONCE per declaration, at the first operation in source
; order: `jsonEscapeFill` holds twenty-one primitive calls, and per-site
; would flood. The span is the operation - an application's span is its
; head's, so it covers the primitive's or the callee's name, and a
; cast's covers `cast` - while the machine-applicable fix lands at the
; declaration's line start, where the tag attaches.
(pub :: checkUndeclaredUnsafe (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkUndeclaredUnsafe tc d e sink forges)
  {
    (let prims (unsafeScanInto e vecNew))
    (let p (unsafeFirstSite prims 1))
    (let c (unsafeFirstSite sink 1))
    (let f (unsafeFirstSite forges 3))
    (let kind
      (unsafeEarliestKind (unsafeSiteStart prims p 1) (unsafeSiteStart sink c 1)
        (unsafeSiteStart forges f 3)))
    (if (let (Some selected) kind)
      (if (== selected 0)
        (if (let (Some index) p)
          (emitUndeclaredUnsafe tc d (vecGet prims index) 0 forges 0)
          0)
        ; Extern references have no checked body establishing their safety.
        (if (== selected 1)
          (if (let (Some index) c)
            (emitUndeclaredUnsafe tc d (vecGet sink index) 3 forges 0)
            0)
          (if (let (Some index) f)
            (emitUndeclaredUnsafe tc d (vecGet forges (* index 3)) 2 forges index)
            0)))
      0)
  })

; The first source-ordered record index, absent when no node has a span.
; Each record occupies stride words and begins with its AST node.
(pub :: unsafeFirstSite (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (unsafeFirstSite v stride)
  {
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut best (:: None (Option Int)))
    (let mut bestAt 0)
    (while (< (* i stride) (vecLen v))
      {
        (let sp (spanOf (vecGet v (* i stride))))
        (if (!= sp 0)
          (if (|| (isNone best) (< (spanStart sp) bestAt))
            {
              (set best (Some i))
              (set bestAt (spanStart sp))
            }))
        (set i (+ i 1))
      })
    best
  })

; Some indices come from unsafeFirstSite and therefore name a spanned node.
(pub :: unsafeSiteStart (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Option Int) Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (unsafeSiteStart v k stride)
  (match k
    ((None) None)
    ((Some index) (Some (spanStart (spanOf (vecGet v (* index stride))))))))

; Some 0 selects a primitive, Some 1 an extern, Some 2 a forging cast.
; Missing sites stay absent. Source ties retain that priority order.
(pub :: unsafeEarliestKind (-> (Option Int) (Option Int) (Option Int) (Option Int)))
(pub fn (unsafeEarliestKind pa ca fa)
  (if (let (Some earliest) (unsafeMinStart (unsafeMinStart pa ca) fa))
    (if (match pa
      ((None) false)
      ((Some start) (== start earliest)))
      (Some 0)
      (if (match ca
        ((None) false)
        ((Some start) (== start earliest)))
        (Some 1)
        (Some 2)))
    None))

(pub :: unsafeMinStart (-> (Option Int) (Option Int) (Option Int)))
(pub fn (unsafeMinStart a b)
  (match a
    ((None) b)
    ((Some left)
      (match b
        ((None) a)
        ((Some right)
          (if (< right left)
            b
            a)
        ))
    )))

; `site` is the primitive's application (`kind` 0), the extern
; reference (3) or the cast (2); `forges` and `k` name the cast's
; record when it is one.
(pub :: emitUndeclaredUnsafe (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (emitUndeclaredUnsafe tc d site kind forges k)
  {
    (let sp (spanOf site))
    (if (== sp 0)
      0
      (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
        0
        {
          (let dg
            (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3073" "undeclared-unsafe" sp
              (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` ")
                (unsafeOpText site kind forges k))
              None None))
          {
            (let at
              (if (let (Some src) (tcEntrySrc tc))
                (effectTagInsertAt src (spanStart (nodeSpan d)))
                None))
            {
              (if (let (Some position) at)
                (diagAddFixHelp dg "write `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)` above the declaration"
                  (mkSpan position position) ";@axiom:effect(unsafe)\n")
                (diagAddHelp dg "write `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)` above the declaration"))
              (if (== kind 2)
                {
                  {
                    (let args (spineArgVec site vecNew))
                    (if (>= (vecLen args) 2)
                      {
                        (let valueSpan (spanOf (vecGet args 1)))
                        (if (!= valueSpan 0)
                          (diagAddSecondary dg valueSpan
                            (concat
                              (concat "source has type `" (tyRender (vecGet forges (+ (* k 3) 2))))
                              "`; the conversion trusts its representation")))
                      })
                  }
                  (diagAddHelp dg
                    "prefer a constructor or a typed library operation. For a raw conversion, prove the target's layout and validity, and keep its backing owner alive. Changing the type does not extend a region's scope")
                })
              (diagAddHelp dg
                "the unsafe tag marks a trusted boundary: you must make every well-typed call safe. It leaves ordinary type, effect and restriction checks in place")
              (emitDiag tc (diagSetLabel dg (unsafeOpLabel site kind)))
            }
          }
        }))
  })

; The rest of AX3073's sentence, after the declaration's name.
(pub :: unsafeOpText (-> ASTNode Int (Vec ASTNode) Int String))
(pub fn (unsafeOpText site kind forges k)
  (if (== kind 3)
    (concat (concat "calls extern `" (srcSpelling (nodeAName site)))
      "`, whose implementation is outside the safety checker, and its declaration does not say so")
    (if (== kind 0)
      (if (== (isAsmPrim (nodeAName (spineHead site))) 1)
        "runs inline assembly and its declaration does not say so"
        "touches raw memory and its declaration does not say so")
      (concat (unsafeForgeText forges k) ", and its declaration does not say so"))))

(pub :: unsafeOpLabel (-> ASTNode Int String))
(pub fn (unsafeOpLabel site kind)
  (if (== kind 3)
    "an extern call"
    (if (== kind 0)
      (graphShownName (nodeAName (spineHead site)))
      "forges a reference")))

; "forges a `Hid` out of an `Int` with `cast`", for the `k`th record
; of `forges`: node, target, source.
(pub :: unsafeForgeText (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int String))
(pub fn (unsafeForgeText forges k)
  (concat
    (concat
      (concat (concat "forges `" (tyRender (castNormTyShown (vecGet forges (+ (* k 3) 1)))))
        "` out of `")
      (tyRender (vecGet forges (+ (* k 3) 2))))
    "` with `as`"))

; The target as the author wrote it, with `(-> A B)` read as the arrow
; it names. `castNormTy` also expands aliases, which a message should
; not: the author wrote the alias.
(pub :: castNormTyShown (-> ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (castNormTyShown t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    t
    (if (&& (== (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
          (&& (strEq (nodeAName t) "->") (>= (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 2)))
      (castArrowOf (nodeBChildren t) 0)
      t)))

; The unsafe role a declaration's tags give it (FnEnt word 9): 1 for
; `effect(unsafe)`, a trusted encapsulation, 0 otherwise.
(pub :: unsafeRoleOfTags (-> TC Axtags Axtags Int))
(pub fn (unsafeRoleOfTags tc sig own)
  (if (== (tagsClaimUnsafe tc sig own) 1)
    1
    0))

(pub :: tcUnsafeRoles (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (tcUnsafeRoles tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
          {
            (let role
              (unsafeRoleOfTags tc
                (match (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName d))
                  ((None) vecNew)
                  ((Some tags) tags))
                (nodeAxtagsOf d)))
            (if (! (== role 0))
              {
                (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
                (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                  (fnEntSetUnsafe ent role)
                  ())
              })
          })
      }
      (tcUnsafeRoles tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; Does either tag set claim `effect(unsafe)`, in any spelling the claim
; check itself resolves to the built-in `Unsafe` (`axtagEffectOf`)?
(pub :: tagsClaimUnsafe (-> TC Axtags Axtags Int))
(pub fn (tagsClaimUnsafe tc sig own)
  (if (== (tagsClaimEffectIn tc sig (builtinEff "Unsafe") 0) 1)
    1
    (tagsClaimEffectIn tc own (builtinEff "Unsafe") 0)))

(pub :: tagsClaimEffectIn (-> TC Axtags String Int Int))
(pub fn (tagsClaimEffectIn tc tags want i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (let t (vecGet tags i))
      (if (&& (strEq (axtagKeyOf t) "effect")
            (strEq (axtagEffectOf tc (strTrimBlanks (axtagTextValue t))) want))
        1
        (tagsClaimEffectIn tc tags want (+ i 1)))
    }))

; Does either tag set claim purity, in either spelling - `effect(pure)`
; or the retired bare `pure`, which AX3078 refuses beside whatever
; else the body draws?
(pub :: tagsClaimPure (-> Axtags Axtags Int))
(pub fn (tagsClaimPure sig own)
  (if (== (tagsClaimPureIn sig 0) 1)
    1
    (tagsClaimPureIn own 0)))

(pub :: tagsClaimPureIn (-> Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (tagsClaimPureIn tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (let t (vecGet tags i))
      (if (|| (strEq (axtagKeyOf t) "pure")
            (&& (strEq (axtagKeyOf t) "effect")
              (== (strEqCI (strTrimBlanks (axtagTextValue t)) "pure") 1)))
        1
        (tagsClaimPureIn tags (+ i 1)))
    }))

; `forgeSites` as a Vec: the forging casts `tcCheckFn` recorded in the body
; being checked, three words each (node, target, source), or an empty
; Vec outside a checked body.
(pub :: unsafeForgeSitesOf (-> TC (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (unsafeForgeSitesOf tc)
  (match (tcForgeSites tc)
    ((None) vecNew)
    ((Some sites) sites)))

; Once a body is typed: of the casts `castNoteForge` recorded, keep the
; ones that forge. Every placeholder the body pins is pinned by now, so
; each source reads as the type it ends up with (`castMayForge` says
; why the cast itself cannot ask).
(pub :: unsafeSettleForges (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (unsafeSettleForges tc)
  (if (let (Some sites) (tcForgeSites tc))
    {
      (let kept vecNew)
      (unsafeSettleFrom tc sites kept 0)
      (tcSetForgeSites tc (Some kept))
      0
    }
    0))

(pub :: unsafeSettleFrom (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (unsafeSettleFrom tc sites kept i)
  (if (>= (+ i 2) (vecLen sites))
    0
    {
      (if (== (castForges tc (vecGet sites (+ i 1)) (vecGet sites (+ i 2))) 1)
        {
          (vecPush kept (vecGet sites i))
          (vecPush kept (vecGet sites (+ i 1)))
          (vecPush kept (vecGet sites (+ i 2)))
        })
      (unsafeSettleFrom tc sites kept (+ i 3))
    }))

; ERASING CASTS. `(cast Int s)` with `s` a `String` keeps the word and
; drops the type: the block's share is never given back, and nothing
; downstream can say what the word was. That is the reverse of a
; forging cast, and the same three types decide it: the target is a
; word nobody dereferences (`castTyClass` 0), and the operand is a
; reference (1) or a type variable the signature wrote, which a caller
; may instantiate at one. A minted `_tN` the body never pinned says
; nothing, and a cast whose value never returns erases nothing
; (`castNoteForge` drops it).
;
; Counted per declaration, once the body is typed, and printed by
; `symbols` as `#erasures=n`. `scripts/check-cast-arg-root.sh` sums the
; compiler's and holds the total to a ratchet.
(pub :: castErases (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (castErases tc tgt src)
  (if (!= (castTyClass tc tgt) 0)
    0
    {
      (let k (castTyClass tc src))
      (if (== k 1)
        1
        (if (&& (== k 2) (== (tyIsSrcVar (castNormTy tc src)) 1))
          1
          0))
    }))

(pub :: eraseSettle (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (eraseSettle tc d)
  (if (let (Some ev) (tcEraseSites tc))
    {
      (let n (eraseCountFrom tc ev 0 0))
      (if (== n 0)
        0
        {
          (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            {
              (vecPush (tcEraseCounts tc) (EraseCount :entry ent :count n))
              0
            }
            0)
        })
    }
    0))

(pub :: eraseCountFrom (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (eraseCountFrom tc sites i acc)
  (if (>= (+ i 2) (vecLen sites))
    acc
    (eraseCountFrom tc sites (+ i 3)
      (+ acc (castErases tc (vecGet sites (+ i 1)) (vecGet sites (+ i 2)))))))

; How many references `ent`'s body erases to a word, 0 for a declaration
; that erases none or was never checked.
(pub :: tcErasuresOf (-> TC FnEnt Int))
(pub fn (tcErasuresOf tc ent)
  (eraseLookup (tcEraseCounts tc) ent 0))

(pub :: eraseLookup (-> (Vec EraseCount) FnEnt Int Int))
(pub fn (eraseLookup pairs ent i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen pairs))
    0
    {
      (let pair (vecGet pairs i))
      (if (== (as pair.entry Int) (as ent Int))
        pair.count
        (eraseLookup pairs ent (+ i 1)))
    }))

; After a body is checked: a function whose body forges is remembered
; (`forgeDecls`), for the late pass to follow and to charge its row.
(pub :: unsafeNoteForgeDecl (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (unsafeNoteForgeDecl tc d)
  (if (== (vecLen (unsafeForgeSitesOf tc)) 0)
    0
    {
      (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        {
          (vecPush (tcForgeDecls tc) ent)
          0
        }
        0)
    }))

; A `restrict(no-unsafe)` whose row carried no `Unsafe` at all - neither
; performed nor possible - is remembered for `unsafeLatePass` (`unsafePending`).
; Only such a claim can still be refuted there, and only by a forging
; callee the fixpoint could not see.
(pub :: unsafeNotePending (-> TC ASTNode Axtags Axtags Strings Int))
(pub fn (unsafeNotePending tc d own sig eff)
  (if (== (effMay eff (builtinEff "Unsafe")) 1)
    0
    {
      (let rs vecNew)
      {
        (restrictCollect sig rs 0)
        (restrictCollect own rs 0)
        (if (== (strInVec rs "no-unsafe" 0) 1)
          {
            (vecPush (tcUnsafePending tc) d)
            0
          }
          0)
      }
    }))

; THE PASS AFTER EVERY BODY IS TYPED. The effect fixpoint runs before
; any body is checked, because a handle's AX3011 asks what a body
; performs and the answer may come from a function declared later - and
; a cast forges by the TYPE of what it is given, which exists only once
; its body has been checked. So a forging cast is charged to its own
; body's claims where it is found (`checkAxtags`), and this pass does
; the two things that needed every body first:
;
;   1. a `restrict(no-unsafe)` the row could not refute is asked whether
;      its call graph reaches a function that forges and does not say
;      `effect(unsafe)` - the "through one indirection" case, answered
;      the same whichever of the two is declared first;
;   2. a forging function's row says `Unsafe`, so `symbols` reports what
;      the body performs. Written last, so that no claim above read a
;      row this pass changed.
;
; In a program the compiler accepts, (1) never fires: the forging
; function it would name draws AX3073 itself. It is the restriction's
; own answer, in the place its author looks.
(pub :: unsafeLatePass (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (unsafeLatePass tc)
  {
    ; Each refusal is reported in its own declaration's unit; put the
    ; unit back afterwards so the passes that follow report in theirs.
    {
      (let unit (tcCurUnit tc))
      {
        (unsafeLateRestricts tc (tcUnsafePending tc) 0)
        (tcSetCurUnit tc unit)
      }
    }
    (unsafeLateRows (tcForgeDecls tc) 0)
  })

(pub :: unsafeLateRestricts (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (unsafeLateRestricts tc pending i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen pending))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet pending i))
        {
          (let _u (tcSetCurUnit tc (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) (nodeModuleOpt d))))
          (let pathFound (graphWitness tc (restrictOwnEnt tc d) "forge"))
          (if (let (Some path) pathFound)
            (emitRestrictViolated tc d "no-unsafe"
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat (concat "the body performs Unsafe: " (graphRender path)) ", where `")
                  (bareOf (fnEntName (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)))))
                "` forges a reference with `as` and does not say `effect(unsafe)`")
              path)
            ())
        }
      }
      (unsafeLateRestricts tc pending (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: unsafeLateRows (-> (Vec FnEnt) Int Int))
(pub fn (unsafeLateRows forged i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen forged))
    0
    {
      (effAdd (fnEntEffects (vecGet forged i)) (builtinEff "Unsafe"))
      (unsafeLateRows forged (+ i 1))
    }))

; A reference the effect walk resolved, while `checkAxtags` walks one
; body (`unsafeSink`): pushed when it names an extern, for AX3073 to
; name. A local or a parameter of the same spelling is not
; the function, and is not pushed.
(pub :: unsafeSinkNote (-> TC ASTNode BScope Int))
(pub fn (unsafeSinkNote tc node bound)
  (if (let (Some sink) (tcUnsafeSink tc))
    (if (== (bsIsLocal bound (nodeAName node)) 1)
      0
      {
        (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName node)))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (if (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 2)
            {
              (vecPush sink node)
              0
            }
            0)
          0)
      })
    0))

; The AXTAGs on the first indexed signature declaring `name`, or None.
; A present empty tag vector remains Some empty.
;
; `nameMatches name (nodeA d)` - the FUNCTION's name against the
; SIGNATURE's, in that order, and the order is load-bearing. Both
; spellings occur: `mangleDecl` rewrites an imported `fn` and `::`
; alike, but an ENTRY-file signature over an IMPORTED definition stays
; bare while the function becomes `Mod$f`.
;
; Reversed - `nameMatches (nodeA d) (bareOf name)` - it accepts ANY
; module's signature for a bare name, and one module's `pure` claim
; drew a false AX3010 in another (tests/diagnostics/342). A strict
; `strEq` instead drops the entry-signature shape above.
(pub :: sigAxtagsFor (-> TC String (Option Axtags)))
(pub fn (sigAxtagsFor tc name)
  ; Built, then READ BACK: `memSetWord` answers the RECORD it wrote
  ; into, not the value (stdlib/Mem.ax:202-208). Binding its result
  ; and walking it as a Vec walks `tc` - a segfault that printed OK
  ; on small files and died at 139 emitting the compiler.
  {
    (let table
      (if (let (Some cached) (tcSigTags tc))
        cached
        {
          (let built (sigTagsBuild (tcAllDeclsVec tc) vecNew 0))
          (tcSetSigTags tc (Some built))
          built
        }))
    (sigAxtagsFrom table name 0)
  })

; One pass over the declaration list, keeping only the signatures that
; carry an AXTAG. None is unbuilt and Some empty is built with no tags,
; so the pass runs once even when no declaration carries a tag.
; Some replacement text for an indexed deprecation, or None when
; `name` is not deprecated. Empty text remains explicit if present.
;
; WHY A TABLE AND NOT A SCAN OF `sigTags`. This is asked at every
; resolved reference. `sigTags` holds every signature carrying ANY
; AXTAG - a few hundred, almost all `effect(io)` - so walking it here
; is a linear scan per reference, which is the O(n^2) `emitTyvarFor`
; already shipped once. This table holds only the deprecated ones, and
; is EMPTY in every program in this repository today, so the common
; path is one `vecLen`.
; AX3048 at a reference to a name its declaration marks deprecated.
;
; A WARNING, not an error, and that is the whole point of the tag: the
; name still works, and the release that removes it is allowed to
; because `compat/BREAKING`'s rule reads the notice from the previous
; release's baseline (`docs/compatibility.md` COMPAT-7). Refusing here
; would make deprecation and removal the same event, which is what a
; deprecation notice exists to separate.
(pub :: warnDeprecated (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (warnDeprecated tc name span)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    (if (let (Some repl) (deprecatedFor tc name))
      (if (== (strLen repl) 0)
        0
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_WARNING "AX3048" "deprecated-name" span
              (concat (concat "`" (srcSpelling name)) "` is deprecated") None
              (Some (concat "the declaration says: " repl))
              "It still works, and it still compiles - a deprecation is a notice, not a refusal. It may be removed in a later release, and `compat/BREAKING`'s rule lets that happen without a declared break precisely because this notice shipped first")
            (concat "declared `;@axiom:deprecated(" (concat repl ")`")))))
      0)))

(pub :: deprecatedFor (-> TC String (Option String)))
(pub fn (deprecatedFor tc name)
  {
    (let table
      (if (let (Some cached) (tcDeprecated tc))
        cached
        {
          (let built (deprecatedBuild (tcAllDeclsVec tc) vecNew 0))
          (tcSetDeprecated tc (Some built))
          built
        }))
    (deprecatedFrom table name 0)
  })

; One pass over the declaration list, keeping the declarations that
; carry the key. Both spellings are kept because a tag attaches to
; whichever declaration it was written above, and the author may have
; put it on the `::` or on the `fn`; the checker reads both
; (`checkAxtags`), and so does this. (`symbols.ax`'s `smTags` used to
; keep only the last non-empty set of the two, which is the measurement
; that made `axiom test` discovery a NAME rule; it unions them since
; 2026-08-29.)
(pub :: deprecatedBuild (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (deprecatedBuild decls acc i)
  (if (== (as decls Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
      acc
      {
        {
          (let d (vecGet decls i))
          (if (|| (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG) (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN))
            (if (let (Some replacement) (axtagValueOf d "deprecated"))
              (if (> (strLen replacement) 0)
                (vecPush acc d))))
        }
        (deprecatedBuild decls acc (+ i 1))
      })))

; The first indexed declaration wins. A missing or empty tag on that
; declaration yields Some empty text; None means no declaration matched.
(pub :: deprecatedFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (deprecatedFrom tbl name i)
  (if (== (as tbl Int) 0)
    None
    (if (>= i (vecLen tbl))
      None
      {
        (let d (vecGet tbl i))
        (if (strEq (bareOf (nodeAName d)) (bareOf name))
          {
            (let replacement (axtagValueOf d "deprecated"))
            (match replacement
              ((None) (Some ""))
              ((Some _) replacement))
          }
          (deprecatedFrom tbl name (+ i 1)))
      })))

(pub :: sigTagsBuild (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (sigTagsBuild decls acc i)
  (if (== (as decls Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
      acc
      {
        {
          (let d (vecGet decls i))
          (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_SIG) (!= (nodeAxtagsWord d) 0))
            (vecPush acc d)
            ())
        }
        (sigTagsBuild decls acc (+ i 1))
      })))

(pub :: sigAxtagsFrom (-> Nodes String Int (Option Axtags)))
(pub fn (sigAxtagsFrom tagged name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tagged))
    None
    {
      (let d (vecGet tagged i))
      (if (== (nameMatches name (nodeAName d)) 1)
        (nodeAxtagsOpt d)
        (sigAxtagsFrom tagged name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; A tag content the signature's set already carries. `checkAxtags`
; checks the signature's tags first, so the function's own walk skips
; what it would only repeat: a `;@axiom:effect(pure)` above BOTH the `::`
; and the `fn` drew every verdict twice, byte-identical down to the
; span, and the summary counted two errors for one wrong claim
; (tests/diagnostics/355-tag-over-approximated.axdl, before this).
; Different claims are still each checked - only the content already
; answered is skipped - which is the union-then-check-once rule
; `restrictSplit` keeps with `strInVec`.
(pub :: axtagContentSeen (-> Axtags String Int Int))
(pub fn (axtagContentSeen tags content i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    (if (strEq (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)) content)
      1
      (axtagContentSeen tags content (+ i 1)))))

; `checkAxtagsFrom`, skipping the contents already checked from
; another set. The signature's walk passes 0 and checks everything;
; the function's own walk passes the signature's set.
(pub :: checkAxtagsFromSkipping (-> TC ASTNode Axtags Int Strings Ints Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (checkAxtagsFromSkipping tc d tags i eff eparams skip lex)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (if (! (== (axtagContentSeen skip (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)) 0) 1))
        (checkOneAxtag tc d (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)) eff eparams lex))
      (checkAxtagsFromSkipping tc d tags (+ i 1) eff eparams skip lex)
    }))

(pub :: checkAxtagsFrom (-> TC ASTNode Axtags Int Strings Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (checkAxtagsFrom tc d tags i eff eparams lex)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (checkOneAxtag tc d (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)) eff eparams lex)
      (checkAxtagsFrom tc d tags (+ i 1) eff eparams lex)
    }))

; `content` is what followed `;@axiom:` - either `key` or
; `key(value)`. Only `pure` and `effect` are validated; every other
; key is metadata stage0 records and does not check. `lex` is 1 when
; the body performs an unsafe operation itself (`unsafeLexical`).
(pub :: checkOneAxtag (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (checkOneAxtag tc d content eff eparams lex)
  ; Purity has one spelling, `effect(pure)`. A bare `pure` key is
  ; refused by `axtagKeyTypo` (AX3078) rather than checked here.
  {
    (let op (strFindByte content 40 0))
    {
      (let key
        (if (let (Some opening) op)
          (strSlice content 0 opening)
          content))
      ; This reader accepts text through the end of an unterminated tag.
      (let valueEnd
        (if (isSome op)
          (match (axtagCloseAt content)
            ((None) (strLen content))
            ((Some closing) closing))
          (strLen content)))
      (let val
        (if (let (Some opening) op)
          (strSlice content (+ opening 1) (- valueEnd (+ opening 1)))
          ""))
      (let trail
        (if (isSome op)
          (strTrimBlanks (strSlice content (+ valueEnd 1) (- (strLen content) (+ valueEnd 1))))
          ""))
      (if (> (strLen trail) 0)
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3138" "axtag-trailing-text" (nodeSpan d)
              (concat
                (concat "text after the closing `)` of this `;@axiom:` tag is not read: `" trail)
                "`")
              None (Some "move the text inside the parentheses, or onto its own comment line")
              "every reader of a tag stops at the last `)`, so a word after it changes nothing the compiler checks; a misspelt `strict` there would silently weaken the claim")
            "this text is not read"))
        (if (strEq key "effect")
          (if (strEq val "pure")
            (axtagContradicted tc d "`effect(pure)` claim" eff)
            {
              (let names (effTagWords val))
              (if (> (vecLen names) 1)
                (emitEffectTagList tc d val names)
                (axtagUnsupported tc d val eff eparams lex))
            })
          (if (strEq key "result")
            (checkResultTag tc d val lex)
            (if (strEq key "scoped")
                (checkScopedTag tc d val)
              (strEq key "compute")
                (checkComputeTag tc d val)
              (axtagKeyTypo tc d key)))))
    }
  })

; Does either half of the declaration say `effect(unsafe)`? `checkAxtags`
; asks about every tag with the `fn` as `d`, so the signature's tags
; are read through `sigAxtagsFor`, as `checkAxtags` itself reads them.
(pub :: declTrusted (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (declTrusted tc d)
  (if (== (declHasAxtag d "effect(unsafe)") 1)
    1
    ; `declHasAxtagFrom` answers 0 for a name with no signature.
    (match (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName d))
      ((None) 0)
      ((Some tags) (declHasAxtagFrom tags "effect(unsafe)" 0)))))

; `result(owned)`: the declaration's type-variable result carries a
; share the caller owns (MM-LIFE-2n). Codegen releases such a result
; at a counted instantiation, so the claim has to be true, and nothing
; in the checker can see that a raw load's share was transferred -
; which is why the claim is accepted only inside a trusted
; encapsulation: a declaration that also says `effect(unsafe)` on this
; half, or whose body performs an unsafe operation itself (`lex`).
; Any other value, or the claim on a safe function, is AX3116.
(pub :: checkResultTag (-> TC ASTNode String Int Int))
(pub fn (checkResultTag tc d val lex)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (if (strEq val "owned")
      (if (|| (== (declTrusted tc d) 1) (== lex 1))
        0
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3116" "result-claim" (nodeSpan d)
              (concat (concat "`result(owned)` on `" (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
                "` is a claim only a trusted function may make")
              None
              (Some
                "put `;@axiom:effect(unsafe)` beside it on the same declaration, or let the result's ownership be inferred")
              "the checker cannot see that a share was transferred through a raw load, so the claim is accepted only inside a trusted encapsulation (MM-LIFE-2n)")
            "safe code claims it")))
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3116" "result-claim" (nodeSpan d)
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`result(" val) ")` on `") (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
              "` is not a claim the compiler reads")
            None (Some "write `;@axiom:result(owned)`, the one value this key takes")
            "`result(owned)` says a type-variable result carries a share the caller must release; codegen acts on it, so a value it does not know is refused rather than ignored")
          "not `owned`")))))

; AX3076: `;@axiom:effect(io, unsafe)`. An `effect(...)` tag names ONE
; effect, and a list was read as one CUSTOM effect named `io, unsafe` -
; which nothing declares and nothing produces, so AX3010 answered
; "claim unsupported: missing io, unsafe" about a body that performed
; both. The message was true of the parse and false of the program.
; `restrict(...)` does take a comma list, which is what makes the
; spelling an easy one to reach for.
;
; Refused rather than accepted: one line per effect is the spelling
; every tool that reads tags (`symbols`, the effect walk, the LSP)
; already agrees on, and a second spelling would have to be taught to
; each of them. Whitespace separates too - `effect(io unsafe)` is the
; same mistake - while a single name with blanks round it is not a
; list and keeps its old reading.
(pub :: effTagWords (-> String Strings))
(pub fn (effTagWords val)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    (let n (strLen val))
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut start (- 0 1))
    {
      (while (<= i n)
        {
          (let b
            (if (< i n)
              (strByte val i)
              44))
          {
            (if (|| (== b 44) (|| (== b 32) (== b 9)))
              (if (>= start 0)
                {
                  (vecPush out (strSlice val start (- i start)))
                  (set start (- 0 1))
                })
              (if (< start 0)
                (set start i)))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          }
        })
      out
    }
  })

(pub :: emitEffectTagList (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Int))
(pub fn (emitEffectTagList tc d val names)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3076" "effect-tag-list" (nodeSpan d)
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "`effect(" val) ")` on `") (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
              "` names ")
            (concat (fmtInt (vecLen names)) " effects, and an `effect(...)` tag names one"))
          None (Some (concat "write one tag line per effect: " (effTagLines names)))
          "a list here was read as ONE effect spelled with the comma in it, which nothing declares - so the claim could never be satisfied, and AX3010 reported it missing from a body that performed it. `restrict(...)` takes a comma list; `effect(...)` does not")
        "one effect per `effect(...)` tag"))))

(:: effTagLines (-> Strings String))
(fn (effTagLines names)
  {
    (let out strBuilderNew)
    (for i in 0..(vecLen names)
      {
        (if (> i 0)
          (strBuilderPush out " and "))
        (strBuilderPush out (concat (concat "`;@axiom:effect(" (vecGet names i)) ")`"))
      })
    (strBuilderFinish out)
  })

; The LAST `)`, scanned backwards, which is stage0's `rfind` - a
; value may contain parens of its own.
; AX3039: a key one slip away from a key the compiler actually checks.
;
; The AXTAG namespace is OPEN by decision - `checkOneAxtag`'s own note
; records that the key is "metadata stage0 records and does not check" -
; and this does not close it. An unknown key stays legal and stays
; silent. What this catches is the one shape that COSTS something:
; `;@axiom:efect(pure)` is not a purity claim, so nothing checks it as
; one, and a body performing IO under it draws no AX3010 at all. A slip
; of `pure` itself is AX3078, since purity has one spelling. The tag reads
; like a guarantee and buys silence instead, which is worse than
; writing nothing - a reader spends trust on it.
;
; AN ERROR, so the dropped claim cannot pass as a clean build. A slip
; is a change of case, one byte inserted, dropped or changed, or two
; neighbours swapped (`axtagSlip`). A key of the author's own that
; happens to sit one slip from a checked one is namespaced, which no
; slip test reads.
;
; A key containing `:` is left alone by construction. `agent:readonly`
; is namespaced and deliberate, and its distance from `pure` is not
; evidence about anything.
(pub :: axtagKeyTypo (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (axtagKeyTypo tc d key)
  (if (|| (== (strLen key) 0)
        (if (let (Some _) (strFindByte key 58 0))
          true
          false))
    0
    ; PURITY FIRST: `pure` and every slip of it (`pur`, `Pure`) are
    ; refused, because purity is written one way, `effect(pure)`.
    ; RESERVED BEFORE NEAR-MISS, and the order is the point. `effects`
    ; is one edit from `effect`, so the near-miss arm would report it
    ; as a typo, which is about the wrong thing.
    ; It is not a misspelling of an author's key; it is the compiler's
    ; key, spelled correctly.
    (if (== (axtagIsPureSpelling key) 1)
      (emitAxtagPureSpelling tc d key)
      (if (== (axtagReservedKey key) 1)
        (emitAxtagKeyReserved tc d key)
        {
          (let suggestion (axtagNearMiss key))
          (if (let (Some want) suggestion)
            (emitAxtagKeyTypo tc d key want)
            0)
        }))))

; `pure`, or one slip from it. A namespaced key never reaches here, and
; a key the compiler checks is never one: `pre` is a slip of `pure` by
; spelling alone.
(pub :: axtagIsPureSpelling (-> String Int))
(pub fn (axtagIsPureSpelling key)
  (if (strEq key "pure")
    1
    (if (== (axtagKnownKey key) 1)
      0
      (axtagSlip key "pure"))))

; AX3078: purity written any way but `;@axiom:effect(pure)`.
(pub :: emitAxtagPureSpelling (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitAxtagPureSpelling tc d key)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3078" "pure-tag-spelling" (nodeSpan d)
          (concat
            (concat
              (concat (concat "purity on `" (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
                "` is written `;@axiom:effect(pure)`, not `;@axiom:")
              key)
            "`")
          None (Some "write `;@axiom:effect(pure)`")
          "an effect claim is always `effect(...)`, so purity has one spelling; the others are refused rather than read as metadata")
        "write `;@axiom:effect(pure)`"))))

; AX3089: a function with no signature, applied before its own body
; has been checked.
;
; A function with no signature holds a minted placeholder variable
; until its body is checked, and declarations are checked in the order
; they are written, so a call from inside that body (recursion) or from
; a declaration above it meets the placeholder. Applying a variable is
; the "function type" mismatch, and it used to say exactly that:
; `expected function type, found _a` for `(fact (- n 1))`, which names
; no mistake the author made. The refusal stays, because inferring the
; callee first would reorder every diagnostic in the file and a
; provisional result type that matched anything would accept
; `(strLen (twice 4))` above `twice` and then fault. What changes is
; that it says what to write: a signature, which is where the answer
; goes (AN-39, `tests/diagnostics/1032-signature-needed.ax`).
;
; The head must be a name no local binds, naming a function entry
; whose type is still a named placeholder and whose arity is known:
; an unchecked `fn` with parameters. A parameter or `let` of unknown
; type keeps AX3004.
(pub :: signatureNeeded (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (signatureNeeded tc hname headIsVar)
  (if (|| (!= headIsVar 1) (isSome (scopeFindFrame tc hname)))
    0
    {
      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc hname))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        {
          (let ty (fnEntTy ent))
          (if (&& (! (nodeSame ty nilNode))
                (&& (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_VAR)
                  (&& (!= (strLen (nodeAName ty)) 0) (fnEntArityPositive ent))))
            1
            0)
        }
        0)
    }))

(pub :: emitSignatureNeeded (-> TC String Int Int))
(pub fn (emitSignatureNeeded tc hname span)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3089" "signature-needed" span
          (concat "`"
            (concat (bareOf hname)
              "` has no signature, and its type isn't known until its own body has been checked"))
          None
          (Some
            (concat "write its signature above its definition, such as `(:: "
              (concat (bareOf hname) " (-> Int Int))`")))
          "a function with no signature is typed by checking its body, and declarations are checked in the order they are written: a call from inside that body, or from above it, needs the signature")
        "called before its type is known"))))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; AX3053: an operation the program reaches with no handler.
;
; The hole this closes is the one `docs/agent-harness.md` records as
; "there is no whole-program discharge check, so this remains a runtime
; failure": an operation reached with no `handle` for its effect
; anywhere compiles clean, and the program writes `axiom: unhandled
; effect` on fd 2 and exits 71 (MM-EXEC-16). `main`'s row already
; answers the question - a `handle` DISCHARGES a custom effect, so an
; effect still in the row at `main` is one nothing handled.
;
; AN ERROR, because a definite custom effect in `main`'s row is now an
; operation the program reaches with no handler on the way. Three rules
; make it so, and each closed a shape that was reported wrongly while
; this was a warning:
;
;   a lambda's custom effects count where it is CALLED, not where it
;       is written (`FnVals`): `(let ((g (lambda (x) (ask x))))
;       (handle (g 2) (Ask) h))` runs and is silent, and a closure
;       built inside a handle and called after it pops traps 71 and is
;       reported, since a handle's value passes out undischarged;
;   a parameter records the handles around its calls (`EpUse`), so a
;       callee that installs the handler around its callback discharges
;       the callback's effect: `(withAsk usesAsk)` is silent;
;   a value the walk loses ESCAPES, and its custom effects arrive
;       possible.
;
; What it does NOT report: an effect that reaches the row only as
; POSSIBLE (`~c:E`) - a function named and handed somewhere the walk
; cannot follow, or a field whose stored functions disagree; an effect
; behind a call the walk could not follow (`?:incomplete`, AX3038's
; territory at a `handle`); and an effect whose declaration says the
; trap is the design. Those are where a correct program and a trapping
; one cannot be told apart, and a refusal would land on both.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Does this effect's declaration say that reaching an operation with
; no handler is a deliberate abort? `;@axiom:unhandled(trap)` above
; `(effect E ...)`.
;
; The value is EXACTLY `trap`. Any other value leaves the check on,
; which is the visible failure rather than the silent one: an author
; who writes `unhandled(abort)` sees AX3053 still reported and can
; correct the spelling, where accepting it would buy silence for a
; word the compiler never agreed to.
(pub :: effectDeclTraps (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (effectDeclTraps tc name)
  (if (let (Some declaration) (findEffectDecl tc name))
    (if (let (Some policy) (axtagValueOf declaration "unhandled"))
      (if (strEq policy "trap")
        1
        0)
      0)
    0))

; The entry file's `main`, or None. `nodeAName` is the MANGLED name for
; an imported declaration (`Mod$name`), so a bare `main` belongs to the
; module being compiled and to no other - the same test `declsHaveMain`
; makes before AX4001.
(pub :: entryMainDecl (-> (Vec ASTNode) Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (entryMainDecl decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    None
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN) (strEq (nodeAName d) "main"))
        (Some d)
        (entryMainDecl decls (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: checkUnhandledAtMain (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkUnhandledAtMain tc decls)
  {
    (let d (entryMainDecl decls 0))
    (if (let (Some d) d)
      {
        (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (checkUnhandledRow tc d ent (fnEntEffects ent) 0)
          0)
      }
      0)
  })

; AX3135. `main` answers the process's exit status, and the wrapper
; that calls it can turn four declared types into one: `Int`, `()`, and
; Err's `(Result Int Error)` and `(Result () Error)`. Any other type
; reached the wrapper as a machine word, and a reference's address
; became the status.
(pub :: checkMainResultType (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (checkMainResultType tc decls)
  {
    (let d (entryMainDecl decls 0))
    (if (let (Some d) d)
      {
        (let entFound (restrictOwnEnt tc d))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          {
            (let ty (tyResolve (fnEntTy ent)))
            (if (tcMainAnswerOk ty)
              0
              (emitMainResultType tc d ty))
          }
          0)
      }
      0)
  })

; A type `main` may answer. A type variable the declaration left open,
; and poison another diagnostic already owns, are not this check's to
; judge.
(pub :: tcMainAnswerOk (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (tcMainAnswerOk ty)
  (if (|| (nodeSame ty nilNode) (|| (== (tyIsErr ty) 1) (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_VAR)))
    true
    (if (|| (tyIsUnit ty) (tcIsNamedCon ty "Int"))
      true
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON) (strEq (nodeAName ty) "Err$Result"))
        {
          (let args (nodeBChildren ty))
          (&& (== (vecLen args) 2)
            (&& (|| (tyIsUnit (vecGet args 0)) (tcIsNamedCon (vecGet args 0) "Int"))
              (tcIsNamedCon (vecGet args 1) "Err$Error")))
        }
        false))))

; A constructor type named `name` with no arguments, after resolution.
(pub :: tcIsNamedCon (-> ASTNode String Bool))
(pub fn (tcIsNamedCon ty name)
  {
    (let r (tyResolve ty))
    (if (nodeSame r nilNode)
      false
      (&& (== (nodeTag r) TAG_T_CON)
        (&& (strEq (nodeAName r) name) (== (vecLen (nodeBChildren r)) 0))))
  })

(pub :: emitMainResultType (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (emitMainResultType tc d ty)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3135" "main-result-type" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat (concat "`main` answers `" (tyRender ty)) "`, which is not an exit status") None
        (Some
          "declare `main` as `Int`, `()`, `(Result Int Error)` or `(Result () Error)`, and print any other value before it returns")))))

; One row entry. A DEFINITE CUSTOM effect is exactly an entry that
; spells itself back: `c:Console` renders `Console` and `customEff`
; re-encodes it unchanged, where `b:IO` re-encodes to `c:IO`, the
; possible `~c:Console` to `c:Console`, and the marker `?:incomplete`
; to `c:incomplete`. So one equality skips the builtins, the possible
; spellings and the marker together, and it cannot fall behind a new
; tag the way three separate tests could.
(pub :: checkUnhandledRow (-> TC ASTNode FnEnt Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (checkUnhandledRow tc d ent eff i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen eff))
    0
    {
      {
        (let e (vecGet eff i))
        (if (strEq e (customEff (effRender e)))
          (emitUnhandledAtMain tc d ent (effRender e)))
      }
      (checkUnhandledRow tc d ent eff (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: emitUnhandledAtMain (-> TC ASTNode FnEnt String Int))
(pub fn (emitUnhandledAtMain tc d ent name)
  (if (== (effectDeclTraps tc name) 1)
    0
    (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
      0
      {
        (let path (graphWitness tc (Some ent) name))
        (emitDiag tc
          (diagSetLabel
            (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3053" "unhandled-operation" (nodeSpan d)
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat (concat "`main` reaches " (unhandledOpText tc name))
                    " with no handler: the process would write `axiom: unhandled effect` and exit 71 there")
                  (unhandledPathText path))
                (unhandledElsewhereText tc d path))
              (diagRelated (unhandledRelSpan tc d path) (unhandledRelText path))
              (Some
                "install one - `(handle BODY (E) HANDLER)` around the call - or, if an unhandled operation of this effect is a deliberate abort as `Assert`'s is, write `;@axiom:unhandled(trap)` above its `effect` declaration"))
            (concat (concat "reaches `" name) "` with nothing handling it")))
      })))

; `operation `log` of effect `Console`` where the effect declares one
; operation, and the effect alone where it declares several - a
; multi-operation effect cannot be handled at all (AX3017), so naming
; one of its operations would point at the wrong half of the problem.
(pub :: unhandledOpText (-> TC String String))
(pub fn (unhandledOpText tc name)
  (if (let (Some declaration) (findEffectDecl tc name))
    (if (== (vecLen (nodeBNames declaration)) 1)
      (concat
        (concat
          (concat (concat "operation `" (vecGetStr (nodeBNames declaration) 0)) "` of effect `")
          name)
        "`")
      (concat (concat "effect `" name) "`"))
    (concat (concat "effect `" name) "`")))

; The path, in `graphRender`'s spelling, so it cross-checks
; `symbols --calls` hop for hop as AX3049's does. A path of one entry
; is `main` performing the operation itself.
(pub :: unhandledPathText (-> (Option (Vec FnEnt)) String))
(pub fn (unhandledPathText pathFound)
  (match pathFound
    ((None) "")
    ((Some path)
      (if (== (vecLen path) 0)
        ""
        (if (<= (vecLen path) 1)
          " - in `main`'s own body"
          (concat " - " (graphRender path))))
    )))

; The witness's declaration span, and ONLY when the witness is
; declared in the same module as `main`.
;
; A span is an offset into ONE unit and carries no unit with it, so a
; related span naming a declaration in an imported module is rendered
; against the wrong bytes. Measured before this guard, on the runner's
; generated driver with `Assert`'s tag removed: the witness was
; `Test$assertEq`, the related span rendered as `143:1476-1484` of the
; DRIVER - a line that is 3 characters long - and the human surface
; drew a caret 1,476 columns out under an elided line. The path in the
; message names `Test$assertEq` in `symbols --calls`' own spelling and
; is the whole answer in that case; a related span that points at the
; wrong file is worse than none.
(pub :: unhandledRelSpan (-> TC ASTNode (Option (Vec FnEnt)) Int))
(pub fn (unhandledRelSpan tc d pathFound)
  (match pathFound
    ((None) 0)
    ((Some path)
      (if (== (vecLen path) 0)
        0
        (if (<= (vecLen path) 1)
          0
          {
            (let dd
              (declByName (tcAllDeclsVec tc) (fnEntName (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1))) 0))
            (if (let (Some dd) dd)
              (if (== (typeModuleSame (nodeModuleOpt dd) (nodeModuleOpt d)) 1)
                (nodeSpan dd)
                0)
              0)
          }))
    )))

; A String either way: `mkDiagFix2` reads this position only when the
; related SPAN is non-zero, which is the predicate `unhandledRelSpan`
; answers, so the empty string is never rendered.
(pub :: unhandledRelText (-> (Option (Vec FnEnt)) String))
(pub fn (unhandledRelText pathFound)
  (match pathFound
    ((None) "")
    ((Some path)
      (if (== (vecLen path) 0)
        ""
        (if (<= (vecLen path) 1)
          ""
          (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (fnEntName (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)))))
            "` performs it")))
    )))

; The witness's own module, when it is not `main`'s: the sentence that
; replaces a related span the renderer could not place, so the report
; still says WHERE without pointing at the wrong bytes.
(pub :: unhandledElsewhereText (-> TC ASTNode (Option (Vec FnEnt)) String))
(pub fn (unhandledElsewhereText tc d pathFound)
  (match pathFound
    ((None) "")
    ((Some path)
      (if (== (unhandledRelSpan tc d (Some path)) 0)
        (if (== (vecLen path) 0)
          ""
          (if (<= (vecLen path) 1)
            ""
            (concat (concat ", and `" (bareOf (fnEntName (vecGet path (- (vecLen path) 1)))))
              "` is what performs it")))
        "")
    )))

; AX3039 over the tags on `(effect ...)` declarations.
;
; `checkAxtags` runs under `tcWalkDecls`' TAG_D_FN arm and nowhere
; else, so before this walk existed a tag above an `effect` was parsed,
; attached to word 7, and read by nobody: `;@axiom:unhandld(trap)`
; checked OK, silently, and the effect kept the check it looked
; exempted from. That is the exact shape AX3039's own note calls worse
; than writing nothing - the tag reads like a guarantee and buys
; silence - so the key that made effect declarations worth tagging
; brings the typo check with it.
;
; The KEY only. The AXTAG namespace is open by decision, and an
; `effect` declaration is as free to carry metadata as a function is;
; what is checked here is the one slip that costs something.
(pub :: checkEffectDeclAxtags (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkEffectDeclAxtags tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EFFECT)
          (checkEffectDeclTags tc d (nodeAxtagsOf d) 0))
      }
      (checkEffectDeclAxtags tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; AX3077: a key the compiler checks, on a declaration it can't check it
; on. A tag belongs to the declaration after it, and `restrict(...)`,
; `effect(...)`, `pure`, `pre`, `post` and `isr` are checked on
; a function alone, `unhandled` on an `effect` alone, and `deprecated`
; on any declaration with a symbol row. Above a `data`,
; a `struct`, an import, a macro or an alias, the claim was recorded
; and nothing ever read it, so `;@axiom:restrict(no-alloc)` in the
; wrong place said nothing while looking like a guarantee. An unknown
; key stays legal: the namespace is open (`axtagKeyTypo`).
(pub :: checkStrayAxtags (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkStrayAxtags tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (isSome (nodeAxtagsOpt d))
          (strayAxtagsInUnit tc d))
      }
      (checkStrayAxtags tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; One declaration's tags, reported in that declaration's own unit. This
; pass runs in `tcNew`, before `tcWalkDecls` stamps word 14 for each
; declaration, so without the stamp a misplaced tag in an IMPORTED
; module rendered its caret against the entry file. The unit is put
; back for the passes that follow.
(pub :: strayAxtagsInUnit (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (strayAxtagsInUnit tc d)
  {
    (let was (tcCurUnit tc))
    (let _u (tcSetCurUnit tc (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) (nodeModuleOpt d))))
    (let _r (strayAxtagsOf tc d (nodeAxtagsOf d) 0))
    (tcSetCurUnit tc was)
    0
  })

(pub :: strayAxtagsOf (-> TC ASTNode Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (strayAxtagsOf tc d tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      {
        (let key (axtagKeyOf (vecGet tags i)))
        (if (&& (== (axtagIsPureSpelling key) 1)
              (&& (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_EFFECT) (== (axtagKeyFits (nodeTag d) "effect") 0)))
          (emitAxtagPureSpelling tc d key)
          (if (&& (== (axtagKnownKey key) 1) (== (axtagKeyFits (nodeTag d) key) 0))
            (emitAxtagStray tc d key)))
      }
      (strayAxtagsOf tc d tags (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: checkStrayImportAxtags (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (checkStrayImportAxtags tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_IMPORT) (isSome (nodeAxtagsOpt d)))
          (strayAxtagsOf tc d (nodeAxtagsOf d) 0))
      }
      (checkStrayImportAxtags tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

; 1 when a declaration of this kind reads the key. A declaration-macro
; invocation hands its tags to what it generates, so its own are
; checked there.
(pub :: axtagKeyFits (-> Int String Int))
(pub fn (axtagKeyFits tag key)
  (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_MACROCALL) (== tag TAG_D_SYNFOR))
      1
    ; `deprecated` is read off a declaration's symbol row, and an
    ; import, an `extern` block and a `subtype` have none.
    (strEq key "deprecated")
      (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_IMPORT) (|| (== tag TAG_D_EXTERN) (== tag TAG_D_SUBTYPE)))
        0
        1)
    (if (|| (== tag TAG_D_FN) (== tag TAG_D_SIG))
      (if (strEq key "unhandled")
        0
        1)
      (if (== tag TAG_D_EFFECT)
        (if (strEq key "unhandled")
          1
          0)
        0))))

(pub :: axtagDeclKind (-> Int String))
(pub fn (axtagDeclKind tag)
  (if (== tag TAG_D_DATA)
    "a `data` declaration"
    (if (== tag TAG_D_STRUCT)
      "a `struct`"
      (if (== tag TAG_FIELD)
        "a struct field, which takes only `effect(...)`"
        (if (== tag TAG_D_IMPORT)
          "an import"
          (if (== tag TAG_D_MACRO)
            "a macro"
            (if (== tag TAG_D_ALIAS)
                "a type alias"
              (== tag TAG_D_SUBTYPE)
                "a `subtype`"
              (if (== tag TAG_D_EFFECT)
                "an `effect` declaration"
                (if (== tag TAG_D_EXTERN)
                  "an `extern` block"
                  "a function")))))))))

(pub :: emitAxtagStray (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitAxtagStray tc d key)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (diagSetLabel
        (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3077" "axtag-misplaced" (nodeSpan d)
          (concat
            (concat (concat (concat (concat "the `" key) "` tag on `") (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
              "` is never checked: it is ")
            (axtagDeclKind (nodeTag d)))
          None
          (Some
            (if (strEq key "unhandled")
                "`unhandled(trap)` belongs above an `effect` declaration; move it there, or drop it"
              (strEq key "deprecated")
                "`deprecated(...)` belongs above a declaration `axiom symbols` lists: a function, a `data`, a `struct`, a type alias, a macro or an `effect`; move it there, or drop it"
              "a checked claim belongs above the function it describes - its `(:: ...)` or its `(fn ...)`; move it there, or drop it"))
          "the tag was recorded and nothing read it, so the claim it looks like was never made; an unrecognised key is still legal metadata")
        "tag not checked here"))))

(pub :: checkEffectDeclTags (-> TC ASTNode Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (checkEffectDeclTags tc d tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (axtagKeyTypo tc d (axtagKeyOf (vecGet tags i)))
      (checkEffectDeclTags tc d tags (+ i 1))
    }))

; The keys the compiler KNOWS, and nothing else. `suggestThreshold`
; is deliberately NOT reused: it scales with name length so a reader
; gets candidates for long identifiers, and at these lengths it admits
; distance-2, which pulls in words that are not slips.
;
; A KNOWN KEY IS NEVER A NEAR MISS, and the order of the two questions
; is what makes that true. `pre` is one insertion from `pure`, so the
; slip test alone answered "did you mean `pure`?" for every `pre` tag
; - measured 2026-08-29, before `pre` was in this set. The exact test
; runs first, so a key this compiler knows is silent here whatever its
; distance from another one it knows.
;
; `pre`, `post` and `restrict` are the three keys the Ada-inspired
; hardening introduces (`docs/reference.md`, AXTAG Keys). `restrict`
; is checked by `checkRestricts`. `pre` and `post` are known ahead of
; their check, so that a contract does not draw a false typo; until
; the contracts work lands they are recorded and not checked, exactly
; as an unknown key is.
;
; `unhandled` is the one key here that is written on an `(effect ...)`
; declaration rather than on a function, and it is read by
; `effectDeclTraps`: `;@axiom:unhandled(trap)` says an operation of
; this effect reaching no handler is a deliberate abort, which is what
; `AX3053` declines to report. A tag on a declaration no walk visits is
; a tag nothing can typo-check, so `checkEffectDeclAxtags` walks them -
; see its own note.
; The keys the COMPILER derives and writes into AXSYM. An AXTAG may
; not spell one, and that is not a narrowing of the open namespace -
; it is the line the openness always had.
;
; `checkOneAxtag`'s note says an unknown key "is metadata, it is
; recorded, and nothing checks it", and AX3039's page says a key of
; your own should be namespaced "so it cannot be mistaken for a
; claim". Both are about keys the compiler has nothing to say about.
; These are the opposite: `symbols.ax` writes them itself, from the
; checker's own answers, and a reader of the stream cannot tell an
; author's copy from the compiler's.
;
; MEASURED, 2026-09-04, before this existed. A file carrying
;
;     ;@axiom:effects(Pure)
;     ;@axiom:mir-result-fresh(1)
;
; on a body compiled clean, and `symbols` wrote
;
;     F liar ... #effects=Pure #mir-result-fresh=1
;
; The `effects` line drew AX3039 - as a TYPO of `effect`, which is the
; wrong reason and a warning. `mir-result-fresh` drew nothing at all,
; because it is not one slip from any key the compiler checks, and the
; near-miss rule is the only thing that had ever looked at a key.
;
; THE ORDERING RULE DOES NOT COVER THIS, and `smTagMetas`'s note
; overstates it. Compiler keys are pushed after the author's so
; `symTagFrom` answers the last one - which holds only where the
; compiler emits that key on that row. It emits no `#effects=` for a
; body with no effects and no `#mir-*` at all for a program that names
; no region, so on exactly the rows where a forged guarantee is worth
; forging there is no later key to win. The ordering is a second line
; of defence; this is the first.
;
; AN ERROR, like AX3052, AX3054 and AX3039. The argument is
; AX3052's verbatim: accepting it would let a tag read like a
; guarantee and buy silence. `restrict`, `pure`, `effect`, `pre`,
; `post` and `unhandled` are the author's to write and are not
; here - they are CHECKED, which is the difference.
(pub :: axtagReservedKey (-> String Int))
(pub fn (axtagReservedKey key)
  (if (strEq key "effects")
    1
    (if (strEq key "effect-params")
      1
      (if (strEq key "effects-incomplete")
        1
        (if (strEq key "effects-overapprox")
          1
          (if (strEq key "effects-possible")
            1
            (if (strEq key "ctors")
              1
              (if (strEq key "of")
                1
                (if (strEq key "fields")
                  1
                  (if (strEq key "tyvars")
                    1
                    (if (strEq key "methods")
                      1
                      (if (strEq key "generated")
                        1
                        (if (strEq key "calls")
                          1
                          ; `symbols` writes `#unsafe=trusted` from the
                          ; tags it read (R-B6), and an author's copy
                          ; would read as the compiler's verdict on the
                          ; trusted set.
                          (if (strEq key "unsafe")
                            1
                            ; `#erasures=` is the checker's count of a
                            ; body's erasing casts (`eraseSettle`).
                            (if (strEq key "erasures")
                              1
                              ; `#field-effects=` is the row each declared
                              ; field is charged (`fieldEffectsMeta`).
                              (if (strEq key "field-effects")
                                1
                                (axtagReservedMir key)))))))))))))))))

; Split out rather than continued inline: the `#mir-*` set is one
; workstream's and moves with it, and a reader adding a seventh key
; there should not have to count sixteen closing parens to find where
; it goes.
(pub :: axtagReservedMir (-> String Int))
(pub fn (axtagReservedMir key)
  (if (strEq key "mir-params")
    1
    (if (strEq key "mir-escapes")
      1
      (if (strEq key "mir-result-fresh")
        1
        (if (strEq key "mir-result-from")
          1
          (if (strEq key "mir-incomplete")
            1
            (if (strEq key "mir-truncated")
              1
              0)))))))

(pub :: emitAxtagKeyReserved (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitAxtagKeyReserved tc d key)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3065" "axtag-key-reserved" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat (concat (concat (concat "AXTAG key `" key) "` on `") (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
          "` is a key the compiler derives and writes itself")
        None
        (Some
          "the AXTAG namespace is open, but not for the keys `axiom symbols` writes from the checker's own answers: a reader of that stream cannot tell an author's copy from the compiler's. Namespace it - `;@axiom:my:effects(...)` - or drop it")))))

(pub :: axtagKnownKey (-> String Int))
(pub fn (axtagKnownKey key)
  ; `deprecated(...)` is read: each use of the name draws AX3048, and
  ; `scripts/check-compat.sh` lets a release retire it.
  (if (strEq key "deprecated")
      1
    (strEq key "effect")
      1
    (strEq key "scoped")
      1
    (strEq key "compute")
      1
    (if (strEq key "pre")
      1
      (if (strEq key "post")
        1
        (if (strEq key "restrict")
          1
          (if (strEq key "unhandled")
            1
            ; `isr` is checked (an implied `no-alloc` plus an
            ; arity rule), so a near miss of it is a guarantee
            ; that buys silence, exactly as for the others.
            (if (strEq key "isr")
              1
              ; `syscall(...)` seeds a syscall number's own effect
              ; row (`syscallTagEffects`), so a slip of it would
              ; drop an effect every caller of the number owes.
              (if (strEq key "syscall")
                1
                ; `ct(...)` is checked (AX3092), so a slip of it
                ; would publish a constant-time promise nobody
                ; checked.
                (if (strEq key "ct")
                  1
                  ; `result(owned)` is read by codegen (MM-LIFE-2n), so
                  ; a slip of it would leak every call silently.
                  (if (strEq key "result")
                    1
                    0))))))))))

(pub :: axtagNearMiss (-> String (Option String)))
(pub fn (axtagNearMiss key)
  (if (== (axtagKnownKey key) 1)
      None
    (== (axtagSlip key "scoped") 1)
      (Some "scoped")
    (== (axtagSlip key "compute") 1)
      (Some "compute")
    (== (axtagSlip key "deprecated") 1)
      (Some "deprecated")
    (if (== (axtagSlip key "effect") 1)
      (Some "effect")
      (if (== (axtagSlip key "pre") 1)
        (Some "pre")
        (if (== (axtagSlip key "post") 1)
          (Some "post")
          (if (== (axtagSlip key "restrict") 1)
            (Some "restrict")
            (if (== (axtagSlip key "unhandled") 1)
              (Some "unhandled")
              (if (== (axtagSlip key "isr") 1)
                (Some "isr")
                (if (== (axtagSlip key "syscall") 1)
                  (Some "syscall")
                  (if (== (axtagSlip key "ct") 1)
                    (Some "ct")
                    (if (== (axtagSlip key "result") 1)
                      (Some "result")
                      None)))))))))))

(pub :: axtagSlip (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (axtagSlip key known)
  (if (strEq key known)
    0
    (if (== (strEqCI key known) 1)
      1
      (if (<= (levenshtein key known) 1)
        1
        (axtagSwapped key known)))))

(pub :: emitAxtagKeyTypo (-> TC ASTNode String String Int))
; The message leads with the DECLARATION, not with the key, because
; the span covers the declaration. `tests/lsp/drive.py` reads the first
; backticked name out of a message and requires the source at exactly
; that range to spell it - so a message opening `pur` over a span
; covering `pure` is a diagnostic pointing one place and naming
; another. Every other AXTAG code here already leads with the name it
; spans (AX3010's "AXTAG mismatch on `x`"); this one did not, and no
; fixture had exercised it through the server to notice.
(pub fn (emitAxtagKeyTypo tc d key want)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3039" "axtag-key-typo" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat
          (concat
            (concat
              (concat (concat "AXTAG key typo on `" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "`: unrecognised key `")
              key)
            "`; did you mean `")
          (concat want "`?"))
        None
        (Some
          "an unrecognised key is recorded and never checked, so the claim this looks like was not made. Fix the spelling, or namespace a key of your own, such as `;@axiom:my:KEY(...)`, so it cannot be mistaken for a claim")))))

; The last closing delimiter at or before the cursor, when present.
(pub :: axtagCloseAt (-> String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (axtagCloseAt content)
  (axtagCloseFrom content (- (strLen content) 1)))

(pub :: axtagCloseFrom (-> String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (axtagCloseFrom content i)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (>= i (strLen content)))
    None
    (if (== (strByte content i) 41)
      (Some i)
      (axtagCloseFrom content (- i 1)))))

; ASCII case-insensitive equality, for matching `effect(console)`
; against a declared `Console`.
(pub :: strEqCI (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (strEqCI a b)
  (if (!= (strLen a) (strLen b))
    0
    (strEqCIFrom a b 0)))

(pub :: strEqCIFrom (-> String String Int Int))
(pub fn (strEqCIFrom a b i)
  (if (>= i (strLen a))
    1
    (if (== (lowerByte (strByte a i)) (lowerByte (strByte b i)))
      (strEqCIFrom a b (+ i 1))
      0)))

(pub :: lowerByte (-> Int Int))
(pub fn (lowerByte c)
  (if (&& (>= c 65) (<= c 90))
    (+ c 32)
    c))

; 1 when `a` is `b` with two neighbouring bytes swapped, `restirct` for
; `restrict`. Levenshtein counts a swap as two edits, so the distance
; test in `axtagSlip` misses the commonest slip of the hand.
(pub :: axtagSwapped (-> String String Int))
(pub fn (axtagSwapped a b)
  (if (!= (strLen a) (strLen b))
    0
    (axtagSwappedAt a b 0)))

(pub :: axtagSwappedAt (-> String String Int Int))
(pub fn (axtagSwappedAt a b i)
  (if (>= (+ i 1) (strLen a))
    0
    (if (== (strByte a i) (strByte b i))
      (axtagSwappedAt a b (+ i 1))
      (if (&& (== (strByte a i) (strByte b (+ i 1))) (== (strByte a (+ i 1)) (strByte b i)))
        (axtagSameFrom a b (+ i 2))
        0))))

(pub :: axtagSameFrom (-> String String Int Int))
(pub fn (axtagSameFrom a b i)
  (if (>= i (strLen a))
    1
    (if (== (strByte a i) (strByte b i))
      (axtagSameFrom a b (+ i 1))
      0)))

; A `pure` claim is contradicted by ANY concrete effect.
(pub :: axtagContradicted (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Int))
(pub fn (axtagContradicted tc d label eff)
  ; A POSSIBLE EFFECT CANNOT CONTRADICT ANYTHING, and this is the arm
  ; `docs/agent-harness.md` §6 named as the reason `AX3010` could not
  ; be promoted: it fired on `(fn (handoff k) shout)`, which performs
  ; nothing and merely hands `shout` back.
  ;
  ; `effRefSite` unions a referent's effects, which is EXACT for a
  ; nullary referent - this language invokes `vecNew` and `__argc` by
  ; writing their names - and an over-approximation for one that takes
  ; arguments, where a bare name is a value. That over-approximation is
  ; load-bearing and is NOT removed: `AX3011` needs it, because a
  ; `handle` list must name every effect the body can reach and
  ; `(handle (apply ask 1) (IO) ...)` reaches `Ask` only through the
  ; bare `ask`. Measured - dropping it turned that hard error into an
  ; `AX3038` warning, which is the wrong direction for a check whose
  ; whole job is refusing an inexhaustive list.
  ;
  ; So the two consumers get different answers from the same walk.
  ; `AX3011` keeps the upper bound. This arm, which accuses the AUTHOR
  ; of writing a false claim, accuses on DEFINITE effects and on
  ; nothing else: a row holding only possible ones, or only the
  ; incompleteness marker, is `AX3037` - the diagnostic that already
  ; means "cannot be checked". Until 2026-08-29 a row-global marker
  ; made the whole row unverifiable the moment any effect in it was
  ; possible, so a body that printed a `{hole}` AND wrote to stdout
  ; under `pure` drew a warning where the `writeStr` is a plain lie.
  (if (== (effHasDefinite eff) 1)
    (emitAxtag tc d (concat (concat label " contradicted: body performs ") (effJoinDefinite eff)))
    ; Nothing definite contradicts the claim - which acquits it only
    ; when the walk resolved every call it met and named nothing it
    ; could not follow. Where it did not, the empty set is the
    ; analysis's silence and not the body's, and reading one as the
    ; other is exactly how `((b.f) x)` passed as `pure` while it
    ; wrote to stdout.
    ;
    ; The two shapes differ in what would answer them, so they say
    ; different things: a call the walk could not resolve, or effects
    ; that are only POSSIBLE - a function the body names without
    ; calling, or a field whose stored functions disagree.
    (if (== (effUnresolved eff) 1)
      (emitAxtagUnverifiable tc d label)
      (if (== (effAnyPossibleOnly eff) 1)
        (emitAxtagPossible tc d label eff)
        0))))

; The possible-only entries of a row, in their definite spelling.
(pub :: effPossibleOnly (-> Strings Strings))
(pub fn (effPossibleOnly v)
  {
    (let out vecNew)
    {
      (effPossibleOnlyInto out v 0)
      out
    }
  })

(pub :: effPossibleOnlyInto (-> Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (effPossibleOnlyInto out v i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    {
      {
        (let e (vecGetStr v i))
        (if (&& (== (effIsPossible e) 1) (== (effIn v (effDefiniteOf e)) 0))
          (effAdd out (effDefiniteOf e)))
      }
      (effPossibleOnlyInto out v (+ i 1))
    }))

; AX3037 for a row with no definite effect and some possible one.
(pub :: emitAxtagPossible (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Int))
(pub fn (emitAxtagPossible tc d label eff)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_WARNING "AX3037" "axtag-unverifiable" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat
          (concat (concat (concat "AXTAG unverifiable on `" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "`: ") label)
          (concat " cannot be checked: the body may perform "
            (concat (effJoin (effPossibleOnly eff))
              ", through a function it names without calling or a field whose stored functions do not all perform it")))
        None
        (Some
          "store only functions that keep the claim in the field, call the function rather than handing it on, or drop the claim")))))

; A claimed effect the body does not perform. stage0 suppresses this
; when the effect COULD arrive through a callback - but only when the
; function has effect-transparent parameters, and this check only
; runs when it has none (the completeness gate), so every missing
; claim here is one stage0 also reports.
(pub :: axtagUnsupported (-> TC ASTNode String Strings Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (axtagUnsupported tc d val eff eparams lex)
  {
    (let want (axtagEffectOf tc val))
    (if (== (strLen want) 0)
      0
      (if (strEq want (builtinEff "Unsafe"))
        (axtagUnsafeUnsupported tc d lex)
        (axtagUnsupportedRow tc d val want eff eparams)))
  })

; `effect(unsafe)` is answered by what the body DOES, not by its row.
;
; `Unsafe` is required lexically (`MM-EXEC-9d`): a declaration that
; performs an unsafe operation itself says so, and a trusted callee's
; `Unsafe` ends at that callee. So no callback, no unresolved call and
; no callee can supply the claim to this declaration - an untagged
; callee that performs one draws AX3073 where it does. Whether the
; body performs one is the same three-way scan AX3073 makes, and it is
; decidable however open the row is.
;
; The row used to decide, through the suppression below: a function
; with an effect parameter, or a row marked `#effects-incomplete`,
; was never asked, so a claim that guarded nothing drew neither
; AX3010 nor AX3037 on a higher-order function. Nineteen such claims
; were found by hand and dropped before this rule existed.
(pub :: axtagUnsafeUnsupported (-> TC ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (axtagUnsafeUnsupported tc d lex)
  (if (== lex 1)
    0
    (emitAxtag tc d
      "`effect(unsafe)` claim unsupported: the body performs no unsafe operation itself")))

; Is there an unsafe operation in THIS body: a raw primitive
; (`unsafeScanInto`), a reference to an extern (`sink`, filled by the
; effect walk), or a forging cast (`forges`)? The three AX3073 names.
(pub :: unsafeLexical (-> ASTNode (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (unsafeLexical e sink forges)
  (if (> (vecLen sink) 0)
    1
    (if (> (vecLen forges) 0)
      1
      (if (> (vecLen (unsafeScanInto e vecNew)) 0)
        1
        0))))

; Every claimed effect but `Unsafe`, answered from the row.
(pub :: axtagUnsupportedRow (-> TC ASTNode String String Strings Ints Int))
(pub fn (axtagUnsupportedRow tc d val want eff eparams)
  ; `Div` is NEVER INFERRED, so a claim of it is answered before the
  ; row is read: no body, no callback and no unresolved call can ever
  ; supply it (`emitAxtagNeverChecked`).
  (if (== (effNeverInferred want) 1)
    (emitAxtagNeverChecked tc d val)
    ; `effMay`: a claimed effect the body MAY perform - through a
    ; value it named, or a dispatch the walk could not resolve - is
    ; not missing. The claim reads the upper bound, as AX3011 does.
    (if (== (effMay eff want) 1)
      0
      ; stage0 suppresses a claimed-but-absent effect when a
      ; CALLBACK could still supply it: the function has
      ; effect-transparent parameters and the effect names
      ; something that exists. An effect no declaration
      ; introduces can never arrive that way, so the
      ; suppression must not swallow it.
      ; ... and an UNRESOLVED call can supply it just as a callback
      ; can. Before the sentinel had a producer this branch reported
      ; `claim unsupported: missing IO` against a body that reached
      ; IO through a field-held function - a mis-report rather than
      ; an omission, which is the one failure this whole check is
      ; documented as not permitted to make.
      (if (&& (== (axtagCouldExist tc want) 1) (|| (> (vecLen eparams) 0) (== (effPartial eff) 1)))
        0
        (emitAxtag tc d
          (concat (concat (concat "`effect(" val) ")` claim unsupported: missing ")
            (effRender want)))))))

; The built-in effect name NOTHING PRODUCES. `Div` is spellable - a
; handle list may write it, and it is not a custom effect - and the walk
; has no producer for it: measured over `core/` and `stdlib/`, ZERO
; of 4,645 rows carry it.
;
; `Err` WAS THE OTHER ONE and is no longer a built-in effect name at all
; (`AX3054`'s commit, 2026-08-30): nothing inferred it, and its own tag
; spelling could not even reach it, so a handle list naming it now draws
; `AX3016` like any other undeclared name.
;
; So `missing Div` was never a fact about the body. Keep this list
; honest: an effect that gains a producer must LEAVE it, or
; a real mismatch starts reporting as unverifiable.
(pub :: effNeverInferred (-> String Int))
(pub fn (effNeverInferred want)
  (if (strEq want (builtinEff "Div"))
    1
    0))

; AX3110: a claim of an effect nothing in the compiler infers. It is
; not a limit a better-placed call would lift, which is what AX3037
; reports: no edit to the body can make it answerable, so the claim is
; a guarantee nothing will ever back, and a reader of `symbols` or the
; tag would spend trust on it. It was AX3037, a warning, until a claim
; the compiler cannot back stopped being allowed to pass as one. The
; intent has places that do not read as a guarantee: a comment, or a
; key of the author's own (`my:div`), which no check reads.
;
; Inferring `Div` is not the fix. The cheapest sound rule (a self-call,
; or any `while`) marks 65% of this compiler divergent and calls the
; syntax zoo's own counted loop non-terminating - measured, and
; recorded in `docs/reference.md`.
(pub :: emitAxtagNeverChecked (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitAxtagNeverChecked tc d val)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3110" "axtag-never-checked" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat (concat (concat "AXTAG never checked on `" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "`: ")
          (concat (concat "`effect(" val)
            ")` names an effect nothing in the compiler infers, so no body can support the claim"))
        None
        (Some
          "keep the intent in a comment, or in a key of your own such as `;@axiom:my:div`, which no check reads. Divergence is not inferred: the cheapest sound rule - a self-call, or any `while` - marks most of a real program non-terminating")))))

(pub :: axtagCouldExist (-> TC String Int))
(pub fn (axtagCouldExist tc e)
  (if (strEq (strSlice e 0 2) "b:")
    1
    (if (isNone (findEffectDecl tc (effRender e)))
      0
      1)))

; `effect(io)` names the built-in IO; a value naming no builtin is a
; custom effect, matched against the declarations case-insensitively
; and canonicalised to the DECLARED spelling - stage0's rule, and the
; reason `effect(console)` validates against `(effect Console ...)`.
(pub :: axtagEffectOf (-> TC String String))
(pub fn (axtagEffectOf tc val)
  (if (strEq val "io")
    (builtinEff "IO")
    (if (strEq val "mut")
      (builtinEff "Mut")
      (if (strEq val "div")
        (builtinEff "Div")
        (if (strEq val "alloc")
          (builtinEff "Alloc")
          (if (strEq val "unsafe")
            (builtinEff "Unsafe")
            (if (strEq val "entropy")
              (builtinEff "Entropy")
              (if (strEq val "spawn")
                (builtinEff "Spawn")
                (if (strEq val "block")
                  (builtinEff "Block")
                  (if (== (strLen val) 0)
                    ""
                    (axtagCustomOrBuiltinCI tc val)))))))))))

; A value that is not one of the five lowercase built-in spellings, in
; the order the two possibilities have to be tried.
;
; DECLARATIONS FIRST. `canonicalEffectName` already matches them
; case-insensitively, which is what makes `effect(console)` validate
; against `(effect Console ...)`. A program may declare `(effect Io
; ...)` of its own, and `;@axiom:effect(Io)` on a body performing it
; builds clean today; resolving the built-in first would swallow that
; and report a missing `IO` the body never performs.
;
; ONLY THEN the built-in, case-folded. `;@axiom:effect(IO)` used to
; become a CUSTOM effect named IO, which no declaration introduces and
; the walk never produces, so the claim could not be satisfied by any
; program - and it was reported as `claim unsupported: missing IO`,
; a message textually identical to the built-in failure. The author saw
; a claim about IO refused against a body that performs IO, with
; nothing in the message naming the case as the reason. An agent
; writing a tag is the likeliest author of that spelling, which is why
; this is the direction the fix goes rather than a new diagnostic.
;
; The only inputs whose meaning changes are the ones that today resolve
; to a custom effect NO declaration introduces - exactly the set that
; produced the unsatisfiable claim.
(pub :: axtagCustomOrBuiltinCI (-> TC String String))
(pub fn (axtagCustomOrBuiltinCI tc val)
  {
    (let canon (canonicalEffectName tc val))
    (if (isSome (findEffectDecl tc canon))
      (customEff canon)
      (if (== (strEqCI val "io") 1)
        (builtinEff "IO")
        (if (== (strEqCI val "mut") 1)
          (builtinEff "Mut")
          (if (== (strEqCI val "div") 1)
            (builtinEff "Div")
            (if (== (strEqCI val "alloc") 1)
              (builtinEff "Alloc")
              (if (== (strEqCI val "unsafe") 1)
                (builtinEff "Unsafe")
                (if (== (strEqCI val "entropy") 1)
                  (builtinEff "Entropy")
                  (if (== (strEqCI val "spawn") 1)
                    (builtinEff "Spawn")
                    (if (== (strEqCI val "block") 1)
                      (builtinEff "Block")
                      (customEff canon))))))))))
  })

(pub :: canonicalEffectName (-> TC String String))
(pub fn (canonicalEffectName tc val)
  (if (isSome (findEffectDecl tc val))
    val
    (if (let (Some declaration) (findEffectDeclCI (tcEffectsVec tc) val 0))
      (nodeAName declaration)
      val)))

(pub :: findEffectDeclCI (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (findEffectDeclCI effs val i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen effs))
    None
    {
      (let dd (vecGet effs i))
      (if (== (strEqCI (nodeAName dd) val) 1)
        (Some dd)
        (findEffectDeclCI effs val (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: emitAxtag (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitAxtag tc d msg)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3010" "axtag-mismatch" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat (concat (concat (concat "AXTAG mismatch on `" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "`: ") msg)
          "")
        None
        (Some
          "an AXTAG is a CLAIM, and this is the compiler answering it: make the body match the tag, or correct the tag to what the body does. Deleting the tag also silences this, and an untagged function is never checked - but that WITHDRAWS the claim rather than answering it, and every reader that trusted the tag loses the guarantee. A claim the walk cannot check is AX3037 and stays a warning; this code fires only where the walk has an answer")))))

; AX3037: a claim the walk is not in a position to check. Distinct
; from AX3010 on purpose - "your tag is wrong" and "your tag cannot be
; checked here" are different facts, and a reader acting on them does
; different things: correct the tag, or resolve the call. AX3033 took
; a new code over sharing AX3020 for the same reason.
;
; A WARNING - and since 2026-08-25 that is the DIFFERENCE from
; AX3010 rather than a property shared with it. What this reports is a
; limit of the analysis and not a defect in the program, so it informs
; where AX3010 refuses; the repository has one diagnostic of that kind
; already (AX3021) whose text says so out loud.
(pub :: emitAxtagUnverifiable (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitAxtagUnverifiable tc d label)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_WARNING "AX3037" "axtag-unverifiable" (nodeSpan d)
        (concat (concat (concat "AXTAG unverifiable on `" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "`: ")
          (concat label
            " cannot be checked: the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve"))
        None
        (Some
          "name the function at the call, or drop the claim: an unresolved call may perform anything")))))

(pub :: tcCheckFn (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (tcCheckFn tc d)
  ; Set BEFORE the entry lookup: `nodeA d` is already this module's
  ; mangled name, so the lookup is unaffected, but every reference the
  ; body makes below resolves module-locally.
  {
    (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
    (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
    (let params (nodeBNames d))
    (let base (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
    (let sigTy
      (:: (match entFound
        ((None) None)
        ((Some entry) (bsTypeOpt (fnEntTy entry)))) (Option ASTNode)))
    {
      (tcSetFrameBase tc base)
      ; The current function's parameters and their callability, for
      ; any handle site inside the body to seed its collector scope
      ; with. Without them a call through a parameter inside a handle
      ; would look like a call to an unknown global.
      (tcSetCurParams tc params)
      (tcSetCurCallable tc (paramCallablesOf sigTy (vecLen params)))
      ; MM-LIFE-2d: the body's evidence context - this function's
      ; own ordered tyvars (what a symbolic witness indexes into)
      ; and the declaration every stamp it writes is tagged with.
      {
        (let ptys vecNew)
        {
          (tcSetCurDeclNode tc (Some d))
          (tcSetCurSigVars tc
            (if (let (Some signature) sigTy)
              (if (== (tyHasSrcVar signature) 1)
                {
                  (let sv vecNew)
                  (sigTyVarsInto signature sv)
                  (if (== (tyHasPoison signature) 1)
                    vecNew
                    sv)
                }
                vecNew)
              vecNew))
          (refuseReservedParams tc params (nodeSpan d) 0)
          (checkDupParams tc d params 1)
          (bindFnParams tc params ptys sigTy 0)
          (let state (tcCheckState tc))
          (set state.unsignedParams (if (if (let (Some signature) sigTy)
            (&& (== (nodeTag signature) TAG_T_VAR) (strStartsWith (nodeAName signature) "_fn_"))
            false)
            ptys
            vecNew))
          ; A LAMBDA BODY IS CHECKED AGAINST THE DECLARED RESULT, for the
          ; reason `checkHandler` gives one line for one: `bindLamParams`
          ; binds every parameter to a FRESH TYPE VARIABLE, and
          ; `checkLamAgainst` binds it to the type the author wrote.
          ;
          ; That difference is memory safety, not precision. MM-LIFE-2g
          ; says a type-erasing store SHALL take a share; the share is
          ; stamped from MM-LIFE-2d's evidence word, and a MINTED
          ; placeholder is what `evClassOf` answers -1 to - no
          ; `__retainref` is emitted at all, for a parameter that is a
          ; reference. Measured at `07ee175`, a factory returning a
          ; logging closure over a struct field, the field then
          ; reassigned: the log reads back a string allocated after it -
          ; 122, `z` - at every optimisation level, with `check`
          ; printing OK. The same program with a named `fn` in place of
          ; the closure answers 97. `tests/stdlib/460-closure-reclaim.ax`
          ; term 128 is that program.
          ;
          ; `checkLamAgainst` had exactly two callers before this and
          ; both were the handler at a `handle`, which is why an inline
          ; handler was always sound and every other lambda was not.
          ;
          ; WHAT THIS DOES NOT REACH, because a narrow fix that reads
          ; like a general one is worse than none. The guard below is
          ; syntactic: the body must BE a lambda and the declared result
          ; must BE an arrow. Every other position where a lambda can
          ; sit has no declaration to bind against - and use-driven
          ; inference (grounding) is what reaches those now: a
          ; lambda's parameters ARE resolved by use, bound to the
          ; argument types each application carries, and a lambda no
          ; use witnesses is flushed rather than guessed. Measured
          ; before grounding landed, all reading 122 at `--opt 0` and
          ; `--opt 3` alike: a lambda bound in a `let` inside the
          ; factory; one returned from an `if`; one bound and applied
          ; in `main` with no declaration anywhere; one passed to a
          ; declared arrow PARAMETER; and one whose factory's declared
          ; result arrow carries a type variable rather than a ground
          ; type.
          ;
          ; The LAST of those is still open: it is emitted correctly
          ; and still under-retains, because nothing at the factory's
          ; call site witnesses the variable and the caller passes 0.
          ; It needs an evidence word contributed at the closure's
          ; APPLICATION rather than at its construction, which
          ; `docs/memory-model.md` MM-LIFE-2g states as the open half
          ; - grounding resolves the TYPES by use, and the evidence
          ; word does not reach the application site today.
          ; A lambda body checked against the declared arrow
          ; never passes the defer either, so it is validated
          ; here, once, exactly as a literal handler is.
          ; A pending body is inferred standalone NOW: the
          ; callers, the declared-return check, and the
          ; recorded arrow below all require a TYPE, never a
          ; pending node.
          {
            (let resTy
              (:: (match sigTy
                ((None) None)
                ((Some signature) (peelArrows signature (vecLen params)))) (Option ASTNode)))
            {
              (let bodyTy
                {
                  (let bodyTy0
                    (match resTy
                      ((Some resTy)
                        (if (&& (!= (nodeC d) 0)
                              (&& (== (nodeTag (nodeCChild d)) TAG_E_LAM)
                                (== (nodeTag resTy) TAG_T_ARR)))
                          {
                            (checkLamValidate tc (nodeCChild d))
                            (checkLamAgainst tc (nodeCChild d) resTy)
                          }
                          (checkExprH tc (nodeCChild d) 0))
                      )
                      ((None) (checkExprH tc (nodeCChild d) 0))))
                  (if (== (isPendingNode bodyTy0) 1)
                    (groundStandalone tc bodyTy0 (spanOf (nodeCChild d)))
                    bodyTy0)
                })
              {
                (if (let (Some resultType) resTy)
                  (checkDeclaredReturn tc d resultType
                    (maybeWrapSubtypeReturn tc d resultType bodyTy)))
                ; The contracts, while the frame is still standing: the
                ; parameters are bound above `base` and `scopeTrunc`
                ; below takes them away, so `checkAxtags` - which runs
                ; after this whole function - has no scope to check a
                ; `pre` against. None means no result type remains after
                ; the signature's parameter spine, so a `post` cannot
                ; name `result` there. Some keeps poison and inference types.
                (checkContracts tc d resTy)
                (emitUnsignedSignature tc)
                (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                  (if (&& (! (nodeSame (fnEntTy ent) nilNode))
                        (== (nodeTag (fnEntTy ent)) TAG_T_VAR))
                    (fnEntSetTy ent (ctorArrowOf ptys bodyTy (- (vecLen ptys) 1)))
                    ())
                  ())
                (scopeTrunc (tcScopeVec tc) base)
                (set state.unsignedParams vecNew)
                ; Every pin this body makes has been made.
                (evSettle tc)
                (tcSetCurModule tc None)
                (tcSetCurSigVars tc vecNew)
                (tcSetCurDeclNode tc None)
              }
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }
    0
  })

; One name, twice, in one `fn`'s parameter list: AX3006, the code for
; two definitions of one name in one scope - which a parameter list
; is. This checked OK until 2026-09-27 and the emitter wrote
; `define i64 @f(i64 %x, i64 %x)`, which `llc` refuses as a
; redefinition of argument, so `build` failed at the toolchain (exit
; 4) on a program `check` had called well-typed; found by the seeded
; fuzzer (`tests/fuzz/dup-param-llc.axfuzz`). The checker bound both
; and the body read the LAST, while the emitter's by-name parameter
; lookups answer the FIRST - two readings of one reference, so the
; refusal is the only sound answer and not merely the cheap one.
;
; `_` is not exempt. In a `fn`'s parameter list it is a name like any
; other - `(fn (f _) _)` answers its argument - so `(fn (f _ _) 0)`
; is the same collision, and it died in `llc` the same way. A LAMBDA
; is a different case: the parser curries `(lambda (x x) x)` into two
; nested one-parameter lambdas before this pass sees it, so there the
; second `x` shadows the first and the emitted code agrees.
;
; Parameters carry no span of their own, so the report anchors at the
; function's name and names the parameter in the message. Reported
; once per repeated name, at its second occurrence.
(pub :: checkDupParams (-> TC ASTNode Names Int Int))
(pub fn (checkDupParams tc d params i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen params))
    0
    {
      (let p (vecGetStr params i))
      {
        (if (== (strCountIn params p 0 i) 1)
          (emitDiag tc
            (diagSetLabel
              (mkDiagNote SEV_ERROR "AX3006" "duplicate-definition" (nodeSpan d)
                (concat
                  (concat
                    (concat (concat "duplicate parameter `" (srcSpelling p))
                      "` in the definition of `")
                    (srcSpelling (bareOf (nodeAName d))))
                  "`")
                None
                (Some
                  "give each parameter its own name; an unused one can be `_a`, `_b` - `_` is a name here like any other, so two of them collide too")
                "the body could only ever read one of them, and the emitted function declared both, which the native toolchain refused after `check` said OK")
              (concat (concat "`" (srcSpelling p)) "` names two parameters of this function"))))
        (checkDupParams tc d params (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; How many of `v`'s first `end` entries equal `s`.
(pub :: strCountIn (-> Names String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (strCountIn v s i end)
  (if (>= i end)
    0
    (+
      (if (strEq (vecGetStr v i) s)
        1
        0)
      (strCountIn v s (+ i 1) end))))

; The signature's result type: its arrows peeled by as many
; parameters as the definition has. None when the signature runs out of
; arrows first, which is the case this cannot say anything about.
(pub :: peelArrows (-> ASTNode Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (peelArrows t n)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    None
    (if (<= n 0)
      (Some t)
      (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_ARR)
        None
        (peelArrows (nodeBChild t) (- n 1))))))

; A function's body against its declared result. The comparison is
; the one an argument gets, `tyCompat`, for every declared result,
; `Int` included (MM-VAL-24). A signature believed over a body that
; disagrees with it is a miscompile in whichever direction it runs:
;
;   (:: g (-> Int Box))  (fn (g x) 7)           reads 7 as a header
;   (:: f (-> Int Float))  (fn (f x) 42)        reads 42 as a double
;   (:: h (-> Int Int))  (fn (h x) (Bx "s"))    erases a counted block
;
; The last is the one `Int` used to be exempt from. A `Box` returned
; through `Int` leaves the caller holding a word the release walk
; never follows, so the block's share is never given back: 48 bytes
; held per call, measured. A function that means to hand out a word
; for a reference says `(cast Int b)`, and one that wants identity
; uses `Mem.addrOf`.
;
; The arms, in order:
;
; - A pending body (a lambda nothing has grounded) is refused only
;   where a ground non-arrow type is declared: a function where a
;   value goes.
; - Poison and the silent wildcard say nothing.
; - A declared SOURCE variable promises the caller a type the caller
;   chooses, so the body may answer only that same variable.
; - A minted `_tN` body means "not known" and stays quiet. A source
;   variable body is known, and meets the declaration like any type.
; - A declared arrow over a non-arrow body is a shape error: the call
;   site would load a code pointer out of a value.
; - Everything else meets `declResultCompat`, part by part through an
;   arrow, so a lambda answering `Box` under `(-> Int (-> Int Int))`
;   is refused at its result.
;
; `(cast Box n)` is the explicit escape for the reverse direction,
; minting a nominal value out of a word, and it is unsafe (AX3073).
(pub :: checkDeclaredReturn (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkDeclaredReturn tc d want got)
  (if (nodeSame want nilNode)
    0
    (if (nodeSame got nilNode)
      0
      ; A function declared to answer `()` drops its body's value: a
      ; procedure ends where its work does, with no value written out.
      (if (&& (tyIsUnit want)
            (&& (! (tyIsUnit got)) (&& (== (tyIsErr got) 0) (== (isPendingNode got) 0))))
        (discardToUnit tc (nodeCChild d) got)
        ; `got` is all but never pending here - the tail infers a
        ; pending body standalone before this runs - but a path that
        ; bypassed the tail must still judge it: where an arrow or a
        ; variable is declared nothing is refused (a factory; a
        ; generic), and where a ground type is declared it is a shape
        ; error - a function where a value goes - refusing audibly
        ; without ever rendering the node.
        (if (== (isPendingNode got) 1)
          (if (|| (== (nodeTag want) TAG_T_ARR) (== (tyIsGround want) 0))
            0
            (refusePendingOperand tc got (tyRender want) (spanOf (nodeCChild d))))
          (if (== (tyIsErr got) 1)
            0
            (if (== (tyIsSilentWild got) 1)
              0
              (if (== (tyIsSrcVar want) 1)
                ; A signature promising a type VARIABLE promises the CALLER a
                ; type the caller chooses, so the body may answer only that same
                ; variable. A different one, or any concrete type, breaks the
                ; promise the same way the `Float`-over-`Int` case does: every
                ; reader downstream believes the signature. A minted `_tN`
                ; body stays quiet, because it means "not known" rather than
                ; "known to be wrong".
                ;
                ;   (:: f (-> a b a))  (fn (f x y) y)   was accepted, answered y
                (if (== (nodeTag got) TAG_T_VAR)
                  (if (== (tyIsSrcVar got) 1)
                    (if (strEq (nodeAName want) (nodeAName got))
                      0
                      (emitMismatchTy tc want got (spanOf (nodeCChild d))))
                    0)
                  (emitMismatchTy tc want got (spanOf (nodeCChild d))))
                (if (&& (== (nodeTag got) TAG_T_VAR) (== (tyIsSrcVar got) 0))
                  0
                  (if (&& (== (nodeTag want) TAG_T_ARR) (!= (nodeTag got) TAG_T_ARR))
                    (if (== (nodeTag got) TAG_T_VAR)
                      0
                      (emitMismatchTy tc want got (spanOf (nodeCChild d))))
                    (if (== (declResultCompat tc want got) 1)
                      0
                      (emitMismatchTy tc want got (spanOf (nodeCChild d))))))))))))))

; A result keeps every declared type argument, including source variables
; inside containers and callbacks. Only a top-level subtype conversion may
; change its type: `maybeWrapSubtypeReturn` inserts the required check.
; Nested callback conversions would need wrappers, so they stay exact.
(pub :: declResultCompat (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (declResultCompat tc want got)
  (subtypeCompatAllows tc (tyResolve want) (tyResolve got)))

; A bare `Int`, no type arguments.
(pub :: tyIsBareInt (-> ASTNode Bool))
(pub fn (tyIsBareInt t)
  (if (nodeSame t nilNode)
    false
    (if (!= (nodeTag t) TAG_T_CON)
      false
      (if (!= (vecLen (nodeBChildren t)) 0)
        false
        (strEq (nodeAName t) "Int")))))

; Parameter types: peel the signature's arrows left to right. The
; walk keeps its own cursor because a non-arrow signature - or none -
; means EVERY parameter is Int (stage0 2185, probed), while arrows
; that run out mid-list leave the REST at fresh `_tN` (stage0
; 2188-2196). The counter bumps once per parameter regardless of
; where the type came from - `_tN` names render, so the discipline is
; not a style choice.
(pub :: bindFnParams (-> TC Names (Vec ASTNode) (Option ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (bindFnParams tc params ptys sigTy i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen params))
    0
    {
      (let pty
        (match sigTy
          ((None) mkIntTy)
          ((Some signature)
            (if (&& (! (nodeSame signature nilNode)) (== (nodeTag signature) TAG_T_ARR))
              (if (let (Some declared) (nthParamTy signature i))
                declared
                (freshTVar tc 0))
              mkIntTy)
          )))
      (tcBumpCounter tc)
      (vecPush ptys pty)
      (pushScope (tcScopeVec tc) (vecGetStr params i) 0 0 pty)
      (bindFnParams tc params ptys sigTy (+ i 1))
    }))

; The i-th parameter type of an arrow chain. None means the signature
; has no arrow/domain at that position; a present poison type stays Some.
(pub :: nthParamTy (-> ASTNode Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (nthParamTy ty i)
  (if (|| (< i 0) (nodeSame ty nilNode))
    None
    (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_ARR)
      None
      (if (== i 0)
        {
          (let parameter (nodeAChild ty))
          (if (nodeSame parameter nilNode)
            None
            (Some parameter))
        }
        (nthParamTy (nodeBChild ty) (- i 1))))))

; ==================================================================
; LEXICAL REGION ORIGINS (MM-RGN-3). A value may be stored only where
; every origin outlives its destination. CUR0 (bit 0) is the caller's
; current allocation scope; parameter i has bit 1+i; lexical extent k
; has bit 1+n+k. Scope identities follow lexical nesting.
;
; rgnRounds computes monotone interprocedural facts: parameter stores,
; fresh stores and result dependencies. rgnCheckAll reports those facts
; when the program uses region or recovery scopes. The same facts
; prove restrict(no-escape), and stamp proven-fresh calls for codegen.
; Unknown calls conservatively store into every argument and capture.
; Overflow and an unfinished fixpoint refuse emission.
; ==================================================================
; One facts record per function. `flowsCUR` bit i means a fresh value
; flows into parameter i. `flowsFrom` is one mask per parameter: bit j
; means parameter j's value flows into parameter i. `resultFrom` is
; that mask for the result. `culprits` names, per parameter, the callee
; a fresh value went in through, or "" for this body. `mayEnter` is 1
; when the function may enter a reclaimed invocation extent.
(pub struct RgnFacts
  (mut state : Int)
  (mut flowsCUR : Int)
  (flowsFrom : Ints)
  (mut resultFrom : Int)
  (mut resultCUR : Int)
  (mut unknownCalls : Int)
  (culprits : Strings)
  (nparams : Int)
  (mut mayEnter : Int))

(pub :: rgnFactsNew (-> Int RgnFacts))
(pub fn (rgnFactsNew n)
  {
    (let ff (rgnIntVec))
    (let cu (rgnStrVec))
    {
      (rgnFillInts ff n 0)
      (rgnFillStrs cu n)
      (RgnFacts
        :state 0
        :flowsCUR 0
        :flowsFrom ff
        :resultFrom 0
        :resultCUR 0
        :unknownCalls 0
        :culprits cu
        :nparams n
        :mayEnter 0)
    }
  })

(pub :: rgnFlowsCUR (-> RgnFacts Int))
(pub fn (rgnFlowsCUR facts)
  facts.flowsCUR)

(pub :: rgnFlowsFrom (-> RgnFacts Ints))
(pub fn (rgnFlowsFrom facts)
  facts.flowsFrom)

(pub :: rgnResultFrom (-> RgnFacts Int))
(pub fn (rgnResultFrom facts)
  facts.resultFrom)

(pub :: rgnResultCUR (-> RgnFacts Int))
(pub fn (rgnResultCUR facts)
  facts.resultCUR)

(pub :: rgnSetResultCUR (-> RgnFacts RgnFacts))
(pub fn (rgnSetResultCUR facts)
  {
    (set facts.resultCUR 1)
    facts
  })

(pub :: rgnUnknown (-> RgnFacts Int))
(pub fn (rgnUnknown facts)
  facts.unknownCalls)

(pub :: rgnSetUnknown (-> RgnFacts RgnFacts))
(pub fn (rgnSetUnknown facts)
  {
    (set facts.unknownCalls 1)
    facts
  })

(pub :: rgnCulprits (-> RgnFacts Strings))
(pub fn (rgnCulprits facts)
  facts.culprits)

(pub :: rgnNparams (-> RgnFacts Int))
(pub fn (rgnNparams facts)
  facts.nparams)

(pub :: rgnMayEnter (-> RgnFacts Int))
(pub fn (rgnMayEnter facts)
  facts.mayEnter)

(pub :: rgnIntVec Ints)
(pub fn (rgnIntVec)
  vecNew)

(pub :: rgnStrVec Strings)
(pub fn (rgnStrVec)
  vecNew)

(pub :: rgnFillInts (-> Ints Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnFillInts v n x)
  (if (>= (vecLen v) n)
    0
    {
      (vecPush v x)
      (rgnFillInts v n x)
    }))

(pub :: rgnFillStrs (-> Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnFillStrs v n)
  (if (>= (vecLen v) n)
    0
    {
      (vecPush v (strDup ""))
      (rgnFillStrs v n)
    }))

; The facts of an entry, made on first demand.
(pub :: rgnFactsOf (-> FnEnt Int RgnFacts))
(pub fn (rgnFactsOf entry n)
  (if (let (Some facts) (fnEntRgn entry))
    facts
    {
      (let facts (rgnFactsNew n))
      (fnEntSetRgn entry (Some facts))
      facts
    }))

; A scoped callback's reference arguments and fresh allocations stay
; inside each invocation. The ordinary region walk checks that promise.
; A callee using raw reset still owns the unsafe implementation proof.
(pub :: scopedCollectAll (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (scopedCollectAll tc decls i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    0
    {
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls i))
        (if (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
          {
            (let entFound (findFnEntAnywhere tc (nodeAName d)))
            (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
              {
                (scopedCollectTags tc d ent (nodeAxtagsOf d) 0)
                (if (let (Some tags) (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName d)))
                  (scopedCollectTags tc d ent tags 0)
                  0)
              }
              ())
          })
      }
      (scopedCollectAll tc decls (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: scopedCollectTags (-> TC ASTNode FnEnt Axtags Int Int))
(pub fn (scopedCollectTags tc d ent tags i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      {
        (let tag (vecGet tags i))
        (if (sizedTyIn (axtagKeyOf tag) "scoped compute")
          (match (strVecIdx (nodeBNames d) (axtagTextValue tag))
            ((Some pos)
              {
                {
                  (let marks
                    (if (strEq (axtagKeyOf tag) "compute")
                      (fnEntCompute ent)
                      (fnEntScoped ent)))
                  (if (== (intInVec marks pos 0) 0)
                    (vecPush marks pos))
                }
                (if (strEq (axtagKeyOf tag) "scoped")
                  (tcSetRgnHasForm tc 1)
                  (tcSetComputeEnabled tc 1))
              }
            )
            ((None) 0)))
      }
      (scopedCollectTags tc d ent tags (+ i 1))
    }))

(pub :: emitScopedCallback (-> TC Int String Int))
(pub fn (emitScopedCallback tc span message)
  (if (== span 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3130" "scoped-callback" span message None
        (Some
          "write a lambda at the call, or forward a parameter declared scoped; a scoped callback returns a scalar and retains no invocation-owned references or allocations")))))

(pub :: checkScopedTag (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (checkScopedTag tc d name)
  (match (strVecIdx (nodeBNames d) name)
    ((None)
      (emitScopedCallback tc (nodeSpan d)
        (concat "scoped callback contract names no parameter: `" (concat name "`")))
    )
    ((Some pos)
      {
        (let entFound (findFnEntAnywhere tc (nodeAName d)))
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (if (let (Some declared) (nthParamTy (fnEntTy ent) pos))
            {
              (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve declared) 0))
              (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_ARR)
                (emitScopedCallback tc (nodeSpan d)
                  "a scoped parameter must have a declared function type")
                (if (== (rgTyScalar tc (sigArrowResultTy ty)) 0)
                  (emitScopedCallback tc (nodeSpan d)
                    "a scoped callback must return a scalar value")
                  0))
            }
            (emitScopedCallback tc (nodeSpan d)
              "a scoped parameter must have a declared function type"))
          0)
      }
    )))

; A parameter carrying the same contract may be forwarded. Shadowed
; names and ordinary local closures do not inherit the assertion.
(pub :: rgnScopedParam (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnScopedParam rc e)
  (if (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
    0
    (if (let (Some position) (rgnEnvFind rc (nodeAName e)))
      (if (>= position rc.nparams)
        0
        (if (== (intInVec rc.ent.scoped position 0) 1)
          1
          0))
      0)))

(pub :: rgnCheckScopedArgs (-> RgnCtx FnEnt Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnCheckScopedArgs rc ent args span)
  {
    (let indices (fnEntScoped ent))
    {
      (let _sat
        (if (&& (> (vecLen indices) 0) (fnEntNeedsArgs ent (vecLen args)))
          (if (== (rgnReport rc) 1)
            (emitScopedCallback (rgnTc rc) span
              "a function with a scoped callback contract requires all declared arguments in one call")
            0)
          0))
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen indices)
          {
            (let pos (vecGet indices i))
            (if (< pos (vecLen args))
              (rgnCheckScopedValue rc (vecGet args pos) span))
          })
        0
      }
    }
  })

; Only a real non-nullary declaration is a static callback. A bare
; nullary factory executes and can return a closure with mutable captures.
(pub :: rgnScopedStatic (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnScopedStatic rc e)
  (if (|| (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR) (isSome (rgnEnvFind rc (nodeAName e))))
    0
    {
      (let entFound (findFnEnt (rgnTc rc) (nodeAName e)))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        (if (&& (fnEntArityPositive ent)
              (&& (== (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0) (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0)))
          1
          0)
        0)
    }))

; Lambda binders keep their checked arrow; applications keep an
; evidence stamp whose first word is their checked result type. Missing
; stamps or missing stamped results are None; poison and wildcard nodes
; remain present so each consumer preserves its existing fallback.
(pub :: rgnCheckedTy (-> ASTNode (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (rgnCheckedTy e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    None
    {
      (let stamp (nodeBinderTyChild e))
      (if (nodeSame stamp nilNode)
        None
        {
          (let ty
            (if (== (nodeTag stamp) TAG_EVSTAMP)
              (nodeAChild stamp)
              stamp))
          (if (nodeSame ty nilNode)
            None
            (Some ty))
        })
    }))

(pub :: rgnCheckScopedValue (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnCheckScopedValue rc e span)
  (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LAM)
    (rgnFormWalk rc e "#scoped callback" span)
    (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_IF)
      {
        (rgnCheckScopedValue rc (nodeBChild e) span)
        (rgnCheckScopedValue rc (nodeCChild e) span)
        0
      }
      (if (== (rgnScopedParam rc e) 1)
        0
        (if (== (rgnScopedStatic rc e) 1)
          0
          (if (== (rgnReport rc) 1)
            {
              (let _diagnostic
                (emitScopedCallback (rgnTc rc) (nodeSpan e)
                  "the compiler cannot prove this callback's invocation scope; its body is opaque here"))
              0
            }
            0))))))

; ---- the per-function walk context ----
; A checker walks one function at a time. Reuse its scratch buffers
; across worklist rounds; per-function facts live in FnEnt.
; Reusable buffers carry no checker, entry or active-walk owner. The
; active RgnCtx can own TC without making the TC cache own itself.
(pub struct RgnScratch
  (names : Strings)
  (origs : Ints)
  (binds : Ints)
  (formParents : Ints)
  (formNames : Strings)
  (capNames : Strings)
  (extents : Nodes)
  (capOrigins : Ints)
  (extentSpans : Ints))

(pub struct RgnCtx
  (mut tc : TC)
  (mut decl : ASTNode)
  (mut ent : FnEnt)
  (mut nparams : Int)
  (names : Strings)
  (origs : Ints)
  (binds : Ints)
  (mut curBit : Int)
  (mut curRid : Int)
  (formParents : Ints)
  (formNames : Strings)
  (mut report : Int)
  (mut facts : RgnFacts)
  (mut changed : Int)
  (mut lamBase : Int)
  (mut capMask : Int)
  (mut capNames : Strings)
  (extents : Nodes)
  (mut capOrigins : Ints)
  (extentSpans : Ints)
  (mut reclaim : Int))

; the environment: names, origins and binding regions
; the enclosing forms, parents and names
; the env index the lambda being walked starts at; -1 outside every lambda
; the capture mask of the lambda being walked, and its captures' names
; and origin masks, for AX3062 to name the capture
; the lexical extent bodies, and their boundary spans for diagnostics
; and the LSP
; whether a reclaimed extent may be entered by the expression walked
(:: rgnScratchOf (-> TC RgnScratch))
(fn (rgnScratchOf tc)
  (if (let (Some old) (tcRegionScratch tc))
    {
      (vecClear old.names)
      (vecClear old.origs)
      (vecClear old.binds)
      (vecClear old.formParents)
      (vecClear old.formNames)
      (vecClear old.capNames)
      (vecClear old.extents)
      (vecClear old.capOrigins)
      (vecClear old.extentSpans)
      old
    }
    {
      (let fresh
        (RgnScratch
          :names (rgnStrVec)
          :origs (rgnIntVec)
          :binds (rgnIntVec)
          :formParents (rgnIntVec)
          :formNames (rgnStrVec)
          :capNames (rgnStrVec)
          :extents nodesNew
          :capOrigins (rgnIntVec)
          :extentSpans (rgnIntVec)))
      (tcSetRegionScratch tc (Some fresh))
      fresh
    }))

(pub :: rgnCtxNew (-> TC ASTNode FnEnt Int RgnCtx))
(pub fn (rgnCtxNew tc d ent report)
  {
    (let facts (rgnFactsOf ent (vecLen (nodeBNames d))))
    (let scratch (rgnScratchOf tc))
    (let names scratch.names)
    (let origs scratch.origs)
    (let binds scratch.binds)
    (let formParents scratch.formParents)
    (let formNames scratch.formNames)
    (let capNames scratch.capNames)
    (let extents scratch.extents)
    (let capOrigins scratch.capOrigins)
    (let extentSpans scratch.extentSpans)
    (RgnCtx
      :tc tc
      :decl d
      :ent ent
      :nparams (vecLen (nodeBNames d))
      :names names
      :origs origs
      :binds binds
      :curBit 1
      :curRid 0
      :formParents formParents
      :formNames formNames
      :report report
      :facts facts
      :changed 0
      :lamBase -1
      :capMask 0
      :capNames capNames
      :extents extents
      :capOrigins capOrigins
      :extentSpans extentSpans
      :reclaim 0)
  })

(pub :: rgnTc (-> RgnCtx TC))
(pub fn (rgnTc rc)
  rc.tc)

(pub :: rgnNames (-> RgnCtx Strings))
(pub fn (rgnNames rc)
  rc.names)

(pub :: rgnOrigs (-> RgnCtx Ints))
(pub fn (rgnOrigs rc)
  rc.origs)

(pub :: rgnBinds (-> RgnCtx Ints))
(pub fn (rgnBinds rc)
  rc.binds)

(pub :: rgnFormParents (-> RgnCtx Ints))
(pub fn (rgnFormParents rc)
  rc.formParents)

(pub :: rgnFormNames (-> RgnCtx Strings))
(pub fn (rgnFormNames rc)
  rc.formNames)

(pub :: rgnReport (-> RgnCtx Int))
(pub fn (rgnReport rc)
  rc.report)

(pub :: rgnFacts (-> RgnCtx RgnFacts))
(pub fn (rgnFacts rc)
  rc.facts)

; All parameter bits of this function, as a mask.
(pub :: rgnParamMask (-> RgnCtx Int))
(pub fn (rgnParamMask rc)
  {
    (let n rc.nparams)
    (if (>= n 62)
      0
      (- (<< 1 (+ n 1)) 2))
  })

; The bits that mean "fresh in some region of this body": CUR0 and
; every form. For the facts a caller reads, both are "fresh".
(pub :: rgnFreshMask (-> RgnCtx Int))
(pub fn (rgnFreshMask rc)
  {
    (let n rc.nparams)
    (let k (vecLen (rgnFormParents rc)))
    (if (>= (+ n k) 62)
      1
      (| 1 (<< (- (<< 1 k) 1) (+ n 1))))
  })

; The region identity of one origin bit.
(pub :: rgnOriginRid (-> RgnCtx Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnOriginRid rc bit)
  {
    (let n rc.nparams)
    (if (<= bit n)
      0
      (- bit n))
  })

; Does region `a` outlive (or equal) region `b`? The order of §2.2.
(pub :: rgnOutlives (-> RgnCtx Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnOutlives rc a b)
  (if (== a b)
    1
    (if (== a 0)
      1
      (if (> b 0)
        (rgnFormNestedIn rc a b)
        0))))

; Is form `b` nested (strictly) inside form `a`? Walk b's parents.
(pub :: rgnFormNestedIn (-> RgnCtx Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnFormNestedIn rc a b)
  {
    (let parent (vecGet (rgnFormParents rc) (- b 1)))
    (if (== parent a)
      1
      (if (== parent 0)
        0
        (rgnFormNestedIn rc a parent)))
  })

; ---- rendering, for the messages ----
(pub :: rgnRidText (-> RgnCtx Int String))
(pub fn (rgnRidText rc rid)
  (if (== rid 0)
    "this function's own region (its caller's current region)"
    {
      (let nm (vecGet (rgnFormNames rc) (- rid 1)))
      (if (strEq nm "#recover")
          "the recovery point (reclaimed on abort)"
        (strEq nm "#scoped callback")
          "this scoped callback invocation"
        (strEq nm "#handler")
          "the handler's extent (the region or recovery point an operation is performed in)"
        (concat (concat "region `" nm) "`"))
    }))

(pub :: rgnOriginText (-> RgnCtx Int String))
(pub fn (rgnOriginText rc bit)
  {
    (let n rc.nparams)
    (if (< bit 0)
      "a static value"
      (if (== bit 0)
        "a value allocated in this function's own region"
        (if (<= bit n)
          (concat
            (concat (concat "parameter `" (vecGetStr (nodeBNames rc.decl) (- bit 1)))
              "`, which lives in ")
            (rgnRidText rc (rgnOriginRid rc bit)))
          (concat "a value allocated in " (rgnRidText rc (rgnOriginRid rc bit))))))
  })

; The same, followed by the comma a relative clause closes with.
(pub :: rgnOriginTextComma (-> RgnCtx Int String))
(pub fn (rgnOriginTextComma rc bit)
  (if (&& (> bit 0) (<= bit rc.nparams))
    (concat (rgnOriginText rc bit) ",")
    (rgnOriginText rc bit)))

; ---- the escape rule ----
; The first (value origin, target region) pair that violates the
; order, answered as `v * 64 + rid + 1`, or 0 when every pair holds.
(pub :: rgnStoreBad (-> RgnCtx Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnStoreBad rc valMask tgtRid)
  {
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut bad 0)
    {
      (while (&& (< i 63) (== bad 0))
        {
          (if (!= (& valMask (<< 1 i)) 0)
            (if (== (rgnOutlives rc (rgnOriginRid rc i) tgtRid) 0)
              (set bad (+ (* i 64) (+ tgtRid 1)))))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      bad
    }
  })

; The same over a TARGET given as an origin mask: every origin of the
; target is a region the value must outlive.
(pub :: rgnStoreBadMask (-> RgnCtx Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnStoreBadMask rc valMask tgtMask)
  {
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut bad 0)
    {
      (while (&& (< i 63) (== bad 0))
        {
          (if (!= (& tgtMask (<< 1 i)) 0)
            (set bad (rgnStoreBad rc valMask (rgnOriginRid rc i))))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      bad
    }
  })

; The source boundary for a lexical origin. Parameter and CUR0 origins
; carry no local reclamation boundary. Each extent body belongs to the
; function currently being reported, so related spans remain same-file.
(pub :: rgnOriginSpan (-> RgnCtx Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnOriginSpan rc bit)
  {
    (let k (- bit (+ rc.nparams 1)))
    (let boundaries rc.extentSpans)
    (if (|| (< k 0) (>= k (vecLen boundaries)))
      0
      (vecGet boundaries k))
  })

; A diagnostic may describe a concrete value, or only a synthetic flow.
; None selects the generic escape message without inspecting capture data.
(pub :: rgnEmitEscape (-> RgnCtx Int Int String String (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (rgnEmitEscape rc bad span into how valNode)
  (if (== (rgnReport rc) 0)
    0
    (if (== bad 0)
      0
      ; S2 refused this exact store already (AX3059): reporting it
      ; again as AX3060 would draw the same refusal twice (631).
      ; set holds S2's store spans, so a callee-mediated store - no
      ; S2 finding anywhere near it - still reports. Inlined loop,
      ; nothing allocated: no new top-level function moves the
      ; effect-distribution pins.
      {
        (let mut i 0)
        {
          (let mut hit 0)
          {
            (let w
              (while (< i (vecLen (tcRgnRefused (rgnTc rc))))
                {
                  (if (== (vecGet (tcRgnRefused (rgnTc rc)) i) span)
                    (set hit 1))
                  (set i (+ i 1))
                }))
            (if (== hit 1)
              0
              {
                (let v (/ (- bad 1) 64))
                (let t (% (- bad 1) 64))
                (let capBad
                  (if (let (Some value) valNode)
                    (rgnCaptureBad rc value (% (- bad 1) 64))
                    0))
                (if (== capBad 0)
                  (emitDiag (rgnTc rc)
                    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3060" "region-escape" span
                      (concat
                        (concat (concat (concat (rgnOriginTextComma rc v) " is stored into ") into)
                          (concat ", which lives in " (rgnRidText rc t)))
                        how)
                      (diagRelated (rgnOriginSpan rc v)
                        "this scope reclaims the stored value before the destination is used")
                      (Some
                        "this store or call crosses a reclamation boundary: move it to the destination's outer scope, or keep the destination and every use inside the value's region; keeping another reference cannot postpone the reset")))
                  (emitDiag (rgnTc rc)
                    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3062" "region-capture" span
                      (concat
                        (concat
                          (concat
                            (concat
                              (concat "the closure captures `"
                                (match valNode
                                  ((None) "")
                                  ((Some value)
                                    (match (rgnCaptureFor rc value (/ (- capBad 1) 64))
                                      ((None) "")
                                      ((Some name) name))
                                  )))
                              "`, which lives in ")
                            (rgnRidText rc (rgnOriginRid rc (/ (- capBad 1) 64))))
                          (concat ", and is stored into " into))
                        (concat (concat ", which lives in " (rgnRidText rc t)) how))
                      (diagRelated (rgnOriginSpan rc (/ (- capBad 1) 64))
                        "this scope reclaims a value the closure captures")
                      (Some
                        "keep the closure and every use inside its captures' region, or create its captured values and the closure in the destination's outer scope; capturing a reference does not extend its region"))))
              })
          }
        }
      })))

; ---- the environment ----
(pub :: rgnBind (-> RgnCtx String Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnBind rc name mask rid)
  {
    (let names (rgnNames rc))
    (vecPush names name)
    (vecPush (rgnOrigs rc) mask)
    (vecPush (rgnBinds rc) rid)
    0
  })

(pub :: rgnEnvLen (-> RgnCtx Int))
(pub fn (rgnEnvLen rc)
  (vecLen (rgnNames rc)))

(pub :: rgnEnvTrunc (-> RgnCtx Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnEnvTrunc rc n)
  (if (<= (vecLen (rgnNames rc)) n)
    0
    {
      (let names (rgnNames rc))
      (let retired (vecPop names))
      (vecPop (rgnOrigs rc))
      (vecPop (rgnBinds rc))
      (rgnEnvTrunc rc n)
    }))

; The innermost binding of `name`, or None.
(pub :: rgnEnvFind (-> RgnCtx String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (rgnEnvFind rc name)
  (rgnEnvFindFrom rc name (- (vecLen (rgnNames rc)) 1)))

(pub :: rgnEnvFindFrom (-> RgnCtx String Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (rgnEnvFindFrom rc name i)
  (if (< i 0)
    None
    (if (strEq (vecGet (rgnNames rc) i) name)
      (Some i)
      (rgnEnvFindFrom rc name (- i 1)))))

; ---- facts recording ----
(pub :: rgnFactsOr (-> RgnCtx Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnFactsOr rc word mask)
  {
    (let r (rgnFacts rc))
    (let cur
      (if (== word 1)
        r.flowsCUR
        (if (== word 3)
          r.resultFrom
          r.mayEnter)))
    (if (== (| cur mask) cur)
      0
      {
        (if (== word 1)
          (set r.flowsCUR (| cur mask))
          (if (== word 3)
            (set r.resultFrom (| cur mask))
            (set r.mayEnter (| cur mask))))
        (set rc.changed 1)
        0
      })
  })

; Reclamation is a monotone function fact, and a local expression fact.
; A handle brackets the latter to distinguish its body from other
; scopes in the same function. Unknown calls may reclaim too.
(pub :: rgnNoteReclaim (-> RgnCtx Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnNoteReclaim rc possible)
  (if (== possible 0)
    0
    {
      (set rc.reclaim 1)
      (rgnFactsOr rc 8 1)
    }))

; A value with origins `valMask` flows into a place with origins
; `tgtMask`: every parameter among the targets learns what flowed in.
; `via` names the callee the flow went through, "" for this body.
(pub :: rgnNoteFlow (-> RgnCtx Int Int String Int))
(pub fn (rgnNoteFlow rc valMask tgtMask via)
  {
    (let f (rgnFacts rc))
    (let params (& tgtMask (rgnParamMask rc)))
    (let fresh (!= (& valMask (rgnFreshMask rc)) 0))
    (let fromP (>> (& valMask (rgnParamMask rc)) 1))
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (while (< i rc.nparams)
        {
          (if (!= (& params (<< 1 (+ i 1))) 0)
            {
              (if fresh
                {
                  (if (== (& (rgnFlowsCUR f) (<< 1 i)) 0)
                    {
                      (let culprits (rgnCulprits f))
                      (vecSet culprits i via)
                      ()
                    })
                  (rgnFactsOr rc 1 (<< 1 i))
                })
              {
                (let ff (rgnFlowsFrom f))
                (if (! (== (| (vecGet ff i) fromP) (vecGet ff i)))
                  {
                    (vecSet ff i (| (vecGet ff i) fromP))
                    (set rc.changed 1)
                  })
              }
            })
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      0
    }
  })

; ---- the walk ----
(pub :: rgnExpr (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnExpr rc e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (rgnVar rc e)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (rgnApp rc e)
          (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
            (rgnLam rc e)
            (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
              {
                (let v (rgnExpr rc (nodeBChild e)))
                {
                  (let mark (rgnEnvLen rc))
                  {
                    (rgnBind rc (nodeAName e) v rc.curRid)
                    {
                      (let r (rgnExpr rc (nodeCChild e)))
                      {
                        (rgnEnvTrunc rc mark)
                        r
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
                {
                  (rgnExpr rc (nodeAChild e))
                  {
                    (let mb (rgnExpr rc (nodeBChild e)))
                    {
                      (let mc (rgnExpr rc (nodeCChild e)))
                      {
                        ; S4 slice 3's witness stamp for joins: this `if`
                        ; answers whichever arm it takes, so when every
                        ; arm value node already carries the stamp - a
                        ; proven-fresh call result, or such a join - the
                        ; join carries it too. Same guards as the call
                        ; stamp (post-fixpoint walks only, converged
                        ; facts only, upgrade 0 to 2), plus both arms
                        ; present: a missing arm is not a stamped one.
                        ; Anything else for an arm - a reader, an unknown
                        ; call, a construction, a name - abstains, and so
                        ; does the join. Inlined here (two arms, no walk
                        ; to thread) so no new top-level function moves
                        ; the effect-distribution pins.
                        (if (&& (== (tcRgnStamping (rgnTc rc)) 1)
                              (&& (== (tcRgnFactsDone (rgnTc rc)) 1)
                                (&& (== (nodeResWord e) 0)
                                  (&& (!= (nodeB e) 0)
                                    (&& (!= (nodeC e) 0)
                                      (&& (== (nodeResWord (nodeBChild e)) 2)
                                        (== (nodeResWord (nodeCChild e)) 2)))))))
                          (setNodeResWord e 2))
                        (| mb mc)
                      }
                    }
                  }
                }
                ; `cond` is AX2004: the parser builds none, so no
                ; arm region-checks one. A variadic `if` arrives as
                ; nested `if`s and walks the `if` arm like any other.
                (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                  (rgnSeq rc (nodeAChildren e) 0 0)
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                    (rgnMatch rc e)
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                      (rgnWhile rc e)
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                        (rgnSet rc e)
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
                          (rgnSetField rc e)
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            (rgnExpr rc (nodeAChild e))
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                              (rgnHandle rc e)
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                                (rgnRegionForm rc e)
                                0)))))))))))))
    }))

(pub :: rgnVar (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnVar rc e)
  {
    (let idx (rgnEnvFind rc (nodeAName e)))
    (if (let (Some idx) idx)
      {
        ; A read below the innermost lambda's base is a CAPTURE, and
        ; the closure's origins grow by it.
        (if (&& (>= rc.lamBase 0) (< idx rc.lamBase))
          (if (!= (vecGet (rgnOrigs rc) idx) 0)
            {
              (set rc.capMask (| rc.capMask (vecGet (rgnOrigs rc) idx)))
              (if (isNone (strVecIdx rc.capNames (nodeAName e)))
                {
                  (let names rc.capNames)
                  (vecPush names (nodeAName e))
                  (vecPush rc.capOrigins (vecGet (rgnOrigs rc) idx))
                })
            }))
        (vecGet (rgnOrigs rc) idx)
      }
      ; A global: a function's name, a constructor, a builtin - static,
      ; with one exception. A NULLARY function written bare, `vecNew`,
      ; is a call, and its answer is fresh in the caller's region like
      ; any call's. Read as static it carried no origin, so
      ; `(let ((v vecNew)) (region r (vecPush v s)))` stored a region
      ; value into a vector nothing checked, and reading it after the
      ; region ended crashed (tests/diagnostics/1301-region-bare-nullary.ax).
      {
        (let entFound (findFnEnt (rgnTc rc) (nodeAName e)))
        {
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            (if (&& (> (vecLen (fnEntScoped ent)) 0) (== (rgnReport rc) 1))
              (emitScopedCallback (rgnTc rc) (nodeSpan e)
                "a function with a scoped callback contract must be called directly; a function value would erase its precondition"))
            ())
          (if (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            (&& (fnEntArityIs ent 0) (&& (== (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0) (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0)))
            false)
            (rgnApp rc e)
            0)
        }
      })
  })

(pub :: rgnSeq (-> RgnCtx (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnSeq rc v i last)
  (if (== (as v Int) 0)
    last
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      last
      (rgnSeq rc v (+ i 1) (rgnExpr rc (vecGet v i))))))

(pub :: rgnArgsUnion (-> RgnCtx Nodes Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnArgsUnion rc v i acc)
  (if (== (as v Int) 0)
    acc
    (if (>= i (vecLen v))
      acc
      (rgnArgsUnion rc v (+ i 1) (| acc (rgnExpr rc (vecGet v i)))))))

; A loop's back edge may carry an origin through any number of local
; bindings before an earlier store observes it. Widen those bindings
; to a fixpoint before reporting. Each changed round adds at least
; one of 63 possible bits to an outer binding, so the bound follows
; from the environment's size. Extent identities stay fixed across
; these walks (`rgnFormWalk`).
(pub :: rgnWhile (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnWhile rc e)
  {
    (let rep rc.report)
    (let mark (rgnEnvLen rc))
    (let before (rgnIntVec))
    (let limit (+ (* 63 (+ mark 1)) 1))
    (let mut round 0)
    (let mut changed 1)
    (let mut i 0)
    {
      (rgnFillInts before mark 0)
      (set rc.report 0)
      (while (&& (== changed 1) (< round limit))
        {
          (set i 0)
          (while (< i mark)
            {
              (vecSet before i (vecGet (rgnOrigs rc) i))
              (set i (+ i 1))
            })
          (rgnExpr rc (nodeAChild e))
          (rgnSeq rc (nodeBChildren e) 0 0)
          (set changed 0)
          (for i in 0..mark
            (if (!= (vecGet before i) (vecGet (rgnOrigs rc) i))
              (set changed 1)))
          (set round (+ round 1))
        })
      (set rc.report rep)
      (if (== changed 1)
        {
          (tcSetRgnFactsDone (rgnTc rc) 2)
          (rgnSetUnknown (rgnFacts rc))
          (set rc.changed 1)
          (if (== rep 1)
            (emitDiag (rgnTc rc)
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3060" "region-escape" (nodeSpan e)
                "region safety cannot be proved: loop lifetime analysis did not converge" None
                (Some
                  "simplify the loop's lifetime dependencies or split the function into smaller functions"))))
        }
        {
          (rgnExpr rc (nodeAChild e))
          (rgnSeq rc (nodeBChildren e) 0 0)
        })
      0
    }
  })

(pub :: rgnMatch (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnMatch rc e)
  {
    (let s (rgnExpr rc (nodeAChild e)))
    (rgnArms rc (nodeBChildren e) 0 s 0 e 1)
  })

(pub :: rgnArms (-> RgnCtx (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnArms rc arms i s acc mnode allok)
  (if (>= i (vecLen arms))
    ; Every arm walked: stamp the match iff every arm body carries
    ; the stamp, with at least one arm (a join of nothing stamps
    ; nothing), under the call stamp's own guards. The flag threaded
    ; down, so no new top-level function moves the
    ; effect-distribution pins.
    {
      (if (&& (== (tcRgnStamping (rgnTc rc)) 1)
            (&& (== (tcRgnFactsDone (rgnTc rc)) 1)
              (&& (== (nodeResWord mnode) 0) (&& (!= i 0) (== allok 1)))))
        (setNodeResWord mnode 2))
      acc
    }
    {
      (let arm (vecGet arms i))
      (let mark (rgnEnvLen rc))
      {
        (rgnBindPat rc (nodeAChild arm) s)
        {
          (let r (rgnExpr rc (nodeBChild arm)))
          {
            (let ok
              (if (&& (== allok 1) (&& (!= (nodeB arm) 0) (== (nodeResWord (nodeBChild arm)) 2)))
                1
                0))
            {
              (rgnEnvTrunc rc mark)
              (rgnArms rc arms (+ i 1) s (| acc r) mnode ok)
            }
          }
        }
      }
    }))

; A pattern's binders take the scrutinee's origins: a field of a
; value lives where the value does.
(pub :: rgnBindPat (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnBindPat rc pat s)
  (if (nodeSame pat nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag pat))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (if (|| (strEq (nodeAName pat) "_")
              (|| (strEq (nodeAName pat) "true") (strEq (nodeAName pat) "false")))
          0
          (rgnBind rc (nodeAName pat) s rc.curRid))
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (rgnBindPats rc (spineArgVec pat vecNew) 0 s)
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (rgnBindPats rc (nodeCChildren pat) 0 s)
            0)))
    }))

(pub :: rgnBindPats (-> RgnCtx Nodes Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnBindPats rc pats i s)
  (if (== (as pats Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen pats))
      0
      {
        (rgnBindPat rc (vecGet pats i) s)
        (rgnBindPats rc pats (+ i 1) s)
      })))

; `(set x v)`: the cell was made in the region current at its `let`,
; and the value must outlive that. Then the binding's origins widen.
(pub :: rgnSet (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnSet rc e)
  {
    (let v (rgnExpr rc (nodeBChild e)))
    (let idx (rgnEnvFind rc (nodeAName e)))
    (if (let (Some idx) idx)
      {
        (rgnEmitEscape rc (rgnStoreBad rc v (vecGet (rgnBinds rc) idx)) (nodeSpan e)
          (concat (concat "the binding `" (nodeAName e)) "`") "" (Some (nodeBChild e)))
        (vecSet (rgnOrigs rc) idx (| (vecGet (rgnOrigs rc) idx) v))
        0
      }
      0)
  })

; `(set b.f v)`: the field's owner is the place. Walk the value even
; when its checked type is scalar: evaluating it can store elsewhere.
; A scalar result itself carries no reference origin, including a
; length read through the raw implementation of `strLen`.
(pub :: rgnSetField (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnSetField rc e)
  {
    (let b (rgnExpr rc (nodeAChild e)))
    (let origins (rgnExpr rc (nodeCChild e)))
    (let v
      (if (if (let (Some ty) (rgnCheckedTy (nodeCChild e)))
        (== (rgTyScalar (rgnTc rc) ty) 1)
        false)
        0
        origins))
    {
      (rgnEmitEscape rc (rgnStoreBadMask rc v b) (nodeSpan e)
        (concat (concat "the field `" (nodeBName e)) "` of a value") "" (Some (nodeCChild e)))
      (rgnNoteFlow rc v b "")
      0
    }
  })

; A closure: created in the current region, and holding a reference
; to every capture. Its parameters arrive from a caller this walk
; cannot see, so they are taken as fresh in the current region - the
; conservative reading. Nested lambdas propagate captures outward: a
; capture of the inner lambda that is below the OUTER lambda's base is
; the outer's capture too.
(pub :: rgnLam (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnLam rc e)
  {
    (let mark (rgnEnvLen rc))
    (let savedBase rc.lamBase)
    (let savedCap rc.capMask)
    (let savedNames rc.capNames)
    (let savedMasks rc.capOrigins)
    (let params (nodeANames e))
    {
      (set rc.lamBase mark)
      (set rc.capMask 0)
      (set rc.capNames (rgnStrVec))
      (set rc.capOrigins (rgnIntVec))
      (rgnBindTypedParams rc params 0 rc.curBit
        (if (if (> rc.curRid 0)
          (strEq (vecGet (rgnFormNames rc) (- rc.curRid 1)) "#scoped callback")
          false)
          (rgnCheckedTy e)
          None))
      (rgnExpr rc (nodeBChild e))
      (rgnEnvTrunc rc mark)
      {
        (let cap rc.capMask)
        {
          (set rc.lamBase savedBase)
          ; what the inner captured below the outer's base, the outer
          ; captured too - and the outer's capture list grows by the
          ; inner's, so a store of the outer names the right capture
          (set rc.capMask (| savedCap (rgnCapBelow rc cap savedBase)))
          (if (! (< savedBase 0))
            (rgnMergeCaps rc.capNames rc.capOrigins savedNames savedMasks 0))
          ; The closure's own origins: where it was made, and what it
          ; holds. Its capture lists stay in words 18/20 for the store
          ; or return that follows this expression to name a capture.
          (| rc.curBit cap)
        }
      }
    }
  })

; Of a capture mask, the part that is ALSO a capture for a lambda
; whose base is `base` - conservatively the whole mask, because the
; mask carries origins and not env indices.
; Add the inner lambda's captures to the outer's lists, by name.
(pub :: rgnMergeCaps (-> Strings Ints Strings Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnMergeCaps names masks outNames outMasks i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    0
    {
      (if (isNone (strVecIdx outNames (vecGetStr names i)))
        {
          (vecPush outNames (vecGet names i))
          (vecPush outMasks (vecGet masks i))
        })
      (rgnMergeCaps names masks outNames outMasks (+ i 1))
    }))

; The name of the last lambda's capture that carries origin `bit`,
; when the value at `node` is that lambda; None otherwise.
(pub :: rgnCaptureFor (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (rgnCaptureFor rc node bit)
  (if (nodeSame node nilNode)
    None
    (if (!= (nodeTag node) TAG_E_LAM)
      None
      (rgnCaptureFrom rc.capNames rc.capOrigins bit 0))))

; When the value at `node` is the last lambda walked: the first
; (capture origin, target) pair that violates the order against
; `tgtRid`, in `rgnStoreBad`'s encoding, or 0. Asked BEFORE the
; closure's own record is blamed, because "the closure captures `s`"
; is the fix's address and "a value allocated here" is not.
(pub :: rgnCaptureBad (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnCaptureBad rc node tgtRid)
  (if (nodeSame node nilNode)
    0
    (if (!= (nodeTag node) TAG_E_LAM)
      0
      (rgnCaptureBadFrom rc rc.capOrigins tgtRid 0))))

(pub :: rgnCaptureBadFrom (-> RgnCtx Ints Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnCaptureBadFrom rc masks tgtRid i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen masks))
    0
    {
      (let bad (rgnStoreBad rc (vecGet masks i) tgtRid))
      (if (!= bad 0)
        bad
        (rgnCaptureBadFrom rc masks tgtRid (+ i 1)))
    }))

(pub :: rgnCaptureFrom (-> Strings Ints Int Int (Option String)))
(pub fn (rgnCaptureFrom names masks bit i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen names))
    None
    (if (!= (& (vecGet masks i) (<< 1 bit)) 0)
      (Some (vecGet names i))
      (rgnCaptureFrom names masks bit (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: rgnCapBelow (-> RgnCtx Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnCapBelow rc cap base)
  (if (< base 0)
    0
    cap))

(pub :: rgnBindTypedParams (-> RgnCtx Strings Int Int (Option ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (rgnBindTypedParams rc params i mask ty)
  (if (== (as params Int) 0)
    0
    (if (>= i (vecLen params))
      0
      {
        (rgnBind rc (vecGetStr params i)
          (if (if (let (Some signature) ty)
            (if (let (Some arrow) (peelArrows signature i))
              (if (== (nodeTag arrow) TAG_T_ARR)
                (== (rgTyScalar (rgnTc rc) (nodeAChild arrow)) 1)
                false)
              false)
            false)
            0
            mask)
          rc.curRid)
        (rgnBindTypedParams rc params (+ i 1) mask ty)
      })))

; `(region r E)` - S2's node, walked against its contract: `r` in the
; name slot, `E` in word 2. Fresh values inside are the form's; the
; only scalar results may cross the lexical reset, checked by checkRegion.
(pub :: rgnRegionForm (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnRegionForm rc e)
  (rgnFormWalk rc (nodeBChild e) (nodeAName e) (nodeC e)))

; One lexical extent reclaimed as a whole: `body` is walked with a fresh
; region bit current, so what it allocates carries the bit and a store
; of it anywhere older is MM-RGN-3's refusal. A `(region r ...)` form
; a recovery point's thunk (`rgnRecoverForm`) and a handler invocation
; (`rgnHandlerValue`) all come here.
(pub :: rgnFormWalk (-> RgnCtx ASTNode String Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnFormWalk rc body name boundary)
  {
    (if (! (strEq name "#handler"))
      (rgnNoteReclaim rc 1))
    (let n rc.nparams)
    (let forms rc.extents)
    (let mut k 0)
    (let savedBit rc.curBit)
    (let savedRid rc.curRid)
    {
      (while (&& (< k (vecLen forms)) (! (nodeSame (vecGet forms k) body)))
        (set k (+ k 1)))
      (if (>= (+ n k) 61)
        {
          ; Reusing the enclosing bit would accept an escape as if the
          ; shorter extent outlived it. An unrepresentable lifetime is
          ; an unproved lifetime, and emission must stop.
          (if (== (rgnReport rc) 1)
            (emitDiag (rgnTc rc)
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3060" "region-escape" (nodeSpan body)
                "region safety cannot be proved: this function exceeds the lifetime origin capacity"
                None
                (Some
                  "split this function into smaller functions so each region has a distinct tracked origin"))))
          (rgnSetUnknown (rgnFacts rc))
          0
        }
        {
          (let bit (<< 1 (+ (+ n 1) k)))
          {
            (if (== k (vecLen forms))
              {
                (vecPush forms body)
                (vecPush (rgnFormParents rc) savedRid)
                (let formNames (rgnFormNames rc))
                (vecPush formNames (strDup name))
                (vecPush rc.extentSpans boundary)
              })
            (set rc.curBit bit)
            (set rc.curRid (+ k 1))
            {
              (let v (rgnExpr rc body))
              {
                (set rc.curBit savedBit)
                (set rc.curRid savedRid)
                (if (!= (& v bit) 0)
                  (| (& v (^ v bit)) savedBit)
                  v)
              }
            }
          }
        })
    }
  })

; Only a shorter extent below the handled body changes the lifetime
; of its operation arguments. The function fact propagates through
; resolved callees, while an unknown invocation may enter one. Other
; regions elsewhere in this function do not shorten these arguments.
(pub :: rgnHandle (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnHandle rc e)
  {
    (let saved rc.reclaim)
    (set rc.reclaim 0)
    (let b (rgnExpr rc (nodeAChild e)))
    (let shorter rc.reclaim)
    (if (isSome (handleOpDecl (rgnTc rc) (nodeBChildren e) 0 None))
      (if (== shorter 1)
        (rgnHandlerValue rc (nodeCChild e))
        (rgnHandlerLocal rc (nodeCChild e))))
    (set rc.reclaim (| saved rc.reclaim))
    b
  })

; Without a shorter body extent, lambda parameters live in the current
; scope. An unseen handler may store fresh values into any argument or
; capture, including references derived from this function's parameters.
; Record those flows so a caller cannot install it in a shorter scope.
; Its invocation may reclaim and therefore constrains an outer handler.
(pub :: rgnHandlerLocal (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnHandlerLocal rc h)
  (if (if (let (Some ty) (rgnCheckedTy h))
    (== (tyIsErr ty) 1)
    false)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_LAM)
      (rgnExpr rc h)
      (if (== (rgnScopedStatic rc h) 1)
        (rgnHandlerValue rc h)
        (rgnUnknownCall rc nodesNew (spanOf h) (| (rgnExpr rc h) (rgnParamMask rc)))))))

; A recovery point, `(__axiom_recover mark thunk)`: an abort resets the
; arena to `mark`, reclaiming everything the thunk allocated, so the
; thunk is an extent reclaimed as a whole, exactly as a region is, and
; it is walked as one. What it allocates may be stored nowhere older:
; the reclamation audit's probe grew a `Vec` built before the point
; inside the thunk, trapped, and read the reclaimed block afterwards
; through the safe surface (AN-42; `tests/diagnostics/1033-recover-escape.ax`).
; A normal return keeps the allocations, but a trap can come after any
; store, so the rule is the region's.
;
; The walk needs the thunk's body. A lambda written at the call has it;
; a top-level function captures nothing and is handed only an `Int`;
; anything else - a `let`-bound closure, a parameter - is a thunk this
; check can't see, and is AX3090. The answer is the status or the
; thunk's word, so it carries no origin.
(pub :: rgnRecoverForm (-> RgnCtx Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnRecoverForm rc args span)
  {
    (let thunk (vecGet args 1))
    {
      (rgnExpr rc (vecGet args 0))
      (if (== (nodeTag thunk) TAG_E_LAM)
        (rgnFormWalk rc thunk "#recover" span)
        (if (!
              (&& (== (nodeTag thunk) TAG_E_VAR)
                (&& (isNone (rgnEnvFind rc (nodeAName thunk)))
                  (isSome (findFnEnt (rgnTc rc) (nodeAName thunk))))))
          (if (! (|| (== span 0) (== (rgnReport rc) 0)))
            (emitDiag (rgnTc rc)
              (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3090" "recover-thunk-unseen" span
                "a recovery point's thunk has to be a lambda written at the call, or a top-level function"
                None
                (Some
                  "an abort reclaims everything the thunk allocated, so the thunk is held to storing none of it anywhere older; that is checked on a lambda written here, and a top-level function captures nothing. Write the lambda at the call"))))))
      0
    }
  })

; The handler of `(handle body (E) handler)` runs where an operation of
; `E` is performed, and that may be inside a region or a recovery point
; anywhere below the form: what the operation hands it and what it
; allocates belong to that extent, and the extent reclaims them when
; it ends (MM-RGN-3). Measured with no rule here: a handler installed
; outside a region that kept the operation's argument in a captured
; cell left the cell naming reclaimed memory, and the next allocation
; trapped inside the allocator (`tests/diagnostics/1474-handler-keeps-argument.ax`).
;
; A lambda written at the form is walked as an extent of its own, so
; its parameters carry the extent's bit as a region's fresh values do:
; storing one, or a copy made in the handler, into a capture is
; AX3060, and a closure over one stored older is AX3062. A top-level
; function handler reaches nothing older than the operation's own
; arguments, so its facts answer: a flow between its parameters, or a
; fresh value into one, is the same store. A handler held in a
; binding, a parameter, a field or a call's answer has an unseen body.
; Refuse it rather than certifying its installation's lifetime for
; calls made in a shorter extent. Built-in-only handle lists never
; evaluate their handler, so the caller skips this walk for them.
(pub :: rgnHandlerValue (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnHandlerValue rc h)
  (if (if (let (Some ty) (rgnCheckedTy h))
    (== (tyIsErr ty) 1)
    false)
    0
    (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_LAM)
      (rgnFormWalk rc h "#handler" (nodeSpan h))
      {
        (let entFound
          (if (== (rgnScopedStatic rc h) 1)
            (findFnEnt (rgnTc rc) (nodeAName h))
            None))
        {
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            (if (let (Some facts) (fnEntRgn ent))
              (rgnNoteReclaim rc (rgnMayEnter facts))))
          (if (&& (== (rgnReport rc) 1) (!= (spanOf h) 0))
            (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
              (if (== (rgnFactsKeep (fnEntRgn ent)) 1)
                (emitDiag (rgnTc rc)
                  (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3060" "region-escape" (nodeSpan h)
                    (concat (concat "the handler `" (nodeAName h))
                      "` may store a value it is handed, or one it allocates, into another of its arguments; an operation performed inside a region or a recovery point hands it values that extent reclaims")
                    None
                    (Some
                      "a handler runs where the operation is performed, so what it is handed and what it allocates belong to that extent: compute a scalar in the handler, or perform the operation outside the region or recovery point"))))
              (emitDiag (rgnTc rc)
                (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3060" "region-escape" (spanOf h)
                  "region safety cannot be proved: the handler's body is not visible at its installation"
                  None
                  (Some
                    "write the handler as a lambda at the handle form, or name a top-level function; an operation may run inside a shorter region or recovery point, so its handler must retain none of that extent's references or allocations")))))
          (rgnExpr rc h)
        }
      })))

; Whether a function may store a parameter, or a fresh value, into
; one of its parameters. Missing facts and unknown calls prove no
; handler lifetime guarantee.
(pub :: rgnFactsKeep (-> (Option RgnFacts) Int))
(pub fn (rgnFactsKeep found)
  (if (let (Some facts) found)
    (if (|| (!= (rgnFlowsCUR facts) 0) (!= (rgnUnknown facts) 0))
      1
      (rgnAnyFlow (rgnFlowsFrom facts) 0))
    1))

(pub :: rgnAnyFlow (-> Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnAnyFlow v i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen v))
    0
    (if (!= (vecGet v i) 0)
      1
      (rgnAnyFlow v (+ i 1)))))

; ---- calls ----
(pub :: rgnApp (-> RgnCtx ASTNode Int))
; The source tree stays owned by the checker. Both helpers return an
; owned scratch share, and the walk answers only an Int. Raw span metadata
; makes escape analysis conservatively keep these owners, so balance the
; temporary argument vector and head after the walk has finished.
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (rgnApp rc e)
  {
    (let head (spineHead e))
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (let tc (rgnTc rc))
    (let result
      (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
        (rgnUnknownCall rc args (spanOf e) (rgnExpr rc head))
        {
          (let n (nodeAName head))
          (if (isSome (rgnEnvFind rc n))
            (if (== (rgnScopedParam rc head) 1)
              {
                (let v (rgnUnknownCall rc args (spanOf e) 0))
                (if (if (let (Some ty) (rgnCheckedTy e))
                  (== (rgTyScalar tc ty) 1)
                  false)
                  0
                  v)
              }
              (rgnUnknownCall rc args (spanOf e) (rgnExpr rc head)))
            (if (&& (strEq n "__axiom_recover") (== (vecLen args) 2))
              (rgnRecoverForm rc args (spanOf e))
              (if (== (isCastLike n) 1)
                (rgnLastArg rc args)
                (if (! (isNone (findCtor tc n)))
                  (| rc.curBit (rgnArgsUnion rc args 0 0))
                  (if (! (isNone (findStructIfConstruct tc n)))
                    (| rc.curBit (rgnArgsUnion rc args 0 0))
                    {
                      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc n))
                      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                        (if (== (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 1)
                          (rgnBuiltinCall rc n args (spanOf e))
                          (if (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 1)
                            ; The installed handler supplies any nested
                            ; reclamation fact. Dispatch alone enters no
                            ; region, while argument stores stay unknown.
                            (rgnOpaqueCall rc args (spanOf e) 0 0)
                            (if (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 2)
                              {
                                (rgnArgsUnion rc args 0 0)
                                rc.curBit
                              }
                              (rgnKnownCall rc ent n args (spanOf e) e))))
                        (rgnUnknownCall rc args (spanOf e) 0))
                    })))))
        }))
    (__release (as args Int))
    (__release (as head Int))
    result
  })

(pub :: rgnLastArg (-> RgnCtx Nodes Int))
(pub fn (rgnLastArg rc args)
  (if (== (vecLen args) 0)
    0
    {
      (let mut last 0)
      {
        (for i in 0..(vecLen args)
          (set last (rgnExpr rc (vecGet args i))))
        last
      }
    }))

; A callee the walk cannot read: assume it stores every argument into
; every argument and allocates its result. A closure may also read
; and write its captures, so those origins participate in both sides
; of the lifetime relation, including calls with no reference arguments.
(pub :: rgnUnknownCall (-> RgnCtx Nodes Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnUnknownCall rc args span captures)
  (rgnOpaqueCall rc args span captures 1))

(pub :: rgnOpaqueCall (-> RgnCtx Nodes Int Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnOpaqueCall rc args span captures reclaims)
  {
    (rgnNoteReclaim rc reclaims)
    (let masks (rgnIntVec))
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut u captures)
    {
      (while (< i (vecLen args))
        {
          (vecPush masks (rgnExpr rc (vecGet args i)))
          (set u (| u (vecGet masks i)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (if (!= captures 0)
        {
          (rgnEmitEscape rc (rgnStoreBadMask rc (| u rc.curBit) captures) span
            "the captures of a call the compiler cannot resolve"
            " - the call may store its arguments or fresh values into a capture" None)
          (rgnNoteFlow rc (| u rc.curBit) captures "<unresolved call>")
        })
      (for i in 0..(vecLen args)
        (if (!= (vecGet masks i) 0)
          {
            (rgnEmitEscape rc (rgnStoreBadMask rc (| u rc.curBit) (vecGet masks i)) span
              (concat (concat "argument " (fmtInt (+ i 1)))
                " of a call the compiler cannot resolve")
              " - a call through a parameter, a closure or a field is assumed to store every argument into every argument"
              None)
            (rgnNoteFlow rc (| u rc.curBit) (vecGet masks i) "<unresolved call>")
          }))
      (if (== (rgnUnknown (rgnFacts rc)) 0)
        {
          (rgnSetUnknown (rgnFacts rc))
          (set rc.changed 1)
        })
      (| rc.curBit u)
    }
  })

; A primitive with no body, classified by name. Stores and reads
; through a handle are the two shapes that matter; everything else
; answers a word.
(pub :: rgnBuiltinCall (-> RgnCtx String Nodes Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnBuiltinCall rc n args span)
  {
    (let masks (rgnIntVec))
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen args)
        (vecPush masks (rgnExpr rc (vecGet args i))))
      (if (|| (strEq n "__store64")
            (|| (strEq n "__atomic_store") (|| (strEq n "__atomic_cas") (strEq n "__atomic_add"))))
        (if (>= (vecLen masks) 2)
          {
            (let v (vecGet masks (- (vecLen masks) 1)))
            {
              (rgnEmitEscape rc (rgnStoreBadMask rc v (vecGet masks 0)) span
                (concat (concat "the block `" n) "` writes") ""
                (Some (vecGet args (- (vecLen args) 1))))
              (rgnNoteFlow rc v (vecGet masks 0) "")
              0
            }
          }
          0)
        (if (|| (strEq n "__load64") (|| (strEq n "__load8") (strEq n "__atomic_load")))
          (if (>= (vecLen masks) 1)
            (vecGet masks 0)
            0)
          (if (strEq n "__alloc")
            rc.curBit
            (if (strEq n "__axiom_arena_reset_keeping")
              (if (>= (vecLen masks) 2)
                (vecGet masks 1)
                0)
              (if (strEq n "__call_word")
                (rgnUnknownCall rc args span 0)
                0)))))
    }
  })

; A user function: its body facts say what it stores where and which
; argument origins reach its result.
;
; `e` is the application node itself, carried so the post-fixpoint
; stamp walks can record a proven-fresh result on it (`nodeResWord`
; 0 to 2; never touching 1). Only `rgnApp` calls this, so the extra
; word moves one site.
(pub :: rgnKnownCall (-> RgnCtx FnEnt String Nodes Int ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (rgnKnownCall rc ent spelled args span e)
  {
    (rgnCheckScopedArgs rc ent args span)
    ; the entry's own name is the mangled one the declaration carries
    ; (`Vec$vecPush`); the spelling at the call may be bare or qualified
    {
      (let masks (rgnIntVec))
      (let callee (fnEntName ent))
      (let sig (fnEntTy ent))
      (let factsFound (fnEntRgn ent))
      (let tc (rgnTc rc))
      (let mut i 0)
      (let mut res 0)
      (let mut ok 1)
      {
        (while (< i (vecLen args))
          {
            (vecPush masks (rgnExpr rc (vecGet args i)))
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        ; The facts say what the callee stores through each parameter.
        (if (let (Some f) factsFound)
          {
            (rgnNoteReclaim rc (rgnMayEnter f))
            (for i in 0..(vecLen args)
              (if (< i (rgnNparams f))
                {
                  (let fresh (!= (& (rgnFlowsCUR f) (<< 1 i)) 0))
                  (let from (vecGet (rgnFlowsFrom f) i))
                  (let into
                    ; Parameter names are diagnostic text. Resolving one
                    ; scans declarations, so defer it until a reporting
                    ; walk has a parameter flow that can use the text.
                    (if (&& (!= (rgnReport rc) 0) (|| fresh (!= from 0)))
                      (concat
                        (concat
                          (concat (concat "parameter `" (rgnCalleeParamName tc callee i)) "` of `")
                          (bareOf callee))
                        "`")
                      ""))
                  {
                    (if fresh
                      {
                        (if (== (rgnReport rc) 1)
                          {
                            (let bad (rgnStoreBadMask rc rc.curBit (vecGet masks i)))
                            (if (!= bad 0)
                              (rgnEmitEscape rc bad span into
                                (concat (concat " - `" (bareOf callee))
                                  "` stores a value it allocates into that parameter")
                                None))
                          })
                        (rgnNoteFlow rc rc.curBit (vecGet masks i) callee)
                      })
                    {
                      (let mut j 0)
                      (while (&& (< j (vecLen args)) (< j (rgnNparams f)))
                        {
                          (if (!= (& from (<< 1 j)) 0)
                            {
                              (if (== (rgnReport rc) 1)
                                {
                                  (let bad (rgnStoreBadMask rc (vecGet masks j) (vecGet masks i)))
                                  (if (!= bad 0)
                                    (rgnEmitEscape rc bad span into
                                      (concat
                                        (concat
                                          (concat (concat " - `" (bareOf callee))
                                            "` stores its parameter `")
                                          (rgnCalleeParamName tc callee j))
                                        "` into it")
                                      (Some (vecGet args j))))
                                })
                              (rgnNoteFlow rc (vecGet masks j) (vecGet masks i) callee)
                            })
                          (set j (+ j 1))
                        })
                    }
                  }
                }))
            (if (!= (rgnUnknown f) 0)
              (if (== (rgnUnknown (rgnFacts rc)) 0)
                {
                  (rgnSetUnknown (rgnFacts rc))
                  (set rc.changed 1)
                }))
          })
        ; The result derives from fresh allocation and/or parameters.
        (if (let (Some f) factsFound)
          {
            (if (!= (rgnResultCUR f) 0)
              (set res rc.curBit))
            ; The facts carry one bit per declared parameter, so a
            ; surplus argument of an over-applied call has none.
            (set i 0)
            (while (&& (< i (vecLen args)) (< i (rgnNparams f)))
              {
                (if (!= (& (rgnResultFrom f) (<< 1 i)) 0)
                  (set res (| res (vecGet masks i))))
                (set i (+ i 1))
              })
            ; S4 slice 2's witness stamp: this call's answer is
            ; freshly allocated by the callee - its facts say the
            ; result derives from CUR0, from no HEAP parameter (a
            ; scalar-typed parameter contributes no alias: an `Int`
            ; field needs no release, so a cell built over words is
            ; still a fresh cell), and from no call the walk could
            ; not resolve - so record it on the node for codegen's
            ; elision. The call must be saturated (every parameter
            ; present, so no bit hides past the argument list); a
            ; partial application abstains. Upgrade only (0 to 2; a
            ; word answer stays 1), only on the post-fixpoint stamp
            ; walks (`rgnStamping`, word 40), never while facts are
            ; still moving, and never when truncation made every row
            ; a lower bound (word 28 is 2 then, not 1). Over-stamping would
            ; leak a share, never free one early - and the RSS half
            ; of the gate watches the ratio - but the rule above is
            ; stated as the guarantee, not the tendency.
            ;
            ; No signature, no stamp: without declared parameter
            ; types there is nothing to prove scalar with, so a
            ; signature-less callee abstains rather than guessing.
            (if (&& (== (tcRgnStamping tc) 1)
                  (&& (== (tcRgnFactsDone tc) 1)
                    (&& (== (nodeResWord e) 0)
                      (&& (!= (rgnResultCUR f) 0)
                        (&& (== (rgnUnknown f) 0) (== (vecLen args) (rgnNparams f)))))))
              {
                (if (nodeSame sig nilNode)
                  (set ok 0)
                  {
                    (set i 0)
                    (while (< i (vecLen args))
                      {
                        (if (!= (& (rgnResultFrom f) (<< 1 i)) 0)
                          (if (let (Some parameter) (nthParamTy sig i))
                            (if (== (rgTyScalar tc parameter) 0)
                              (set ok 0))
                            (set ok 0)))
                        (set i (+ i 1))
                      })
                  })
                (if (== ok 1)
                  (setNodeResWord e 2))
              })
            res
          }
          rc.curBit)
      }
    }
  })

; The i-th parameter's NAME of a callee, from its `fn` declaration,
; or its index when the declaration is not at hand.
(pub :: rgnCalleeParamName (-> TC String Int String))
(pub fn (rgnCalleeParamName tc callee i)
  (if (let (Some d) (rgnDeclOf tc callee))
    (if (< i (vecLen (nodeBNames d)))
      (vecGetStr (nodeBNames d) i)
      (fmtInt i))
    (fmtInt i)))

(pub :: rgnDeclOf (-> TC String (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (rgnDeclOf tc name)
  (rgnDeclOfFrom (tcAllDeclsVec tc) name 0))

(pub :: rgnDeclOfFrom (-> (Vec ASTNode) String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(pub fn (rgnDeclOfFrom decls name i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen decls))
    None
    {
      (let d (vecGet decls i))
      (if (&& (== (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN) (strEq (nodeAName d) name))
        (Some d)
        (rgnDeclOfFrom decls name (+ i 1)))
    }))

; ---- one function ----
(pub :: rgnWalkFn (-> TC ASTNode FnEnt Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnWalkFn tc d ent report)
  {
    (let _u (tcSetCurUnit tc (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) (nodeModuleOpt d))))
    (let rc (rgnCtxNew tc d ent report))
    (let params (nodeBNames d))
    (let mut i 0)
    (if (> (vecLen params) 61)
      {
        (if (== report 1)
          (emitDiag tc
            (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3060" "region-escape" (nodeSpan d)
              "region safety cannot be proved: this function has more than 61 parameter origins"
              None
              (Some
                "split this function into smaller functions so every parameter has a distinct tracked origin"))))
        (rgnSetUnknown (rgnFacts rc))
        0
      }
      {
        (while (< i (vecLen params))
          {
            (rgnBind rc (vecGetStr params i) (<< 1 (+ i 1)) 0)
            (set i (+ i 1))
          })
        {
          (let body (rgnExpr rc (nodeCChild d)))
          {
            (rgnFactsOr rc 3 (>> (& body (rgnParamMask rc)) 1))
            (if (!= (& body (rgnFreshMask rc)) 0)
              (if (== (rgnResultCUR (rgnFacts rc)) 0)
                {
                  (rgnSetResultCUR (rgnFacts rc))
                  (set rc.changed 1)
                }))
            rc.changed
          }
        }
      })
  })

; ---- the driver ----
(pub :: rgnCheckAll (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (rgnCheckAll tc)
  (if (== (tcRgnHasForm tc) 1)
    {
      (rgnEnsureFacts tc)
      (tcSetRgnStamping tc 1)
      (rgnPass tc (tcAllDeclsVec tc) 0 1)
      (tcSetRgnStamping tc 0)
      0
    }
    0))

; The facts fixpoint, run once per module and on first demand - the
; region pass demands it, and so does a `restrict(no-escape)` claim
; checked in the middle of `tcWalkDecls`. TC word 28 records that it
; has run.
(pub :: rgnEnsureFacts (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (rgnEnsureFacts tc)
  (if (!= (tcRgnFactsDone tc) 0)
    0
    {
      (tcSetRgnFactsDone tc 1)
      ; Word 33 is the fixpoint's own verdict: 1 when it gave up. Kept
      ; beside word 28 ("has run") because "ran" and "converged" are
      ; different facts and only one of them licenses a silence. The
      ; bound is the program's own size, as `inferEffects` passes it:
      ; a monotone chain over N functions needs up to N rounds, so
      ; `len+1` converges every real program and the cap below is the
      ; safety net it always was, not a number any program reaches.
      {
        (let decls (tcAllDeclsVec tc))
        (tcSetRgnGaveUp tc (rgnRounds tc decls 0 (+ (vecLen decls) 1)))
      }
      0
    }))

; THE ROUND CAP WAS A TRUNCATION, AND THE BOUND IS THE PROGRAM NOW.
;
; 40 was a bare constant, not a bound derived from the program. A
; monotone chain fixpoint over N functions needs up to N rounds, so a
; call chain deeper than the cap stopped propagating BEFORE it had
; converged - and the facts that came out were an under-approximation
; the walk then treated as final.
;
; Measured 2026-09-03 on generated chains f0 -> f1 -> ... -> fN whose
; leaf does `(memSetWord p 0 (memAlloc 8))`, with
; `restrict(no-escape)` on f0: `check` reported AX3049 at depths 5, 20,
; 30, 38 and 39, and printed `OK` at 40, 41, 42 and 60. The bisect was
; exact - depth 39 refused, depth 40 accepted a claim the analysis can
; itself refute one round later. Timing confirms saturation rather
; than convergence: 0.05s at depth 5, 0.11s at 20, 0.24s at 39, and
; 0.24s at 60, linear in rounds and then flat.
;
; Fixed the way `inferEffects` is fixed: the bound is `(vecLen decls)
; + 1`, so every chain the program can write converges - a chain is
; never longer than the declaration list holding it. The truncation
; machinery below stays exactly as it is (word 28 goes to 2, word 33
; carries 1, `#mir-truncated` prints): a net, not a number any program
; reaches, and the gate that watched the old boundary now watches a
; 60-deep chain converge instead. Rounds after the first are a
; worklist over `callersIdxBuild`, the same shape `inferEffectsWork`
; runs: only callers of functions whose facts grew are re-walked.
; A final full pass verifies the frontier result, because the index
; is built from call edges that a mid-walk demand may see only in
; part; a change there restarts the rounds rather than trusting a
; quiet frontier.
;
; The value is 2 rather than a second word because every reader of
; word 28 asks `!= 0`; 2 is still "done".
(pub :: rgnRounds (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))

; Answers 0 when the facts CONVERGED and 1 when the round cap was
; reached with facts still moving. Both used to answer 0, so a caller
; could not tell a proven claim from an abandoned one - and
; `restrictNoEscape` read that silence as proof. Measured before the
; fix, with the callees declared BELOW their callers so one level
; propagates per pass: a chain of depth 38 was refused and depth 39 was
; ACCEPTED, on a body that provably stores a fresh allocation into its
; parameter. Declaration order is why this hid so long - the same chain
; written leaf-first converges in two passes at any depth.
; One declaration's region walk; answers whether its facts changed.
; Split out of `rgnPass` so the worklist rounds walk single
; declarations, as `inferEffectsOne` does for the effects fixpoint.
(pub :: rgnWalkOne (-> TC ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnWalkOne tc d report)
  (if (!= (nodeTag d) TAG_D_FN)
    0
    {
      (let _m (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
      (let _m0 (tcSetCurModule tc None))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        {
          (let _m1 (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d)))
          (let changed (rgnWalkFn tc d ent report))
          (let _m2 (tcSetCurModule tc None))
          changed
        }
        0)
    }))

; One worklist pass over `work`: walk each, and collect the callers
; of every function whose facts grew into `next`. Answers 1 when
; anything grew. The frontier is visited from its last element, for
; the reason `inferEffectsDirtyPass` states.
(pub :: rgnDirtyPass (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) CallerIndex Ints Ints Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnDirtyPass tc decls bs work marks next i)
  {
    (let mut j (- (vecLen work) 1))
    (let mut changed 0)
    (while (>= j i)
      {
        (let d (vecGet decls (vecGet work j)))
        (if (!= (rgnWalkOne tc d 0) 0)
          {
            (markCallers tc bs marks next d)
            (set changed 1)
          })
        (set j (- j 1))
      })
    changed
  })

; Worklist rounds: each walks the previous round's frontier. A quiet
; frontier is verified by one full pass before convergence is
; claimed, and a change there restarts the ordinary rounds.
(pub :: rgnWork (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) CallerIndex Ints Ints Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnWork tc decls bs work marks round limit)
  (if (>= round limit)
    {
      (tcSetRgnFactsDone tc 2)
      1
    }
    {
      (let next vecNew)
      (if (== (rgnDirtyPass tc decls bs work marks next 0) 0)
        (if (== (rgnPass tc decls 0 0) 0)
          0
          (rgnRounds tc decls (+ round 1) limit))
        {
          (clearMarks marks next 0)
          (rgnWork tc decls bs next marks (+ round 1) limit)
        })
    }))

(pub fn (rgnRounds tc decls round limit)
  (if (>= round limit)
    ; The cap was reached with facts still moving. TWO consumers need
    ; to know, and both are set here so they cannot drift: word 28
    ; becomes 2, which `rgnTruncated` reads for AXIR's
    ; `#mir-truncated`, and the ANSWER becomes 1, which word 33
    ; carries to `restrictNoEscape` so an abandoned fixpoint stops
    ; buying the silence a proven one does. Word 28 == 2 and word 33
    ; == 1 are one fact spelled twice, written at one site.
    {
      (tcSetRgnFactsDone tc 2)
      1
    }
    (if (== (rgnPass tc decls 0 0) 0)
      0
      (rgnWork tc decls (callersIdxBuild tc decls) (allIndexes (vecLen decls))
        (flagsNew (vecLen decls)) (+ round 1) limit))))

; Whether the facts fixpoint stopped at its round cap rather than
; converging. Read by the AXSYM/AXIR projection, which must not
; publish an under-approximation without saying so.
(pub :: rgnFactsTruncated (-> TC Int))
(pub fn (rgnFactsTruncated tc)
  (if (== (tcRgnFactsDone tc) 2)
    1
    0))

; One walk over every `fn`; answers whether any facts changed.
(pub :: rgnPass (-> TC (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (rgnPass tc decls i report)
  (if (&& (== i 0) (&& (== report 1) (== (rgnFactsTruncated tc) 1)))
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3060" "region-escape"
          (if (== (vecLen decls) 0)
            0
            (nodeSpan (vecGet decls 0)))
          "region safety cannot be proved: lifetime analysis did not converge" None
          (Some
            "simplify the lifetime dependencies or split the call graph into smaller functions")))
      0
    }
    {
      (let mut changed 0)
      (for index in i..(vecLen decls)
        (if (!= (rgnWalkOne tc (vecGet decls index) report) 0)
          (set changed 1)))
      changed
    }))

; ---- `restrict(no-escape)` ----
; The claim: this declaration stores nothing it allocates into any of
; its parameters. Read off the facts, on the rail every restriction
; rides: refuted (AX3049, naming the parameter and the callee the
; store went through), unverifiable (AX3051, or AX3057 under
; `strict`) when the body calls something the walk cannot resolve,
; silent when proven.
; The first parameter a fresh value flows into through a RESOLVED
; door - this body or a named callee - or None. A flow recorded by an
; unresolved call is the conservative assumption, not a finding, and
; the claim over it is unverifiable rather than refuted.
(pub :: rgnResolvedCulprit (-> RgnFacts Int (Option Int)))
(pub fn (rgnResolvedCulprit f i)
  (if (>= i (rgnNparams f))
    None
    (if (&& (!= (& (rgnFlowsCUR f) (<< 1 i)) 0)
          (if (strEq (vecGet (rgnCulprits f) i) "<unresolved call>")
            false
            true))
      (Some i)
      (rgnResolvedCulprit f (+ i 1)))))

(pub :: restrictNoEscape (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (restrictNoEscape tc d r)
  {
    (rgnEnsureFacts tc)
    {
      (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName d)))
      (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
        {
          (let factsFound (fnEntRgn ent))
          (if (let (Some f) factsFound)
            {
              (let i (rgnResolvedCulprit f 0))
              (if (let (Some i) i)
                (emitRestrictViolated tc d r
                  (concat
                    (concat
                      (concat "the body stores a value it allocates into its parameter `"
                        (rgnCalleeParamName tc (nodeAName d) i))
                      "`")
                    (if (== (strLen (vecGet (rgnCulprits f) i)) 0)
                      " in its own body"
                      (concat (concat " through `" (bareOf (vecGet (rgnCulprits f) i))) "`")))
                  vecNew)
                (if (!= (rgnUnknown f) 0)
                  (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
                    "the body calls a value the compiler could not resolve - a parameter, a closure or a field - so where its allocations flow is not something the walk can settle"
                    vecNew)
                  ; No culprit and no unresolved call - but that is only
                  ; proof if the facts SETTLED. If the fixpoint hit its
                  ; round cap the facts are a lower bound, not an answer,
                  ; and the claim is unproven rather than proven.
                  (if (!= (tcRgnGaveUp tc) 0)
                    (emitRestrictUnverifiable tc d r
                      "the region fixpoint reached its round limit before the facts settled, so this claim is unproven rather than proven - the call chain below this declaration is deeper than the walk resolves. `strict` already means an unproven claim is refused; without it this is a warning"
                      vecNew)
                    0)))
            }
            0)
        }
        0)
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Every diagnostic for one module, in stage0's emission order.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(pub :: checkModule (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Units Diags))
(pub fn (checkModule entryDecls allDecls units)
  (tcOutVec (checkModuleTc entryDecls allDecls units)))

; The same three passes, answering the CHECKER rather than its
; diagnostics alone - `symbols` needs the populated tables after a
; clean check, and the diagnostics are still there behind `tcOut`.
(pub :: checkModuleTc (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Units TC))
(pub fn (checkModuleTc entryDecls allDecls units)
  {
    (let tc (checkModuleTcTyped entryDecls allDecls units))
    {
      (wordLowerApply tc)
      tc
    }
  })

; Typed source is available before representation lowering. Binding
; synthesis reads these identities and checks its accessors before the
; combined module lowers word structs for emission.
(pub :: checkModuleTcTyped (-> (Vec ASTNode) (Vec ASTNode) Units TC))
(pub fn (checkModuleTcTyped entryDecls allDecls units)
  ; Both indexes are built once, here, rather than by the
  ; passes - each is used by exactly one pass and neither
  ; survives the call.
  {
    (let tc (tcNew entryDecls allDecls units))
    {
      (let out (tcOutVec tc))
      {
        (checkDuplicates (dupIdxBuild entryDecls) entryDecls out 0)
        (checkReservedNames entryDecls out 0)
        (checkImportNames entryDecls allDecls out 0 0)
        (checkImportedImportNames units allDecls out 1)
        (checkInternalImports units entryDecls out)
        ; An import leaves the list before `allDecls` is built, so its
        ; tags are asked here, over the entry file's own.
        (checkStrayImportAxtags tc entryDecls 0)
        (checkMissingDefs (defIdxBuild allDecls) entryDecls out 0 (tcUnknownCallees tc))
        (scopedCollectAll tc allDecls 0)
        (tcWalkDecls tc 0)
        ; A witness noted outside any function body.
        (evSettle tc)
        ; The unsafe boundary's questions that needed every body typed
        ; first (R-B6): a forging callee under `restrict(no-unsafe)`,
        ; and the forging bodies' rows.
        (unsafeLatePass tc)
        (computeCheckRequests tc 0)
        ; Stage S3's escape rule, after every body has been checked:
        ; it reads the call graph the effect fixpoint built and the
        ; signatures as declared, and it is ABSENT - not silent - for a
        ; program whose signatures name no region (`rgnCheckAll`).
        (rgnCheckAll tc)
        ; After every body: an unsigned `main`'s type is its body's,
        ; which `tcCheckFn` writes back only once it has checked it.
        (checkMainResultType tc allDecls)
        tc
      }
    }
  })

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Constant time: `;@axiom:ct(a, *p)` (AX3092, AX3093).
;
; The claim names a function's secret parameters: `a` for a secret
; value and `*p` for an address whose memory is secret. Every other
; parameter is public. The body is walked with a taint on every
; binding - 0 public, 1 an address into secret memory, 2 a secret
; value - one round after another until no taint grows, and the last
; round's findings are reported: a branch on a secret (`if`, `while`,
; `&&`, `||`, a `match` of more than one arm), a load or store at an
; address computed from one, a division with a secret operand, and a
; secret handed to a function whose own claim doesn't name that
; parameter. A call to a claimed function answers the taint of that
; function's result, found once by walking its body under its own
; claim.
;
; A taint lives on the binding, keyed by the node that made it, so two
; bindings that share a name in different scopes stay apart. This is
; the source half of the evidence: `scripts/check-crypto.sh` asks the
; same questions of the optimised IR (`scripts/lib/ct-taint.py`),
; where a branch the optimiser put back would show.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
(struct CtWalk
  (tc : TC)
  (fnName : String)
  (claim : String)
  (keys : (Vec Addr))
  (taints : (Vec Int))
  (names : (Vec String))
  (rows : (Vec Int))
  (spans : (Vec Int))
  (msgs : (Vec String))
  (labels : (Vec String))
  (grew : (Vec Int)))

(pub :: ctJoin (-> Int Int Int))
(pub fn (ctJoin a b)
  (if (> a b)
    a
    b))

; Every name in every `ct(...)` tag of `tags`, pushed once each; a
; tag naming nothing pushes the empty name, so it is reported rather
; than read as a claim of nothing.
(pub :: ctCollect (-> Axtags Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (ctCollect tags cs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen tags))
    0
    {
      (let content (axtagContent (vecGet tags i)))
      {
        (let op (strFindByte content 40 0))
        {
          (if (strEq
                (if (let (Some opening) op)
                  (strSlice content 0 opening)
                  content)
                "ct")
            (restrictSplit (axtagListValue content op) cs))
          (ctCollect tags cs (+ i 1))
        }
      }
    }))

; A function's claim, from its signature and its `fn` together. Empty
; when it makes none.
(pub :: ctClaimOf (-> TC ASTNode Strings))
(pub fn (ctClaimOf tc d)
  {
    (let cs vecNew)
    {
      (if (let (Some tags) (sigAxtagsFor tc (nodeAName d)))
        (ctCollect tags cs 0)
        0)
      (ctCollect (nodeAxtagsOf d) cs 0)
      cs
    }
  })

; What a claim says of parameter `p`: 2 a secret value, 1 an address
; into secret memory, 0 public.
(pub :: ctParamKind (-> Strings String Int))
(pub fn (ctParamKind cs p)
  (if (== (strInVec cs p 0) 1)
    2
    (if (== (strInVec cs (concat "*" p) 0) 1)
      1
      0)))

; The claim as written, `a, *p`, for messages.
(pub :: ctClaimText (-> Strings String))
(pub fn (ctClaimText cs)
  {
    (let mut s "")
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen cs)
        (set s (if (== i 0)
          (vecGet cs i)
          (concat s (concat ", " (vecGet cs i))))))
      s
    }
  })

; The declarations that make a claim, found once per compilation and
; cached beside typed result states: unseen, being walked, or a known
; result taint. A recursive walk answers the conservative secret taint.
(pub :: ctTable (-> TC (Vec ASTNode)))
(pub fn (ctTable tc)
  (if (let (Some table) (tcCtClaimed tc))
    table
    {
      (let decls (tcAllDeclsVec tc))
      (let tab vecNew)
      (let sums vecNew)
      (for dd in decls
        (if (== (nodeTag dd) TAG_D_FN)
          (if (> (vecLen (ctClaimOf tc dd)) 0)
            {
              (vecPush tab dd)
              (vecPush sums CtUnseen)
            })))
      (tcSetCtResults tc sums)
      (tcSetCtClaimed tc tab)
      tab
    }))

(pub :: ctSums (-> TC (Vec CtResult)))
(pub fn (ctSums tc)
  tc.unsafe.ctResults)

; The declared name a call's head resolves to, the way the checker
; resolves it: a call names its callee bare, even across modules.
(pub :: ctResolve (-> TC String String))
(pub fn (ctResolve tc n)
  {
    (let entFound (findFnEnt tc n))
    (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
      (fnEntName ent)
      n)
  })

; Run `f` over `d` with the checker's current module set to `d`'s,
; which is what `findFnEnt` resolves a bare name against.
(pub :: ctInModule (-> TC ASTNode (-> ASTNode Int) Int))
(pub fn (ctInModule tc d f)
  {
    (let saved (tcCurModule tc))
    {
      (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
      {
        (let r (f d))
        {
          (tcSetCurModule tc saved)
          r
        }
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: ctTableIndex (-> Nodes String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (ctTableIndex tab name)
  {
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut at None)
    (while (&& (< i (vecLen tab)) (isNone at))
      {
        (if (strEq (nodeAName (vecGet tab i)) name)
          (set at (Some i)))
        (set i (+ i 1))
      })
    at
  })

; The result taint of the `k`th claimed function: its body walked once
; under its own claim. A function reached again while its own walk is
; under way answers secret, which is never wrong.
(pub :: ctSummary (-> TC Int Int))
(pub fn (ctSummary tc k)
  {
    (let sums (ctSums tc))
    (match (vecGet sums k)
      ((CtWalking) 2)
      ((CtKnown taint) taint)
      ((CtUnseen)
        {
          (let dd (vecGet (ctTable tc) k))
          (vecSet sums k CtWalking)
          (let cs (ctClaimOf tc dd))
          (let result
            (ctInModule tc dd
              (lambda (x) (ctRounds (ctWalkNew tc (nodeAName x) (ctClaimText cs)) x cs))))
          (vecSet sums k (CtKnown result))
          result
        }
      ))
  })

; A walk of the function named `name`, whose claim reads `claim`.
(pub :: ctWalkNew (-> TC String String CtWalk))
(pub fn (ctWalkNew tc name claim)
  (CtWalk
    :tc tc
    :fnName (bareOf name)
    :claim claim
    :keys vecNew
    :taints vecNew
    :names vecNew
    :rows vecNew
    :spans vecNew
    :msgs vecNew
    :labels vecNew
    :grew (vecPush vecNew 0)))

; The row a binding's key names, made on first sight.
(pub :: ctRowFor (-> CtWalk Addr Int))
(pub fn (ctRowFor w key)
  {
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut at (- 0 1))
    {
      (while (&& (< i (vecLen w.keys)) (< at 0))
        {
          (if (addrEq (vecGet w.keys i) key)
            (set at i))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      (if (< at 0)
        {
          (vecPush w.keys key)
          (vecPush w.taints 0)
          (- (vecLen w.keys) 1)
        }
        at)
    }
  })

(pub :: ctRaise (-> CtWalk Int Int Int))
(pub fn (ctRaise w row t)
  (if (> t (vecGet w.taints row))
    {
      (vecSet w.taints row t)
      (vecSet w.grew 0 1)
      0
    }
    0))

(pub :: ctBind (-> CtWalk String Addr Int Int))
(pub fn (ctBind w name key t)
  {
    (let row (ctRowFor w key))
    {
      (ctRaise w row t)
      (vecPush w.names name)
      (vecPush w.rows row)
      0
    }
  })

; The row the innermost binding of `name` uses, or None for a name bound
; outside the body (a function, a constant), which is public.
(pub :: ctScopeRow (-> CtWalk String (Option Int)))
(pub fn (ctScopeRow w name)
  {
    (let mut i (- (vecLen w.names) 1))
    (let mut at (:: None (Option Int)))
    {
      (while (&& (>= i 0) (isNone at))
        {
          (if (strEq (vecGet w.names i) name)
            (set at (Some (vecGet w.rows i))))
          (set i (- i 1))
        })
      at
    }
  })

(pub :: ctUnscope (-> CtWalk Int Int))
(pub fn (ctUnscope w mark)
  {
    (while (> (vecLen w.names) mark)
      {
        (vecPop w.names)
        (vecPop w.rows)
      })
    0
  })

(pub :: ctHit (-> CtWalk Int String String Int))
(pub fn (ctHit w span msg label)
  {
    (vecPush w.spans span)
    (vecPush w.msgs
      (concat msg (concat " (`" (concat w.fnName (concat "` claims `ct(" (concat w.claim ")`)"))))))
    (vecPush w.labels label)
    0
  })

; The name a node's span covers, for a message that leads with it:
; a variable's, or an application's head's.
(pub :: ctSpanText (-> ASTNode String))
(pub fn (ctSpanText e)
  (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
    (bareOf (nodeAName e))
    (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
      {
        (let h (spineHead e))
        (if (== (nodeTag h) TAG_E_VAR)
          (bareOf (nodeAName h))
          "")
      }
      "")))

; A branch on a secret, reported at its condition: an `if` node carries
; no span of its own.
(pub :: ctBranchHit (-> CtWalk ASTNode ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (ctBranchHit w e cond kw)
  {
    (let txt (ctSpanText cond))
    (ctHit w
      (if (== (nodeSpan cond) 0)
        (nodeSpan e)
        (nodeSpan cond))
      (if (== (strLen txt) 0)
        (concat (concat "`" kw) "` branches on a secret")
        (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" txt) "` is secret, and `") kw) "` branches on it"))
      "a branch on a secret")
  })

(pub :: ctEvalVec (-> CtWalk (Vec ASTNode) Int))
(pub fn (ctEvalVec w es)
  {
    (let mut t 0)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen es)
        (set t (ctEval w (vecGet es i))))
      t
    }
  })

(pub :: ctEvalArgs (-> CtWalk Nodes Ints))
(pub fn (ctEvalArgs w es)
  {
    (let ts vecNew)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen es)
        (vecPush ts (ctEval w (vecGet es i))))
      ts
    }
  })

; The highest taint among the first `n` of `ts`.
(pub :: ctMaxTo (-> Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (ctMaxTo ts n)
  {
    (let mut i 0)
    (let mut m 0)
    {
      (while (&& (< i n) (< i (vecLen ts)))
        {
          (set m (ctJoin m (vecGet ts i)))
          (set i (+ i 1))
        })
      m
    }
  })

(pub :: ctIsArith (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (ctIsArith n)
  (|| (strEq n "+")
    (|| (strEq n "-")
      (|| (strEq n "*")
        (|| (strEq n "&")
          (|| (strEq n "|") (|| (strEq n "^") (|| (strEq n "<<") (strEq n ">>")))))))))

(pub :: ctIsCompare (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (ctIsCompare n)
  (|| (strEq n "==")
    (|| (strEq n "!=")
      (|| (strEq n "<") (|| (strEq n ">") (|| (strEq n "<=") (|| (strEq n ">=") (strEq n "!"))))))))

(pub :: ctIsCtor (-> String Bool))
(pub fn (ctIsCtor b)
  (if (== (strLen b) 0)
    false
    (&& (>= (strByte b 0) 65) (<= (strByte b 0) 90))))

(pub :: ctEvalApp (-> CtWalk ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (ctEvalApp w e)
  {
    (let head (spineHead e))
    (let ts (ctEvalArgs w (spineArgVec e vecNew)))
    (if (== (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
      (ctEvalNamed w e (nodeAName head) ts)
      {
        (ctEval w head)
        (if (> (ctMaxTo ts (vecLen ts)) 0)
          (ctHit w (nodeSpan e) "a call through a value gets a secret"
            "a secret to an unclaimed call"))
        0
      })
  })

(pub :: ctEvalNamed (-> CtWalk ASTNode String Ints Int))
(pub fn (ctEvalNamed w e n ts)
  {
    (let m (ctMaxTo ts (vecLen ts)))
    (let b (bareOf n))
    (if (ctIsArith n)
      m
      (if (ctIsCompare n)
        (if (== m 2)
          2
          0)
        (if (|| (strEq n "/") (strEq n "%"))
          {
            (if (== m 2)
              (ctHit w (nodeSpan e) (concat (concat "`" n) "` has a secret operand")
                "a division by or of a secret"))
            (if (> m 0)
              2
              0)
          }
          (if (|| (strEq n "&&") (strEq n "||"))
            {
              (if (== m 2)
                (ctHit w (nodeSpan e) (concat (concat "`" n) "` short-circuits on a secret")
                  "a branch on a secret"))
              m
            }
            (if (strStartsWith n "__load")
              (if (== m 2)
                {
                  (ctHit w (nodeSpan e)
                    (concat (concat "`" n) "` reads at an address computed from a secret")
                    "an address from a secret")
                  2
                }
                (if (== m 1)
                  2
                  0))
              (if (strStartsWith n "__store")
                {
                  (if (== (ctMaxTo ts 2) 2)
                    (ctHit w (nodeSpan e)
                      (concat (concat "`" n) "` writes at an address computed from a secret")
                      "an address from a secret"))
                  0
                }
                (if (== (isAsmPrim n) 1)
                  (if (> m 0)
                    2
                    0)
                  (if (ctIsCtor b)
                    (if (> m 0)
                      2
                      0)
                    (ctEvalCall w e n ts m)))))))))
  })

; A call to a named function. Public arguments make a public result.
; A secret may go only where the callee's own claim names that
; parameter - a secret value to `x`, an address into secret memory to
; `x` or `*x` - and the result is then the callee's result taint.
(pub :: ctEvalCall (-> CtWalk ASTNode String Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (ctEvalCall w e n ts m)
  (if (== m 0)
    0
    {
      (let k (ctTableIndex (ctTable w.tc) (ctResolve w.tc n)))
      (if (let (Some index) k)
        {
          (let dd (vecGet (ctTable w.tc) index))
          {
            (ctCheckArgs w e dd (ctClaimOf w.tc dd) ts 0)
            (ctSummary w.tc index)
          }
        }
        {
          (ctHit w (nodeSpan e)
            (concat (concat "`" (bareOf n)) "` gets a secret, and makes no `ct(...)` claim")
            "a secret to an unclaimed call")
          0
        })
    }))

(pub :: ctCheckArgs (-> CtWalk ASTNode ASTNode Strings Ints Int Int))
(pub fn (ctCheckArgs w e dd cs ts i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ts))
    0
    {
      (let t (vecGet ts i))
      (let p (restrictParamName dd i))
      {
        (if (&& (> t 0) (== (ctParamKind cs p) 0))
          (ctHit w (nodeSpan e)
            (concat
              (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName dd))) "` gets a secret for `") p)
              "`, which its `ct(...)` claim doesn't name")
            "a secret to a public parameter")
          (if (&& (== t 2) (== (ctParamKind cs p) 1))
            (ctHit w (nodeSpan e)
              (concat
                (concat
                  (concat
                    (concat
                      (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName dd))) "` gets a secret value for `") p)
                    "`, which its claim names as an address, `*")
                  p)
                "`")
              "a secret to a public parameter")))
        (ctCheckArgs w e dd cs ts (+ i 1))
      }
    }))

; A pattern's binders take the scrutinee's taint.
(pub :: ctBindPat (-> CtWalk ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (ctBindPat w pat t)
  (if (nodeSame pat nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag pat))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (ctBind w (nodeAName pat) (addrOf pat) t)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          {
            (ctBindPat w (nodeBChild pat) t)
            (ctBindPatHead w (nodeAChild pat) t)
          }
          (if (== tag TAG_P_CONNAMED)
            (ctBindPats w (nodeCChildren pat) 0 t)
            0)))
    }))

(pub :: ctBindPatHead (-> CtWalk ASTNode Int Int))
(pub fn (ctBindPatHead w pat t)
  (if (== (nodeTag pat) TAG_E_APP)
    {
      (ctBindPat w (nodeBChild pat) t)
      (ctBindPatHead w (nodeAChild pat) t)
    }
    0))

(pub :: ctBindPats (-> CtWalk (Vec ASTNode) Int Int Int))
(pub fn (ctBindPats w pats i t)
  (if (>= i (vecLen pats))
    0
    {
      (ctBindPat w (vecGet pats i) t)
      (ctBindPats w pats (+ i 1) t)
    }))

(pub :: ctEvalArms (-> CtWalk (Vec ASTNode) Int Int))
(pub fn (ctEvalArms w arms s)
  {
    (let mut r 0)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen arms)
        {
          (let a (vecGet arms i))
          (let mark (vecLen w.names))
          {
            (ctBindPat w (nodeAChild a) s)
            (set r (ctJoin r (ctEval w (nodeBChild a))))
            (ctUnscope w mark)
          }
        })
      r
    }
  })

(pub :: ctEval (-> CtWalk ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (ctEval w e)
  (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
        (if (let (Some row) (ctScopeRow w (nodeAName e)))
          (vecGet w.taints row)
          0)
        (if (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (ctEvalApp w e)
          (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
            {
              (let t (ctEval w (nodeBChild e)))
              (let mark (vecLen w.names))
              {
                (ctBind w (nodeAName e) (addrOf e) t)
                {
                  (let r (ctEval w (nodeCChild e)))
                  {
                    (ctUnscope w mark)
                    r
                  }
                }
              }
            }
            (if (== tag TAG_E_IF)
              {
                (let c (ctEval w (nodeAChild e)))
                {
                  (if (== c 2)
                    (ctBranchHit w e (nodeAChild e) "if"))
                  (ctJoin c (ctJoin (ctEval w (nodeBChild e)) (ctEval w (nodeCChild e))))
                }
              }
              (if (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
                {
                  (let c (ctEval w (nodeAChild e)))
                  {
                    (if (== c 2)
                      (ctBranchHit w e (nodeAChild e) "while"))
                    (ctEvalVec w (nodeBChildren e))
                    0
                  }
                }
                (if (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
                  (ctEvalVec w (nodeAChildren e))
                  (if (== tag TAG_E_MATCH)
                    {
                      (let s (ctEval w (nodeAChild e)))
                      (let arms (nodeBChildren e))
                      {
                        (if (&& (== s 2) (> (vecLen arms) 1))
                          (ctBranchHit w e (nodeAChild e) "match"))
                        (ctEvalArms w arms s)
                      }
                    }
                    (if (== tag TAG_E_SET)
                      {
                        (let t (ctEval w (nodeBChild e)))
                        (if (let (Some row) (ctScopeRow w (nodeAName e)))
                          (ctRaise w row t))
                        0
                      }
                      (if (== tag TAG_E_REGION)
                        (ctEval w (nodeBChild e))
                        (if (== tag TAG_E_LAM)
                          {
                            (ctEval w (nodeBChild e))
                            0
                          }
                          (if (== tag TAG_E_FIELD)
                            (if (> (ctEval w (nodeAChild e)) 0)
                              2
                              0)
                            (if (== tag TAG_E_SETF)
                              {
                                (ctEval w (nodeAChild e))
                                (ctEval w (nodeCChild e))
                                0
                              }
                              (if (== tag TAG_E_HANDLE)
                                {
                                  (let t (ctEval w (nodeAChild e)))
                                  {
                                    (ctEval w (nodeCChild e))
                                    t
                                  }
                                }
                                0)))))))))))))
    }))

(pub :: ctBindParams (-> CtWalk Strings Strings Int Int))
(pub fn (ctBindParams w ps cs i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen ps))
    0
    {
      (ctBind w (vecGet ps i) (addrOf (- 0 (+ i 1))) (ctParamKind cs (vecGet ps i)))
      (ctBindParams w ps cs (+ i 1))
    }))

; Walk `d`'s body under claim `cs` until no taint grows, and answer the
; taint of its result. `w`'s findings are the last round's.
(pub :: ctRounds (-> CtWalk ASTNode Strings Int))
(pub fn (ctRounds w d cs)
  {
    (let mut round 0)
    (let mut r 0)
    (let mut more true)
    {
      (while more
        {
          (vecSet w.grew 0 0)
          (while (> (vecLen w.spans) 0)
            {
              (vecPop w.spans)
              (vecPop w.msgs)
              (vecPop w.labels)
            })
          (ctBindParams w (nodeBNames d) cs 0)
          (set r (ctEval w (nodeCChild d)))
          (ctUnscope w 0)
          (set round (+ round 1))
          (set more (&& (== (vecGet w.grew 0) 1) (< round 12)))
        })
      r
    }
  })

; AX3093 for a claim that names nothing, or names something that isn't
; a parameter. Answers 1 when the claim is well formed.
(pub :: ctClaimShape (-> TC ASTNode Strings Int))
(pub fn (ctClaimShape tc d cs)
  {
    (let ps (nodeBNames d))
    (let mut ok 1)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen cs)
        {
          (let c (vecGet cs i))
          (let p
            (if (strStartsWith (vecGet cs i) "*")
              (strSlice (vecGet cs i) 1 (- (strLen (vecGet cs i)) 1))
              (vecGet cs i)))
          (if (== (strLen p) 0)
            {
              (set ok 0)
              (emitCtMalformed tc d
                (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d)))
                  "` claims `ct(...)` and names no parameter"))
            }
            (if (! (== (strInVec ps p 0) 1))
              {
                (set ok 0)
                (emitCtMalformed tc d
                  (concat (concat (concat (concat "`" (bareOf (nodeAName d))) "` claims `ct(") c)
                    (concat (concat ")`, and `" p) "` isn't one of its parameters")))
              }))
        })
      ok
    }
  })

(pub :: emitCtMalformed (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(pub fn (emitCtMalformed tc d msg)
  (if (== (nodeSpan d) 0)
    0
    (emitDiag tc
      (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3093" "ct-claim-malformed" (nodeSpan d) msg None
        (Some
          "`ct(...)` names the parameters that carry secrets: `name` for a secret value, `*name` for an address whose memory is secret. Name only this function's own parameters")))))

(pub :: emitCtHits (-> TC ASTNode CtWalk Int Int))
(pub fn (emitCtHits tc d w i)
  (if (>= i (vecLen w.spans))
    0
    {
      (emitDiag tc
        (diagSetLabel
          (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3092" "ct-claim-violated"
            (if (== (vecGet w.spans i) 0)
              (nodeSpan d)
              (vecGet w.spans i))
            (vecGet w.msgs i) None
            (Some
              "a function that claims `ct(...)` promises its timing doesn't depend on the parameters it names. A branch, a memory address or a division that depends on one breaks the promise, and so does handing one to a function whose own claim doesn't name it. Compute both sides and choose between them with the masks in `Crypto.Ct` (`ctSelect`, `ctMaskEq`), claim the callee's parameter, or take the parameter out of this claim"))
          (vecGet w.labels i)))
      (emitCtHits tc d w (+ i 1))
    }))

; The check itself, for one function: nothing to do without a claim.
(pub :: checkCtClaim (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(pub fn (checkCtClaim tc d)
  {
    (let cs (ctClaimOf tc d))
    (if (== (vecLen cs) 0)
      0
      (if (== (ctClaimShape tc d cs) 0)
        0
        {
          (let w (ctWalkNew tc (nodeAName d) (ctClaimText cs)))
          {
            (ctInModule tc d (lambda (x) (ctRounds w x cs)))
            (emitCtHits tc d w 0)
          }
        }))
  })

(pub :: checkExprH (-> TC ASTNode Int ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkExprH tc e inHead)
  {
    (let cache (tcFormatChecked tc))
    {
      (let ty
        (if (if (>= (vecLen cache) 2)
          (nodeSame (vecGet cache (- (vecLen cache) 2)) e)
          false)
          (vecGet cache (- (vecLen cache) 1))
          (checkExprHCore tc e inHead)))
      {
        (if (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
          false
          (&& (== (isPendingNode ty) 0) (|| (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LAM) (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP))))
          (setNodeBinderTyChild e
            (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
              (nodeBuildEvstampResultOptional (tyResolve ty) (evClassOf tc (tyResolve ty))
                (tcCurDeclNode tc))
              (tyResolve ty)))
          e)
        (computePostExpr tc e inHead)
        ty
      }
    }
  })

(pub :: checkLam (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkLam tc e)
  {
    (let ty (checkLamCore tc e))
    {
      (if (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
        false
        (&& (== (isPendingNode ty) 0) (|| (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LAM) (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP))))
        (setNodeBinderTyChild e (tyResolve ty))
        e)
      ty
    }
  })

(pub :: checkLamAgainst (-> TC ASTNode ASTNode ASTNode))
(pub fn (checkLamAgainst tc e arrow)
  {
    (let ty (checkLamAgainstCore tc e arrow))
    {
      (if (if (nodeSame e nilNode)
        false
        (&& (== (isPendingNode ty) 0) (|| (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LAM) (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP))))
        (setNodeBinderTyChild e (tyResolve ty))
        e)
      ty
    }
  })

; A compute callback is a closed numeric program. Local scalar mutation
; remains Mut; no reference, runtime observer or opaque callee is admitted.
(struct ComputeRequest
  (body : ASTNode)
  (bound : Names)
  (module : (Option String))
  (unit : Int)
  (site : Int)
  (siteUnit : Int))

(struct ComputeWalk
  (tc : TC)
  (site : Int)
  (unit : Int)
  (seen : (Vec FnEnt))
  (mut failed : Int))

(:: emitCompute (-> TC Int String Int))
(fn (emitCompute tc span message)
  (emitDiag tc
    (mkDiag SEV_ERROR "AX3131" "compute-callback" span message None
      (Some
        "pass a numeric lambda, a checked named function, or the exact parameter carrying compute; use canonical primitive captures, numeric operations and local accumulators; raw/runtime observers, references, heap stores, allocation and opaque calls are refused"))))

(:: computePrimitive (-> TC ASTNode Bool))
(fn (computePrimitive tc ty0)
  (if (nodeSame ty0 nilNode)
    false
    {
      (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve ty0) 0))
      (if (|| (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_CON) (!= (vecLen (nodeBChildren ty)) 0))
        false
        {
          (let name (nodeAName ty))
          (&& (== (evClassOf tc ty) 0)
            (|| (> (integerTypeCode name) 0) (sizedTyIn name "Float Bool Char")))
        })
    }))

(:: computeWordArrow (-> TC ASTNode Bool))
(fn (computeWordArrow tc ty0)
  (if (nodeSame ty0 nilNode)
    false
    {
      (let ty (tyExpandAliases tc (tyResolve ty0) 0))
      (if (!= (nodeTag ty) TAG_T_ARR)
        false
        (&& (tyIsBareInt (tyExpandAliases tc (nodeAChild ty) 0))
          (tyIsBareInt (tyExpandAliases tc (nodeBChild ty) 0))))
    }))

(:: checkComputeTag (-> TC ASTNode String Int))
(fn (checkComputeTag tc d name)
  {
    (let entFound (findFnEntAnywhere tc (nodeAName d)))
    (match (strVecIdx (nodeBNames d) name)
      ((None)
        (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan d)
          (concat "compute contract names no parameter: `" (concat name "`")))
      )
      ((Some pos)
        (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
          (if (if (let (Some parameter) (nthParamTy (fnEntTy ent) pos))
            (computeWordArrow tc parameter)
            false)
            0
            (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan d)
              "a compute parameter requires the declared callback type (-> Int Int)"))
          0)
      ))
  })

; A spelling is not a proof: compare the actual frame entry with the
; declaration's parameter entry, so a shadowing let/lambda cannot inherit it.
(:: computeForwarded (-> TC ASTNode Bool))
(fn (computeForwarded tc e)
  (if (let (Some d) (tcCurDeclNode tc))
    (if (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR)
      false
      (match (strVecIdx (nodeBNames d) (nodeAName e))
        ((None) false)
        ((Some pos)
          (if (let (Some ent) (findFnEntAnywhere tc (nodeAName d)))
            (if (let (Some frame) (scopeFindFrame tc (nodeAName e)))
              {
                (let index (+ (tcFrameBase tc) pos))
                (&& (< index (vecLen (tcScopeVec tc)))
                  (&& (== (as frame Int) (as (vecGet (tcScopeVec tc) index) Int))
                    (== (intInVec (fnEntCompute ent) pos 0) 1)))
              }
              false)
            false)
        )))
    false))

(:: computeCaptureNames (-> TC ASTNode Names Bool))
(fn (computeCaptureNames tc lam names)
  {
    (let occ (capScanInto lam vecNew vecNew))
    (let mut valid true)
    {
      (for i in 0..(vecLen occ)
        {
          (let v (vecGet occ i))
          (let name (nodeAName (vecGet occ i)))
          (if (let (Some entry) (scopeFindFrame tc name))
            (if (isNone (strVecIdx names name))
              (if (computePrimitive tc entry.ty)
                (vecPushStr names name)
                {
                  (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan v)
                    (concat "compute callback captures non-primitive `"
                      (concat name (concat "` of type " (tyRender (tyResolve entry.ty))))))
                  (set valid false)
                })))
        })
      valid
    }
  })

(:: computeRequest (-> TC ASTNode Names (Option String) Int Int Int))
(fn (computeRequest tc body bound module unit site)
  {
    (vecPush (tcComputeRequests tc)
      (ComputeRequest
        :body body
        :bound bound
        :module module
        :unit unit
        :site site
        :siteUnit (tcCurUnit tc)))
    0
  })

(:: computeValue (-> TC ASTNode Int))
(fn (computeValue tc e)
  (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_IF)
    {
      (computeValue tc (nodeBChild e))
      (computeValue tc (nodeCChild e))
      0
    }
    (if (computeForwarded tc e)
      0
      (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_LAM)
        {
          (let names vecNew)
          (if (computeCaptureNames tc e names)
            {
              (capPushAll (nodeANames e) 0 names)
              (computeRequest tc (nodeBChild e) names (tcCurModule tc) (tcCurUnit tc) (nodeSpan e))
            }
            0)
        }
        (if (&& (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_VAR) (isNone (scopeFindFrame tc (nodeAName e))))
          {
            (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName e)))
            (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
              (if (&& (fnEntArityIs ent 1)
                    (&& (== (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0) (== (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0)))
                (if (let (Some d) (rgnDeclOf tc (fnEntName ent)))
                  (computeRequest tc (nodeCChild d) (capBoundOf (nodeBNames d)) (nodeModuleOpt d)
                    (unitIndexFor (tcUnits tc) (nodeModuleOpt d)) (nodeSpan e))
                  (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan e) "compute callback has no visible function body"))
                (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan e)
                  "compute callback must be a non-nullary numeric function with a visible body"))
              (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan e)
                "compute callback must be a non-nullary numeric function with a visible body"))
          }
          (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan e)
            "compute callback is opaque here or its parameter has no matching compute contract"))))))

(:: computePostExpr (-> TC ASTNode Int Int))
(fn (computePostExpr tc e inHead)
  (if (|| (nodeSame e nilNode) (|| (!= inHead 0) (== (tcComputeEnabled tc) 0)))
    0
    {
      (let head
        (if (== (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
          (spineHead e)
          e))
      (if (|| (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR) (isSome (scopeFindFrame tc (nodeAName head))))
        0
        {
          (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName head)))
          (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
            {
              (let indices (fnEntCompute ent))
              (if (== (vecLen indices) 0)
                0
                (if (!= (nodeTag e) TAG_E_APP)
                  (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan e)
                    "a function with a compute contract must be called directly; a function value erases the precondition")
                  {
                    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
                    {
                      (if (fnEntNeedsArgs ent (vecLen args))
                        (emitCompute tc (nodeSpan e)
                          "a function with a compute contract requires all declared arguments in one call"))
                      (for i in 0..(vecLen indices)
                        {
                          (let pos (vecGet indices i))
                          (if (< pos (vecLen args))
                            (computeValue tc (vecGet args pos)))
                        })
                      0
                    }
                  }))
            }
            0)
        })
    }))

(:: computeFail (-> ComputeWalk String Int))
(fn (computeFail walk message)
  (if (!= walk.failed 0)
    0
    {
      (let tc walk.tc)
      (let saved walk.tc.curUnit)
      {
        (set walk.failed 1)
        (tcSetCurUnit tc walk.unit)
        (emitCompute tc walk.site message)
        (tcSetCurUnit tc saved)
        0
      }
    }))

; This is a closed operation basis, not inference from an empty effect
; row: trusted loads and runtime observers can have empty caller rows.
(:: computeBuiltin (-> String Bool))
(fn (computeBuiltin name)
  (|| (== (isNumOp name) 1)
    (sizedTyIn name "&& || ! __intToFloat __floatToInt __floatToBits __floatFromBits")))

(:: computeOpaqueHelper (-> ComputeWalk FnEnt Int))
(fn (computeOpaqueHelper walk ent)
  (computeFail walk
    (concat "compute callback reaches an opaque or foreign function `"
      (concat (fnEntName ent) "`"))))

(:: computeWalkHelper (-> ComputeWalk FnEnt Int))
(fn (computeWalkHelper walk ent)
  (if (== (fnEntryIn walk.seen ent 0) 1)
    0
    {
      (let tc walk.tc)
      (if (let (Some d) (rgnDeclOf walk.tc (fnEntName ent)))
        (if (!= (fnEntIsEffectOp ent) 0)
          (computeOpaqueHelper walk ent)
          {
            (let savedModule (tcCurModule tc))
            {
              (vecPush walk.seen ent)
              (tcSetCurModule tc (nodeModuleOpt d))
              (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeCChild d) (capBoundOf (nodeBNames d)) vecNew)
              (tcSetCurModule tc savedModule)
              0
            }
          })
        (computeOpaqueHelper walk ent))
    }))

(:: computeWalkVar (-> ComputeWalk ASTNode Names Int))
(fn (computeWalkVar walk e bound)
  {
    (let name (nodeAName e))
    (if (|| (isSome (strVecIdx bound name)) (sizedTyIn name "true false"))
      0
      (if (let (Some ent) (findFnEnt walk.tc name))
        (if (fnEntMayTakeArgs ent)
            (computeFail walk
              (concat "compute callback uses an opaque or function value `" (concat name "`")))
          (!= ent.isBuiltin 0)
            (computeFail walk
              (concat "compute callback observes runtime state through `" (concat name "`")))
          (computeWalkHelper walk ent))
        (computeFail walk
          (concat "compute callback uses an opaque or function value `" (concat name "`")))))
  })

(:: computeWalkVec (-> ComputeWalk Nodes Names Names Int))
(fn (computeWalkVec walk expressions bound mutable)
  {
    (for i in 0..(vecLen expressions)
      (computeWalkExpr walk (vecGet expressions i) bound mutable))
    0
  })

(:: computeWalkApp (-> ComputeWalk ASTNode Names Names Int))
(fn (computeWalkApp walk e bound mutable)
  {
    (let tc walk.tc)
    (let head (spineHead e))
    (let args (spineArgVec e vecNew))
    (if (!= (nodeTag head) TAG_E_VAR)
      (computeFail walk "compute callback calls an opaque expression")
      {
        (let name (nodeAName head))
        (let entFound (findFnEnt tc (nodeAName head)))
        (if (isSome (strVecIdx bound name))
          (computeFail walk (concat "compute callback calls opaque local `" (concat name "`")))
          (if (&& (isValueCastForm name)
                (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                  (!= (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0)
                  true))
            (if (&& (> (vecLen args) 0)
                  (if (let (Some ty) (rgnCheckedTy e))
                    (computePrimitive tc ty)
                    false))
              (computeWalkExpr walk (vecGet args (- (vecLen args) 1)) bound mutable)
              (computeFail walk "compute callback converts to a non-primitive type"))
            {
              (computeWalkVec walk args bound mutable)
              (if (let (Some ent) entFound)
                (if (!= (fnEntIsBuiltin ent) 0)
                  (if (computeBuiltin name)
                    0
                    (computeFail walk
                      (concat "compute callback reaches non-numeric primitive `"
                        (concat name "`"))))
                  (computeWalkHelper walk ent))
                (computeFail walk
                  (concat "compute callback reaches unknown operation `" (concat name "`"))))
              0
            }))
      })
  })

(:: computeWalkExpr (-> ComputeWalk ASTNode Names Names Int))
(fn (computeWalkExpr walk e bound mutable)
  (if (|| (nodeSame e nilNode) (!= walk.failed 0))
    0
    {
      (let tag (nodeTag e))
      (if (|| (== tag TAG_E_INT) (|| (== tag TAG_E_FLT) (== tag TAG_E_CHAR)))
          0
        (== tag TAG_E_VAR)
          (computeWalkVar walk e bound)
        (== tag TAG_E_APP)
          (computeWalkApp walk e bound mutable)
        (|| (== tag TAG_E_LET) (== tag TAG_E_LETM))
          {
            (let mark (vecLen bound))
            (let mutMark (vecLen mutable))
            {
              (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeBChild e) bound mutable)
              (vecPush bound (nodeAName e))
              (if (== tag TAG_E_LETM)
                (vecPush mutable (nodeAName e)))
              (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeCChild e) bound mutable)
              (scopeTrunc bound mark)
              (scopeTrunc mutable mutMark)
              0
            }
          }
        (== tag TAG_E_SET)
          (if (isNone (strVecIdx mutable (nodeAName e)))
            (computeFail walk "compute callback assigns to captured or non-local state")
            (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeBChild e) bound mutable))
        (== tag TAG_E_IF)
          {
            (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeAChild e) bound mutable)
            (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeBChild e) bound mutable)
            (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeCChild e) bound mutable)
            0
          }
        (== tag TAG_E_BEGIN)
          (computeWalkVec walk (nodeAChildren e) bound mutable)
        (== tag TAG_E_WHILE)
          {
            (computeWalkExpr walk (nodeAChild e) bound mutable)
            (computeWalkVec walk (nodeBChildren e) bound mutable)
            0
          }
        (computeFail walk
          "compute callback contains a reference, allocation, heap store or non-numeric form"))
    }))

(:: computeCheckRequests (-> TC Int Int))
(fn (computeCheckRequests tc i)
  {
    (let requests (tcComputeRequests tc))
    (if (>= i (vecLen requests))
      0
      {
        (let request (vecGet requests i))
        (let savedModule (tcCurModule tc))
        (let savedUnit (tcCurUnit tc))
        {
          (tcSetCurModule tc request.module)
          (tcSetCurUnit tc request.unit)
          (computeWalkExpr
            (ComputeWalk :tc tc :site request.site :unit request.siteUnit :seen vecNew :failed 0)
            request.body request.bound vecNew)
          (tcSetCurModule tc savedModule)
          (tcSetCurUnit tc savedUnit)
          (computeCheckRequests tc (+ i 1))
        }
      })
  })

; Syntax lookup views are confined to the type index's tagged entry boundary.
(:: tyFindSyntax (-> TC Int String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(fn (tyFindSyntax tc kind name last)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFind tc kind name last))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyAlias node) (Some node))
      ((TySubtype node) (Some node))
      ((TyDecl node) (Some node))
      ((TyStruct _) None)
      ((TyData _) None)
      ((TyCtor _ _) None))
    None))

(:: tyFindAnyVisSyntax (-> TC Int String (Option ASTNode)))
(fn (tyFindAnyVisSyntax tc kind name)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFindAnyVis tc kind name))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyAlias node) (Some node))
      ((TySubtype node) (Some node))
      ((TyDecl node) (Some node))
      ((TyStruct _) None)
      ((TyData _) None)
      ((TyCtor _ _) None))
    None))

(:: tyFindAnyVisFromSyntax (-> TC Int String Int (Option ASTNode)))
(fn (tyFindAnyVisFromSyntax tc kind name last)
  (if (let (Some record) (tyFindAnyVisFrom tc kind name last))
    (match record.payload
      ((TyAlias node) (Some node))
      ((TySubtype node) (Some node))
      ((TyDecl node) (Some node))
      ((TyStruct _) None)
      ((TyData _) None)
      ((TyCtor _ _) None))
    None))
